{"@context":"https://www.openphar.org/ontology/context/pharmacopoeia.jsonld","@graph":[{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/2-phenoxyethanol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"2-Phenoxyethanol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"2-Phenoxyethanol\n.............. o^-OH\nCgH10O2 \nMol. Wt. 138.2\nPhenoxyethanol is 1 -hydroxy-2-phenoxyethane.\nPhenoxyethanol contains not less than 99.0per cent and not\nmore than 100.5 per cent of CsH ;0O2.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A colourless, slightly viscous liquid.\nIdentification ; \n< \n; ,\nTest A may be omitted iftests B and C are .carried out. Tests B \nand C may be omitted if test A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained wfRphenoxyethanol \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of phenoxyethanol.\nB. When examined in the range, 240 nm to 350 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.008 per cent w/v solution in water, shows two absorption \nmaxima at 269 rim and 275 nm and specific absorbance at \n269 nm is 95 to 105 and at 275 nm is 75 to 85; ; . '\nC. Shake 2 ml of Phenoxyethanol with a mixture of 4.0 g of \npotassium permanganate, ■ 5.4 g of sodium carbonate and\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/abacavir-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Abacavir Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Abacavir Oral Solution\nAbacavir Sulphate Oral Solution\nAbacavir Oral Solution contains Abacavir Sulphate equivalent \nto not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent \nof the stated amount of abacavir Ci4H18N6O. It may contain \none or more suitable buffers, colours, flavours, preservatives, \nstabilizers, sweeteners, and suspending agents.\nUsual strength. 20 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/abacavir-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Abacavir Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Abacavir Sulphate\n(Ci4Hi8N6O)2,H2SO4 \nMol. Wt. 670.8\nAbacavir Sulphate is {(LS\\47^)-4-[2-amino-6-(cyclopropyL \namino)977-purin-9-yl]cyclopent-2-enyl}methanol sulphate.\nAbacavir Sulphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of (CWH i8N6O)2, H2SO4, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/abiraterone-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Abiraterone Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Abiraterone Acetate\nC26H33NO2 \nMol.Wt.391.6\nAbiraterone Acetate is 17-(3-pyridinyl)androsta-5,l 6-dien-3//- \nyl acetate.\nAbiraterone Acetate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C2(SH33 NO2, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/absent","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Absent"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Absent \n(Monoclonal) \t\n0-ve (rr or r'r or Cr) \t\nAbsent\n(1gM) Monoclonal blood grouping reagent is mixed with\nThree varieties ofAnti-Human Globulin Serum are recognized: \n(1) a general-purpose polyspecific reagent which, as a \nminimum, contains antibodies specific for immunoglobulin IgG \nand at least the C3d component of human complement (for \nuse in the direct antiglobulin test, it contains this Anti-C3d \nanclAriti-Ig0-activity) and which may be artificially coloured \ngreen; -(2) a-reagent containing antibodies only against\n1 volume of a 40 per cent v/v suspension of human red\nRouleaux. It is defined as a form of pseudo agglutination in \nWhich the red blood cells look like pile of coins. The red blood \ncells lacking the antigen corresponding to the reagent antibody \nshould not show rouleaux formation. For this--f volume of \nAnti- D (IgM) Monoclonal blood grouping reage .Os mixed \na test tube with I volume of 2-3 per cent recteell suspension\ncorpuscles of specific group and compared with the time taken \nby that of the reference standard. Rapid mixing is performed \nby spreading with use of agitation, with a centrifugal spiral \ncm in\nmovetfient to-form a circular reaction surface a ou \ndiamOser. The value for avidity is the mean of three\nimritunoglobulin  IgG (not heavy chain specific) intended for\nmeasurements.\n;- \n3894\nuse in the indirect antiglobulin test, and which may be \nartificially coloured green; and (3) reagents containing \nantibodies specific for individual or selected components of \nhuman complement, such as Anti-C3, and Anti-C3b-C3d-C4, \nor a single class of immunoglobulins, such as Anti-IgG (heavy \nchain specific), used only to identify plasma components \ncoated on the surface of red blood cells. Anti-Human Globulin \nSerum containing Anti-IgG complies with the test for potency, \nin parallel with the Reference Anti-Human Globulin \n(Anti-IgG) Serum (at a 1:4 dilution) when tested with red cells \nsuspended in isotonic saline sensitized with decreasing \namounts of nonagglutinating Anti-D (Anti-Rh o) serum, and \nwith cells sensitized in the same manner with an \nimmunoglobulin IgG Anti-Fya serum of similar potency. \nAnti-Human Globulin Serum containing one or more \nAnti-complement components complies with the tests for \npotency in giving a 2+ agglutination reaction (i.e., agglutinated \ncells dislodged into many small clumps of equal size) by the \nlow-ionic sucrose or sucrose–trypsin procedures when tested \nas recommended in the labelling. Anti-Human Globulin Serum \ncontaining Anti-3Cd activity meets the requirements for \nstability, by potency testing of representative lots every \n3 months during the dating period.\nExpiration date. Its expiration date is not later than 1 year after \nthe date of issue from manufacturer's cold storage (5°, I year; \nor 0°, 2 years).\nStorage. Store at a temperature between 2° and 8°.\nLabelling. The label states the animal source of the product, \nthe specific antibody activities present; the application for \nwhich the reagent is intended; a cautionary statement that it \ndoes not contain antibodies to immunoglobulins or that it \ndoes not contain antibodies to complement components, \nwherever and whichever is applicable; and states that it is for \nin vitro diagnostic use.\nNOTE—The lettering on the label of the general-purpose \npolyspecific reagent is black on a white background. The\nlabel of all other Anti-Human Globulin Serum containers is \nin white lettering on a black background.\nAntithrombin III Concentrate\nHuman Antithrombin III Concentrate\nCategory. Anticoagulant factor.\nAntithrombin Ill concentrate is a preparation of a glycoprotein \nfraction obtained from human plasma that inactivates thrombin \nin the presence of an excess of heparin. It is 015tained from \nplasma that complies with the requirements of th .4 monograph \non Plasma for Fractionation.\nikiNTI-HUMAN GLOBULIN SERUM \nIP 2018 \nBLOOD GROUPING LECTINS ANTI -A 1 \nIP 2018\nWhen reconstituted in the volume of solvent stated on th e\nCover"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/absorbent-cotton-wool","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Absorbent Cotton Wool"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Absorbent Cotton Wool\nAbsorbent Cotton consists of the new fibres or good quality \nnew combers obtained from the seed coat of various species \nof the genus Gossypium Linn., cleaned, purified, bleached \nand carded. It does not contain any compensatory colouring \nmatter.\nCategory. Surgical dressing.\nDescription. White, well-carded fibres of average staple length \nnot less than 10 mm, containing not more than traces of leaf \nresidue, seed coat and other impurities. It offers appreciable \nresistance when pulled and does not shed a significant \nquantity of dust when shaken gently."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/absorbent-lint","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Absorbent Lint"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Absorbent Lint\nLint; Cotton Lint; Unmedicated Lint\nAbsorbent Lint is a cotton cloth of plain weave, on one side \nof which a nep has been raised from either warp or weft yams. \nIt absorbs water readily but its absorbency may be\n2769\nwww.webofpharma.com\nconsiderably reduced by medication, the absorbency of the \nproduct depending upon the medicament incorporated.\nCategory. Surgical dressing.\nDescription. Cotton cloth of plain weave, reasonably free from \nweaving defects, readily tearable in both directions and \nbleached to a good white having on one side a nep raised \nfrom either the warp or weft yams and reasonably free from \nneps; it is clean and reasonably free from leaf, shell and other \nforeign substances. It is made of yarn that is reasonably free \nfrom slubs, snarls and other defects.\nAbsorbent Lint has not less than 98.0 per cent of the \ndimensions stated on the label."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acacia","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acacia"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acacia\nGum Acacia; Indian Gum\nAcacia is the air-hardened, gummy exudation from the stem \nand branches of Vachellia nilotica (Linn.) P.J.H. Hurter and \nMabb. (Fam. Leguminosae), or other species of Acacia. It is \navailable as pieces (tears) or in the form of a powder.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid, antidiabetic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acamprosate-calcium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acamprosate Calcium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acamprosate Calcium\n2\nCioH2OCaN2Q8S2\nMol.Wt.400.5\nAcamprosate Calcium is calcium bis(3-acetamidopropane-\n1-sulphonate).\nAcamprosate Calcium contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of Cl0H?llCaN2OgS2, calculated \non the dried basis.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCategory. Indicated for treatment of alcohol dependence.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acarbose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acarbose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acarbose\nC25H43NO18 \nMol. Wt. 646.0\nAcarbose is O-4,6-dideoxy-4-[[( 15,45, 55,65)-\n4,5,6-trihydroxy-3 -(hydroxymethyl)cyclohex-2-enyl] amino- \na-D-glucopyranosyl-( 1 —>4)-0-a-D- gluco pyranosyl-(l —>4)- \nD-glucopyranose, which is produced by certain strains of \nActinoplanes utahensis.\nAcarbose contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C25H43NOI8, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\nCategory. Antidiabetic. :\nDescription. A white or yellowish, amorphous powder, \nhygroscopic.\n1363\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acebutolol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acebutolol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acebutolol Hydrochloride\nCISH28N2O4,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 372.9\nAcebutolol Hydrochloride is (7f5)-3,-acetyl-4’-(2-hydroxy-\n3-isopropylaminoprdpoxy)butyranilide hydrochloride.\nAcebutolol Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C! gH28N264, HCI, calculated \non the . dried basis.\nCategory. 0rreceptor antagonist; antihypertensive; \nantianginal; antiarrhythmic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aceclofenac","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aceclofenac"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aceclofenac\nCi6H13C12NO4 \nMoi: Wt. 354.2\nAceclofenac is 2-[(2,6-dichlorophenyl)amino] \nphenylacetoxyacetic acid. \n, .\nAceclofenac contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C16Hi3Cl2NO4, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Nonsteroidal antiinflammatory.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acepromazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acepromazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acepromazine Injection\nAcepromazine Maleate Injection\nAcepromazine Inj ection isa sterile solution of Acepromazine\nMaleate in Water for Injections.\nAcepromazine Injection contains not less than 92.5 per cent \nand not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \nacepromazine, C,>H»N,OS.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent \no of 10 mg, of acepromazine \nper’ mi. \n."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acepromazine-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acepromazine Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acepromazine Maleate\nch3\nCOOH\nCOOH\nC19H22N2OS,C4H4O4 \nMol. Wt. 442.5\nAcepromazine Maleate is 2-acetyl-1 0-(3-dimethylaminopropyl) \nphenothiazine hydrogen maleate.\nAcepromazine Maleate contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Cj^^NjOSjC^O^ \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic.\nDescription. Ayellow coloured, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acesulphame-potassium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acesulphame Potassium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acesulphame Potassium\nC4H4KNO4S \nMol. Wt. 201.2\nAcesulphame Potassium is potassium 6-methyl-\n1,2,3-oxathiazin-4-olate 2,2-dioxide.\nAcesulphame Potassium contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101,0-per cent of C4H4KNO4S, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Sweetening agent.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acetaminophen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acetaminophen"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acetaminophen\nhn^ch3\nOH\nC8IlyNO2 \nMol. Wt. 151,2\nParacetamol is 4-hydroxyacetanilide.\nParacetamol contains not jess than 99.0 per cent and not mpre \nthan 101.0 per cent of C8H9NO2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Analgesic; antipyretic.\nDescription. White crystals or a white, crystalline powder.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nPARACETAMOL\nIP2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acetate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acetate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acetate Injection\nDesoxycortone Acetate Injection is a sterile solution of \nDesoxycortone, Acetate in Ethyl Oleate or other suitable ester, \nin a suitable fixed oil, or in any mixture of these. It may contain \nsuitable alcohols.\nDesoxycortone Acetate Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated \namount of desoxycortone acetate, C23H32O4.\"\nUsual strength. 5 mg per ml.\n2044\nip 2022 /"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acetazolamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acetazolamide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acetazolamide\nO H\nh2n Vl-N &\nC4H6N.AS2 . . \nMol. Wt. 222.2\nAcetazolamide is 7V-(5-sulphamoyl-l,3,4-thiadiazol-2-yI) \nacetamide.\n1371\nwww.webofpharma.com\nAcetazolamide contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C4H6N4O3S2, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor; used in the treatment \nof glaucoma.\nDescription. A white to faintly yellowish-white, crystalline \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acetic-acid-ear-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acetic Acid Ear Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acetic Acid Ear Drops\nAcetic Acid Otic Solution\nAcetic Acid Ear Drops is a solutionof Glacial Acetic Acid in a \nsuitable non-aqueous solvent.\nAcetic Acid Ear Drops contain not less than 85.0 per cent and \nnot more than 130.0 per cent of the stated amount of acetic \nacid, C2H4O2.\nUsual strength. 2 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aceton","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aceton"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aceton\nC. Not more than 2.0 per cent.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nwith the following additional requirement.\nDeterm\nine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nDose.] g or 2 g every 12 hours; in severe infections, 8 g per \nday; in gonococcal urethritis, intramuscularly, 500 mg.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.20 Endotoxin \nUnit per mg of cefoperazone sodium.\nTest s( \nexamin\nthition. Dissolve 0.5 g of the substance under \nat t on in 10.0 ml of water.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powders\nReferei \nwater.]\nice solution. Dissolve 0.35 g of acetone in 100.0 ml of \nDilute 10.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with water.\nCefoperazone Sodium intended for use in the manufacture of \nparenteral preparations without a further appropriate \nsterilisation procedure complies with the following \nadditional requirement."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acetylsalicylic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acetylsalicylic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acetylsalicylic Acid\nCOOH\no^ch3 ;\nO\nC*HA \nMol. Wt 180.2\nAspirin is 2-acetoxybenzoic acid.\nAspirin contains not less than 99.5 per cent and not more than\n100.5 per cent of C9HRO4, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory;;Non-steroidal antiinflammatory; anlirheumatic; \nantithrombotic.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aciclovir-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aciclovir Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aciclovir Cream\nAcyclovir Cream\nAciclovir Cream contains Aciclovir in a suitable cream base.\nAciclovir Cream contains not less than 95.0 per. cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of aciclovir, \nQHhNsO,.\nUsual strength. 5 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aciclovir-eye-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aciclovir Eye Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aciclovir Eye Ointment\nAcyclovir Eye Ointment\nAciclovir Eye Ointment is a sterile preparation containing \nAciclovir in a suitable base.\nAciclovir Eye Ointment contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \naciclovir, CsHnN5O3.\nUsual strengths. 3 per cent w/w; 5 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aciclovir-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aciclovir Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aciclovir Oral Suspension\nAcyclovir Oral Suspension\nAciclovir Oral Suspension is a suspension of Aciclovir in a \nsuitable flavoured vehicle.\nAciclovir Oral Suspension contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \naciclovir, C8HnN5O3.\nUsual strengths. 200 mg per 5 ml; 400 mg per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aciclovir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aciclovir"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aciclovir\nAcyclovir\nC8HnN5O3 \nMol. Wt; 225.2\nwww.webofpharma.com\nAciclovir is 2-amino-9-[(2-hydroxyethoxy)methyl]-\n1,9- dihydro-6//-punn-6-one.\nAciclovir contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C8H, >N5CL, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acitretin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acitretin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acitretin\nCACh \nMol. Wt. 326.4\nAcitretin is (2//,4C,6//,8/?)-9-(4-Methoxy-2,3, \n6-trimethylphenyl)-3,7-dimethylnona-2,4,6,8-tetraenoic acid.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nAcitretin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C2iH26O3, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsoriatic.\nDescription. A yellow or greenish yellow ciystalline powder. \nIt shows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/acyclovir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Acyclovir"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Acyclovir\nCSH llN50 3\nMol. Wt. 225.2\nAciclovir is 2-amino-9-[(2-hydroxyethoxy)methyl]-1,9-\ndihydro-6H-purin-6-one.\nAciclovir contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0per cent ofCSHllN50 3, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\n.\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDose. 200 to 800 mg 4 to 5 times daily. By intravenous infusion,\n5 to 10 mg per kg of body weight every 8 hours. By topical\napplication as cream or eye ointment, as appropriate, every 4\nhours.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/additive-06","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Additive 06"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Additive 06\n(EINECS)/Pigment blue 29 (CI77007) \nultramarine blue\nAdditive 07\n2,6-bis(l, l-dimethylethyl)-4-methylphenol\n- butylhydroxytoluene,\n- 2,6-bis (1,1 -dimethylethyl)-4-methylphenol,\n- 2,6-di-terr-butyl-4-methylphenol\nAdditive 08\nethylene bis[3,3 -bis[3 -(1,1 -dimethylethyl)-\n- ethylene bis[3,3-bis[3-(l,l-dimethylethyl)-4-\n4—hydroxyphenyl] butanoate]\nhydroxyphenyl] butanoate],\n- butanoic acid, 3,3-bis[3-(I,l-dimethylethyl)-4-hydroxyphenyl]-\nArsenic, lead, cadmium, mercury, cobalt, nickel, and \nvanadium. Report the measured value in solution S3 at values \nabove 0.01 mg per litre (ppm), corresponding to 0.025 pg per g. \nIf the measured values are below these values, report the \nresult as less than 0.01 mg per litre (ppm), corresponding to \nless than 0.025 pg per g. Additional acceptance criteria for \ncertain metals are provided as follows.\n6.2.1.2.10. Polymer Additives\nThe polymer additive is given in Table 2.\n6.2.1.3. Plastic Containers for Ophthalmic Preparations\nPlastic containers for ophthalmic preparations are made from \nplastic composed of a mixture of homologous compounds \nhaving a range of molecular weights. Such plastics frequently \ncontain other substances such as residues from the \npolymerisation process, plasticisers, stabilisers, antioxidants, \nlubricants and pigments. For deciding the suitability of a plastic \nfor use as a container for ophthalmic preparations, factors \nsuch as the composition ofthe plastic, processing and cleaning \nprocedures, contacting media, adhesives, adsorption and \npermeability of preservatives, conditions of storage, etc. should \nbe evaluated by appropriate additional specific tests.\nPlastic containers for ophthalmic preparations comply with \nthe following tests.\nLeakage test; Collapsibility test. Comply with the tests \ndescribed under Plastic Containers for Parenteral Preparations \n(6.2.1.1).\nSynonyms Name\n1,2-ethanediyl ester,\n- ethylene bis[3,3-bis(3-tert-butyl-4-hydroxy-phenyl) butyrate].\ncont.\n1261\nwww.webofpharma.com\nName of Polymer additive as per \nIUPAC Rules\nPolymer \nAdditive\nAdditive 09\nmethanetetryltetramethyl tetrakis[3-[3,5- \nbis (1,1 -dimethylethyl)-4-hydroxyphenyl] \npropanoate]\nAdditive 10\n4,42,43 -[(2,4,6-trimethylbenzene-: \n(1,3,5-triyl)tris methylene)]tris[2,6-bis\n(1,1 -dimethylethyl)phenol]\nAdditive 11\noctadecyl 3-[3,5-bis(l ,l-dimethylethyl)-4- \nhydroxyp henyl] prop ano ate\nhydroxyphenyl]-, octadecyl ester.\nAdditive 12\ntris[2,4-bis( 1,1 -dimethylethyl)phenyl] • \nphosphite\nAdditive 13\nl,3,5-tris[3,5-bis(l,l-dimethyIethyl)-4- \nhydroxybenzyl]-1,3,5-triazine-2,4,6 \n(17/,3£/,57/)-trione\nAdditive 15\n1,12 -disulfanediyldioctadecane\n- dioctadecyl disulfide,\n- octadecane, 1,12 -dithio-.\nAdditive 16\ndidodecyl 3,32 -sulfanediyldipropanoate\n- didodecyl 3,32 -thiodipropionate,\nAdditive 18 ComponentI\n6.2. CONTAINERS\nIP 2022\nSynonyms Name\n- pentaerythrityl tetrakis[3-(3,5-di-Zer/-butyl-4-\nhydroxyphenyl)propionate],\n- 2,2-bis[[[3-[3,5-bis(l,l-dimethylethyI)-4-hydroxyphenyl]\npropanoyl]oxy]methyl]propane- 1,3-diyl 3-[3,5-bis( 1,1 - \ndimethylethyl)-4-hydroxyphenyl] propanoate,\n- benzenepropanoic acid, 3,5-bis(l,l-dimethyl-ethyl)-4-hydroxy-\n2,2-bis(hydroxymethyl) propane-1,3-diol ester (4:1),\n- 2,2-bis(hydroxymethyl)propane-l,3-diol tctrakis[3-(3,5-di-fe?7-\nbutyl-4-hydroxyphenyl) propionate].\n- 2,22 ,23 ,6,62 ,63 -hexa-tert-butyl-4,42 ,43 -[(2,4,6-trimethyl-\n1,3,5-benzenetriyl)trismethylene] triphenol,\n- 1,3,5-tris [3,5-di-ter/-butyl-4-hydroxybenzyl]-2,4,6-trimethyl­\nbenzene,\n- phenol, 4,42 ,43 -[(2,4,6-trimethyl-l,3,5-benzenetriyl)tris\n(methylene)] tris [2,6-bis( 1,1-dimethylethyl).\n- octadecyl 3-(3,5-di-tert-butyl-4-hydroxyphenyl)propionate,\n- propanoic acid, 3-[3,5-bis(l,l-dimethylethyl)-4-\n- tris(2,4-di-tcr/-butylphenyl) phosphite,\n- phenol, 2,4-bis( 1,1 -dimethylethyl)-, phosphite (3:1), \n- 2,4-bis(l,l-dimethylethyl)phenyl, phosphite.\n- l,3,5-tris(3,5-di-tert-butyI-4-hydroxybenzyl)-s-triazine-\n2,4,6(l//,3//,57T)-trione,\n- l,3,5-triazine-2,4,6(l/43#,577)-trione,.l,3,5-tris[[3,5-b"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/adefovir-dipivoxil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Adefovir Dipivoxil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Adefovir Dipivoxil\nC20H32N5O8P \nMol. Wt. 501.5\nAdefovir Dipivoxil is 9-[2-bis[(pivaloyloxy)methoxy] \nphosphonyl]-methoxy]ethyl]adenine.\nAdefovir Dipivoxil contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C20H32N5()8P, calculated on the \ndried basis? ■\nCategory, Antiretroviral.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/adenosine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Adenosine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Adenosine Injection\nAdenosine Injection is a sterile solution of Adenosine in Water \nfor Injection. It may contain Sodium Chloride.\nAdenosine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nadenosine, Ci0Hi3N5O4.\nUsual strength. 3 mgperml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/adenosine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Adenosine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Adenosine\nC10Hi3N5O4 \nMol. Wt. 267.2\nAdenosine is 9-p-D-ri bofuranosy 1-9//-purin-6-ami ne.\nAdenosine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of CioHi3N504j calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiepileptic.\nDescription. A white or off white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/adipic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Adipic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Adipic Acid\nHOOC\nCOOH\nCgH10O4 \nMol. Wt. 146.1\nAdipic Acid is 1,6-Hexadioic acid.\nAdipic Acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C6Hi0O4, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/adjuvant","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Adjuvant"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Adjuvant \nsystem 04\n(AS 04)\nLipid A) and\nAluminium phosphate\nAlum adsorbed\nTLR4 agonist\n• Influanza vaccine\nMF 59\nOil in water \nemulsion\n• Pandemic HlN1 \nInfluanza vaccine\nAS03\nOil in water \nemulsion\nAS01b\nMonophosphoryl • Herpes zoster vaccine \nlipid A (MPL) and \nQS-21, a natural \ncompound extracted \nfrom the Chilean \nsoapbark tree, \ncombined in a \nliposomal formulation\nCpG 1018\nTLR 9 agonists\n• HEPLISAV-B\n453\nwww.webofpharma.com\nwww.webofpharma.com\nJKDIAN pharmacopoeia 2022\n2.8. TESTS ON BLOOD AND BLOOD-RELATED PRODUCTS\n2.8. TESTS ON BLOOD AND BLOOD-RELATED PRODUCTS\n? 8.1. Nucleic Acid Amplification Technique\n457\n2.8.2. Assay of Human Anti-D Immunoglobulin\n460\n2.8.3- Activated Coagulation Factors\n464\n2.8.4- Assay of Human Coagulation Factor II\n464\n2.8.5- Assay of Human Coagulation Factor VII\n465\n2.8.6. Assay of Human Coagulation Factor Vin\n466\n2.8.7. Assay of Human Coagulation Factor IX\n467\n2.8.8. Assay of Human Coagulation Factor X\n468\n2.8.9. Assay of Heparin in Coagulation Factors\n469\n2.8.10. Determination of ABO Blood Group and Rh Group\n469\n2.8.11. Determination of Haemoglobin by Photometry\n473\n2.8.12. Assay of Human Anti-thrombin HI\n473\n2.8.13. Anti-AandAnti-B Haemagglutinins (Indirect Method)\n474\n2.8.14. Assay of Human Coagulation Factor XI\n474\n2.8.15. Assay of Human Protein C\n474\n2.8.16. Assay of Human Protein S\n475\n2.8.17. Assay of Human Plasmin Inhibitor (a2-Antiplasmin)\n476\n455\nwww.webofpharma.com\nwww.webofpharma.com\n2.8.1, NUCLEIC ACID AMPLIFICATION TECHNIQUES\nIP 2022'\n2 8.1. Nucleic Acid Amplification Techniques\nNucleic acid amplification techniques are based on two different \napproaches.\nj Amplification of a target nucleic acid sequence using\npolymerase chain reaction (PCR), ligase chain reaction \n(LCR), or isothermal ribonucleic acid (RNA) amplification;\n? Amplification of a hybridisation signal using, for example,\nfor deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA), the branched DNA \n(hDNA) method. In this case signal amplification is \nachieved without subj ecting the nucleic acid to repetitive \ncycles of amplification.\nTest method\n[nthis general chapter, the PCR method is described as the \nreference technique. Alternative methods may be used, if \nthey comply with the quality requirements described below.\nScope. This section establishes the requirements for sample \npreparation, in vitro amplification of DNA sequences and \ndetection ofthe specific PCR product. With the aid of PCR, \ndefined DNA sequences can be detected. RNA sequences \ncan also be detected following reverse transcription of the \nRNA to complementary DNA (cDNA) and subsequent \namplification.\nPrinciple of the method. PCR is a procedure that allows \nspecific in vitro amplification of segments of DNA or of RNA \nafter reverse transcription into cDNA.\nFollowing denaturation of double-stranded DNA into single- \nstranded DNA, two synthetic oligonucleotide primers of \nopposite polarity, anneal to their respective complementary \nsequences in the DNA to be amplified. The short double; \nstranded regions which form as a result of specific base pairing \nbetween the primers and the complementary DNA sequence, \nborder the DNA segment to be amplified and serve as starting \npositions for in vitro DNA synthesis by means of a heat- \nstable DNA polymerase.\nAmplification of the DNA occurs in cycles consisting of (a) \nheat denaturation of the nucleic acid (target sequence) into 2 \nsingle strands; (b) specific annealing of the primers to the \ntarget sequence under suitable reaction conditions; (c) \nextension of the primers, which are bound to both single \nstrands, by DNA polymerase at a suitable temperature (DNA \nsynthesis).\nRepeated cycles of heat denaturation, primer annealing and \nDNA synthesis results in an exponential amplification of the \nDNA segment limited by the primers.\nThe specific PCR product known as an amplicon can be \ndetected by a variety of methods of appropriate specificity'\nand sensitivity.'\nMultiplex PCR assays use several primer pairs designed for\nsimultaneous amplific"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/administration","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Administration"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Administration\nHuman Normal Immunoglobulin for Intravenous \nAdministration is a liquid or, freeze-dried preparation containing \nimmunoglobulins, mainly immunoglobulin G (IgG). Other \nproteins may be present. Human normal immunoglobulin for \nIntravenous Administration contains the IgG antibodies of \nnormal subjects. This monograph does not apply to products \nintentionally prepared to contain fragments or chemically \nmodified IgG.\nHuman Normal Immunoglobulin for Intravenous \nAdministration is obtained from plasma that complies with \nthe requirements of the monograph on Plasma for \nFractionation. No antibiotic is added to the plasma used: :\nProduction ;\nThe method of preparation includes a step or steps that have \nbeen shown to remove or to inactivate known agents of \ninfection; if substances are used for inactivation of viruses, it \nshall have been shown that any residues present in the final\nHUMAN NORMAL IMMUNOGLOBULIN FOR INTRAVENOUS USE\nIP 2022\n[1’2022\nHUMAN NORMAL IMMUNOGLOBULIN FÖR INTRAVENOUS USE\nRubella antigen. Suitable rubella antigen for \nhaémagglutination-inhibition titre (HIT). Titre > 256 HA units;\nproduct have no adverse'effects on the patients treated wit}) \nimmunoglobulin.\ncentrifugation (1000 g forTO minutes) of the cuvette/tést-tube \nand remove 1900 pl of the: supernatant: Replace the\n1900 pl with albumin barbital buffer solution- and repeat the \nwhole of the washing procedure; finally leaving a volume of \n200 pl. Test samples may be stored in scaled cuvette/test- \ntubes at4° for24 hours. \n; : ;i !. . ;\nThe product shall have been shown, by suitable tests*j| \nanimals and evaluation during clinical trials, to be well toleratef \nwhen administered intravenously. \n$\npreparationof tanned human red blood cells. Separate human \nred blood cells by centrifuging an appropriate volume of \nstabilised human blood and wash the cells at least 3 times \nwith phosphate-buffered saline pH 7.2 and suspend at 2 per \ncent v/v in phosphate-buffered saline pH 7.2. Dilute 0.1 ml of \ntannic acid solution to 7.5 ml with phosphate-buffered saline \npH 7.2 (final concentration 1.3 mg per litre). Mix 1 volume of\n■ > W- ■: ■ ■ :■ \n■ \n■ ■ - \n; \n' . \n.\nHuman Normal Immunoglobulin for Intravenous \nAdministration is prepared from pooled material from not fewer \nthan 1,000 donors by a method that has been shown to yield \na product that (a) does not transmit infection; (b) at an \nimmunoglobulin concentration of 50g per litre, contains: \nantibodies for at least 2 of which (one viral and one bac terial) \nan International Standard or Reference Preparation is available, \nthe concentration of such antibodies being at least 3 timqs \nthat in the initial pooled material; (c) has a defined di str ib ution: \nof immunoglobulin G subclasses; (d) complies with the test \nfor Fc function of immunoglobulin.\nComplement-initiated haemolysis. To measure haemolysis, \nadd 600 pl of albumin barbital buffer solution warmed to 37° \nto the test sample, fe-suspend the cells carefully by repeated \npipetting (not fewer than 5 times) and place the cuvette in the \nthermostated cuvette holder of a spectrophotometer.-After \n2 minutes, add 200 pl of diluted guinea-pig complement \n(125 to 200 CH50/ml), mix thoroughly by pipetting twice and \nstart immediately after the second pipetting the time-dependent \nrecording of absorbance at 541 nm, using albumin barbital \nbuffer solution as the compensation liquid. Stop the \nmeasurement if absorbance as a function of time has clearly \npassed the inflexion point.\nthe freshly prepared dilution with 1 volume of human red \nblood bell suspensión and incubate at 37° for 10 minutes. \nCollect the cells by centrifugation (400 to 800 g for 10 minutes), \ndiscard, the supernatant and:.wash the cells once with \nphosphate-buffered saline pH 7.2. Resuspend the: tanned cells\nat 1 per cent v/v in phosphate-buffered, saline pH 7.2.\nAntigen coating of tanned human red blood cells. Take a \nsuitable volume (Rs) of tanned cells, add 0.2 ml of rubella \nantigen per"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/adrenaline-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Adrenaline Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Adrenaline Injection\nAdrenaline Bitartrate Injection; Adrenaline Acid Tartrate\nInjection; Adrenaline Tartrate Injection; Epinephrine \nTartrate Injection\nAdrenaline Injection is a sterile, isotonic solution containing \n0.18 per cent w/v of Adrenaline Tartrate in Water for Injections.\nAdrenaline Injection contains adrenaline tartrate equivalent \nnot less than 90.0 per cent and not more than 115.0 per cent \nw/v of the stated amount of adrenaline, C9II (3NO3.\nUsual strength. 0.1 per cent w/v.\nDescription. Aclear, colourless or almost colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aerosol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aerosol"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aerosol\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/agomelatine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Agomelatine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Agomelatine\nAgomelatine isAL[2-(7-methoxy-l-naphthyl)ethyl] acetamide.\nAgomelatine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C15HnNO, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. A white to cream colour crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aitor","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aitor"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aitor\n• --\non  the anhydrous and acetone-free basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nThe relative retention times with respect to cefuroxime axetil \ndiastereoisomer A for cefuroxime axetil diastereoisomer B is \nabout 0.9, for cefuroxime axetil D 3-isomers is about 1.2 and for \nE-isomers is about 1.7 and 2.1.\nDose. Intr,i muscularly or intravenously, 750 mg thrice daily; in\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the two principal peaks is at least 3.0.\nsevere infection, 1.5 g thrice daily; total dose 3 to 6 g daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram at least twice the retention time of the principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the \narea of any secondary peak is not greater than the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c) (1.0 per cent) and the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not greater than 5 times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (c) (5.0 per cent). Ignore any peak with an area less \nthan 0.1 times the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) (0.1 per cent).\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks corresponding to cefuroxime \naxetil diastereoisomer A and cefuroxime axetil 1) 3-isomer is \nnot less than 1.5."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/albendazole-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Albendazole Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Albendazole Oral Suspension\nAlbendazole Oral Suspension is a suspension of albendazole\nin a suitable vehicle.-\nAlbendazole Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nalbendazole C|2Hi5N?O2S. ............ ■\nUsual strength. 200 mg per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/albendazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Albendazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Albendazole\nH Ox CH3\nHc £ ¥ /^H\nC12H15N3O2S \nMol. Wt 265.3\nAlbendazole is methyl 5 -propylthio-1 ff-benzimidazol- \n2-yl-carbamate.\nAlbendazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than:I02;0 per cent of Cl2H]5N3O2S, calculated bn the \ndried basis. • \n\" \n'\nCategory. Anthelmintic.\nDescription. A white to pale buff-coloured powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/albuterol-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Albuterol Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Albuterol Sulphate\n(Ch^NO^SCh \nMol. Wt. 576.7\nSalbutamol Sulphate is (R5^-l-(4-hydroxy-3-hydro^ymethyl- \nphenyI)-2-(terZ-butylamino)ethanol sulphate. \n;;\nSalbutamol Sulphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than lOl.Oper cent6f(CI3H21NO3)2, IT;SO4,calculated \non the dried basis. .\nCategory .Beta-adrenoceptor agonist.\nDescription^ A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/albuterol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Albuterol"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Albuterol\nOH\nHO\nOH\nMol. Wt. 239.3\nSalbutamol is (RS)-I-(4-hydroxy-3-hydroxymethylphenyl)-\n2-(tert-butylamino)ethanol.\nSalbutamol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0per cent ofC13Hz1N03, calculatedon the dried basis.\nCategory. Beta-adrenoceptor agonist.\nDose. Orally, 6 to 16 mg daily, in divided doses; by inhalation,\nfor chronic bronchial asthma oras a prophylactic, 2 inhalations\nof 100 !!g, 3 or 4 times daily; for the relief of acute\nbronchospasm, 1 or 2 inhalations of 100 !!g as a single dose\nwhen required, up to a maximumof8 inhalations in 24 hours;\nby subcutaneous orintramuscular injection, 500 !!g, repeated\nevery 4 hours, if necessary; by slow intravenous injection,\n250 !!g, repeated ifnecessary.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSALBUTAMOL"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/alcohol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alcohol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Alcohol\nHjC^/OH\nCH3CH2OH \nMol. Wt. 46.1\nEthanol contains not less than 99.0 per cent v/v of CJ16O.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (solvent).\nDescription. A clear, colourless, mobile and volatile liquid; \nhygroscopic. Readily volatilises even at low temperature; boils \nat 78°; flammable, burning with a blue, smokeless flame.\nfdentification\nA. Mix 0.25 ml in. a small beaker with 1 ml of potassium \npermanganate solution and 0.25 ml of dilute sulphuric acid\nand cover the beaker immediately with a filter paper, moistened \nwith a solution freshly prepared by dissolving 0.1g of sodium \nnitroprusside and 0.5 g of piperazine hydrate' in 5 ml of water, \nan intense blue colour is produced on the filter paper, the \ncolour becoming lighter after a few minutes.\nB. \nTo 5 ml of a 0.5 per cent v/v solution add 1 ml of 1Msodium \nhydroxide followed by slow addition of 2 ml of iodine solution-, \nthe. odour of iodoform develops and a yellow, precipitate is \nproduced.\nTests -\nRelative density (2.4.29). 0.7871 to 0.7902, determined at 25°.\nAppearance of solution. Dilute 5.0 ml to 100.0 ml with water. \nThe solution is clear (2.4.1). Cool to 10° for 30 minutes; the \nsolution remains clear.\nAcidity or alkalinity. To 20 ml add 0.25 ml ofphenolphthalein \nsolution-, the solution, remains colourless and requires not \nmore than 0.2 ml of 0.1 Msodium hydroxideXo produce a pink \ncolour.\n2286\nIP 2022\nMethanol. To 1 drop, add 1 drop of water, 1 drop of du \nphosphoric acid and 1 drop of potassium P^manga^\nsolution. Mix, allow to stand for. 1 minute and add sodiuln\nbisulphite solution dropwise until the permanganate colour \nis discharged. If a brown colour remains, add 1 drop of diht^ \nphosphoric acid. To the colourless solution add 5 ml of freshf\nprepared chromotropic acid solution and heat on a water \nbath at 60° for 10 minutes; no violet colour is produced.\nForeign organic substances. Clean a glass-stoppered cylinder \nthoroughly with hydrochloric acid, rinse with water and finally \nrinse with the substance under examination. Put 20 ml jn the \ncylinder, cool to about 15° and then add from a carefully \ncleaned pipette 0.1 ml of 0.1Mpotassium permanganate. Mix \nat once by inverting the stoppered cylinder and allow to stand \nat 15° for 5 minutes; the pink colour does not entirely disappear\n2-Propanol and 2-methyl-2-propanol. To 1 ml add 3 ml ofwer \nand 10 ml of mercuric sulphate solution and heat in a boiling \nwater-bath; no precipitate is formed within 3 minutes.\nAldehydes. Not more than 10 ppm, determined by the following \nmethod. To 5.0 ml add 5 ml of water and 1 ml of decolorised \nmagenta solution and allow to stand for 30 minutes. Any \ncolour produced is not more intense than that produced by \ntreating in the same manner 5.0 ml of a 0.001 per cent w/v \nsolution of redistilled acetaldehyde in aldehyde-free ethanol \n(95 per cent).\nBenzene and related substances. Determine by gas \nchromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution (a). The substance under examination.\nTest solution (b). A 0.03 per cent v/v solution of 4- \nmethylpentan-2-ol in test solution (a). '\nReference solution (a). A 0.02 per cent v/v solution of \nanhydrous methanol in test solution (a).\nReference solution (b).KQA per cent v/v solution, each of, \nanhydrous methanol arid acetaldehyde in test solution (a). \nDilute 0.1ml of the solution to 10.0 ml with test solution (a).\nReference solution (c). A 0.3 per cent v/v solution of acetal in \ntest solution (a). Dilute 0.1 mlofthe solution to 10.0ml with\ntest solution (a).\nReference solution (d). A 0.1 per cent v/v solution of benzene\nin test solution (a). Dilute 0.1 ini ofthe solution to 50.0 ml with\ntest solution (a). ....\nChromatographic system ■\n. - a capillary column 30 mx 0.32 nun, packed with 6 pet\ncent cyanopropylphenyl and 94 per cent of dimetny\n■■ polysiloxane (film thickness 1.8 pm), \n- temperature /\ncolumn 40° for 0-12 minutes, 40° to 240° for 12-\nminutes and 240° for 32-42 minutes.\nwww.webofpharma.com\ninlet port at 200° and detector at 280°,\nflarne ion"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/alfuzosin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alfuzosin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Alfuzosin Hydrochloride\nC19H27N5O4,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 425.9\nAlfuzosin Hydrochloride is (2/?5)-W-[3-[(4-amino-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-2-yl)methylamino]propyl]tetrahydro- \nfuran-2-carboxamide hydrochloride.\nAlfuzosin Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C9H27N5O4,1IC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Indicated in benign prostatic hyperplasia.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder, \nslightly hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/alginic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alginic Acid","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Alginic Acid\nPolymannuronic Acid\nAlginic acid is a hydrophilic colloidal mixture of polyuronic\nacids, [(CftHgOejn], composed of residues of D-mannuronic \nacid and L-guluronic acid extracted with dilute alkali from \nvarious species of.brown seaweeds (Fam. Phaeophyceae).\nAlginic Acid contains not less than 19.0 per cent and not more \nthan 25.0 per cent of carboxylic acid groups (COOH), calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white to yellowish-white, fibrous powder.\nIP 2022\nALGINIC ACID"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/allantoin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Allantoin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Allantoin\nC4H6N4O3 \nMol. Wt 158.1\nAllantoin is (7?S)-(2,5-dioxo-4-imidazolidinyl)urea.\nAllantoin contains not less than 9,8.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 percent of C4H6N4O3, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Astringent.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/allopurinol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Allopurinol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Allopurinol\nC5H4N4O \nMol.Wt. 136.1\nAllopurinol is a tautomeric mixture of l#-pyrazolo[3,4-cZ] \npyrimidin-4-ol and 1,5-dihydro-47/-pyrazolo[3,4-6(jpyrimidin- \n4-one.\nAllopurinol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 percent of C5H4N4O, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Uricosuric agent.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aloes","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aloes"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aloes\nAloes is the dried juice of the leaves of Aloe barbadensis \nMiller (A. vera Linn), known in commerce as Curacao Aloes or \nBarbados Aloes, or oIvL/mvi Miller and hybrids of this species \nwith A. africana Miller and A. spicata Baker, known in \ncommerce as Cape Aloes (Fam. Liliaceae). Indian Aloes of \ncommerce is obtained from A. barbadensis.\nAloes contains not less than 50.0 per . cent of water-soluble \nextractive. Curacao Aloes contains not less than 18.0 per cent \nand Cape Aloes not less than 28.0 per cent of \nhydroxyanthracene derivatives, calculated as ^anhydrous \nbarbaloin.\nCategory. Laxative.\nIP 2022\nALOES\nDescription. Unground Curacao Aloes— Brownish-black, \nopaque masses; fractured surface uneven, waxy and somewhat \nresinous.\nUnground Cape Aloes — Dark-brown or greenish-brown to \nolive-brown masses; fractured surface shiny and conchoidal."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/alpha-amylase","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alpha Amylase"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Alpha Amylase\nDiastase\nAlpha Amylase is an amylolytic enzyme or amixture of enzymes \nobtained from fungi such as Aspergillus oryzae or from a \nnon-pathogenic variant of bacteria'such as Bacillus subtilis \nand with the specific activity for converting starch into dextrin \nand maltose. It may contain suitable harmless, diluents such \nas Lactose or Dibasic Calcium Phosphate.\nAlpha Amylase has afnylase activity of not less than 800 Units \nwhich represents the number of grams of dry,, soluble maize or \ncorn starch digested by 1.0 g of Alpha Amylase , under the \nconditions of the Assay.\nCategory. Digestive enzyme\nDescription. A. cream to light brown-coloured powder; \nhygroscopic.\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/alprazolam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alprazolam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Alprazolam\nC17H13C1N4 \nMol. Wt. 308.8\nAlprazolam is 8-chloro-l-methyl-6-phenyl-4#-l,2,4-triazolo \n[4,3-u][ 1,4]benzodiazepine.\nAlprazolam contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102;0 percent of C17Hi3C1N4, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anxiolytic.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystallirie powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/alprostadil-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alprostadil Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Alprostadil Injection\nAlprostadil Injection is. a sterile solution of Alprostadil in \nDehydrated Alcohol.\nAlprostadil Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 115.0 per. cent of the stated amount of \nalprostadil, C2oH3405.\nUsual strength. 500 pg per ml for intravascular or. intramuscular \nuse."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/alprostadil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alprostadil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Alprostadil\nC20H34O5 \nMol. Wt. 354.5\nAlprostadil is (11 a, 13E, 15S)-1 1,15-dihydroxy-9-oxo-prost-\n13-en-l-oicacid.\nAlrostadil contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.5 per cent of C20H34O5, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Indicated in erectile dysfunction.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellowish, crystalline powder.\nALPROSTADIL\nIP 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/alum-treated","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Alum Treated"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Alum Treated\nHaemorrhagic:Septicaemia Vaccine Alum Treated is 4 formalized \nculture.of | Pasteuréila :multocida.in nutrient broth: treated\nwith potash alum: The-vaccine is‘a’ white suspension containing \ndead-bactetia:and:alum:: \nAe \n: \nHag\nINCLUSION BODY HEPATITIS (BH) VACCINE, INACTIVATED"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aluminium-hydroxide-gel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aluminium Hydroxide Gel"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aluminium Hydroxide Gel\nAluminiumHydroxide Suspension;AluminiumHydroxide\nMixture\nAluminium Hydroxide Gel is an aqueous suspension of\nhydrated aluminium oxide together with varying quantities of\nbasic aluminium carbonate and bicarbonate. It may contain\nGlycerin, Sorbitol, Sucrose or Saccharin as sweetening agents\nand Peppermint Oil or other suitable flavours. It may also\ncontain suitable antimicrobial agents.\nAluminium Hydroxide Gel contains not less than 3.5 per cent\nand not more than 4.4 per cent w/w ofAl20 3•\nCategory. Antacid.\nIP2010\nDose. 7.5 to 15 ml.\nDescription. A white, viscous suspension, translucent in thin\nlayers; small amounts ofclearliquidmayseparate on standing."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aluminium-magnesium-silicate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aluminium Magnesium Silicate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aluminium Magnesium Silicate\nAl2MgO8Si2 \nMol. Wt. 262.4\nAluminium Magnesium Silicate is mixture of particles with \ncolloidal particle size of montmorillonite and saponite, free \nfrom grit and non-swellable ore.\nAluminium Magnesium Silicate contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent each of the \nstated amount of aluminium, Al and magnesium, Mg, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A almost white powder, granules or plates."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amalaki","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amalaki"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amalaki\nEmblicMyrobalan; Indian Gooseberry; Phyllanthus \nemblica\nAmalaki consists of the dried fruit pericarp of Phyllanthus \nemblica Linn. (Fam. Euphorbiaceae).\nAmalaki contains hot less than 1.0 per cent wAv gallic acid \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiscorbutic, antacid, carminative, hcpato- \nprotective, raktapitta\nDescription. The dried fruit has a highly shriveled and wrinkled \nexternal surface. The taste is sour and astringent followed by \ndelicately sweet tinge."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amaltas","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amaltas"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amaltas\nRS \t\nTEST \n(Under UV light at 366 nm)\nTLC Chromatogram of Amaltas\nAmarbel\nRS \t\nTEST\n(Under UV light at 2506\nIP 2018 \n3.2. THIN LAYER CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND PROCESSED HERBS \nIP 201S \n3.2. THIN LAYER CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND PROCESSED HERBS\nAmla Juice Powder\n**4***ANO,404\nRS \t\nTEST\nRS TEST\nRS \t\nTEST\n(Under day light after spraying\n(Under UV light at 366 nm after\n(Under UV light at 254 nm)\nwith anisaldehyde sulphuric\nspraying with anisaldehyde\nacid reagent)\nsulphuric acid reagent)\nTLC Chromatogram ofAmla Juice Powder\nAmra\nRS \t\nTEST\nRS \t\nTEST \n(Under day light after spraying\nRS \t\nTEST\nRS \t\nTEST\nLJnder-U`Ulight at 366 nm)\nUnder. UVtat-366nm \n )\n(Under UV light at 254 nm)\nwith vanillin glacial acetic acid\nreagent) \nTLC Chromatogram Of Amarbel\nTLC Chromatogram ofAmra\nRS \t\nTEST \n(Under UV light at 366 nm)\nRS \t\nTEST \n(Under UV light at 254 nm)\nTLC Chromatogram of Anantmula"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amantadine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amantadine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amantadine Hydrochloride\nnh2\n’ HCI\nC-,H17N,HC1 \n, \n■ -Mol. Wt. 1.87.7\nAmantadine Hydrochloride is tricyclo[3.3.1.13,7]dec-\n1-ylamine hydrochloride.\nAmantadine Hydrochloride contains not less then 98:5 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of CioHi7N,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiviral; antiparkinsonian.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nsublimes when heated."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amarbel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amarbel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amarbel\nAkashbel; Nirmali; Cuscuta reflexa\n4166\n^2022.\nAmarbel contains not less than 0.1 per cent w/w of quercetin \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory, \npurgative.\nAntidiabetic, aphrodisiac, anhagmg, hepatopmtective\nDescription. The dried whole plant is yellowish brown to dark \nbrown in colour and 5 -15 cm in length and 1 -2 mm in thickness \nand has characteristic odour."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ambroxol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ambroxol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ambroxol Hydrochloride\nC13HlsBr2N3O,HCl \nMol. Wt. 414.6\nAmbroxol hydrochloride is Ac;/7.y-4-|(2-amino-\n3,5- dibromobenzyl)amino]cyclohexanol hydrochloride.\nAmbroxol Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C13H]gBr2N2O,HCI, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Mucolytic.\nDescription. A white or yellowish crystalline powder.."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amidone-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amidone Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amidone Hydrochloride\nC2iH27NO,HC1\nMol. Wt. 345.9\nMethadone Hydrochloride is (7?5)-dimethyl-(l-methyl- \n4-oxo-3,3-diphenylhexyl)amine hydrochloride.\nMethadone Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 100.5 per cent of C21H27NO,HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Narcotic analgesic.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amifostine-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amifostine for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amifostine for Injection\nAmifostine for Injection is a sterile, crystalline material suitable \nfor parenteral use.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile 0.9 per cent \nw/v sodium chloride injection, immediately before use?'\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under. \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nAmifostine for Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent , of the stated amount of \namifostine, CTUN2O3PS.\nThe contents of the. sealed containers comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations (powder \nfor Injections), and with the following requirements.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amifostine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amifostine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amifostine\nOH\n(\nH\nC5H15N2O3PS,3H2O \nMol. Wt. 268.3\nAmifostine is Ethanethiol, 2-[(3-aminopropyI)amino]-, \ndihydrogen phosphate (ester), trihydrate. .\nAmifostine contains not less than 78.0 per cent and not more \nthan 82.0 per cent of CjHiih^OjPS, calculated on as-is basis.\nCategory; Cytoprotective agent.'\nDescription. A white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amikacin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amikacin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amikacin Injection\nAmikacin Sulphate Injection\nAmikacin Injection is a sterile solution of Amikacin Sulphate \nin Water for Injections or of Amikacin in Water for Injections \nprepared with the aid of Sulphuric Acid.\nAmikacin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of amikacin, \nC22H43N5O]j.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 100 mg or 500 mg of \namikacin in2 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amikacin-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amikacin Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amikacin Sulphate\nC22H43N5OI3,1.8H2SO4 \nMol. Wt. 762.1\nC22H43N5O]3,2H2SO4 \nMol, Wt. 781.8\nAmikacin Sulphate is (5)-O-3-amino-3-deoxy-a-D-gluco- \npyranosyI-(l-->6)-O-[6-amino-6-deoxy-a-D-glucopyranosyl \n(l->4)]-7V1-(4-amino-2-hydroxy-l-oxobutyl)- 2-deoxy-D- \nstreptamine sulphate (1:2 or 1:1.8)(salt).\nAmikacin Sulphate having a molar ratio of Amikacin to H2SO4 \nof 1:2 contains the equivalent of not less than 674 pg and not \nmore than 786' pg of C22H43N5OI3 per mg, calculated on the \ndried basis. Amikacin Sulphate having a molar ratio of Amikacin \nto H2SO4 of 1:1.8 contains the equivalent of not less than \n691 pg and nbt more than 806 pg of 'C22H43N56l3'per mg, \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n. . ,\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nAMIKACIN SULPHATE\nDescription. A white to yellowish-white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amikadn-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amikadn Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amikadn Sulphate\n, xH2S04\n(x = 1.8; 2)\nOH\nMol. Wt.762.1\nMol. Wt. 781.8\nC22~3N5013,1.8H2S04\nC22~3N5013,2H2S04\nArnikacin Sulphate is (S)-O-3-amino-3-deoxy-a.-o-\nglucopyranosyl-(1--76)-O-[6-amino-6-deoxy-a.-o-\n793\nwww.webofpharma.com\nglucopyranosyl(1 ~4)]-N1-(4-amino-2-hydroxy-1-oxobutyl)-\n2-deoxY-D-streptamine sulphate (1:2 or 1:1.8)(salt).\n.Amikacin Sulphate having a molarratio ofAmikacin to H2S04\nof 1:2 contains the equivalent of not less than 674 Ilg and not\nmore than 786 Ilg of C22~3N5013per mg, calculated on the\ndried basis. Amikacin Sulphatehaving a molarratio ofAmikacin\nto H2S04 of 1:1.8 contains the equivalent of not less than\n6911lg and not more than 806 Ilg of C22~3N5013 per mg,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDose. By intramuscular or slow intravenous injection or by\ninfusion, upto 1.5 g daily, in two divided doses.\nDescription. A white to yellowish-white crystalline powder;\nalmost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amiloride-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amiloride Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amiloride Hydrochloride\nO NH\nCI^N\nH2N^N\nCACHOJ1CI£H2O \nMol. Wt. 302.1\nAmiloride Hydrochloride is A-amidino-3,5-diamino- \n6-chloropyrazine-2-carboxamide hydrochloride dihydrate.\nAmiloride Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C6H Ad\\7O/HCL calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDescription. A pale yellow to greenish-yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aminocaproic-acid-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aminocaproic Acid Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aminocaproic Acid Injection\nAminocaproic Acid Injection is a sterile solution of \nAminocaproic Acid in Water for Injections.\nAminocaproic Acid Injection contains not less than 92.5 per \ncent and not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \naminocaproic acid, C6H13NO2.\nUsual strength. 400 mg per ml. \n,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aminocaproic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aminocaproic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aminocaproic Acid\no\nC(JCNO2 \nMol. Wt. 131.2\nAminocaproic Acid is 6-aminohexanoic acid. ,\nAminocaproic Acid contains not less than 98.5 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C6H13NO2, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Haemostatic; antifibrinolytic.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aminophylline-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aminophylline Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aminophylline Injection\nTheophylline and Ethylenediamine Injection\nAminophylline Injection is a sterile solution of Aminophylline \nin Water for Injections or is a sterile solution of Theophylline \nin a solution of Ethylene diamine Hydrate in Water for \nInjections free from carbon dioxide. Aminophylline Injection \nmay contain an excess of ethylene di amine but no other \nsubstance may be added.\nAminophylline Injection contains theophylline, CTffhLCK \nequivalent to not less than 73.25 per cent and not more than \n88.25 per cent of the stated amount of aminophylline, and not \nmore than 0.295 g of ethylenediamine, C2H8N2 for each g of \nanhydrous theophylline, C7H8N4O2, determined in the Assay \nfor theophylline.\nIP 2022\nAMINOPHYLLINE INJECTION\nUsual strengths. 250 mg in 10 ml; 500 mg in 20 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amiodarone-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amiodarone Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amiodarone Hydrochloride\nC25Hy2NO3,HCl\nMol. Wt. 681.8\nAmiodarone Hydrochloride is 2-butylbenzofuran-3-yl-4-(2- \ndiethylaminoethoxy)-3,5-diiodophenyl ketone hydrochloride.\nAmiodarone Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C25H29I2NO3,HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiarrhythmic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, fine crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amiodarone-sterile-concentrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amiodarone Sterile Concentrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amiodarone Sterile Concentrate\nAmiodarone Sterile Concentrate is aj sterile solution of \nAmiodarone Hydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nThe concentrate complies with the requirements for \nConcentrates for Injections or Infusions stated under \nParenteral Preparations and with the following \nrequirements.\nAmiodarone Intravenous Infusion contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated \namount of amiodarone hydrochloride, C25H29I2NO3,HC1.\nUsual strengths. Concentrate, 150 mg per 3 ml ; infusion, \n450 mg per 250 ml; 360 mg per 200 ml; 150 mg per 100 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amisulpride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amisulpride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amisulpride\nC17H37N3O4S \nMol. Wt. 369.5\nAmisulpride is 4-amino-A-[(l-ethyl-2-pyrrolidinyl)methyl]- \n5-(ethylsulphonyl)-2-methoxybenzamide.\nAmisulpride contains not less than 99.0per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of Ci7H27N3O4S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amitraz-dip-concentrate-powder","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amitraz Dip Concentrate Powder"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amitraz Dip Concentrate Powder\nArnitraz Dip Concentrate Powder consists ofArnitraz mixed\nwith suitable wetting, dispersing and suspending agents. It\nmay contain a suitable stabilising agent.\nAmitraz Dip Concentrate Powder contains not less than\n92.0 per cent and not more than 108.0 per cent of the stated\namount ofamitraz, C19H23N3.\nUsual strengths. 25 per cent w/w and 50 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amitraz","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amitraz"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amitraz\nH3C \nN=C-N-C=N \nCH3\nCioHagN; \nMol, Wt 293: 4\nAmitraz is NN di-(2, ‘xylyliminomethy!}mettivlamine.\nAmitraz contains not less than 95.0. per cent and.not more\nthan 101.5 per cent of CjgH,3N;,,calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Acaricide....\nDescription. A white to buff powder.”"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amitriptyline-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amitriptyline Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amitriptyline Hydrochloride\nC2CJI,3N ,11CI \nMol. Wt. 313.9\nAmitriptyline Hydrochloride is 3-(10,l 1 -dihydro-5/7-dibenzo\n[ar/Jcyclohept-5-ylidene)propyldimethylaminc hydrochloride.\n1443\nwww.webofpharma.com\nAmitriptyline Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of (-W tN,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white or almost white \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amla-juice-powder","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amla Juice Powder"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amla Juice Powder\nAmla Juice Powder is obtained by spray drying cold pressed \njuice of fresh fruits of Phyllanthus emblica Linn. (Fam.\nAmla Juice Powder contains not less than 90.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 110 per cent w/w of the stated amount of \npolyphenols, calculated on the anhydrous basis. It may contain \nsuitable added substances.\nCategory. Antiscorbutic, antacid, carminative, hepato- \nprotective, raktapitta.\nUsual strength. 40 per cent w/w.\nDescription. Abeige to off-white powder.\nDetermine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nplate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 15 volumes of ethyl acetate,\n1.5 volumes of methanol and 1 volume water.\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.5 g,of the extract under examination \nwith 10 ml of water and filter.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of gallic \nacid IPRS in water.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution as bands 10 mm by \n2 mm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air \nand examine under ultraviolet fight at 254 nm. Spray the plate \nwith anisaldehyde-sulphuric acid reagent. Heat the plate at\n110° for 1Q minutes and examine the plate under 366 nm and in \nday light The chromatographic profile of the test solution is \nsimilar to that of the réference solution.\nWater - soluble extractive (2.6.3). Not less than 85.0 per cent \nby Method 1.\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 15.0 per cent\nWater (2.3.43)., Not more than 5.0 per cent.\n4167\nwww.webofpharma.com\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination tests.\nGallic acid. Not less than 0,1 per cent and not more than \n2.0 percent\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve about 50 mg of the extract under \nexamination or quantity equivalent to 10 mg of gallic acid in \nwater, by frequent shaking and dilute to 50.0 ml with water \nand filter. \n....\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of gallic \nacid IPRS in water.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilanè bonded porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. 0.1 per cent formic acid in water,\nB. methanol,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 0.7 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 272 run,\n■ \ninjection volume: 20 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n95\n5\n25\n95\n5\n30\n0\n100\n40\n0\n100\n42\n95\n■ 5\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of the gallic acid in extract.\nAssay. Weigh about 2 g ofthe extract, ad<i 50 ml boiling water \nand heat it on a water-bath for 30 minutes with frequent stining. \nAllow the solution to settle and carefully decant it through a \npiece of cotton wool to a 500 ml volumetric flask. Repeat the \nextraction for 5 times with 50 ml of boiling water. To confirm \nthat all tannins have been extracted, add 3-4 drops of ferric \nammonium sulphate solution to 5 ml of the extract. Blue colour \nwill not be produced, if all tannins have been extracted. If blue \ncolour develops extract again 2 times with 50 ml of boiling \nwater and check, with ferric ammonium sulphate solution. \nCool the extracts and make up to the mark with water.Take \n50 ml of this into a 250.0 ml conical flask and add 50 ml of \nindigo sulphonic acid solution, prepared by dissolving 1 g \nof indigo carmin in 50 ml of sulphuric acid, add 500 ml Of\n4168\nAMLA JUICE POWDER\nIP 2022\nwater and dilute this solution to 1000 ml; Titrate, with 0 [ ÿ \npotassium permanganate using indigo sulphonic acid\nindicator until a golden yellow colour is produced. Carry out2 \nblank titration. \n;;\n1 mlof. 0.1 M potassium permanganate is equivalent \n0.004157 g of polyphenols calculated as tannic acid;\nStorage. Sto"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amlodipine-besilate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amlodipine Besilate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amlodipine Besilate\nMol.Wt. 567.1\nC^CINAS\nAmlodipine Besylate is 3-ethyl 5-methyl (47?.$)- \n2-[(2-aminoethoxy)methyl]-4-(2-chlorophenyl)-6-methyl- \nl,4-dihydropyridine-3,5-dicarboxylate benzene sulphonate.\nAmlodipine Besylate contains not less than 97.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C26H3iClN2O8S, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive; antianginal.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amlodipine-besylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amlodipine Besylate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amlodipine Besylate\n^-Amlodipine Besilate\nC26H3lCIN2OsS, 216 H2O \n: Mol. Wt. 612.1\nIP 2022\n5-AMLODIPINE BESYLATE\nS-Amlodipine Besylate is (5)-2-[(2-aminoethoxy)methyl]- \n4-(2-chlorophenyl)-l,4-dihydro-6-methyl-3,5-pyridine- \ndicarboxylic acid 3-ethyl 5-methyl ester hemipentahydrate.\nS-Amlodipine Besylate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C20H25ClN2O5,C6H6O3S, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive, antianginal.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ammonium-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ammonium Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ammonium Chloride\nNH»C1 \nMol. Wt. 53.5\nAmmonium Chloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent ofNH4Cl, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Expectorant; diuretic; systemic acidifier.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amodiaquine-dihydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amodiaquine Dihydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amodiaquine Dihydrochloride\nC20H22CIN30,2HCJ, 2H2O \nMol. Wt. 464.8\nAmodiaquine Hydrochloride is 4-(7“Chloro-4-quinolylamino)- \n2-(diethylaminomethyl)phenol dihydrochloride dihydrate.\nAmodiaquine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.5 per cent of C20H22ClN3O,2HC1, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antimalarial.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amodiaquine-hydrochloride-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amodiaquine Hydrochloride Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amodiaquine Hydrochloride Tablets\nAmodiaquine Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \namodiaquine, C201-122CIN30.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amodiaquine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amodiaquine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amodiaquine Hydrochloride\nCZoH22CIN30, 2HCl, 2HzO\nMol. Wt. 464.8\nAmodiaquine HydrocWoride is 4-(7-cWoro-4cquinolylamino)-\n2-(diethylaminomethyl)phenol dihydrochloride dihydrate.\nAmodiaquine HydrocWoride contains not less than 98.0 per\ncent and not more than 101.5 per cent of CzOHzzCIN30, 2HCl,\ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antimalarial.\nDose. Suppressive, the equivalent of 400 mg of amodiaquine\nweekly. Therapeutic, the equivalent of 400 to 600 mg of\namodiaquine daily for three days.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder; odourless or almost\nodourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amorolfine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amorolfine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amorolfine Hydrochloride\nHCI\nC2tH35NO,HCl \nMol. Wt. 354.0\nAmorolfirie Hydrochloride is (27?*,61S'*)-2,6-Dimethyl-4-{2- \nmethyI-3 -[4-(2-methylbutan-2-yl)phenyl]propyl }morpholine.\nAmorolfine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C2iH35NO, HCI, calculated \non the dried basis.'\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder.'"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amoxapine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amoxapine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amoxapine\nc17h16cin30\nMol. Wt. 313.8\nAmoxapine is Dibenz [b,f] [1,4] oxazepine, 2-chloro-l1-(1- \npiperazinyl)-; 2-chloro-l l-(l-piperazinyl) dibenz[b,f] [1,4] \noxazepine.\n.1461\nwww.webofpharma.com\nAmoxapine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Ci7Hi6C1N3O, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. A white to yellowish crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amoxycillin-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amoxycillin Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amoxycillin Oral Suspension\nAmoxicillin Oral Suspension\nAmoxycillin Oral Suspension is a mixture consisting of \nAmoxycillin Trihydrate with buffering agents and other \nexcipients. It contains a suitable flavouring agent. It is filled in \na sealed container.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of Water just \nbefore use.\nAmoxycillin Oral Suspension contains Amoxycillin Trihydrate \nequivalent to not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\n120.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of amoxicillin Ci6Hi9N3OsS.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of amoxycillin Ci6Hi9N3O5S.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°.\nUsual strengths. Amoxycillin 125 mg per 5 ml; Amoxycillin \n250 mgper5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amoxycillin-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amoxycillin Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amoxycillin Sodium\nC]6H18N3NaO5S \nMol. Wt. 387.4\nAmoxycillin Sodium is sodium (6A)-6-((x-4-hydroxyphenyl- \nD-glycylamino)penicillanate.\nAmoxycillin Sodium contains not less than 85.0 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of C16H18N3NaO5S, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; very \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amoxycillin-trihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amoxycillin Trihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amoxycillin Trihydrate\nC16H19N3O5S,3H2O \nMol. Wt, 419.4\nAmoxycillin Trihydrate is (67?)-6-(a-4-hydroxyphenyl- \nD-glycylamino)penicillanic acid trihydrate.\nAmoxycillin Trihydrate contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C16H19N3O5S, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. Awhite or. almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amphotericin-b-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amphotericin B Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amphotericin B Injection\nAmphotericin B Injection is a sterile freeze dried mixture of \nAmphotericin B and deoxycholate sodium with one or more \nbuffering agents. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate Matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections)\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nAmphotericin B Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \namphotericin B,C471173NOI7.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Injection) and with the following-requirements.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per ml.\nIP 2022\nAMPICILLIN"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amphotericin-b","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amphotericin B"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amphotericin B\nC47H73NOi7 \nMok Wt. 924.1\nAmphotericin B is a mixture consisting mainly of amphotericin \nB which is (3/?,5/?.87?,9R, 11S,13R, 15S, 16R, 1 IS, 197?.34.8',3 5/? \n,367?,37S)-19-(3-amino-3,6-dideoxy-P-D-mannopyranosyIpxy)-,\n16-carboxy-3,5,8,9,l 1,13,15,35-octahydroxy-34,36-dimcthyl- \n13,17-epoxyoctatriaconta- 20,22,24,26,28,30,32-heptaen-\nAMOXYCILLIN AND POTASSIUM CLAVULANATE TABLETS \nIP 2022\n37-olide and other antifungal polyenes produced by the growth \nof certain strains of Streptomyces nodosus or by any other \nmeans.\nAmphotericin B has a potency of not less than 750 pg of \nC47H73NO17 per mg, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. A yellow to orange powder. Even in the absence \nof light, it is gradually decomposed in a humid environment, \ndegradation being faster at higher temperatures. In solutions, \nit is inactivated in the presence of light and at low pH values."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ampicillin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ampicillin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ampicillin Injection\nAmpicillin Sodium Injection\nAmpicillin Injection is a sterile material consisting of Ampicillin \nSodium with or without buffering agents and other excipients. \nIt is filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInj ections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the. period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nAmpicillin Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of ampicillin, \nC16Hl9N3O4S.\nCategory. Antibacterial\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 100 mg, 250 mg, 500 mg \nand 1 g of ampicillin.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; hygroscopic.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Inj ections) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ampicillin-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ampicillin Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ampicillin Oral Suspension\nAmpicillin Oral Suspension is a mixture consisting of Ampicillin \nor Ampicillin Trihydrate with buffering agents and other \nexcipients. It contains a suitable flavouring agent. It is filled in \na sealed container.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of Water just \nbefore issue.\nAmpicillin Oral Suspension contains Ampicillin Trihydrate \nequivalent to not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\n120.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of ampicillin, C56Ht9N3O4S.\nThe constituted suspension, when stored at the temperature \nand for the period stated on the label during which it may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, contains not less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of ampicillin, Ci6H19N3O4S.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°.\nUsual strengths. Ampicillin 125 mgper 5 ml; Ampicillin 250 mg \nper5ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ampicillin-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ampicillin Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ampicillin Sodium\nH COONa\nh 1 r vCH3 \nn”H'SAch3\nH H\nC16H18N3NaO4S \nMol. Wt. 371.4\nAmpicillin Sodium is sodium (6/?)76-(a-phenyl-D-glycyl- \naminojpenicillinate\nAmpicillin Sodium contains not less than 92.5 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of C16H]8N3NaO4S, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial,\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ampicillin-trihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ampicillin Trihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ampicillin Trihydrate\nC!6H19N3O4S,3H2O \nMol. Wt. 403.5\nAmpicillin Trihydrate is (67?)-6-(a-phenyl-D-glycyl- \namino)penicillanic acid trihydrate.\nAmpicillin Trihydrate contains not less than 96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of C16H19N3O4S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. Awhite, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ampicillin-veterinary-oral-powder","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ampicillin Veterinary Oral Powder"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ampicillin Veterinary Oral Powder\nAmpicillin TrihydrateVeterinary Oral Powder\nAmpicillin Veterinary Oral Powder is a mixture ofAmpicillin\nTrihydrate and Lactose or other suitable diluent.\nAmpicillin Veterinary Oral Powder contains not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated\namount ofampicillin, C16H19N304S.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 10 per cent\nw/w of\nampicillin.\nDescription. Afine granularpowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ampicillin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ampicillin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ampicillin\nC16H19N3O4S \nMol. Wt. 349.4\nAmpicillin is (6R)-6-(a-phenyl-D-glycylamino)penicillanic \nacid.\nAmpicillin contains not less than 96.0 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of C16H19N3O4S', calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\n1473\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCategory. Antibacterial,\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amplification","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amplification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amplification \n(NAT), Method BC TA)). \n—— \n~\nExtraneous agents. Monograph prescribes s¢ \nset- tof measures\nthat. taken: together. give-an, acceptabledegree. of . assurance\nthat-the. final produet does not:contain infectious extraneous \nagents. These measures includes:.\n1). . Production, within.seed lot system and cell seed system,\nfie: whereyer possible: \n, \n. \nges \nal\n2). Extensive testing: of: seed: lots: and cell seed for extraneous \nagents. \n\" \n2 \nashes\n3) \nRequirements for SPF flocks used for providing substrate\nfor vaccine production. \n- \n:\n4) \nTesting of substances of animal origin, which must \nwherever possible, undergo inactivation procedure...\n5) .. For live vaccines, testing of final product for infectious \nextraneous agents, such tests are-less extensive than \nthose carried out at earlier stages because of guarantees \ngiven by in-process testing. - \nuy\nAbnormal Toxicity, Where stated in the individual mono graph \nvaccines comply with the following | tést. Inject 0.5 ml \nsubcutaneously into each of five tice and 2 ml intraperitoneally\ninte each of two guinea pigs. If the vaccine being examined \ncontains ‘an adjuvant, inject 2 ml of the vaccine subcutaneously\ninto each guinea pig. Observe the animals for? days. None of\nthe animals shows significant local or systemic reaction. If \none animal dies or shows signs of ill health during the \nobservation, period repeat the test. None of the animals of the \nsecond group dies or. show signs of ill health.\nSterility (2.2.11): Unless otherwise stated in the individual \nmonograph, use Method A. Incubate the media for not less \nthan 14 days at 30° to 35° in the test for detecting bacteria and\nat 20° to 25° in the test for detecting fungi, However, for live\nbacterial vaccines growth of the organisms from which the \nvaccine was prepared is permitted.\nThe number of containers-to be'drawn for the test should bel\nper cent of the containers in a batch, with minimum of 3 anda \nmaximum.-of 10; assuming that the preparation has been \nmanufactured under ‘appropriately validated conditions \ndesigned to exclude contamination. \n; \n‘\nFor avian live viral vaccines, for né1i- parenteral use only, the \nrequirement for sterility'is usually replaced by requirements\nfor absence of pathogenic micro- organisms and for amaximum\nof one (1) non-pathogenic micto- organism per dose.\nSafety Test. Uniless ‘otherwise stated in the’ individual \nmonograph, vaccines other than live viral vaccines intended\nfor poultry comply with following test. \n°”\nInject at least 2 healthy, susceptible’ animals ‘of one of the \nspecies in which the vaccine ‘is interided'to be used by the \nroute recommended by the manufacturer for field use. The\nquantity to be injected in each animal is twice the apprepriaté \nvaccinating dose. Observe ‘the’ animals ‘for: not less: ‘than\n7 days. No animal exhibits an ‘abnormal reaction.” \n;\nPotency. Determine the potency of the vaccine using the \nmethod described in the individual monograph. The vaccine \ncomplies with the level of immune response specified in the \nmonograph. A combined vaccine complies with the level\nspecified in the respective monographs for each individual \ncomponent. If the immunogenicity (Potency test) has been \nperformed with, satisfactory. results on representative. batch\nof live wacciles from the same seed lot, \nif: mays ‘omitted \na asa \nee\n4815\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022 \nGENERAL MONOGRAPHS\nViruses\nThe seed virus used in the preparation of vaccine shall, before \nbeing used for-preparing a.batch, be thoroughly tested for\npurity, safety, sterility and antigenicity by generally accepted:\ntests applicable to a particular virus. It shail not be more than, \nfive passages away: ‘from the stock seed virus, unless otherwise\nprescribed for a particular virus. The stock seed virus shall be \nmaintained by seed-lot system at specified passage level and\ntested for ‘bacterial, . mycoplasmal and extraneous ‘viral\ncontamination.\nViral Vaccine\nViral vaccines, live or inactivated are made f"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/amprolium-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Amprolium Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Amprolium Hydrochloride\nSodium Iodide ( 123 1) Injection\nBlood Grouping Reagent Anti-K,\nAnthrax Spore Vaccine, Live\n(From 124 X