ries\nClonidine Tablets\nBlackquarter Vaccine\nClosantel Sodium Dihydrate\nBrimonidine Tartrate Eye Drops\nCrospovidone\nBnicella Abortus (Strain 19 Vaccine)\nVaccine, Live\nCrotaminton\nBumetamide Injection\nCyclosporine\nButylparaben\nDaruharidra Roots\nCalcitonin (Salmon) Injection\nDecoquinate\nCalcium Folinate (Leucovorin Ca)\nDesferrioxamine Injection\nCalcium Magnesium Borogluconate\nDexamethasone Sodium Phosphate\nInjection\nDexamethasone Tablets\nCalcium Stearate\nDexlansoprazole\nCanine Adenovirus Vaccine, Live\nDiazoxide\nCanine Coronavirus Vaccine,\nDiclazuril\nInactivated\nDichlorophen Tablets\nCanine Distemper Vaccine, Live\nDiclofenac Prolonged-release Tablets\nCanine Infectious Contagious\nHepatitis Vaccine, Inactivated\nDicloxacillin Sodium\nCanine Parainfluenza Virus Vaccine,\nDiloxanide Furoate\nLive\nDiluted Pentaerythritol Tetranitrate\nCanine Parvovirus Vaccine, Inactivated\nDimethicone\nCanine Parvovirus Vaccine, Live\nDinitolamide\nCaprofen\nDiphtheria Vaccine (Absorbed)\nCarboxymethylcellulose Sodium\nDiphtheria, Tetanus and Pertussis\nCarnauba Wax\nVaccine (Adsorbed)"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anaesthetic-ether","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anaesthetic Ether"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anaesthetic Ether\nh3c^o^ch3\nC4Hi0O .. \nMol. Wt. 74.1\nAnaesthetic Ether is diethyl ether to which a suitable non­\nvolatile stabiliser in a proportion not greater than 0.002 per \ncent w/v may have been added.\n2290\nETHANOLAMINE\nIP 20^\nCategory. General anaestheic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless, very mobile liquid- hiphi \nflammable. \n’\nNOTE — It is absolutely essential that a preservative\ntype of sodium pyrogallate, hydroquinone or propyl gallon \nin suitable concentrations shall be added in Anaesthetic \nEther intended for use in tropical climates unless the \nAnaesthetic Ether is stored in a copper container or in a\ncontainer copper-plated internally. The preservative used \nand its concentration shall be declared on the label."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/analgin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Analgin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Analgin\nMetamizole Sodium Monphy drate; Dipyrone\nC|3H16N3NaO4S,H2O \n' Mol. Wt.351.4\nAnalgin is Sodium [W-(2,3-dihydro-l,5-dimethyl-3-oxo-2- \nphenyl-17Z-pyrazdl-4yl)-jV-methylamino]methanesulphonate \nmonohydrate.\nAnalgin contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.Oper cent of Ci ;ni6N3NaO.iS, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Analgesic.\nIP 2022\nANALGIN\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anastrozole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anastrozole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anastrozole\nC17H19N5 \nMol. Wt. 293.4\nAnastrozole is a,a,a',a'-tetramethyl-5-(17/-l,2,4-triazoT\n1-ylmethyl)-1,3-benzene diacetonitrile\nAnastrozole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CI7H19N5, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white to off white, crystalline powder;\nCA UTION — Anastrozole is cytotoxic; extra care required \nto prevent inhaling particles and exposing the skin to it.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nANASTROZOLE TABLETS\nri| IP 20.22\n1"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anhydrous-dextrose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anhydrous Dextrose"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anhydrous Dextrose \n906 \n(Indirect Method) \n387\nAnhydrous Dibasic Sodium Phosphate \n946 \nAnti-A Blood Grouping Reagent \n383\nAnhydrous Disodium Hydrogen Phosphate \n911,946 \nAnti-A Blood Grouping Serum \n3887\nAnhydrous Disodium Hydrogen Orthophosphate \n911 \nAnti-AB Blood Grouping Reagent \n3890\nAnhydrous Docetaxel \n1871 \nAnti A, B (Group 0) Blood Grouping Reagent \n383\nAnhydrous Ferric Chloride \n914 \nAnti-B Blood Grouping Reagent \n383\nAnhydrous Formic Acid \n915 \nAnti-B Blood Grouping Serum \n3888\nAnhydrous Glacial Acetic Acid \n890 \nAntibody specifications for sera\nAnhydrous Iron(III) Chloride \n914 \nused in extraneous agents testing \n358\nAnhydrous Lactose \n2371 \nAnti-D (Rho) Immunoglobulin \n3892\nAnhydrous Lanolin \n3519 \nAnti-D Immunoglobulin for Intravenous Use \n3893\nAnhydrous Methanol \n925 \nAnti-D Immunoglobulin Human for Intravenous Use \n3893\nAnhydrous Niclosamide \n2719 \nAnti-D (IgM) Monoclonal Blood Grouping Reagent \n3894\nAnhydrous Potassium Carbonate \n935 \nAnti-Human Globulin Serum \n3895\nAnhydrous 2-Propanol \n938 \nAnti-gas-gangrene (Oedematiens) Serum \n3617\nAnhydrous Propan-2-ol \n, 938 \nAnt i-gas-gangrene (Perfringens) Serum \n3618\nAnhydrous Pyridine \nAnti-gas-gangrene (Septicum) Serum \n3619\nAnhydrous Silica Gel \nAnti-Rh B1(xod Group Serums \n3910\nINDIAN PHARMACOPOEIA 2018"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anhydrous-lactose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anhydrous Lactose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anhydrous Lactose\nC12H22On. \nMol. Wt. 342.3\nAnhydrous Lactose is 4-(?-p-D-galactopyranosyl-D- \nglucose.\nCategory^ Pharmaceutical aid (excipient).\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anhydrous-lanolin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anhydrous Lanolin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anhydrous Lanolin\nWool Fat is purified, anhydrous, waxy material obtained from\nthe wool ofsheep. It may contain Butylated Hydroxytoluene\nas an antioxidant.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (absorbent ointment base).\nDescription. A pale yellow, unctuous substance; odour,\ncharacteristic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anhydrous-niclosamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anhydrous Niclosamide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anhydrous Niclosamide\n~~xrN02\nyH\nCI\nMol. Wt. 327.1\nNiclosamide is 2' ,5-dichloro-4' -nitrosalicylanilide.\nNiclosamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent ofC 13HgChNz0 4, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Anthelmintic.\nDose. 2 g as a single dose after a light breakfast followed by a\npurgative 2 hours later.\nDescription. A yellowish white to yellowish, fine crystals or\npowder; odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anionic-emulsifying-wax","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anionic Emulsifying Wax"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anionic Emulsifying Wax\nEmulsifying Wax is a waxy solid containing 90 parts of \nCetostearyl Alcohol, 10 parts of Sodium Lauryl Sulphate or \nsodium salts of similar sulphated higher, primary, aliphatic \nalcohols, and 4 parts of Purified Water.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (emulsifying agent).\nDescription. An almost white or pale yellow, waxy solid or \nflakes. It becomes soft on wanning."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anthrone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anthrone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anthrone \n1073\nAnti-A and Anti-B Haemagglutinins\n(Indirect Method) \n474\nAnti-A Blood Grouping Reagent \n470\nAnti-A Blood Grouping Serum \n4499\nAhti-AB Blood Grouping Reagent \n4502\nAnti A, B (Group O) Blood Grouping Reagent \n470\nVolume 1: i to xxxiv and 1 to 1276; 1-1 to I-\nVolume 4: xliii to xlvi and 4785 to 5024; 1-1\nAnti-B Blood Grouping Reagent \n470\nAnti-B Blood Grouping Serum \n4500\nAntibody specifications for sera\nAnti-D Blend (IgM + IgG) Monoclonal Reagent 4507\nused in extraneous agents testing \n440\nAnti-D (Rh J Immunoglobulin \n4504\nAnti-D Immunoglobulin for Intravenous Use . \n4505\nAnti-D Immunoglobulin Human for Intravenous Use 4505\nAnti-D (IgG) Monoclonal Reagent \n4509\nAnti-D (IgM) Monoclonal Blood Grouping\nReagent \n4506\nAnti-Human Globulin (AHG) Reagent \n4509\nAnti-Human Globulin (AHG) Serum \n4509\nAnti-gas-gangrene (Oedematiens) Serum \n4391\nAnti-gas-gangrene (Perfringens) Serum \n4392\nAnti-gas-gangrene (Septicum) Serum \n4394\nAnti-Rh Blood Group Serums \n4525\nAnticoagulant Citrate Dextrose Solution \n1486\nAnticoagulant Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Solution 1487\nAnticoagulant Citrate Phosphate Dextrose\nAdenine Solution \n1488\nAnticoagulant Heparin Solution \n4504\nAntihaemophilic Fraction, Dried Human \n4528\nAntimicrobial Preservatives, Effectiveness of \n29\nAntimony Compounds, Tests for \n163\nAntimony Standard Solution (100 ppm Sb) \n1141\nAntimony Trichloride \n1073\nAntimony Trichloride Reagent \n1073\nAntimony Trichloride Solution \n1073\nAntirabic Serum \n4460\nAntirabies Serum \n4460\nAnti Scorpion Venom Serum \n4469\nAntisera \n4329\nAntithrombin III Concentrate \n4511\nApparatus \n21\nApparatus, General Notices \n15,1289,3003,4797\nAppearance of Solution \n211\nApproach to Alternative Microbiological Methods 130\nApproximate pH of Solutions \n345\nApremilast \n266,505,1489\nApremilast Tablets \n1491\nAprepitant \n266,505,1493\nAprepitant Capsules \n1494\n108; Volume 2: \\xxv toxxxviii and 1277 to 2990; Volume 3: xxxix to xlii and 2991 to 4784; \n(W tul-216.\n1-11\nwww.webofpharma.com\nArginine- \n\"\n266,506,1073,4039\nAspirin Gastro-resistant and Atorvastatin\nArjuna Dry Extract\n877,946,4173\nAspirin Gastro-resistant and Rosuvastatin ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/antibody-production-tests","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Antibody Production Tests"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Antibody Production Tests\nSpeciesspecific viruses present in rodent cell lines may be \ndetected by inoculating test article into virus-free animals and \nexamining the serum antibody level or enzyme activity after a \nspecified period. Examples of such tests are the mouse \nantibody production (MAP) test, rat antibody production \n(RAP) test, and hamster antibody production (HAP) test. \nAbsence of antibody is generally expected from these tests.\nVirus Testing\nIt is important that all the raw materials used for cell line, MCB \nand WCB generation as well as in the production process \nshall be of animal free components and does not pose any risk \nof viral contamination. If any raw materials used are of animal \norigin the appropriate testing shall be ensured for such raw \nmaterials from manufacturers and shall come with appropriate \ncertifications.\nA. Cell Line Qualification\nAn important part of qualifying a cell line-for use in the \nproduction of a biotechnology product is the appropriate \ntesting for the presence of virus. It is recognized that some \ncell lines used for the manufacture of product will Contain \nendogenous retroviruses, other viruses, or viral sequences. \nThe acceptability of cell lines containing viruses other than \nendogenous retroviruses , can be considered based-upon a \nrisk/benefit analysis. This may include nature of the product \nand its intended clinical use, the nature ofthe contaminating \nviruses, their potential for infecting humans or for causing \ndisease in humans, the purification process for the product \n(e.g., viral clearance evaluation data), and the extent ofthe \nvirus tests conducted on the drug substance. \n: ’\n(a) Master Cell Bank \n.............. ■\nViruses can be introduced into the MCB by several routes \nsuch as: 1) derivation of cell lines from infected animals; 2)s.\n■■■(20 \nfittiiiHHiM\n2.2.28. VIRAL SAFETY EVALUATION OF BIOTECHNOLOGY DERIVED PRODUCTS FROM CELL LINES OF HUMAN OR ANIMAL ORIGIN \n]p 2022\n‘For. the first WCB, this fest should be performed on ceils at the limit of lit vitro cell age, generated from that WCB; for WCBs subsequent to the first \nWCB, a single in vitro and in wto test can be done either directly on the WCB or on the cells at the limit of in vitro cell age.\nuse of virus to establish the cell line; 3) use of contaminated \nbiological reagents such as animal serum components; 4) \ncontamination during cell handling.\nExtensive screening for both endogenous and non- \nendogenous viral contamination should be performed on the \nMCB. Testing for non-endogenous viruses should include in\nvitro and in vivo inoculation tests and any other specific \ntests, including species specific tests such as the MAP test, \nthat are appropriate, based on the passage history of the cell \nline, to detect possible contaminating viruses.\n(b) Working Cell Bank\nEach WCB should be tested for adventitious viruses either \nby direct testing or by analysis of cells at the limit of in vilro \ncell age, initiated from the WCB. Antibody production tests \nare usually not necessary for the WCB. If non-endogenous \nvirus tests have been performed on the MCB and cells cultured \nup to or beyond the limit of in vilro cell age have been derived \nfrom the WCB and used for testing for the presence of \nadventitious viruses, similar tests need not be performed on \nthe initial WCB. An alternative approach in which full tests \nare carried out on the WCB rather than on the MCB would \nalso be considered acceptable,\n(c) Cells at the Limit of In Vitro Cell Age/End of Production\nThe limit of in vilro cell age used for production should be \nbased on data derived from production cells expanded under \npilot-plant scale or commercial scale conditions to the proposed \nin vitro cell age or beyond using the MCB or the WCB. Cells \nat the limit of in vilro cell age/EPC should be evaluated once \nfor those endogenous viruses that.may have been undetected \nin the MCB and WCB to provide assurance that the production \nprocess is not prone to contami"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anticoagulant-citrate-phosphate-dextrose-adenine-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anticoagulant Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Adenine Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anticoagulant Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Adenine Solution \nis a sterile solution of Citric Acid, Sodium Citrate, Sodium \nDihydrogen Phosphate Dihydrate, Dextrose and Adenine in \nWater for Injections.\nAnticoagulant Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Adenine Solution \ncontains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 \nper cent of the stated amounts of total Sodium, Na, Sodium \nDihydrogen Phosphate Dihydrate, NaH2PO4,2H2O and \nDextrose Monohydrate, C6H12O6,H2O, not less than 90.0 per. \ncent and not more than 11,0.0 per cent ofthe stated amounts of \ntotal Citrate, C6H5O7 and Adenine,C5H5N5. It contains no \nantimicrobial agent.\nCategory .Anticoagulant for storage of whole blood."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/anticoagulant-heparin-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Anticoagulant Heparin Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Anticoagulant Heparin Solution\nCategory. Anticoagulant, c \nA\nAnticoagulant Heparin Solution is a sterile solution of Heparin \nSodium in Sodium Chloride injection.\nAnticoagulant Heparin Solution contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \npotency in terms of Units per ml. It contains not less than \n0.85 per cent and not more than 0; 95 per cent of Sodium \nChloride. It may be buffered. It contains no antimicrobial \nagents. \nnj -rnhnon-Anrtn ¡nnjrnf:\nHeparin Sodium ’ \\ \n. y^OOO units\nSodium Chloride Injection, a \n...\nsufic lent quantity to make ; \n1000 ml <. ■ t\nAdd the Heparin Sodium, in solid form or in solution, to the \nSodium Chloride Injection, mix, filter ifnecessary, and sterilize.\nTests ■ -' ■ ■\npH (2.4.24). 5.0 to7.5.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 2.5 Endotoxin Units \nper ml.\nOther tests. Complies with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections). ■: \n,\nAssay for heparin sodium. Proceed äs directed in the Assay \nunder Heparin Sodium lnjection, substituting Anticoagulant \nHeparin Solution for the injection.\nAssay for sodium chloride. Pipet 10 ml of solution in to a \nsuitable container, add 2, nüpfpotassium chromate, and titrate \nwith 0.1 Msilver nitrate. .\n1 ml of 0.1Msilver nitrate is equivalent to 0.005844 g ofNaCK\nStorage. Store in colourless, transparent single dose containers \nof type I or type II glass, or of a suitable plastic material.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the number of heparin units; \n(2) the number of ml of solution required per TOO ml of whole \nblood.\nAnti-D (Rh0) Immunoglobulin\nHuman Anti-D Immunoglobulin\nHuman anti-D! immunoglobulin is a liquid dr freeze-dried \npreparation containing immunoglobulins, mainly\nimmunoglobulin G. The preparation is intended for \nintramuscular administration. It contains specific antibodies \nagainst, erythrocyte D-antigen and may also; contain small \nquantities of other blood-group antibodies. Human normal \nimmunoglobulin may be added. /, f .......\n4504\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIt complies with the monograph on Human Normal \nImmunoglobulin, except for the minimum number of donors \nand the minimum total protein content. For products, prepared \nby a metliod that eliminates immunoglobulins with specificities \nother than anti-D, where authorised, the test for antibodies to. \nhepatitis B surface antigen is not required."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/apparatus-b","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Apparatus B"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Apparatus B\nFor tablets, boluses and capsules of large size\n352\n2;5:1. -DISINTEGRATION TEST\nIP 2022\n(Dimensions in mm) . \n. \n.\nFig. 2.5.1-2: Apparatus for Disintegration of Tablets and capsules\nof large size\npiece of woven gauze made from stainless steel wire 0.63 ± \n0.03 mm in diameter and having mesh apertures of 2.0 ± 0.2 \nmm. The plates are held rigidly in position and77.5 mm apart \nby vertical metal rods at the periphery. A metal rod is also fixed \nto the centre of the upper plate to enable the assembly to be \n3 attached to a mechanical device capable ofraising and lowering\n< it smoothly at a constant frequency of between 29 and 32 \n;: cycles per minute, through a distance of 5 5 ± 2 mm.\nDisc. Each tube is provided with a cylindrical disc 31.4 ± 0.13\nmm in diameter and 15.3 ±0,15 mm thick, made of transparent \nplastic with a relative density of 1,18-1.20'. Each disc is pierced \nby 7 holes, each 3.15 ± 0.1 mm in diameter, One hole is in the \ncentre and the other 6 spaced equally'on a circle of radius 4.2 \nmni from the centre of the disc.\nMedium. The assembly is-suspended in the specified liquid \nmedium in a suitable vessel, preferably a 1-liter beaker. The \nvolume of the liquid is such that when the assembly is in the \nhighest position the wire mesh is at least 15 mm below the \nsurface ofthe liquid, and when the assembly is in the lowest \nposition the wire mesh is at least 25 mm above the bottom of \nthe beaker and the upper open ends of the tubes remain\nBasket rack assembly. TheBasketrack assembly (Fig. 2.5.1.- ■' \n2.j is a rigid basket- rack assembly supporting 3 cylindrical \ntransparent tubes 77.5 ± 2,5 mm long, 33.0 - 0.5 mm in internal \ndiameter, and with a wall thickness of 2.5 ± 0.5 mm, The tubes\nare held vertically by 2 separate and superimposed rigid plastic \nplates 97 mm in diameter and 9 mm thick, with 3 holes. The \nholes are equidistant from the centre ofthe plate and equally\nspaced. Attached to the under side of the lower plate is a \nthe temperature oftheliquid at 35-39°.\nabove the surface ofthe liquid. A suitable device maintains\nwww.webofpharma.com\nThe design of the basket-rack assembly may be varied \nprovided the specifications for the tubes and wire mesh are\nniaintaipe;:.\nMethod. Unless otherwise stated inthe individual monograph, \nintroduce one tablet or capsule into each of the three tubes \nand, if directed >n the appropriate general monograph, add a \ndisc to each tube. Suspend the assembly in the beaker \ncontaining the specified liquid and operate the apparatus for \nthe specified time, Remove the assembly from the liquid. The \ntablets or capsules pass the test if all six have disintegrated.\nNOTE— Test six tablets or capsules either by using 2 basket- \nrack assemblies in parallel or by repeating the procedure.\nApparatus\nFor pessaries and suppositories\na) \nA transparent sleeve of glass or plastic, 60 mm high with \nan internal diameter of 52 null and an appropriate wall \nthickness (Fig. 2.5.l-3a).\nb) A metal device consisting of two stainless steel discs\neach of which contains 39 holes, each 4 mm in diameter, \nbeing distributed as indicated inFig.2.5.l-3a. The diameter \nof the disc is closely similar to the internal diameter ofthe \nsleeve, The discs are separated by a distance of about 30 \nmm. The metal device is attached to the outer sleeve by \nmeans of three equally spaced hooks.\n(Dimensions tn mmj\nrig. 2.5.1 -3a: Apparatus for Disintegration ofPessaries and\nSuppositories\n2.5.1. DISINTEGRATION TEST\nIP 2022\nFor Compressed Pessaries use with the hook-end downwards \nas inFig. 2.5;l-3b. ■\nA-Compressed pessary; B-Glass Plate;. C-Water surfre\nFig. 2.5.1-3b: Apparatus for Disintegration of compressed\npessaries\nFor Moulded Pessaries, Moulded Suppositories, Shell \nPessaries and Shell Suppositories\nPlace a pessary or suppository on the lower perforated disc of \nthe metal device and then insert the device into the cylinder \nand attach this to the sleeves. Repeat the operation with a \nfurther two pessaries or supposi"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/apparatus","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Apparatus"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Apparatus\n4\n(Dimensions in mm)\nFig. 2.5.1-2a: Apparatus for Disintegration of Pessaries and\nSuppositories\na)\nA transparent sleeve of glass or plastic, 60 mm high with\nan internal diameter of 52 mm and an appropriate wall\nthickness (Fig.2.5.! ~2a).\nb)\nA metal device consisting of two stainless steel discs\neach of which contains 39 holes, each 4 mm in diameter,\nbeing distributed as indicated in Fig 2. The diameter of\nthe disc is closely similar to the internal diameter of the\nsleeve. The discs are separated by a distance of about\n30min. The metal device is attached to the outer sleeve by\nmeans of three equally spaced hooks.\nFor Compressed Pessaries use with the hook-end downwards\nas in Fig. 2.5.1-2b.\nB\nc\n!11r===\n!jJj\nA-Compressed pessary; B-Glass Plate; C-Water surfare\nFig. 2.5.1-2b: Apparatus forDisintegration of\ncompressed pessaries\n188\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nFor Moulded Pessaries, Moulded Suppositories, Shell\nPessaries and Shell Suppositories\nPlace a pessary or suppository on the lower perforated disc of\nthe metal device and then insert the device into the cylinder\nand attach this to the sleeves. Repeat the operation with a\nfurther two pessaries or suppositories and metal devices and\nsleeves. Unless otherwise specified, place each piece of\napparatus in a vessel containing at least 4litres ofwater at 36°\nto 37° and fitted with a slow stirrer and a means ofholding the\ntop of the apparatus 90 mrn below the surface of the water. A\nsuitable thermostatic arrangement may be provided for\nmaintaining the temperature ofthe bath. Alternatively, all three\npieces of apparatus may be placed together in a vessel\ncontaining at least 12 litres of water. After each 10 minutes\ninvert each apparatus without removing it from the liquid.\nDisintegration is considered to be complete when the moulded\npessary or suppository\na)\nis completely dissolved or\nb)\nhas dispersed into its componentparts, which may remain\non the surface (in the case of melted fatty substances),\nsink to the bottom (in case of insoluble powders) or\ndissolve (in case of soluble components) or may be\ndistributed in one or more of these ways or\nc)\nhas become soft with appreciable change in shape,\nwithout necessarily separating into its components, and\nthe mass has no solid core which cannot be pressed with\na glass rod.\nFor Compressed Pessaries\nPlace the apparatus in a vessel ofsuitable diameter containing\nwater at 36° to 37° .Adjust the level ofthe liquid by the gradual\naddition of water at 36° to 37° until the perforations in the\nmetal disc are just covered by a uniform layer of water. Place\none compressed pessary on the upper perforated disc and\ncover the apparatus with a glass plate to ensure a humid\natmosphere. Repeat the operation with a further two\ncompressed pessaries.\nDisintegration is considered to be complete when\na)\nthere is no residue on the perforated plate or\nb)\nifa residue remains, it consists only of a soft mass having\nno solid core which cannot be pressed with a glass rod.\n2.5.2. Dissolution Test\nThis test is designed to determine compliance with the\ndissolution requirements for solid dosage forms administered\norally. The test is intended for a capsule or tablet.\nUse Apparatus 1 unless otherwise directed. All parts of the\napparatus that may come into contact with the preparation\nunder examination or with the dissolution medium are\n2.5.2. DISSOLUTION TEST\nchemically inert and do not adsorb, react or interfere with the\npreparation underexamination. All metal parts ofthe apparatus\nthat may come into contact with the preparation or the\ndissolution medium must be made from stainless steel, type\n316 or equivalent or coated with a suitable material to ensure\nthat such parts do not react or interfere with the preparation\nunder examination or the dissolution medium.\nNo part of the assembly, including the environment in which\nthe assembly is placed, contributes significant motion,\nagitation or vibration beyond that due to the smoothly rotating\nelement.\nAn apparatus that p"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/apparatusl","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Apparatusl"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Apparatusl\nI\n168±8\nB\n1 1\n1-·I(\"\"')2.-c+\"7~---··:125t2­\n(Internal)\nFig. 2.5.2-1\n-r---~\n35_'81___\n<;\n4.0±1\n~:il­\nj /_._. -·745±O.5-·---·'\n--::~\n1\\G.__\\.%'@'~]fu'@!.''M\n1\nFig. 2.5.2-2\nAn assembly consisting of the following:\na.\nA cylindrical vessel, A, made ofborosilicate glass or any\nother suitable transparent material, with a hemispherical\nbottom and with a nominal capacity of 1000rnl and an\ninside diameter of98-106 mm (Fig.2.5.2-1). The vessel has\na flanged upper rim and is fitted with a lid that has a\nnumber of openings, one of which is central.\nb.\nA motor with a speed regulator capable of maintaining\nthe speed of rotation of the paddle within 4 per cent of\nthat specified in the individual monograph. The motor is\nfitted with a stirring element which consists of a drive\nshaft and blade forming a paddle, B (Fig.2.5.2-2).\nThe blade passes through the diameter of the shaft so that the\nbottom of the blade is flush with the bottom of the shaft. The\nshaft is positioned so that its axis is within 2 mm ofthe axis of\nthe vessel and the lower edge ofthe blade is 23 to 27 mm from\n189\nwww.webofpharma.com\nthe inside bottom of the vessel. The apparatus operates in\nsuch a way that the paddle rotates smoothly and without\nsignificant wobble.\nc. A water-bath set to maintain the dissolution medium at 36.so\nto 37.so. The bath liquid is kept in constant and smooth motion\nduring the test. The vessel is securely clamped in the water-\nbath in such a way that the displacement vibration from other\nequipment, including the watercirculation device, is minimised.\nApparatus2\nI\n168\n±8\n~\nt\n5.I±O.5\n102±4\n(IntemaI)\nFig. 2.5.2-3\nFig. 2.5.2-4\nThe assembly is the same as in Apparatus I except that in the\nstirring element the paddle is replaced by a basket, D (see\nFigs. 2.5.2-3 and 2.5.2-4). The metallic shaft rotates smoothly\nand without significant wobble. The basket consists of two\ncomponents. The top part, with a vent, is attached to the shaft\nC, it is fitted with three spring clips, or other suitable means,\nthat allow removal of the lower part for introduction of the\npreparation under examination and that firmly hold the lower\npart ofthe basket concentric with the axis ofthe vessel during\nrotation. The lower detachable part of the basket is made of\nwelded-steam cloth, with a wire thickness of0.254mmdiameter\nand with 0.381mm square openings, formed into a cylinder\nwith narrow rim ofsheet metal around the top and the bottom.\nThe basket may be plated with a 2.5 mm layer of gold for use\nwith acidic media. The distance between the inside bottom of\nthe vessel and the basket is maintained at 23 to 27 mm during\nthe test.\nDissolution medium. Use the dissolution medium specified in\nthe individual monograph. Ifthe mediumis a buffered solution,\nadjust the solution so that its pH is within 0.05 units ofthe pH\nspecified in the monograph. The dissolution medium should\nbe deaerated prior to testing.\n2.5.2. DISSOLUTION TEST\nIP 2010\nTime. Where a single time specification is given in the\nmonograph, the test may be concluded in a shorter period if\nthe requirement for the minimum amount dissolved is met. If\ntwo or more times are specified, specimen are to be withdrawn\nonly at the stated times, within a tolerance of ± 2 per cent.\nMethod\nConventional and prolonged-release solid dosage forms\nPlace the stated volume of the dissolution medium, free from\ndissolved air, into the vessel of the apparatus. Assemble the\napparatus and warm the dissolution medium to 36.5° to 37.so.\nUnless otherwise stated, place one dosage unit in the\napparatus; taking care to exclude air bubbles from the surface\nofthe dosage unit. When Apparatus I is used, allow the tablet\nor capsule to sink to the bottom of the vessel prior to the\nrotation of the paddle. A suitable device such as a wire of\nglass helix may be used to keep horizontal at the bottomofthe\nvessel tablets or capsules that would otherwise float. When\nApparatus 2 is used, place the tablet or capsule in a dry basket\nat the beginning of each test. Lo"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/apremilast","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Apremilast"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Apremilast\nC22H24N2O7S \nMol. Wt. 460.5\nApremilast is N-(2-(l-(3-ethoxy~4-methoxyphenyl)-2-\n(methylsulfonyl) ethyl)-l,3-dioxoisoindolin-4-yl) acetamide.\n1489\nwww.webofpharma.com\nApremilast contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102. 0 per cent of C22H24N2O7S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anti-inflammatory.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aprepitant","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aprepitant"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aprepitant\nC23H2IF7N4O3\nMol. Wt: 534.4\nAprepitant is 377-l,2,4-triazol-3-one, 5-[[(27?,3S)-2-[(ll?)-l-[3,5- \nbis(trifluoromethy l)pheny 1] ethoxy] -3 -(4-fluorophenyl)-4- \nmorpholinyl]methyl]-l,2-dihydro.\nAprepitant contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of \ncalculated on the\nanhydrous and solvent-free basis.\nCategory. Antiemetic.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aprotinin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aprotinin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aprotinin Injection\nAprotinin Injection is a sterile solution of Aprotinin in Water \nfor Injection that also contains Sodium Chloride. One \nAprotinin Unit is equivalent to 1800 Kallikrein Inhibition Units \n(K.I.U.)\nAprotinin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of aprotinin,\nC284H432N84O79S7, expressed in K.I.U. per ml.\n1498\nAPROTININ\nIP 2022\nUsual strength. 20000 K.I.U. per ml. <"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/arachis-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arachis Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Arachis Oil\nMoongphali ka tail; Groundnut Oil; Peanut Oil\nArachis Oil is the refined fixed oil obtained from the seed \nkernels of one or more of the cultivated varieties of Arachis \nfypogaea Linn. (Fam. Fabaceae) and may contain suitable\nantjoxidants as stabilisers.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/arbidol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arbidol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Arbidol Hydrochloride\nArbidol Hydrochloride Monohydrate\nC22H25BrN2O3S, HC1, H2O \nMol Wt. 531.9\nArbidol Hydrochloride is Ethyl 6-bromo-5-hydroxy-4-\n[(dimethylamino)methyl]-l-methyl-2’[(phenylthio)methyl]-l£/- \nindole-3 -carboxylate hydrochloride.\nArbidol Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C22H25BrN2O3S, HC1, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription.. An off white to yellow colour powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/arginine-vasopressin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arginine Vasopressin"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Arginine Vasopressin\nVasopressin, 8-L-arginine\nC461165N 150 1252 \t\nMol. Wt. 1084.2\nof causing the contraction of vascular and other smooth \nVasopressin is a polypeptide hormone having the properties\nmuscles and of antidiuresis. It is prepared by chemical\nsynthesis or by animal origin.\nVasopressin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C46H65N15012S2, calculated on anhydrous \nand acetic acid- free basis.\nto 410 \n1 mg of anhydrous Vasopressin is equivalent\nVasopressin Units.\nCategory. Antidiuretic.\nDose. For diabetes insipidus, 5 units to 10 units intramuscularly \nor subcutaneously 2 to 4 times daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white powderi\nVASOPRESSIN \nIP 2018 \nip 2018 \nVASOPRESSIN INJECTION\nInject the test solution. In the chromatogram obtained with"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/arginine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arginine"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Arginine\nL-Arginine\nNH\n0\nH2N)l~~OH\nNH2\nMol. Wt. 174.2\n834\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nArginine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more\nthan 101.5 per cent ofC6H14N40Z, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. a- amino acid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aripiprazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aripiprazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aripiprazole\nCaH27Cl2N3O2 \nMol. Wt. 448.4\nAripiprazole is 7-{4-[4-(2,3-Dichlorophenyl)piperazin-\nl-yl]butoxy}-3,4“dihydroquinolin-2(li7)-one.\nAripiprazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C23H27C12N3O2, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nARIPIPRAZOLE\nCategory. Antipsychotic; Neuroleptic.\nDescription. A white to light yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/arjuna-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arjuna Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Arjuna Dry Extract\nArjuna Dry Extract is obtained by extracting Arjuna \n(Terminal ia arjuna (Roxb ex DC.) Wight and Arn., \n^aiheCombretaceae) bark with methanol or any other suitable \nsolvent and evaporation of solvent.\nArjuna Dry Extract contains not less than 90.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 110.0 per cent w/w of stated amount of the \naijunolie acid on the dried basis. It may contain suitable added \nsubstances. \n:\nARJUNA DRY EXTRACT\nCategory. Antihyperlipidaemic, antihypertensive, astringent, \ncardioprotective, hridroga.\nUsual strength. 60 per cent w/w.\nDescription. A light brown to beige powder with or without \nred tinge."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/arjuna","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arjuna"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Arjuna\nArjuna consists of dried stem bark of Termiñalia arjuna (Roxb \nex DC;) Wight and Am. (Fam. Combretaceae)\n4172\nIP20221\nArjuna contains not less than 0.02 per cent of arjungenin \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihyperlipidaemic, antihypertensive, astringent, \ncardioprotective, hridroga. \n. ;. ; •-•••■ c •.. ? ?\nDescription. A flat or minutély curved thick pieces of bark \nwith reddish gray colour and astringent taste. ■ ' gf."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/armodafinil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Armodafinil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Armodafinil\nArmodafinil is 2-[(R)-(diphenylmethyl)sulfmyl]acetamide.\nArmodafinil contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Ci5HI5NO2S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Wakefulness-promoting agent.\nDescription. Awhite to off white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/arteether","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arteether"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Arteether\na-p Arteether\nC17H28O5 \nMol. Wt. 312.4\na-arteether is (3R,5aS,6R, 107?, 1 IS, 12a7?)-1O-Ethoxydecahydro- \n3,6,9-trimethyl-3,12-epoxy-127/-pyrano[4,3-j]-l,2- \nbenzodioxepin.\np-arteether is (37?.5aiS\\67?, 1 OS’. 12SJ 2 a/?)-1O-Ethoxydecahydro- \n3,6,9-trimethyl-3,12-epoxy-12£Apyrano[4,3-j]-l ,2- \nbenzodioxepin.\nArteether contains a-isomer not less than 25.0 per cent and \nnot more than 35.0 per cent and P-isomer not less than 65.0 per \ncent and not more than 75;0 per cent and total arteether is not \nless than 95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of \nC17H28O5, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antimalarial.\nDescription. A light yellow coloured lipophilic semi-solid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/artemether","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemether"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Artemether\nMol. Wt. 298.4\nArtemetheris (3R,5aS,6R,8aS,9R,lOS,12R,12aR)-Decahydro-\n1O-methoxy-3,6,9-trimethyl-3,12-epoxy-12H-pyrano[4,3\":J1-1,2-\nbenzodioxepin.\nArtemether contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent of C16H260S, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory.Antimalarial.\nDescription. Awhite crystals or a white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/artemisia","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisia"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Artemisia\nflowering tops of Artemisia annua Linn. (Fam. Asteraçeae), \nknown as Qinghao.\nArtemisia contains not less than 0.8 per cent of artemisinin,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antimalarial, antibacterial, cytotoxic, antihelmintic.\nDescription. Slightly camphoraceops odour and bitter in taste."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/artemisinin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artemisinin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Artemisinin\no\nIF 2010\nARTEMISININ\nArtemisinin is (3R,5aS,6R,8aS,12S,12aR)-octahydro-3,6,9-\ntrimethyl-3,12-epoxy-12H-pyrano[4,3-j]-1,2-benzodioxepin-.\n1O(3H)-one.\nArtemisinin contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent ofartemisinin, ClsH220S. calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory.Antimalarial.\nDescription. Colourless needles orawhitecrystallinepowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/arterolane-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Arterolane Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Arterolane Maleate\nWW \nMol Wt. 508.6\nArterolane Maleate is [(W-(2-amino-2<nethy]propyl)-2-cis- \ndispiro(adamantane-2,3,-[l,2,4]trioxolane-5,,l\"-cyclohexane)- \n4”- yl]acetamide maleate.\nArterolane Maleate contains not less than'96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C26H40N2OB, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCategory. Antimalarial.\nDescription. A white to off- white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/artesunate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artesunate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Artesunate Injection\nArtesunate Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nArtesunate with or without buffering agents and other \nexcipients. It is filled in a sealed container.\nArtesunate injection is constituted by dissolving the contents \nof the sealed container in the requisite amount of 5 per cent \nw/v sodium bicarbonate injection, shake vigorously for \n5 minutes and add requisite amount of 0.9 per cent w/v sodium \nchloride injection, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any. case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nArtesunate Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nartesunate, C19H28O8.\nIP 2022\nARTESUNATE INJECTION\nUsual strengths. 60 mg per vial and 120 mg per vial.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder.\nThe content of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations (powder \nfor Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/artesunate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Artesunate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Artesunate\nMol. Wt. 384.4\nArtesunate is (3R,5aS,6R,8aS,9R,1OR,12R,12aR)-decahydro-\n3,6,9-trimethyl-3,12-epoxy-12H-pyrano-[4,3-J1-1,2-\nbenzodioxepin~ 10-01 hydrogen succinate.\nArtesunatecontains not less than 97.0 per cent arid notmoie\nthan 102.0 per centof C19H2s0s, calculated on the dried basis.\nCateg()ry, Antimalarial..\nDescription. A white crystalline powder.\nARTEMISININ\nIP 2010"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ascorbic-acid-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ascorbic Acid Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ascorbic Acid Injection\nVitamin C Injection; l-Ascorbic Acid Injection ,.\nAscorbic Acid Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium \nAscorbate or of Aseorbic Acid prepared with the aid of Sodium\n4040\nww.webofpharma.com\nwww.webofpharma.com\n...... ...................... ........................................................\nHydroxide or Sodium \nWater for Injections.\nCarbonate or Sodium.Bicarbonate^\nAscorbic Acid Injection contains not less than 95.0 percent \nand not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nascorbic acid, C6H8O6.\nUsual strength. 500 mg in 2 ml. \n. \n: i.\nDescription. Aclear, colourless liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ascorbic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ascorbic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ascorbic Acid\nVitamin C; ¿-Ascorbic Acid\nCH2OH\nhqh6^q^q\nHOZ OH\nCJIA \nMol. Wt. 176.1\nAscorbic Acid is (7f)-5-[(S)-1,2-dihydroxyethyl)-3,4-dihydroxy- \n5(7Z)-furan-2-one.\nAscorbic Acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of C6HgO6.\nCategory. Vitamin (antiscorbutic) and pharmaceutical aid \n(antioxidant).\nDescription. A white to almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless crystals, becoming discoloured on exposure to air \nand moisture. \n■ '"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ascorbyl-palmitate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ascorbyl Palmitate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ascorbyl Palmitate\nAscorbyl Palmitate is (21S)-2-[(22f)-3,4-Dihydroxy-5(2/7)-6xo- \n2-furyl]-2-hydroxyethyl hexadecanoate.\nAscorbyl Palmitate contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of C22H38O7, calculated on the. \ndried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid. \n:\nDescription. Awhite or yellowish-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/asenapine-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Asenapine Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Asenapine Maleate\nCjiHjoCINOs \n.... MolWt.401.8\nAsenapine Maleate is 5-chloro-2,3,3a,12b-tetrahydro-2- \nmethyl-l/Mibenz[2,3:6,7]oxepino[4,5-c]pyrrolemaleate.\nAsenapine Maleate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0percentofCi7H16ClNO.C4H4O4, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic.\nDescription. A white to off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ashwagandha-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ashwagandha Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ashwagandha Dry Extract\nAshwagandha Dry Extract is obtained by extracting \nashwagandha (fVithania somnifera (Linn.) Dunal \nFam.Solanaceae) roots with methanol, alcohol, water dr \nmixture of these solvents and evaporation of solvent. :.\nAshwagandha Dry Extract contains not less than 90.0 per\ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nwithanolides, calculated on the dried .basis as sum of \nwithanolideaglycones calculated as withanolide A and \nwithanolide glycosides calculated as withanoside IV. The \nextract may contain suitable added substances.\nCategory. Adaptogenic, alterative, antidepressant, ksaya. \nrasayana. \n■ ;\nDescription. A light brown to brown powder:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/aspartame","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Aspartame"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Aspartame\n9 H H NH2 \nh3c^o>L,n_X^cooh\n... VS I\nC14H18N2O5 \nMol. Wt. 294.3\nAspartame is 2V-L-a -aspartyl-L-phenylalanine -1 -methyl ester.\nAspartame contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CI4Hi8N2O5, calculated on the dried \nbasis. \n:'-\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (sweetening agent).\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/associate-president","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Associate President"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Associate President \nResearch & Development \nAPL Research Centre II \n(A Division ofAurobindo Pharma Limited) \nSurvey No. 71&72, Indrakaran (V) \nSangareddy (M) \nMedak District \nTelengana-502 329.\nBiologics\nMember \t\nProf. Naveet Wig \nProfessor of Medicine \nAll India Institute of Medical Sciences \nAnsari Nagar \nNew Delhi.\nDr. Surinder Singh (Chair), Dr. Anurag S. Rathore and \nDr. S.S. Jadhay.\nBlood and Blood related Products\nDr. R.K. Chaudhary (Chair), Dr. Kabita Chatterjee. \nDr. Debasish Gupta, Dr. J.P. Prasad and Mrs. Kanchan Ahuja\nMember- \nDr. G N. Singh \nSecretary \nSecretary-cum-Scientific Director \n(ex-officio) \nIndian Pharmacopoeia Commission \nSector-23, Rajnagar \nGhaziabad-201 002.\nExcipients\nDr. P.V. Kanitkar (Chair), Mr. Subodh Priolkar, \nDr. D.B.A. Narayana and Dr. Shailesh Nagarsenkar.\nMember \t\nDr. Raman Mohan Singh \nDirector, \nCentral Drugs Testing Laboratory \nZonal FDA Bhawan, Bellasis Road \nGovt. Medical Stores Depot Compound \nMumbai Central \nMumbai-400 008.\nGeneral Chapters\nPrevious Scientific Body\nDr. V.G. Nayak (Chair), Dr. Raman Mohan Singh, \nDr. Sunil Nadkrani, Mr. Antony R. Gomes, Dr. Vinay Aroskar, \nDr. Pramod Dalvi, Dr. Sunder Kalyana Balaji, Mr. Kundan D. Patil, \nMr. Deepak Jakate, Dr. Sheetal Pise, Mr. Sanjay Deshpandey, \nDr. V. B. Malkar, Mr. Mohan Jain, Dr. Luis Countinho.\nIndian Pharmacopoeia Commission also acknowledges \ncontribution of previous Scientific Body Members of IPC for \ntheir valuable inputs in upgrading and publication of IP 2018."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/asthisamhrta","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Asthisamhrta"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Asthisamhrta\nHadjod, Harjora, Cissus quadrangularis\nAsthisamhrta consists of dried stem of Cissus quadrangularis \nLinn. (Fam. Vitaceae).\nAsthisamhrta contains not less than 0.3 per cent of quercetin, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anti-osteoporotic, anti-haemorrhoidal, analgesic, \nanti-oxidant, anti-inflammatory.\nDescription. Stems are longitudinally striated, transversely \ncracked anti pale, green in colour. Fracture short and fibrous; \nodour characteristic and taste bitter."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atazanavir-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atazanavir Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atazanavir Sulphate\nMol. Wt. 802.9\nAtazanavir Sulphate is salt With, sulphuric acid of\n(3S,8S,9S,12S)-3,12-bis(l,1-dimethylethyl)-8-hydroxy-4,11-\ndioxo-9-(phenylmethyl)-6-[[4-(2-pyridinyl)phenyl]methyl]-\n2,5,6,1O,13-pentaazatetradecanedioic acid dimethyl ester.\nAtazanavir Sulphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C3gHs2N607,H2S04, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCa,tegory. Ap.tiretroyiral.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow crystalline powder.\n844\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atenolol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atenolol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atenolol\nAtenolol is (/?5)-4-(2-hydroxy~3-isopropylaminopropoxy) \nPhenylacetamide.\nIP 2022\nATENOLOL\nAtenolol contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C^^NaOs, calculated on the dried \nbasis. ■\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atomoxetine-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atomoxetine Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atomoxetine Capsules\nAtomoxetine Capsules contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \natomoxetine, CI7H21NO.\nUsual strengths. 18 mg; 25 mg; 40 mg; 60 mg; 80 mg; 100 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atomoxetine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atomoxetine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atomoxetine Hydrochloride\nCi7H21NO,HCI \nMol. Wt. 291.8\nAtomoxetine Hydrochloride is (7?)-7V-Methyl-3 -phenyl-3 ~(p- \ntolyloxy)propylamine hydrochloride. \n;\nAtomoxetine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of Ci7H2iNO,HCl \ncalculated oh the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Cerebral activator.\nDescription. A white to creamish yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atorvastatin-calcium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atorvastatin Calcium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atorvastatin Calcium\nC&HegCaFaN^ 10,3H2O\nMol. Wt. 1209.4\nAtorvastatin Calcium is calcium salt of (P7?,87?)-2-(4- \nfluorophenyl)-a,5-dihydroxy-5-( 1 -methylethyl)-3-phenyU4- \n[(phenylamino)carbonyl]-1//-pyrrole-1 -heptanoic acid \ntrihydrate. . \n■\nAtorvastatin Calcium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C66H63 CaF2N4O10j calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis..\nCategory. Antihyperlipidaemic.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11).'"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atorvastatin-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atorvastatin Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atorvastatin Tablets\nAtorvastatin Calcium Tablets\nAtorvastatin Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \natorvastatin, C33H3SFN2O5.\nUsual strengths. 10 mg; 20 mg; 40 mg; 80 mg (1 mg of \nAtorvastatin Calcium (C66H68CaF2N4O10.3H2O) is equivalent \nto 923.8 pg of atorvastatin C33H35FN2O5."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atosiban-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atosiban Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atosiban Acetate\nC43H67N11O12S2 \n. Mol Wt. 994.2\nAtosiban Acetate is [l-(3-Sulfanylpropanoyl)-2-(4-O-ethyl-D- \ntyrosine)-4-threonine-8-omithine] oxytocin.\nAtosiban Acetate contains not less than 93.0 per cent and not \nmore than 107.0 per cent of C43H67NnOi2Si, calculated on the \nanhydrous and aCetic. acid free basis..\nCategory. Oxytocin antagonist..\nDescription. A white to off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atracurium-besylate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atracurium Besylate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atracurium Besylate Injection\nAtracurium Besylate Injection is a sterile solution containing \nnot less than 90.0 per cent arid not morethan 115.0 per cent of \nthe stated amount of atracurium besylate, C65HS2 N2OlsS2.\nAtracurium Besylate Injection contains an amount of the trans- \ntrans-x&oxa&t equivalent to not less than 5.0 per cent and not \nmore than 6.5 per cent of the stated amount of atracurium \nbesylate, an amount of the cis-trans-iswaer equivalent to not \nless than 34.5 per cent and not more than 38.5 per cent ofthe \nstated amount of atracurium besylate and an amount of the \ncis-c/s-isomer equivalent to not less than 55.0 per cent and \nnot more than 60.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of atracurium \nbesylate.\nNOTE— The injection is unstable at room temperature. Store \nall samples in the refrigerator. Analyze all preparation as \nsoon as possible or use a refrigerated sample.\nUsual strength. 10 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atracurium-besylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atracurium Besylate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atracurium Besylate\nAtracurium Besylate is isoquinolinium, 2,2’-[l ,5-pentanediyl \nbis[oxy(3-oxo-3,l-propanediyl)]]bis [l-[(3,4-dimethoxy- \nphenyl)methyl]-1,2,3,4-tetrahydro-6,7-d imethqxy-2-methyl-, \ndibenzenesulphonate.\n1540\nATOSIBAN ACETATE\nIP 2022\nAtracurium Besylate contains not less than 96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C65H82N2OI8S2, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nIt contains not less than 5.0 per cent and not more than 6.5 per \ncent of the trans-trans isomer, not less than 34.5 per cent and \nnot more1 than 38.5 per cent of the cis-trans isomer, and not\nless than 55.0 per cent and not more than 60.0 per cent of the \ncis-cis isomer.\nCategory. Neuromuscular blocking agent.\nDescription. A white to off-white solid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atropine-eye-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atropine Eye Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atropine Eye Ointment\nAtropine Sulphate Eye Ointment\nAtropine Eye Ointment is a sterile preparation of Atropine \nSulphate in an eye ointment base.\nAtropine Eye Ointment contains not less than 92.5 per cent \nand not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \natropine sulphate, (CnH^NOajaJIzSO^HjO.\nUsual strength. 1.0 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atropine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atropine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atropine Injection\nAtropine Sulphate Injection\nAtropine Injection is a sterile solution of Atropine Sulphate in \nWater for Injections.\nAtropine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110 0 per cent of the stated amount of atropine \nsulphate, (C^HjjNO^HsSO^F^O.\nUsual strengths. 500 pg per ml; 600 pg per ml; 1 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/atropine-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Atropine Sulphate","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Atropine Sulphate\n(C17H23NO3)2,H2SO4, H2O \nMol. Wt. 694.8\nAtropine Sulphate is (7?S)-(LR,3r^S)“3-fropoyloxytropanium \nsulphate monohydrate.\nAtropine Sulphate contains not legs than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of atropine sulphate, (Ci7H23NO3)2, \nH2SO4, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticholinergic; antidote to cholinesterase \ninhibitors.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline \npowder.\n1545\nwww.webofpharma.com\nATROPINE SULPHATE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/avidity","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Avidity"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Avidity\nAnti-AB \t\nA I \t3 - 4 seconds \n(Monoclonal)\nA2 \t 5 - 6 seconds\nRouleaux. It is defined as a form of pseudo agglutination in \nwhich the red blood cells look like pile of coins. The red blood \ncells lacking the antigen corresponding to the reagent antibody \nshould not show rouleaux formation. For this 1 volume of \nAnti-AB Monoclonal Blood Grouping Reagent is mixed in a \ntest tube with 1 volume of 2-3 per cent red cell suspension \nwhich lacks the antigen corresponding to reagent antibody \nand the tube is centrifuged at 1000 rpm for 1 minute and \nobserved microscopically for rouleaux formation.\nB \t3 - 4 seconds\nIntensity. It is defined by the appearance of agglutination \nobtained after mixing undiluted Anti-AB Monoclonal Blood \nGrouping Reagent with washed red blood cell suspension. For \nthis 1 volume of the reagent is added to 1 volume of 40 per cent \nred cell suspension on a slide and mixed for 2 minutes. At the \nend of 2 minutes, reaction grade is compared with the strength \nof reaction obtained with the reference preparation."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/azacitidine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Azacitidine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Azacitidine\nC8Hi2N4O5 \nMol Wt. 244.2\nAzacitidine is 4-amino-l-B-D-ribofuranosyl-l,3,5-triazin- \n2(IH)-one.\nAzacitidine contains not less than 98,0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C8HI2N4O5, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white to off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/azathioprine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Azathioprine"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Azathioprine\nMol. Wt. 277.3\n855\nwww.webofpharma.com\nAzathioprine is 6-[(1-methyl-4-nitro-1H-imidazol-5yl)\nsulphanyl]-7H-purine.\nAzathioprine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0percentofC9H7N70 zS, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Immunosuppressant.\nDescription. A pale-yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/azelastine-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Azelastine Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Azelastine Eye Drops\nAzelastine Hydrochloride Eye Drops\nAzelastine Eye Drops is a sterile solution of Azelastine \nHydrochloride in purified water.\nCorrection\nAzelastine Eye Drops Contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of azelastine \nhydrochloride, C22H24C1N3O,HC1.\nfactor\nUsual strength. 0.5 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/azelastine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Azelastine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Azelastine Hydrochloride\nC22H24C1N3O,HC1 \nMol. Wt.418,4\nAzelastine Hydrochloride is (7fS)-4-(4-Chlorobenzyl)-2-(l- \nmethylazepan-4-yl)phthalazin-1 (2//)-one hydrochloride.\nAzelastine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C22H24C1N3O,HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistamine.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/azelnidipine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Azelnidipine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Azelnidipine Tablets\nAzelnidipine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot Chore  than 1 10.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nCategory Difiydropyridine calcium channel blocker.\nDescription. A light yellow to yellow crystalline powder.\nazelnidipine, C33H34N406•\nAZELNIDIPINE TABLETS\nUsual Strengths. 8 mg; 16 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/azelnidipine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Azelnidipine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Azelnidipine\nC33H34NA \nMol. Wt. 582.7\nAzelnidipine is 3-[l-(Diphenyhnethyl)azetidin-3-yl] 5-(l- \nmethylethyl)(4RS)-2-amino-6-methyl -4-(3-nitrophenyl)-1,4- \ndihydropyridine-3,5-dicarboxylate.\nAzelnidipine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C33H34 N4O6, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Calcium channel blocker\nDescription. A light yellow to yellow crystalline powder. It \nshows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/azithromycin-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Azithromycin Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Azithromycin Eye Drops\nAzithromycin Eye Drops is a sterile solution of Azithromycin \nin a suitable oily vehicle.\nAzithromycin Eye Drops contain not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nazithromycin,\nUsual strength. 1.0 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/azithromycin-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Azithromycin Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Azithromycin Oral Suspension\nAzithromycin Oral Suspension is a dry mixture of Azithromycin \nwith buffering agents and other excipients, or is a \nhomogeneous suspension in a suitable vehicle.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of Water just \nbefore use.\nAzithromycin Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and notmorethan 110.0 percent ofthe stated amount of \nazithromycin, C3SIl72N2OI2.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of azithromycin, CasH^N^O^.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nfollowing test.\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 13 per cent, determined on 0.5 g.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nThe constituted suspension or the suspension complies with \nthe tests stated under Oral liquids and with the following \ntests."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/azithromycin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Azithromycin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Azithromycin\nC38H72N2O]2.xH2Owithx= lor2 Mol. Wt. 749.0 (anhydrous)\nAzithromycin is (2R,3S,4R,5R,8R,IQR,\\\\R, 125,135,147?)-\n13-[2,6-dideoxy-3-C-methyl-3-<2“methyl-a-L-rz&o- \nhexopyranosyl)oxy]-2-ethyl-3,4,10-trihydroxy-3,5,6,8,10,12,14- \nheptamethyl-1 l-[[3,4,6-trideoxy-3-(dimethylamino)-p-D-xyZo- \nhexopyranosyl]oxy]-l-oxa-6-azacyclopentadecan- 15-one \nmonohydrate or dihydrate.\nAzithromycin contains not less than 96.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C38H72N2Oi2, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bacitracin-zinc","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bacitracin Zinc"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bacitracin Zinc\nBacitracin Zinc is the zinc complex ofBacitracin.\nBacitracin Zinc has a potency of not less than 60 Units of\nbacitracin activity per mg, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial (for topical use).\nDescription. A white or light yellowish-grey powder;\nodourless or with a faint odour; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bacitracin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bacitracin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bacitracin\nBacitracin is a mixture ofpolypeptides produced by the growth\nof certain strains of Bacillus licheniformis and B. subtilis\n(Fam. Bacillaceae). Its main components are BacitracinAl, B1,\nB2andB3.\nBacitracin has a potency ofnot less than 60 Units ofbacitracin\nactivity per mg, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial (for topical use).\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; odourless or\nwith a faint odour; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/baclofen-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Baclofen Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Baclofen Oral Solution\nBaclofen Oral Solution is a solution of Baclofen in a suitable t \naqueous vehicle. \n, \ni.\nBaclofen Oral Solution contains not less than 95.0 per cent ■/ \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of ; \nbaclofen, C!0Hi2C1NO2.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/baclofen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Baclofen","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Baclofen\nC10H32CINO2 \nMol. Wt. 213.7\nBaclofen is (RS)-4-ammo-3-(4-chlorophenyl)butyric acid.\nBaclofen contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan .101.0 per cent of C^IfyClNCL, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Muscle relaxant.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\n1573\nwww.webofpharma.com\nBACLOFEN"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bakuci","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bakuci"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bakuci\n(Under UV light at 254 nm)\n(Under UV light at 366 nm)\nBala"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bambuterol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bambuterol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bambuterol Hydrochloride\nC18H29N3O5,HC1 \nMol Wt. 403.9\nBambuterol Hydrochloride is 5-[(17?S)-2-[( 1,1-dimethylethyl) \namino]-1-Hydroxyethyl]-1,3-phenylene bis(dimethyl- \ncarbamate) hydrochloride.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nBambuterol Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 101.5 per cent ofthe C^^C^HC! \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Bronchodilator.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder .It \nshows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/barium-sulphate-for-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Barium Sulphate for Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Barium Sulphate for Suspension\nBarium Sulphate .for Suspension is a dry mixture of Barium\nSulphate with suitable dispersing agents and may contain \nsuitable flavours and suitable antimicrobial preservatives.\nBarium Sulphate for Suspension contains not less than 90.0 \npercent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount\nof barium sulphate, BaSO4.\nDescription. A fine, white or creamy white powder.\n1579\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/barium-sulphate-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Barium Sulphate Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Barium Sulphate Oral Suspension\nBarium Sulphate Oral Suspension is a suspension of Barium \nSulphate in a suitable aqueous vehicle.\nBarium Sulphate Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 \nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount \nof barium sulphate, BaSO4.\nDescription. A smooth, white or creamy white suspension."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/barium-sulphate-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Barium Sulphate Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Barium Sulphate Suspension\nBarium Meal\nBarium Sulphate Suspension is a dry mixture of Barium\nSulphate with suitable flavours, colours, preservatives and\nsuspending/dispersing agents.\nBarium Sulphate Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per\ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nbarium sulphate, BaS04.\nDescription. A white or coloured, fine powder or granules.\nIP 2010\nBECLOMETHASONE DIPROPIONATE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/barium-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Barium Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Barium Sulphate\nBaSO4 \n’ \nMol. Wt. 233,4\nCategory. Diagnostic aid (radio-opaque medium for \ngastrointestinal tract).\nDescription. A. fine, heavy, white powder, free from gritty \nparticles; odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bassant-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bassant Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bassant Dry Extract\nBassant Dry Extract is obtained from dried flowering tops or \naerial parts of Hypericum perforatum Linn.(Fam. \nHypericaceae).\nBassant Dry Extract contains not less than 0.1 per cent of \nhypericin, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant, anxiolytic, sedative, anti­\ninflammatory and anti-ulcerogenic.\nDescription. A greenish brown colored powder.\nDetermine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nplate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 6 volumes of formic acid; 9 \nvolumes of water and 90 volumes of ethyl acetate.\nTest solution. Dissolve about 0.25. g of extract under \nexamination in 5 ml of methanol.\nReference solution. Reflux 0.25 g of bassant dry extract IPBRS \nwith 5 ml. of methanol.\nApply to the plate 10.pl of each solution as bands of 10 mm by \n2 mm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air \nand examine in ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 366 nm. Spray \nwith 10.0 per cent y/v<^ \nacid\naminoethyl ester in methanol and heat the plate at 105°.\nExamine the plate in . visible light. The chromatographic \nprofile of the test solution is(siiffil^f ofttfiat\" of the reference \nsolution. \n.A-A.’\nTests -iT 'r'i ■' \n'■\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). : \n.\nLoss on drying (2.4; 19). Not more than 5.0 per cent determined \non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nMicrobiaLcoritammation (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination tests.\n4183\nwww.webofpharma.com\nBASSANT DRY EXTRACT\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve about. 70 mg of the extract under \nexamination in 25.0 ml of methanoltssA filter: \n:\nReference solution.A 0.006 per Cent w7v solution of hypericin \nIPRS in methanol.\nChromatographic system :\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mmpacked with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \ncolumn temperature: 40°,\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 39 volumes of ethyl acetate,\n160 volumes of methanol and 41 volumes of buffer \nsolution prepared by dissolving 15.6 g of sodium di­\nhydrogen orthophosphate in 1000 ml of water, adjusted \nto pH 2.0 with orthophosphoric acid,\n- flowrate: l.O.mlperminute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 590 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of hypericin.\nStorage. Store protected from heat, moisture and against attack \nby insects and rodents.\nBelladonna Leaf consists of the dried leaf and flowering tops \nof Atropa bella-donna Linn, or of A. acuminata Royle ex \nLindley (Fam. Solanaceae) or a mixture of both species. <\nBelladonna Herb contains not less than 030 per cent of total \nalkaloids, calculated as hyoscyamine with reference to the \nmaterial dried at 100° to 105°.\n4184\nIP 2022\nCategory. Anticholinergic. \n,\nDescription. Green to greenish-brown leaves, slightly darker \non the upper surface, often crumpled and rolled and partly \nmatted together in the drug. When whole, thélamina is 5 to \n25 cm Ipng and 3 to 12 cm wide, elliptical to ovate; acuminate \nat the apex, narrowing at the base; margin entire. Petiole 0.5 to \n4 cm in length. The young leaves are highly pubescent, the \nolder leaves are slightly pubescent along the veins. In the \nflowering tops, the stems are hollow and flattened, with leaves \nin pairs of unequal size, in the axils of which are single flowers \nwith campanulate corolla, about 2 cm long and 1.5 cm wide' \npurple or yellow-brown in colour, with five shorty reflexed lobes, \nfive epipetalous stamens and one bilocular; ovary with \nnumerous ovules."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bassant","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bassant"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bassant\nDendhu; St. Johns wort; Hypericum perforatum\n$2022\nBassant consists of whole or cut, dried flowering tops or aerial \nparts of Hypericum perforatum Linn. (Fam. Ilypericaceae)\nBassant contains not less than 0.08 per cent of total hypericin^ \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n; \n,\nCategory. Antidepressant, anxiolytic, sedative, anti-okidant \nand anti-ulcerogenic. \n' \n’ 7'‘-'Ly;\nDescription. The flower is yellow with conspicuous ¡black \ndots. The,sepals are pointed, with glandular dots, ft has a \ncharacteristic balsamic odour, and a bitter resinous, astringent \ntaste."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/beclomethasone-dipropionate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Beclomethasone Dipropionate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Beclomethasone Dipropionate\nC28H37C1O7 \nMol. Wt. 521.1\n1580\nBARIUM SULPHATE FOR SUSPENSION \nIP 2022\nBeclomethasone Dipropionate is 9a-chloro-11 P-hydroxy-16[L \nmethyl-3,20-dioxopregna-l ,4-diene-l 7,21 -diyl dipropionate.\nBeclomethasone Dipropionate is anhydrous or contains one \nmolecule of water of hydration.\nBeclomethasone Dipropionate contains not less than 96.0 per \ncent and not more than 103.0 per cent of C2SH37C1O7, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nDescription. A white to creamy-white, crystalline powder; \nodourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/beclomethasone-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Beclomethasone Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Beclomethasone Inhalation\nBeclomethasone Dipropionate Inhalation;\nBeclomethasone Inhalation Aerosol\nBeclomethasone Inhalation is a suspensionofBeclomethasone\nDipropionate. in a suitable liquid in a suitable pressurised\ncontainer.\nBeclomethasone Inhalation delivers not less than 80.0 per\ncent and not more than 120.0per cent ofthe stated amount per\ninhalation of beclomethasone dipropionate, C2sH37Cl07, by\nactuation of the valve.\nUsual strength. 50 flg per metered dose.\nA. Discharge the container a sufficient number oftimes at low\nrelative humidity into a mortar to obtain about 2 mg of\nanhydrous Beclomethasone Dipropionate. Heat at 110o for 2\nhours at a pressure of2kPa, cool, grind the residue thoroughly\nwith 0.1 g ofpotassiumbromide, add afurther 0.2 g ofpotassium\nbromide and mix thoroughly.\nOn the resultant dispersion determine by infrared absorption\nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that\nobtained with beclomethasone dipropionate RS or with the\nreference spectrum of beclomethasone dipropionate.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak due\nto beclomethasone dipropionate in the reference solution.\n874\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/belladonna-dry-extract-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Belladonna Dry Extract Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Belladonna Dry Extract Tablets\nBelladona Dry Extract Tablets contain not less thah'-9010 per \ncent w/w and not more than 110.0 per cent w/w stated amount \nof belladonna dry extract equivalent to alkaloids of belladonna \ncalculated as sum of atropine and hyoscine, on the dried basis\nCategory. Anticholinergic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/belladonna-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Belladonna Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Belladonna Dry Extract\nBelladonna Dry Extract is obtained from dried Belladonna leaf \nand root of one or more of the cultivated varieties of Atropa \nbella-donna Linn, or A. acuminata Royle ex Lindley (Fam \nSolanaceae) or a mixture of both species by extraction with a \nsuitable solvent using an appropriate procedure.\nBelladonna Dry Extract contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of stated amount of alkaloids \nof belladonna calculated as sum of atropine and hyosine \ncalculated on dried basis.\nCategory. Anticholinergic. -\nUsual strength. 1.0 per cent,\nDescription. Greenish brown colored powder.\nDetermine by thin-layer Chromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nplate with silica gel GF254. :\nMobile phase. A mixture of 90 volumes of acetone, 7 volumes \nof water and 3 volumes of strong ammonia solution.\nTest solution: Disslove 0.6 g of the substance under \nexamination in 20 ml of 0.05 M sulphuric acid, shake for\n4185\nwww.webofpharma.com\nBELLADONNA DRY EXTRACT\n15 minutes, filter and wash the filter with 0.05 M sulphuric \nacid until 20 ml filtrate is obtained; add l;ml of strongammonia \nsolution to the filtrate, extract with two quantities, each of \n20 ml of peroxide-free-ether,separate the ether layer by \ncentrifugation, if necessary, dry the combined ether extract \nover anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter evaporate to dryness \ndna water-bath and dissolve the residue in (15 m\\cAmethanol.\nReference solution. Dissolve 50 mg of atropine sulphate 1PRS \nin 9 ml of methanol (solution A). Dissolve 15 mg of hyoscine \nhydrobromide IP RS in 10 ml of \n(solution B).Mix 1.8\nml of hyoscine hydrobromide solution; (solution; B) and 8 ml \nof the atropine sulphate solution (solution A).\nApply to the plate 20 pl of each solution as bands 10 mm. \nAllow the mobile phase to rise 12 cm. Dry the plate at 105° for\n15 minutes, allow to cool and examine under ultraviolet light at \n254 nm and 366 nm, spray with 10 ml of modified po/ij.sMzwz \niodobismuthate solution until the bands become visible as \norange or brown on a yellow background. The \nchromatographic profile of the test solution is similar to that \nof the reference solution,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/belladonna-soft-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Belladonna Soft Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Belladonna Soft Extract\nIPRS2 IPRSl IPRS1+2 TEST\n(Under UV light at 366 nm)\nIP 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/belladonna-tincture","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Belladonna Tincture"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Belladonna Tincture\nBelladonna Tincture obtained from Belladonna leaf or roots \nof one or more of the cultivated varieties of Atropa bella­\ndonna Linn, or A. acuminata Royle ex Lindley (Fam. \nSolanaceae) or a mixture of both species.\nBelladonna Tincture contains not less than 90 per cent w/w \nand not more than HOper cent w/w of total alkaloids, calculated \nas hyoscyamine, C17H23NO3.\nCategory. Anticholinergic.\nUsual strength. 0.03 per cent w/w.\nDescription. A clear green or brownish green liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benazepril-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benazepril Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benazepril Hydrochloride\nC24H3SN2O5,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 461.0\nBenazepril Hydrochloride is {(3S)-3-[(liS)-l-Ethoxycarbonyl- \n3-phenylpropyl amino]-2,3,4,5-tetrahydro-2-oxo-1/7- \n1-benzazepin-l -yl}acetic acid hydrochloride.\nBenazepril Hydrochloride contains not less than 97.5 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent ofCa^WW* HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription.. A white or almost white, crystalline powder, \nhygroscopic. It shows polymorphism (2.5.11).\n1583\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bendamustine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bendamustine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bendamustine Injection\nBendamustine Hydrochloride Injection\nBendamustine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbendamustine hydrochloride Ci6H2iC12N3O3.HC1.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nNOTE—Bendamustine hydrochloride is cytotoxic; extra care \nrequired to prevent inhaling particles and exposing the skin \nto it.\nUsual strength. 100 mg.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations (Powder \nfor Injections) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bentonite","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bentonite"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bentonite\nBentonite is a natural, colloidal, hydrated aluminium silicate \nthat has been processed to remove grit and non-swelling \ncomponents of the ore.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (suspending agent).\nDescription. A very fine, pale buff or cream-coloured to \ngreyish-white powder, free or almost free from gritty particles."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzalkonium-chloride-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzalkonium Chloride Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzalkonium Chloride Solution\nBenzalkonium Chloride Solution is a solution of a mixture of \nalkylbenzyldimethylammonium chlorides, the alkyl groups \nhaving chain lengths of Cs to Cj8. It may contain ethanol \n(95per cent). In making Benzalkonium Chloride Solution, the \nethanol (95 per cent) may be replaced by Industrial \nMethylated Spirit, diluted so as to be of equivalent strength.\nBenzalkonium Chloride Solution contains not less than \n49.0 per cent w/v and not more than 51.0 per cent w/v of \nalkylbenzyldimethylammonium chlorides, calculated as \nCnHwClN. It may contain not more than 16.0 per cent v/v of \nethanol, C2H6O.\nCategory. Antiseptic detergent.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or slightly yellow, syrupy \nliquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzalkonium-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzalkonium Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzalkonium Chloride\nBenzalkonium Chloride is a mixture of alkylbenzyl­\ndimethylammonium chlorides, the alkyl groups having chain \nlengths of C8 to Ci8.\nBenzalkonium Chloride contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 104.0 per cent of alkylbenzyldimethyl­\nammonium chlorides, calculated as C22H4OC1N on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiseptic.\nDescription. A white or yellowish-white powder or gelatinous, \nyellowish-white fragments, hygroscopic, soapy to the touch."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzathine-penicillin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzathine Penicillin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzathine Penicillin Injection\nBenzathine B enzylpenicillin Inj ection; Benzathine \nPenicillin G Injection\nBenzathine Penicillin Injection is a sterile material consisting \n°f Benzathine Penicillin with or without suspending agents,\nbuffering agents and other excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe injection is constituted by suspending the contents of \nthe sealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water \nfor Injections, immediately before use.\nUsual strength. 450 mg (600,000 Units) (Each mg of Benzathine \nPenicillin is approximately equivalent to 1,330 Units of \npenicillin).\nStorage. The constituted suspension should be used \nimmediately after preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nBenzathine Penicillin Injection contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbenzathine penicillin, Cj6H20N2, (Ci6Hl8N2O4S)2.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder, almost odourless.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzathine-penicillin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzathine Penicillin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzathine Penicillin\nBenzathine Benzylpenicillin; Benzathine Penicillin G\nCl6H20N2,(C16H18N2O4S)2 \nMol. Wt. 909.0\nBenzathine Penicillin is .V,.V-dibcnzylcthylenediammonjum \nbis[(67?)“6“(2-phenylacetamido)penicillanate] containing a \nvariable amount of water.\nBenzathine Penicillin contains not less than 96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per centofCi6H20N2,(C16Hi8N2O4S)2 and \nnot less than 24.0 per cent and not more than 27.0 per cent of \nC16H20N2, both calculated'on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzocaine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzocaine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzocaine\nCategory. Local anesthetic {for gastric:s sedation and: opie \nwound dressing). \n' \neas \nens \na\nfor Description, Identification \nand Tests, refer to ip \nVolume II: \n:\nBenzyl Benzoate Application \n.\nCategory. Topical acaricide anti-parasitic (for treatment \nt \nof\nscabies) and insecticide. - \npO \nODM \nsite \nook\n       \nwww.webofpharma.com\nCA UT. TON — Not to be used i in cats.\nF or Meniification and: Tests refer to IP Fohune Me\n‘For. Description, Identification and Tests s efer to IP \nVolume IE; \n:\nBenzylpenicillin Potassium |\nIP.2022 \nBUPARVAQUONE ‘INJECTION\nBenzylpenicillin Sodium\nFor: Description, Identification and Tests: ‘refer to IP\nVolume IL \neg \nhua \na \na \nwah \nwots\nBenzylpenicillin Injection —-\nFor Identification and Tesis réfer to IP Volume II.\nBetamethasone Sodium Phosphate:\nFor Deseription, Identification and Tests \nrefer te to \nBP \nVolume If. \nme\nBetamethasone: Injection \n-.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 2 mg oft betamethasone per\nFe or identification and Tests refer to IP. Vive i.\nBuparvaquone _\nCHa \nMol. Wt, 326.4\nBuparvaquone. is 2-(trans-4- “tbutyleyciohexylmethyl)-3 3. \nhydroxy-1, 4-naphthaquinone: ’. \nthea \nWs\nBupatvaquone’ conidins not less than: 98. 0 pet cent and. tot \nmore, than 101.0 ) per cent of CaFhacOs,\nCategory. Antiprotozoal,\nDescription:.A: pale: greenish-yellow to brownish-yellow \npowder. \nBP,\nidentific ation.\nDetermine by infrared absosption apectrophotometry \nQ. 4, 6). \nCompare: the spectrum, with that.obtained with Puparvagquone \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of ‘buparvaquone..\nRelated Substances. Determine ‘by thin-layer. chromato, \n‘ography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica. gel GF 254:.. \n*\nMobile phase. Asmixture'of 65-volumes of foluene; 35 volumes\nof hexane'and:5-volumes of glacial acetic acid: :\nTest solution (a). A 4:0 percent wiv: solution: ofthe substance \nunder examination int dichloromethane: \nPe\nunder examination in \\ dichloromethane.\nApply to the plate 5 1] of test solution (a) and’1;2.and 4 yl of\ntest solution (b): Allow: the mobile phase to rise'15:em. After \ndevelopment, dry the plate in a current of warm air and examine \nunder ultraviolet light at 254 nm.‘Any secondary'spot inthe \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution (a) is ‘not more\nintense than the spot in the chromatogram obtained: with the\ntest solution (b). \nee\nSulphated ash (2.3. 18). Not more ‘than 0. \nper cent.\nAssay.. Weigh accurately about 0.45 2 and dissolve i in 60 ml of \nethanol. Titrate with 0.1M methanolic sodium hydroxide \nusing a glass/calomel eléctrode conditionéd'by storing’in an \nequal-volume mixturé“of ethylene glyzol and propanol, \ndetermining the endpoint Potentiometrically (2.4.25). Carry\nout a blank, titration.. \n:\n1.ml of 0: iM methanolic sodiuin ivdroxidle i is equivalent to \n0.03254 g of buparvaquone, C2;H,,.O;. \n.\nCalculate the. content.of C2)H».O5 correcting the: content.of \ntoluene and petroleum spirit. \n-\nStorage. Store cool and dry place in close containers, \nvs protecied \nfrom light and moisture.\nBuparvaquone Injection\nBuparvaquone injectionis a sterile solution of Buparvaquone\nin ethyl oleate or other suitable ester, ina suitable fixed oil or\nina mixture ofthese.:. \nSon \nwT \ncos \ntoe\nBuparvaquone Injection contains not less than 90, 0 per cent\nand not more than 110.0 per cent of stated amount of \nbuparvaquone, C2;Hy.03. \n. \n.\nUsual strength. 30 mg epet ml\n: Identification’:\nIn the Assay, the principal peak i in the tetra, obtained"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzoic-acid-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzoic Acid Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzoic Acid Solution\nBenzoic Acid Solution contains 50 g of benzoic acid, 750 ml of \npropylene glycol, diluted to 1000 ml with purified water.\nBenzoic Acid Solution contains not less than 4,75 per cent \nw/v and not more than 5.25 per cent w/v of benzoic acid, \nCWV\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP-2022\nBENZOIN\nCategory. Antifungal."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzoic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzoic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzoic Acid\nCOOH\nC7H6O2 \nMol. Wt. 122.1\nBenzoic Acid contains not less than 99.5 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of C7H6O2, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antifungal agent; pharmaceutical aid (anti- microbial \npreservative).\nDescription. Colourless, light crystals, scales or needles."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzoin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzoin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzoin\nBenzoin is the balsamic resin obtained from Styrax benzoin \nDryander or Styrax paralleloneurus Perkins, known in \ncommerce as Sumatra Benzoin or from Styrax ionkinensis \n(Pierre) Craib ex Hartwich, or other species of the Section \nAnthostyrax of the genus Styrax, known in commerce as Siam \nBenzoin (Fam. Styraceae).\nBenzoin contains not less than 25.0 per cent of total balsamic \nacids, calculated as cinnamic acid. C9HgO2, in Sumatra Benzoin \nand as benzoic acid, C7H6O2s in Siam Benzoin, calculated on \nthe dried basis..\nCategory. Topical protectant; expectorant and comforting by \nsteam inhalation in acute laryngitis.\nDescription. Unground Sumatra Benzoin — Blocks or lumps \nof varying size, made up of tears compacted together, with a \nreddish-brown, reddish-grey or greyish-brown resinous mass, \nknown in commerce as block behzoin. It also occurs in the \nform of tears with cream-coloured to yellowish surfaces; when \nfractured they exhibit milky-white surfaces.\nUnground Siam Benzoin — Pebble-like tears of variable size \nand shape, compressed, yellowish-brown to rusty-brown\nexternally, milky white on fracture, hard and brittle at ordinary \ntemperatures but softened by heat. ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzoyl-peroxide-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzoyl Peroxide Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzoyl Peroxide Cream\nBenzoyl Peroxide Cream contains Hydrous Benzoyl Peroxide \nin a suitable basis.\nBenzoyl Peroxide Cream contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of anhydrous benzoyl \nperoxide, CI4H|0O4.\nUsual strength. lOpercentw/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzoyl-peroxide-gel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzoyl Peroxide Gel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzoyl Peroxide Gel\nBenzoyl Peroxide Gel is a solution of Hydrous Benzoyl Peroxide \nin a suitable water- soluble basis..\nBenzoyl Peroxide Gel contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of anhydrous benzoyl peroxide,\nC14H10O4,\nUsual strengths. 2.5 per cent w/v; 5 per cent w/v; 10 per cent \nw/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzyl-alcohol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzyl Alcohol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzyl Alcohol\nch2oh\nC,HSO \nMol. Wt. 108.1\nBenzyl Alcohol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 percent ofC7H8O.\nCategory. Local anaesthetic; disinfectant.\nDescription. Clear, colourless, oily liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzyl-benzoate-application","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzyl Benzoate Application"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzyl Benzoate Application\nBenzyl Benzoate Application contains 25 per cent w/w of \nBenzyl Benzoate in a suitable oil-in-water emulsified base.\nBenzyl Benzoate Application contains not less than 22.5 per \ncent and not more than 27.5 per cent w/w of benzyl benzoate,\nC|4Hi2C>2-\nAssay. Dissolve 8.0 g in 10 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) \npreviously neutralised with 0.1 Msodium hydroxide contained \nin a hard-glass flask and neutralise the free acid in the solution \nwith 0.5 M ethanolic potassium hydroxide using 0.2 ml of \nphenolphthalein solution as indicator. Add 40 ml of 0.5 M\nethanolic potassium hydroxide and boil under a reflux \ncondenser on a water-bath for 1 hour. Add 20 ml of water and \ntitrate the excess of alkali with 0.5 Mhydrochloric, acid using \na further 0.2 ml of phenolphthalein solution as indicator. \nRepeat the operation without the substance under examination. \nThe difference between the titrations represents the alkali \nrequired to saponify the benzyl benzoate.\n1 ml of 0.5 Methanolic potassium hydroxide is equivalent to \n0.1061 gofC14H12O2.\nLabelling. The label states that the contents should be shaken \nbefore use.\nBenzylpenicillin Potassium\nPenicillin G Potassium\n0 HI COOK \nuVn \\ -cH3 \n” I XCH\n1 o °\nH H\nc16h17kn2o4s\nMol. Wt 372.5\nBenzylpenicillin Potassium is potassium (67?)-6-(2- pheriyl- \nacetamido)penicillanate, produced by the growth of certain \nstrains of Penicillium notatum or related organisms,, or \nobtained by any other means.\nBenzylpenicillin Potassium contains not less than 96.0 per \ncent and not more than 100.5 per cent of penicillins, calculated \nas Ci6Hi7KN2O4S on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzyl-benzoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzyl Benzoate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzyl Benzoate\nC14H12O2 \nMol. Wt. 212.2\nBenzyl Benzoate is the benzyl ester of benzoic acid.\nBenzyl Benzoate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent w/w of Ci4H12O2.\nCategory. Anti-parasitic (for topical treatment of scabies).\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a clear, colourless, oily \nliquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/benzylpenicillin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Benzylpenicillin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Benzylpenicillin Injection\nPenicillin G Injection\nBenzylpenicillin Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nBenzylpenicillin Potassium or Benzylpenicillin Sodium with or \nwithout buffering agents and other excipients. It is filled in a \nsealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInj ections, immediately before use.\n1611\nwww.webofpharma.com\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 150 mg (250,000 Units), \n300 mg (500,000 Units) and 600 mg (1,000,000 Units) of \nBenzylpenicillin.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nBenzylpenicillin Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \npenicillins, calculated as Ci6Hi8NaO4S.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements, stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betahistine-dihydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betahistine Dihydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betahistine Dihydrochloride\n|| Y CH3 >2HC[\nH\nC8H12N2,2HC1 \nMol. Wt. 209.1\nBetahistine Hydrochloride is W-methyl-2-(2-pyridyl) \nethylamine dihydrochloride.\nBetahistine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of CSHI2N2,2HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder; sometimes \nclumped, odourless or almost odourless, very hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betahistine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betahistine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betahistine Hydrochloride\nH\n~N'CH3\n,2HCI\nCgHI2N2,2HCI\nMol. Wt. 209.1\nBetahistine Hydrochloride is N-methyl-2-(2-pyridyl)\nethylarnine dihydrochloride.\nBetahistine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per cent\nand not more than 102.0 per cent ofCgH12N2,2HCI, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder;\nsometimes clumped, odourless or almost odourless, very\nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betahistine-mesilate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betahistine Mesilate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betahistine Mesilate\nCsH12N2,2CH3SO3H \nMol Wt. 328.4\nBetahistine Mesilate is 2-[(2-Methylamino)ethyl]pyridine \nbis(methanesulphonate).\nBetahistine Mesylate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C8H 12N2, 2CH3SO3H, calculated \non anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antivertigo.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betahistine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betahistine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betahistine Tablets\n(Perfringens)\n223,3763\n223,1362\nBetahistine Mesylate \n-. 958 \t-\nBiological Assay of Gas-gangrene Antitoxin\nBlackleg Vaccine \n3619 Blank- Determinat ions \n5' lach\nwdcr \nVolume I: i to xxx and 1 to 1068; I-1 to 1-102 Vole-.,2,1.'xxXi'takikiVitria 1069 65'5l6 \nVolume 4: xxxix to xxxxii and 4145 to 4385 ;1-0)*204.\n4314\n223,1362\n(Septicum)\n.Betahistine Mesilate\n966\n 23,426,1363\nBiological Indicators\n958 Betamethasone\n902\n58; Volume 3: xxxv to xxxviii and 2659 to 4144;\n1-15\nINDIAN PHARMACOPOEIA 2018"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone Cream\nBetamethasone Dipropionate Cream .\nBetamethasone Cream contains an amount of betamethasone \ndipropionate, C28H37FO7 equivalent to not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbetamethasone, C22H29FO5 in a suitable cream base.\nUsual strength. 0.05 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone-dipropionate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone Dipropionate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone Dipropionate\nBetamethasone Dipropionate is 9a-fluoro-110,17a,21- \ntrihydroxy-16p-methylpregna-l ,4-diene-3,20-dione 17a,21- \ndipropionate.\n1619\nwww.webofpharma.com\nBetamethasone Dipropionate contains not less than 97.0 per \ncent and not more than 103.0 per cent ofC28H37F07, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Topical Steroid.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone Injection\nBetamethasone Sodium Phosphate Injection\nBetamethasone Injection is a sterile solution of Betamethasone \nSodium Phosphate in Water for Injections.\nBetamethasone Injection contains not less than 92.5 per cent \nand not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \nbetamethasone, C22H29FO5.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 4 mg of betamethasone \nper ml. (5.2 mg of Betamethasone Sodium Phosphate is \napproximately equivalent to 4 mg of betamethasone).\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone-lotion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone Lotion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone Lotion\nBetamethasone Dipropipnate Lotion\nBetamethasone Lotion contains an amount of betamethasone \ndipropionate, C28H37FO7 equivalent to not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbetamethasone, C22H29FO5 in a suitable lotion base. :\nUsual strength. 0.05 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone Ointment\nBetamethasone Dipropionate Ointment\nBetamethasone Ointment contains an amount of \nbetamethasone dipropionate, C28H37FO7 equivalent to not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated amount of betamethasone, C22H29FO5t in a suitable \nointment base.\nUsual strength. 0.05 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone-sodium-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone Sodium Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone Sodium Phosphate \nTablets\nBetamethasone Sodium Phosphate Tablets contain not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated amount of betamethasone, C22H29FO5.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 0.5 mg of betamethasone \n(0-65 mg of Betamethasone Sodium Phosphate is approximately \nequivalent to 0.5 mg of betamethasone)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone-valerate-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone Valerate Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone Valerate Cream\nBetamethasone Valerate Cream contains Betamethasone \nValerate in a suitable cream base.\nBetamethasone Valerate Cream contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbetamethasone, C22H29FO5.\nUsusal strengths. 0.1 percent; 0.12 per cent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone-valerate-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone Valerate Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone Valerate Ointment\nBetamethasone Valerate 'Ointment Contains Betamethasone \nValerate in a suitable ointment base.\nBetamethasone Valerate Ointment contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated \namount of betamethasone, C22H29FO5.\nIP 2022'\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 0.025 per cent w/w and \n0.1 per cent w/w of betamethasone (120 mg of Betamethasone \nValerate is approximately equivalent to 100 mg of \nbetamethasone)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone-valerate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone Valerate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone Valerate\nC27H37FO6 \nMol. Wt. 476.6\nBetamethasone Valerate is a 9d-fluoro-l lp,17a,21-trihydroxy-\n16p-methylpregna-l,4-diene-3,20-dione-17-valerate.\nBetamethasone Valerate contains not less than 96.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C27H37FO6, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nDescription. A white to creamy-white powder.\n1628\nBETAMETHASONE SODIUM PHOSPHATE TABLETS\nIP 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betamethasone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betamethasone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betamethasone\nBetamethasone is 9a-fluoro-l 1 £, 17a,21-trihydroxy-\n16 p-methylpregna-1,4-diene-3,20-dione.\n1616\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nbetamethasone\nBetamethasone contains not less than 96.0 per cent and not \nmore than 104.0 per cent of \ncalculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nDescription. A white to creamy-white powder; odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/betaxolol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Betaxolol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Betaxolol Hydrochloride\nC!8H29NO3HC1 \n, Mol Wt. 343.9\nBetaxolol Hydrochloride is (/?.S')-l-[4-[2-(Cyclopropyl \nmethoxy)ethyl]phenoxy]-3-[(l-methylethyl)amino]propan-2-ol \nhydrochloride.\nBetaxolol Hydrochloride contains not.less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.5 per cent of the Ci8H29NO3> HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bezafibrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bezafibrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bezafibrate\nC!9H,0ClNO4 \nMol. Wt. 361.8\nBezafibrate is 2-[4-[2-(4-Chlorobenzamido)ethyl]phenoxy]- \n2-methylpropanoic acid.\nBezafibrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C19H20CINO4, calcuated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Hypolipidaemic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder. It \nshows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bhibhitaki-aqueous-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bhibhitaki Aqueous Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bhibhitaki Aqueous Extract\nBhibhitaki Aqueous Extract is obtained by extracting Bhibhitaki \n(Terminalia bellirica (Gaertn.) Roxb;, Fam. Combretaceae) fruit \nwith water.\nBhibhitaki Aqueous Extract contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent w/w and not more than 120.0 per cent w/w of the stated \namount of gallic acid and ellagic acid.\nCategory. Purgative, expectorant, antihyperlipidaemic, \nsvarabheda.\nUsual strength. 7.0 per cent w/w.\nDescription. A light brown to dark brown powder.\nDetermine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nplate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 3 5 volumes of benzene, 10 volumes \nof methanol, 4 volumes of acetone and 1 volume of water.\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.5 g of the extract under examination \nin 20 ml of methanol under reflux at 80° on a water bath and \nfilter.\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of gallic\nacid IPRS in methanol.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution as bands 10 mm by \n2 mm. Allow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air \nand examine under ultraviolet light at 254 nm and 366 nm. The \nchromatographic profile of the test solution is similar to that \nof the reference solution.\nAcid insoluble ash (2.3.19). Not more than 4.0 per cent.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with theTimit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). .5 .\nLoss of drying (2.4.19). Not more than 5.0 per cent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°. ....\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with thc microbial \ncontamination tests. C-XC. • \n.X—\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4;) 4).:\nTest solution. Dissolve about 0.21 g of the extract under \nexamination or a quantity containing 10mg of gallic acid and\n1 mg of ellagic acid in 80 ml of methanol and dilute to \n100.0 ml with water and filter. 1\nReference solution (a): A O.Olper cent w/v solution of gallic \nacid IPRS in 80 per cent vIv methanol in water. 1\nReference solution '(b). A 0.001 per cent w/v solution of ellagic\nacid IPRS in 80 per cent v/v methanol in water.\n4191\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bhibhitaki","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bhibhitaki"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bhibhitaki"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bhringraj","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bhringraj"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bhringraj\nBhringraj contains not less than 0.1 per cent of wedelolactone, \ncalculated on the dried basis. .\n4192\nIP 2022\ni ■ i j \n■ cm\nalopecia, yakrdroga. \n,\nCategory. Hepatoprotective, cardiotonic,aXphrodkiaA •\nDescription. A green to greenish brown colour when \ncompletely dry. \n, . \n. . . ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bhuiamla-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bhuiamla Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bhuiamla Dry Extract\nPhyllanthus amarus Schum. and Thonn.\nBhuiamla Dry Extract is obtained by extracting Bhuiamla with \naqueous ethanol or ethanol or any other suitable solvent and \nevaporation of solvent:\nBhuiamla Dry Extract contains not less than 90.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 120.0 per cent w/w of the stated amount of \nsum of phyllanthin and hypophyllanthin, calculated on the \ndried basis. It may contain suitable added substances.\nCategory. Antiviral, hepatoprotective, antidiabetic, kasaaya.\nDescription. A greenish brown to green powder.\nA. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 2 volumes of toluene and f; volume \nof ethyl acetate.\nTest solution. Dissolve 500 mg of the extract under examination \nwith methanol and filter. i '.....P : \n:\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.05 per cent w/v\neach (of phyllanthinlPRS and hypophyllanthin IPRSfm. \nmethanoF \n';\"v. I P \n:\n\"■ \nA\" ./ \n4,/ : v i\nApply to the plate TO pl of each solution as bands 10 mm by \n2 mm; Allow the mobile phasejtoifise 8 cm. Eiryme plate inair \nand examine under uUraviolct light at 254 rim and 366 qm, \nspray4 with methan.olic sillphiiric acii^f^ftfi per cent, iv/y). \nHeat the plate.at 120° for 10 minutes ^fiexaminejinday light. \nThe chromatographic profile of the tfest-sojptipnjis similar to \nthat of the reference solution'; ~\"r \n\\ v ' ; :\nr—= \n....;\nTests _ \n_ _ \n_ J\nAcid-msoluble ash (2.319). Notmofe than 3.0 per cent.: ..\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\n4193\nwww.webofpharma.com\nBHUIAMLA DRY EXTRACT\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 5.0 percent, determined \non 5.0 g by drying in oven at 105°. \n< ; : \n2 ;\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination tests. : ; < r 1 ; \n:\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTest solution. Dissolve about. 500 mg of the extract under \nexamination in methanol by gently heating, cool and make up \nthe volume to 100 ml and filter.\nReference solution (a). A 0.05 per cent, w/v solution of \nphyllanthin IPRS in methanol. ..........................\nReference solution (b). À 0.05 per cent w/y solution of \nhypophyllanthin IPRS in methanol.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of methanol and\n35 volumes of water, \nflow rate: 1.5 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 230 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 jil.\nInject reference solutions (a) and (b). The test is. not valid \nunless the relative standard deviation for the replicate \ninjections is not more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution.\nCalculate the contents of phyllanthin and hypophyllanthin.\nStorage. Store protected from heat, moisture and against attack \nby insects and rodents\nBirmi\nChalisa patra; Taxus wallichiana\nBirmi consists of dried leaves .of Taxus wallichiana Zucc.\n(Fam.Taxaceae). \n'mm \n;\n4194\nBirmi contains not less than 0.04 per cent of paclitax r e ­\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticancer, < analgesic, anti inflammatory; anti \nconvulsant and anti pyretic. \n: L .. v-.'\nDescription. Dorsal surface of leaf is greenish brown and \nventraT surface is greenish yellow in colour;! odour \ncharacteristic and agreeable; taste astringent and bitter. IZ"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bhuiamla","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bhuiamla"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bhuiamla\nPhyllanthus amarus\nBhuiamla consists of the dried aerial parts of Phyllanthus\namarus Schum. & Thorn. (Fam. Euphorbiaceae).\nBhuiamla contains not less than 0.25 per cent w/w of total\nphyllanthin and hypophyllanthin, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nDescription. A green to greenish yellow in colour, taste,\nslightly bitter."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/biapenem","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Biapenem"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Biapenem\nCiiH18N4O4S \nMol Wt. 350.4\nBiapenem is (4R,55',65)-3-(6,7-dihydro-5/f-pyrazolo[l ,2-a]\n[1,2,4] triazol-8-ium-6-yl sulfanyl-6-(l-hydroxyethyl)-4,7-oxo- \n1-azabicyclo [3.2.0] hept-2-ene-2 carboxylate.\nBiapenem contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of of Ci5Hi8N4O4S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibiotic.\nDescription. Awhite to pale yellowpowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bicalutamide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bicalutamide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bicalutamide Tablets\nBicalutamide Tablets contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbicalutamide CI8H14F4N2O4S.\nUsual strengths. 50mg; 150 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bicalutamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bicalutamide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bicalutamide\nC18H14F4N2O4S \nMol, Wt. 430.4\nBicalutamide is (/?S)-Ar-[4-Cyano-3-(trifluordmethyl)phenyl]- \n3-[(4-fluorophenyl)sulfonyl]-2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanamide.\nBicalutamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of Q8H14F4N2O4S, calculated on the \ndried basis.\n1636\nwww.webofpharma.com\nn>;do22\nBICALUTAMIDE\nCategory. Antineoplastic.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bifonazole-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bifonazole Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bifonazole Cream\nBifonazole Cream contains Bifonazole in a suitable cream base.\nBifonazole Cream contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of bifonazole,\nC22H18N2'.\n1639\nwww.webofpharma.com\nUsual strength. 1.0 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bifonazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bifonazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bifonazole\nC22Hi8N2 \nMol. Wt. 310.4\nBifonazole is l-[(/?5)-(biphenyl-4-yl)phenylmethyl]-\n1//-imidazole\nBifonazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of C^Hi8N2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/biological","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Biological"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Biological\nAssay of Gas-Gangrene Antitoxin\n(Septicum)\nThe potency of the gas-gangrene antitoxin (septicum) is\ndetermined by comparing the dose necessary to protect mice\nor other suitable animals against the lethal effects of gas-\ngangrene toxin (septicum) with the dose of the Standard\nPreparation of gas-gangrene\nantitoxin\n(c. septicum)\nnecessary to give the same protection. For this purpose the\nGAS-GANGRENE ANTITOXIN (SEPTICUM)\nStandard Preparation ofgas-gangrene antitoxin (c. septicum)\nand a suitable preparation of gas-gangrene toxin (septicum),\nfor use as a test toxin, are required. The test dose ofthe toxin\nis determined in the relation to the Standard Preparation and\nthe potency of the preparation under examination is then\ndetermined in relation to the Standard preparation using the\ntest toxin."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/biperiden-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Biperiden Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Biperiden Hydrochloride\nC^NO^CI \nMol. wt.. 347.9\nBIFONAZOLE CREAM\nIP 2022\nBiperiden Hydrochloride is (AS)-l-[(ZP/?5',27?5',47?S)-bicyclo-\n[2.2.1] hept-5-en-2-yl]-l-phenyl-3-(piperidin-l-yl)propan-kol \nhydrochloride.\nBiperiden Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C2iH29NO,HCl, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticholinergic.\nDescription. Awhite, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/biphasic-insulin-aspart-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Biphasic Insulin Aspart Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Biphasic Insulin Aspart Injection\nBiphasic Insulin Aspart Injection is a sterile buffered \nsuspension of insulin aspart complexed with protamine \ncrystallized in a solution of insulin aspart.\nIt is prepared by the procedure described under Insulin \nPreparations. It is a mixture of insulin aspart and protamine \ncrystallized insulin aspart in defined ratios.\nBiphasic Insulin Aspart Injection contains not less than 90.0 \nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount \nof Insulin Aspart.\nUsual strength. 100 IU per ml (30:70); 100 IU per ml (50:50);\n1001U per ml (70:30).\nDescription. A white suspension."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/biphasic-insulin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Biphasic Insulin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Biphasic Insulin Injection\nBiphasic Insulin Injection is a sterile suspension of crystals \ncontaining bovine insulin in a solution of porcine insulin.\nBiphasic Insulin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated number of IU of \nInsulin.\nUsual strength. 40 IU per ml (30: 70).\nDescription. A white suspension. When examined under a \nmicroscope, the majority of the particles appear as \nrhombohedral crystals, with a maximum dimension of the \ncrystals greater than 10 pm but rarely exceeding 40 pm.\n2605\nwww.webofpharma.com\nINSULIN ZINC SUSPENSION"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/biphasic-isophane-insulin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Biphasic Isophane Insulin Injection","la":"MethodA"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Biphasic Isophane Insulin Injection\nBiphasic Isophane Insulin Injection is a sterile buffered \nsuspension of either porcine or human insulin, complexed \nwith protamine sulphate or any other suitable protamine, in a\nsolution of insulin of the same species.\nIt is prepared by the procedure described under Insulin \nPreparations. It is produced by mixing, in defined ratios, soluble \ninsulin injection and isophane insulin injection.\nBiphasic Isophane Insulin Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \nnumber of IU of Insulin.\nUsual strengths. 40 IU per ml (30:70); 100 IU per ml (30:70); \n40 IU per ml (50:50); 100 IU per ml (50:50).\nDescription: A white suspension which on standing deposits \na white sediment and leaves a colourless or almost colourless \nsupernatant liquid; the sediment is readily resuspended by \ngently shaking. When examined under a microscope, the \nparticles are seen to be rod-shaped crystals, the maj ority with \na maximum dimension greater than 1 pm but rarely exceeding \n60 pm, free from large aggregates."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bisacodyl","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bisacodyl"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bisacodyl\nC22H19NO4 \n. Mol. Wt 361.4\nBisacodyl is bis(4-acetoxyphenyl)-2-pyridylmethane.\nBisacodyl contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C22H19NO4, calculated on the dried basis;\nCategory. Laxative.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nodourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bismuth-carbonate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bismuth Carbonate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bismuth Carbonate\nBi2(CO3)O2 \nMol. Wt. 510.0\nBismuth Subcarbonate contains not less than 80.0 per cent \nand not more than 82.5 per cent of Bi, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antacid.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bisoprolol-fumarate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bisoprolol Fumarate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bisoprolol Fumarate\nBile-Tolerant Gram-Negative Bacteria\nBiological Assay of Gas-gangrene Antitoxin\nBiological Assay of Gas-gangrene Antitoxin\n(Perfringens)\nBiological Assay of Gas-gangrene Antitoxin"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/biuret","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Biuret"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Biuret\n1074\n4393\nBivalent Poliomyelitis Vaccine Type 1 and 3,\nLive (Oral)\n4363\n(Septicum)\n. 4394\nBlackquarter Vaccine ,\n. • . 4952\nBiological Indicators\n1153\nBlack Pepper\n4262\nBiological Indicators, Type of\n. 1153\nBlack Pepper Oil\n268,4195\nBiological Methods \n; ;\n27\nBlackleg Vaccine\n4952\nBiological Reactivity, In Vitro, Test for , \n;\n101\nBlank Determinations\n. 1144\nBiological Reactivity, In Vivo, Test for \n;\n'■ \n104\nBleaching Powder\n1079\nBiological Veterinary Monographs\n4945\nBleomycin Injection\n1650\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVolume 4: xliii to xlvi and 4785 to 5024; I-104 to 1-216. \n__________________________________J - _ ■ • -* ■ ■ ~ ■ ■\nINDIAN PHARMACOPOEIA 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/black-pepper-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Black Pepper Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Black Pepper Oil\nKali Mirchi katail\nBlack: Pepper Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam \ndistillation of the dried unripe berries of Piper nigrum \nbinn,(Fam. Piperaceac).\nBlack Pepper Oil contains not less than 10.0 per cent and not\nwore than 17.0 per cent of limonene, and not less than 12.0 per\ncent and riot more than 29.0 per cent of ¡3-caryophyllene.\nDescription. A clear, colourless to bluish green liquid, free \nfrom sediment and suspended matter.\nBLACK PEPPER OIL\nA. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of the oil under \nexamination in ethanol (95 per cent).\nReference solution, k 2.0 per cent w/v solution of black \npepper oil 1PRS in ethanol (95per cent). : < ■; .. \\\nUse chromatographic system described in the Assay.\nThe chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to .the pcak in the Chromatogram obtained with \nthereferencesoliition. \ni\nB. \nFlash point (Î A44). 46 0° to 50 0® \n- \n-\nRelative density (2.4.29). 0.864 to 0.880.................\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.478 to 1,487, \n.\nOptical rotation (2.4.22).-18.0° to-7.0°.\nAcid value (2.3.23). Notmore than 1,0.\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. A 21) per cent w/v solution of the oil under \nexamination in ethanol (95 per cent).\nReference solution (a). A2.0 per cent w/v solution of limonene\n¡PRS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nReference solution (b). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of\n[¡¡-caryophyllene IPRSfn. ethanol (95 per cent).,\nChromatographic system\n- \na capillary column 30 mx 0.25 mm x 0.25 pm, coated with \nmethyl 5 per cent phenylpolysiloxane,\n- \ntemperature:\ncolumn. 50° for 1 minute, increase from 50° to 220° at a \nrate of 10° per minute, and maintain at this temperature \nfor 13 minutes, \n.\ninlet port at 250® and detector at 280°,\nv- detection by flame ionization detector,\n; ; flow rate 1 ml per minute using nitrogen as carrier\ngas, \n.. T . ■\n- injection volume: 1.0 pl, \n- split ratio : 1:25.\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of limonene and fl-caryophyllene in the \noilusing area normalisation procedure.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\n4195\nwww.webofpharma.com\nBRAHMI -\nBrahmi\nBaçopa monnieri\nBrahmi consists of the dried whole plant, preferably leaves \nand stem of Bacopa monnieri (Linn-). Wettst. (Fam. \nPlantaginaceae).\nBrahmi contains not less than 2.5 per cent w/w ofbacoside A, \ncalculated on the dried basis. ..\nCategory. Braintonic, antiinflammatory, adaptogenic, kustha.\nDescription. A brown to reddish brown colour when \ncompletely dried or green colour when partially dried with \nslightly bitter taste. \n- z"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/blackquarter-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Blackquarter Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Blackquarter Vaccine\nBlackleg Vaccine; Clostridium Chauvoei Vaccine .\nBlackquarter Vaccine is a culture ofa suitable strain or strains\nof Clostridium chaiivoei grown in a suitable anaerobic fluid \nmedium and rendered sterile and. non-toxic by addition of \nformaldeh yde in uch i a manner that it ‘retains its ‘immunizing\nproperties. The vaccine may. contain’ 2 suitable adjuvant, This \nmonograph applies to the Vaccines intended for active \nimmunization of animals against.disease caused by. Cc. \nchauvoei. \n| \n:\nProduction. .\nPreparation of vaccine. C: chewvoei strain uséd for productioti\nis grown ina suitable anaerobic fluid medium and the whole\ncultures are ‘inactivated by addition ‘of suitable quantity of \nformaldehyde. \nA suitable adjuvant may ‘be added” ‘to the \ninactivated cultures.\nChoice of vaccine strain, A reference strain of C, “chauvoet \nobtained from .an authentic-source should be.used. However,\na local isolate from a particular area may also be used-if the\nstrain is shown to:be.satisfactory with respéct to safety and \nimmunogenicity for-the animals. for which: the vaccine is \nintended. \n:\nTestsonMasterseedlot\nThe master’ seéd lot ‘of the’ vacciiié ‘strain’ of C chauvoel is \nmaintained within, thre passages in artificial media-from the \nculture-obtained after target aninal passage. The master seed\nlot complies ‘with ‘the tests ‘of purity arid identity for the \norganism: and a‘batch of vaccine prepared from the’ master \nseed lot showld comply with’ full range ‘of contro! t tests o he!\nidentification, safety'and poteticy. =: \n; \nSa\nVaccine composition. The vaccine contains inactivated strain\nor. strains: of immunogenic; C..chauvoei. with or: without a\nsuitable adjuvant: The vaccine-is shown-to.be. satisfactory \nwith respect to.identification,-safety and immuno genicity f \n‘for\nthe animal species for which \ni it is intended. .\nIdentification.\nThe vaccine protects ‘susceptible ‘animals against infection \nwith C. chauvoei. The potency test may also servé for \nidentification,\nSafety. Carry out sately test either on sheep oron cate as per\nthe following prgcédure. \n‘ \n:\n \n  \n  \nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022 \nBLUETONGUE VACCINE, INACTIVATED\nSheep. Inoculate three one-year-old sheep not vaccinated \nagainst Blackquarter vaccine with double the dose of the \nvaccine ‘product by subcutaneous route ‘at the inner face of\nthe thigh.’ Observe for ten days and record their temperature\nin the morning and evening.\nCattle. Incculate three one-year-old cattle. not vaccinated \nagainst Blackquarter vaccine with double the dose of the \nproduct inthe neck by subcutaneous’ rotite. Observe the \ninoculated-animals for ten days and record their temperature \ntwice a day i in the morhing and ¢ evening, \n-\nThe s¢ed-lot passes the test, if there i is no untoward reaction \nexcept slight swelling at the site of inoculation which subsides\nin four to five days.\nPotency. Inoculate each of ten healthy guinea-pigs weighing \nbetween 350 g and 450 g subcutaneously with 2 ml of the \nvaccine or a quaatity of the- vaccine not greater than the \nthinimuny dosé stated on the label'as‘a primary dose. After\n7 days, re-inoculate these guinea-pigs with 2 ml of the vaccine\nor @ quantity of the vaccine not greater than the minimum \ndose stated on the label as a secondary dose. None ‘of thé \nvaccinated guinea-pigs shows any systemic reaction. However\na minimal focal reaction may be observed in the animals.\nFourteen ‘days ‘after the second vacciiiation; challengé’all \nvaccinated’ guinea- pigs’ along with ‘five controls: by\nintramuscular route with 0:2 mi of virulent culture or 25 viable \nspore suspension of viable culture of virulent C. chauvoei in \nsaline suspension'contaming'2.5 pét cent calcium chloride?\nThe vaccine complies with the test ifnot more than 10 per. cent\nof the vaccinated guinea-pigs die from the C. chauvoei \ninfection-within 5-days; and-all control guinea-pigs die from \nthe C: chauvoei infection within: 72, hours. of challenge,”\nIf more than 10 per ‘vent but. ‘"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bleomycin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bleomycin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bleomycin Injection\nBleomycin Sulphate Injection\nBleomycin Injection is a sterile freeze dried material consisting of \nBleomycin Sulphate with or without excipients. It is filled in a \nsealed container\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2022\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of the liquid stated \non the label before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the tests for Clarity \nof solution and Particulate matter stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nBleomycin injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of bleomycin \nand the content of bleomycins is: bleomycin A2, between \n55 per cent and 70 per cent;bleomycin B2, between 25 and \n32 per cent; sum of bleomycin A2 and bleomycin B2, not less \nthan 85 per cent; demethylbleomycin A2, not more than 5.5 per \ncent; other related substances, not more than 9.5 per cent.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the tests \nstated under Parenteral Preparations (Powders for \nInjection) and with the following requirements.\nUsual strengths. 15 units per vial; 30 units per vial."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bleomycin-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bleomycin Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bleomycin Sulphate\nC5JH84N17O21S3,H2SO4 ' \nMol. Wt. 1513.6\n(Bleomycin A2 Sulphate)\nC5SH84N20O21S2,H2SO4 \nMol. Wt. 1523.6\n(Bleomycin B2 Sulphate)\nBleomycin Sulphate is the sulphate salt of bleomycin, a mixture \nof basic cytotoxic glycopeptides produced by the growth of \nStreptomyces verticillus or produced by other means. Its main \ncomponents are bleomycin A2 and bleomycin B2. Bleomycin A2 \nsulphate is N]-[3 (dimethylsulphonio)propyl]bleomycina-mide \nhydrogen sulphate and Bleomycin B2 is A;7-(guanidinobutyI) \nbleomycinamide sulphate.\nBleomycin Sulphate contains not less than 1.5 and not more \nthan 2.0 Units of bleomycin per mg and the. content of \nbleomycins is: bleomycin A2, between 55 per cent and 70 per \n,cent;bleomycin B2, between 25 per cent and 32 percent; sum \nof bleomycin A2 and bleomycin B2, not less than 85 per cent; \ndemethylbleomycinA2, not more than 5.5 per cent; other related \nsubstances, not more than 9.5 per cent.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white or cream-coloured, amorphous powder.\nCA UTION—• Bleomycin Sulphate must be handled with care, \navoiding contact with the skin and inhalation of airborne \nparticles.\n1649\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/blood-grouping-serums","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Blood Grouping Serums"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Blood Grouping Serums\nCategory. Blood product. \n>\nAntimicrobial preservatives. It contains a suitable \nantimicrobial preservative. \n•\nNOTE—This monograph deals with those, bipod grouping, \nserums for which there are no individual monographs and \nwhich are not routinely used or required for the testing of \nblood or blood products for transfusion.\nStorage. Store at a temperature between 2° to 8°.\nLabelling. The label states (1). the; source! of the product if \nother than human and if of human origin, the source material \nwas not reactive for hepatitis B surface antigen; (2)¡the \nproducts derived from human blood will not transmithepatitis; \n(3) it is for in vitro diagnostic use. \n' ■, ■ -\nEach is a sterile, liquid or dried;preparation containing one or \nmore of the particular blood group antibodies derived from: \nhigh-titered blood plasma or serum of human subjects, with or; \nwithout stimulation by the injection of red cells or other\nEach serum for slide or rapid tube test complies with the tests\nfor ¡avidity with the cells as indicated under tests for potency \nabove.\n4524\n4525\nwww.webofpharma.com\n-•>, Table-2 \n.y,..; \n: .\nSerum . \n/• \n? Phenotype of Cells\nAnti-D\n'■ CDe \n‘ < P . ; ■ ■\n■ . Anti-C\n■■ D ; Cede\nAnti-E\ncdEe\nAnti-CD\nc De, Cede\nAnti-DE\nc De, cdEe\nAnti-CDE\ncdEe, cDe, Cede\nAnti-c \n. .\nCcDEe ,\nAnti-e\ncdEe\nEach serum complies with the tests for specificity by the most \nsensitive method recommended by the manufacturer, in which \nnot less than 4 positive and 4 negative phenotypes are included \n(see Table 3), and confirms the absence of contaminating \nantibodies reactive with Mg, Wr\" antigens as well as other \nantigens having an incidence of 1.0 per cent or greater in the \ngeneral population, except where some of these confirmatory \ntests can not be done by the manufacturer, in which event \nsuch omissions are noted.\nTable-3\nSerum Cells\nAnti-D CcDe, cDe, Cede, cdEe, A! cde, B cde, 0 .cde,\nand where recommended for use by indirect \nantiglobulin technique, cde Bg(a+) cells from 3 \ndifferent donors\nAnti-C \ncDe, Cede, cdEe, C + rfr neg. cells, A! cde, B\ncde, Ocde \n; ;\nAnti-E \ncDe, Cede, cdEe, A] cde, B cde, O cde\nAnti-CD \ncDe, Cede, cdEe, A, cde, B cde, O cde, and\nwhere recommended for detection of the G \nantigen, r^r\nAnti-DE cDe, Cede, cdEe, Aj cde, B cde, O.cde\nAnti-CDE \ncDe, Cede, cdEe, Aj cde, B cde, O cde, and\nwhere recommended for detection of the G \nantigen, rGr\nAnti-c Cede, Ai CDe, B CDe, O CDe, and CDEe or\nCDEorCdE ''' 7 \n,\nAnti-e cdEe, Aj cDE, B cDE, OcDE, andCcDEPr\n< \n■ \n: CDEorCdE ms ■ G • \n.\nAll fresh or frozen red blood cell suspensions used for these \ntests are prepared under; specified conditions and meet \nspecified criteria.\nBLOOD GROUPING SERUMS ANTI-D, ANTI-C,' ANTI-E, ANTI-c, ANTI-e\nIP 2|g if/2022\nCRYOPRECIEITATED ANTIHAEMOPHILIC FACTOR\nEach 100 ml citrate phosphate dextrose (CPD) contains ;\nAssay. Determine the .haemoglobin content by photometric \nhaemoglobinometry (2.8.12).\nMonoclonal Rh (D) antibodies: Monoclonal antibodies ^ \nderived from hybridoma cell lines produced by fusing mo^ \nantibody produced by B lymphocyte with mouse myelq| \ncells or are derived from a human B cell line through Epste^ \nBarr Virus (EBV) transformation. Each hybridoma cell li| \nproduces homogenous antibodies of only one immiinoglobt| \nclass, which are identical in their chemical structure \nimmunological activity.\n■ ■ \nCitric acid (monohydrate) \n0.327 g\nStorage. Store in containers which are made of colourless and \ntransparent glass, or of a suitable plastic material; are sterile \nand sealed so as to exclude micrororganisms. Store at a \ntemperature between 2° and 8°.\nj. ; \nSodium citrate (dihydrate) \n2.63 g\n\" j,. .. Sodium acid phosphate (dihydrate) \n0.251 g\n■ ■ I\nThe type of monoclonal anti-D reagents are:\nDextrose (monohydate) \n2.55 \ng\nWater for injection \n100 ml\nLabelling. The label, states (1) the reference number of the \nWhole Human Blood from which the preparation was made; \n(2) the ABO and .Rh groups of the Whole Human Blood; "}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/blue-to-red","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Blue to Red"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Blue to Red\nsolution to 5.3 ± 0.1, if necessary (Solution A). Dissolve 0.4 g \nof bromocresol purple in 30 ml of water, add 6.3 ml of \n0.1 Msodium hydroxide and dilute with water to make 500 ml \n(Solution B). Shake equal volumes of solution A, solution B \nand chloroform in a separator, allow the layers to separate \nand discard the chloroform. If appreciable colour  is extracted \nby the chloroform, repeat with additional quantities of \nchloroform until no further colour is extracted.\nAlizarin Red S; CI 58005; Alizarin S; Mordant Red B; Sodium\nBromocresol Green \n3.6 to 5.2 \nMethyl Red \n4.2 to 6.3 \nLitmus \n5.0 to 8.0\nYellow to Blue\nAlizarine Sulphonate; 9,10-dihydro-3,4-dihydroxy-9,10-dioxo l\nRed to Yellow\n 2-anthrancesulphonic Acid Monosodium \nsalt,\nRed to Blue\nCI4H7Na07S,H20= 360.27\nBromocresol Purple \n5.2 to 6.8\nYellow to Blue-\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nviolet\nYellowish brown or orange-yellow powder.\nBromothymol Blue \n6.0 to 7.6 \nNeutral Red \n6.8 to 8.0 \nPhenol Red \n6.8 to 8.4 \nPhenolphthalein \n8.3 to 10.0 \nThymolphthalein \n9.3 to 10.5 \nTitan Yellow \n12.0 to 13.0\nYellow to Blue\nAlizarin Red S Solution; Alizarin S Solution: A 0.1 per cent w/v \nsolution of alizarin red S. Complies with the following test.\nRed to Orange\nBromophenol Blue; 4,4'4311-2,1-Benzoxathio1-3-ylidene)- \nbis(2,6-dibromophenol)S,S-dioxide: C I9H10Br405S = 669.96 \nPinkish crystals.\nYellow to Red\nSENSITIVITY TO BARIUM - To 5 ml of 0.05 M sulphuric acid\nColourless to Red\nAfeOrif, 50 ml of acetate buffer pH 3.7 and 0.5 ml of\nCol\nr examination. Add, dropwise, 0.05 M barium\nBromophenol Blue Solution; Strong Bromophenol Blue \nSolution; Ethanolic Bromophenol Blue Solution: Dissovle\nYel\ncolour changes from yellow to orange-red.\n.. ,.,...-:e \n\t\n-,,,-/-\n7 \n's • =.7,\n_\nAtP *1 ''\n_or\n-\nr \n-\n-\n...,\n0.1 g of bromophenol blue with gentle heating in 1.5 ml of \n0.1 M sodium hydroxide and 20 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) \nand add sufficient water to produce 100 ml.\nComplies with the following test.\nSENSITIVITY-  A mixture of 0.05 ml of the solution and 20 ml \nof carbon dioxide-free water to which 0.05 ml of 0.1 M \nhydrochloric acid has been added is yellow. Not more than \n0.1 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is required to change the \ncolour to bluish violet.\nBromophenol Blue Reagent: Dissolve 50 mg of bromophenol\nblue with gentle heating in 3.73 ml of 0.02 Msodium hydroxide \nand dilute to 100 ml with water.\nBromothymol Blue; 4,4'-(31-1-2,l -Benzoxathio1-3-ylidene) - \nbis(2-bromothymol) S,S-dioxide: C 27H28Br205S = 624.39.\nCream-coloured powder.\nBromothymol Blue Solution; Aqueous Bromothymol Blue \nSolution: Dissolve 50 mg of bromothymol blue in 4 ml of \n0.02 Msodium hydroxide and 20 ml of ethanol (95 per cent). \nAfter solution is effected, add sufficient water to produce 100 ml.\nComplies with the following test.\nSENSITIVITY - A mixture of 0.3 ml of the solution and 100 ml \nof carbon dioxide- free water is yellow. Not more than 0.1 ml \nof 0.02 Msodium hydroxide is required to change the colour \nto blue.\nBRP Indicator Solution: Dissolve 0.1g of bromothymol blue, \n20 mg of methyl red and 0.2 g of phenolphthalein in sufficient \nethanol (95 per cent) to produce 100 ml and filter.\nCalcon; CI 15705; Solochrome Dark Blue; Mordant Black 17; \nSodium 2-hydroxy-1-(2-hydroxy-l-naphthylazo)-naphthalene -\n4-sulphonate: C 20H 13N2NaO5S = 416.38\nGeneral laboratory reagent grade of commerce.\nBrownish black powder with a violet sheen. Gives a purple-\nred colour with calcium ions in alkaline solution. When metal\nions are absent, for example, in the presence of an excess of \ndisodium edetate, the solution is blue.\nCalcon Mixture: A mixture of 1 part ofcakon with 99 parts of \nfreshly ignited anhydrous sodium sulphate.\nComplies with the following test.\nSENSITIVITY - Dissolve 0.1 g in 2.5 ml of water. To lml of the \nsolution add 50 ml of water, 10 ml of 1 Msodium hydroxide \nand 1 ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of magnesium sulphate; \nthe solution is blue. Add 0.1 ml of a 0.15 per cent w/"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/boric-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Boric Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Boric Acid\nOH\nho-b'\nOH\nH3BO3 \nMol. Wt. 61.8\nBoric Acid contains not less than 99.5 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of H3BO3, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Local anti-infective.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder or colourless shiny \nplates unctuous to the touch or white crystals; odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bortezomib-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bortezomib Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bortezomib Injection\n70 volumes of water and 0.1 volume of formic acid,\n:, B. a mixture of 8,0 volumes of acetonitrile, \n20 volumes of water and 0.1 volume offormic acid, \n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\nBortezomib Injection is a sterile freeze dried material consisting \nof Bortezomib with or without excipients. It is filled in a \nsealed container.\n1652\n1653\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v) ;\n0\n70\n30\n15\n70\n30\n30\n40\n60\n60\n40\n60\n65\n70\n30\n70\n70\n30\nInject the test solution. The area of any secondary peak is not \nmore than 1.0 per cent and the sum of the areas of all the \nsecondary peaks is not more than 2.0 per cent, calculated by \narea normalisation.\nTertiary Butanol (Ifpresent). Not more than 5000 ppm.\nDetermine by gas chromatography (2.4.13);\nTest solution. Transfer mixed contents of the containers \ncontaining 0.025 g of bortezomib to a 20 ml vial, add 1.0 ml of \ndimethylformamide.\nReference solution (a). A 0.025 per cent w/v solution of\ntertiary butanol in dimethylformamide.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 5.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 10.0 ml with dimethylformamide. Transfer 1.0 ml ofthe \nsolution to a 20.0 ml vial.\nChromatographic system\n-■ a capillary column30mx0.53 mm, packed with bonded\nand crosslinked siloxane (3 pm),\n- temperature:\ncolumn \ntime \ntemperature\n(in min.) \n(°)\n0 8 '• •• ' \n40\n8-10 \n80\n10-14.5 \n90\n- inlet port at 200° and detector at 250°,\n- flame ionization detector,\n- flow rate: 4 ml per minute, helium as the carrier gas.\nInject 1 pl of vapour phase from the reference solution (b) and \nthe test solution.\nCalculate the. content of tertiary butanol from the peak \nresponses of tertiary butanol m the test solution and the \nreference solution (b).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 75 Endotoxin Units \nper mg of bortezomib.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 35 volumes of acetonitrile, 65 volumes of \nwater and 0.1 volume offormic acid.\nIP.2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bortezomib","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bortezomib"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bortezomib\n0\nCH 3\nC19H25BN404 \t\nMol. Wt. 384.2\nBortezomib is [(1R)-3-methy1-1-[[(25)-1-oxo-3-phenyl-2- \n[(pyrazinylcarbony1)-amino]propyllaminolbutyliboronic acid.\nBortezomib contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Ci9H25BN404, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDose. For lymphoma, 1.3 mg per square meter as bolus \nintravenous injection or subcutaneously twice weekly for two \nweeks; followed by ten day rest period; third week considered \na treatment cycle.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bosentan-monohydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bosentan Monohydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bosentan Monohydrate\nC27H29NsO6S,H2O \nMol. Wt. 569.6\nBosentan Monohydrate is 4-(l, 1- Dimethylethyl)-A-[ 6-(2- \nhydroxyethoxy)-5-(2- methoxyphenoxy) [2,2 -bi pyri m i d inj M-y 1 j \nbenzenesulfonamide monohydrate.\nBosentan Monohydrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C27H29N5O6S,.calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive. ,\nDescription. A white to yellowish powder.\n1654\nwww.webofpharma.com\nBOSENTAN MONOHYDRATE\nO*2*\nfaehtification\ndetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \n£0Hipare the spectrum with that obtained with bosentan \nmonohydrate IPRS or with the reference spectrum of bosentan \njBonphydrate.\n3. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/brahmi-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Brahmi Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Brahmi Extract\nBrahmi Extract is obtained by extracting Brahmi (Bacopa \nwwher/(Linn.) Wettst., Fam. Plantaginaceae) from the dried\nleaves and stems of with aqueous ethanol or any other suitable \nsolvent. ' \n■\nBrahmi Extract contains not less than 90.0 per cent w/w and \nnot more, than 120,0 percent w/w of the stated amount of \nbacoside-A (sum of bacoside-A3, bacopaside-II, \nbacppasaponin-C, jujubogenin isomer of bacopasaponin-C).\nCategory. Braintonic, antiinflammatory, adaptogenic, kustha.\nUsual strengths. 10 per cent w/w; 20 per cent w/w.\nDescription. Light green to dark green powder with \ncharacteristic odour and bitter taste."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/brahmi","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Brahmi"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Brahmi\nBacopa monnieri\n6\nBrahmi consists of the dried whole plant, preferably leaves \nand stem of Bacopa monnieri (Linn.) Pennell (Fam.\nScrophulariaceae).\nBrahmi contains not less than 2.5 per cent w/w of bacoside A, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Braintonic, antiinflammatory, adaptogenic, kustha.\nDescription. A brown to reddish brown colour when \ncompletely dried or green colour when partially dried with \nslightly bitter taste."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/brimonidine-tartrate-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Brimonidine Tartrate Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Brimonidine Tartrate Eye Drops\nBrimonidine Tartrate Eye Drops is a sterile solution of \nBrimonidine tartrate in Purified Water. \n. ,\nBrimonidine Tartrate Eye Drops contain not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nbrimonidine tartrate, CnHI0BrNs, C4H()O(,\n1660\nIP 2022\nUsual strengths. 0.1-5 per cent w/v; 0.2 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/brinzolamide-ophthalmic-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Brinzolamide Ophthalmic Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Brinzolamide Ophthalmic Suspension\nBrinzolamide Ophthalmic Suspension is a sterile, aqueous \nsuspension of Brinzolamide containing a suitable antimicrobial \npreservative.\nBrinzolamide Ophthalmic Suspension contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of brinzolamide, Ci2H2iN3O5S3.\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/brinzolamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Brinzolamide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Brinzolamide\nCi2H2iN3O5S3 ; \nMol Wt 383.5\nBrinzolamide is(7?)-4-(ethyIamino)-3,4-dihydro- \n2-(3-methoxypropyl)-2//-thieno[3,2-e]-1,2-thiazine- \n6-sulphonamide 1,1 -dioxide.\nBrinzolamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 102.0 per cent of C12H2iN3O3S3, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Antiglaucoma agent.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/brivaracetam-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Brivaracetam Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Brivaracetam Tablets\nBrivaracetam Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nbrivaracetam, Ci 1 H2o N2O2.\nUsual strengths. 2 5 mg; 5 0 mg; 7 5 mg; 100 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/brivaracetam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Brivaracetam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Brivaracetam\no\nh3c\nh2n\nCnH20N2O2 \nMol. Wt. 212.3\nBrivaracetam is (2S)-2-[(4R)-2-oxo-4-propylpyrrolidin-l- \nyl]butanamide.\nBrivaracetam contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of CuHm N3O2, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\nDescription. A white to off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bromhexine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bromhexine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bromhexine Hydrochloride\nC14H2OBr2N2,HCI \nMol, Wt. 412.6\nBromhexine Hydrochloride is 2,4-Dibromo-6-[[cyclohexyl \n(methyl)amino] methyl] aniline hydrochloride.\nBromhexine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of Ci4H2oBr2N2,HCl, \ncalculated bn the dried basis.\nCategory. Expectorant\n1666 \nMjlgMIMll\nBRIVARACETAM TABLETS\nIP 2022\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder, ft \nshows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bromhexine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bromhexine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bromhexine Tablets\n(Strain 99)\n4365\nBuspirone Hydrochloride Tablets \t\n1428\n130\nBromides, Test for\nBrucella Abortus (Strain 19 Vaccine) Vaccine, Live\n4317\nBuspirone Tablets \t\n1428\nBrominated Hydrochloric Acid AsT\n917\nBrucella Abortus Working Standard Serum\n4365\nBusulphan \t\n224,433,1429\nBromine\n898\n1412\nBuclizine Dihydrochloride\nBusulphan Tablets \t\n1430\nBromine, 0.0167 M\n899\n223,1412\nBuclizine Hydrochloride\n1-Butaneboronic Acid \t\n899\n899,967\nBromine, 0.05 M\n223,431,1413\nBudesonide\n1,2-Butanediol \t\n899\n899\nBromine Solution\n1414\nBudesonide Inhalation\n1,3-Butanediol \t\n899\n899\nBromine Solution, Acetic\nBudesonide Powder for Inhalation\n1415\n2,3-Butanedione dioxime \t\n909\n899\nBromine Water\nBuffer pH, Capillary Electrophoresis for\n265\n1-Butanesulphonic Acid Sodium Salt \t\n943\n899\na-Bromo-2-acetonaphthone\n887\nBuffer Solution pH 2.5\n1-Butanol \t\n899\n899\n4-Bromoaniline\n887\nBuffer Solution pH 7.2\nButan-l-ol \t\n899\n899\np-Bromoaniline\n887\nBuffer (HEPES) Solution pH 7.5\n2-Butanol \t\n899\n899\n4 -Bromoaniline Solution\n885\nBuffer Solutions\nButan-2-ol \t\n899\n957\nBromocresol Blue\n885\nBuffer Solutions, Standard\n2-Butanol Reagent \t\n899\n957\nBromocresol Green\nBuffer type and concentration, Capillary\n264 \n2-Butanone \t\n899\n957\nBromocresol Green Reagent\nElectrophoresis for\n886 \nButan-2-one \t\n899 \nBut9pic \t\n900\n957\nBromocresol Green Solution\nBuffered Acetone Solution\n957\nBrompere_ol Green-Methyl Red Solution\nBuffered Cupric Sulphate Solution pH 2.0\nZ)-But-2-ene-44-dioic Acid\n957\n923\nBtornocresOl-P4rple\nBuffered Cupric Sulphate Solution pH 4.0\nj/1 Acetate \t\n899\n957\nBromocresol Purple Solution\nBuffered Cupric Sulphate Solution pH 5.2\n1064 to 265\n. \tVolume 3: xxxv to xxxviii and 2659 to 4144;\nVolume 1: i to xxx and 1 to 1068; 1-1 to 1-102 \nVolume 4: xxxix to xxxxii and 4145 to 4385 ; I-"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bromocriptine-mesylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bromocriptine Mesylate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bromocriptine Mesylate\nC32H4oBrN505,CHi03S \nMol. Wt. 750.7\nBromocriptine Mesylate is (5'1S')-2-bromo-12'-hydroxy- \n2'-(l-methylethyl)-5 -(2-methylpropyl)ergotaman-3 8- \ntrione methanesulphonate\nBromocriptine Mesylate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of (^ICoBrNsOjXlII^S, \ncalculated on the dried basis; \n.\n1668\nIP 2022:\nCategory. Antiparkinson.\nDescription. A white or slightly coloured, fine crystalline \npowder; very sensitive to light.\nNOTE —- Carry out the tests as rapidly as possible without\nexposure to daylight and with minimum exposure to artificial \nlight."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bromocriptine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bromocriptine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bromocriptine Tablets\n10 e ml\nDescription. White or almost white crystals or crystalline \npowder.\nBromocriptine Mesylate Tablets\nReference solution (d). Dilute 1 ml of reference solution (a) to \n20 ml with the solvent mixture.\nBromocriptine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbromocriptine, C32H40BrN505."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bronopol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bronopol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bronopol\nBr NO2\nC3H6BrNO4 \nMol. Wt 200.0\nBronopol is 2-bromo-2-nitropropane- 1,3-diol.\nBronopol contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C3H6BrNO4, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiseptic; local anaesthetic.\nDescription. White or almost white crystals or crystalline \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/brucella-abortus-rose-bengal-plate-test-antigen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Brucella Abortus Rose Bengal Plate Test Antigen"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Brucella Abortus Rose Bengal Plate Test Antigen\n(Strain 99) \n5003\nBrucella Abortus (Strain 19) Vaccine, Live \n4955\nBrucella Abortus Working Standard Serum \n5003\nBuclizine Dihydrochloride \n1673\nBuclizine Hydrochloride \n268,1673\nBudesonide \n268,531,1674\nBudesonide Inhalation \n1675\nBudesonide Powder for Inhalation \n1676\nBudesonide and Formoterol Fumarate Powder for\nInhalation \n2425\nBuffer Solution pH 2.5 \n1063\nBuffer Solution pH 7.2 \n' \n. 1063\nBuffer Solutions \n1061\nBuffer Solutions, Standard \n1061\nBuffered Acetone Solution \n1062\nBuffered Cupric Sulphate Solution pH 2.0; - \n; 1063\nBuffered Cupric Sulphate Solution pH 4.0 \n; ; 1063\nBuffered Cupric Sulphate Solution pH 5.2 \n1063\nBuffered Palladium Chloride Solution \n■ \n1064\nBuffered Sodium Chloride-Peptone Solution\"piH 7.0 48,130\nBulk Density arid Tapped Density of Powders -- 1 - \n319\n- \n-...............  . '* ■ -A\n- \n7;\nINDEX\nINDIAN PHARMACOPOEIA 2022\nBulk Density \n319\nBumetanide \n268,531,1678\nBumetanide Injection \n1679\nBumetanide Oral Solution \n1680\nBumetanide Tablets \n1681\nBuparvaquone \n4841\nBuparvaquone Injection \n4841\nBupivacaine Hydrochloride ' \n268,532,1682\nBupivacaine Hydrochloride Injection \n1684\nBupivacaine Injection \n1684\nBuprenorphine Hydrochloride \n268,1685\nBuprenorphine Hydrochloride Injection \n1686\nBuprenorphine Hydrochloride Tablets \n1687\nBuprenorphine Hydrochloride and Naloxone Hydrochloride\nSublingual Tablets \n1688\nBuprenorphine Injection \n1686\nBuprenorphine and Naloxone Sublingual Tablets \n1688\nBuprenorphine Sublingual Tablets \n1687\nBupropion Hydrochloride \n268,532,1690\nBupropion Hydrochloride Extended-release Tablets \n1692\nBupropion Hydrochloride Prolonged-release Tablets \n1692\nBupropion Hydrochloride Sustained-release Tablets \n1692\nBurkholderia cepacia Selective Agar \n130\nBurow’s Solution \n1407\nBuserelin \n268,4842\nBuserelin Acetate Injection \n4844\nBuserelin Injection \n4844\nBuspirone Hydrochloride \n268,533,1694\nBuspirone Hydrochloride Tablets \n! \n1695\nBuspirone Tablets \n1695\nBusulphan \n268,533$ 1696\nBusulphan Tablets \n1697\n1,4-Butanediamine ' \n\"■ ■ : :1116\n1-Butaneboronic Acid \n1076\n1,2- Butanediol \n1076\n1,3- Butanediol \n1076\n2,3-Butanedione dioxime \n1086\n1-Butanesulphonic Acid Sodium Salt \n1120\n1-Butanol \n1076\nButan-T-ol \n1076\n2-Butanol \n1076\nButan-2-oI \n- 6 \n1076\n2-Butanol Reagent \n1076\n' ■ \n‘ \n7; : \n,■■■ . ..■ ■ ■ ■ \n'\n....... .......... -...............\nwww.webofpharma.com\nINDIAN PHARMACOPOEIA 2022\nINDEX\n2-Butanone\n1076 Calcipotriol Ointment\n4043\nButan-2-one\n1076 Calcitonin (Salmon)\n4584\nButanoic Acid\n1077 Calcitonin (Salmon) Injection\n4586\n(Z)-But-2-ene-1,4-dioic Acid\n1101 Calcitriol\n269,4044\nButyl Acetate\n1077 Calcitriol Capsules\n4045\nn-Butyl Alcohol\n1076 \nCalcium Acetate\n1077\nsec-Butyl Alcohol\n1076 Calcium and Vitamin D3 Tablets\n4059\ntert-Butyl Alcohol\n1104 Calcium Aspirin Tablets\n1517\nButylamine\n1077 Calcium Borogluconate Injection\n4844\nn-Butylamine\n1077 Calcium Carbonate\n269,1077,4045\na?-Butyl Chloride\n1077 Calcium Carbonate Tablets\n4046\nZerf-Butyl Methyl Ether\n1086 Calcium Chloride\n269,1077,4046\nButyl Hydroxybenzoate\n1698 Calcium Chloride, Anhydrous\n1077\nButyl-4-Hydroxybenzoate\n1698 Calcium Chloride Dihydrate\n4046\nButylated Hydroxytoluene\n268,534,1077,1698 Calcium Chloride Dihydrate Injection\n4047\n1,2-Butylene Glycol\n1076 Calcium Chloride Injection\n4047\n1,3-Butylene Glycol\n1076 Calcium Chloride Solution\n1077\nButylparaben\n268,1077,1698 Calcium Chloride, xM\n1077\nButylsilyl Silica Gel\n1118 Calcium Citrate Malate\n4048\nButylsilyl Silica Gel for Chromatography\n1118 Calcium Dobesilate Monohydrate\n269\nButyric Acid\n1077 Calcium Folinate\n269,4049\nn-ButyricAcid\n1077 Calcium Folinate Injection\n4050\nCalcium Gluconate\n269,1077,4051\nc\nCalcium Gluconate Inj ection\n4052\nCalcium Gluconate Tablets\n4053\nCabergoline\n269,1711 Calcium Hydrogen Phosphate\n4057\nCabergoline Tablets\n1712\nCalcium Hydroxide Phosphate\n4058\nCadmium Iodide\n1077\nCalcium in Adsorbed Vaccines, Limit Test foi\n172\nCadmium Iodide Solution\n1077 Calcium Lactate\n269,4054\nCadmium Standard Solution (10 ppm Cd)\n."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/buclizine-dihydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Buclizine Dihydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Buclizine Dihydrochloride\nCI\nCH3\nCH3\nMol. Wt. 506.0 \nC281-133C1N2,2HC1\nBRONOPOL \t\nIP\nBUDESONIDE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/buclizine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Buclizine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Buclizine Hydrochloride\nBuclizine Dihydrochloride\n^JWM,2HC1 . \n' \n, Mol. Wt 506.0\nBuclizine Hydrochloride is (7?S')-l“(4-teFf-butylbenzyl)- \n4-(4-chlorobenzhydryl)piperazine dihydrochloride\nBuclizine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of C28H33C1N3,2HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic; antiemetic.\nDescription. Awhite or slightly yellowish, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/budesonide-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Budesonide Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Budesonide Inhalation\nBudesonide Inhalation is a suspension of Budesonide in a \nsuitable liquid, filled in a suitable pressurized container.\nBudesonide Inhalation delivers not less than 80.0 per cent \nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbudesonide, C25H34O6 per inhalation by actuation of the valve.\nUsual strengths. 100 pg; 200 pg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/budesonide-powder-for-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Budesonide Powder for Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Budesonide Powder for Inhalation\nBudesonide Powder for Inhalation consists of Budesonide in \nmicrofine powder either alone or admixed with Lactose in a \npre-metered unit for use in a suitable powder inhaler.\nBudesonide Powder for Inhalation contains not less than 80.0 \nper cent and not more than 120.0 per cent ofthe stated amount \nof budesonide, C25H34O6 per unit dose.\nUsual strengths. 100pg;200pg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/budesonide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Budesonide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Budesonide\nC25H34O6 \nMol. Wt 430.5\nBudesonide is a mixture of the C-225 (epimer A) and the \nC-22R (epimer B) epimers of 16a, 17-[(lkS)- \nbutylidenebis(oxy)]-ll|3,21-dihydfoxypregha-\nl,4-diene-3,20-dione.\nBudesonide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of a mixture of epimers A and B, C25H34O6, \ncalculated on the dried.basis.\nCategory. Glucocorticoid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bumetanide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bumetanide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bumetanide Injection\nBumetanide Injection is a sterile solution of Bumetanide in \nWater for Injections.\nThe injection complies with the requirements stated under \nParenteral Preparations and with the following \nrequirements.\nBumetanide Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbumetanide, Ci7H2oN205S.\nUsual strength. 0.25 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bumetanide-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bumetanide Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bumetanide Oral Solution\nBumetanide Oral Solution is a solution of Bumetanide in a \nsuitable flavoured vehicle.\nThe oral solution complies with the requirements stated under \nOral Liquids and with the following requirements.\nBumetanide Oral Solution contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbumetanide, C17H2oN205S.\nUsual strength. 0.25 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bumetanide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bumetanide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bumetanide Tablets\nBumetanide Tablets contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbumetanide, C17H20N2O5S.\nUsual strengths. 0.5 mg; 1 mg; 2 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bumetanide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bumetanide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bumetanide\nMol. Wt. 36404\nBumetanideis 3-(aminosulphonyl)-5-(butylamino)-4-\nphenoxybenzoic acid\nBumetanide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent of C17HzoNzOsS, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bupivacaine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bupivacaine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bupivacaine Hydrochloride\nBupivacaine Hydrochloride is (/?5)“1-butyl W-(2,6-dimethyl- \nphenyl)-2-piperidinecarboxamide hydrochloride monohydrate.\n1682\n•BUMETANIDE. TABLETS\nIP 2022\nBupivacaine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C18H28N2O,HC1 \ncalculated bn the dried basis.\nCategory. Local anaesthetic.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder or colourless crystals- \nalmost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/bupivacaine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Bupivacaine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Bupivacaine Injection\nBupivacaine Hydrochloride Injection\nBupivacaine Injection is a sterile solution of Bupivacaine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injection.\nBupivacaine Injection contains not less than 92.5 per cent \nand not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \nanhydrous bupivacaine hydrochloride, C18H2SN2O,HC1.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 25, 50 and 75 mg of \nanhydrous bupivacaine hydrochloride in 10 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/buprenorphine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Buprenorphine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Buprenorphine Hydrochloride\nCsHmWICI \nMol. Wt. 504.1\nBuprenorphine Hydrochloride is (6R,7R, 14Y)-\n17-cyclopropylmethyl-7,8-dihydro-7-[( 1S)- 1-hydroxy-' \nl,2,2-trimethylprOpyl]-6-(9-methyl-6,14-ethano-\n17-normorphine hydrochloride.\nBuprenorphine Hydrochloride contains not less than 97.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of CjgH^NO^HCl, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Narcotic analgesic.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystallinepowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/buprenorphine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Buprenorphine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Buprenorphine Injection\nBuprenorphine Hydrochloride Injection\nBuprenorphine Injection is a sterile solution of Buprenorphine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injection.\nBuprenorphine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nbuprenorphine, C29H41NO4.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 300 pg of buprenorphine \nper ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/buspirone-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Buspirone Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Buspirone Hydrochloride\nC2|H31N5O2,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 422.0\nBuspirone Hydrochloride is 8-[4-[4-(pyrimidin-2-yl) piperazin-\nl-yl]butyl]-8-azaspiro[4.5]decane-7,9-dione hydrochloride.\nBuspirone Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C2iH3IN5O2,HCl, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Anxiolytic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/busulpban","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Busulpban"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Busulpban\nD. Not less than 80 per cent of the stated amount of\nCJI140 6Sz\nMol. Wt. 246.3\nCZIH31NjOz.\nBusulphan is 1,4-butanediol dimethanesulphonate.\n939\nwww.webofpharma.com\nBUSULPHAN\nBusulphan contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more\nthan 100.5 percent ofC6HI40 6SZ,calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDose. 2. to 4 mg daily; maintenance dose, 0.5 to 2 mg daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/busulphan","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Busulphan"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Busulphan\nh3c' 0 \n/<O\nC6Hi4O6S2 \n■ \\ \nMol. Wt. 246.3\nBusulphan is 1,4-butanediol dimethanesulphonate.\nBusulphan contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of C6Hl4O6S2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/butasteride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Butasteride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Butasteride\nC27H30F6N2O2 \nMol. Wt. 528:5\nDutasteride is 3-oxo-7V-(2,5-bistrifluorbmethyl)phenyl-4-aza- \n5 a-androst-1 -ene-17^carboxamide.\nDutasteride contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more, \nthan 102.0 per cent of C27H30F6N2O2y calculated on the: \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Benign prostatic hypertrophy agent.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/butyl-hydroxybenzoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Butyl Hydroxybenzoate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Butyl Hydroxybenzoate\nCllH140 3\nMol. Wt. 194.2\nButylparaben is n-butyl p-hydroxybenzoate.\nButylparaben contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 102.0percentofCllH140 3•\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or\ncolourless crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/butylated-hydroxytoluene","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Butylated Hydroxytoluene"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Butylated Hydroxytoluene\nBHT\nC15H24O \nMoLWt 220.4\nButylated Hydroxytoluene is 2,6-bis( 1,1 -dimethylethyl)- \n4-methylphenol.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antioxidant).\nDescription. A white to yellowish white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/butylparaben","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Butylparaben"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Butylparaben\nButyl Hydroxybenzoate; Buty1-4-hydroxybenzoate\nButylparaben is M-butyl p-hy dr oxy benzoate.\nButylparaben contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmorethan 102.0percentofCnH1403.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cabergoline","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cabergoline"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cabergoline\nQH37N.O2 \nMol. Wt. 451.6\nCabergoline is l-[(6-Allylergolin-8p-yl)-carbonyl]-l-[3- \n(dimethylamino)propyl]-3-ethylurea.\nCabergoline contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C26H37N5O2, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Prolactin Inhibitor.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder. It \nshows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/caffeine-citrate-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Caffeine Citrate Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Caffeine Citrate Oral Solution\nCaffeine Citrate Oral Solution is a solution of caffeine citrate, \nprepared by the interaction of caffeine and citric acid \nmpnohydrate, in a suitable aqueous vehicle.\nCaffeine Citrate Oral Solution contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncaffeine citrate, CgH10N4O2, C6H8O7. .\nUsual strength. 20 mg per ml (equivalent to 10 mg of caffeine \nbase)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/caffeine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Caffeine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Caffeine\nQH10N4O2 \nMol. Wt. 194.2 (anhydrous)\nCsH10N4O2,H2O \nMol. Wt. 212.2 (monohydrate)\nCaffeine is 3,7-dihydro-l ,3,7-trimethyl-17/-purme-2,6-dione or \nits monohydrate.\nCaffeine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.5 per cent of C8Hi0N4O2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Central nervous system stimulant.\nDescription. Silky white crystals, white glistening needles or \na white crystalline powder; odourless; sublimes readily."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calamine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calamine"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calamine"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calciferol-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calciferol Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calciferol Injection\nCalciferol Injection is a sterile solution ofCholecalciferol or\nErgocalciferol in Ethyl Oleate.\nCalciferol Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ncholecalciferol, C27~O orergocalciferol, C28~O.\nUsual strength. 7.5 mg of Cholecalciferol or Ergocalciferol\nperml.\n[Cholecalciferol or Ergocalciferol contains 40,000 Units of\nantirachitic activity (vitamin D) in each mg].\nDescription. Apale yellow, oily liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calciferol-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calciferol Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calciferol Oral Solution\nCalciferol Oral Drops; Calciferol Solution\nCalciferol Oral Solution is a solution of Cholecalciferolor\nErgocalciferol in a suitable vegetable oil and may be prepared\nby warming to 40° a 1percentw/v suspension ofCholecalciferol\nor Ergocalciferol ina suitable vegetable oil, sllt::lias AIaclll''''s---\nOil, carbon dioxide being bubbled through it to facilitate\nsolution, and adding a sufficient quantity ofthe oil to produce\na solution containing the stated amount of Cholecalciferol or\nErgocalciferol.\nCalciferol Oral Solution contains not less than 85.0 per cent\nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ncholecalciferol, C27~O orergocalciferol, C28~O.\nUsual strength. 75 flg of Cholecalciferol or Ergocalciferol\nperml.\n[Cholecalciferol or Ergocalciferol contains 40,000 Units of\nantirachitic activity (vitamin D) in each mg].\nDescription. Apale yellow, oily liquid; odour, slight butnot\nrancid.\n958\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calciferol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calciferol"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calciferol\nErgocalciferol; Vitamin D 2\nCHs\nCHs\nC28I-lM0\nMol. Wt. 396.7\nCalciferol is (5Z,7E,22E)-(3S)-9,1O-secoergosta-5,7,10(19),22-\ntetraen-3-01.\nCalciferol contains not less than-97.0 per cent and not more\nthan 103.0percentofC28I-lMO.\nCategory. VitaminD (antirachitic).\nDose. Or3ny, in prevention ofrickets, not more than 20 flg (800\nUnits) daily, allowance being made for vitamin D obtained\nfrom other sources; inthe treatment ofrickets and osteomalacia,\n125 flg to 1.25 mg (5,000 to 50,000 Units) daily; in the treatment\nofhypocalcaemia ofhypoparathyroidism, 1.25 to 5 mg (50,000\n955\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCALCIFEROL\nto 200,000Units) daily. By intramuscularinjection,S to 10mg.\n[Calciferol contains 40,000 Units of antiiachiticactivity\n(vitamin D) in each mg].\nDescription. White Or almost white crystals or. a .white or\nslightly yellowish, crystalline powder~ It is sensitive tomr,\nheat and light. A reversible isomerisation to pre-ergocalciferol\nmay occur in solution, depending on temperature and time."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcipotriol-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcipotriol Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcipotriol Ointment\nCalcipotriol Ointment contains anhydrous Calcipotriol in \nsuitable ointment base.\nCalcipotriol Ointment contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent w/w of the stated amount of \ncalcipotriol, C27H40O3.\nUsual strength. 0.005 percent w/w.\nDescription. A white viscous ointment."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcitriol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcitriol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcitriol \n7\nC27H44O3 \nMol. Wt.416.6\nCalcitriol is- (5Z,7£)-9,10-secocholesta-5,7,I0(19)-tnerie'\nla,3P,25-triol. \nuni;-v?/..\nCalcitriol contains not less than 97.0 per cent and hot more \nthan 103.0 per cent of C27H.UO3.\nCategory. Vitamin D analogue. \n. \n; •\nDescription. A white or almost white crystals. = /u. u; ::;, a\nA reversible isomerisation to pre-calcitriol takes place in \nsolution, depending on temperature and time: Theactivity is \ndue to both compounds,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-borogluconate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Borogluconate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Borogluconate Injection\nCalcium Borogluconate Injection is a sterile solution ofCalcium\nGluconate and BoricAcid in Water for Injections. The solution\nmay contain up to 0.2 per cent w/v ofChIorocresol.\nCalcium Borogluconate Injection contains not less than\n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated\namount of calcium, Ca, and boric acid, H3B03 equivalent to\nnot more than 2.3 times the stated content of calcium.\nCategory. Hypocalcaemic.\nDose. All species. By subcutaneous, intravenous or\nintraperitoneal injection, the equivalent of 7.5 to 22.5 mg of\ncalcium per kg ofbody weight.\n(Each 100 mg of calcium borogluconate is approximately\nequivalent to 7.5 mg ofcalcium).\nUsual strength. 25 per cent w/v solution equivalent to 1.9 per\ncent w/v ofcalcium (approximately)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-chloride-dihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Chloride Dihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Chloride Dihydrate\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined\nCaC12,2H20 \t\nMol. Wt. 147.0\nDescription. A fine, white, microcrystalline powder.\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 200°."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-chloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Chloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Chloride Injection\nCalcium Chloride Dihydrate Injection\nCalcium Chloride Injection is a sterile solution of Calcium \nChloride Dihydrate in Water for Injections.\nCalcium Chloride Injection contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 percent of the stated amount of \ncalcium chloride dihydrate, CaCl2,2H2O.\nUsual strengths. Each gram of calcium chloride dihydrate \nrepresents approximately 6.8 mmol (13.6 mEq) calcium and\n13.6 mmol (13.6 mEq) chloride. Each ml of the 10 ml ampoule \ncontains 0.68 mmol (1.36 mEq) calcium.\nA. It gives reaction (A) of calcium salts (2.3.1).\nB. Dilute 1 volume of the injection to 50, ml with water. Gives \nthe reactions of chlorides (2.3.1).\npH. (2.4.24). 5.0 to 8.0.\nAppearance of solution. The injection is nol more, intensely \ncoloured than reference solution BYS6 (2.4.1).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.2 Endotoxin Unit \nper mg of calcium chloride.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Measure a volume of the injection containing about \n0.3 g of Calcium Chloride Dihydrate, add 300 ml of water, \n6.0 ml of sodium hydroxide solution (40 per cent w/v) and \n0.1 g of calcon mixture. Titrate with 0. IM disodium edetate \nuntil the colour changes from violet to blue.\n1 ml of 0.1 M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0.0147 g of \nCaCl,,2IW. \n. .\nLabelling. The label states.(l) the percentage w/y of Calcium \nChloride Dihydrate; (2) the concentration of calcium ion as \nmillimoles in a suitable volume; (3) the concentration of chloride \nion as millimoles in a suitable volume; (4) that the injection \nshould be used in accordance with the manufacturer’s \ninstructions; (5) that solutions containing visible solid particles \nmust not be used.\n1 \n4047\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCALCIUM CITRATE MALATE\nCalcium Citrate Malate\n3Ca++\n'° r^Y0’\nIHO\nOH O\nCa3(C6H5O7)2,3CaC.tH4O5,6H2O, \n; Mol. Wt.l 123.0\nCalcium Citrate Malate is 1,2,3-Propanetricarboxylic acid, 2- \nhydroxy- calcium salt mixture with 2-hydroxybutanedioic acid \ncalcium salt (2:3:6), hexahydrate.\nCalcium Citrate Malate is a complex of calcium, citrate and \nmalate. \n■\nCalcium Citrate Malate contains not less than 23.0 per cent \nand not more than 24.2 per cent of calcium, calculated on the \ndried basis. The sum of citrate and malate is not less .than \n73,0 per cent,.calculated on;the dried basis.\nCategory: Calcium supplement.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Chloride \nAw\nCalcium Chloride Dihydrate \nA A BBSy\nCaCl2,2H2O \nMoLMlM^Qi\nCalcium Chloride contains not less than97.0 percenfandriA\nmore than 103.0 per cent of CaCl2,2H2O. ;, \non\n■ . ■; < Wrisfrfry\nCategory. Calcium replenisher. \n'\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCA LCIW CHLORIDE INJECTION\n. .¿J/\n.'sálÉte ■\nOria p ti o n A white, crystalline powder or fragments or \nii^^fej^OSCOpiC. . • \n■ ;\nO^p)B'rcactions (A) and(B) of calcium salts (2.3.1).\n^Oo'per cent w/v solution in carbon dioxide-free water \n■d from distilled water (solution A) gives reaction A of\n^(2.3.1).\nT$sts^v'\n||$¿afimceof solution. Solution A is clear (2.4.1) and not \n^g^iisely coloured than reference solution YS6(2.4.1).\nAcidityor alkalinity. To 10ml of a freshly prepared lOper\nsolution add 2 drops of phenolphthalein solution.\nTitrate with 0.01. M hydrochloric, acid ov 0.01 M sodium \nf^pyi-de; not more than 0.2 ml is required.\nArsenic(2.3.10). Dissolve 3.33 g in 15 ml of brominated \nhydrochloric acid and 45 ml of water and remove the excess \nof bromine with a few drops of stannous chloride solution \ndsTThe resulting solution complies with the limit test for \narsenic(3 ppm). • \n- \n■"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-dobesilate-monohydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Dobesilate Monohydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Dobesilate Monohydrate\nC12H10CaO10S3,H2O \nJ Mol Wt. 436.4\nCalcium Dobesilate Monohydrate is calcium di(2,5rdihydroxy- \nbenzenesulfonate) monohydrate. ;\nCalcium Dobesilate Monohydrate contains not less than 99.0 \nper cent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C12H10CaOioS2, \ncalculated on, the anhydrous basis..\n1716\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCALCIUM STEARATE\nIP. 2022\nCategory- Indicated in prostatic hypertrophy.\nDescription, A white to almost white, hygroscopic powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-gluconate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Gluconate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Gluconate Injection\nCalcium Gluconate Injection is a sterile solution of Calcium \nGluconate in Water for Injections. Not more than 5.0 per cent \nof the Calcium Gluconate may be replaced with a suitable \ncalcium salt as a stabilising agent.\nCalcium Gluconate Injection contains a quantity-of calcium \nequivalent to not less than 8.5 per cent and notjmore; than \n9.4 per cent of the stated amount of calcium giuconate, \nCI2H220i4Ca, H2O. \n- w ;\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of500 mg and 1 g of calcium \ngluconate in 5 ml; the equivalent of 1 g ofcalcium gluconate in\n10 ml. (A 10 per cent w/v solution of Calcium Gluconate \ncontains approximately 0.45mEq of Ca:' per ml). -/AA-w/G\nA. Determine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel G. '/ \" \" '(ff j"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-hydrogen-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Hydrogen Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Hydrogen Phosphate\nCaHPO4 \nMol. Wt. 136.1 (anhydrous)\nCaHPO4,2H2O \nMol. Wt. 172.1 (dihydrate)\nDibasic Calcium Phosphate is anhydrous or contains two \nmolecules of water of hydration.\nDibasic Calcium Phosphate contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent of CaHPO4 (for anhydrous \nmaterial) or of CaHPO4,2H2O (for the dihydrate).\nCategory. Calcium supplement; pharmaceutical aid (excipient).\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder.\nA, Gives reaction A of calcium salts (2.3.1).\nB. Dissolve 0,1 g in a mixture of 5 ml of 2 Mnitric acid and 5 ml \nof water, the solution gives reaction, Ç, of phosphates (2.3.1).\nAcid-insoluble substances. Heat 5.0 g with a mixture of 40 ml \nof water and 10 mi of hydrochloric acid and dilute to 100 ml \nwith water. Filter, wash with hot water until the last washing \nis free from chloride and dry the residue at 105° for 1 hour \n(0.1 percent).\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 1.0 g in 15 ml of brominated \nhydrochloric acid, add 45 ml of water and remove the excess\nof bromine with a few drops of stannous chloride solution \nAsT. The resulting solution complies with the limit test for\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dissolve 2.5. g in 20 ml of 2 M \nhydrochloric acid, filter if necessary, and add 6 M ammonia \nuntil a precipitate is formed. Add 2 M hydrochloric acid just \nenough to dissolve the precipitate and dilute to 50 ml with \ndistilled water (solution A). 10 ml of the solution complies \nwith the limit test for heavy metals, Method A (40 ppm).\nBarium. To 10 ml of solution A add 0.5 ml of 7 Msulphuric \nacid, mix and set aside for 15 minutes. The solution is not \nmore opalescent than a mixture of 10 nil of solution A and \n0.5 ml of distilled water treated in thé same manner.\n4057\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDIABASIC CALCIUM PHOSPHATE\nIron (2.3.14). 2.0 ml of solution Adi luted to 10 ml with .water \ncomplies with the limit test for Iron (400 ppm).\nCarbonate. Suspend 1 g in .5 ml of water and add 2 ml of \nhydrochloric acid’,, no effervescence is produced.\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 0.2 g in water by the.addition of \n2 ml of nitric acid. The solution complies with the limit test for \nchlorides (0.125 per cent).\nSulphates (2.3.17). Dissolve 30.0 mg in 25 ml of water, by the \naddition of 2 ml of hydrochloric «ck/. The solution complies \nwith the limit test for sulphates (2.3.17) (0.5 per cent).\nNitrate. To 0.1 g add 10 ml of water, 10 ml of nitrogen-free \nsulphuric acid and 3 ml of indigo carmine solution and heat\nto boiling; the, blue colour does not disappear..\nReducing substances. Shake 1.0 g with 5 ml of water and 5 ml \nof 3 M sulphuric acid for 1 minute. Add 0.1 ml of 0.005 M \npotassium permanganate and shake for 20 seconds. The slight\npink colour is not less intense than that produced by treating\n1 g of calcium carbonate in the same manner.\nProteinous impurities. Heat 0.5 g gently in a dry test-tube; \nno change in colour is observed and no unpleasant odour is\nemitted. \n. \n,\nMonocalcium and tricalcium phosphates. Dissolve 2.0 g in \n30.0 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid, add 20 ml of water and \n0.05 ml of methyl orange solution and .titrate the excess of \nacid with 1Msodium hydroxide. Not less than 14.0 ml and not \nmore than 15.5 nil of 1 'Msodium hydroxide (for anhydrous \nmaterial) and not less than 11.0 ml and not more than 12.5 ml of\nI Msodium hydroxide (for the dihydrate) is required.\nLoss on ignition (2.4.20). 6.5 to 8.5 per cent (for anhydrous \nmaterial) .and 24.5 to 26.5 per cent (for the dihydrate), \ndetermined bn 1.0 g by igniting at 800° to 825°.\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.3 g and dissolve in a mixture \nof 5 ml of water and 1 ml of 7 Mhydrochloric acid, add 25.0 ml \nof 0.1 M disodium edetate and dilute to 200 ml with water. \nNeutralise with 5Zro«g ammonia solution, add 10 ml of \nammonia buffer pH 10.0 and 50 mg of mordant black \n11 mixture and titrate the excess of disodium edetate with \n0.1 Mzinc sulphate. Carry out the blank titration.\n1 ml of 0.1 M disodium edetate is equivalent to 0"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-lactate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Lactate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Lactate Tablets\nDescription. White granules or powder; odourless or with \nslight but not unpleasant odour. The pentahydrate is \nsomewhat efflorescent.\nCalcium Lactate Tablets contain Calcium Lactate equivalent \nto not less than 95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent \nof the stated amount of calcium lactate pentahydrate, \nc611,0Ca06,5H20.\nDescription. A white, crystalline or amorphous powder; odour, \nfaint and suggestive of burnt sugar."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-lactate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Lactate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Lactate\nH \nOH \nCa\" H3C COO\n2\nC6H1oCa06,xH20 \t\nMol. Wt. 218.2 (anhydrous)\nCalcium Lactate is hydrated calcium (RS)-2-hydroxy-\npropionate or mixtures of the calcium salts of (R)-, (S)- and\n(RS)-2-hydroxypropionic acid.\nCalcium Lactate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C6H1oCa0 6, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Calcium replenisher.\nDose. Upto 8 g daily, in divided doses."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-levulinate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Levulinate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Levulinate\nCIOH14Ca06,2HzO\nMol. Wt. 306.3\nCalciumLevullnate is calciumdi(4-oxopentanoate) dihydrate.\nCalcium LevuIinate contains ,ndt less than 97.5 per cent and\nnotmore than 100.5 per centofCIOH14Ca06, calculated on the\ndried basis.\n\" .\n.\nCategory. Calciumreplenisher.\nDose. By intramuscular or intravenous injection, 1 g once a\nday\"\n.\nDescription;',Awhite,crystalline'or'amorphous'powder;'odour,\nfaint and suggestive of burnt sugar.\nCALCIUM LACTATE TABLETS\nIP2010\nA; Dissolv~O.5 ginS mlofwater,add5 ml of 1 Msodium\nhydroxide and filter. To the fIltrate add 5 ml ofiodine solution;\na precipitate of iodoform is produced.\nB. Dissolve 0.1 gin 2ml ofwater, add 5 ml ofdinitrophenyl-\nhydrazine solution and allow the mixture to stand in aD. ice-\nbath for 1 hour. Collect the precipitate on a fIlter, wash well\nwith cold water and dry at 105° for 1 hour; the hydrazone so\nobtained melts between 198° and 206°(2.4.21).\nC. Gives the reactions ofcalcium salts (2.3.1).\nD. Meltingrange (2.4.21) 119° to 125°."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-pantothenate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Pantothenate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Pantothenate\nOH\nCa++ HO \t N \t\ncod \nH3C CH 0\nCI8H32CaN2O10 \t\nMol. Wt. 476.5\nCalcium Pantothenate is the calcium salt of(R)-3-(2,4-dihydroxy-\n3,3-dimethylbutyramido)propionic acid.\nCalcium Pantothenate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of CI8H32CaN2010,  calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. B-group vitamin (enzyme co-factor).\nDose. 10 to 100 mg daily, in divided doses.]\nDescription. A white powder; slightly hygr\nCALCIUM PANTOTHENATE TABLETS \nCALCIUM PANTOTHENATE \nIP 2018 \t\nip 201 8\n1 ail of 0. 1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.02383 g of\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablets."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/calcium-stearate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Calcium Stearate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Calcium Stearate\nOctadecanoic acid, calcium salt\nCalcium Stearate, is a compound of calcium with a mixture \nof solid organic acids obtained from fats and consists chiefly \nof variable proportions of calcium stearate and calcium \npalmitate.\nCalcium Stearate contains the equivalent of not less than 9.0 \nper cent and not more than 10.5: per cent of calcium oxide \n(CaO). Stearic acid in the fatty acid fraction is not less than \n40.0 per cent and sum of stearic acid and palmitic acid in the \nfatty acid fraction is not less than 90.0 per cent.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\n1717\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/candesartan-cilexetil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Candesartan Cilexetil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Candesartan Cilexetil\nMol. Wt. 610.7\nCandesartan Cilexetil is (±)-l-Hydroxyethyl 2-ethoxy-l-[p-(o-\nltf-tetrazol-5-yIphenyl)benzyl]-7-benzimidazolecarboxylate, \ncyclohexyl carbonate (ester).\nCandesartan Cilexetil contains not less than 98.7 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C33H34N6O6, calculated on \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/capecitabine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Capecitabine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Capecitabine\nC1SH22FN3O6 \nMol. Wt359.4\nCapecitabine is 5'-deoxy-5-fluoro-A-[(pentyloxy)carbonyl] \ncytidine.\nCapecitabine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C15H22FN3O6i calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/capillary-electrophoresis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Capillary Electrophoresis"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Capillary Electrophoresis\nCapillary Electrophoresis is a method of analysis based on \nthe migration, inside a capillary, of charged analytes dissolved \nin an electrolyte solution, under the influence of a direct-current \nelectric field.\nApparatus\n.An apparatus fqr capillary electrophoresis consists of;\na) a high-voltage, controllable direct-current power supply;\n,b) buffer reservoirs, held at the same level, containing the\nprescribed anodic and cathodic solutions;\nc) \n. electrode assemblies (the cathode and the anode),\nimmersed in the buffer reservoirs and connected to the \npower supply;.\nd) \n. a separation capillary, (usually made of fused-silica) which, \n... -... when.used with some speckle types of detectors, has a-■\n■ optical:viewing window aligned with the. detector. The\nends of the capillary are placed in.the buffer reservoirs.\n,, The capillary is filled with the solution prescribed in the\nmonograph,.;\ne) a suitable.injection system; . . -\n310\n2'. 4.32. CAPILLARY ELECTROPHORESIS\nIP '022\nfl a detector able to monitor the amount of substances of p\ninterest passing through a segment- of the separation p \ncapillaryatagiventime;itisusuallybasedonabsorpLi(>n £ \nSpectrophotometry (UV and visible) or tluorunelry bin ■/\n' cohductimetric, amperometric or mass spectromctric I\ndetection can be useful for specific applications; indirect <• \ndetection is an alternative method used to detect non \nUV-absorbing and non-fluorescent compounds; 1\ng) \na thermostatic system able to maintain a constant = \ntemperature inside the capillary is recommended to obtain ‘ \na good separation reproducibility;\nh) \na recorder and a suitable integrator or a computer.\nThe definition ofthe injection process audits automation are \ncritical for precise quantitative analysis. Modes of injection \ninclude gravity, pressure or vacuum injection <md \nelectrokinetic injection. The amount of each sample component \nintroduced electrokinetically depends on its electrophoretic \nmobility, leadingto possible discrimination using this injection \nmode.\nUse the capillary, the buffer solutions, the preconditioning \nmethod, the sample solution and the migration conditions \nprescribed in the monograph of the considered substance. \nThe employed electrolytic solution is filtered to remove \nparticles and degassed to avoid bubble formation that could\ninterfere with the detection system or interrupt the electrical \ncontact in the capillary during the. separation run. A rigorous \nrinsing procedure should be developed for each analytical \nmethod to achieve reproducible migration times of the solutes.\nCapillary Zone Electrophoresis\nIn capillary zone electrophoresis, analytes are separated in a \ncapillary containing only buffer without any anticonvective \nmedium. With this technique, separation takes place because \nthe different components of the sample migrate as discrete \nbands with different velocities'. The velocity of each band \ndepends on the electrophoretic mobility of the solute and the \nelectro-osmotic flow in the capillary (see General Principles). \nCoated capillaries can be used to increase the separation \ncapacity of those substances adsorbing on fused-silica \nsurfaces.\nUsing this mode of capillary electrophoresis, the analysis of \nboth small (M < 2000) and large molecules (2000 < M <\n100 000) can be accomplished. Due to the high efficiency \nachieved in capillary zone electrophoresis, separation of \nmolecules having only minute differences in their charge-to- \ntnass ratio can be effected. This separation mode also allows \nthe separation of chiral compounds by addition of chiral \nselectors to the separation buffer.\nOptimisation\nOptimisation of the separation is a complex process whets \nseveral separation parameters can play a major role. The main\nwww.webofpharma.com\n2.4.32. CAPILLARY ELECTROPHORESIS\nIP 2022\nfactors to be considered in the development of separations \nare instrumental and electrolytic solution parameters.\nInstrumental parameters\nVoltage /Uoule heating plot is us"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/capreomycin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Capreomycin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Capreomycin Injection\nCapreomycin Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nCapreomycin Sulphate with or without auxiliary agents. It is \nfilled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use:\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCAPTOPRIL\njp2022\nfhe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nparenteral Preparations (Injections).\nUsual strength. 1 g.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCapreomycin injection contains an amount of Capreomycin \nSulphate equivalent to not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 115.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of capreomycin.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/capreomycin-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Capreomycin Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Capreomycin Sulphate\nCapreomycin 1A (free base)\nC25H,;N,4O8 \nMol. Wt. 668.71\nCapreomycin 1A is 3,6-Diamino-M-({(21S',5.S’,lkS',155'.Z)-!5~ \namino-2-(iiydroxymethyl)-H-|(//)-iminohexahydropyrimidinM- \ny 1] -3,6,9,12,16-pentaoxo-8-(ureidomethylene)-1,4,7,10,13- \npentaazacydohexadecan-5-yl} methyljhexanamide.\nCapreomycin IB (free base)\nC25H44Nl4O7 \nMol. Wt. 652.71\nCapreomycin IB is 3,.6-Diamino-A-({(2S,55,llS,15S,2)-15.- \namino-2-(hydroxymethyl)-1 l-[(/?)-iminohexahydropyrimidin-4- \nyl]-3,6,9,12,16-pentaoxo-8-(ureidomethylene)-1,4,7,10,13- \npentaazacyclohexadecan-5 -yl} methyljhexanamide.\nCapreomycin Sulphate is the disulphate salt of capreomycin, \na polypeptide mixture produced by the growth of Streptomyces \ncapreolus, suitable for parenteral use.\nIt has a potency equivalent to not less than 700 pg and not \nmore than 1050 pg of capreomycin per mg.\nCategory. Antituberculosis.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Capsules\nEsomeprazole Gastro-resistant Capsules contains not less \nthan 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of\nesomeprazole, C17Hi9N3O3S.\nUsual strengths. 20 mg; 40 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/captopril-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Captopril Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Captopril Tablets \nI\nCaptopril Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not I \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of captopril I\nC9H15NO3S. \n■ ’ I\nUsual strengths. 12.5 mg; 25 mg; 50 mg. \n|"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/captopril","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Captopril"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Captopril\nC9H15NO3S \nMol. Wt 217.3\nCaptopril is 1 -[(2S)-3-mercapto-2-methylpropionyl]- \nL-proline.\nCaptopril contains not less than 97.5 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CUljNCfS. calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/caraway-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Caraway Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Caraway Oil\nShah Jeera ka tail\nCaraway Oil is the essential oil obtained by stéam disfillatitìn \nof the ripe fruits (dried and crushed) of Carunì carvi L. (Fam. \nUmbelliferae).\nCaraway Oil contains not less than 35/0 per cent and not more \nthan 45.0 per cent of limoneneand not less than 48.0 per cent \nand not more than 60.0per cent of carvone. :\nDescription. A clear, colourless or amber yellow liquid, free \nfrom sediment and suspended matter. ;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carbamazepine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carbamazepine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carbamazepine\nC15H12N2O \nMol. Wt. 236.3\nCarbamazepine is 5//<iibenz[b./jazcpme-5-carboxamide.\nCarbamazepine contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of Ci5Hi2N2O, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\nDescription. A white or yellowish-white, crystalline powder; \nalmost odourless; exhibits polymorphism."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carbenicillin-disodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carbenicillin Disodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carbenicillin Disodium\n0 H CoONa\no V-N A,ch3\nCH3\nNaOOC H H H\nC!7HI6N2Na2O6S \nMol. Wt. 422.4\nCarbenicillin Sodium is the disodium (6J?)-6-[(27?S')-2- \ncarboxylato-2-phenylacetamido]penicillinate.\nCarbenicillin Sodium contains the equivalent of not less than \n770 pg of carbenicillin per mg, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellowish powder; odourless; \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carbenicillin-sodium-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carbenicillin Sodium Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carbenicillin Sodium Injection\nCarbenicillin Injection; Carbenicillin Disodium Injection\nCarbenicillin Sodium Injection is a sterile material consisting \nof Carbenicillin Sodium, with or without auxilliary substances. \nIt is filled in sealed containers.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of a \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Water for Injections \nimmediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the. period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCarbenicillin Sodium Injection contains the equivalent of not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe \nstated, amount of carbenicillin, CnHuNaOgS.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 1 g and 5 g of carbenicillin.\n1734"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carbenoxolone-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carbenoxolone Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carbenoxolone Sodium\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; odourless- 1 \nhygroscopic.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). +132° to +140°, determined \nin a 1.0 per cent w/v solution in a mixture of equal volumes of \nmethanol and 0.02 M sodium carbonate:\n' ' \n' ■ \" \n' ■' \n\" : ■■■■■■■■■■■■■ • ■ J\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the | \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparation\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel F254 (such as \nprecoated Merck silica gel 60 F254 plates).\n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carbidopa","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carbidopa"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carbidopa\n.COOH\nJL H3C NHNH2 HoO\nHO\nOH\nC10H14N2O4,H2O \nMol. Wt. 244.2\nCarbidopa is (S)-3-(3,4-dihydroxyphenyl)-2-hydrazino- \n2-methylpropionic acid monohydrate.\nCarbidopa contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of Ci0H14N2O4, calculated with on the dried \nbasis. .\nCategory. Antiparkinson.\nDescription. A white to creamy white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carbimazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carbimazole"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carbimazole\nC7HlONzOzS\nMol. Wt. 186.2\nCarbimazoleis ethyI3-methyl-2-thioxo-4-imidazoline-1-\ncarboxylate.\n5 mg of 3-0-methylcarbidopa RS in sufficient 0.1 M\nhydrochloric acid to produce 100 ml.\nCarbimazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nChromatographic system\nDose. Controlling dose, 30 to 60 mg daily, in divided doses;\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6mm, packed with\nmaintenance dose, 5 to 20 fig daily.\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 fJlIl),\n_ mobile phase: a mixture of98 volumes ofa 1.4 per cent\"\nDescription. A white or creamy-white, crystalline powder;\nodour, characteristic.\nw/v solution of potassium dihydrogenphosphateand\n-- --2voltimesofmethanol,--- --~------ ------ ----- .---"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carbomers","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carbomers"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carbomers\nCarbomers are high molecular mass polymers of acrylic acid\ncross-linked with polyalkenyl ethers of sugars or polyalcohols.\nCarbomers contains not less than 56.0 per cent and not more \nthan 68.0 per cent of carboxylic acid groups (-COOH), calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Excipient.\ndescription. Awhite, fluffy powder, hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carboplatin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carboplatin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carboplatin Injection\nCarboplatin Injection is a sterile solution of Carboplatin in \nWater for Injections.\nCarboplatin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncarboplatin, C6Hi2N2O4Pt.\nUsual strength. 10 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carboplatin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carboplatin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carboplatin\nCsH12N2O4.pt \n... Mol Wt .371.3\nCarboplatin is (i£P-4-2)-diamrnine[l ,1-cyclo-butanedi \n(carboxylato-&(9)(2-)]platinum. .\nCarboplatin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C6Hi2N2O4.Pt, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A colourless crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carboprost-tromethamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carboprost Tromethamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carboprost Tromethamine Injection\nCarboprost Tromethamine Injection is a sterile solution of \nCarboprost Tromethamine in Water for Injections. It may \ncontain Benzyl alcohol, Sodium Chloride and Tromethamine.\nCarboprost Tromethamine Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of carboprost, C2iH3605.\nUsual'strengths, the equivalent of 250 pg and 500 pg of \ncarboprost in 1 ml.\nDescription. A colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carboprost-tromethamine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carboprost Tromethamine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carboprost Tromethamine\nC2|H36O5, C4HhNO3 \nMol. Wt. 489.70\nCarboprost Tromethamine is. a salt of (5Z,13£)-(87f,9iS,llJ?,\n122?, 15S)-9,11,15 -trihydroxy-15-methyi-prosta-5,13-dienoic \nacid with 2-amino-2-hydroxymethyI-1,3-propanediol.\nCarboprost Tromethamine contains not less than95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of C2!H36O5,C4HliNO3, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCAUTION— Great care should be taken to prevent inhaling \nparticles of Carboprost Tromethamine and-exposing the skin\nio it.\nCategory. Uterine stimulant; abortifacient.\nDescription. A white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carisoprodol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carisoprodol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carisoprodol\nChHmNA \nMol. Wt. 260.3\nCarisoprodol is (7?5)-2-{[(Aminocarbonyl)oxy]methyl} \n-2-methylpentylisopropylcarbamate.\nCarisoprodol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of \ncalculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Muscle relaxant.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carmellose-calcium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carmellose Calcium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carmellose Calcium\nR=H; R=CH2COOH\nCarboxymethylcellulose Calcium is the calcium salt of a partially \nsubstituted poly(carboxymethyl) ether of cellulose.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white to yellowish-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carmustine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carmustine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carmustine Injection\nCarmustine Injection is a sterile lyophilized preparation of \nCarmustine.\nThe injection is prepared immediately before use by dissolving \nthe contents of the sealed container which contains \nCarmustine with or without auxiliary substances in a suitable \ndiluent and then diluting with the requisite volume of a suitable \ndiluent in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCARMUSTINE INJECTION\n। p 2022\nconstituted solution complies with the requirements for\n1 Clarity °J'.solution and Particulate matter stated under \nRental Preparations (Injections).\ngiorage The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCarmustine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncarmustine, C5H9CI2N3O2.\nUsual strength. 100 mg.\nCAVTIOU— Use appropriate surgical gloves,- arm covers \nand a dust mask. Perform all work under a-fume hood \napproved for testing of cytotoxic agents, when possible.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(powdersfor Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carmustine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carmustine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carmustine\nC5H9CI2N3O2\nMol. Wt. 214.1\nCarmustine is urea, W,NMns(2-chloroethyl)-A-nitroso-; 1,3- \nbis(2-chloroethyl)- 1 -nitrosourea.\nCarmustine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C5H9CI2N3O2, calculated on the \nanhydrous and.solvent- free basis. ■■\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. Lightyellowpowder.\nCA UTION — Use appropriate surgical gloves, arm covers \nand a dust mask. Perform all work under a fume hood\napproved for testing cytotoxic agents when possible.\n1750\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCARMUSTINE\n[p;2022\n[dentification\ndetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nMelt a sufficient quantity of sample between 33° to 40° in a \nwater-bath or oven, prepare a thin film between two previously \nwanned bromide plates and record the spectrum immediately. \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with carmustine \n$$#&&& nl ^ie same manner or with the reference spectrum \nof carmustine.\nB In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carnauba-wax","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carnauba Wax"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carnauba Wax\nCarnauba Wax is.obtained from the.leaves of Copernicia \ncerifera Mart. (Fam. Palmae) after purification to remove \nforeign matter.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Apale yellow to light brown coarse powder, flakes \nor lumps of hard brittle wax; odour, characteristic and free \nfrom rancidity."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carprofen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carprofen"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carprofen \ni is (RS): 2 (6+ -Chloro- 9H- carbazol -2-yl)propanoic acid, .\nCarprofen contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more\nthan 101. 5 per cent of C, sH,CINO,, calculated, on the.dried.\nbasis.\nCategory. .Cyclo-oxygenase inhibitor; analgesic; anti-.\ninflammatory, \n. \nLee\nDescription: A white or almost white cryotaline powder It \nshows Polymorphism \n( @. 5: M1).\nIdentifieation pe\n   \nDetermine by infrared abortion: spectrometry (2.4.6\n). Compare, \nthe spectrum with that obtained carprofen IPRS ¢ or wath the.\nreference spectrum of carprofén.”\nif the spectra obtained i in ‘solid state, show differences, dissolve .\nthe substance under examination and the reference, substance, : \nsepatately in acetone, evaporate to dryness and record new \nspectra ‘using the residues.\""}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/carvedilol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Carvedilol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Carvedilol\nc24h26n2o4\nMol. Wt. 406.5\nCarvedilol is (RS)-1 -(9/7-carbazol-4-yloxy)\n-3 -[[2-(2-methoxyphenoxy)ethyl] amino]propan-2-ol.\nCarvedilol contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not mote \nthan 101.0 per cent of C24H26N2O4, calculated on the dried basis.\n1754\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCARVEDILOL TABLETS\nIP 2022\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\npescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nIdentifica^on\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with carvedilolIPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of carvedilol."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cassia-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cassia Oil"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cassia Oil\nDescription. A clear, colourless to pale yellow liquid, \ncharacteristic spicy and anise-like odour.\nCassia oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation of \nthe leaves and young branches of Cinnamomum cassia Blume \n(C. aromaticum Nees) (Fam. Lauraceae)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/castor-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Castor Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Castor Oil\n4: ' c r:\nIndustrial Methylated Spirit Sufficient to produce 1000 ml.\n3694\nCategory. Skin disinfectant. ’"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/caustic-soda","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Caustic Soda"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Caustic Soda\nNaOH \t\nMol. Wt. 40.0\n1 ml of 0.05 M sulphuric acid is equivalent to 0.01121 g of \nC3H5NaO3.\nSodium Hydroxide contains not less than 97.0 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of total alkali, calculated as NaOH.\n1 ml of 1 Mhydrochloric acid used in the second part of the \ntitration is equivalent to 0.1060 g of Na2CO3 .\nStorage. Store in single dose containers of glass or plastic. \nOn keeping, small solid particles may separate from the solution \nin glass containers.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (alkalising agent).\n1 ml of 1 Mhydrochloric acid used in the combined titrations \nis equivalent to 0.0400 g of total alkali, calculated as NaOH.\nDescription. White, crystalline masses supplied as sticks, \npellets or slabs; deliquescent. Readily absorbs carbon dioxide.\nprotected from moisture, in non-metallic\nLabelling. The label states (1) that the Injection is one-sixth \nmolar and contains, in one litre, approximately 167 millimoles \neach of sodium ions and of bicarbonate ions (as lactate); \n(2) that the injection should not be used if the solution contains \nvisible solid particles.\ncontainers.\nCAUTION - Great care should be exercised in handling \nSodium Hydroxide as it rapidly destroys tissues."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefaclor-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefaclor Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefaclor Capsules\nCefaclor Capsules contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of cefaclor, \nC15H14C1N3O4S.\nUsual strengths. 250 mg; 500 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefaclor-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefaclor Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefaclor Oral Suspension\nCefaclor Oral Suspension is a mixture consisting of Cefaclor \nwith buffering agents and other excipients. It contains a \nsuitable flavouring agent. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of Water just \nbefore use.\nCefaclor Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncefaclor, C15H14C1N3O4S.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of cefaclor, C15H14C1N3O4S.\nUsual strengths. 125 mg per 5 ml; 250 mg per 5 ml.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°,\nThe constituted suspension complies with the tests stated \nwider Oral liquids and with the following tests."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefaclor","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefaclor","la":"Hentification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefaclor\nC15H14C1N3O4S,H2O \nMol. Wt 385.8\nCefaclor is (62?,7R)-7-[[(27?)-2-amino-2-phenylacetyl]aniino]-\n3-chloro-8-oxQ75-thia-lrazabicyclo[4.2.0]oct-2-ene \n-2-carboxylic;acid.monohydrate. .....\nCefaclor contains not less than 9*6.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CisHmCINjCXS; calculated on the \nanhydrous basis. \n> ■ ’\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. Awhite or slightly yellow powder.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2022\nCEFACLOR\nHentification\npetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with cefaclor IPRS \nor with the reference spectrum of cefaclor."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefadroxil-monohydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefadroxil Monohydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefadroxil Monohydrate\nCi6H17N3O5S,H2O \nMol. Wt. 381.4\nCefadroxil is 7-[(/?)-2-amino-2-(4-hydroxyphenyl)acetamido]- \n3-methyl-3-cephem-4-carboxylic acid monohydrate.\nCefadroxil contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C16H17N3O5S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder.."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefadroxil-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefadroxil Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefadroxil Oral Suspension\nCefadroxil Mixture\nCefadroxil Oral Suspension is a mixture of Cefadroxil with \nbuffering agents and other excipients. It contains a suitable \nflavouring agent. It is filled in a sealed container. . ..\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water just \nbefore use.\nCefadroxil Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \n,C16H17N3OsS... \n.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of cefadroxil.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 125 mg and 250 mg of \nanhydrous cefadroxil per 5 ml after reconstitution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefadroxil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefadroxil"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefadroxil\nCH;H17N30S~,H20\nMol. Wt. 381.4\nexamination in 50.0 ml ofmobile phaseA.\nIP 2010"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefamandole-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefamandole Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefamandole Injection\nCefamandole Nafate Injection\nCefamandole Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nCefamandole Nafate with or without buffering agents and other \nexcipients. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requiremens for \nthe Clarity of Solution and. Particulate matter stated under \nParental Preparations (Injections).\n\"I\nIp202z >\nCEFAMANDOLE NAFATE\n!P2022\nCEFAZOLIN SODIUM\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 1 g; 2 g and 10 g Of I \ncefamandole. \nI\n/^i-ornatographic A'^cm\nsodium IPRS or with the reference spectrum of cefazolin \nsodium.\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \nmobile phase: a mixture of 25 volumes of acetonitrile \nand 75 volumes of a 10 per cent v/v solution of triethyl-\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately | \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period I \nrecommended by the manufacturer. \n|\nCefamandole Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent I\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution. '\n:amine, adjusted to pH 2.5 with orthophosphoric acid, \n_ flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\nC. It gives reactions of sodium salts (2.2.1)..\nand not more than 115.0 per cent of stated amount of | \ncefamandole, ClgHlgN6O5S2. \n|\nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \n„ injection volume: 20 ul."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefamandole-nafate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefamandole Nafate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefamandole Nafate\nC19Hl7N6NaO6S2 \n' . \nMol. Wt 512.5\nCefamandole Nafate is 7-D-mandeIamido-3-[[(l-methyl-\n177-tetrazol-5-yI)thio]methyl]-3-cephem-4-carboxylic acid.\nwww.webofpharma.com\njp2022\nCEFAMANDOLE NAFATE\nCefarnandolc Nafate containt is not less than 93.0 per cent\nand not more than 102.0 per cent of Ci9Hi7N6NaO6S2, calculated \non the anhydrous and sodium carbonate-free basis, for the \nsum of the content of cefamandole nafate, and cefamandole \nsodium expressed as cefamandole nafate.\nCefamandole Sodium containt is not more than 10.0 per cent \nofCigHiyNfiNaC^, calculated on the anhydrous and sodium \ncarbonate-free basis.\nSodium Carbonate contains not less than 4.8 per cent and not \nniore.than 6.4 per cent of Na2CO3.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefazolin-sodium-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefazolin Sodium Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefazolin Sodium Injection\nCefazolin Injection; Cephazolin Sodium Injection; \nCephazolin Injection\nCefazolin Sodium Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nCefazolin Sodium with or without excipients. It is filled in sealed \ncontainers.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 125 mg; 250 mg; 500 mg; \nand 1 g of cefazolin.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCefazolin Sodium Injection contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncefazolin, C I4H14N804S•\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder; \nodourless.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefdinir-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefdinir Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefdinir Oral Suspension\nCefdinir Oral Suspension is a dry mixture consisting of \nCefdinir with buffering agents and other excipients. It contains \na suitable flavouring agent. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nsealed container in the specified volume of water just before \nuse.\nCefdinir Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 120.6 per cent of the stated amount of \nCefdinir, C14Hi3N5O5S2.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains hot less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of Cefdinir C-.JI; 3N5C>5S2.\n1776\nIP 2022\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nUsual strength. Cefdinir 125 mg per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefepime-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefepime Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefepime Hydrochloride\nC19H25C1N6O5S2,HC1,H2O \nMol. wt. 571.5\nCefepime Hydrochloride is 1-[[(6A, 77?)-7-[[(2Z)-(2- \naminothiazoM-yl)(methoxyimino)acetyl]amino]-2-carboxy-8- \noxo-5-thia-l-azabicyclo[4.2.0]oct-2-en-3-yl]methyl]-l-methyl- \npyrrolidinium chloride monohydrochloride monohydrate.\nCefepime Hydrochloride contains not less than 825 pg \nnot more than 911 pg of cefepime, Ci9H24N6O5S2, per mg, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefepime-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefepime Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefepime Injection\nCefepime Hydrochloride Inj ection\nCefepime Injection is sterile mixture ofCefepime Hydrochloride \nand Arginine. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents ofthe \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period not \nexceeding 7 days, recommended by the manufacturer provided \nthe solution is stored in a refrigerator (2° to 8°).\nCefepime Injection contains Cefepime Hydrochloride \nequivalent to not less than 90,0 per cent and not more than\n115.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of cefepime, Cl9H24N6OsS;.\nUsual strengths. 250 mg; 500 mg; 1 g.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow powder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for injection) and with the following,requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefixime-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefixime Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefixime Oral Suspension\nCefixime Oral Suspension is a mixture consisting of Cefixime \nwith buffering agents and other excipients. It contains a \nsuitable flavouring agent. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of Water just \nbefore use.\nCefixime Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncefixime CI6H15N507S2.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of cefixime C 161-1 15N507Si.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°.\nUsual strengths. 100 mg per 5 ml; 200 mg per 5 ml; 400 nigper \n5 ml. \t\nJJ\nCEFIXIME ORAL SUSPENSION \nIP \nCEFIXIME TABLETS\nDetermine the weight per ml (2.4.29) of the oral suspension\nChromatographic system"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefixime","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefixime"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefixime\n;Ci6H15N5O7S2,3H2O \nMol.Wt.507.5\nCefixime is (67?,7i?)-7-[[(2Z)-2-(2-arriinothiazol“4-yl)- \n[(carboxymethoxy)imino] acetyl] amino] -3 -ethenyl-8-oxo-5- \nthia-1 -azabicyclo [4.2.0]oct-2-ene-2-carboxylic acidtrihydrate.\nT782\nS’2022\nCefixime contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not mOre \nthan 101.0 per cent of Ci6Hi5N5O7S2, calculated on \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white to light yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefoperazone-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefoperazone Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefoperazone Injection\nCefoperaZonc Sodium Injection\nCefoperazone Injection is a sterile material consisting of \n£ef0perazone Sodium with or without excipients. It is filled in \na sealed container.\nThe inj ection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nparenteral Preparations (Injections).\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of250 mg; 500 mg; 1 g and \n2 g of cefoperazone.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within,the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCefoperazone Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent\nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncefoperazone, C25H27N9O8S2.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\nThe contents of the sealed'container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefotaxime-sodium-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefotaxime Sodium Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefotaxime Sodium Injection\nCefotaxime Injection\nCefotaxime Sodium Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nCefotaxime Sodium with or without excipients. It is filled in \nsealed containers.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use. r\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated, under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).. .\nStorage^ The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation1 but, in ariy-case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCefotaxime Sodium Injection contains a quantity of Cefotaxime \nSodium equivalent to not less than 90.0 per cent and not more \nthan 115.0 per cent of the stated, amount of cefotaxime,\nCijHpNjOvSs.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 250 mg, T g and 2 g of \ncefotaxime. ■ . . ■ \n. \n. .. \n.\nDescription. An off-white to pale yellow, crystalline powder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Pdrenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefpirome-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefpirome Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefpirome Injection\nCefpirome Sulphate Injection\nCefpirome Injection is sterile material consisting of Cefpirome \nsulphate with or without auxiliary substances. It is filled in \nsealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents ofthe \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Water for Injections \nimmediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nclarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections),\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCefpirome Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of cefpirome, \nC^NAS;.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injections) and with the following requirement.\nUsual strengths. 250 mg; 500 mg; 1000 mg per vial.\nDescription. An off-white to pale yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefpirome-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefpirome Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefpirome Sulphate\nC22H22N6O5S2,H2SO4 \nMol. Wt 612.66\nCefpirome Sulphate is -[[(6R,7R)-7-[[(2Z)-(2-amin‘b- \n4-thiazolyI)(methoxyimino)acetyl] amino]-2-carboxy-8-oxo- \n5-thia-l -azabicyclo[4.2.0]oct-2-en-3-yl]methyl]-6,7-dihydro- \n5 77-cy c 1 opent a [ h] pyrindin iumsulphate.\nCefpirome Sulphate is a . sterile mixture of sterile cefpirome \nsulphate and sodium carbonate.\nCefpirome Sulphate contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of cefpirome sulphate, calculated\non the dried and sodium carbonate free basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial. .\nDescription. An off-white to pale yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefpodoxime-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefpodoxime Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefpodoxime Oral Suspension\nCefpodoxime Proxetil Oral Suspension\nCefpodoxime Oral Suspension is a mixture consisting of \nCefpodoxime Proxetil with buffering agents and other \nexcipients. It contains a suitable flavouring agent.'\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water Just \nbefore use.\nCefpodoxime Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncefpodoxime, Ci5H17N5O6S2.\nUsual strengths. 50 mg per 5 ml; 100 mg per 5 ml.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to. be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per pent of the stated amount of cefpodoxime\nCi5Hi7N5O6S2."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefpodoxime-proxetil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefpodoxime Proxetil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefpodoxime Proxetil\n%ch3\n' \n1 \n. Mol.Wt.557.6\nCefpodoxime Proxetil is l-(isopropoxycarbohyloxy)ethyl (6& \n77?)-7-[2-(2-amino-4-thiazolyl)^(Z)-2-(methoxyimlIt0)\nacetamido] -3-methoxymethy 1-3 -cephem-4-carboxylate.\nCefpodoxime Proxetil contains not less than 690 pg and not \nmore than 804 pg per mg of cefpodoxime, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefpodoxime-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefpodoxime Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefpodoxime Tablets\nDiphtheria, _ Tetanus, PortpgiS (Whole\nCO and Hepatitis A) Vaecihe \n(AasoTbed)"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ceftazidime-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ceftazidime for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ceftazidime for Injection\nCeftazidime for Injection is a sterile mixture of Sterile Ceftazidime \nand Sodium Carbonate or Arginine.\nCeftazidimp for Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not inore than 105.0 per cent o f ceftazidime, C22H22N6Q7S2, \non the dried and sodium carbonate or argininerfree basis, and \nnot less than 90.0 per cent and not more than 120.0 per cent of \nthe labeled amount of ceftazidime; C22H22N6O7S2. - '\nUsual strengths. 500 mg; 1 g. ?\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ceftazidime-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ceftazidime Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ceftazidime Injection\nCeftazidime Injection is a sterile material consisting of\nCeftazid.iJ.ileforInjection with or without auxiliary substances.\nIt is filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the\nsealed container in the requisite amount ofWater for Injections\nimrilediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCeftazidime Injection contains not less than 90.0per cent and\nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nceftazidime, CzzH22N607Sz,\n.Usual strengtbs: The equivalent of 250 mg; 500 mg; 1 g of\nceftazidime.\nDescriptiou. A white or almost white crystalline pOWder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ceftazidime","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ceftazidime"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ceftazidime\nC22H22N6O7S2,5H2O \n' Mol. Wt. 636.6\nCeftazidime is pentahydrate of .the inner salt of (7/?)-7-[(Z)- \n2-(2-aminothiazol-4-yl)-2-( 1 -carboxy-1 -methylethoxyimino)- \nacetamido]-3-(l-pyridinomethyl)73-cepham-4-carboxylate \npentahydrate,\nCeftazidime contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C22H22N6O7S2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\n1795\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCEFTAZIDIME\nDescription. A white to cream-coloured, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ceftiofur-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ceftiofur Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ceftiofur Sodium\nCi9H]6N5NaO7S3 \nMol. Wt. 545.5\nC19H16N5NaO7S3,H2O \nMol. Wt. 563.5\nC19H16N5NaO7S3,3H2O \n. Mol. Wt. 599.5\nCeftiofur Sodium is Sodium 7-[(Z)-2-(2-amino-l ,3-thiazol-4-yl). \n2-(methoxyimino)acetamido]-3-(2-furoylthiomethyl)- \n3-cephem-4-carboxylate.\nCeftiofur Sodium contains not less than 82.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of Ci9H 16N5NaO7S3j calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial. . \n. \n. .\nDescription. An off white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ceftizoxime-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ceftizoxime Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ceftizoxime Sodium\nCysHigNsNaO,8j-0 5-0. \nMol. Wt405.4\nCeftizoxime Sodium is Sodiuim 7:{(Z)-2-(2-Amino’1,3-thiazol” \n4-yl)-2-(methoxyimino)acetamido]-3-cephem-4-carboxylate,:\nCéftizoximé Sodium cofitains 1iét‘lesé than 850 pg and not \nmore ‘than.995 wg of'ceftizoxime CaHsNOS: \n; pér mg, \ncalculated: on the anhydrous basis: \nPLN\nCategory, Antibacterial.\nDescription. Awhite to pale yellow. cnn pov."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ceftriaxone-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ceftriaxone Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ceftriaxone Injection\nCeftriaxone Injection is a sterile material consisting of\nCeftriaxone Sodium with or without excipients. Itis filled in a\nsealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the\nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for\nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements\nfor Clarity of solution and·Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 250 mg; 500 mg; 1 g and\n2 g ofceftriaxone.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by themanufacturer.\nCEFfRIAXONE INJECTION\nCeftriaxone Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nceftriaxone, ClsHlsNs07S3'\nDescription. A white or almost white powder;\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ceftriaxone-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ceftriaxone Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ceftriaxone Sodium\nC18H16N8Na2O7S3,31/2H2O \nMol. Wt. 662.0\nCeftriaxone sodium is disodium (6/?,77?)-7-[[(Z)- \n(2-aminothiazol-4-yl)(methoxyimino)acetyl]amino]- \n3-| | (2-methyl-6-oxido-5-oxo-2,5-dihydro-l ,2,4-U‘iazin-3-yl)\ns ulphanyl] methyl]-8-oxo-5-thia-l -azabi cyclo [4.2.0]oct- \n2-ene-2-carboxylatehemiheptahydrate.\nCeftriaxone sodium contains not less than 96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of Cl8H16N8Na2O7S3, calculated \non the anhydrous basis. \n- > !\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or yellowish, crystalline powder, slightly \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefuroxime-axetil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefuroxime Axetil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefuroxime Axetil\nQoH,2N4010S \nMol. Wt.510.5\nCefuroxime Axetil is a mixture of the 2 diastereoisomers of \n^^)-l-(acetyloxy)ethyl (6A’,7P)-3-[(carbamoyk)xy)methyl]-\n7-[[(Z)-2-(furan-2-yl)-2-(methoxyimino)acetyl] amino]-8-oxo- \n5-thia-l-azabicyclo[4.2.0]oct-2-ene-2-carboxylate.\nCefuroxime Axetil contains not less than 79.8 per cent and not \nmore than 84.8 per cent of cefuroxime, C16HI6N4O8S, calculated \non the anhydrous and acetone-free basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefuroxime-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefuroxime Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefuroxime Injection\nCefuroxime SodiumInjection\nCefuroxime Injection is a sterile material consisting of\nCefuroxime Sodium: with or without auxiliary substances. It\nis filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of a\nsealed containerin the requisite amount ofWaterfor Injections\nimmediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCefuroximeInjection contains a quantity ofCefuroximeSodium\nequivalent to not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\n120.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofcefuroxime, Cl6HuiN40gS.\nUsnal strengths: The equivalent of 250 mg, 750 mg and 1.5\ng of cefuroxime.\nDescription. A white or faintly yellow powder.\nThe contents ofthe sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cefuroxime-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cefuroxime Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cefuroxime Sodium\nCefuroxime Sodium is sodium (7A)-3-carbamoyloxymelhyl-7-| (Z)- \nfuran-2-yl-2-methoxyiminoacetamido]-3-cephem-4-carboxylate.\nCefuroxime Sodium contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of cefuroxime sodium, \nC16H15N4NaO8S, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or faintly yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cellulose-acetate-phthalate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cellulose Acetate Phthalate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cellulose Acetate Phthalate\nCellacephate; Cellacefate\nCellulose Acetate Phthalate is a cellulose, some of the . . \nhydroxyl groups of which are esterified by acetyl groups \nand others by hydrogen phthaloyl groups.\nCellulose Acetate Phthalate contains not less than 17.0 per \ncent and not more than 26.0 per cent of acetyl groups, C2H3O \nand not less than 30.0 per cent and not.more than 40.0 per cent \nof hydrogen phthaloyl groups, C8H5O3 both calculated on the \nanhydrous, acid free basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (for enteric coating of tablets).\nDescription. A white, free-flowing powder or colourless flakes; \nodourless or with a faint odour of acetic acid; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/central-drugs-laboratory","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Central Drugs Laboratory"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Central Drugs Laboratory \n3, Kyd Street, Kolkata-700 016.\n(Dr Shailesh Ayyangar)\nMember \t\nThe President \n(ex-officio) \nIndian Pharmaceutical Alliance (IPA) \nMumbai.\n(Dr. Hariharan)\n(Professor N. K. Ganguly)\nMember \t\nFrom Regulatory Bodies \n(ex-officio) \nCentral Drugs Standard Control Organisation \nDirectorate General of Health Services \nFDA Bhawan, Kotla Road, \nNew Delhi-110 002.\nMember \t\nThe Director General (DGHS) \n(ex-officio) \nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare \nNirman Bhawan \nNew Delhi-110 011.\n(Dr. Rao V. S. V. Vadlamudi)\nMember \t\nThe President, \n(ex-officio) \nPharmacy Council of India \nCombined Councils' Building \nKotla Road, Aiwan-E-Ghalib Marg \nNew Delhi-110 002.\n(Dr. Jagdish Prasad)\nMember \t\nThe Director \n(ex-officio) \nNational Institute of Pharmaceutical Education \nand Research (NIPER) \nSector 67, SAS Nagar \nMohali-160 062.\nMember \t\nThe Additional Secretary & D.Q (CGHS) \n(ex-officio) \nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare \nNirman Bhawan \nNewDelhi-110011.\n(Professor B. Suresh)\nMember \t\nDr. Kiran Mazumdar Shaw \nC&MD, \nBiocon Ltd. \n20th KM, Hosur Road \nElectronics City \nBangalore- 560 100\n(Dr. R. K. Vats)\n(Dr. USN Murty, Officiating Director)\nMember \t\nThe Additional Secretary & Finance Advisor \n(ex-officio) \nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare \nNirman Bhawan \nNew Delhi -110 011.\nMember \t\nCommissioners in-charge of Drug Control \n(ex-officio) \t\nAdministration. Andhra Pradesh\nMember- \t\nThe Secretary-cum-Scientific Director \nSecretary \nIndian Pharmacopoeia Commission \n(ex-officio) \nSector-23, Rajnagar \nGhaziabad-201 002.\nMember \t\nCommissioners in-charge of Drug Control \n(ex-officio) \t\nAdministration, Gujarat\n(Ms. Vijaya Srivastava)\nMember \t\nCommissioners in-charge of Drug Control \n(ex-officio) \t\nAdministration, Himachal Pradesh\nMember \t\nThe Joint Secretary (Regulation) \n(ex-officio) \nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare \nNirman Bhawan \nNew Delhi-110 011.\n(Dr. G. N. Singh)\nMember \t\nCommissioners in-charge of Drug Control \n(ex-officio) \t\nAdministration, Sikkim\nThe Scientific Body\nMember \t\nCommissioners in-charge of Drug Control \n(ex-officio) \t\nAdministration, Uttar Pradesh\n(Mr. Sudhir Kumar) \nJoint Secretary\nThe composition of the Scientific Body is given below:\nDr. (Prof.) N. K. Ganguly \nFormer Director General, ICMR \nC-191, 9th Floor\nMember \t\nThe Joint Secretary \n(ex-officio) \nDepartment of Pharmaceuticals \nMinistry of Chemicals and Fertilizers \n_Shastri Bhawan\nChairman\nDr. C. Adithan \nDirector-Professor, Dept. of Pharmacology \nJawaharlal Institute of Postgracigate Medical \nEducation and Research \nPondicherry-605 006.\nMember \n(ex-officio)\n, __\n...-... \n, \n4,9\nView Apartment \nof No. 199, Sector-35\nNew Delhi-110 011.\n7\nida-201 301.\n-!'- --.--i._.,;_,__- ,,\nr•-• ,:)-`. \t\n......, \n„.._,\n$hri Sudhanshu Pant)\n--s,\nMember \t\nDr. Nitin Bhatia \nSr. General Manager (Tech. & Regulatory) \nIntas Animal Health \nIntas Pharmaceuticals Ltd. \n44th Floor, Premier House \nOpposite Gurudwara, S. G. Road, \nBodakdev, \nAhmedabad (Gujarat).\nMember \t\nProl(Dr.)Vmod Kumar Dixit \nFormer Dean, Faculty of Pharmacy & \nRetired Professor of Pharmacognosy \nDr. Hari Singh Gour University \n(formerly University of Sagar.) \nSagar, \nMadhya Pradesh.\nMember \t\nDr. Nitya Gogtay \nAssociate Professor in Clinical Pharmacology \nDepartment of Clinical Pharmacology \nKEM and Seth G S. Medical College \nMumbai.\nMember \t\nDr. Shahid Jameel \nCEO, Wellcome Trust, DBT India Alliance\n1110 DLF Tower B \nJasola \nNew Delhi-110 025.\nMember \t\nDr. Praveen Khullar \nSr. Director Development Centre Goa & \nRegional Co-ordinator APJ Generics BU \nSanofi Synthelabo (India) Pvt. Limited \nL-121, Phase III A, GIDC \nVerna Industrial Estate \nVerna, Goa-403722.\nMember \t\nDr. Arun Kumar Mishra \nExecutive Vice President \nGSK Consumer Health Care Ltd. \nDLF Phase-5, Golf course Road \nGurugram.\nMember \t\nDr. Amulya K. Panda \nScientist (Biotechnology) \nProduct Development Cell \nNational Institute of Immunology \nAruna Asaf Ali Marg \nNew Delhi —110 067.\nMember \t\nShri A. K. Pradhan \nDeputy Drugs Controller (I) \nCDS"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cephalexin-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cephalexin Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cephalexin Oral Suspension\nCephalexin Dry Syrup; Cephalexin Mixture\nCephalexin Oral Suspension is a mixture of Cephalexin with \nbuffering agents and other excipients. It contains a suitable'; \nflavouring agent: It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water just \nbefore use.\nCephalexin Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 120.6 per cent of the stated amount of \nCiJLWhS. ■ ■■ \n■ < C-' / • ■ - —A)\nCEPHALEXIN CAPSULES\nIP 2022\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of cephalexin, Ci6H17N3O4S\nUsual strengths. 125 mg; 250 mg of anhydrous cephalexin per \n5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cephalexin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cephalexin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cephalexin\nCOOH\nC16Hi7N3O4S,H2O \nMol. Wt. 365.4\nCephalexin is. (77?)-3-methyl-7-(a-D-phenylglycylamino)- \n3-cephem-4-carboxylic acid monohydrate.\n1813\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCephalexin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Ci6H17N3O4S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cephaloridine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cephaloridine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cephaloridine Injection\nCephaloridine Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nCephaloridine with or without auxiliary substances: It is filled \nin a sealed container;\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Water for Injections \nimmediately before use.\n^he constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections). .\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer..\nCephaloridine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nanhydrous cephaloridine, Ci^HpNaC^Sj.\nUsual strengths. 250 mg; 500 mg; 1 g.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cephaloridine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cephaloridine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cephaloridine\nCji)HI'jNjO/.S? : \nMol. Wt, 415.5\nCephaloridine is (77?)-3-(l-pyridiniomethyl)-7-[(2-thienyl)- \nacetamido]-3-cephem-4-carboxyIate (a-form or 5 -form);\nCephaloridine contains riot less than 96.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C19H17N3O4S2, calculated on the \nanhydrous .basis. ,\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nCEPHALEXIN TABLETS\nIP 2022\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cetirizine-dihydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cetirizine Dihydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cetirizine Dihydrochloride\nQ^CIHCUHCl \nMol.Wt. 461.8\n1820\nCetirizine Hydrochloride is [2-[4-[(4-chlorophetiv(1 \nphenylmethyl]-l-piperazinyl]ethoxy]acetic acid dihydrg \nchloride.\nCetirizine Hydrochloride coritains not less than 99.0 percen* \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of QALjClNjO.^Htj \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cetirizine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cetirizine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cetirizine Hydrochloride\nCetirizineDihydrochloride\nCI\nMol. Wt. 461.8\n1037\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCetirizine Hydrochloride is [2-[4-[(4-chlorophenyl)\nphenylmethyl]-l-piperazinyl]ethoxy]acetic acid\ndihydrochloride.\nCetirizine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent\nand not more than 100.5 per cent of C21H25CIN203,2HC1,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cetirizine-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cetirizine Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cetirizine Syrup\nCetirizine Oral Liquid\nCetirizine Syrup contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of cetirizine \nhydrochloride C21H25CIN2O3,2HC1,\nUsual strength. 5 mg per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cetostearyl-alcohol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cetostearyl Alcohol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cetostearyl Alcohol\nCetostearyl Alcohol is a mixture of solid aliphatic alcohols\n'onsisting chiefly of stearyl and cetyl alcohols.\nCetostearyl Alcohol contains not less than 40.0 per cent of \nstearyl alcohol and sum of stearyl alcohol and cetyl alcohol is\nnot less than 90.0 per cent.\nCategory* Pharmaceutical aid (ointment base).\nDescription. A white or pale yellow, wax like mass, plates,\nHakes or granules."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cetrimide-emulsifying-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cetrimide Emulsifying Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cetrimide Emulsifying Ointment\nCetrimide Emulsifying Ointment is an ointment containing \ncetrimide in a suitable base.\nCetrimide Emulsifying Ointment contains not less than \n2.5 per cent and not more than 3.3 per cent w/w of the stated \namount of cetrimide, C I7H38BrN."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cetrimide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cetrimide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cetrimide\nCetrimide consists chiefly oftetradecyltrimethylammonium\nbromide together with smaller amounts of dodecyl- and\nhexadecyltrimethylammoniumbromides.\nCetrimide contains not less than 96.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent of alkyltrimethylammonium bromides,\ncalculated as C17H38BrN (336.4) on the dried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid; bactericide.\nDescription. A white or creamy-white, voluminous, free-\nflowing powder; odour, faint and characteristic.\n."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cetyl-alcohol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cetyl Alcohol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cetyl Alcohol\nPalmityl Alcohol; 77-Hexadecyl Alcohol; 1-Hexadecanol\nCetyl Alcohol is a mixture of solid alcohols consisting mainly \nofl -hexadecanol, C16H34O. \n.\nCetyl Alcohol contains not less than 95.0 percent of C16H34O.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (stiffening, emulsifying and \ntablet coating agent).\nDescription. Awhite, unctuous mass, powder, flakes or granules."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cetyl-pa-imitate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cetyl Pa Imitate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cetyl Pa Imitate\nIn the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram obtain \nwith the test solution corresponds to the principal peak in thA \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\ncH3(cH2)14coocH2(cH2)14cH3\nC321-16102 \t\nMol. Wt. 480.9\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Not more than 0.2 per cent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cetyl-palmitate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cetyl Palmitate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cetyl Palmitate\n,C32H64O2 \nMol. Wt. 480.9\nCetyl Palmitate is hexadecyl palmitate.\nCetyl Palmitate is a mixture of Ct4 to Ci8 esters of lauric acid \n(dodecanoic), myristic acid (tetradecanoic), palmitic acid \n(hexadecanoic) and stearic acid (octadecanoic) (Cetyl esters \nwax). \n.: \n.\nCetyl Palmitate contains not less than 10.0 per cent and not \nmore than 20.0 per cent for cetyl palmitate 15; not less.than \n60.0 per cent and more than 70.0 per cent for cetyl palmitate 65 \nand not less than 90.0 per cent for Cetyl palmitate 95.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, waxy plates, flakes or \npowder. ■ \n, ■"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chaulai","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chaulai"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chaulai\nRamdana; Amaranthus tricolor\nChaulai consists of dried seeds of Amaranthus tricolor L. \n(Fam. Amaranthaceae). Chaulai seed contain not less than 2.0 \nper cent of squalene, calculated on the dried, basis..,. ■■■ _, . A\nCategory. Antioxidant, astringent, antipyretic and demulcent\nDescription.,Seeds are small, yellowish to brown colour,-, .;?"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chiorocresoi","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chiorocresoi"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chiorocresoi\n6.\nY\"CH3\nCI\nMol. Wt. 142.6\nChlorocresol is 4-chloro-3-methylphenol.\nChlorocresol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent of C7H7CIO.\nCategory. Antiseptic; pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial\npreservative).\nDescription. Colourless or almost colourless crystals or a\nwhite, crystalline powder; odour, characteristic and not tarry;\nvolatile in steam."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorambucil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorambucil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorambucil\nCOOH\nCi4Hi9Cl2NO2 \nMol. Wt. 304.2\nChlorambucil is 4-[4-bis(2-chloroethyl)amino] phenylbutyric \nacid.\nChlorambucil contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore'than 101.0 per cent of C]4Hi9Cl2NO2, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. Awhite, crystalline powder.\nCA UTION — Chlorambucil must be handled with care; \ncontact with the skin and inhalation of airborne particles \nmust be avoided."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloramphenicol-eye-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Eye Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Eye Ointment\nChloramphenicol Eye Ointment contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated \namount of chloramphenicol, CnHuCy^Os.\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloramphenicol-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Injection\nChloramphenicol Injection is a sterile suspension of\nChloramphenicol in Water for Injections containing suitable\nsuspending and stabilising agents.\nChloramphenicol Injection contains not less than 95.0 percent\nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nchloramphenicol, CjjHI2CIzNzOs.\nUsual strengths. 4.5 gin 30 ml; 5.0 g in 50 ml; 11.25 gin 75 ml;\n15 gin 100 ml;20 g in 100 mI."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloramphenicol-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Oral Suspension\nChloramphenicol Palmitate Oral Suspension; \nChloramphenicol Palmitate Mixture\nChloramphenicol Oral Suspension is a suspension of \nChloramphenicol Palmitate in a suitable flavoured vehicle.\nChloramphenicol Oral Suspension contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not.more than 115.0 per cent of the stated \namount of chloramphenicol, C11H12CI2N2O5.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 125 mg of chloramphenicol \nper 5 ml. (175 mg of chloramphenicol palmitate is approximately\nequivalentto lOOmg of chloramphenicol)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloramphenicol-palmitate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Palmitate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Palmitate\nMol. Wt. 561.6\nChloramphenicol Palmitate is (2R,3R)-2-(2,2-\ndichloroacetamido)-3-hydroxy-3-(4-nitrophenyl)propyl\nhexadecanoate.\nChloramphenicol Palmitate contains not less than 97.0 per\ncent and not more than 103.0 per cent of C27~~ChN206'\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDose. For an adult, the equivalent of 1.5 to 3 g of\nchloramphenicol daily, in divided doses; for a child, the\nequivalent of 25 to 50 mg of chloramphenicol per kg ofbody\nweight daily, in divided doses. (175 mg of chloramphenicol\npalmitate is approximately equivalent to 100 mg of\nchloramphenicol).\nDescription: A fme, white or almost white, unctuous powder;\nodour, faint."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloramphenicol-sodium-succinate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Sodium Succinate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloramphenicol Sodium Succinate\nCisHlSChN2NaOg\nMol. Wt. 445.2\nChloramphenicol Sodium Succinate is a mixture of variable\nproportions of sodium (2R,3R)-2-(2,2-dichloroacetamido)-\n3-hydroxy-3-(4-nitrophenyl)propyl succinate (3-isomer)\nand of sodium (1R,2R)-2-(2,2-dichloroacetamido)-3-\nhydroxy-1-(4-nitrophenyI)propyl succinate (I-isomer).'\nChloramphenicol Sodium Succinate contains not less than\n98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per cent of\nClsHlSChN2NaOg, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDose. By intravenous injection, the equivalent of 3 to 4 g of\nchloramphenicol daily, in divided doses. (140 mg of\nchloramphenicol sodium succinate is approximately equivalent\nto 100 mg of chloramphenicol)..\nDescription. A white oryellowish-white powder; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloramphenicol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloramphenicol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloramphenicol\nChH12C12N2O5 \nMol. Wt. 323.1\nChloramphenicol is 2,2-dichloro-W[(17?,2/?)-2-hydroxy-\nl-hydroxymethyl-2-(4-nitrophenyl)ethyl]acetamide. It is \nproduced by the growth of certain strains of Streptomyces \nvenezuelae in a suitable medium, but is normally prepared \nby synthesis.\nChloramphenicol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 percent of CuHiiClaNjOs, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white to greyish-white or yellowish-white, fine \ncrystalline powder or fine-crystals, needles or elongated plates."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorcyclizine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorcyclizine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorcyclizine Hydrochloride\nC]8H2IC1N2JIC1 ■' j \nMol. Wt. 337.3\nChlorcyclizine Hydrochloride is l\"(4-chlorobenzhydryl)- \n4-methylpiperazine hydrochloride.\nChlorcyclizine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nC18H21CIN2iHCI, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic. .. \n..\nDescription. Awhite crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlordiazepoxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlordiazepoxide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlordiazepoxide\nC;6H:4C1N3O \nMol. Wt. 299.8\nChlordiazepoxide is 7-chloro-2-methylamino-5-phenyl- \n37f-l,4-benzodiazepine4-oxide. .\nChlordiazepoxide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and.not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of Ci6H14ClN3O, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory.Anxiolytic. .\nDescription. Ah almost white to light yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorhexidine-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Acetate\nMol. Wt. 625.6\nChlorhexidine Acetate is 1,I'-(hexane-1,6-diyl)bis[5-(4-\nchlorophenyl)biguanide] diacetate.\nCWorhexidine Acetate contains not less than 98.0 per cent\nand not more than 101.0 per cent of chlorhexidine diacetate,\nCzzH30ClzNIO,2CzRt02' calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiseptic.\nDescription.\nA white or almost white, microcrystalline\npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorhexidine-diacetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Diacetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Diacetate\nCnHsoCbN^,20^02 \nMol. Wt. 625.6\nChlorhexidine Acetate is l,l’-(hexane-l,6-diyl)bis[5-(4- \nclilorophenyl)biguanide] diacetate.\nChlorhexidine Acetate contains, not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of chlorhexidine diacetate,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiseptic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, microcrystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorhexidine-dihydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Dihydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Dihydrochloride\n, 2 HC1\nCaH30CI2N10,2HCl \nMol. Wt. 578.4\nchlorophenyl)biguanide] dihydrochloride. .\nChlorhexidine Hydrochloride is 1,1 '-(hexane- l,6-diyl)bis[5-(4r\nChlorhexidine Hydrochloride contains not less than\n8.0 per cent and not more than 101.0 per cent of chlorhexidine \n^hydrochloride, C^H3oCI2N10,2HC1 calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiseptic. \n.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorhexidine-gluconate-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Gluconate Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Gluconate Solution\nChlorhexidine Gluconate Solution is an aqueous solution of\n1,1 ’-hexamethylenebis [5-(4-chlorophenyl)biguanide] \ndi gluconate.\nChlorhexidine Gluconate Solution contains not less than\n19.0 per cent w/v and not more than 21.0 per cent w/v of \nC22H30Cl2N10,2C6H12O7.\nCategory. Antiseptic.\nDescription. An almost colourless or pale yellowish, clear or \nslightly opalescent liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorhexidine-mouthwash","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Mouthwash"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorhexidine Mouthwash\nChlorhexidine Mouthwash contains Chlorhexidine Gluconate \nSolution in a suitable flavoured and coloured vehicle.\nChlorhexidine Mouthwash contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nchlorhexidine gluconate,C23H3oC12Nio2C6H1207.\nUsual strengths. 0.2 per cent w/v; 0.4 per cent w/v; 0,5 per \ncent w/v; 4 per cent w/v; 20 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloride\nChloride:\n(NaCl) . :\n(W6)„\n, (NaCl) . . :\nOW\n0.11\n. \n5\n0.45\n2.5\n0.18\n5\n0.45\n10\n. 0.225 ;\n5\n. 0-9\n2.5 .\n0.33\n5\n0.9\n5\n0.45\n5\n0.9\n10\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured \nsolution.\nA. To 1 ml add 0.05 ml ofpotassium cupri-tartrate solution', \nthe solution remains blue and clear. Heat to boiling, a copious \nred precipitate is formed.\nB. The solution prepared in the Assay is laevo-rotatory.\nC. It gives reaction (B) of sodium salts and reaction (A) of \nchlorides (2.3.1).\n3601\nwww.webofpharma.com\nSODIUM CHLORIDE AND FRUCTOSE INJECTION\n5-IIydroxymethylfurfural and related substances Dilute à \nvolume containing 1.0 g of Fructose to 500.0 ml with -water \nand measure the absorbance of the resulting; solution? at the \nmaximumat about 284 nm (2.4.7); absorbance at about 284 nm, \nnot more than 0.50.   \n,\nHeavy1 metals (2.3.13): Evaporate a volume containing 4:0 g of \nFructose to 10 ml and add 2 ml of dilute acetic acid and \nsufficient water to produce 25 ml. The solution complies with \nthe limit test for heavy metals, Method A (5 ppm).\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 0.5 Endotoxin Unit \nper ml. \n.. \n. ,\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections). \n> : ; ■\nAssay. For sodium chloride — Titrate a measured volume \ncontaining about 0.1 g of Sodium Chloride with 0.1 M'silver \nnitrate using potassium chromate solution as indicator.:\n1 ml of Ò.1 Msvfvrv wrate is eqiiivalent to 0.005844 g ofNaCI.\nFor fructose — To a measured volume containing about 5.0 g \nof Fructose, add 0.2 ml of 6 M.ammonid and sufficient water \nto pròdùce 100.0 ml. Mix well, allow to stand for 30 minutes\nand measure the optical rotation in a 2-dm tube (2.4.22). Thè \nobserved rotation in degrees multiplied by 0,5427 represents \nthe weight, in g, of fructose, C6F[12O6, in the volume taken for \nassay.................. - ■ • \n..............\nStorage. Store in single dose containers. On keeping, small \nsolid particles may separate from glass containers., : \n,\nLabelling.-The label states (1 ) the strength as the percentages \nw/v of Sodium Chloride and Fructose'; (2) when the preparation \nis intended for intravenous infusion, the approximate \nconcentrations, in millimoles per litre, of the sodium and \nchloride ions and the number of grams per litre of fructose; \n(3) that a solution containing visible particles should not be \nused.\nCompound Sodium Chloride and \nDextrose Injection\nCompound Sodium Chloride and Dextrose intravenous\nInfusion .\nCompound Sodium Chloride and Dextrose Injection is a sterile \nsolution containing 0.86 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride, \n0.03 per cent w/v of PotassiumChloride, 0.033 per cent w/v of \nCalcium Chloride and 5 per cent w/v of Dextrose in Water for \nInjections. \ni 7\nCompound Sodium Chloride and Dextrose Inj ection contains \nnot less than 0.82 per cent and not more than 0.90 per cent \nw/v of sodium chloride, NaCl, not less than 0.0285 per cent\n3602 \nmIMmiìIìIIìI\nIP 2022 11\nand not more than 0.0315 per cent w/v df potassium chloride \nI\nKC1, not less than 0.030 per cent and not more than 0:036 per \nI\ncent w/v of calcium chloride, CaCb^H^O, and notddss<than t \n0.523 per cent and not more than 0.580 per cent w/vof chloride \nI\nCl, and not less than 4.7 5 per cent and not more than 5/25 per f \ncent w/v of dextrose, C6H12O6. It contains no antimicrobial | \nagent. . \n........|\nCategory. Fluid and electrolyte replenisher. \nI\n' * ■ \n.v- A? \nH\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured |\nsolution. \n. \n|"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorinated-lime","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorinated Lime"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorinated Lime\nCa(ClO)2 \nMol. Wt. 143.0\nChlorinated Lime contains not less than 30 per cent w/w of \navailable chlorine.\nCategory. Disinfectant.\nDescription. An off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorobutanol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorobutanol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorobutanol\nOH\nh3c^^cci3 .1/2h2o\nch3\nC4H7C13O, % H2O \nMol.Wt. 186.5\nChlorbutol is l,l,l-trichloro-2-methylpropan-2-ol \nhemihydrate.\nChlorbutol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C4H7CI3O, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial preservative), \nanalgesic; local anaesthetic.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder; \nsublimes readily."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorocresol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorocresol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorocresol\nGILC1O \nMol. Wt. 142.6\nChlorocresol is 4-chloro-3-methylphenol.\nChlorocresol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C7H7C1O.\nCategory. Antiseptic; pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial \npreservative). '\nDescription. Colourless or almost colourless crystals or a \nwhite, crystalline powder; volatile in steam.\n1845\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroform","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroform"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroform\nci\nC| <\nCl\nCHCL \nMol. Wt. 119.4\nChloroform is trichloromethane to which either 1.0 per cent to \n2.0 per cent v/v of ethanol or 50 mg per litre.of amylene has \nbeen added.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (solvent and antimicrobial \npreservative).\nDescription. A colourless, volatile liquid.\nNOTE — Care should be taken not to vaporise chloroform in \nthe presence of aflame because of the pro,duction of harmful \ngases."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroguanide-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroguanide Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroguanide Hydrochloride\nCjlHj6CINs, HCl\nMol. Wt. 290.2\nProguanil Hydrochloride is 1-(4-chlorophenyl)-5-\nisopropylbiguanide hydrochloride.\nProguanil Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent\nand notmore than 101.0percent ofCjjH16ClNs, HCl, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory.Antimalarial.\nDose. Suppressive, 100 to 300 mg daily.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroquine-phosphate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroquine Phosphate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroquine Phosphate Injection\nChloroquine Phosphate Injection is a sterile solution of \nChloroquine Phosphate in Water for Injections.\nChloroquine Phosphate Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of chloroquine, C^H^ClNa;\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 40 mg of chloroquine per\nml. (250 mg of chloroquine phosphate is approximately \nequivalentto 155 mg of chloroquine).\nDescription. A clear, colourless or almost colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroquine-phosphate-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroquine Phosphate Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroquine Phosphate Suspension\nChloroquine Phosphate Suspension is a suspension of \nChloroquine Phosphate in a suitable flavoured vehicle.\nChloroquine Phosphate Suspension contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of chloroquine, CjSH26C1N3.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 50 mg of chloroquine in \n5 ml. (80 mg of chloroquine phosphate is approximately \nequivalent to 5.0 mg of chloroquine)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroquine-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroquine Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroquine Phosphate\nG18H26CIN3,2H3PO4 \nMol. Wt. 515.9\nChloroquine Phosphate is (2?S)~7-chIoro-4-(4-diethylamino-\nl-methylbutylamino)quinoline diphosphate.\nChloroquine Phosphate contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C1SH26C1N3,2H3PO4> \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antimalarial; antiamoebic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder. It\nslowly gets discoloured on exposure to light. It may exist in \ntwo polymorphic forms differing in their behaviour, one of \nwhich melts at about 195° and the other at about 218°."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroquine-sulphate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroquine Sulphate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroquine Sulphate Injection\nChloroquine Sulphate Injection is a sterile solution of \nChloroquine Sulphate in Water for Inj ections.\nChloroquine Sulphate Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of chloroquine, ClgH26ClN3.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 40 mg of chloroquine per \nml. (200 mg of chloroquine sulphate is approximately \nequivalent to 147 mg of chloroquine).\nDescription. A clear, colourless or almost colourless solution. -"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroquine-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroquine Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroquine Sulphate\nC!8H26C1N3,H2SO4,H2O \n, Mol. Wt. 435.9\nChloroquine Sulphate is (7?S)-4-(7-chloro-4- quinolyl­\namino) pentyldiethylamine sulphate monohydrate.\nChloroquine Sulphate contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of CigH^ClNa^SO* \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis. '\nCategory^ Antimalarial; antiamdebic.;\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroquine-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroquine Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroquine Syrup\nChloroquine Syrup is a solution of Chloroquine Phosphate or \nChloroquine Sulphate in a suitable flavoured vehicle.\nChloroquine Syrup, contains Chloroquine Phosphate or \nChloroquine Sulphate .equivalent.to. not less than .95,0 pet \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nchloroquine, C1SH26C1N3.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 50 mg of chloroquine in \n5 ml. (80 mg of Chloroquine Phosphate or 67 mg of Chloroquine \nSulphate is approximately equivalent to 50 mg of chloroquine)"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorothiazide-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorothiazide Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorothiazide Oral Suspension\nChlorothiazide Oral Suspension is a dry mixture of \nChlorothiazide with buffering agent and other excipients. It \ncontains a suitable flavouring agent. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the content of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water just \nbefore use.\nChlorothiazide Oral Suspension, contains not less than 90.0 \nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount \nof chlorothiazide, C7H6C1N3O4S2.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of chlorothiazide, \nC7H6C1N3O4S2.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per ml.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture, at a temperature not \nexceeding 30°. \n■.,\nThe constituted suspension complies with the tests stated \nunder Oral Liquids and with the following tests."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorothiazide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorothiazide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorothiazide\nC7H6C1N3O4S2 \nMol. Wt. 295.7\nChlorothiazide is 6-chloro-2H-1,2,4-benzothiadiazine- \n7-sulphonamide 1,1-dioxide\nChlorothiazide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C7H6C1N3O4S2, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Diuretic; antihypertensive.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroxylenol-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroxylenol Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroxylenol Solution\nChloroxylenol solution is a solution, of Chloroxyleno[ \nsolubilised in a saponaceous base containing Ethanol (95 per \ncent) and essential oils. Ethanol (95 per cent) may be replaced \nby Industrial Methylated Spirit in making Chloroxylenol \nSolution.\nChloroxylenol Solution contains not less than 4.75 per cent \nand not more than 5.25 per cent of C8H9C1O.\nUsual strength. 5 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chloroxylenol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chloroxylenol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chloroxylenol\nOH\nC8H9C1O \n. Mol. Wt. 156.6\nChloroxylenol is 4-chloro-3,5-dimethylphenol.\nChloroxylenbl contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of CSH9C1O.\nCategory. Antiseptic; disinfectant.\n1855\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCHLOROXYLENOL\nDescription. A white or creamy-white crystals or crystalline \npowder. It is volatile in steam."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorpheniramine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorpheniramine Injection","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorpheniramine Injection\nChlorpheniramine Maleate Injection\nChlorpheniramine Injection is a sterile solution of \nChlorpheniramine Maleate in Water for Injections free from \ndissolved air and containing suitable buffering and stabilising \nagents.\nChlorpheniramine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and notmore than 110.0 percent ofthe statedampunt of \nchlorpheniramine maleate,^ pCR^CJCC^.\nUsual strength. 10 mg in 1 ml.\nDescription. A colourless solution.\n1857\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCHLORPHENIRAMINE INJECTION"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorpheniramine-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorpheniramine Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorpheniramine Maleate\nCOOH\nCOOH\nMol. Wt. 390.9\nChlorpheniramine Maleate is (RR)-3-(4-chlorophenyl)- \n3-(pyrid-2-yl)propyldimethylamine hydrogen maleate.\nChlorpheniramine Maleate contains not less than 98.0 per cent\nand not more than 101.0 per cent of CigH^CnS^C^CO^ \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic.\nDescription. Awhite, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorpromazine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorpromazine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorpromazine Hydrochloride\nChlorpromazine Hydrochloride is 2-chloro-10-(3-dimethyl- \naminopropyl)phenothiazine hydrochloride.\nChlorpromazine Hydrochloride contains not less than \n99.0 per cent and not more than 101.0 per cent of \nC]7H19C1N2S,HC1, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nDescription. A white or creamy-white, crystalline powder. It \ndecomposes on exposure to air and light becoming yellow, \npink and finally violet."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorpromazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorpromazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorpromazine Injection\nChlorpromazine Hydrochloride Inj ection\nChlorpromazine Injection is a sterile solution of Chlorpromazine \nhydrochloride in Water for Injections free from air and \ncontaining buffering and stabilizing agents.\nChlorpromazine Hydrochloride contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of chlorpromazine hydrochloride, Ci7H|9ClN2S,HCl.\nNOTE — Protect the solutions from light throughout the\ntests.\nUsual strength. 25 mg per ml.\nDescription. A colourless or almost colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorpropamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorpropamide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorpropamide\ncigHbC1N2O3S\nMoLWt. 276.7\nChlorpropamide is l-(4-chlorobenzenesulphonyl)-3- \npropylurea.\nChlorpropamide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of Ci0H13C1N2O3S, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Hypoglycaemic.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chlorthalidone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chlorthalidone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chlorthalidone\nO\nNH O O\nq|V nh2\nHO\nCmH||C1N2O4S \nMol. Wt. 338.8\nChlorthalidone is (RS)-2-chloro-5-(l-hydroxy-3-oxoisoindolin-\nl-yl)benzenesulphonamide.\nChlorthalidone contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C14Hi ^INaC^S, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDescription. Awhite to yellowish-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cholecalciferol-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cholecalciferol Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cholecalciferol Injection\nColecalciferol Injection"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cholecalciferol-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cholecalciferol Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cholecalciferol Tablets\n100,000 IU per g.\nCategory. Vitamin D (antirachitic).\nColecalciferol Tablets\nDose. Orally, in prevention of rickets, not more than 20 1.1.g \n(800 Units) daily, allowance being made for vitamin D obtained \nfrom other sources; in the treatment of rickets and osteomalacia,\nCholecalciferol Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 125.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncholecalciferol, C271-1440.\n125 lig to 1.25 mg (5,000 to 50,000 Units) daily; in the treatment \nof hypocalcaemia ofhypoparathyroidism, 1.25 to 5 mg (50,000 \nto 200,000 Units) daily. By intramuscular injection, 5 to 10 mg.\nChromatographic system\nUsual strengths. 0.25 mg; 1 mg.\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nporous silica or ceramic microparticles (3 to 10 pm) (Such \nas Nucleosil 50-S 5 pm), \n- mobile phase: a mixture of 997 volumes of hexane and"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cholecalciferol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cholecalciferol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cholecalciferol\nVitamin D3; Colecalciferol\nQ/I-EmO \nMol Wt. 384.6\nCholecalciferol is .(5Z7£X35)-940-secocholesta-5,7,10(19> \ntriene-3-ol.\nCholecalciferol contains hot less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of C27H44O.\nCategory. Vitamin D (antirachitic).\nDescription. White or almost white crystals. It is sensitive to \nair, heat and light. A reversible isomerisation to \nprecholecalciferol may occur in solution, depending on \ntemperature and time."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/choline-fenofibrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Choline Fenofibrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Choline Fenofibrate\nC22H28C1NO5 \nMol Wt. 421.9\nCholine Fenofibrate is 2-[4-(4-Chlorophenylcarbonyl) \nphenoxy] -2-methylpropanoate choline salt.\nCholine Fenofibrate contains fenofibric acidnot less than 72.0 \nper cent and not more than 76.0 per cent and choline not less \nthan 23.0 per cent and not more than 26.0 per cent, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihyperlipidaemic.\nDescription. A white to off white crystalline powder"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chorionic-gonadotrophin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chorionic Gonadotrophin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chorionic Gonadotrophin Injection\nChorionic Gonadotrophin Injection is a sterile material \nconsisting of Chorionic Gonadotrophin with or without \nexcipients such as buffers, diluents or other inert substances \nsuch as Lactose or Sodium Chloride. It may also contain an \nantimicrobial agent. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for\nInjections or a suitable diluent supplied by the manufacturer, \nimmediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nChorionic Gonadotrophin Injection contains not less than \n80.0 per cent and not more than 125.0 per cent ofthe stated \npotency.\nUsual strengths. 500,1000,2000, 5000 and 10,000 Units in\neach sealed container.\ndescription. A white or almost white powder.\nie contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements for Powders for Injections stated under\nParenteral Preparations and . with the following \nrequirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chorionic-gonadotrophin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chorionic Gonadotrophin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chorionic Gonadotrophin\nHuman Chorionic Gonadotrophin\nChorionic Gonadotrophin is a dry, sterile preparation of \nplacental glycoproteins that has luteinising activity. It is \nextracted from the urine of pregnant women. The material is \nsterilised by filtration and dried under reduced pressure or \nfreeze-dried.\nChorionic Gonadotrophin contains not less than 2500 Units \nper mg.\nCategory. Gonadotrophic hormone.\nDescription. A white or almost white, amorphous powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chromium-picolinate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chromium Picolinate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chromium Picolinate\nChromium Tripicolinate * \n''T//\nC18Hi2N3O6Cr \nMol.Wt.418.3\nChromium Picolinate contains not.less than 98.ó per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of CisH^N'jOóCr, calculated on \nthe dried basis. \n. j 7'"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/chymotrypsin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Chymotrypsin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Chymotrypsin\nChymotrypsin is a proteolytic enzyme obtained by the \nactivation of chymotrypsinogen extracted from the pancreas' \nof beef (Bos taurus L.). It has an activity of not less than 5.0 \nmicrokatals per milligram. In solution it has maximal enzymic \nactivity at about pH 8; the activity is reversibly inhibited at \npH 3, the pH at which it is most stable.\nCategory. Proteolytic enzyme."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ciclesonide-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ciclesonide Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ciclesonide Inhalation\nCiclesonide Inhalation is a suspension or solution of microfine \nCiclesonide in a suitable liquid filled in a suitable pressurized \ncontainer. It may contain suitable pharmaceutical aids such as \nsurfactants, stabilizing agents.\nCiclesonide Inhalation delivers not less than 80.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nciclesonide, C32H44O7, per inhalation by actuation of the \nvalve.\nUsual strengths. 80 pg per metered dose; 160 pg per metered \ndose."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ciclesonide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ciclesonide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ciclesonide\nCiclesonide is (llp,16a)-16,17-[(/?)-cyclohexylmethylene] \nbis(oxy)-11 -hydroxy-21 -(2-methyl-1 -oxopropoxy)pregna-\n1,4-diene-3,20-dione.\nCiclesonide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of ciclesonide, C/^H.j.-O.-, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Glucocorticoid.\nDescription. A white to-off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cilastatin-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cilastatin Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cilastatin Sodium\nCj6Hi5N2NaOsS \nMol. Wt. 380.4\nCilastatin sodium is sodium (Z)-7-[[(I?)-2-amino-2-\nCarboxyethyI]sulphanyl]-2-[[[(lS)-2,2-dimethyl-cyclopropyI] \ncarbonyl] amino]hept-2-enoate.\nCilastatin Sodium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.5 per cent of C]6H25N2NaO5S, calculated on the \nanhydrous and solvent free basis.\nCategory- Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or light yellow amorphous powder, \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cilnidipine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cilnidipine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cilnidipine\nC27H28N2O7 \nMol. Wt. 492.5\nCilnidipine is l,4-Dihydro-2,6-dimethyl-4-(3-nitrophenyl)-3,5- \npyridinedicarboxylic acid 2~methoxyethyl (2E)-3 -phenyl-2- \npropenyl ester.\nCilnidipine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C27H28N2O7, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive, calcium channel blocker.\nDescription. Lightyellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cimetidine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cimetidine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cimetidine\nC10H16N6S. \nMol Wt. 252.3\nCimetidine is 2-cyano-l-methyl-3-[2-(5-methylimidazol- \n4-ylmethylthio)ethyljguanidine.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nCIMETIDINE\nCimetidine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.5 per cent of CioH16N6S, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory- Antiulcer.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\nIdentified011\nTest A may be omitted if tests B, C and D are carried out. Tests \nB C and D may be omitted if test A is carried out.\nA Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6), \nusing a potassium bromide dispersion obtained from the solid \nstate without prior solvent treatment. Compare the spectrum \nwith that obtained with cimetidine IPRS or with the reference \nspectrum of cimetidine. No shoulder or peak should be \ndiscernible at 1190 cm'1.\nB. When examined in the range 210 nm to 360 nm, a 0.0008 per \ncent w/v solution in 1 M sulphuric acid shows an absorption \nmaximum at 218 nm and a minimum at 260 nm (2.4.7).\nC. In the test for related substances, the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\nD. Dissolve about 1 mg in a mixture of 1 ml of ethanol and 5 ml \nof a freshly prepared 2 per cent w/v solution of citric acid in \nacetic anhydride. Heat in a water-bath for 10 to 15 minutes; a \nreddish violet colour is produced."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cinacalcet-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinacalcet Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cinacalcet Hydrochloride\nCsHsFjNJICI \nMol. Wt. 393.9\nCinacalcet Hydrochloride is (7?)-7/-(3-(3-(trifluoromethyl) \nphenyQpropyl)-1 -(1 -napthyl)ethylamine hydrochloride.\nCinacalcet Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per centof CaH^FjNjHCl, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihyperparathyroid.\nDescription, A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cinnamon-bark-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinnamon Bark Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cinnamon Bark Oil\nDaalchini ka tail\nCinnamon Bark Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam \ndistillation of the bark of the shoots of: Cinnamomum \nzeylanicum Nees (Fam. Lauraceae) \na.„/¡Rs.,..\nCinnamonBark Oil contains not less,than 1.0 per cent and not \nmore than 6.0 per cent of linalool, not less than 55.0 percent \nand not more than 75 per cent of trans-cinnamic aldehyde and \nnot less than 1.0 per cent, not more than 4.0 per cent of \np-caryophyllene, not more than 7.5 per cent eugenol, not more \nthan 3.0 per cent of cineole, not more than 0.5 per cent pf \ncoumarin, not more than 0.5 per cent of safrole, not more than\n1.0 per cent of benzyl benzoate and not less than 0.1 per cent \nand not more than 1.0 per cent of trans-2 methoxy \ncinnamaldéhyde. \" \n•\nCategory. Antiemetic, antidiarrhoeal, anti-inflammatory.\nDescription. A clear, pale yellow to amber liquid, free from \nsediment and suspended matter, characteristic odour, \nreminiscent of cinnamic aldehyde. \nr uw."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cinnamon-leaf-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinnamon Leaf Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cinnamon Leaf Oil\nDaalchini Ka Tail\nCinnamon Leaf Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam \ndistillation:of the leaves of Cinnamomum verum J.S. Presl. \n(Fam. Lauraceac).\nCinnamon Leaf Oil contains not more than 1.0 per cent of \ncineole, not less than 1.5 per cent and not more than \nV Per cent of linalool, not less than 1.5 per cent and not more \nthan 7.0 per cent of 3-caryophyllene, not more than \n3.0 per cent of safrole, not more than 3.6 per cent of \ntraws-cinnamic aldehyde, not more than 2.0 per cent of \ncinnamyl acetate, not less than 70 per cent not more than \n85.0 per cent of eugenol and not more than 1.0 per cent of\ncoumarin.\nCINNAMONIjEapq^\nCategory. Antiemetic, antidiarrhoeal, anti-inilammatory.\nDescription. A clear, pale yellow to amber liquid, free from \nsediment and suspended matter, characteristic odour, \nreminiscent of eugenol. \n■ ; \n:\nA. Determine by gas chromatography'(2.4.13). p ; .\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of the oil under \nexamination in acetone. \n'\nReference solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of cinnamon\nleaf oil- IPRS in acetone.\nUse chromatographic system described under Assay.\nThe peaks in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to the peaks in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution.\nB. Flash point (2.4.44). Not less than 60°.\nRelative density (2.4.29). 1.030 to 1.059.\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.527 to 1.540.\nOptical rotation (2.4.22). - 2.5° to+2.0°.\nAssay. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4,13). ..\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of the oil under \nexamination in acetone. \n; v; ;: v ■■ g\nReference solution. Dissolve. 10 pl of cineole IPRS,. 10. pl. of \nlinaloolIPRS, 10 pl of p-caryophyllene IPRS, 10 pl of safrole \nIPRS, 10 pl of trans-cinnamic aldehyde IPRS, 10 efi cinnamyl \nacetate IPRS, 100 pl of eugenol IPRS and 10 mg of coumarin \nIPRS in 1 ml of acetone.\nChromatographic system\n- a capillary column 60 m x 0.25 mm, coated with macrogol\n20000,\n- temperature: \n.. \n: .?!7. -g\ncolumn. 60° for 10 minutes, increase from 60° to 190° at \na rate of 2° per minute and maintain at this temperature \nfor 125minutes, ,\n- inlet port at 200° and detector at 240°,\n- detection by flame ionization detector,\n- flow, rate: 1.5 ml per minute.using helium as carrier gas,\n• \n- injection volume: 0.2 pl, \n: ; \n-\n- split ratio: 1:100.\nInject the reference solutions and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of cineole, linalol, ^-caryophyllene, \nSafrole, trans-cinnamic aldehyde, cinnamyl acetate, eugenol \nand coumarin in the oil using area normalization procedure.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\n4203\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCitronella Oil (Geraniol Type)\nCymbopogon nardus\nCitronella oil is essential oil obtained by steam distillation of \nfresh or partially dried aerial parts of Cymbopogon nardus \n(Fam. Cardiopteridaccae).\nCitronella oil contains not less than 1.0 per cent and not more \nthan, 5.0 per cent of limonene, not more than 0.1 per cent \ncitronelial, not more than 0.1 per cent citronellyl acetate, not \nless than 2.0 per cent and not more than 5.0 per cent of neral, \nnot less than 2.0 per cent and not more than 5.0 per cent of \ngeranial, not less than 5.0 per cent and not more than 10.0 per \ncent of geranyl acetate,, not less than 1.0 per cent apd not \nmore; than 5.0 per cent of citronellol and not less than 50.0 per \ncent of geraniol.\nCategory. Astringent, anti spasmodic, anti inflammatory.\nDescription. A clear, pale yellow to brownish yellow, mobile \nliquid, free from sediment and suspended matter. It has fresh, \nfloral-rose type odour."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cinnarizine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cinnarizine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cinnarizine\nC26H2SN2 \n' Mol. Wt. 368.5\nCinnarizine is (£’)-l-(diphenylmethyI)-4-(3-phenylprop- \n2-enyl)piperazine.\nCinnarizine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent bfC26H28N2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ciprofloxacin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ciprofloxacin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ciprofloxacin Hydrochloride\nC17HiSFN3O3,HCl,H2O \nMol. Wt 385.8\nCiprofloxacin Hydrochloride is l-cyclopropyl-6-fludro-\n1,4- dihydro-4-oxo-7-( 1 -piperazinyl)-3-quinolinecarboxylic \nacid hydrochloride monohydrate. ■\nCiprofloxacin Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C17Hi8FN3O3,HCl, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A pale yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ciprofloxacin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ciprofloxacin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ciprofloxacin Injection\nCiprofloxacin Injection is a sterile solution of Ciprofloxacin or \nCiprofloxacin Hydrochloride in 5 per cent Dextrose Injection \nor in Sodium Chloride Injection prepared with the aid of Lactic \nAcid.\nCiprofloxacin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nciprofloxacin, Ci7Hl8FN3O3.\nUsual strength. 2 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ciprofloxacin-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ciprofloxacin Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ciprofloxacin Tablets\nAss\nay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nFI6C1 N 2Pt \t\nMol. Wt. 300.0\nCiprofloxacin Hydrochloride Tablets\nTest \na q \ncipr \nshat\nCiprofloxacirilablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and  -\nnot more than - I 1 0.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nciprofloxacin, CrH isFN30: .\nsolution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh accurately \nlantity of the powder containing abouf 1.25 =g of \npfloxacin, add about 400 ml of 0.01 M hydrOchlorieacid, \nto for 20 minutes, dilute to 500.0 ml* with 0.01 M\nCisplatin Is ciSliamminedichloroplatinum(II).\nCisplatin contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of H,C1 2N2Pt.\nDescription. A yellow powder or orange yellow crystals.\nCAUTION - Cisplatin is potentially cytotoxic. Great care \nshould be taken in handling the powder and preparing \nsolutions.\nNOTE - Carry out all the tests and the Assay, except \nIdentification tests A and C and the test for Silver, protected \nfrom light.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDose. By intravenous infusion, 15 to 20 mg per sq. m. of body \nsurface daily for 5 days."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ciprofloxacin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ciprofloxacin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ciprofloxacin\nC17H18FN3O3 \nMol. Wt 331.4\nCiprofloxacin.is l-cyclopropyl-6-fluoro-l,4-dihydro-4-oxo- \n7-(piperazin-l-yl)quinoline-3-carboxylicacid.\nCINNARIZINE TABLETS\nIP 2022\nCiprofloxacin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of Ci7H;8FN3O3, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cisplatin-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cisplatin for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cisplatin for Injection\nCisplatin for Injection is a freeze dried mixture of Cisplatin, \nMannitol and Sodium Chloride. It is supplied in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents ofthe \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use..\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections). . .\nCisplatin for Injection contains Cisplatin not less than 95.0 \nper cent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount \nof cisplatin, Cl2H6N2Pt.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations (Powders \nfor injection) and with the following requirements.\nNOTE — With the exception of Identification test A, cany\nout the tests protected from light."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cisplatin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cisplatin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cisplatin Injection\nCisplatin Injection is a sterile solution of Cisplatin in Water for\nInjections. It is either supplied as a ready-to-use solution or it\nls prepared by dissolving Cisplatin for Injection in the requisite \namount of Water for Injections immediately before use.\ninjection complies with the requirements stated under \n?a> enteral Preparations,\nWhen supplied as a ready-to-use solution; the injection\nc°mplies with the following requirements. ..\nCisplatin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent arid \nnot more, than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of cisplatin,\nCTlLHEt.\nUsual strengths. 50 mg per 50 ml; 10 mg per 20 ml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless to pale yellow solution.\nNOTE — Except identification test A, carry out the tests \nprotected from light."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cisplatin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cisplatin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cisplatin\nCkp/NH3\ncr xNH3\nHA^Pt.\nMol. Wt. 300.0\nCisplatin is cA-diamminedichloroplatinum(II).\nCisplatin contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.Oper cent ofH6Cl2N2Pt.\n1887\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDescription. A yellow powder or orange yellow crystals. -; • :\nCA UTION — Cisplatin is potentially cytotoxic. Great care \nshould be taken in handling the powder and preparing \nsolutions.\nNOTE — Carry out all the tests and the Assay, except \nIdentification tests A and C and the test for Silver, protected \nfrom light.\nCategory. Anticancer."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/citalopram-hydrobromide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Citalopram Hydrobromide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Citalopram Hydrobromide\nCoH21FN20,HBr \nMol.Wt. 405.3\nCitalopram Hydrobromide is (7?S)-l-(3-dimethylaminopropyl)-l- \n(4-fluorophenyl)-l,3-dihydroisobenzofuran-5-carbonitrile \nhydrobromide.\nCitalopram Hydrobromide contains not less than 98.0 per cent\nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C?fjIL| I'N?O J IBr, calculated \n°n the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antidepressent.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/citicoline-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Citicoline Sodium","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Citicoline Sodium\nCi4H25N4NaO.1]P2 ■ \nMol. Wt. 510.3\nCiticoline Sodium is Cytidine-5’-diphosphocholine sodium.\nCiticoline Sodium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of Cl4H25N4NaOnP2, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Nootropic.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder.\n1893\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCITICOLINE SODIUM"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/citric-acid-monohydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Citric Acid Monohydrate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Citric Acid Monohydrate\nC6H80 7,HzO\nMol. Wt. 210.1\nCitric Acid Monohydrate is 2-hydroxypropane-l,2,3-\ntricarboxylic acid monohydrate.\nCitric Acid Monohydrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent\nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C6Hg0 7, calculated on\nthe,anhydrous basis...\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid; anticoagulant for storage of\nwhole-b100d-(along-with-sodium-citrate);---------·---\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline\npowder; slightly efflorescent in warm, dry air."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/citric-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Citric Acid"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Citric Acid\nHO, COOH\nHOOC \tCOON\nC61-1807 \t\nMol. Wt. 192.1\nCitric Acid is 2-hydroxypropane-1,2,3-tricarboxylic acid.\nCitric Acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C 6H807, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid; anticoagulant for storage of \nwhole blood (along with sodium citrate).\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white powder; slightly \nhygroscopic in moist air."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clarithromycin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clarithromycin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clarithromycin\nC38H69NO13 \nMol. Wt. 748.0\nClarithromycin is (3R,4S,5S,6R,1R,9R, nR,12R,13S,14Rf \n4-[(2,6-Dideoxy-3-C-methyl-3-O-methyl-a-L-ribo-hexo- \npyrano sy l)oxy] -14-ethy 1-1.2,13-dihy droxy-7-methoxy- 3,5, \n7,9,1 l,13-hexamethyl-6-[[3,4,6-trideoxy-3-(dimethyIamino)- \noc-D-xylo-hexopyranosyl]oxy]oxacyclotetradecane-2,10-dione (6- \nO-methylerythromycin A).\nClarithromycin contains not less than 96.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C3gH69NOi3, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/class-3","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Class 3"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Class 3\nn \n< X>F=O\n(CH3)2SO\nN-Methylpyrrolidone\n1-Methylpyrolidine-2-one\nClass 2\nJ l-Methyl-2-pyroIidinone \n- -\n0\nNitromethane\nClass 2\nCH3NO2\nClass 2\nPentane\nn- Pentane\n' Class 3\nCH3[CH2]3CH2\n0\nAmyl alcohol , \n.......\n1-Pentanol\nClass 3\nCH3[CH2]3CH2OH\n' Pentan-l-ol. .\nClass 3\nCH3CH2OH\nPentyl alcohol\n; CH3CH2CH2OH\n' Class 2 \nj\nCH3CH2OCH2CH2OH.\n1-Propanol\nPropan-1 -ol\nClass 3\n. Propylalcohol\nClass 3 \nj\nCH3COOCH2CII3\n! HOCH2CH2OH\n(CH^CHOH ?\nClass 2\n.2- Propanol\nPropan-2-ol\nClass 3\nIsopropyl alcohol ;\n1173\nwww.webofpharma.com\n1\n. i\n5.5; IMPURITIES . . ; \nIP 2022\nSolvent\nOther Names\nStructure\nClass\n• • • •\nPropyl acetate\nAcetic acid propyl ester\nCH3COOCH2CH2CH3\nClass 3\nPyridine\nAza benzene\nSulfonane\nTetrahydrothiophene 1,1-dioxide\nClass 2\nTetrahydrofuran\nTetramethylene oxide\nOxacyclopentane\nTetralin\n1,2,3,4-Tetrahydronaphthalene\nClass 2\nToluene\nMethylbenzene\nl[ J\nClass 2\n1,1,1 -Trichloroethane\nMethylchloroform\nCH3CC13\nClass 1\n1,1,2-Trichloroethene\nTrichloroethene\nhciocci2\nClass 2\nTriethylamine\nA.jV-Diethylethanamine\nN(CH2CH3)3\nClass 3\nXylene\n*\nDimethylbenzene\nXylol\n* Usually 60 per cent m-xylene. 40 per cent p-xylene. 9 per cent of o- xylene with 17 per cent ethylbenzene.\n5.5. Impurities\nThis chapter provides guidance on the control of impurities in \ndrug substances and formulated preparations described in IP \nmonographs. It applies mainly to totally synthetic organic \nmedicinal substances and those substances obtained by \nsynthetic modification of a naturally produced precursor. It is \nnot necessarily relevant to other, organic substances e.g., \nherbal products and crude products of plant or animal origin, \nbiological and biotechnology products, oligonucleotides, \nradiopharmaceuticals, fermentation products and semi­\nsynthetic products derived there from; inorganic substances\nand pharmaceutical excipients. Although the thresholds stated \nin this general chapter do not apply, the general concepts of \nreporting, identification (wherever possible) and qualification \nof impurities are equally valid for these classes. It provides an\napproach to the setting of limits for impurities in articles for\nClass' 2\nv°\nClass 2 .,\nClass'!\nwhich the individual monographs do not provide either a test \nor specific limits.\nImpurities are critical quality attributes of drug substances \nand drug products because they have the potential to affect \nthe.safety and efficacy ofthe product. The monographs of\nthe Pharmacopoeia have been designed to ensure the minimum \nacceptable quality of drug substances and drug products for \nusers. Tests for related substances have also been provided \nin many monographs to limit impurities and degradation \nproducts. Although one of the primary objectives of the \nPharmacopoeia; is to guarantee the identity, strength, purity \nand quality of official articles, it is not possible to include in\neach monograph a test for every impurity or contaminant or \neven an adulterant that might be present. The exclusion of a \nlimit for impurities in a monograph does not absolve the \nmanufacturer of providing assurance to the user on the safety\n1174\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022 \n5.5. IMPURITIES\nof a drug. It is incumbent on the manufacturer to follow Good \nManufacturing Practices (GMP) and to ensure the limitation \nof impurities based on knowledge of the properties of the \nchemical entity and the likelihood of related substances being \nassociated with the end product during production and\nsubsequent storage.\nImpurity measurements for drug products using \nchromatographic methods could present a challenge to the \nPharmacopoeial standards setting due to low concentrations \nof the impurity and complexity of matrix. As a consequence,\nsome ofthe monographs for Pharmacopoeial preparations rely\non chromatographic assays. Where more significant impurities\nare known, some monographs set forth specific tests for these \nimpurities.\nIn general, the tests in a monograph are tests for purity that \nprovide information on the extent of known potential or actu"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clavulanate-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clavulanate Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clavulanate Oral Suspension\nAmoxicillin and Potassium Clavulanate Oral Suspension\nAmoxycillin and Potassium Clavulanate Oral Suspension is a \nmixture of Amoxycillin Trihydrate and Potassium Clavulanate \nor Potassium Clavulanate Diluted with buffering agents and \nother excipients. It contains a suitable flavouring agent. ,\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of Water just \nbefore use. \n:i' ''■-■■■\nAmoxycillin and Potassium Clavulanate Oral Suspension \ncontains not less than 90.0 per cent and hot more:than\n1470\nAMOXYCILLIN AND POTASSIUM CLAVULANATE INJECTION \nIP 2022\n120.0 per cent of the stated amount of amoxycillin, C^H^bLOsS \nand not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than 125.0 per \ncent of the stated amount of clavulanic acid, C8H9NO5.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent ofthe stated amounts of amoxycillin, Ci6Hi9N3O5S \nand clavulanic acid, CsH9NO5.\nUsual strength. Amoxycillin 200 mg and Clavulanic acid \n28.5 mgper5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clemastine-fumarate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clemastine Fumarate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clemastine Fumarate\nCTH26C1NO,C.,H4O. \nMol.Wt.460.0\nClemastine Fumarate is (2/?)-2-{2-[(17?)-l-(4-Chlorophenyl)-\n1 -phenylethoxy]ethyl} -1 -methylpyrrolidine fumarate.\nClemastine Fumarate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C21 \n4NO.CJ f O.;. calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiallergic.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clemastine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clemastine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clemastine Oral Solution\nClemastine Fumarate Oral Solution\nTest solution. Dissolve 40 mg of the substance under \nexamination in methanol and dilute to 2.0 ml of methanol.\nClemastine Oral Solution contains Clemastine Fumarate in a \nsuitable vehicle. Clemastine Oral Solution contains not less \nthan 90.0 per„cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the \nstatecLamonnt of clemastine, C21H26C1NO.\nReference solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution of fumaric \nacid RS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nImpurity C. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14). \nSolvent mixture. 25 volumes of acetonitrile and--75 -voltithes \nof 1.0 per cent w/v solution of ammonium - dihvdrogen\nApply, to the -plate 5 p1 of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 15 cm. Dry the plate in air, heat at 105° for\nUsual strength. 0.1 mg per ml. \nphosphate.\n1146 \n`SI .,:-.;,--\":k.. \n--.--- \t\nc--,:p,, \n%.,. \t\n2,.."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clindamycin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clindamycin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clindamycin Hydrochloride\nC1sH33C1N2O5S3C1 \n.... Mol. Wt. 461.5\nClindamycin Hydrochloride is methyl 7-chloro-6,7,8-trideoxy- \n6\"[[[(2S,4/?)-l-methyl-4-propyl-2-pyrrolidinyl] ’ carbonyl] \nanuno]-I-thio-L-/^red-d-D-g£rZacfo-octopyranoside \nhydrochloride. \n' ' ; ■\nClindamycin Hydrochloride contains not less than 91.0 per\ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C18H33C1N2O5S,HC1, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\ndescription, A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clindamycin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clindamycin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clindamycin Injection\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 10 mg of lincw\nhydrochloride RS in 5 ml of reference solution (a).\nClindamycin Phosphate Injection\nApply to the plate 5µl of each solution. Allow the mobile\nnobile phase: a mixture of 200 volumes of acetonitrile\nClindamycin Injection is a sterile solution of Clindamycin \nPhosphate in Water for Injections.\nDry \nphase to rise 12 cm. D the plate at 100 to 105° for 30 %\n tad 800 volumes of a 1.36 per _cent w/v solution of\nand spray with a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of pota\niotassium dihydrogen phosphari previously adjusted \nto pH 2.5 with orthophosphoric acid,\nClindamycin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nclindamycin, C 181/33C1N205S.\npermanganate. The principal spot in the chromatogra m\nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the principal \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution \n(a). The chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) \nshows 2 principal spots.\nlow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\nnjection volume: 20111.\nDescription. An almost colourless solution.\nreference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the\nUsual strength. 150 mg per ml.\nC. Dissolve 10 mg in 2 ml of dilute hydrochloric acid and heat \nin a water-bath for 3 minutes. Add 4 ml of sodium carbonate \nsolution and 1 ml of a 2.0 per cent w/v solution of sodium \nnitroprusside. Prepare a reference solution in the same manner \nusing clindamycin phosphate RS. The colour of the test \nsolution corresponds to that of the reference solution.\nion between the peaks due to clindamycin phosphate \nak) and clindamycin impurity E (3rd peak) is not less\nt\n(rI2ensn p \nl\njdoecuet\nOH \tSCH 3"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clindamycin-palmitate-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clindamycin Palmitate Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clindamycin Palmitate Hydrochloride\no\nC34H63C1N2O6S,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 699.9\nClindamycin Palmitate Hydrochloride is L-threo-a-D-galacto- \nOctopyranoside, methyl 7-chloro-6,7,8-trideoxy-6-[[(l-methyl- \n4-propyl-2-pyrrolidinyl)carbonyl]amino]-l-thio-2- \nhexadecanoate, monohydrochloride, (2.SAram)-; Methyl 7- \nchloro-6,7,8-trideoxy-6-(l-methyl-4-propyl-L-2- \npyrrolidinecarboxamido)-l-thio-L threo-a-D-galacto- \noctopyranoside 2-palmitate monohydrochloride.\nClindamycin Palmitate Hydrochloride has a potency equivalent \nto not less than 540 pg of clindamycin per mg.\nCategory. Antibiotics.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\n1907\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clindamycin-phosphate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clindamycin Phosphate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clindamycin Phosphate Injection\nClindamycin Injection is a sterile solution of Clindamycin \nPhosphate in Water for Injections.\nClindamycin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nclindamycin, C18IfiClNi/XS.\nDescription. An almost colourless solution.\nUsual strength. 150 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clindamycin-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clindamycin Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clindamycin Phosphate\nC3H34CIKAPS \n; Mol. Wt. 505.0\nClindamycin Phosphate is methyl 7-ch loro-6,7,8-lridcoxy-6- \n[[[(2S',47?)-1 -methyi-4-propylpyrrohdin-2- yl]carbonyl] amino] -\n1 -thio-L-threo-a-D-galacto-octopyranoside 2-(dihydrogen \nphosphate). It is a semi-synthetic product derived from a \nfermentation product.\nClindamycin Phosphate contains not less than'95.0 per cent\nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C j ^^CINjOgP’Scalculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Lincosamide. antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white, slightly hygroscopic\npowder. It shows polymorphism (2.5:11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clobazam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clobazam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clobazam\nCi6Hi3ClN2O2 \nMol. Wt. 300.7\nClobazam is 7-chloro-l-methyl-5-phenyl-l ,5-dihydro- \n3/7-1,5-benzodiazepine-2,4-dione.\nClobazam contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more \nthan 103.0 per cent of Ci6Hi3C1N2O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clobetas","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clobetas"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clobetas\n )1 impurity E\n8\n2.1\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nStorage. Store protected from light.\n'betameth\nisone 17-propionate,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clobetasol-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clobetasol Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clobetasol Ointment\nClobetasol Propionate Ointment\nClobetasol Ointment contains Clobetasol Propionate in a \nsuitable base.\nClobetasol Ointment contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 115.0 per cent w/w of clobetasol propionate, \nC25H32CIFO5.\nUsual strength. 0.05 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clobetasol-propionate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clobetasol Propionate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clobetasol Propionate\nC25H32C1FO5 \nMol. wt. 467.0\nClobetasol Propionate is 21-chloro-9a-fluoro-l Ib-hydroxy-\n16p-methylpregna-l,4-diene-3,20-dion-17a-yl propionate..\nClobetaol propionate contains not less than 97.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C25H32C1FO5> calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Glucocorticoid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clobetasone-butyrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clobetasone Butyrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clobetasone Butyrate\n1916\nCLOBETASOL OINTMENT\n3’2022\nClobetasone Butyrate is (160)-21-chloro-9-fluoro-lfj. \nmethylpregna-1,4-dien—3,11,20-trione-17-butyrate.\nClobetasone Butyrate contains not less than 97.0 per cent \nand hot more than 102.0 per cent of C26H32C1FO5, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Glucocorticoid. . ..\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clobetasone-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clobetasone Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clobetasone Cream\nClobetasone Butyrate Cream\nClobetasone Cream contains clobetasone butyrate in a suitable \ncream base.\nClobetasone Cream contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nclobetasone butyrate, C26H32C1FO5.\nUsual strength. 0.05 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clofazimine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clofazimine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clofazimine\nC27H22C12N4 \nMol. Wt. 473.4\nClofazimine is 3-(4-chloroanilino)- 10-(4-chlorophenyl)- \n2,10-dihydro-2-(isopropylimino)phenazine.\nClofazimine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.5 per cent of Cf: l l ^C'I CN ,, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antibacterial (antileprotic).\nDescription. Dark red crystals or a reddish-brown, fine powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clomiphene-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clomiphene Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clomiphene Citrate\nHO\neOOH\n.Hooc~eooH\nC26H2gClNO,C6Hg07\nMol. Wt. 598.1\nClomifene citrate is a mixture of E- and Z-isomers of 2-[4-(2-\nchloro-1,2-diphenylvinyl)phenoxy]triethylariiine dihydrogen\ncitrate.\nClomifene Citrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C26H2gCINO,C6Hg07,\ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nC~lte~~or'y, Ovulation inducer.\n\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\"\",\"',\nDose. 50 mg daily for 5 consecutive days starting on fifth\n(l~y oflnellstruation.\nDescription. A white or pale yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clomipramine-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clomipramine Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clomipramine Capsules\nClomipramine Hydrochloride Capsules\nClomipramine Capsules contain Clomipramine Hydrochloride.\nClomipramine Capsules contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nclomipramine hydrochloride, CI9H23C1N2,HC1.\nUsual strengths. 25 mg; 75 mg; 100 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clomipramine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clomipramine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clomipramine Hydrochloride\nC19HSC1N2,HC1 \nMol. Wt 3513\nClomipramine is 3-(3-chloro-10,1 l-dihydro-5//-dibenzo|/?/| \nazepin-5 -yl)-Y, A-dimethylpropan-1 -amine hydrochloride.\nClomipramine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C19H23C1N2,HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow, crystalline powder, \nslightly hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clonazepam-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clonazepam Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clonazepam Injection\nReference solution (a). Dissolve 25 mg of clonazepam RS in 5 ml \nof methanol and dilute to 50 ml with the solvent mixture. \nReference solution (b). Dilute 1 ml of reference solution (a) to\nClonazepam Injection is a sterile solution of Clonazepam. It is \nprepared immediately before use by diluting Sterile \nClonazepam Concentrate with Water for Injections in \naccordance with the manufacturer's instructions.\nnot more than the area of the peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution (b) (1.0 per cent).\n100 ml with solvent mixture. \nChromatographic system\nOther test. Comply with the tests stated under Capsules.\nAssay.. Shake 4'cluantity of the mixed contents of 20 capsules \ncontaltriing 50-mg of Clomipramine Hydrochloride with 200 ml\nTfieiciion'k' constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 min -packed-with \noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\nof\"). I AI hydrochloric acid for 1 hour, dilute to 250m1 v,  ith 0.1\nThe injection complies with the requirements stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clonazepam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clonazepam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clonazepam\nC15H10ClN3O3 \n' Mol. Wt. 315.7\nClonazepam is 5-(2-chlorophenyl)-7-nitro-l,3-dihydro- \n1H-1,4-benzodiazepin-2-one.\nClonazepam contains not less than 99.0 per cent arid not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of Ci5Hi0C1N3O3, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\nDescription. A slightly yellowish, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clonidine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clonidine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clonidine Hydrochloride\nC9H9C12N3,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 266.6\nClonidine Hydrochloride is 2-[(2,6-dichlorophenyl)imino]- \nitnidazolidine hydrochloride.\nClonidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per cent\nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C9H9C12N3,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clonidine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clonidine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clonidine Injection\nClonidine Hydrochloride Inj ection\nClonidine Injection is a sterile solution of Clonidine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nClonidine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and. \nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of clonidine \nhydrochloride, C9H9C12N3,HC1.\nUsual strength. 150 gg in 1 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clopidogrel-bisulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clopidogrel Bisulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clopidogrel Bisulphate\n, h2so4\nC16Hl6dNO2S,H2SO4 \nMol. Wt. 419.9\nClopidogrel Bisulphate is methyl (S)-a-(o-chlorophenyl)-\n6,7-dihydrothieno[3,2-c]pyridine-5-(4/Z)-acetate sulphate.\nClopidogrel Bisulphate contains not less than 97.0 per cent\n2nd not more than 101.5 per cent of Ci6Hi6C1NO2S,H2SO4, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antithrombotic.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cloprostenol-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cloprostenol Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cloprostenol Sodium\n\\ \na \n<<?\n~o\nCyHogCiNaQ, \nMol. Wt. 446.9\nCategory. Prostaglandin (PGFiu) analogue. «\nCioprostenol Sodium is (5Z)-7-((1R,2R,, 3R ‘55)-2-(R, E}- 4. (3- \nchlorophenoxy)}-3-hydroxybut-1-enyl)-3 »5-dihydroxy- \ncyclopenty|hept-5-enoic acid.\nCloprostenol Sodium contains not less than 97.5 per.cent and\nnot more than102.5 per cent of CozHagCINaQg, calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis.\nNOTE—Cloprosienol sodium \nis extremely potent and \nextraordinary care should be taken in any procedure in which\nit is used. \nLo \noe\nDescription. Awhite or.almost white, \n=; amorphous hygroscopic \nPowder. \n:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/closantel-sodium-dihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Closantel Sodium Dihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Closantel Sodium Dihydrate \ni is .N-[5-chloro-4-[(RS)-(4-\nchloropheny!l) cyanomethyl]-2- methylphenyl]-2- -hydroxy-3,5- \ndiiodobenzamide sodium salt dihydrate.”\nClosantel Sodium Dihydrate contains not less than 98. 5 per\ncent and not more than 101.5 per cent of CroHChlNoNa0:, \n210, calculated on the anhydrous basis. \n©\nCategory. “ Antihelminthic.\nDescription. Ayellow, powder, slightly hygroscopic It shows \npolymorphism @. 5. WW)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clotrimazole-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clotrimazole Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clotrimazole Cream\nClotrimazole Cream contains Clotrimazole in a suitable cream\nbase.\nClotrimazole Cream contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nclotrimazole, C^HpClNj.\nL'sual strengths. 1 per cent w/w; 2 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clotrimazole-lotion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clotrimazole Lotion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clotrimazole Lotion\nClotrimazole Lotion contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nclotrimazole, CtjH^CIN^\nUsual strength. 1.0 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clotrimazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clotrimazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clotrimazole\nC^HnCIN,\nMol. Wt. 344.8\nClotrimazole is l-[(2-chlorophenyl)diphenylmethyl]-\nI//-imidazole.\n1935\nwww.webofpharma.com\nClotrimazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C22H17C1N25 calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clove-bud-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clove Bud Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clove Bud Oil\nClove Bud Oil is the essential oil obtained by steamdistillation \nfrom the dried flower buds of Syzygium aromaticuni L.:, Merr. \nand Perry syn. Eugenia caryophyllus (Sprengel) Bullock and \nS. Harrison (Fam. Myrtaceae).\nClove bud oil contains not less than 75.Ô per cent and.not \nmore than 85.0 per cent of eugenol, not less than 8.0 per cent \nand not more than 15.0 per cent of eugényl acetate arid not \nless than 5.0 per cent and not more than 14.0 per cent p- \ncaryophyllene.\nCategory. Used in toothache, headache, kasa.\nDescription. A clear, yellow to brown liquid, free from sediment \nand suspended matter.\n< ■ ■■■ ■/ U-\": : \n■ !■: \n'■ ./ Ù. A-.i V ■■Si \n; i-’ \n■. /?.<; ■■ . • 0 ; : L"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clove-leaf-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clove Leaf Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clove Leaf Oil\nClove Leaf Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nof the leaves of Syzygium aromaticum L. Merr. and Perry syn. \nEugenia caryophyllus ( Sprengel ) Bullock and S. Harrison) \nof the (Fam. Myçtaçae).\nClove Leaf Oil contains not less than 80.0, per cent and not \nmore than 92.0 per cent of eugenol/not less than 0.5 per cent \nand not more than 4.0 per cent of eugenyl âcétatei\nDescription. A clear, yellow to light brown liquid, free from \nsediment and suspended matter,\" spicy and characteristic of \neugenol-like odour. \n- ;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clove-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clove Oil"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clove Oil\nClove oil is the oil distilled from the dried flower buds of\nSyzygium aromaticum (Linn.) Merrill and Perry Eugenia\ncaryophyllus (Spreng.) Bull. and Harr.(Fam. Myrtaceae).\nDescription. A clear, colourless or pale yellow liquid when\nfreshly distilled, becoming darker and thicker by ageing or\nexposure to air; odour as of clove.\nClove Oil contains not less than 85.0 per cent w/w and not\nmore than 95.0 per cent w/w ofphenolic substances, chiefly\neugenol, CIOHI20 2•"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clove-stem-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clove Stem Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clove Stem Oil\nClove Stem Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nfrom the dried stems of Syzygium aromaticum L. Merr. and \nPerry syn. Eugenia caryophyllus (Sprengel), Bullock, and \nS. Harrison (Fam, Myrtacae). . , \n, \n; y \\ v.\nClove Stem oil contains not less than 83.0 per cent and not \nmore than 92.0 per cent of eugenol, not less than 0.5 per cent \nand not more than 4.0 per cent of eugenyl acetate.\nDescription.. A .clear, yellow to brown liquid, free from\nsediment and suspended matter; spicy and. characteristic \neugenol-like odour. \n-a"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cloxacillin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cloxacillin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cloxacillin Injection\nCloxacillin Sodium Injection\nCloxacillin Injection is a sterile material consisting of Cloxacillin \nSodium with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer. .\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving.the contents ofthe \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCloxacillin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of cloxacillin, \nC19H18C1N3O5S.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 250 mg and 500 mg of \ncloxacillin.\n1941\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; very \nhygroscopic.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cloxacillin-intramammary-infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cloxacillin Intramammary Infusion"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cloxacillin Intramammary Infusion\n2649\n2649\n(Lactating CowlBuffalo)\nCloxacillin Intramammary Infusion (LCIB)\n1125\n153,299,1123\nCloxacillin Oral Solution\nCloxacillin Sodium\n1124\nCloxacillin Sodium Capsules\n1125\nCloxacillin Sodium Elixir\n1125\nCloxacillin Sodium Injection\nCloxacillin Sodium Intramammary\n2649\nInfusion (Lactating CowlBuffalo)\n1125\nCloxacillin Sodium Oral Solution\n1125\nCloxacillin Sodium Syrup\n1125\n153,1126\nCloxacillin Syrup\nClozapine\n1127\nClozapine Tablets\n572\nCMC\n752\nCoated Tablets\n1576\nCo-careldopa Tablets\n2690\n2692\nCo-trimazine Injection\nCo-tr