ith Dextrose\n2128\n2130\nIntravenous Infusion, Modified\nCompound Sodium Lacate Solution for Irrigation\n·2562\n1424\n2562\nConcentrate ofHuman Red Blood Cells\nConcentrated Glyceryl Trinitrate Solution\nConcentrated Human Red Blood Corpuscles\n2774\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nINDEX\n1450\nConcentrated Hydrochloric Acid\n1424\nConcentrated Nitroglycerin Solution\n1909\nConcentrated Phosphoric Acid\n2588\nConcentrate Platelet\nConcentrated Solutions for Injection\n749\n2602\nConcentrated Solution Erythropoietin\n2610\nConcentrated Solution Filgrastim\n2613\nConcentrated Solution Interferon Alfa-2\nConcentrated Vitamin A and D Solution\n2308\nConcentrated Vitamin D Solution\n2308\nConductivity\n119\nCongealing Range or Temperature\n121\nCongo Red\n623\nCongo Red Fibrin\n623\n681,683,744\nContainers\n693\nContainers for Blood and Blood Components\n683\nContainers for Pharmaceutical Products\nContents of packaged Dosage Forms\n193\nContainers ofParenteral Products, Closures for\n691\nContinuous Extraction of Drugs\n23\n576\nCoomassie Staining Solution\nCopper\n576\nCopper Cliloride-Pyridine Reagent\n576\nCopper Foil\n576\nCopper Solution, Alkaline\n576\nCopper Standard Solution\n629\nCopper Standard Solution (10 ppm Cu)\n629\nCopper Sulphate Solution\n576\nCopper Turnings\n576\n576\nCopper(I) Chloride\nCopper(II) Acetate\n576\nCopper(II) Chloride\n576\nCopper(II) Sulphate\n576\nCopper-Citric Solution\n576\nCorainder Oil\n2494\nCortisol\n1453\n1454\nCortisol Acetate\nCortisol Acetate Eye Ointment\n1455\n1456\nCortisol Acetate Injection\n1457\nCortisol Hydrogen Succinate\nCortisol Sodium Succinate Injection\n1459\n153,301"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cloxacillin-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cloxacillin Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cloxacillin Sodium\nC19H17ClN3NaO5S, H2O \nMol. Wt. 475.9\nCloxacillin Sodium is sodium (67?)-6-[3-(2-chlorophenyl)- \n5-methylisoxazole-4-carboxamido]penicillanate monohydrate.\nCloxacillin Sodium contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of Ci9Hi7ClN3NaO5S, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nhygroscopic.\n1939\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cloxacillin-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cloxacillin Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cloxacillin Syrup\nCioxacillin Sodium Syrup; Cloxacillin Elixir; Cloxacillin \nSodium Elixir; Cloxacillin Oral Solution; Cloxacillin \nSodium Oral Solution\nCloxacillin Syrup is a mixture consisting of Cloxacillin Sodium \nwith buffering agents and other excipients. It contains a \nsuitable flavouring agent. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water just \nbefore use.\nCloxacillin Syrup contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of C^H^CIN^S.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of cloxacillin, C19H i8C1N3OsS.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 125 mg of cloxacillin per 5 ml \nwhen reconstituted with water."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clozapine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clozapine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clozapine Tablets\nClozapine Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of clozapine, \nCi8HI9C1N4-\nUsual strengths. 25 mg; 50 mg; 100 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/clozapine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Clozapine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Clozapine\nC18H19QN4\nMol. Wt. 326.8\nClozapine is 8-chloro-ll-(4-methylpiperazin-l-yl)-5H- \ndibenzo[/>,e][l}4]diazepine.\nClozapine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\n}han 102.0 per cent of C^H^CIN^ calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory, Antipsychotic.\nDescription, Ayellow crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/coconut-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Coconut Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Coconut Oil\nNariyal ka tail\nCoconut Oil is the refined fixed oil obtained from the dried/ \nsolid part of the endosperm of Cocos nucífera L. (Fam. \nArecaceae).\nCoconut oil contains not less than 0.5 per cent w/w of caproic\nacid, not less than 5.0 per cent and not more than 11.0 per cent\nof caprylic acid, not less than 4.0 per cent and not more\nthan 9.0 per cent w/w of capric acid, not less than 40.0 per cent \nand not more than 50.0 per cent w/w of lauric acid, not less \nthan 15.0 per cent and not more than 20.0 per cent w/w of \nmyristic acid, not less than 7.0 per cent and not moe than\n12.0 per cent w/w of palmitic acid, hot less than 1.5 per cent \nand not more than 5.0 per-cent w/w of stearic acid, not less \nthan 4.0 per cent and not more than 10.0 per cent w/w of oleic \nacid, not less than 1.0 per cent and not more than 3.0jper cent \nw/w of linoleic acid, not less than 0.2 per cent w/wand not \nmore than 3.0 per cent w/vyoif linolenic acid, hot less than .0.06 \nper cent w/w and hot more than 0.2 per cent w/w of arachidic \nacid and not less than 0,07 per cent w/wand riot mofe than 0.2 \nper cent w/w of eicosenoic acid. \\.Z;: ■ \n'• \n.•\nCategory. Antieczematous, antipsoriatic, Kesya. \n’. _ ■\nDescription. White to light yellow mass or colourless to light \nyellow, clear oil. \nryy\nA. It complies with the test for melting range (2.4.21).\nB. It complies with the test for composition of fatty acids.\nMelting range (2.4.21). 23 ° to 26°.\nRefractive index (2.4.27). About 1.449, at 40°.\nPeroxide valúe (2.3.35), Not more than 5.0.\nAcid value (2.3.23). Not more than 0.5, determined on 20.0 g.\nUnsaponifiable matter (2.3.39). Not more than 1.0 per cent, \ndetermined on 5.0 g. \n\"\nIodine value (2.3.28). 82 to 90.\nComposition of fatty acids\nTest Solution. Refined coconut oil is melted under gentle \nheating to a homogeneous liquid. \n■ • •\nReference solution. Dissolve 15 mg of tricaproin IPRS, 80 mg \nof tristearin IPRS, 0.15 g of tricaprin IPRS, 0.2 g oftricaprylin \nIPRS, 0.45 g of trimyristin IPRS and 1.25 g dftrilaurin IPRS \nin a mixture of 2 volumes of dichloromethane;and8 ^volumes \nof heptane, then dilute to 50 ml with the same mixture of \nsolvents heat at45°to 50°. Transfer 2 ml of this mixture tp a 10 \nml centrifuge tube with a screw cap.and evaporate the solvent \nin a current of nitrogen. Dissolve with 1 ml oí heptane and 1 \nml of dimethyl carbonate arid mix vigoforisly under gentle \nheating (50° to 60°); Add, while still warm) 1 ml of a 1.2 per cent \nw/v solution of sodiumin anhydrous methanol, prepared with \nthe necessary precautions, and mix vigorously for about 5 \nminutes. Add 3 ml of distilled water and mix vigorously for \nabout 30 seconds. Centrifugo for 15; minutes at 1500 rpm, Inj ect\n1 pl of the organic phase. . \n. ; • \n:\nStorage: Store protected'from light in well-filled containers.\n4207\nwww.webofpharma.com\nColeus contains not less than 0,4 per cent w/w of forskolin, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Cardiac stimulant, hypotensive, spasmolytic.\nDescription. The roots are light brown in color, generally long \nand radially spread. They have an aromatic characteristic odour \nand the taste is slightly pungent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/codeine-phosphate-hemihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Codeine Phosphate Hemihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Codeine Phosphate Hemihydrate\nCi8H2lNO3,H3PO4,1/2 H2O ' \nMol. Wt. 406.4\nCodeine Phosphate is (5R,6S)-7,8-didehydro-4,5a-epoxy- \n3-methoxy-17-methylmorphinan-6-ol dihydrogen phosphate \nhemihydrate, an alkaloid occurring in Papaver somniferum.\nCodeine Phosphate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and\n’lotmore than 101.0 per cent of ClgH2]NO3,H3PO4, calculated \n°n the dried basis.\nCategory. Analgesic; antidiarrhoeal; cough suppressant.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/codeine-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Codeine Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Codeine Syrup\nCodeine Phosphate Syrup\nCodeine Syrup is a 0.5 per cent w/v solution of Codeine \nPhosphate in a suitable flavoured vehicle.\nCodeine Syrup contains not less than 0.48 per cent w/v and \nnot more than 0.52 per cent w/v of codeine phosphate, \nC]8H21NO3,H3PO4,/2H2O."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/coichicine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Coichicine"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Coichicine\nOCH3\nC22H25N06 \t\nMol. Wt. 399.4\nColchicine is (5)-N-(5,6,7,9-tetrahydro-1,2,3,10-tetramethoxy- \n9-oxobenzo[a]heptalen-7-yflacetamide, an alkaloid which \noccurs in the corm and seeds of various species of Colchicum.\nCoichicine contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more \nthan 103.0 per cent of C 22H25N06, calculated on the anhydrous \nand solvent-free basis.\nCategory. Gout suppressant.\nDose. Initial dose, 1 mg; subsequent doses, 500 lig every two \nhours.\nrate'l ml per minute, \nPale yellow crystals, amorpllo \t\na ea--or\nDescription. \npowder.\nCOLCHICINE TABLETS\nP 2018 \nII,\n- flow rate: 30 ml per minute, using nitrogen as the carrier"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/colchicine-and-probenecid-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Colchicine and Probenecid Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Colchicine and Probenecid Tablets\nColchicine and Probenecid Tablets contain not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated\namount of colchicine, C22H25N06, and not less than 90.0 per\ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nprobenecid, C13H19N04S,\nCategory. Uricosuric agent.\nDose. Colchicine, 1 mg and Probenecid, 250 mg twice daily\nafter food.\nUsual strength. Colchicine, 1 mg and Probenecid, 250 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/colchicine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Colchicine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Colchicine\nT jf Y-nhcoch3 ..\nH3CO \\ \n1\noch3\nC^H^NOe \nMol. Wt. 399.4\nColchicine is7V-[(75,12aM)-l,2,3,10-tetramethoxy-9-oxo-5, \n6,7,9-tetrahydrobenzo[a]heptalen-7-yl] acetamide, an alkaloid \nwhich occurs in the corm and seeds of various species of\nColchicum.\nColchicine contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C?2I L<NO6, calculated on the anhydrous \nand solvent free basis.\nNOTE — Colchicine is extremely poisonous, handle with\ncaution.\nCategory. Gout suppressant.\nDescription. A pale yellow, amorphous or crystalline powder.\n1948\nIP 2022 W"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/coleus-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Coleus Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Coleus Dry Extract\nC0£us Dry Extract is obtained by extracting Coleus (Coleus \nFors^oh^i Willd. Briq., Fam. Lamiaceae) roots with\nffletfianol or any other suitable solvent and evaporation of\nsplye9t;\nColeus Dry Extract contains not less than 90 per cent w/w to\nthan 120 per cent w/w of the stated amount of \nforskolin, calculated on the dried basis. It may contain suitable \nadded substances.\nCalory- Cardiac stimulant, hypotensive, spasmolytic.\nUsual strengths. 10 per cent w/w; 20 per cent w/w.\nDescription. A light brown to brown colour powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/colistimethate-sodium-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Colistimethate Sodium for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Colistimethate Sodium for Injection\nColistimethate Sodium for Injection is a sterile material\nconsisting of Colistimethate Sodium with or without excipients, \nh is supplied in a sealed container.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements for Powders for Injections or Infusions stated \nunder Parenteral Preparations and with the following \nrequirements.\nColistimethate Sodium for Injection contains not less than\n■0 per cent and not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated \namount of colistimethate sodium.\nUsual strengths. 1 million IU and 2 million IU per vial."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/colistin-sulphate-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Colistin Sulphate Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Colistin Sulphate Oral Suspension\nColistin Sulphate Oral Suspension is a dry mixture of Colistin \nSulphate with or without one or more suitable buffers, colours, \ndiluents, dispersants, and flavouring agent. It is filled in a \nsealed container.\n.The suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of\nthe sealed container in the specified volume of Water just \nbefore use.\nColistin Sulphate Oral Suspension contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated \namount of colistin.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated.on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of colistin."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/colloidal-anhydrous-silica","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Colloidal Anhydrous Silica"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Colloidal Anhydrous Silica \n. ,\nSiO2 \nMol. Wt. 60.1\nColloidal Silicon Dioxide is a submicróscopic fumed silica \nprepared ;by: the vapour-phàsé ; hydrolysis of a silicon \ncompound. \n' •'\nColloidal Silicon Dioxide contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not moré than 100.5 per cent of SiO2, calculated on the \nignite basis. ’’ \n' \n. ' /\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (tablet excipient)..\nDescription?A light, fine; white, amorphous powder. It has a \nparticle size of about 15 nm."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/colloidal-silicon-dioxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Colloidal Silicon Dioxide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Colloidal Silicon Dioxide\nColloidalAnhydrous Silica\n. Mol. Wt. 60.1\nSi02\nColloidal Silicon Dioxide is a submicroscopic finned silica\nprepared by the vapour-phase hydrolysis of a silicon\ncompound.\nColloidal Silicon Dioxide contains not less than 99.0 per cent\nand not more than 100.5 per cent of Si02, calculated on the\nignite basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (tablet excipient).\nDescription. A light, fine, white, amorphous powder. It has a\nparticle size ofabout 15 nm."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/complex-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Complex Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Complex Injection\nTechnetium (99mTc) MDP Complex Injection, (99mTc) \nDihydrogen Methylene Diphosphonate (MDP) Complex\nInjection.. .\nCategory. Diagnostic. \nbsi/nrrn sxnq\ni\nDescription. A clear colourless solution. Technetium (99mTc) \nMedronate (MDP) Complex Injection is a sterile solution and \ncontains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than \n110.O pcr cent of the stated technetium-99m radioactivity, at \nthe date and-time stated on the label. Not less than 95 percent \nof the total technerium-99m is present as 99mTc-MDP complex. \nUse the 99mTc-MPP complex inj ection within 6h from the date \nand time of preparation.\nRadiopharmaceutical preparation. Technetium (99mTc) \nMedronate Complex Injection is prepared aseptically following \nmanufacturer’s instructions provided with the kit. The kit \ncontains sodium methylene diphosphonate (sodium \nmedronatefand a stannous salt; The kit may have preadjusted \npH and may contain reducing, chelating; stabilizing, filling \nand antioxidizing agents as well as antimicrobial preservatives \nand buffers. The injection may also be prepared under aseptic \nprocessing combined with sterilization by filtration. Sodium \npertechnetate 99nlTc (fission or non-fission), complying with\nspecification given for it, is used with the kit to make the \n(99m Tc) M DP Injection."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/complex","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Complex"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Complex\nmg of total protein, before the addition of any \namount Prote^n by multiplying the result\nstabilizer. \n.....\nCategory. Coagulation factor. \n\\\nThe factor IX fraction is dissolved in a suitable liquid, \nsome products, especially those without a protein\nanti-thrombin and other auxiliary substances svic^i^lfiepsuch as albumin, this method may not be applicable. \nstabilizer may be included. No anti-microbial pr&serva^^er validated method for protein determination must \nadded. The solution is passed through a bacteriax'Qt^Sfi¡grrfprf'be performed.\nfilter, distributed aseptically into the final contain»coagulation faetor(2.8.3),ifnecessary, dilute the \nimmediately frozen It is subsequently freeze-dried \nexamination t0 contain 20 IU of factor IX\ncontainers are closed under vaccum or under an inert \nofthe dilution the coagulation timéis not less\nIt is a sterile, freeze dried powder consisting of partially purified \nFactor IX fraction, as well as concentrated factors II, VII and \nX fractions, of venous plasma obtained from healthy human \ndonors. It contains no preservative.\nIt complies with the requirements of the test for potency in \nhaving not less than 80.0 per cent and not more than 120.0 per \ncent of the potency stated on the label in factor IX Units by \ncomparison with the factor IX standard or with a working \nreference that has been calibrated with it.\nConsistency ofthe method \n: .gthaiiiSO seconds.\njgeparin (2.8 9). If heparin has been added during preparation, \n^termine-.the. amount by the assay of heparin in coagulation \n^factot concentrates. The preparation under examination\nThe consistency of the method of production is evaluated! \nsuitable analytical procedure that are determine during pro \ndevelopment and which normally include (1) assay of.tó \nIX (2) determination of activated coagulation facto! \n(3) determination of activities of factor II, VII and X.w \nshall be shown to be not more than 5 per cent of the activity! \nfactor IX. \n|\nExpiration date. The expiration date is not later than 2 years \nfrom the date of manufacture.\ncontains not more than the amount of heparin stated on the\nlabel and in any case not more than 0.5 IU of heparin per IU of\nStorage. Store in hermetic containers in a refrigerator.\n[factorlX.\nLabelling. The label states (1) a warning that it is to be used \nwithin 4 hours after constitution; (2) that it is for intravenous \nadministration; (3) that a filter is to be used inthe administration \nequipment.\nWateiC Determine by suitable method such as semi micro \ndetenriination of water (2.3.43), loss on drying (2.4.19) or near \ninfrared spectrophotometry(2.4.6), the water content is within\nDescription. A white or pale yellow, hygroscopic powder! \nfriable solid. \nJ\nthe limits approved by the competent authority.\nReconstitute the preparation under examination as siali \non the label, immediately before carrying out \nIdentification, Tests (except those for solubility and waitf \nand assay.\nSterility (2,2.,11 ). Complies with .the test for sterility.\nHuman Normal Immunoglobulin\nPyrogeiis (22! 8) or Bacterial Endotoxin (BET) (2.2.3). Complies\nwith the test for pyrogen or, preferably and where justified\nNormal Immunoglobulin; Immune Human Serum\nand authorized with a validated in-vitro test such as the \nBacterial Endotoxin test.\nSolubility. To a container of the preparation to be examines \nadd the volume of the liquid stated on the label àt ri \nrecommended temperature. The preparation dissòlve \ncompletely with gentle swirling within 10 minute, giving! \nclear or slightly opalescent, colorless or slightly -yelkn \nsolution\nGlobulin; Human Gamma Globulin\nHuman Normal Immunoglobulin is a, liquid or freeze-dried \npreparation containing immunoglobulins, mainly IgG. Other \nproteins may be present. Human normal immunoglobulin \ncontains the IgG antibodies of normal subjects. It is intended \nfor intramuscular or subcutaneous administration.\n[Pyrogens. Complies with the test for pyrogens. Inject per kg\n[ofthe rabbit’s mass"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/compound-sodium-chloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Compound Sodium Chloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Compound Sodium Chloride Injection\nRinger’s Injection ; ;\nCompound Sodium Chloride Injection is a sterile solution \ncontaining 0,86 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride, 0.03 pet cent \nw/v of Potassium Chloride and 0.033 per cent w/v of Calcium \nChloride in Water for Injections. It contains no antimicrobial \nagent \np.: \n.... . .. ,\nCompound Sodium Chloride Injection.containsnot less than \n0.82 per cent w/v and not more than 0.90 per cent w/v of \nsodium chloride, NaCl, not less than 0.0285 per cent w/v and \nnot more than 0.0315 per cent w/v of potassium chloride, KC1, \nand not less than 0.030 per cent w/v and. not more than \n0.036 per cent w/v of calcium chloride, CaCl^ 2H2Q.\nDescription; Aclear, colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/compound-sodium-chloride-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Compound Sodium Chloride Solution"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Compound Sodium Chloride Solution\nRinger's Solution\nCompound Sodium Chloride Solution is a solution containing \n0.86 per cent w/v of Sodium Chloride, 0.03 per cent w/v of \nPotassium Chloride and 0.033 per cent w/v of Calcium Chloride \nin Purified Water. The solution may be clarified by filtration.\nCompound Sodium Chloride Solution contains not less  than \n0.82 per cent w/v and not more than 0.90 per cent of sodium \nchloride, NaC1, not less than 0.025 per cent and not more than \n0.035 per cent w/v of potassium chloride, KCI, and not less \nthan 0.030 per cent and not more than 0.036 per cent w/v of \ncalcium chloride, CaCl 2, 2H20.\nCategory. Irrigation solution (for external use).\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/compound-sodium-lactate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Compound Sodium Lactate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Compound Sodium Lactate Injection\nCompound SodiumLactate IntrFlvenous Infusion; Ringer-\nLactate Solution for Injection; Hartrnann's Solution for\nInjection\nCompound Sodium Lactate Injection is a sterile solution\ncontaining 0.24 per cent v/v of Lactic Acid (equivalent to\n0.32 per cent w/v ofsodium lactate) with 0.6 per cent w/v of\nSodium Chloride, 0.04 per cent w/v ofPotassium Chloride and\n0.027 per cent w/v ofCalcium Chloride in Water for Injections.\nCompound Sodium LaCtate Injection contains not less than\n0.27 per cent and not more than 0.32 per cent w/v ofsodium,\nNa, not less than 0.019 per cent and not more than 0.022 per\ncent w/v ofpotassium, K, not less than 0.37 per cent and not\nmore than 0.42 per cent w/v oftotal chloride, CI, not less than\n0.025 per cent and not more than 0.029 per cent wIv ofcalcium\nchloride, CaCh,2H20, and not less than 0.23 per cent and not\nmore than 0.28 per cent w/v oflactate, calculated as C3H60 3•\nCategory. Fluid and electrolyte replenisher.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/concentrated-hydrochloric-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Concentrated Hydrochloric Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Concentrated Hydrochloric Acid\nHydrochloric Acid contains not less than 35.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 38.0 per cent w/w of HC1.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (acidifying agent).\nDescription, A clear, colourless, filming liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/concentrated-vitamin-d-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Concentrated Vitamin D Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Concentrated Vitamin D Solution\nConcentrated Vitamin D Solution is a solution of \nCholecalciferol or Ergocalciferol in an edible vegetable oil. It \nmay contain suitable stabilising agents such as antioxidants.\nConcentrated Vitamin D Solution contains not less than 10,000 \nUnits of vitamin D per g and not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent ofthe stated number of Units of \nvitaminD.\nCategory. Antirachitic vitamin.\nDescription. Apale yellow to yellow, oily liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/concern","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Concern"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Concern \n(PPrn) \nBenzene \t\n2 \t Carcinogen \nCarbon Tetrachloride \t\n4 \t\nToxic and environmental\n* Not readily detected by head-space injection gas chromatography.\nOther appropriate procedures should be used for control purposes.\nOptions for describing limits of class 2 solvents-Two \noptions are available when setting limits for Class 2 solvents.\nThe integrity of an assembled sterilising filter should be \nverified before use and confirmed after use by carrying out \ntests appropriate to the type of filter used and the stage of \ntesting, e.g., bubble-point, pressure hold or diffusion rate tests.\nhazard \n1, 2-Dichloroethane \t\n5 \t\nToxic \n1, 1-Dichloroethene \t\n8 \t\nToxic \n1, 1, 1-Trichloroethane \t\n1500 \t\nEnvironmental hazard\nOption 1. The concentration limits in ppm stated in Table 2 \ncan be used. They were calculated using equation (1) below \nby assuming a product mass of 10 g administered daily.\nDue to the potential additional risks of the filtration method as \ncompared with other sterilisation processes, a prefiltration \nthrough a bacteria-retentive filter (e.g. 0.45 1 m pore size) may \nbe necessary in cases where a low bioburden cannot be \nensured by other means.\nClass 2 solvents - These solvents are associated with less \nsevere toxicity but should be limited in pharmaceutical \nproducts for the protection of consumers from potential \nadverse effects. Concentration limits shown in Table 2 are \napplicable if any solvent of this class is used.\n1000 \n   x PDE \nConcentration (ppm)  -\ndose \nHere, PDE is given in terms of mg/day and dose is given in \ng per day.\nThese limits are considered acceptable for all substances, \nexcipients, or products. Therefore this option may be applied \nif the daily dose is not known or fixed. If all excipients and \ndrug substances in a formulation meet the limits given in Option\n5.4. Residual Solvents\nTable 2\nResidual solvents are defined as organic volatile impurities \nthat may remain in active pharmaceutical substances, \nexcipients, or medicinal products after processing. During the \nmanufacturing processes, the solvents are not completely \nremoVed....Th6: solvents may be used to improve the yield in \nthe synth is of active pharmaceutical substances besides\nSolvent \nPDE \n(mg/day)\nConcentration\nLimit (ppm)\n1, then these components may be used in any proportion. No \nfurther calculation is necessary provided the daily dose does \nnot exceed 10 -g.. Products that are administered in doses more\nAcetonitrile\n4.1\nChlorobenzene\n3.6\nChloroform\n0.6\n10: per day should be considered under Option 2.\nOption 2. It is not considered necessary for each component \nof the drug product to comply with the limits given in Option\n1. The PDE in terms of mg per day as stated in Table 2 can be \nused with the known maximum daily dose and equation (1) \nabove to determine the concentration of residual solvent \nallowed in a drug product. Such limits are considered acceptable \nprovided that it has been demonstrated that the residual\nsolvent has been reduced to the practical minimum. The limits \nshould be realistic in relation to analytical precision, \nmanufacturing capability, reasonable variation in the \nmanufacturing process, and the limits should reflect \ncontemporary manufacturing standards.\nOption 2 may be applied by adding the amounts of a residual \nsolvent present in each of the components of the drug product. \nThe sum of the amounts of the solvent per day should be less \nthan that given by the PDE.\nConsider an example of the use of Option 1 and Option 2 \napplied to acetonitrile in a drug product. The permitted daily \nexposure to acetonitrile is 4.1 mg/day; thus the Option 1 limit \nis 410 ppm. The maximum administered daily mass of a drug \nproduct is 5.0 g , and the drug product contains two excipients. \nThe composition of the drug product and the calculated \nmaximum content of residual acetonitrile are given in the \nfollowing table.\nDaily \nexposure\nAcetonitrile\nComponent \t\nAmount in \nformulation\ncontent\nDrug substance \n0.3 g \n800 "}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/conjugate-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Conjugate Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Conjugate Vaccine\nHaemophilus influenzae Type b Conjugate Vaccine is a liquid \nor freeze-dried preparation of a polysaccharide, derived from \na suitable strain of Haemophilus influenzae Type b, \ncovalently bound to a carrier protein. The polysaccharide, \npolyribosylribitol phosphate, referred to as PRP, is a linear \ncopolymer composed of repeated units of 3-£-D- \nribofuronosyl-( 1 -> 1 )-ribitol-5-phosphate [(C।0H19O12P)n], with \na defined molecular size. The carrier protein, when conjugated \nto PRP, is capable of inducing a T-cell-dependent B-cell immune \nresponse to the polysaccharide.\nGeneral provisions\nThe production method shall have been shown to yield \nconsistently Haemophilus influenzae Type b conjugate \nvaccines of adequate safety and immunogenicity in humans. \nThe production method is validated to demonstrate that the \nproduct, if tested, would comply with the test for safety and \nefficacy of Vaccines. The stability of the final lot and relevant \nintermediates is evaluated using one or more indicator tests. \nSuch tests may include determination of molecular size, \ndetermination of free PRP in the conjugate and the\n4395\nwww.webofpharma.com\nHAEMOPHILUS INFLUENZAE TYPE b CONJUGATE VACCINE\nimmunogenicity test on mice. Taking account of the results of \nthe stability testing, release requirements are set for these \nindicator tests to ensure that the vaccine will be satisfactory \nat the end of the period of validity."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/connection-to","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Connection to"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Connection to \nBurette\n150\n100\n(D4nenrions in min unliss o0erivise stated) \nFig'2.3.33- I : Apparatus for AssEw*Oxygen\n14$\n2.3.31. NITRITE TITRATION \t\nIP 2018 \nIP 2018\n2.3.34. OXYGEN-FLASK METHOD\nThe weight, in mg, of the substance to which each ml of 0. 1 M \nsodium nitrite is equivalent is as stated in the individual \nmonograph.\nconnected to a vertical capillary arm to form a four-way \njunction. The descending arm of the junction is connected to\n2.3.33. Assay of Oxygen\nApparatus\na condenser (C) of about 60-m1 capacity, and the right arm of \nthe junction is connected to a mercury manometer (M). Tap D \non the upper vertical arm of the junction opens to the air. The \nlower part of the gas burette is fitted with a one-way tap \nconnected by a rubber tube to a mercury reservoir. The upper \npart of the burette is graduated from 0 to 5 ml, and the lower \npart from 99.5 to 100.5 ml, both in increments of 0.1 ml.\nFor the assay of Tablets of the sulfonamides or other drugs, \nreduce not less than 20 tablets to a fine powder, equivalent to \nabout 500 mg if a sulfonamide, or the quantity of drug specified \nin the individual monograph, and proceed as directed in the \nforegoing, beginning with \"transfer to a suitable open vessel\".\nThe apparatus shown in Fig 2.3.33-1 comprises a gas burette of \nthe type described under assay of nitrous oxide (Fig 2.3.32-1) \nin which tube B is connected to a gas pipette comprising two \nbulbs of suitable size.\nMethod\nFor the assay of Injections and other liquid forms where the \nnitrite titration is specified, pipet a portion, equivalent to about \n500 mg if a sulfonamide, or the quantity of drug specified in \nthe individual monograph, into a suitable open vessel, and \nproceed as directed in the foregoing, beginning with \"Add \n20 ml of hydrochloric acid'.\nCharge the pipette with the reagents specified in the \nmonograph. With the two-way tap open to tube B, draw the \nsolution just to the level of the tap by moving the mercury \nreservoir. Open the two-way tap to tube A, completely fill the \nburette and tube A with mercury and close the two-way tap. \nConnect a rubber tube to the exit valve of the cylinder of the \ngas under examination through a suitable pressure reducing \ndevice and pass a current of the gas through the tube for\nMethod\nClose the three taps and immerse the condenser in liquid \nnitrogen, keeping the level slightly above the upper part of \nthe condenser. By manipulating the two-way tap and the mobile \nreservoir create a partial vacuum in the apparatus, choosing \nan arbitrary pressure, P„, between 6.7 and 8 kPa (50 to 60 torr), \naccurately measured. This pressure must remain constant for\nNOTE—It will be necessary to adjust the sensitivity of the \ngalvanometer or the applied voltage before the titration is\nbegun in order to obtain an adequate deflection at the end-\npoint.\n1 minute.\n10 minutes to demonstrate that the apparatus is gas-tight.\nOpen the two-way tap to tube A and completely fill the burette \nand tube A with mercury. Close the two-way tap. Connect a \nrubber tube through a suitable pressure-relieving device to \nthe exit valve of the cylinder of the gas under examination and \npass a current of the gas through the rubber tube for 1 minute. \nWhilst the gas is still flowing, connect the rubber tube to the \nend of tube A and immediately open the two-way tap to tube \nA. Allow the specified volume of the gas to enter the burette \nby lowering the mercury reservoir. Disconnect the rubber tube \nand expel the gas from the burette by slowly raising the mercury \nreservoir above the capillary tube. Allow the specified quantity \nof the gas under examination to enter the burette by lowering \nthe mercury reservoir and ensure that the pressure of the gas \nis equal to atmospheric pressure. Close the two-way tap.\nWhilst the gas is still flowing, connect the rubber tube to tube \nA, immediately open the two-way tap to tube A, allow the \nspecified quantity of the gas to enter the burette by lowering \nthe mercury reser"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/coriander-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Coriander Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Coriander Oil\nCoriander Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nof the fruits of Coriandrum sativum (Fam. Apiaceae).\nCoriander Oil contains not less than 65.0 per cent and not \nmore than 78.0 per cent of linalool, not less than 3.0 per cent \nand not more than 7.0 per cent of cc-pinene, and not less than \n4.0 per cent and not more than 6.0 percent of camphor.\nCategory. Carminative, j vara, deepan-pacahn.\nDescription. Aclear, colourless pale yellow liquid, free from \nsediment, suspended matter, characteristic, spicy and odour \nrecalling that of linalool. \nL? •\nA. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of the oil under \nexamination in ethanol (95 per cent).\nReference solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution.of coriander \noil IPRS m ethanol (95 per cent). ...... \n:\nUse chromatographic system described in the Assay.\nThe peaks in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncorresponds to the peaks in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution \n' \"\nB. \nFlash point (2.4.44).-54.0° to 58.0°.\nRelative density (2.4.29). 0.862 to 0.878.\nRefractive index (2.4.27). 1.462 to 1.470.\n4209\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/corn-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Corn Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Corn Oil\n, 01 OH is the refined fixed oil obtained from the embryo of\na mays Linne (Fam. Gramineae).\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Aclear, light yellow-coloured, oily liquid with a \nfaint characteristic odour and slightly nutty, sweet taste \nresembling cooked sweet com."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/correction","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Correction"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Correction \nretention time \t\nfactor\nVoriconazole impurity A' \t\n0.25 \t0.7\nCategory. Antifungal.\nVoriconazole impurity C 2\n0.3 \t 0.7\nVoriconazole impurity B 3 \t\n0.6 \t 2.1\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cortisol-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortisol Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cortisol Acetate\nHydrocortisone Acetate is II [fl 7 a-di hydroxy- \n3,20- dioxopregn-4-en-21-yl acetate.\nHydrocortisone Acetate contains not less than 96.0 per cent \nand not more than 104.0 per cent of C23H32O6, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cortisol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortisol"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cortisol\nOH\n-OH\no\nMol. Wt. 362.5\nHydrocortisone is ll~,17a ,2l-trihydroxypregn-4-ene-3,20-\ndione.\nHydrocortisone contains not less than 96.0 per cent and not\nmore than 104.0 per cent of CZIH300S, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nDose. Orally, in the treatment of adrenocortical insufficiency,\n20 to 30 mg daily, in divided doses; by intramuscular injection\nor by slow intravenous injection or infusion, 100 to 500 mg, 3\nto 4 times in 24 hours or as required.\nDescription. A white to practically white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cortisone-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortisone Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cortisone Acetate\nC23H30O6 \nMol. Wt 402.5\nCortisone Acetate is 17a,21-dihydroxypregn-4-ene-\n3,11,20- trione 21-acetate.\nCortisone Acetate contains not less than 96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 104.0 per cent of C23H30O6, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory, Adrenocortical steroid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cortisone-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cortisone Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cortisone Injection\nCortisone Acetate Injection"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cottonseed-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cottonseed Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cottonseed Oil\nCottonseed Oil is the refined fixed oil obtained from the seed \nof cultivated plants of various varieties of Gossypium hirsutum \nLinne or of other species of Gossypium (Fam. Malvaceae). It\nmay contain suitable antioxidants.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. APale yellow or bright golden yellow-coloured, \nclear oily liquid. It is odourless, or nearly so, with a bland,\nnutty taste. At temperatures below 10° particles of solid fat \nmay separate from the oil, and at about -5° to 0° the oil becomes \nsolid or ncarly so. If it solidifies, the oil should be remelted \nand thoroughly mixed before use."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cream\nHydrocortisone Cream contains Hydrocortisone Acetate in a \nsuitable cream base.\nHydrocortisone Cream contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of hydrocortisone acetate, C23H32O6.\nUsual strengths. 1 per cent; 2.5 per cent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cresol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cresol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cresol\nCresol is a mixture of cresols and other phenols obtained from \ncoal tar.\nCategory. Disinfectant; pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial \npreservative).\nDescription. An almost colourless to pale brownish-yellow \nliquid, becoming darker on keeping or on exposure to light; \nodour, resembling that of phenol but more tarry."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/croscarmellose-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Croscarmellose Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Croscarmellose Sodium\nCroscarmellose sodium (cross-linked sodium carboxymethyl \ncellulose) is the sodium salt of a. cross-linked, partly \nO-carboxymethylated cellulose.\nCategory. Excipient.\nDescription. Awhite or greyish-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/croscarmellose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Croscarmellose"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Croscarmellose\nsodium\n(cross-linked\nsodium\ncarboxymethylcellulose) is the sodium salt of a cross-linked,\npartly O-carboxymethylated cellulose.\nCategory. Excipient.\nDescription. A white or greyish-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/crospovidone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Crospovidone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Crospovidone\nl-Ethenyl-2-pyrrolidinone homopolymer; 1-Vinyl- \n2-pyrrolidinone homopolymer\n(C6H)NO)„ \nMol. Wt. (Ill .If\nCrospovidone is a water-insoluble synthetic crosslinked \nhomopolymer ofW-vinyl-2-pyrrolidinone.\nCrospovidone contains not less than 11.0 per cent and not \nmore than 12.8 per cent of nitrogen (N), calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Excipient.\nDescription. A white to creamy white hygroscopic powder \nhaving a faint odour."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/crotamiton-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Crotamiton Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Crotamiton Cream\nCrotamiton Cream contains Crotamiton in a suitable cream\nbase.\nCrotamiton Cream.contains not less than 93.0 per cent and not \nmore than 107.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of crotamiton, \ncbh17no. ■ ■ ■■ / / \n■ \n7\nUsual strength. 10 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/crotamiton","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Crotamiton","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Crotamiton\nCl3HI7NO\nMol. Wt. 203.3\nCrotamiton is (£)-?V-Ethyl-W-(2-methylphenyl)-2-butenamide.\nCrotamiton is the sum of the (E)- and (Z)-isomers contains not \nless than 96.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per cent, and \n(Z)-isomer is not more than 15.0 per cent.\nCategory. Scabicide and pediculicide.\nDescription. A colourless or pale yellow, oily liquid.\n1967\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCROTAMITON"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cumin-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cumin Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cumin Oil\nJeera ka tail\nCumin Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation of \nthe fruits or seeds of Cumihum cyminum L. (Fam. Apiaceae).\nCumin Oil contains not less than 18.0 per cent and not more \nthan 30.0 per cent of cuminic aldehyde, not less than 14.0 per \ncent and hot more than 18.0 per cent oi'72-mcntha-1,3-diene-7- \nal, not less than 1.1 per cent and not more than 2.0 per cent of \np-mentha-1,4-dieneo-7-al, not less than 12.0 per cent and not\nmore than 16.0 per cent of p-pinene, not less than 20.0 per cent \nand not more than 28.0 per cent of v-terpinene and not less \nthan 10.0 per cent and not more than 15.0 per cent ofp-cymene.\nDescription. Aclear, light yellow to dark ycllow liquid, free \nfrom sediment and suspended matter."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyanocobalamin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyanocobalamin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyanocobalamin Injection\nVitamin B12 Injection\nCyanocobalamin Injection is a sterile solution of \nCyanocobalamin in Water for Injections containing sufficient \nacetic acid or hydrochloric acid to adjusted to pH about 4. It \nmay contain suitable buffering agents.\nCyanocobalamin Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0;per,cent, of the stated amount of \nanhydrous cyanocobalamin, C63H8SCo'N uQnP.\nUsual strength.rThe equivalent of 500 pg and 1 mg of \nanhydrous cyanocobalamiri per ml. \n:\nWhen examined in the range 260 nm to 610 nm, a 0.0025 percent \nw/v solution shows absorption maxima at about 278 rim, 361 nm \nand 547 nm id 559 nm. The ratio of the absorbance at about \n361 nm to that at about 547 nm to 559 nm is 3 ■ !5 to’ 3.45 arid the \nratio of the absorbance at about 361 nm to that at about 278 nm \nis 1.70 to 1.90(2.4.7).\npH (2.4.24). 3.8 to 5.5.\nRelated substances. Determine -by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). * \nr. . ■ .\nNOTE- - The following solutions should? be used within\n1 hour of preparation.\nTest solution (a). Dilute a suitable volume of the injection, if \nnecessary, with the mobile phase to produce a solution \ncontaining 0.0001 per cent w/v of Cyanocobalamin.\n4069\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCYANOGOBALAMIN INJECTION\nTest solution (b). Dilute asuitable volume of the injection, if\nnecessary, withthemobile phase; to produce al solution \ncontaining 0.05 per cent w/v of Cyanoeobalamin. \ne . v !\nReference solution: (d). Dilute a suitable volume of the\ninjection, if necessary, with the mobile phase to produce a \nsolution containing 0.003 per cent w/'v of Cyanocobalamin.\nReferencesolution (b).Add 1 ml of a 0.I per cent w/v solution \nof chloramineT arid- 0.1 ml of 0:05 M hydrochloric acid Id a \nvolume containing 5 mg of Cyanocobalamin, dilute to l 0 ini \nwith water, shake and allow to stand for 5 minutes. Dilute 2 ml \neA the solution to 16 ml with the mobile, phase and. use \nimmediately. ; \n..\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4 mm, packed; with\noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 147 volumes of al .0 per cent\nw/v solution of disodium hydrogen phosphate and \n53 volumes of methanol, adjusted to pH 3.5 with \northophosphoric acid (to be used within 2 days of \npreparation), \n.-jv- 7 VT'A: A y .f.\n- flow rate: 0.8 ml per minute,\n• \n- spectrophotometer set at 361 rim, \ninjection volume: 20 pl. \n. ;\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between two principal peaks is not less than 2.5.\nInject reference solution (a), test solutions (a) and (b). Run \nthe chromatogram 3 times the retention time of the principal \npeak. In the chromatogram obtained with test solution (b), the \nsum of the areas of al 1 the secondary peaks is not more than \nthe area of the principal, peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (a). Ignore any peak with an area less \nthan that of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith test solution (a). \n-A; ...■ y ■, A\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3), Not more than 0.4 Endotoxin Unit \nper pg of cyanocobalamin.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections). .;; ;i ...\nAssay.; Carry out. the following procedure protected front \nlight. \n.■ \"A. A w .;\nDilute the injection, if necessary, with water to produce a \nsolution containing not more than the equivalent of \n0.0025 per cent w/v of anhydrous cyanocobalamin and measure \nthe absorbance at the maximum at about 361 nm (2.4.7). \nCalculate the content of C63HS8CoN 14OI4P taking:207 as the \nspecific absorbance at 361 nm. \n; : _\nStorage. Store protected from light in single dose or multiple \ndose containers. \n> r , \n' r\nLabelling. The label states the strength in terms oT the\n. equivalent amount of anhydrous cyanocobalamin in a suitable\ndose-volume. \n'A A/ www /.\n4070\nww.webofpharma.com\nwww.webofpharma.com\nC28H44O \n; ; Mol.Wt. 3967\nErgocalciferol is (3p,5Z,7E,22E)-9,10-Secoergosta-5,7,10(19), \n22"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyanocobalamin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyanocobalamin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyanocobalamin\nH2NOCH2CH2y\nH2NOCH2C\nHsC'\nHsC'\nMol. Wt. 1355.4\nCyanocobalamin is Coa-[a-(5,6-dimethylbenzimidazolyl)]-\nCo~-cyanocobamide.\nCyanocobalamin contains not less than 96.0 per cent and\n....not-more-than·l02:0··per·cent-ofC6jHliliCoNi:iOi4P;calculated--\non the dried basis.\nCategory. B-group Vitamin; haematoiJoietic.\nDose. In the treatment of megaloblastic anaemia, by\nintramuscular injection, 1 to 2 mg, in divided doses, in the\nfIrst week. Subsequent doses, 250 Ilg weekly until the blood\ncount is normal, maintenance dose, 250 Ilg every 3 or 4\nweeks;\nDescription. Adarkred, crystalline powder; very hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyclizine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyclizine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyclizine Hydrochloride\nC18H22N2,HC1 \nMol. Wt 302.8\nCyclizine Hydrochloride is l-(diphenylmethyl)-4- \nmethylpiperazine hydrochloride.\nCyclizine Hydrochloride contains not less thari 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci8H22N2,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiemetic.\nDescription. Awhite, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyclopentolate-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyclopentolate Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyclopentolate Hydrochloride\nCi7HsNO3,HCl \nMol.Wt.327.8\nCyclopentolate Hydrochloride is 2-(dimethylamino)ethyl \na-(l-hydroxycyclopentyl)(phenyl)acetate hydrochloride.\nCyclopentolate Hydrochloride contains.not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 10L5rper cent of C17H25NO3,HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticholinergic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline ponder. It \nshowspolymorphism(2.5.11).'"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyclophosphamide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyclophosphamide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyclophosphamide Injection\nCyclophosphamide Injection is asterile material consisting of \nCyclophosphamide with or without auxiliary substances. It is \nfilled in a sealed container. \n...........\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted, solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The,constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCyclophosphamide Injection contains not less than 92.5 per­\ncent and not more than 107.5 per cent ofthe stated amount of \ncyclophosphamide, C7H15CI2N2O2P.\nUsual strengths. 100 mg; 200 mg; 500 mg; 1 g.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated- under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyclophosphamide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyclophosphamide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyclophosphamide Tablets\nCyclophosphamide Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nanhydrous cyclophosphamide, C7]]|sC17N7()7P.The tablets are \ncoated.\nUsual strengths. 10 mg; 50 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyclophosphamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyclophosphamide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyclophosphamide\n[ \n' n ,H2O\nC7H15C12N2O2P,H2O \n/ . \nMol.Wt.279J\nCyclophosphamide is (l?5)-2-bis(2-chloro-ethyl) amino- \nperhydro-1,3,2-oxazaphosphorinane 2-oxide monohydrate.\n1978\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nCYCLOPHOSPHAMIDE\nlonhosphamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nre than 102.0 per cent of CyH^CV^C^P, calculated on \nnoti»OIV\nthe anhydro basis.\nCategory-Anticancer\npcscrjption. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cycloserine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cycloserine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cycloserine\nA \n< NH\nh2n' xo\nC3H6N2O2 \nMol. Wt. 102.1\nCycloserine is (Z?)-4-aminoisoxazolidin-3-ohe, an antimicrobial \nsubstance produced by the growth of certain strains of \nStreptomyces orchidaceous or S. garyphalus or obtained by \nsynthesis.\nCycloserine contains not less than 98:0 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of C3H6N2O2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or pale yellow, crystalline powder; \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyclosporine-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyclosporine Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyclosporine Eye Drops\nCyclosporine Eye Drops is a sterile solution of Cyclosporine \nin a suitable vehicle.\nCyclosporine Eye Drops contain not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncyclosporine, C62HinN]iOi2.\nUsual strengths. 0.05 per cent w/v; 0.1 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyclosporine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyclosporine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyclosporine Injection\nCyclosporine Injection is a sterile solution of Cyclosporine in \na suitable vehicle.\nCyclosporine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncyclosporine, C62H111N!1O]2.\nUsual strengths. 50 mg per ml; 100 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyproheptadine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyproheptadine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyproheptadine Hydrochloride\n&1H2IN,HCl,r/2H2O\nMol. Wt. 350.9\nCyproheptadine Hydrochloride is 4-(5/-/-clibenzo[a7</]- \ncyclohepten-5-ylidene)-l-methylpiperidine hydrochloride \nsesquihydrate.\nCyproheptadine Hydrochloride, contains not less than \n98.5 per cent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C21H3)N,HC1, \ncalculated on anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Histamine H!-receptor antagonist.\nDescription. Awhite or slightly yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyproheptadine-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyproheptadine Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyproheptadine Syrup\nCyproheptadine Hydrochloride Syrup\nCyproheptadine Syrup contains not. less than 90.0 per cen! \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncyproheptadine hydrochloride, C-ThiKHCl.\nUsual strength. 2 mg in 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cyproterone-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cyproterone Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cyproterone Acetate\nC24H29CIO4 \nMol. Wt. 416.9\nCyproterone Acetate is (1/?, 2/7)-6-chloro-l,2-dihydro-17- \nacetyloxy-3 ’/./-cyclopropaf 1,2]pregna-l ,4,6-triene- \n3,20-dione.\nCyproterone Acetate contains not less than 97.0 per cent and \nriot more than 103.0 per cent of C24H29CIO4, calculatedbri the \ndriedbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cytarabine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cytarabine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cytarabine Injection\nCytarabine Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nCytarabine with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed\ncontainer.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nCytarabine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ncytarabine, C9H43N3O5.\nUsual strengths. 100 mg per vial; 500 mg per vial; 1 g per vial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/cytarabine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Cytarabine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Cytarabine\nP-Cytosine Arabinoside \n..\nC9H13N3O5 \nMol.Wt.243.2\nCytarabine is 1-p-D-arabinofuranosylcytosine.\nCytarabine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nCA UTION:— Cytarabine is very poisonous. Great care should \nbe taken to avoid inhaling the particles of cytarabine and \nexposing the skin to the dried substance."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dacarbazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dacarbazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dacarbazine Injection\nDacarbazine Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nDacarbazine with or without excipients. It is filled in a sea'ed \ncontainer.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nclarity of solution and particulate matter stated under \nparenteral preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter'preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nDacarbazine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndacarbazine, C6HI0N6O. :\nUsual strengths. 100 mg per vial; 200 mg per vial.\nDescription. A white or pale yellow powder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under parenteral preparations (Powders \nfor Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/daclatasvir-dihydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Daclatasvir Dihydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Daclatasvir Dihydrochloride\nC4oH50N8O6^HCI \nMol. Wt. 811.8\nDaclatasvir Dihydrochloride is Methyl(( 15)-1 -(((25)-2-(5-(4- \n(2-((25)-l-((2S)-2-((methoxycarbonyl)amino)-3- \nmethylbutanoyl)-2-pyrrolidinyl)-l/f-imidazol-5-yl)-4- \nbiphenylyl)-177-imidazol-2-yl)-l-pyrrolidinyl) carbonyI)-2- \nmethylpropyl) carbamate dihydrochloride.\nDaclatasvir Dihydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent, of C40HSoNs06,HC1 \ncalculated on the anhydrous and solvent free basis. .\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription. A White to yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dalteparin-sodium-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dalteparin Sodium Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dalteparin Sodium Injection\nNitrite. Not more than 5 ppm.\nDalteparin Sodium Injection is a sterile solution of Dalteparin \nSodium in a suitable diluent.\nDetermine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nThe estimated activity of anti-factor Xa is not less than 90 per \ncent and not more than 110 per cent of the stated activity.\nInject reference solutions (c) and (e). The test is not valid \nunless the correlation factor for a linear relationship between \nconcentration and response for reference solutions (c), (d) \nand (e) is not less than 0.995 and the signal-to-noise ratio for \nreference solution (c) is not less than 5.\nNOTE --- Rinse all volumetric flasks at least three time\nwater before the preparation of the solutions."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/danazol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Danazol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Danazol\nDanazol is 17a-pregna-2,4-diene-20-yno[2,3-^|isoxazol-17’Ol.\nDanazol contains not less than 97,0 per cent and not more \nthan 102,0 per cent of C22H27NO2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antigonadotrophin.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dapoxetine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dapoxetine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dapoxetine Hydrochloride\nDapoxetine Hydrochloride monohydrate\nC2 iHtjNOjHCI \nMol wt. 341.9\nDapoxetine Hydrochloride is (7?S)-lV,7V-Dimethyl- \n3-(I-naphthyloxy)-l -phenylpropylamine hydrochloride.\nDapoxetine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent\nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C2iH23NO,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Selective serotonin re-uptake inhibitor (SSRI).\nDescription. Awhite crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dapoxetine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dapoxetine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dapoxetine Tablets\nDapoxetine Hydrochloride Tablets.\nDapoxetine Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndapoxetine, C^H^NC).\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 30 mg; 60 mg of \ndapoxetine."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dapsone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dapsone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dapsone\nCi2Hi2N2O2S \nMol. Wt. 248.3\nDapsone is the bis(4-aminophenyl)sulphone.\nDapsone contains not less, than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C12Hj2N2O2S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antileprotic.\nDescription. A white or creamy-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/darifenacin-hydrobromide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Darifenacin Hydrobromide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Darifenacin Hydrobromide\nC28H3()N2O2,HBr \nMol. Wt. 507.5\nDarifenacin Hydrobromide is (3S)-l-[2-(2,3- \ndihydrobenzofuran-5-yl)ethyl]-3-pyrrolidnyl}-2,2- \ndiphenylacetamide. hydrobromide..\nDarifenacin Hydrobromide contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C28H3oN202,HBr, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis. \n. .\nCategory. Antispasmodic.\nDescription. Awhite to almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/daruharidra-roots-and-stems","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Daruharidra Roots and Stems"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Daruharidra Roots and Stems"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/darunavir-ethanolate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Darunavir Ethanolate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Darunavir Ethanolate\nC27H37N3O7S C2H5OH \nMol. Wt. 593.7\nDarunavir Ethanolate is [(15,27?)-3-[[4-aminophenyl) sulfonyl] \n(2-methylpropyl)amino]-2-hydroxy-l-(phenylmethyl)propyl]- \ncarbamic acid (37?, 3 a.S', 6a7?)-hexahydrofuro [2,3-b] furan-3-yl \nester monoethanolate.\nDarunavir Ethanolate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than.102.0 per cent of C27H37N3O7S, calculated on \nanhydrous and ethanol free basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDescription. A white to off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/daunorubicin-hydrochloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Daunorubicin Hydrochloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Daunorubicin Hydrochloride Injection\nDescription. A crystalline, orange-red powder, hygroscopic.\nDaunorubicin Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nDaunorubicin Hydrochloride and Mannitol, with or without \nbuffering agents and other excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/daunorubicin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Daunorubicin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Daunorubicin Hydrochloride\nC^HjqCINOk, \nMol. Wt. 564.0\nDaunorubicin Hydrochloride is (8S-cis)-8-acetyl-\n10-[(3-amino-2,3,6-trideoxy-a-L-Zyxo-hexopyranosyl)oxy]- \n7,8,9, lO-tetraliydro-6,8,11-trihydroxy-l-methoxy- \n5,12-naphthacenedione hydrochloride.\nDaunorubicin Hydrochloride contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C27H3oClNOio, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A crystalline, orange-red powder, hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/daunorubicin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Daunorubicin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Daunorubicin Injection\nDaunorubicin Hydrochloride Injection\nDaunorubicin Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nDaunorubicin Hydrochloride and Mannitol, with or without \nbuffering agents and other excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirement for \nthe Appearance of solution and Particulate matter stated \nunder Parenteral Preparations (Injections).\nUsual strength. 20 mg.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDAUNORUBICIN INJECTION\nStorage. Store protected from light:\nDaunorubicin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent\nand not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ndaunorubicin, C27H39NO10.\nDeferasirox"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/decimal","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Decimal"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Decimal \nPer cent w/w \n(PPb) \n(ppm)\nQuantity \t\nName of \tSymbol \nsupplementary \nSI units\n1 \n0.001 \n0.001 \n1/1000000000 \n0.000000001 \n0.0000001 per cent \n10 \n0.01 \n0.01 \n1/100000000 \n0.00000001 \n0.000001 per cent \n100 \n0.1 \n0.1 \n1/10000000 \n0.0000001 \n0.00001 per cent\nPlane angle \t\nradian \t\nrad\nSolid angle \t\nstreradian \tsr\nI(XX) \nI \n1 \n1/1000000 \n0.000001 \n0.0001 per cent\n50(X) \n5 \n5 \n5/1000000 \n0.000005 \n0.0005 per cent\nI(XX)0 \n10 \n10 \n1/100000 \n0.00001 \n0.001 per cent \n20 \n20 \n2/100000 \n0.00002 \n0.002 per cent \n50 \n50 \n5/100000 \n0.00005 \n0.005 per cent\n100 \n100 \n1/10000 \n0.0001 \n0.01 per cent \n200 \n200 \n2/10000 \n0.0002 \n0.02 per cent \n1000 \n1000 \n1/1000 \n0.001 \n0.1 per cent \n5000 \n5000 \n5/1000 \n0.005 \n0.5 per cent\nSI units \nbasic SI units \nother units\ngray \nGy \nM2 S- 2 \nI Gy =1 joule per kg\n7.3. Abbreviations and Symbols\nALI\nAnnual Limit of Intake\nATCC\nAmerican Type Culture Collection\nSpecific optical rotation at 2G1' .41..the wayelengtil of tho-D line,of,sodium\nL'LJ D\n1 kPa ----- 7.5 mm Hg = 7.5 torr\n•\nI \t,t • 2\n1 Bq = 2.7036 X 10.\" curies \n-eAwre-cA. \nzoFr\nSpecific optical rotation at 25.' at the wavelength octhe I) line of sodium\n.• \t\n• \t\n• - \n•\n-\nby \tBoiling point\nBS \tBritish Standard\nCurie\nCOA \t\nCertificate of Analysis\nDNA \t\nDeoxyribonucleic Acid\nED50 \tEffective Dose 50 (the dose of the preparation that will be effective in 50 per cent of the treated animals)\ng \t Gram\ng \t Acceleration due to gravity\nHIV \tHuman Immunodeficiency Virus\nID50 \tInfective Dose 50 ( the dose of the micro-organism that infects 50 per cent of the animals inoculated)\nIPRS \t\nIndian Pharmacopoeia Reference Substance\nIS \t Indian Standard\nISO \tInternational Organisation for Standardization\nIU \t International Unit"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/decolorising-charcoal","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Decolorising Charcoal"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Decolorising Charcoal \n. ? •\nActivated Charcoal is obtained from vegetable matter by \nsuitable carbonisation processes intended to confer a hrih \nadsorbing power.\nCategory. Adsorbent.\nDescription. A light, blackpowder, free from grittiness."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/decoquinate-premix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Decoquinate Premix"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Decoquinate Premix\nDecoquinate Premix contains not less ‘than 95.0 per cent'and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndecoquinate, C2,H;;NO,.\nUsual strength. 6.0 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/definitions","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Definitions"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Definitions\nestimate of slope of regression line of response\non log dose based on all preparations in the assay.\nb\nc'\nconstant used in evaluation of fiducial limits\n(Table 7)\nnumber of dose levels for each preparation in a\nbalanced assay\nd\ndegrees of freedom which is the number of\nindependent observations in a set of data.\nf\nIP 2010\n5.7. STATISTICAL ANALYSIS OF RESULTS\nh\nnumber of preparation in an assay, including the\nstandard preparation.\nk\nnumber of different treatments within an assay\n(k=dh)\nn\nnumber ofreplicates in each treatment.\nn'\nnumber of individual potency estimates\nestimate of variance given by error mean square\nin analysis ofvariance -\nalso used with a letter\nsubscript, e.g. SM2 is variance of log potency M\nestimate ofstandard deviation = square root of S2\nlow and high doses of standard preparation S\nmeasured in units ofactivity or weight.\nstudent's statistic (Table 1)\ndoses of test preparation 11 ... ooZ measured as\nspecified in the monographs\nweighing co-efficient in probit analysis (Table 16)\nw\ny\nindividual response or transformed response.\ny'\ncalculated response to replace as missing value.\n'mean responses for standard and unknown\npreparations\npotency assumed for test preparation 11 when\nmaking up doses.\ntotal response for each subject (1 to 2 n) in twin\ncross-over assay.\nincomplete total response in a block or row\ncontaining one missing value.\nB'\nstatistic used in calculation of fiducial limits\n(Equation 17)\ntotal response on day I or day II in a cross -over"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dehydroacetic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dehydroacetic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dehydroacetic Acid\nHSCI(0yO\nyYo\n°\nCHs\nMol. Wt. 168.1\nDehydroacetic Acid is a tautomeric mixture of 3-acetyl-\n6-methyl-2H-pyran-2,4(3H)-dione and 3-acetyl-4-hydroxy-\n6-methyl-2H-pyran-2-one\nDehydroacetic Acid contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 100.5 per cent of CSHS0 4, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial preservative).\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless or practically odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dehydroemetine-dihydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dehydroemetine Dihydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dehydroemetine Dihydrochloride\nC29H38N2O4,2HC1 \nMol. Wt. 551.6\nDehydroemetine Hydrochloride is 2,3-didehydro- \n6',7',10, 11 -tetramethoxyemetan dihydrochlofide\nDehydro emetine Hydrochloride contains not less than \n98.5 per cent and not more than 101.5 per cent of \nC39H3gN2O4,2HCl, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiamoebic.\nDescription. A white to yellowish-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dehydroemetine-hydrochloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dehydroemetine Hydrochloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dehydroemetine Hydrochloride Injection\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline pc  wder; \nodourless or practically odourless.\nDehydroemetine Injection is a sterile solution of \nDehydroemetine Hydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nC29F138N204,2HC1 \t\nMol. Wt. 551.6 \nDehydroemetine Hydrochloride is 2,3-didehydro- \n6',7',10, 11-tetramethoxyemetan dihydrochloride \nDehydroemetine Hydrochloride contains not less than \n98.5 per cent and not more than 101.5 per cent of \nC29H38N204,2HC1, calculated on the dried basis."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dehydroemetine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dehydroemetine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dehydroemetine Injection\nDehydroemetine Hydrochloride Injection\nDehydroemetine Injection is a sterile solution of \nDehydroemetine Hydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nDehydroemetine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent\nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndehydroemetine hydrochloride, C^HssNaO^HCl.\nUsual strength. 30 mg per ml.\nDescription. A clear solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/deltamethrin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Deltamethrin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Deltamethrin\nCxHBr,NO; \nMol: We. 50522\nDeltamethrin is (S)-c-cyano-3-phenoxybenzyl(1R,3R)- \n3-(2,2-dibromoviny)-2. 7 dimethyleyclopropanecarboxyiaie\nDeltamethrin contains not less than 97.0. per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of CaaHipBroNOs. \n:\nCategory. Insecticide.\nDescription. Awhite to butt: coloured, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dequalinium-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dequalinium Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dequalinium Chloride\nCH3 h3c\nC5G1 Li0Cl2N4 \nMol. Wt, 527.7\nDequalinium Chloride is 4,4'-diamino-2,2'-dimethyi- \nN, RT-decamethylenedi(quinolinium chloride).\nDequalinium Chloride contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C3oH4oC12N4, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Antiseptic.\nDescription. A creamy white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/desferrioxamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Desferrioxamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Desferrioxamine Injection\nDesferrioxamine Mesylate Injection; Desferrioxamine \nMesilate Injection\nDesferrioxamine Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nDesferrioxamine Mesylate with or without excipients. It is filled \nin a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nDesferrioxamine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndesferrioxamine mesylate, C25H48N6Os,CH4SO3.\nUsual strength. 0.5 g.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; very \nhygroscopic.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following \nrequirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/desferrioxamine-mesylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Desferrioxamine Mesylate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Desferrioxamine Mesylate\nDeferoxamine Mesylate; Deferoxamine Mesilate\nMol. Wt. 656.8\nDesferrioxarnine Mesylate is 30-arnino-3,14,25-trihydroxy-\n3,9,14,20,25-pentaazatriacontane-2,10,13,21,24-pentaone\nmethanesulphonate.\n1167\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/desmopressin-intranasal-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Desmopressin Intranasal Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Desmopressin Intranasal Solution\nDesmopressin Intranasal Solution is a solution of \nDesmopressin containing suitable buffering agents and \npreservatives.\nDesmopressin Intranasal Solution contains not less than 90.0 \nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount \nof the desmopressin, C46H64Ni4OJ2S2.\nUsual strength. 10 pg per ml.\nDescription. A colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/desmopressin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Desmopressin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Desmopressin\nSCH2CH2CO-Tyr-Phe-Gln-Asn-Cys-Pro-D-Arg-Gly~N^\nC45H54NJ4O12S2 \nMol. Wt. 1069 ?\nDesmopressin is 1 -Desamino,8-o-arginine vasopressin.\nDesmopressin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of C46H64Nl4O12S2, calculated on the \nanhydrous and acetic acid free basis.\nCategory. Antidiuretic hormone.\nDescription. A white to off-white, fluffy powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/desogestrel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Desogestrel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Desogestrel\nC22H30O \nMol. Wt. 310.5\nDesogestrel is 18,19-Dinorpregn^4-en-20-yn-17-ol,13-ethyH 1- \nmethylene-,( 17 a).\nDesogestrel contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C22H30O, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory . Steroidal hormone.\nDescription. A white or almost-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/desoxycortone-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Desoxycortone Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Desoxycortone Acetate\nDesoxycorticp sterone Acetate; Deoxycorticosterone \nAcetate; Deoxycortone Acetate\nDesoxycortone Acetate contains not less than 96.0 per cent \nand not more than 104.0 per cent of C23H32O4, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nDescription. A white or creamy-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dexamethasone-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dexamethasone Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dexamethasone Injection\nDexamethasone Sodium Phosphate Injection\nDexamethasone Injection is a sterile solution of \nDexamethasone Sodium Phosphate in Water for Injections.\nDexamethasone Injection contains not less than'95.0 per cent\nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndexamethasone phosphate, C22H30FO8P.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 4 mg of dexamethasone \nphosphate per ml. (4.4 mg of dexamethasone sodium \nphosphate is approximately equivalent to 4rmg of \ndexamethasone phosphate).\nDescription. A clear solution.."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dexamethasone-sodium-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dexamethasone Sodium Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dexamethasone Sodium Phosphate\nC22H28FNa2OsP \nMol. Wt. 516.4\ndexamethasone Sodium Phosphate is disodium 9a-fluoro-\n3,17 a-dihydroxy-16a-methyl-3,20-dioxo-1,4-pregnadien-21 - \nphosphate.\nDEXAMETHASONE SODIUM PHOSPHATE\nDexamethasone Sodium Phosphate contains not less than \n97.0 per cent and not more than 103.0 per cent of C22H28FNa2O8P, \ncalculated on the anhydrous, and ethanol-free or solvent-free \nbasis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid (anti-inflammatory).\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow, crystalline powder; \nvery hygroscopic. It shows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dexamethasone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dexamethasone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dexamethasone\nC22H29FO5 \nMol.Wt.392.5\nDexamethasone is 9a-fluoro-l ip,17a,21-trihydroxy-16a- \nmethyl-1,4-pregnadiene-3,20-dione.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDEXAMETHASONE\nI?2p22\ngexamethasone contains not less than 96.0 per cent and not\nfjjQfe than 104.0 per cent of C22H29FO5, calculated on the dried\nbasis\nCategory* Adrenocortical steroid (anti-inflammatory)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dexchlorpheniramine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dexchlorpheniramine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dexchlorpheniramine Oral Solution\nDexchlorpheniramine Maleate Oral Solution\nDexchlorpheniramine Oral Solution is a mixture consisting of \nDexchlorpheniramine Maleate with buffering agents and other \nexcipients. It contains a suitable flavouring agent. It is filled in \na sealed container.\n2051\nwww.webofpharma.com\nThe oral solution is constituted by. dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specific volume of water just before \nuse. -\nDexchlorpheniramine Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 \nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount \nof dexchlorpheniramine maleate, C (6H19CIN2 ,C4H4O4.\nUsual strength. 2 mg per 5 ml.\nStorage. Store the constituted solution in a refrigerator (2° to \n8°). Discard any unused portion after 30 days of reconstitution.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the test \nrequirements stated under Oral Liquids and with the \nfollowing requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dexlansoprazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dexlansoprazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dexlansoprazole\nC16Hl4F3N3O2S \nMol wt. 369.4 (anhydrous)\nC16Hi4F3N3O2S, 1 !4 H2O \nMol wt. 3 96.4 (sesquihydrate)\nDexlansoprazole is (+)-2-[[4-(2,2,2-Trifluoroethoxy)-3- \nmethylpyridin-2-yl]methylsulfrnyl]-l//-benzo[d]imidazoleorits \nsesquihydrate.\nDexlansoprazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of Gi6Hi4F3N3O2S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory, Antiulcerative.\nDescription. A white to brownish white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextran-1","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextran 1"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextran 1\nDextran 1 is a low molecular weight fraction of dextran, \nconsisting of a mixture of isomaltooligosaccharide.lt is \nobtained by controlled hydrolysis and fractionation of dextrans \nproduced by fermentation of Leuconostoc mesenteroides in\nrhe presence of sucrose. It is a gluco se polymer in . which the \nlinkages between glucose units are almost exclusively a-1,6.\nIts weight-average molecular weight is about 1000.\nCategory. Plasma substitute.\nDescription. A white to off white hygroscopic powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextran-110-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextran 110 Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextran 110 Injection\nDextran 110 Intravenous Infusion\nDextran 110 Injection is a sterile solution, in Dextrose Injection \nor in Sodiuhi Chloride Injection, bf dextrans of average \nmolecular weight of about 110,000, derived from the dextrans \nproduced by the fermentation of sucrose by means of a certain \nstrain of Leuconostoc mesenteroides. The. dextrans are. \npolymers of dextrose in which the linkages between, the \ndextrose units are almost entirely of the a -1—>6 type..\nDextran 110 Injection contains not less than 5.5 per-cent and\nnot more than 6.5 per .cent w/v of dextrans.\nCategory. Plasma substitute.\nDescription. An almost colourless, slightly viscous solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextran-40-infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextran 40 Infusion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextran 40 Infusion\nDextran 40 Injection; Dextran 40 Intravenous Infusion\nDextran 40 Infusion is a sterile solution containing Dextran 40 \nin Dextrose Injection or in Sodium Chloride Injection.\nDextran 40 Infusion contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110:0 per cent of the stated amount of dextran \n40.\nDescription. An almost colourless, slightly viscous solution.\nAcidity. Titrate 25.0 ml of infusion with 0.01 M sodium \nhydroxide using phenol red solution as indicator. Not more \nthan 2.0 ml of 0.01 Msodium hydroxide is required to neutralise \nthe solution.\nMolecular size. For solutions in Dextrose Injection, before \nproceeding with tests A, B and C add 4 volumes of ethanol\n(95 per cent), centrifuge and. dissolve the residue in a .volume \nof Sodium Chloride Injection sufficient to restore the original \nvolume.\nA. Determine the viscosity (2,4.28) ratios by Method A, using \nsize C U-tube viscometer at 37°, of solutions in saline solution \ncontaining about 3.5,2.5,1.5 and 0.75 per cent w/y of dextrans,\n2057\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDEXTRAN 40 INFUSION\naccurately determined. Calculate the viscosity ratio by dividing \nthe time taken for the meniscus to fall from E to F using the \nliquid being examined by the time taken using saline solution. \nFor each solution, plot (viscosity ratio - 1.00)/concentration \n(in per cent w/v) against concentration (in per cent w/v). The \nintercept on the viscosity axis of the straight line joining the \npoints represents the intrinsic viscosity. The intrinsic viscosity \nis0.16to0.20.\nB. Place in each of five stoppered flasks 100 ml of a solution in \nsaline solution containing 6 per cent w/v of dextrans and add \nslowly, with continuous stirring, sufficient ethanol to produce \na faint cloudiness (about 45 ml is usually required). Add 0.5,\n1.0,1.5,2.0 and 2.5 ml of ethanol to the separate flasks, stopper \nthe flasks and immerse in a water-bath at about 35° with \noccasional shaking until clear solutions are obtained. Transfer \nthe flasks to a water-bath maintained at25.0°±0.1° and allow \nto stand overnight or until two clear liquid phases are formed.\nReject the supernatant liquids, dissolve separately the syrupy \nresidues in sufficient saline solution to produce 25.0 ml, \nremove the ethanol by evaporation at a pressure of about 2 \nkPa, dilute to 25.0 ml with water and determine the optical \nrotation (2.4.22), From the optical rotations calculate the \namount of dextrans precipitated as described under Assay. \nChoose that fraction containing as nearly as possible but not \nmore than 10 per cent of the dextrans present in the injection \nand determine its intrinsic viscosity by the method described \nunder test A; the intrinsic viscosity is not more than 0.27.\nC. Place in each of four stoppered flasks 100 ml of a solution in \nsaline solution containing 6 per cent w/v of dextrans and add \nslowly, with continuous stirring, 80, 90, 100 and 110 ml \nrespectively of ethanol. Stopper the flasks, transfer to a water­\nbath maintained at 25.0° ±0.1° and allow standing overnight \nor until two clear liquid phases are formed. Separate the \nsupernatant solution from the syrupy residues. Remove the \nethanol from each supernatant solution separately by \nevaporation at a pressure of 2 kPa, dialyse, in cellophane \ntubing against water to remove sodium chloride, adjust the \nvolume to 25.0 ml with water, add sufficient sodium chloride \nto produce solutions containing 0.9 per cent w/v and determine \nthe optical rotation (2,4.22). From the optical rotations, calculate \nthe amounts of dextrans present as described under Assay.\nChoose that fraction containing as nearly as possible but not \nmore than 10 per cent of the dextrans present in the injection \nand determine the intrinsic viscosity, by the method in test A \nabove; the intrinsic viscosity is not less than 0.08.\nContent of dextrose///’present). 4.5 per cent to 5.5 per cent \nw/v.\nDilute 15.0 ml of infusion to 50.0 ml with water. To 5.0 ml in a \nstoppered flask, add 25 m"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextran-40-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextran 40 Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextran 40 Injection\nDextran 40 Intravenous Infusion\nDextran 40 Injection is a sterile solution, in Dextrose Injection\nor in Sodium Chloride Injection, of dextrans of average\nmolecular weight of about 40,000, derived from the dextrans\nproduced by the fermentation of sucrose by means ofa certain\nstrain of Leuconostoc mesenteroides. The dextrans are\npolymers of dextrose in which the linkages between the\ndextrose units are almost entirely of the a-I-76 type.\nDextran 40 Injection contains not less than 9.0 per cent and\nnot more than 11.0 per cent w/v of dextrans.\nCategory. Plasma substitute.\nDescription. An almost colourless, slightly viscous solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextran-40","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextran 40"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextran 40\nDextran 40 is a mixture of polysaccharides, principally Of \na-1,6-giucan type. Average relative molecular mass is ab0-G; \n40,000. It is obtained by hydrolysis and fractionation of clextra \nproduced by fermentation'of sucrose using strain of \nLeuconostoc mesenteroides.\nCategory. Plasma substitute.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextran-70-infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextran 70 Infusion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextran 70 Infusion\nDextran 70 Injection; Dextran 70 Intravenous Infusion\nDextran 70 Infusion is a sterile solution containing Dextran 70\nin Dextrose Injection or in Sodium Chloride Injection.\nDextran 70 Infusion contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of dextran \n70.\nDescription. An almost colourless, slightly viscous solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextran-70-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextran 70 Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextran 70 Injection\nDextran 70 Injection is a sterile solution, in Dextrose Injection\nor in Sodium Chloride Injection, of dextrans of average\nmolecular weight about 70,000, derived from the dextrans\nproduced by the fennentation ofsucrose by means ofa certain\nstrain of Leuconostoc mesenteroides. The dextrans are\npolymers of dextrose in which the linkages between the\ndextrose units are almost entirely of the a-I~6 type.\nDextran 70 Injection contains not less than 5.5 per cent and\nnot more than 6.5 per cent wlv of.dextrans...\nCategory. Plasma substitute.\nDescription. An almost colourless, slightly viscous solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextran-70","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextran 70"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextran 70\nDextran 70 is a mixture of polysaccharides, principally of the \na-1, 6-glucan type. Average relative molecular mass is about \n70,000. It is obtained by hydrolysis and fractionation of dextran \nproduced by fermentation of sucrose using strain of\nLeuconostoc mesenteroides.\nCategory. Plasma substitute.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDEXTRAN 70\nIP2P22 ~.......................................................................................\n(dentification\npgtermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2,4,6). \nC^pare the spectrum with that obtained with dextran 70 \njpRS or with the reference spectrum of dextran.\ng Specific optical rotation (see Test).\n6 Molecular mass distribution (see Test) the peaks in the \n^Histogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to\npeaks in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextrin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextrin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextrin\nDextrin is starch partially hydrolysed by heat with or without \nthe aid of suitable acids and buffers.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (tablet excipient).\nDescription. A white or pale yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextromethorphan-hydrobromide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextromethorphan Hydrobromide\nC1SH25NO,HBr,H20\nMol. Wt. 370.3\nDextromethorphan Hydrobromide is ent-3-methoxy-9a-\nmethylmorphinan hydrobromidemonohydrate.\nDEXTRIN\nIP 2010\ndiluted·to 15· ml complies .with the limit test for chlorides\nDextromethorphan Hydrobromide containsnoUessthan\n(0.2 percent).\n99.0 pei cent and notmore than 101.0percentofC1sH25NO,HBr\n_. __ ._..__ Eth~!!ol-sQlul:!!~ subsJ!lll~~§.!1'lo!.mo!:t;Ul1ll:.I!JJ.J.~!\ncen.!~\ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\ndeterIiliiied b:fthe following rtieth6d:-B6i1 under a reflux-\nCategory: COugh suppressant-;---.-.---------\nProtein. Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined by the"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextropropoxyphene-napsilate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextropropoxyphene Napsilate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextropropoxyphene Napsilate\nC22H29N02,C1oH803S,H20 \nMol. Wt. 565.8\nDextropropoxyphene Napsylate is (lS',27?)-l-benzyl- \n3-dimethylamino-2-methyl-1 -phenylpropyl propionate \nnaphthalene-2-sulphonate mohohydrate.\nDextropropoxyphene Napsilate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent-and not more than 101.0 per cent of \nC2iH29NO2,Ci0H8O3S, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Analgesic.\nDescription. A white powder. It shows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextrose-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextrose Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextrose Injection\nDextrose Intravenous Infusion; Glucose Intravenous \nInfusion\nDextrose Injection is a sterile solution of Dextrose in Water for \nInjections.\nDextrose Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nanhydrous dextrose, C6H12O6.\nUsual strengths. 5,10,25, and 50 per cent w/v.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. Solutions containing \n20.0 per cent w/v or more of Dextrose may be not more than \nfaintly straw-coloured."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextrose-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextrose Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextrose Solution\nCPD Solution\nAnticoagulant Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Solution is a sterile \nsolution of Sodium Citrate, Citric Acid, Sodium Dihydrogen \nPhosphate Dihydrate and Dextrose in Water for Injection.\nAnticoagulant Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Solution contains \nnot less than 95.0 per cent and not more than .105.0 per cent of \nthe stated amounts of sodium citrate, C6H5Na3O7,2H2O, sodium \ndihydrogen phosphate dihydrate, NaH2PO4,2H2O and \ndextrose, C6H13O6,H2O, not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore thanl 10.0 per cent of the stated amount of citric acid \nmonohydrate, C6H8O7,H2O (or anhydrous citric acid, C6HsO7).\nIt contains no antimicrobial agent.\nCategory. Anticoagulant for storage of whole blood.\nUsual strengths. /\nSodium Citrate \n’ \n2.630 g\nCitric Acid (Monohydrate) \n0.327 g\nDextrose (Monohydrate) \n2.550 g"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dextrose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dextrose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dextrose\n(KC1)__________ (C6Hl2Ob) , ;\n0.075 \n■ - .. ; .... ■ 5 , s:\n0.15 \n■ \n5\n0225 '■ \n; - \n5\n: ... \n030 ■ 11 \n 5\nDescription. A clear, colourless or. faintly straw-coloured \nsolution. \n■"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diacerein","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diacerein"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diacerein\nc1?h12os\nMol. Wt. 368.3\nDiacerein is 9,10-dihydro-4,5-dihydroxy-9,10-dioxo- \n2-anthranoic acid diacetate.\nDiacerein contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent of Ci9Hi2O8, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Antirheumatic.\nDescription. A fine yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diatrizoate-sodium-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diatrizoate Sodium Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diatrizoate Sodium Injection\ncontents gently and cool in ice, protected from light. After \n5 minutes, slowly add 2 ml of hydrochloric acid, mix and allow \nto stand for 5 minutes. Add 2 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of\nCategory. Urographic radio-opaque substance.\nSodium Diatrizoate Injection is a sterile solution of Sodium \nDiatrizoate in Water for Injections. It may contain small \namounts of suitable buffers, stabilisers and antimicrobial agents \nbut the preparation intended for intravenous administration \ncontains no antimicrobial preservative.\nDose. To be decided by the physician in accordance with the \nneeds of the patient.\nsodium nitrite, mix  and allow to stand for 5 minutes. Add 1 ml\nof an 8 per cent w/v solution ofsulphamic  acid, mix and allow \nto stand for 5 minutes. Add 2 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution \nof N-(1-naphthyl)ethylenediamine dihydrochloricie in a \n70 per cent w/v solution of 1,2-propanediol and mix. Remove \nthe flask from the ice and allow to stand in water at 25° ± 2° for\nDescription. A white powder; odourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diatrizoate-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diatrizoate Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diatrizoate Sodium\nCIIHsI3N2Na04\nMol. Wt. 635.9\nSodium Diatrizoate is sodium 3,5-diacetamido-2,4,6-triiodo-\nbenzoate.\nSodium Diatrizoate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 101.0 per cent ofCIIHsI3N2NaO4, calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis. .\n.\nCategory. Urographic radio-opaque substance.\nDose. To be decided by the physician in accordance with the\nneeds of the patient.\nDescription. A white powder; odourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diazepam-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diazepam Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diazepam Injection\nDiazepam Injection is a sterile solution of Diazepam in Water \nfor Injections or other suitable solvent.\nDiazepam Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnotmorethan 110.0 percentofthe stated amount of diazepam, \nC16H13CIN2O.\nUsual strengths. 5 mg per ml; 10 mg per ml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or almost colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diazepam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diazepam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diazepam\nCategory. Anticonvulsant and i in ithe treatment of behavioural\ndisorders. \n*\nFor Description, Identification and Tests refer to IP Volume \nil \n. \n. \n. \n: \n. \noy\nDiazepam Injection\nUsual strengths. 10 mg in2 ml; 20 mgin 4 ml.\nFor Identification and Tests refer to IP VolumeIL.\nDichlofenthion\n. \n. \ncl \nHeoN \ni\n™~ \nUp Oo” \nOT \n.\nHye \n. \nCl...\nCygH,3CLO,PS \n\"Mol, Wt.315.2\nDichlofenthion is-0-2,4- Gichloropheny!- 0,0-diethyl \nphosphorothioate.\nDichlofenthion contains not less than 95.0: per cent and.not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of CiQHisChOsPs.\nCategory. Insecticide. \n7 \n. \n;\nDescription. A colourless or pale yellow, oily substance. \n_"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diazoxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diazoxide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diazoxide\nOxX//O\nCl S'NH\nN CH3\nCjH7C1N2O2S \nMol. Wt 230.7\nDiazoxide is 7-chloro-3-methyl-2H-l ,2,4-benzothiadiazine 1,1- \ndioxide.\nDiazoxide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C8H7C1N2O2S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or almost white, fine or crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dichlofenthion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dichlofenthion"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dichlofenthion\nMol. Wt. 315.2\nDichlofenthion is 0-2,4-dichlorophenyl-O,O-diethyl\nphosphorothioate.\nDichlofenthion contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not\nmore than 100.5 per cent ofC IOH I3Clz03PS.\nCategory. Insecticide.\nDescription. A colourless or pale yellow, oily substance."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dichlorophen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dichlorophen"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dichlorophen\nCsHyClO, \nne \nLos \nMol. Wt. 269.1\nDichlorophert ig 2,2 *~nethylenebis(4-chlordphenol).\n4863\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDICHLOROPHEN\nDichlorophen ‘contains: not less than'97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C,;H,)Cl,0,, calculated. on the \ndried basis. \n| \n= \nee\nCategory. Anthelmintic and fungicide. 2 \n;\nDescription. Awhite or almost white powder. \n:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diclofenac-diethylamine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diclofenac Diethylamine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diclofenac Diethylamine\nCi8H22C12N2O2. \nMol. Wt. 369.3\nDiclofenac Diethylamine is diethylammonium 2-[(2,6-\ndichloroanilirio)phenyl] acetate.\nDiclofenac Diethylamine contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C i8H22C12N2O2, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Analgesic; antiinflammatory. \nj\nDescription. A white to light beige, crystalline powder. \nI"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diclofenac-gel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diclofenac Gel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diclofenac Gel\nDiclofenac Gel is a Diclofenac Diethylamine in a suitable base.\nDiclofenac Gel contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated equivalent amount of \ndiclofenac diethylamine,\nUsual strength. 1.16 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diclofenac-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diclofenac Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diclofenac Injection\nDiclofenac Sodium Injection\nDiclofenac Injection is a sterile solution of Diclofenac Sodium\nin Water for Injections. It may contain Propylene Glycol, Benzyl \nAlcohol and sufficient Sodium Hydroxide to adjust the pH of \nthe solution.\nDiclofenac Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of diclofenac \nsodium, Ci4Hi0Cl2NNaO2.\nUsual strength. 25 mg per ml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless to yellowish liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diclofenac-potassium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diclofenac Potassium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diclofenac Potassium\nK+\nChHAKNQz \nMoL Wt 334-2\nDiclofenac Potassium is benzene acetic acid, 2-[(2,6- \ndichlorophehyl)amino]->monopotassium salt.\nDiclofenac Potassium contains not less than 99.0 per cent and\nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C 14H10Cl2KNO25 calculated on\nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Analgesic, anti-inflammatory.\nDescription. A white to off white or slightly yellowish \ncrystalline powder, slightly hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diclofenac-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diclofenac Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diclofenac Sodium\nCnHioCIjbJNaCb\nMol. Wt. 318.1\nDiclofenac Sodium is sodium 2-[(2,6-dichlorophenyl)- \namino]phenylacetate.\nDiclofenac Sodium contains not less than 98.5 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of CuHioCbNNaOs, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Analgesic; anti-inflammatory.\nDescription. A white to slightly yellowish crystalline powder; \nslightly hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dicloxacillin-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dicloxacillin Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dicloxacillin Oral Suspension\nDicloxacillin Sodium Oral Suspension\nDicloxacillin Oral Suspension is a dry mixture of Dicloxacillin \nwith buffering agents and other excipients. It contains a\nsuitable flavouring agent. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nsealed container in the specified volume of water just before \nuse.\nDicloxacillin Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 120.0 per cent of the labeled amount of \ndicloxacillisi, CjyHnCfiNjOjS.\nWhen stored at the temperature and.for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than\n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of dicloxacillin, \nC19H17C12N3O5S.\nUsual strength. 12.5 mg per mf"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dicloxacillin-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dicloxacillin Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dicloxacillin Sodium\nh2o\nMol. Wt. 510.3\nC19H16Cl2N3NaO5S,H2O\nDicloxacillin Sodium is sodium(67?)-6-[3-(2,6-dichlorophenyl)- \n5-mcihylisoxazole-4-carboxamido] penicillanate monohydrate.\n2087\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDICLOXACILLIN SODIUM\nDicloxacillin Sodium contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of Ci^HjeCkNjNaOsS, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder, \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dicyclomine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dicyclomine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dicyclomine Injection\nDicyclomine Hydrochloride Injection; Dicycloverine \nHydrochloride Injection\nDicyclomine Injection is a sterile, isotonic solution of \nDicyclomine Hydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nDicyclomine Injection contains not less than 93.0 per cent \nand not more than 107.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndicyclomine hydrochloride, C19H35NO2,HC1.\nUsual strength. 10 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dicyclomine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dicyclomine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dicyclomine Oral Solution\nDicyclomine Hydrochloride Oral Solution; Dicycloverine \nHydrochloride Oral Solution\nDicyclomine Oral Solution is a solution of Dicyclomine \nHydrochloride in a suitable flavoured vehicle.\nDicyclomine Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 percent ofthe stated amount of \ndicyclomine hydrochloride, Cidl^NOyiCl.\nUsual strength. 10 mg in 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dicycloverine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dicycloverine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dicycloverine Hydrochloride\nC19H35NO2,HC1\nMol. Wt. 346.0\nDicyclomine Hydrochloride is 2-diethylaminoethyl \nbicyclohexyl-1 -carboxylate hydrochloride.\nDicyclomine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci9H35NO2,HCl, \ncalculated on the dried basis;\nCategory. Antispasmodic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIdenHfication\nDissolve a suitable quantity in acetone and evaporate to\n«e The residue complies with the following test.\ndtyness. iuv\n^ermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with dicyclomine \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \njicyciomine hydrochloride.\ng To 3 ml ofa 0-1 Per cent W/ V s°frition of sodium dodecyl \nsulphate, add 5 ml of chloroform and 0.05 ml of a 0.25 per cent\nw/v solution of methylene blue, mix gently and allow to \nseparate; the chloroform layer is blue. Add 20 mg of the \nsubstance under examination dissolved in 2 ml of water, mix \n«ently and allow to separate; the aqueous layer is blue and \ndie chloroform layer is colourless.\nC Dissolve 10 mg in 5 ml of water and add 0.2 ml of 2 M nitric \nacid and 0.5 ml of silver nitrate solution’, a white precipitate is\nproduced.\nTests . \n.\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with silica gel G..\nMobile phase. A mixture of 50 volumes of 1-propanol, \n30 volumes of ethyl acetate, 15 volumes of water and 5 volumes \nol strong ammonia solution.\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.5 g of the substance under \nexamination in 10 ml of methanol.\nReference solution. Dilute 5.0 ml ofthe test solution to 50.0 ml \nwith methanol and mix. To 2.0 ml of the solution add sufficient \nmethanol to produce 100.0ml.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. After development, \ndry the plate in air and spray with dilute potassium \ntodobismuthate solution. Any secondary spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution is not more \nintense than the spot in the chromatogram obtained with the \nreference solution.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent.\nboss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 1.0 per cent, determined\non 1.0 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nAssay. Dissolve 0.6 g in 20 ml of anhydrous glacial acetic \n°rii/and add 10 ml of mercuric acetate solution. Titrate with\nM perchloric acid, using crystal violet solution as \nindicator. Carry out a blank titration.\ni ml of 0.1 Mperchloric acid is equivalent to 0.03460 g of \nC19H35NO2,HC1.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/didanosine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Didanosine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Didanosine\nCl0H12N4O3 \nMol. Wt. 236.2\nDidanosine is 2f,3'--dideoxyinosine.\nDidanosine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CjoHuN^, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dienestrol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dienestrol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dienestrol\nC18H18O2 \nMol. Wt. 266.3\nDienoestrol is (Ej£M,4'-[bis(ethylidene)ethylene]diphenol.\nDienoestrol contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.5 per cent of Ci8H18O2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Oestrogen.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dienogest","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dienogest"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dienogest\nGoOCh .. . \nMolWt.311.4\nDienogest is 2-[(85, 13 S, 145,177?)-17-hydroxy-13-methyl“ \n3-oxo-l ,2,6,7,8,11,12,14,15,16-decahydrocyclopenta \nphenanthren-17-yl] acetonitrile.\nDienogest contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent of (fJLjNCL, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiandrogen ic.\nDescription. An oif-white to slightly yellow powder. ,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diethanolamine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diethanolamine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diethanolamine\nC4H11NO2\nMol. Wt. 105.14\nDiethanolamine is bis(hydroxyethyl)amine.\nDiethanolamine is a mixture of ethanolamines, consisting \nlargely of diethanolamine.\n2099\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDIETHANOLAMINE\nDiethanolamine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and riot \nmore than 101.0 per cent of ethanolamines, NH(C2H4OH)2, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diethyl-phthalate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diethyl Phthalate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diethyl Phthalate\nC1zH140 4\nMol. Wt. 222.2\nDiethyl phthalate is diethyl benzene-1,2-dicarboxylate.\nDiethyl phthalate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent ofdiethyl phthalate, C 12H I40 4•\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A clear, oily liquid, colourless or very slightly\nyellow."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diethylcarbamazine-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diethylcarbamazine Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diethylcarbamazine Citrate\nC10H21N3(),C6H8O? \nMol. Wt. 391.4\nDiethylcarbamazine Citrate isMA-diethyl-4- mcthylpiperazine-\n1-carboxamide dihydrogen citrate. ■'\nDiethylcarbamazine Citrate contains not less than 9g q \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C10H21N3O,C6H 0* \ncalculated on the dried basis. : \n. ■ \n. ■ \n. 4 *\nCategory. Anthelmintic; antifilarial.\nI\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; slightly hygro^^"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diethylcarbamazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diethylcarbamazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diethylcarbamazine Injection\nDiethylcarbamazine Citrate Injection.\nDiethylcarbamazine Injection is a sterile solution ofDiethy1-\ncarbamazine Citrate in Water for Injections.\nDiethylcarbamazine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per\ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of\ndiethylcarbamazine citrate, CIQH21N30,C6Hg07.\nUsual strength. 400 mg in 1mi."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diethylene-glycol-monoethyl-ether","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diethylene Glycol Monoethyl Ether"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diethylene Glycol Monoethyl Ether\nh3c\nCAA \nMol, Wt, 134.2\nDiethylene Glycol Monoethyl Ether contains not less than \n99.0 per cent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C6Hu0Jt \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis?\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (humectant; solvent).\nDescription. A clear, colourless liquid, hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diethylstilboestrol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diethylstilboestrol"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diethylstilboestrol\nOH\nHO\nMol. Wt. 268.4\nStilboestrol is (E)-a,~-diethylstilbene-4,4'-diol.\nStilboestrol contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent ofClsHzoOz, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Oestrogen.\nDose. In the treatment of menopausal symptoms, 100 Ilg to\nI mg daily; for the suppression oflactation, 5 mg thrice daily\nfor 3 days, followed by 5 mg daily for 6 days; in the treatment\nofcarcinoma ofthe prostate and mammary carcinoma, 10 to 20\nmgdaily.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diethyltoluamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diethyltoluamide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diethyltoluamide\nDeet\n0\nH C\nCH3\nC121417NO\nDiethyltoluamide is N.N-diethyl-3-toluamide:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/digitoxin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Digitoxin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Digitoxin\nC4[H«O13 \nMol. Wt. 764.9\nDigitoxin is 3 p-[((>2,6-dideoxy-p-D-nfro-hexopyranosyl-\n(1 4)-0-2,6-dideoxy-P-D-rz?>o-hexopyranosyl-( 1 -> 4)-2,6- \ndideoxy-p-D-ri£>o-hexopyranosyl)oxy]-14f}-hydroxy-5p-card-\n20(22)-enolidc. \n.: \n. -\nDigitoxin contains riot less than 95.0 per cerit and not more\nthan 103.0 per cent of C41H64O13, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Cardiotonic. .\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/digoxin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Digoxin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Digoxin Injection\nDigoxin Injection is a sterile solution of Digoxin in Water for \nInjections and Ethanol or other suitable solvents.\nDigoxin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of digoxin, \nC4]H&4O]4. ■\nUsual strength. 250 pg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/digoxin-paediatric-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Digoxin Paediatric Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Digoxin Paediatric Solution\nPaediatric Digoxin Elixir\nDigoxin Paediatric Solution is a solution of Digoxin in a suitable \nflavoured vehicle.\nDigoxin Paediatric Solution contains not less than 90.0 per\ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent w/v of the stated amount \nofdigoxin,C4iH64014.\nUsual strength. 50 pg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dihydroergocristine-mesilate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dihydroergocristine Mesilate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dihydroergocristine Mesilate\n, CH3SO3H\nCjf,H.15N5OsS •• \nMol. Wt. 70S'\nDihydroergocristine Mesylate is (5'a)-l2'-hydroxy- \n2f-(l-methylethyl)-5:-(phehylmethyl)dihyroergotanian- \n36r, 18-trione methanesulphonate.\nDihydroergocristine Mesylate contains not less than 98.0per \n.cent and not more than 102.0 per. cent of CjetUNjOsS,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antimigraine."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dihydroergotamine-mesilate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dihydroergotamine Mesilate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dihydroergotamine Mesilate\nC33H37N5O5,CH4SO3 \nMol. Wt. 680.0\nDihydroergotamine Mesylate is (5'a)~ 12'-hydroxy- \n2 '-methyl-5 '-(phenylmethyl)dihyroergotaman-3 ',618-trione \nmethanesulphonate.\nDihydroergotamine Mesylate contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C33H37N5O5,CH4SO3, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antimigraine."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dihydroergotamine-mesylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dihydroergotamine Mesylate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dihydroergotamine Mesylate\n, CHsSOsH\nC33H37NsOs,CH4S03\nMol. Wt. 680.0\nDihydroergotamine Mesylate is (5'a)-12'-hydroxy-2'-\nmethyl-5'-(phenylmethyl)dihyroergotaman-3',6',18-trione\nmethanesulphonate.\n\"\nDihydroergotamine Mesylate contains not less than 98.0 per\ncent and not more than 101.0 per centofC33H37NsOs,CH4S03,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory.Antimigraine."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dihydrostreptomycin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dihydrostreptomycin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dihydrostreptomycin Injection\nDihydrostreptomycin Sulphate Injection.\nDihydrostreptomycin Injection is a sterile solution of\nDihydrostreptomycin Sulphate in Water for Injections. It is\nprepared by dissolving the contents of a sealed container in\nthe requisite amount of Water for Injections immediately\nbefore use.\nDIHYDROSTREPTOMYCIN INJECTION\nDihydrostreptomycin Injection contains not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated\namount of dihydrostreptomycin, CZ1H41N701Z, calculated on\nthe dried basis.\nUsual\nstrength.\nThe\nequivalent\nof 250\nmg\nof\ndihydrostreptomycin.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder which yields a\nclear, colourless or faintly yellow solution when dissolved in\nwater.\nThe injection complies with the tests stated under Parenteral\nPreparations (Powders for Injection).\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nfollowing requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dihydrostreptomycin-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dihydrostreptomycin Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dihydrostreptomycin Sulphate\nzr.\nN\n; BHSO,\n(CoyHy{NjOi),3H,SO; \nMol. Wt: 1461.4\nDihydrostreptomycin sulphate is O-2- déoxy- 2-methylamino-\nd-t-lyxofuranosy!-(1>4) -N! :N’-diamidino-p-sireptamine\nsulphate. \n.\nDihydrostreptomycin Sulphate contains not less than\n95.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per cent.sum of\nCoHssNsOa683 and CoHeN yO36Ss, calculated on dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial, \na.\nDescription. Awhite. oraimost white ‘powder: \nCoe"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diloxanide-furoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diloxanide Furoate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diloxanide Furoate\nCt4HnCl2NO4 ■ \nMol. Wt 328.2\nDiloxanide Furoate is 4-(2V-methyl-2,2-dichloroacetarnid°)\nphenyl 2-furoate.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nnide Furoate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nthan 102.0 per cent of C^nCENO^ calculated on\nthedriedl>asls\n^ry-Antiamoebic.\np£^pti6n. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nIdentiniatio\"\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with diloxanide \nfafoaie IPRS or ^1C reference spectrum of diloxanide\nfuroate.'\ng When examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a\n0 QOl per cent w/v solution in ethanol (95 per cent) shows an \nabsorption maximum only at about 258 nm; absorbance at \nabout 258 nm, about 0.70.\nC. On 20 mg determine by the oxygen-flask method (2.3.34), \nusing 10 ml of 7 Msodium hydroxide as the absorbing liquid. \nWhen the process is complete, acidify the liquid with nitric \nacid and add silver nitrate solution', a white precipitate is \nproduced.\nfuroate."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diltiazem-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diltiazem Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diltiazem Hydrochloride\nQ2H25N2O4S,IIC1 \n. Mol. Wt. 451.0\nDiltiazem Hydrochloride is (2S,f3S)-2,3,4,5-tetrahydro-5-(2- \ndimethylaminoethyl)-2-(4-methoxyphenyl)-4-oxobenzo[£>] \nthiazepin-3-yl acetate hydrochloride.\nDiltiazem Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.5 per cent of C22H26N2O4S,HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antianginal; (calcium-channel blocker).\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder or small crystals. ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diluted-isosorbide-dinitrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diluted Isosorbide Dinitrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diluted Isosorbide Dinitrate\nDiluted Sorbide Dinitrate; Diluted Sorbide Nitrate\nH ONO2\nCb\no2no h\nC^O,. \nMol. Wt. 236.1\nDiluted Isosorbide Dinitrate is a dry mixture of 1,4:3, \n6-dianhydro-D-glucitol 2,5-dinitrate with Lactose, Mannitol \nor other suitable inert diluent: It may contain a suitable \nstabilising agent. .\nDiluted Isosorbide Dinitrate contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nisosorbide dinitrate, C6H8N2OS and usually contains.20 per \ncent to 50 per cent of isosorbide dinitrate.\nCategory. Antianginal.\nDescription. Afme, white, crystalline powder.:\nCA UTION Undiluted isosorbide dinitrate is a powerful \nexplosive and can be exploded with percussion or excessive \nheat. Proper precautions must be taken in handling it and \nonly exceedingly small amounts should be isolated."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diluted-isosorbide-mononitrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diluted Isosorbide Mononitrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diluted Isosorbide Mononitrate\nC6H,NO6 \nMol.Wt.191.1\nDiluted Isosorbide Mononitrate is a dry mixture of isosorbide \nmononitrate and lactose monohydrate or mannitol.\nDiluted Isosorbide Mononitrate is 1,4:3,6-dianhydro-D-glucitol \n5-nitrate.\nDiluted Isosorbide Mononitrate contains not less than 95.0 \nper cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount \nofC6H9N06.\nCategory. Antianginal.\nDescription. Undiluted isosorbide mononitrate is a white, \ncrystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diluted-nitroglycerin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diluted Nitroglycerin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diluted Nitroglycerin\nONO2 \n°2NO\\/J\\/ONO2\nC3HsN3O9 \nMol. Wt. 227.1\nDiluted Glyceryl Trinitrate is 1,2,3-Propanetriol trinitrate.\nDiluted Glyceryl Trinitrate is a mixture of nitroglycerin with \nlactose, dextrose, alcohol, propylene glycol, or other suitable \ninert excipient to permit safe handling.\nDiluted Glyceryl Trinitrate contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nC3H5N3O9. It usually contains not more than 10.0 per cent of \nglyceryl trinitrate.\nCA UTION — Undiluted glyceryl trinitrate can be exploded \nby percussion or excessive heat. Proper precautions should \nbe exercised in handling it and only exceedingly small \namounts should be isolated.\nCategory. Coronary vasodilator.\nDescription .A clear, colourless to pale yellow solution or a \nwhite powder-depending upon the diluent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diluted-pentaerythritol-tetranitrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diluted Pentaerythritol Tetranitrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diluted Pentaerythritol Tetranitrate\n°2N-O X/^ON°2\no2n ° \n°'no2\nC5H8N40i2 \nMol. Wt. 316.1\nDiluted Pentaerythritol. Tetranitrate is a dry mixture of \n2,2-bis(hydrpxymethyl)prbpane-l,3-dibl tetranitrate with \nLactose dr Mannitol or a mixture of Lactose and Starch or any \nother suitable inert excipients which permit safe handling.\nDiluted Pentaerythritol Tetranitrate contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of pentaerythritol tetranitrate, C5H8N4O12. .\nCategory. Antianginal.\nDescription. A white or almost white, powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dimerca-1","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dimerca 1"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dimerca 1 \nmore tha\nDrol Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nbismuthate, previously heated to 200° for 2 hours, in a mixture \nof 6 ml of water and 2.8 ml of a 10 per cent w/w solution of\nDescription. A translucent, grey viscous liquid; almost \nodourless.\n.1110.0 per cent of the stated amount of dimercaprol,\nC3Hx0S,.\nphosphoric acid. Add 0.2 ml of the substance under\nUsual str\nength. 50 mg per ml.\nexamination,' nix and allow to stand for 10 minutes shaking\nI dentjfication\ntni of a \nfrequently. RYA ml of the supernatant liquid add\nDescrip \nPungent,\nion. A clear, \ndisagreeable\noyeolulor.w, viscous solution, having a \nd\nA. To 50 mg add 25 ml of toluene and swirl to disperse. Add \n50 ml of dilute hydrochloric acid-and shake for 5 minutes.\n0.4 per cent w/v solution of chromotropic acid sodium salt in\n1 .845\nTransfer to a separating funnel and remove about 5 ml of the \nupper layer to a stoppered tube containing 0.5 g of anhydrous\nsodium sulphate. Shake vigorously and centrifuge the mixture\nuntil a clear supernatant liquid is obtained. The resulting liquid \ncomplies with the following test.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with dimethicone\nRS. Ignore the region of the spectrum from 850 to 750 cm . '\nsince slight differences may be observed depending on the \ndegree of polymerisation.\nB. Heat 0.5 g in a test-tube over a small flame until white fumes \nare evolved. Invert the test-tube over a second tube containing\n1 ml ofa 0.1 per cent w/v solution of chromotropic acid sodium \nsalt in sulphuric acid so that the fumes reach the solution.\nShake the second tube for about 10 seconds and heat on a \nwater-bath for 5 minutes; the solution is violet.\nC. To 50 mg in a platinum crucible add 0.15 ml of sulphuric\nacid and ignite until a white residue is obtained; the residue \ngives the reaction of silicates (2.3.1)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dimercaprol-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dimercaprol Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dimercaprol Injection\nB.A.L. Injection\nDimercaprol injection is a sterile solution of Dimercaprol in a \nmixture of Benzyl Benzoate and Arachis Oil.\nDimercaprol Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of dimercaprol, \nQIIsOS>. . \n.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per ml.\nDescription. A clear, yellow, viscous solution, having a \npungent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dimercaprol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dimercaprol"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dimercaprol\nB.A.L.\nSH\nHS~OH\nMol. Wt. 124.2\nDimercaprol is (RS)-2,3-dimercaptopropanol.\nDimercaprol contains not less than 98.5 per cent w/w and not\nmore than 101.5 percentw/w ofC3HsOSz.\nCategory. Antidote in heavy metal poisoning; metal\ncomplexing agent.\nDose. By intramuscular injection, 2 to 3 mg per kg of body\nweight every 4 hours during the ftrst day and subsequently,\nin accordance with the needs of the patient.\nDescription.Aclear, colourless or slightly yellow liquid; odour,\nstrong, characteristic and alliaceous."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dimethicone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dimethicone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dimethicone\nDimethicone is a-(Trimethylsilyl)-co-methylpoly[oxy \n(dimethylsilylene).\nDimethicone is a mixture of folly methylated linear siloxane \npolymers containing repeating units of the formula; \nHCH3)2SiO-]„ stabilized with trimethylsiloxy end-blocking\nunits ofthe formula; [(CH3)3SiO-] where in n has an average \nvalue such that the corresponding nominal viscosity is in a \ndiscrete range between 20 and 30,000 centistokes.\nDimethicone contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not\nmore than 103.0 per cent of polydimethylsiloxane, \n(HCH3)2SiO-]„).\nDescription. A Clear, colourless liquid of various viscosities."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dimetridazole-premix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dimetridazole Premix"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dimetridazole Premix\nDimetridazole Premix contains not less than 95.0 per cent and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ndimetridazole, CSH7N30 Z•\nUsual strength. 22.5 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dimetridazole-veterinary-oral-powder","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dimetridazole Veterinary Oral Powder"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dimetridazole Veterinary Oral Powder\nDimetridazole Veterinary Oral Powder is a mixture of\nDimetridazole and a suitable water-soluble diluent.\nDimetridazole Veterinary Oral Powder contains not less than\n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated\namount ofdimetridazole, CSH7N30 Z•\nUsual strength. 40 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dimetridazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dimetridazole"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dimetridazole\nCsH7N30 2\nMol. Wt. 141.1\nDimetridazole is 1,2-dimethyl-5-nitro-lH-imidazole.\n2657\nDIMETRIDAZOLE\nDimetridazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per centofthe stated amount ofdimetridazole,\nCsH7N30 2, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiprotozoal.\nDose. In drinking water. Pigs. 250 mg per litre. Poultry. 75 to\n250 mg per litre. In feed. Pigs. Therapeutic dose, upto 500 g\nper tonne; prophylactic dose, upto 200 g per tonne. Pouluy.\n75 to 500 g per tonne.\nDescription. An almost white to brownish yellow powder;\ndarkens on exposure to light, odourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dinitolmide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dinitolmide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dinitolmide\nONT\nMol. Wt. 225.2\nGEHLN;O5\nDinitolmide is 3,5- -dinitro-2-methylbenzamide,\nDinitolmide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and riot more \n.\nthan 100.5 per cent of CaELNsOs, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Coccidiostal:\nReference solution \n(a). A0.0125 per cent w/v of the substance \nunder examination in acetone. \n  \n  \nwww.webofpharma.com\nDescription. Acteam to light tan powder.” \n7"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dinoprostone-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dinoprostone Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dinoprostone Oral Solution\nDinoprostone Oral Solution is a solution of dinoprostone in a\nsuitable aqueous vehicle.\nDinoprostone Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per\ncent and not more than 105;0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndinoprostone, C20H32O5."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dinoprostone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dinoprostone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dinoprostone\nC2cH32O5\nDinoprostone is (Z)-7-[(lR,2R,3R)-3-hydroxy-2-[(E)-(3S)-3- \nhydroxyoct-l-enyl]“5-dxocyclopentyl]hept-5-enoic acid \n(PGE2).\nDinoprostone contains not less than 95,0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per .cent of C2oH3205, calculated, on the \nanhydrous basis............\nCategory. Prostaglandin E2( PGE2); Abortifacient; uterine \nstimulant. \n/ ■■ ■.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless crystals. \n. ,.T - ;\n2124"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diphenhydramine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diphenhydramine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diphenhydramine Hydrochloride\nC17H21NO,HC1\nMol. Wt. 291.8\nDiphenhydramine Hydrochloride is 2-benzhydryIoxy- \nethyldimethylamine hydrochloride.\nDiphenhydramine Hydrochloride contains not less than \n99.0 per cent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C,-H2; NO.HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Histamine H:-receptor antagonist. ■\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diphenoxylafe-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diphenoxylafe Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diphenoxylafe Hydrochloride\n~'/CH3'HCI\nC3oH32N202,HCl\nMol. Wt. 489.1\nDiphenoxylate Hydrochloride is ethyll-(3-cyano-3,3-\ndiphenylpropyl)-4-phenylpiperidine-4-carboxylate\nhydrochloride.\nDiphenoxylate HydrochlOlide contains not less than 98.0 per\ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C30H32N202,HCl,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidiarrhoeal.\nDose. 5 to 30 mg daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diphenoxylate-hydrochloride-and-atropine-sulphate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diphenoxylate Hydrochloride and Atropine Sulphate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diphenoxylate Hydrochloride and Atropine Sulphate Tablets \ncontain not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110(0 per \ncent of the stated amount of diphenoxylate hydrochloride, \nC30H32N2O2,HCl and not less than 80.0 per cent and not more \nthan 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of atropine sulphate, \n(CI7h23no3 )2,H2SO4,H2O.\nUsual strength. Diphenoxylate Hydrochloride, 2.5 mg and \nAtropine Sulphate, 0.025 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diphenoxylate-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diphenoxylate Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diphenoxylate Hydrochloride\nC3qH32N2O2 ,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 489,1\nDiphenoxylate Hydrochloride is ethyl l-(3-cyano-3,3- \ndipheny!propyl)-4-phenyIpiperidi ne-4-carboxylate \nhydrochloride.\nDiphenoxylate Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C3oH32N202,HCI, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidiarrhoeal.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diphenylhydantoin-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diphenylhydantoin Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diphenylhydantoin Sodium\nMol. Wt. 274.3\nPhenytoin Sodium is4-oxo-5,5-diphenyl-2-imidazolidin-2-01il.te\nPhenytoin Sodium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 101.0 per cent ofClsHIINzNaOz, calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant; antiarrhythmic.\nDose. As anticonvulsant, orally, 150 to 300 mg daily, increasing\ngradually to 600 mg in accordance with the needs ofpatient.\nIn status epileptieus, by slow intravenous injection, 10 to 15\nmgper kg at a rate not exceeding 50 mg perminute as a loading\ndose; maintenance doses of 100 mg thereafter every 6 hours.\nIn arrhythmias, by intravenous injection, 3.5 to 5 mg per kg at\na rate not exceeding 50 mg per minute and repeated once if\nnecessary.\nDescription. A white powder; odourless; somewhat\nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diphenylhydantoin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diphenylhydantoin"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diphenylhydantoin\nC151-112N202 \t\nMol.Wt. 252.3\nPhenytoin is 5,5-diphenylimidazolidine-2,4-dione.\nPhenytoin contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of CI5H12N202, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nI dentification\nTest A may be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. Tests B \nand C may he omitted if test A is carried out.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophoton -ietry (2.4:6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with phenytoin RS \nor with the reference spectrum of phenytoin,\nPHENYRAMIDOL TABLETS \nIP 2018 \nw 2018 \nPHEN YTOIN SODIUM\nB. In the test for Related substances, the principal peak in th e\n23a,6a-diphenyltetrahydroimidazo[4,5-d]imidazole-2,5(1H,3H)-dione,\nPhenytoin Sodium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of CI5H111•12Na02, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\n chromatogram obtained with the test solution coresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (a).\n3(carbamoylamino)(diphenyl)acctic acid.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the\nC. To about 10 mg, add 1 ml of water and 0.05 ml of ammonia. \nHeat until boiling begins. Add 0.05 ml of a 5 per cent w/v\ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant; antiarrhythmic.\nDose. As anticonvulsant, orally, 150 to 300 mg daily, increasing \ngradually to 600 mg in accordance with the needs of patient. \nIn status epileptieus, by slow intravenous injection, 10 to 15 \nmg per kg at a rate not exceeding 50 mg per minute as a loading \ndose; maintenance doses of 100 mg thereafter every 6 hours. \nIn arrhythmias, by intravenous injection, 3.5 to 5 mg per kg at \na rate not exceeding 50 mg per minute and repeated once if \nnecessary.\n solution of \ncopper sulphate in dilute ammonia and shake, a \npink crystalline precipitate is formed.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area \nof any peak corresponding to phenytoin impurity E is not \nmore than 3 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) (0.3 per \ncent). The area of any peak corresponding to phenytoin \nimpurity C is not more than twice the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b)"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diphtheria-and-tetanus-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diphtheria and Tetanus Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diphtheria and Tetanus Vaccine \n(Adsorbed) for Adults and Adolescents\nI f an animal dies or loses weight in the second test, the vaccine \nfails the test.\nAntimicrobial preservative. Where applicable, determine  the \namount of antimicrobial preservative by a suitable chemical \nmethod. The amount is not less than 85.0 per cent  and not \ngreater than 1 15.0 per cent of the intended amount.\nDiphtheria and Tetanus Vaccine (Adsorbed) for Adults and \nAdolescents is a preparation of diphtheria formol toxoid and\nB.Tetanus toxoid is identified by a suitable immuno-chemical \nmethod (2.2.14).\n1 toxoid adsorbed on a mineral carrier. The formol \ntoxoids are prepared from the toxins produced by the growth \nopfroC do: \ntie ob nacterium diphtheriae and Clostridium tetani,\nThe clear supernatant liquid obtained during test A reacts\nwith a suitable tetanus antitoxin, giving a precipitate or visible\nFree formaldehyde (2.3.20). Not more than 0.02 per cent w/v.\nrespectively.\nfloccules.\nSterility (2.2.11). Carry out the test for sterility using 10 ml for \neach medium."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/diphtheria-antitoxin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Diphtheria Antitoxin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Diphtheria Antitoxin\nDiphtheria Antitoxin is a preparation containing the specific \nantitoxic globulins or their derivatives obtained by purification \nof hyperimmune serum or plasma of healthy horses or other \nsuitable animals and having the specific activity of neutralising \nthe toxin formed by Corynebacterium diphtheridé.'ïhiYïc^^ \npreparation may contain a suitable antimicrobial preservative.\nDiphtheria Antitoxin has a potency of not less than 1000 Units \nper ml when obtained from horse serum and not less than\n500 Units per ml when obtained from other animals. !\nDescription. Aclear, colourless or pale yellow liquid or a freeze- \ndried, cream-coloured powder or pellet. \n; \n1"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dipivefrine-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dipivefrine Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dipivefrine Eye Drops\nDipivefrine Hydrochloride Eye Drops\nDipivefrine Eye Drops are a sterile solution of Dipivefrine \nHydrochloride in purified water.\nDipivefrine Eye Drops contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of dipivefrine hydrochloride, \nC19H29NO5,HC1.\nUsual strength. 0.1 per cent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dipivefrine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dipivefrine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dipivefrine Hydrochloride\nC19H29NO5JIC1 \nMol. Wt. 387.9\nDipivefrine Hydrochloride is (J?.S)-4-[l-Hydroxyl \n(methylamino)ethyl]-o-phenylene dipivalate hydrochloride.\nDipivefrine Hydrochloride contains not less than 97.5\ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of \ncalculated on the dried basis.\n2130\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDIPIVEFRINE HYDROCHLORIDE\n$2022\nC^ry. Miotic.\nP^rjptioh. A white to off-white, crystalline powder.\nIdentified*01*\n\\ petennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompal the spectrum with that obtained with dipivefrine \nArochlor ide IPRS or with the reference spectrum of\n(2.4.14).\ndipivefrine hydrochloride.\nB It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/disodium-edetate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Disodium Edetate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Disodium Edetate Injection\nDisodium Edetate Injection is a sterile solution of Disodium \nEdetate in Water for Injections, containing varying amounts \nof the disodium and trisodium salts as a result of pH \nadjustment.\nDisodium Edetate Injection contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndisodium edetate, C i0Hi4N2Na2O8.\nUsual strengths. 3 g per 15 ml; 3 g per 20 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/disodium-edetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Disodium Edetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Disodium Edetate\n•CO OH\nNaOOC\nCOO Na\n,2H2O\nCiE)H14N2Na2O8,2H2O \nMol. Wt. 372.2\nDisodium Edetate is disodium ethylenediaminetetraacetate \ndihydrate.\nDisodium Edetate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C l0H14N2Na2O8,2H2O.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid; chelating agent in metal \npoisoning.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/disodium-hydrogen-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Disodium Hydrogen Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Disodium Hydrogen Phosphate \t\n910, 3232 \nDisodium Hydrogen Phosphate, 0.2 M\nHaemophilus influenzae Type b Conjugate Vaccine \n(Adsorbed) \t\n3609\n885 \nDisodium Hydrogen Phosphate, Anhydrous \t\n911,946 \nDisodium Hydrogen Phosphate, Dihydrate \t 911 \nDisodium Hydrogen Phosphate Dodecahydrate \t\n3232 \nDisodium Hydrogen Phosphate Solution \t\n911 \nDisodium Hydrogen Phosphate, xM\nDiphtheria Vaccine (Adsorbed) \t\n3612\n230,493,1863 \nDithranol Ointment\nDiphtheria Vaccine (Adsorbed) for Adults and\n1864 \nDivalproex Extended-release Tablets \t\n1866 \nDivalproex Gastro-resistant Tablets \t\n1866 \nDivalproex Prolonged-release Tablets \t\n1866 \nDivalproex Sodium \t\n230,494,1865 \nDivalproex Sodium Gastro-resistant Tablets \t1866 \nDivalproex Sodium Prolonged-release Tablets \t\n1866 \nDivalproex Sustained-release Tablets \t\n1866 \nDivalproex Sodium Tablets \t\n1866 \nDivalproex Tablets\n3615\nAdolescents \nDiphtheria Vaccine (Adsorbed) Reduced antigen\n3615 \ncontent\n917 \nDipicrylamine\n911 \nDisodium 1 -nitroso-2-naphthol -3,6-disulphonate \t\n911 \nDisodium Tetraborate \t\n898 \nDisopyramide \t\n230,492, 1859 \nDisopyramide Capsules \t\n1860 \nDisopyramide Phosphate Capsules \t\n1860 \nDisopyramide Phosphate Extended-release Capsules 1861\n230, 1853 \nDipivefrine Hydrochloride\n1855 \nDipivefrine Eye Drops\n1855 \nDipivefrine Hydrochloride Eye Drops\n230,491, 1856 \nDipyridamole\n1857 \nDipyridamolc Tablets\n1866 \nDNA-Based Authentication Techniques \t\n72 \nDobutamine Concentrate, Sterile \t\n1869 \nDobutamine Hydrochloride \t\n230, 1868 \nDobutamine Hydrochloride for Injection \t\n1871 \nDobutamine Injection \t\n1869 \nDobutamine Intravenous Infusion \t\n1869 \nDocetaxel Anhydrous \t\n1871 \nDocetaxel Concentrate \t\n1874 \nDocetaxel Injection \t\n1874, 4233 \nDocetaxel Trihydrate \t\n230, 1872 \nDocusate Sodium \t\n230, 1875 \nDocusate Sodium Tablets \t\n1876 \nDocusate Tablets \t\n1876 \nDodecamolybdophosphoric Acid\n910 \nDipotassium Hydrogen Orthophosphate\n910 \nDipotassium Hydrogen Phosphate\nDisopyramide Phosphate Prolonged-release\n910 \nDipotassium Hydrogen Phosphate, 0.1 M\nCapsules\n1861 \nDisopyramide Phosphate Sustained-release Capsules 1861\n938 \nDipotassium Sulphate\n910 \nDipotassium Tartrate\nDispersible Amoxici I lin Tablets \t\n1232 \nDispersible Amoxycillin Tablets \t\n1232 \nDispersible Ampicillin Tablets \t\n1240 \nDispersible Aspirin Tablets \t\n1277 \nDispersible Tablets \t\n1120 \nDispersible Zinc Sulphate Tablets \t\n3544 \nDissolution Test\n1247 \nDipyrone\n216 \nDirect Titrations\n299 \nDisintegration Test \nDisintegration Test for Pessaries and\n301\nSuppositories \nDisintegration Test for Compressed Pessaries \nDisintegration Test for Tablets, Bolues and\n301\n302 \nDissolution Test for Conventional and Prolonged-\n299\nCapsules of normal size\nrelease solid dosage forms \t\n305 \nDissolution Test for Conventional-release\n3574 Disintegration Test for Tablets, Bolues and \nCapsules of large size \t\n300\ndosage forms \t\n306, 307 \nDissolution Test for Prolonged-release\n933 \nDodecatungstosilicic acid\nDisodium Arsenate \t\n910\n942 \nDodecyldimety1-2-phenoxyethylammonium Bromide 911\n3572 Disodium 4-[(2-arsonophenyl)azo]-3-hydroxynaphthalene -\ndosage forms \t\n306, 307 \nDissolution Test for Modified-release dosage\n2,7-disulphonate \t\n927\nDodecyltriniethylammoni um Bromide \t\n911 \ntphen Bromide \t\n911\nDisodium (4,4'-biphenylbis-2,2-azo) bis( 1 -\nforms \t\n306, 307\narninonaphthalene-4-sulphonate) \n958\nVolume 1: i to xxx and I to 1068; I-I to 1-102 VOluMe xxxi to xx;XW-\n6t658; Volume 3: xxxv to xxxviii and 2659 to 4144; \nVolume 4: xxxix to xxxxii and 4145 to 4385 : \ni tri?04:\n,..‘\n1-35\nINDEX\n230,494, 1877\nDomperidone \nDomperidone and Omeprazole Capsules\n2785\n230,495, 1878\nDomperidone Maleate\n1879\nDomperidone Maleate Tablets\n1879\nDomperidone Suspension\n1879\nDomperidone Tablets\n230,495, 1881\nDonepezil Hydrochloride\n1882\nDonepezil Hydrochloride Tablets\n1882\nDonepezil Tablets\n2415\nL-Dopa \nL-Dopa Capsules\n2417\n2418\nL-Dopa Tablets \nDopamine Concentrate, Sterile\n1885\n230,496, 1883\nDopamine Hydrochloride \nDopamine Hydrochloride and Dextrose Injection\n1886\n1884\nDopamine Hydrochloride Inj"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/disopyramide-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Disopyramide Phosphate","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Disopyramide Phosphate\nC21H32N3O5P \nMol. Wt. 437.5\nDisopyramide Phosphate is (2&S)-4-[bis(l -methylethyl) \namino]-2-phenyl-2-(pyridin-2-yl)butanamide dihydrogen \nphosphate.\nDisopyramide Phosphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C2iH32N3O5P, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiarrhythmic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\n2137\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDISOPYRAMIDE PHOSPHATE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/disopyramide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Disopyramide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Disopyramide\nC2iH29N3O \nMol. Wt. 339.5\nDisopyramide is (RS)-4-(diisopropylamino)-2“phenyL \n2-(pyridin-2-yl)butyramide.\nDisopyramide contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.5 per cent of C2!H29N3O, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiarrhythmic.\nDescriptid n. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/disulfiram","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Disulfiram"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Disulfiram\nc10H20N2s4\nMol.Wt. 296.5\nDisulfiram is thioperoxydicarbonic diamide[(H2N)C(S)]2S2, \ntetraethyl-; Bis(diethylthiocarbamoyl) disulfide.\n2139\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDisulfiram contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CiOH2oN2S4.\nCategory. Used in the treatment of alcoholism.\nDescription.A white to off-white, crystalline powder"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dithranol-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dithranol Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dithranol Ointment\nDithranol Ointment contains Dithranol, in fine powder, in a \nsuitable base.\nDithranol Ointment contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of dithranol, \nCmHkjOj.\nUsual strengths. 0.1 to 2 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dithranol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dithranol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dithranol\nAnthralin; Dioxyanthranol\nOH O OH\nC14H10O3 \nMol. Wt.226.2\nDithranol is 1,8-dihydroxyanthrone. \n\\\nDithranol contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of Ci4Hi0O3, calculated on the dried basis.\nn>20?2'\nCategory. Topical antipsoriatic.\nDescription. A yellow or orange-yellow, microcrystalli^ \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/divalproex-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Divalproex Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Divalproex Sodium\nMol. Wt. 166.2\nSodium Valproate is sodium 2-propylpentanoate.\nSodium Valproate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per centofCaR,sNaOz, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\nDose. 600 mg to 1.6 g daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dobutamine-hydrochloride-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dobutamine Hydrochloride for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dobutamine Hydrochloride for Injection\nDobutamine Hydrochloride for Injection is a sterile material \nconsisting of Dobutamine Hydrochloride with or without \nexcipients. It is supplied in a sealed container.\nDobutamine Hydrochloride for Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of dobutamine, C18H23NO3.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powdersfor Injection) and with thefollowing requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dobutamine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dobutamine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dobutamine Hydrochloride\nC18H23NO3,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 337.9\nDobutamine Hydrochloride is (/?S)-4-[2-[[3-(4-hydroxyphenyl)-\n1 -methylpropyl] amino] ethyl]benzene-1,2-diol hydrochloride.\nDobutamine Hydrochloride.contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of ClsH23NO3,HCh\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Vasopressor.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2022\nDOBUTAMINE INJECTION\npesCi-jpti°n . A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with dobutamine \n/(drochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of\nB It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1), using a mixture of \nequal volumes of methanol and water."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dobutamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dobutamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dobutamine Injection\nDobutamine Intravenous Infusion\nDobutamine Injection is a sterile solution containing \nDobutamine Hydrochloride. It is; supplied as a ready-to-use \nsolution or it is prepared by diluting either Sterile Dobutamine\n2147\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDOBUTAMINE INJECTION\nConcentrate or Dobutamine Hydrochloride for Injection with \na suitable diluent in accordance with the manufacturer’s \ninstructions.\nThe injection complies with the requirements stated under \nParenteral Preparations and with the following \nrequirements.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 10 Endotoxin Units \nper ml of a 1 per cent w/v solution of Dobutamine in water for \ninjections.\nStorage. Dobutamine Injection prepared from Sterile \nDobutamine Concentrate or from Dobutamine Hydrochloride \nfor Injection should be used immediately after preparation \nbut, in any case, within the period recommended by the \nmanufacturer when prepared and stored strictly in accordance \nwith the manufacturer’s instructions.\nWhen supplied as ready to use solution, the inj ection complies \nwith the following requirements\nDobutamine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ndobutamine, Ci8H33NO3."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/docetaxel-anhydrous","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Docetaxel Anhydrous"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Docetaxel Anhydrous\nC43H53NO14 \nMol. Wt. 807.9\nDocetaxel Anhydrous is 1 p ,7 p, 1 Op -tr ihydroxy-9-oxo- \n5p,20-epoxytax-l l-ene-2a,4a,13a-triyl 4-acetate 2-benzoate \n13- {(2R,3S)-3-[(/eri-butoxycarbonyl)amino]-2-hydroxy \n-3-phenylpropanoate}.\nDocetaxel Anhydrous contains not less than 97.5 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C43H53NOi4, Calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline, hygroscopic \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/docetaxel-concentrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Docetaxel Concentrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Docetaxel Concentrate\nDocetaxel Concentrate is a sterile solution of Docetaxel in a \nsuitable vehicle.\nThe concentrate complies withthe requirements stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Concentrated solutions for\ninjections) and with the following requirements.\nDocetaxel Concentrate contains not less than 90.6 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndocetaxel, C43H53NO14."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/docetaxel-trihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Docetaxel Trihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Docetaxel Trihydrate\nC«H53NOl4,3H2O \nMol. Wt. 861.9\nDocetaxel Trihydrate is AMebenzbyl-7V-(tert-butoxycarbbnyl)-\n10-deacetyltaxol trihydrate.\nDocetaxel Trihydrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C43H53NOK calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/docusate-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Docusate Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Docusate Sodium\nDioctyl Sodium Sulphosuccinate.\nC2oH37Na07S \nMol. Wt. .444.6\nDocusate Sodium is sodium 1,4-bis[(2-ethylhexyl)oxy]-\n1,4-dioxobutane-2-sulphonate.\nDocusate Sodium contains not less than 98.0,per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C2oH37Na07S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\n2154\nCategory. Emollient laxative or purgative.\nDescription. White or almost white, waxy masses or flakes \nhygroscopic. \n’"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dolutegravir-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dolutegravir Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dolutegravir Sodium\nC2oH18F2N3Na05 \nMol. Wt. 441.4\nDolutegravir Sodium is sodium (47?,12aS)-9-[(2,4- \ndifluorobenzyl)carbamoyl]-4-methyl-6,8-dioxo-3,4,6,8,12,12a-\n2155\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDOLUTEGRAVIR SODIUM\nhexahydro-27/-pyrido[l ’,2 ’ :4,5]pyrazino[2,1 -b] [ 1,3]oxazin-7- \nolate.\nDolutegravir Sodium contains not less than 98,0 per cent and \nnot more than 102. 0 per cent of C2QHi8F2N3NaO5, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDescription. A white to light yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/domperidone-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Domperidone Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Domperidone Maleate\nCaH^CINsOz.C.HA \nMol. Wt. 542.0\nDomperidone Maleate is 5-chloro-l-[l-[3-(2,3-dihydro- \n2-oxo-lH-benzimidazol-l-yl)propyl]-4-piperidinyl]- \n1,3-dihydro-2H-benzimidazol-2-one maleate.\nDomperidone Maleate contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of domperidone maleate, \nQzH^CINjC^^ELCU, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiemetic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/domperidone-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Domperidone Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Domperidone Suspension\nDomperidone Suspension contains not less than 90.0 percent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndomperidone, C22H24CIN5O2?\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml. >"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/domperidone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Domperidone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Domperidone\nDomperidone is 5-chloro-l-[l-[3-(2-oxo-2,3-dihydrO'\n1 /T-benzi midazo 1-1 -ylpropyl]piperidin-4-yl]-1,3-dihyTo-\n2/-/-benzimidazol-2-one.\nDomperidone contains not than 99.0 per cent and more than\n101.0 per cent of C22H24CIN5O2, calculated on the dried basis-\nCategory. Antiemetic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/donepezil-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Donepezil Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Donepezil Hydrochloride\nC24H29NO3,HC1\nMol. Wt. 416.0\nCmH^NO^HCI^O\nMol. Wt. 434.0\nDonepezil Hydrochloride is (/?S)-2-[(l-benzyI-4-piperidyl) \nmethyI]-5,6-dimethoxy-l-indanone hydrochloride;\n(2RS)-2-[(l-Benzylpiperidin-4-yl)methyl]-5,6-dimethoxy-2,3- \ndihydro-lH-inden-l-one hydrochloride monohydrate.\nDonepezil Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 percent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C24H29NO3,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antialzheimer.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dopamine-hydrochloride-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dopamine Hydrochloride for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dopamine Hydrochloride for Injection\nDopamine Hydrochloride for Injection is a sterile material \nconsisting of Dopamine Hydrochloride with or without \nexcipients. It is supplied in a sealed container.\nDopamine Hydrochloride for Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated\namount of dopamine hydrochloride, CsHl!NO2,HCl.\n7^e contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements for Powders for Injections or Injections stated\nunder Parenteral Preparations and with the following \nrequirements.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dopamine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dopamine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dopamine Hydrochloride\nCsHhNO.HCI \n■ . Mol.wt 189.6\nDopatnineHydrochlonde is 4-(2-aminoethyl)behzene-l,2’diol \nhydrochloride. ’ -v \n'■ ;;-w. :■\nDopamine Hydrochloride contains not.less than 99.0 percent \nand not more than T01.0 per cent of C8HhNO2,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Vasopressor. \n■■ -\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dopamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dopamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dopamine Injection\nDopamine Hydrochloride Injection\nDopamine Injection is a sterile solution containing Dopamine. \nHydrochloride. It is supplied as a ready-to-use solution or it is \nprepared by diluting either Sterile Dopamine Concentrate dr \nDopafnine Hydrochloride for Injection with a suitable diluent \nin accordance with 'the- manufacturer’s instructions. :\nDopamine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of dopamine \nhydrochloride, C8HnNO2,HCl. \n'\nThe injection complies with the requirements stated under \nParenteral Preparations and with she following \nrequirements. ■' \n-\nDescription. Acolourless liquid.\nIdentification ....\nA. Saturate a volume containing 0.1 g of Dopamine \nHydrochloride with sodium chloride and extract with three \n2 0rml quantities of butan-l-olFilter the combined extracts \nthroughunhydrous sodium sulphate and evaporate the filtrate\n2169.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nto dryness. On the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with dopamine hydrochloride IPRS treated in the \nsame manner or with the reference spectrum of dopamine \nhydrochloride.\nB. To a volume containing 10 mg of Dopamine Hydrochloride, \nadd 0.1 ml of a 10.5 per cent w/v solution of iron(III) chloride \nhexahydrate. An intense green colour is produced."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dorzolamide-and-timolol-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dorzolamide and Timolol Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dorzolamide and Timolol Eye Drops\nDorzolamide and Timolol Eye Drops are a sterile solution of \nDorzolamide Hydrochloride and Timolol Maleate in Purified \nWater.\nDorzolamide and Timolol Eye Drops contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated\namount each of dorzolamide, Ci0H16N2O4S3 and timolol, \nCt3H24N4O3S.\nOsual strength. Dorzolamide 2 per cent w/v and timolol 0.5 \nper cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dorzolamide-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dorzolamide Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dorzolamide Hydrochloride\n: \nMoLWt 360.9\nDorzolamide Hydrochloride is (45\\6\\S')- 4-(ethylammo)-5,6- \ndihydro-6-methyl-4#4-thieno [2,3-6] thiopyran-2-sulfonamide\n7.7-dioxide, monohydrochloride.\nDorzolamide Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per\ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent ofCioIT:(.NAXiS-.. HC1, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.'\nCategory. Antiglaucoma agent.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dosulepin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dosulepin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dosulepin Hydrochloride\n,Hel\nC1gH21NS,HCl\nMol. Wt. 331.9\nDothiepin Hydrochloride is 3-(6H-dibenzo[b,e]thiepin-ll-\nylidene)propyldimethylamine hydrochloride, consisting\npredominantly ofthe E-isomer.\nl)othiepin Hydrochloride contains not less th3.ii 98.0percerit\nand not more than 101.0per cent ofC19H21NS,HCI, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDose. Initially75 mgdaily, in divideddoses, increased gradually,\nas necessary, to 150 mg daily.\nDescription. A white to faintly yellow, crystalline powder;\nodourless or almost odourless.\nA. Determineby infraredabsorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nReference solution. A 0.01 per cent w/v solution of donepezil\nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with dothiepin\nhydrochloride RS in mobile phase.\nhydrochloride RS or with the reference spectrum ofdothiepin\nChromatographic system\nhydrochloride.\n-\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\nB. Dissolve 1mgin 5 mlofsulphuric acid; a dark red colouris\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 lJIIl),\nproduced.\n-\ncolumn temperature. 40°,\n-\nmobile phase: a mixture of 60 volumes ofbufferpH 2.2\nC. Oil 20fig dlitel'IIlin.ebY the oxygen~fla.skmethod (2.3.34),\nprepared by dissolving about 6.8 g of potassium\nusing amixtureofl5 mlofwaterand 1mlofhydrogenperoxide\ndihydrogenphosphatein 1000 ml ofwater. Add 5ml of\nsolution (20 volume) as the absorbing liquid. The solution\n'Trfetl1yTami,ie'and·'-a'd]ust'iJle\"plf'to\"'2::f:Wfffi .·givest1fereactionsofsulphates(2.3~1):'-~\"\"--'-'-\""}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dothiepin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dothiepin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dothiepin Hydrochloride\nDosulepin Hydrochloride\nCI9H2!NS,HC1 . \n. . Mol. Wt. 331.9\nDothiepin Hydrochloride is 3-(677-dibenzo[6,e]thiepin- 11- \nylidene) propyldimethyl amine hydrochloride, consisting \npredominantly of the E-isomer.'\nDothiepinHydrochloride.contains notless than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C l9H2INS,HCl, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant\nDescription. A white to faintly yellow, crystalline'pbwder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/doxapram-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Doxapram Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Doxapram Hydrochloride\nC24H30N2O2,HCl,H2O \nMol. Wt. 433.00\n'2180\nDoxapram Hydrochloride is (7?5)-l-Ethyl-4-(2-morpholinQt \nethyl)-3,3-diphenyL2-pyrrolidonc hydrochloride monohydra;e\nDoxapram Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 perce . \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of C24H30N2O2,HCl, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Respiratory stimulant.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/doxapram-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Doxapram Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Doxapram Injection\nDoxapramHydrochloride Injection,\nDoxapram Injection is a sterile solution of Doxapram \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nDoxapram Injection contains not less than 90.0;per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of doxapram. hydrochloride, \nc24h30n2o2,hci,h2o. \n; ’\nUsual strength. 20 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/doxepin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Doxepin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Doxepin Hydrochloride\nC19H21NO,HC1\nMol. Wt. 315.8\nDoxepin Hydrochloride is 3-(6H-dibenz[Z)Je]oxepin-l 1- ylidene) \npropyldimethylamine hydrochloride. It consists of a mixture \nof Z and E isomers.\nDoxepin Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C19H2iNO,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/doxofylline","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Doxofylline"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Doxofylline\nC11H14N4O4 \n' Mol. Wt. 266.3\nDoxofylline is 7-(l,3-dioxolan-2-ylmethyl)-3,7-dihydrd-\n1,3-dimethyl-1 /7-purinc-2,6-dione.\nDoxofylline contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more, \nthan 102.0 per cent of C11HI4N4O4j calculated on the dried \nbasis. \n7\nCategory .Bronchodilator. ?: ?,., ? • • • \n.\nDescription. A white to off white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/doxorubicin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Doxorubicin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Doxorubicin Hydrochloride\nC27H29NOn>HCl \nMol. Wt. 580'0\nDoxorubicin Hydrochloride is (8S',10S)-10-[(3-aminp-2,3,6- \ntrideoxy-a -^/jao-hexopyranosy^oxyl-d^,! 1-trihydroxy- 8- \nhydroxyacetyl- l-methoxy-7,8,9,1O-tetrahydronaphthacene- \n5,12-dione hydrochloride, a substance produced by the growth \nof certain strains of Streptomyces coeruleorubidus or \npeucetius or obtained by any other means.\nDoxorubicin Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C^H^NOibHCl, \ncalculated on the anhydrous and solvent-free basis.\nCategory. Cytotoxic.\nDescription. An orange-red, crystalline powder; hygroscopic.\nCAUTION — Doxorubicin Hydrochloride is poisonous. It \nmust be handled 'with care avoiding contact with skin and \ninhalation of airborne particles."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/doxorubicin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Doxorubicin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Doxorubicin Injection\nDoxorubicin Hydrochloride Injection\nDoxorubicin Injection is a sterile solution of Doxorubicin \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections made isotonic with \nSodium Chloride, Dextrose or other suitable added \nsubstances. It is either supplied as preformed solution or it is \nprepared by dissolving the contents of . a sealed container \ncontaining Doxorubicin Hydrochloride with or without auxiliary \nsubstances in the requisite amount of Water for Injections or \nSodium Chloride Inj ection as directed on the label.\nDoxorubicin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nn°t more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount' of \ndoxorubicinhydrochloride,C27H29N011,HCl.\nUsual strength. 2 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/doxycycline-hyclate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Doxycycline Hyclate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Doxycycline Hyclate\nC22H24N2O8JHC1,,/2C2H6O,1/2H2O \nMol. Wt. 513.0\nDoxycycline Hydrochloride is (41S, 4a/?,55',5a7?,6/?,12aX)-4- \ndimethylamino-l,4,4a,5,5a,6,ll,12a-octahydro-3,5,10,12,12a- \npentahydroxy-6-methyl-l,l l-dioxonaphthacene-2- \ncarboxamide hydrochloride hemiethanolate hemihydrate, an \nantimicrobial substance obtained from oxy tetracycline or \nmethacycline or by any other .means.\nDoxocycline Hydrochloride is not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C22H25C1N2O8, calculated on \nanhydrous and ethanol free basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. Ayellow, crystalline powder; hygroscopic,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/draksha","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Draksha"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Draksha"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dried-aluminium-hydroxide-gel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dried Aluminium Hydroxide Gel"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dried Aluminium Hydroxide Gel\nDriedAluminiumHydroxide; HydratedAluminium Oxide\nDried Aluminium Hydroxide Gel consists largely ofhydrated\naluminium oxide together with varying quantities of basic\naluminium carbonate and bicarbonate.\nDried Aluminium Hydroxide Gel contains not less than\n47.0 per cent and not more than 60.0 per cent ofAl20 3•\nDose. 500 mg to Ig.\nDescription. A white, light, amorphous powder containing\nsome aggregates; odourless; tasteless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dried-ferrous-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dried Ferrous Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dried Ferrous Sulphate\nDried Ferrous Sulphate is Ferrous Sulphate from which a part \nof the water of crystallization has been removed by drying.\nDried Ferrous Sulphate contains not less,than 86.0per cent \nand not more than 90.0 percent of FeSO4. - ;.:q: ; \n:\nCategory. Haematinic. \n...\nDescription. A greyish white to buff coloured powder.\nIt gives reaction (A) of ferrous' salts and the reactions of \nsulphates (2.3.1). .\nCopper. Dissolve 8.0 g in 40 ml of hydrochloric acid, add\n10 ml of nitric acid and 15ml ofwater, boil gently for 5 minutes\n4079\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDRTED.FERROUS SULPHATE\n¿rid cool. Shake with fourquanti ties, each of 30 ml; ofether \nand discard thé ether extracts. Heat the acid solution on a \nwater-bath to remove the dissolved e/Aer, cool arid add \nsufficient water to produce 100.0 rril (solution A). To 10.0 ml of \nsolution A add 1 g of citric acid, make alkaline with dilïite \nammonia solution, add 25 rill of water ¿nd 5 ml of sodium \ndiethyldithiocarbamate solution. Extract successively with \n5,3 and 2 nil quantities of carbon tetrachloride, mix the carbon \ntetrachloride extracts and add sufficient carbon tetrachloride \nto produce 100.0 rnl. The iesulting solution is hot more intensely \ncoloured than a solution prepared by treating 4.0 ml of copper \nstandard solution (10 ppm Cu) and 7.5 ml of water in the \nsame manner (50 ppm).\nLead.Make25.0 ml of solution Aalkaline with dilute ammonia \nsolution, add 1 mlet potassium cyanide solution and sufficient \nwater to produce 50.0 ml. Add 0.1 ml of sodium sulphide \nsolution; the solution is not more intensely coloured than a \nmixture of 10 nd.of.hydrochloric acid, 0.5 ml of nitric acid, \n5.0 ml oîleadstandardsolution.fëOppm Pb), 0.1 ml of sodium \nsulphide solution and; sufficient water to produce 50.0 ml\n(50ppm).\nZinc. To 2.5 ml of solution A add 1 g of,citric acid and 1 g of \nresorcinol, neutralise the solution with dilute ammonia \nsolution usings thymol blue solution as indicator and shake \nfor 1 minute with two quantities, each of 20 ml, of dithizone \nsolution. To the combined extracts add 1Ô ml of 0.1 M \nhydrochloric acid and shake for 1 minute. Separate the acid\nlayer, add 3 ml of 1Mhydrochloric acid and 20 rnl Of ammonium \nchloride solution arid adjust the volume to 50.0 ml with water. \nAdd 1.0 ml of potassium ferrocyanide solution and allow to\nstand for 15 fninutes. Any turbidity produced is not greater \nthan that developed in 15 minutes by the addition of 1.0 ml of \npotassium ferrocyanide solution to a freshly prepared mixture\nof 10.0 ml of zinc standard solution (10 ppm Zn), 4 ml of 1 M \nhydrochloric acid, 20 ml of ammonium chloride solu tion and \nsufficient wafer to produce 50.0 ml (500 ppm).\nManganese. Dissolve 1.0 g in 40 ml of water, add, 10 ml of \nnitric acid and boil until red firmes are evolved. Add 0.5 g of \nammoniumpersùlphate and boil for TO rriinutes.' Discharge \nany pink colour by the dropwise addition of a 5 per Cent w/v \nsolution of sodium sulph ite and boil until any odour of sulphur \ndioxide is eliminated. Add 10 ml of water, 5 ml of phosphoric \nacid and 0.5 g of sodium periodate, boil for 1 minute arid \nallow to cool. The resulting solution is not more.intensely \ncoloured than that of a solution prepared at the same time arid \nin the same manner using 1.0 mb of 0.02 M potassium \npermanganate in place of the substance under examination\n(0.1 per cent).\nArsenic (2.3.10). Dissolve 3.3 g in 10 ml of water, add 15 ml of \nstannated hydrochloric acid and distill 20 ml.- To the distillate \nadd a few drops of bromine solution; remove the excess of\n4080\n.................................................................................._ ^202|\nbromine with a few drops of stannous chloride solution^ \nand add 40, ml of water. The resulting solution, complies with \nthe limit test for arsenic (3 ppm).\nBasic sulphate. 2.0 g dissolves slowly in a mixture of 7.5\nfreshly boiled and cooled water and Q.5 mlof 0.5 M sulphur \nacid, producing a solution that is not inor"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/drospirenone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Drospirenone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Drospirenone\nCACh ' \n1 \n- Mol.Wt.366.5\nDrospirenone is 3-Oxo-60, 70: 150, 160 -dimethylene- 17a- \npregh-4-en-21,17-carbo lactone.\nDrospirenone contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 102.0 per cent of \nE0O5, calculated on the dried\nbasis. \n' \n'\nCategory. Aldosterone receptor antagonist.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/drotaverine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Drotaverine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Drotaverine Hydrochloride\nC24H3!NO4,HC1 \n. Mol.wt.434.0\nDrotaverine Hydrochloride is (Z)-l-(3,4-Diethbxybenzylidene)- \n6,7-diethoxy-l ,2,3,4-tetrahydroisoquiholine hydrochloride.\nDrotaverine Hydrochloride contains hot less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more, than 102.0 per cent of C24H3iNO4>HCl,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticholinergic.\nDescription. Light yellow to yellow with greenish tinge’ \ncrystalline powder.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2022\nIdentified011\n, petertnine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4,6). \nCompare the. spectrum with that obtained by drotaverine\nArochlor ide IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \ndrotaverine hydrochloride.\nB [t gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1)..\nTests .\npH (2.4-24)- 3.5 to 5.0, determined on 1.0 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2.414).\nTest solution. Dissolve 25 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 25.0 ml with the \nmobile phase. -\nReference solution. Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to 200.0\nml with the mobile phase.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column, 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: amixture of 48 volumes of acetonitrile, 8\nvolumes of methanol and 44 volumes of buffer solution \nprepared by dissolving 21.8 g of sodium acetate in about \n50.0 ml of water, add 60 ml of acetic acid and dilute to \n1000.0 ml with water,\n- flow rate: T ml per minute, \n1\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm, ■\n- injection volume: 20 pL\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid'unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 1500 theoretical plates and\nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nInject the reference^ solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained withlhe test solution, the area of any \nsecondary -peak is not more than the' area of principal peak in \nthe chromatogram obtained, with the .reference solution (0.5\nper cent) and the sum ofthe areas of all the secondary peaks\n!S not more than twice the area of principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained, with the. reference solution (1.0 per\ncent). Ignore any peak with an area.less than 0,1 times the area \nof the principal peak in the. chromatogram obtained, with the \nreference solution (0.05 per cent).\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \n^cavy metals, Method B (20 ppm) \n■ \n? ’\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 percent.\nUss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 5.0 per cent, determined \n°n 1.0 g by drying in oven at 105° for 3 hours. i • .\n^ssay. Dissolve 0.4 g in a mixture of 50 ml glacial acetic acid\nafld 10 ml of mercuric acetate solution. Titrate with 0.TM\nDROTAVERINE TABLETS\nperchloric acid, determining the end point potentiometrically\n(2.4.25) Carry out a blank titration.\n1ml of 0. IM perchloric acid is-equivalent to 0.0434 g of \nCnHhNO^HCI.\nStorage. Store protected from moisture.\nDrotaverine Tablets\nDrotaverine Hydrochloride Tablets\nDrotaverine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated aniount of \ndrotaverine hydrochloride, C2JL-.NO4.HCI.\nUsual strengths. 40 mg; 80 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/duloxetine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Duloxetine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Duloxetine Hydrochloride\nC18Hi9NOS JICI \nMol. Wt. 333.9\nDuloxetine Hydrochloride is (S)-jV-Methyl-3-(l-naphthyloxy)- \n3-(2-thienyl) propan-1-amine hydrochloride.\nDuloxetine Hydrochloride contains not less than 97.5 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C^H^NOS, HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dutasteride-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dutasteride Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dutasteride Capsules\nTime \t\nMobile phase B \n(in mil.) \n(per cent w/v) \t\n(per cent v/v)\nDutasteride Capsules contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndutasteride, C271130F6N202.\n40 \n0 \n60 \n6 \n60 \n40\nUsual strength. 0.5 mg.\n10 \n50 \n50 \n25 \n40 \n60 \n35 \n40 \n60 \n40 \n30 \n70 \n45 \n60 \n40  \n50 \n60 \n40"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/dydrogesterone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Dydrogesterone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Dydrogesterone\nC21H2SO2\n■Mol. Wt. 312.5\nDydrogesterone is 9(3,10a-pregna-4.6-diene-3,20-dione.\nDydrogesterone contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C2iH28O2 calculated on the dried \nbasis/\nCategory. Progestogen\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ebastine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ebastine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ebastine Tablets\nEbastine Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of ebastine, \nC32H39NO2. \n='• \n-\nUsual strengths. 5 mg; 10 mg; 20 mg. \n■■"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/eberconazoie-nitrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eberconazoie Nitrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Eberconazoie Nitrate\nCfgHwCWHNCb \nMol. Wt. 392.2\nEberconazoie Nitrate is (7?*S)-l-(2,4-DichIoro-10,l 1-dihydro- \n5ET-dibenzo[o;£7]-5-cycloheptenyl)-177-imidazple nitrate.\nEberconazoie Nitrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of CigHwCljN^HNCb, calculated \non the dried basis,\nCategory. Antifungal. \n.\nDescription: A white or off-white, crystalline powder. ’"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/econazole-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Econazole Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Econazole Cream\nEconazole Nitrate Cream\nEconazole Cream contains Econazole Nitrate in a suitable cream \nbase.\nEconazole Cream contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of econazole \nnitrate, C18H15C13N2O, HNO3.\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w. ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/econazole-nitrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Econazole Nitrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Econazole Nitrate\nC18H15C13N2O,HNO3 \nMol. Wt. 444,7\nEcdnazole Nitrate is (R5)-l-[2-[(4-chlorophenyl)methoxy]- \n2-(2,4-dichlorophenyl)ethyl]-1//-imidazole nitrate.\nEconazole Nitrate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.5 per cent of Ci8Hi5Cl3N2O,HNO3, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/efavirenz","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Efavirenz"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Efa