rs\nce\n0.016-0.017\n0.027-0.029\n0.052\n0.084\n0.153\n; 0.054\nThal hum-202 (2CCT1)\n12.23 (2) days\n.eA •\nce ’\n•. 0.357\nLead-203 (203Pb)\n•51:873 (9) hours\n' Va\n0.055\n■ 3.0''\nX\n0.010\n0.071-0.073\n■ce:\n0.194\n13.3\nAstatine-211 (21 'At)\n7.214 hours\nOt:\n5.869 .\n41.80\ny\n0.0792\n20.7\nBismuth-213 (213Bi)\n45.59 min.\na\nP t\n5.869\n0.4922 (max: 1.423)\n0-3204(max: 0.983)\nActinium-225 (225Ac)\n10 days\na :\n5.830\n5.792\n(l)Mean energy of the p spectrum.. .\n^Maximum emission probability corresponding to a total annihilation in the 'source per 100 disintegrations.\n(^Monographie BIPM-5, Table of radionuclides (2004 and updated 2006).\nElectronic emission \n■\nPhoton emission .\nType\nEnergy1 1 Emission \n.. : (MeV)=; \nprobability\nEmission \nprobability\n(per 100 \ndisintegrations)\n(per 100 \ndisintegrations)\n69\n0.083\n19\n. 8-5\n2\nY .\n. .0.331\n79\n9.9\n0.316\n2.3\n0.361\n. 2.0\n0.406\n3.6\n0.585\n: 0.692\n4.3\n0.767\n3.2\n0.826\n2.4\n0.908 '\n5.7\n0.946\n1.099\n7.9\n1.8\n1277\n1.6\nX\n0.010\n0.069-0.071\n46.0\n17.7\n73.7\n20.4\n4.1\n0.080\n7.2\n15.4\n2.6.\ny\n0.135 .\n2.6\n10.0\n0.167\n. 2.8\nX\n31.0\n0,010\n61.6\n0.069-0.071\n= 2.4\n•• •' 17.1\n91.4\n0.080\ny :\n0.440\n37.0\n69.6\n19.4\n0.083\n80.8\n0.279\nY\n0.401\n3.4\n1.94\n65.8\n■■■Y\n0.440\n25.94.\n30.74\n0.6\nY\n0.150\n50.7\n0.45\n0.188\n18.1\n4729\nwww.webofpharma.com\nRADlOPHARMACEU'nCALPRLPARATlO'NS\nIP;2022\n6.0 Positron Emission Tomography \nRadiopharmaceuticals (PET-RPs) and Associated \nRadiopharmacy Practices\n6.1 Introduction \n...<\nMost of the radionuclides used in Positron Emission \nTomography Radiopharmaceuticals (PET-RPs) are ones with \nshort physical half-lives, T1/2 (e.g.,Ti/2of 18F = 109.8 min,68Ga \n= 67.7 min,1 '(' = 20.4 min, 13N = 9.96 min, 15O = 2.03 min and\n82Rb =’ 70 s). As a result, these radionuclides usually are \nproduced using dedicated cyclotrons (particle accelerators, \nthat produce a proton or deuteron beam) or from generators\nTable I. Differences and similarities between 99mTc-And PET-Radiopharmacy(9\n99mTc Radiopharmacy\nT'A of 99mTc = 6 h .\nSterile 99nlTcO4 produced on site from Generator.\n99tnTc RPs are complexes of 99niTc and a chelating agent \nwith required properties and are, hence, easy to prepare - \nno chemical synthesis.\n‘Cold’ sterile reagents available in a vial - just add sterile\n99m\n1 \nand mix well (no further processing or minimal\nwithout opening the vial).\nLow risk Radiopharmacy: Quality Control of radiopharma­\nceutical is minimal since the generator produced sterile a \nand endotoxin free 99mTcO4 is aseptically added to the sterile \nand endotoxin free hold kit’ for making the desired 99mTc-RP.\n‘Parametric release ’permitted if quality assurance is \nvalidated.\nThe prepared radiopharmaceutical can be used throughout \nthe dayyatleast 8 hours.\ny = 140 keV, 20 mm lead shielding is adequate.\nMulti-patient dose in a single vial possible. Upto 13GBq \n(~350 mCi) of 99mTc-RP\nShould be Sterile\nEndotoxin levels below 175 per Va(IU)b \nEndotoxin levels below .175 per Va(IU)b s .\nJV: Maximum recommended dose. bIU: International units. \n.. .....\n’loGBq of WmTc and 1.85GBq of ,SF point sources would have a comparable radiation field at I cm. Hence, dose to the personnel handling them \ncould be comparable (i.e., administering them to patients etc) provided they follow the ALARA principle of minimizing exposure to radiation,.\n4730\n(similanin principle to the \"Mo/99mTc generators), and then \nare processed Anto the final PET-RPs for use in PET- \ninvcstigations. Duc to their short T^ the PET-RPs have to be' \nproduced cldse to the nuclear medicine centres where they \nare used. Further, the short T1/2 poses several constraints in \nthe formulation and.testing of PET-RPs. This chapter on PET- \nRPs--is. an annexure to .the main General Chapter op; \nRadiopharmaceuticals to emphasize the differences that exist \nvis-a-vis. the production and use of 99mTc-RPs, the workhorse \nof SRECT (Single Photon Emission Computed Tomogra"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/enoxaparin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Enoxaparin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Enoxaparin Injection\nEnoxaparin Sodium Injection\nEnoxaparin Injection is a sterile solution of Enoxaparin Sodium \nin Water for Inj ections.\nThe potency is not less than 90,0 per cent and not more than\n110.0 per cent stated in terms of International Anti-factor X„ \nUnits (IU). It may contain, in multiple-dose containers, , a \nsuitable antimicrobial preservative, such as benzyl alcohol.\nUsual strengths. 20 mg (2000 Anti-factor Xa units); 40 mg \n(4000 Anti-factor Xa units); 60 mg (6000 Anti-factor Xa units); \n80 mg (8000 Anti-factor Xaunits)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/enrofloxacin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Enrofloxacin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Enrofloxacin\nCigH2FN3O, sie \n“ \n“Mal. Wt 359.4\nEnrofloxacin is !- Cyclopropyl- 7- -(4-ethyl L-piperazinyl)-\n6-fluoro-1 ,4-dihydro-4-oxo-3-quinolonecarboxylic acid:::\nEnrofloxacin contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more\nthan 101-5 percent of CisHaaFN:Os, calculated on the dried\nbasis:\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription, A. Pale: yellowish or light yellow, crystalline \npowder. \n: \n:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/entacapone-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Entacapone Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Entacapone Tablets\nEntacapone Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nentacapone, C^HjiNjOs.\nUsual strength. 200 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/entacapone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Entacapone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Entacapone\nC!4HISN3O5 \nMol .Wt. 305.3\nEntacapone is (2E)-2-Cyano-3-(3,4-dihydroxy-5-nitrophenyl)~ \n;V,A'-diethyl-2-propenamide.\nEntacapone contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Cl4H15N3O5 ,calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiparkinson."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/entecavir-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Entecavir Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Entecavir Tablets\nEntecavir Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nthan 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of entecavir, \nci2H15N5O3.\nUsual strengths. 0.5 mg; 1.0 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/entecavir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Entecavir"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Entecavir\nEntecavir Monohydrate\nCl2H15N5O3,H2O \nMol,Wt.295.3\nEntecavir is 2-amino-l,9-dihydrO“9-[(15',37?,45)-4hydroxy-3- \n(hydro xymethyl)-2-me thy len ecyclop entylj-6//-pur in-6- \none,monohydrate.\n2238\nIp202j\nEntecavir contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C!2H15N5O3, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription, A white or almost white, crystalline powder"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/epalrestat","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Epalrestat"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Epalrestat\nC15H13NO3S2 \nMol. Wt. 319.4\nEpalrestat is 2-{(5Z)-5-[(2E)-2-Methyb3-phenylprop-2-en-l- \nylidene]-4-oxo-2-thioxothiazolidin-3-yl} acetic acid.\nEpalrestat contains not less than 98.0 per cent and.not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C15Hi3NO3S2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antidiabetic.\nDescription. A yellow to orange crystalline powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11).'"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ephedrine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ephedrine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ephedrine Hydrochloride\nEphedrine Hydrochloride is (17?,2S)-2-methylamim>\n1 - phenylpropan-1-ol hydrochloride.\n2242\nEPALRESTAT TABLETS\nIP 20$\nEphedrine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci0H15NO,HC1 caIcu|aE^ \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Sympathomimetic; bronchodilator.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder \nIt is affected by light."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ephedrine-nasal-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ephedrine Nasal Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ephedrine Nasal Drops\nEphedrine Nasal Drops are a solution of Ephedrine \nHydrochloride in a suitable aqueous vehicle.\nEphedrine Nasal Drops contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nephedrine hydrochloride, C!0H15NO,HC1.\nUsual strengths. 0.5 per cent; 0.75 per cent; 1.0 per cent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ephedrine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ephedrine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ephedrine Oral Solution\nEphedrine Hydrochloride Oral Solution; Ephedrine \nHydrochloride Elixir; Ephedrine Elixir\nEphedrine Oral Solution is a solution containing 0.3 per cent \nw/v of Ephedrine Hydrochloride in a suitable flavoured vehicle \ncontaining a sufficient volume of Ethanol (95 per cent) or of \nan appropriate dilute ethanol to give afinal concentration of \nnot more than 3 per cent v/v of ethanol. . . .\n=2244\nEPHEDRINE NASAL DROPS\nIP 2022\nEphedrine Oral Solution contains riot less than 0.27 per Cet] \nand not more than 0.33 per cent w/v of ephedr' \nhydrochloride, CI0Hj5N(LHC1. \nItle"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/epinastine-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Epinastine Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Epinastine Eye Drops\nEpinastine Hydrochloride Eye Drops\nEpinastine Eye Drops is a sterile solution of Epinastine \nHydrochloride in Purified Water.\nEpinastine Eye Drops contain not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnoi more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of epinastine \nhydrochloride, C16H15N3, HC1.\nEPINASTINE EYE DROPS\nUsual strength. 0.05 percent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/epinastine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Epinastine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Epinastine Hydrochloride\nC!6H15N3,HC1\nMol. Wt 285.8\nEpinastine Hydrochloride is (/?S)-3-Amino-9,13b-dihydro-\n17Z-dibenz[c,f]imidazo[l,5-a]azepinehydrochloride.\nEpinastine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci6Hi5N3,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\n2246\n^2022\nCategory. Antihistaminic\nDescription. A white or almost white, hygroscopic, crystalli \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/epinephrine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Epinephrine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Epinephrine\nC9H13NO3 \nMol. Wt. 183.2\nAdrenaline is (7?)-l-(3,4-dihydroxyphenyl)-2-methylamino- \nethanol.\nAdrenaline contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 percent of C5H13NO3, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Sympathomimetic.\nDescription. A white or creamy-white, microcrystalline powder \nor granules. It gradually darkens on exposure to light and air, \ndecomposition being faster in the presence of moisture and at \nhigher temperatures."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/epirubicin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Epirubicin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Epirubicin Hydrochloride\nO OH O\nNH2\nC27H30CLNO;i \nMol. Wt. 580.0\nEpirubicin Hydrochloride is (85',10iS)-10“[(3“AminO“2,3,6- \ntrideoxy-a-Z-araZu«o-hexopyranosyl)oxy]-6,8,ll-trihydrqxy- \n8-(hydroxyacetyl)-l-methoxy-7,8,9,10-tetrahydrotetracene- \n5,12-dione hydrochloride.\nEpirubicinHydrochloride contains not less than 97.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C27H30ClNOib calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticaricer.\nDescription. An orange-red powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/epirubicin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Epirubicin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Epirubicin Injection\nEpirubicin Hydrochloride Inj ection \n\"\nEpirubicin Injection is a sterile solution of Epirubicin \nhydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nEpirubicin Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of epirubicin \nhydrochloride, Cfyl fyClNO: j.\nUsual strengths. 2 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/eplerenone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eplerenone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Eplerenone\nC24H30O6 \nMol. wt. 414.5\nEplerenone is 9a, 1 l-Epoxy-7a-methoxycarbonyl-3-oxo-17a-\npregn-4-ene-21,17-carbolactone.\nEplerenone contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C24H30O6, calculated on the dried basis-\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nlderitifica^on\nrmine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare ^ie sPectrum T^at °^ta’ne^ with eplerenone\npHS or with the reference spectrum of eplerenone."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/epsom-salts","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Epsom Salts"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Epsom Salts\nMgSO4,7H2O \nMol.Wt.246.5\nMagnesium Sulphate contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of MgSO4, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Osmotic laxative; used in the treatment of electrolyte \ndeficiency.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white,.crystalline powder.\nIf 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/eptitlbatide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eptitlbatide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Eptitlbatide Injection\nEptifibatide Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \neptifibatide, C351149N1109%.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nUsual strength. 0.75 mg per ml; 2 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ergometrine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ergometrine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ergometrine Injection\nErgometrine Maleate Injection; Ergonovine Injection, \nErgonovine Maleate Injection\nErgometrine Injection is a sterile solution of Ergometrine \nMaleate in Water for Injections containing suitable stabilising \nagents.\nErgometrine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nflot more than 110,0 per cent of the stated amount of \nergometrine maleate, Ci^NsO^^. .\nUsual strength. 500 pg per ml.\nDescription . A clear, colourless or faintly yellow solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ergometrine-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ergometrine Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ergometrine Maleate\nErgonovine Maleate\nC19H23N3O2,C4H4O4 \n. Mol.WL441.5t\nErgometrine Maleate is 9,10-didehydro-A-[(iS)-2-hydroxy- .\n1 -methylethyl] -6-methylergolme-8£-carboxamide hydrogen - \nmaleate.\n2254\n2022\nErgometrine Maleate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C]91 UNTKCJ LtO.;, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Uterine stimulant.\nDescription. A white or faintly yellow, crystalline powder. It js \naffected by light."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ergotamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ergotamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ergotamine Injection\nErgotamine Tartrate Injection\nErgotamine Injection is a sterile solution of Ergotamine Tartrate \nin Water for Injection containing Ethanol (95 per cent), Glycerin \nand sufficient Tartaric Acid to adjust the pH of the solution to \n3.3.\nErgotamine Injection contains a quantity of total alkaloids, \ncalculated as (C33H35N5O5)2,C4H6O6, equivalent to not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of ergotamine tartrate.\nUsual strength. 500 pg per ml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or almost colourless solution.\n2258\n<1*2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ergotamine-tartrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ergotamine Tartrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ergotamine Tartrate\nH OH r—y\nr\\ M O i / l\nOH\nhooc^cooh\nOH\n(C33H35N5O5)2£4HA \nMol. Wt. 1313.4\nErgotamine Tartrate is (5'5)-12'-hydroxy-2'-methyl-\n3',6', 18-trioxo-5-benzylergotaman tartrate.\nErgotamine Tartrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of (C33H35N5O5)2,C4H6O6, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antimigraine.\nDescription. Colourless crystals, or a white or almost white, \ncrystalline powder; slightly hygroscopic. It may contain two \nmolecular equivalents of methanol of crystallisation."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/erlotinib-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Erlotinib Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Erlotinib Hydrochloride\nErlotinib Hydrochloride is N-(3-ethynylphenyl)-.\n6,7“bis(2-methoxyethoxy)-4-quinazolinamine hydrochloride.\nErlotinib Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 Perce^ \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C22H23N3O4. HC1, calculi\non anhydrous basis.\nwww.webofpharma.com\n[p 2022\nERLOTINIB TABLETS\nCategory- Anticancer.\npescription. An off-white to pale yellowpowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/erlotinib-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Erlotinib Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Erlotinib Tablets\nErlotinib Hydrochloride Tablets\nErlotinib Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the' stated amount of erlotinib, \nC22H23N3O4\nUsual strengths. 25mg; 100 mg; 150 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/erythromycin-stearate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Erythromycin Stearate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Erythromycin Stearate\nC37H67NO13,C18H36O2;• ■ ■ \n' ’ Mol. Wt. 10184\nErythromycin Stearate is a mixture of the stearate of \nErythromycin with an excess of stearic acid.\nErythromycin Stearate has a potency not less.than 600 Units \nof erythromycin per mg, calculated on anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. Colourless or slightly yellow crystals or a white \nor slightly yellow, crystalline powder. -"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/erythromycin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Erythromycin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Erythromycin\nC37H67NO13 \nMol. Wt. 733.9\nErythromycin is a mixture of macrolide antibiotics consisting \nlargely of erythromycin A, (3R,4S,5S,6R,7 R,9R,112?,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/erythropoietin-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Erythropoietin for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Erythropoietin for Injection\nErythropoietin for Injection is a freeze dried sterile preparation \nprepared from erythropoietin concentrated solution. It is \nsupplied in a sealed container.\nWhere the product contains human serum albumin, it does \nnot comply with the test for Dimers and related substances of \nhigher molecular weight; however the manufacturing process \nis validated to: show that aggregation does hot occur.\nUsual strengths. 1000IU per container, 2000IU per container, \n3000IU per container, 4000 -IU per container, 5000 IU per \ncontainer, 6000 IU per container and 10,000 IU per container."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/escitalopram-oxalate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Escitalopram Oxalate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Escitalopram Oxalate\nCOOH\n’ COOH \n3\nC20H21FN2O,C2H2O4 \nMol. Wt. 414.4\nEscitalopram Oxalate is (5)-l-[3-(dimethylamino)propyl]-\n1 -(4-fluorophenyl)-1,3-dihydro-5-isobenzofurancarbonitrile \noxalate.\nEscitalopram Oxalate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C20H2iFN2O.C2H2O4, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. A white to slightly yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/eserine-salicylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eserine Salicylate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Eserine Salicylate \n...\nH3C-NH\nCl5H2[N3O2, C.lfy); \nMol. Wt. 413.5\n' \n3269\nwww.webofpharma.com\nPhysostigmine Salicylate is (3aS,8aR)- l,2,3,3a,8,8a-hexahydro-\n1,3 a, 8-trimethylpyrrolo[2,3 r&]indol-5-yl. methylcarbamate \nsalicylate. \n; ••. \n■ r •\nPhysostigmine Salicylate contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci;H2iN3O2, C7H6O3, \ncalculated on the dried basis.'\nCategory. Anticholinesterase.\nDescription. A colourless or faintly yellow crystals, turning \nred gradually under the action of air and light and rapidly in \npresence of moisture."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/eslicarbazepine-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eslicarbazepine Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Eslicarbazepine Acetate\no bcH3\nQ\nO^NH2\nC17H16N2O3 \nMol Wt 296.3\nEslicarbazepine Acetate is (5)-10-Acetoxy- 10.11-dihydro- \n5H-dibenz[b, f] azepine- 5-carboxamide.\nEslicarbazepine Acetate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of Ci7H16N2O3, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiepileptic\nDescription. Awhite powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/esmolol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Esmolol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Esmolol Hydrochloride\nCusH^NCMHCl \nMol. Wt. 331.8\nEsmolol Hydrochloride is Methyl (7?tS')-3-[4-(2-hydroxy-3 - \nisopropylaminopropoxy)phenyl]propanoate hydrochloride.\nEsmolol Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of Ci6H25NO4,HCl, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive,\nDescription. A white to off white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/esomeprazole-magnesium-trihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Esomeprazole Magnesium Trihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Esomeprazole Magnesium Trihydrate\n(Ci7H18N3O3S)2Mg.3H2O \n, \nMol. Wt. 767.2\nEsomeprazole Magnesium Trihydrate is 5-methoxy-2-{(/?)-\n[(4-methoxy-3,5-dimethyl-2-pyridinyl)methyl]sulflnyl}“ \n177-benzirnidazole magnesium trihydrate.\nEsomeprazole Magnesium Trihydrate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent arid not more than 102.0 per cent of \n(Ci7H!8N3O3S)2,Mg, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiulcer.\nDescription. A white to slightly coloured powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/etacrynic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Etacrynic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Etacrynic Acid\nMol. Wt. 303.1\nEthacrynic Acid is 2-[2,3-dichloro-4-(2-ethylacryloyl)\nphenoxy]acetic acid\nEthacrynic Acid.contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 102.0 per cent of C13HI2Ch04, calculated on the\n.. \"driedbasis:\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDose. 50 to 200 mg daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nCAUTION - As Ethacrynic Acid irritates the skin, eyes and\nthe mucous membranes it should be handled with care."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethacrynic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethacrynic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethacrynic Acid\nEtacrynicAcid\nO Cl\nC13H12C12O4 \nMol. Wt 303.1\nEthacrynic Acid is 2-[2,3-dichloro-4-(2-ethylacryloyl) \nphenoxy] acetic acid;\nEthacrynic Acid.contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C13H12CI2Q4, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder.\nCA UTION—As Ethacrynic Acid irritates the skin, eyes and \nthe mucous membranes it should be handled with care."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethambutol-dihydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethambutol Dihydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethambutol Dihydrochloride\nC10H24N2O2^HCl\nMol. Wt. 277.2\nEthambutol Hydrochloride is (5',.S’)-A/',;V'-ethylenebis(2- \naminobutan-l-ol) dihydrochloride.\n2281\nwww.webofpharma.com\nEthambutol Hydrochloride contains not less than 97.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C10H24N2O2,2HCl, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antituberculosis.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethambutol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethambutol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethambutol Hydrochloride\nCIOH~202,2HCI\nMol. Wt. 277.2\nEthambutol Hydrochloride is (S,S)-N,N'-ethylenebis(2-\naminobutan-1-01) dihydrochloride.\nEthambutol Hydrochloride contains not less than 97~0 per\ncent and not morethan 101.0 per cent of ClOH24N202,2HCl,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antitubercular.\nDose. 15 to 25 mg perkg ofbody weightdaily, for two months,\nfollowed by 15 mg per kg body weight daily.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethambutol-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethambutol Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethambutol Injection\nEthambutol Hydrochloride Injection\nEthambutol Injection is a sterile solution of Ethambutol \nhydrochloride in water for injection.\nEthambutol Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent arid\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nethambutol hydrochloride, C10H24N2O2,2HCl.\nUsual strength. lOOmgperml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nfaentification\nA- In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the. test, solution corresponds to that in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution..\nETHAMBUTOL INJECTION\nB. When examined in the range 400 nm to 750 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.04 per cent w/v solution exhibits absorption maxima at \n620 nm."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethanol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethanol"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethanol\nAbsolute Alcohol; DehydratedAlcohol\nCH3CH20H\nMol. Wt. 46.1\nEthanol contains not less than 99.0 per cent w/w and not more\nthan 100.0 per cent w/w, corresponding to not less than\n99.4 per cent v/v and not more than 100.0 per cent v/v, at\n15.56°,ofC2~O.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (solvent).\nDescription. A clear, colourless, mobile and volatile liquid;\nodour, characteristic and spirituous; hygroscopic. Readily\nvolatilises even at low temperature; boils at 78°; flanunable,\nburning with a blue, smokeless flame."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethinylestradiol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethinylestradiol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethinylestradiol\nEthinylestradiol is 19-nor-17a-pregna-l,3,5(10)-trien-20-yne-\n3,17fJ*diol.\nEthinyloestradiol contains not less than 97.0 percent and not\nmore than 102.0 per cent of C20H24O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Oestrogen.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellowish-white, crystalline \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethionamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethionamide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethionamide\nEthionamide is 2-ethylpyridine-4-carbothioamide.\nEthionamide contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of CaH10N2S, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antituberculosis.\nDescription. A yellow crystalline powder or small yellow \ncrystals. \n• •"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethopabate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethopabate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethopabate\nMol. Wt. 237.3\nEthopabate is methyI4-acetamido-2-ethoxybenzoate.\n2659\nETHOPABATE\nEthopabate contains not less than 96.0 per cent and not more\nthan 104.0 per cent of ethopabate, CI2HlSN04, calculated on\nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Coccidiostat.\nDose. PoultlY. 4 to 8 g per tonne offeed.\nDescription. A white or pinkish white powder, odourless or\nalmost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethopropazine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethopropazine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethopropazine Hydrochloride\n,HCI\nMol. Wt. 348.9\nEthopropazine Hydrochloride is I 0-[2-(diethylamino)- \npropyI Jphenothiazine hydrochloride.\nEthopropazine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.5 per cent of Cl9H24N2S,HCI. \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiparkinsonian.\nDescription. A white or slightly creamy-white, crystalline \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethosuximide-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethosuximide Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethosuximide Syrup\nEthosuximide Oral Solution\n1.0 g.\nEthosuximide Syrup is a solution ofEthosuximide in a suitable\nAssay. Weigh accurately about 0.12 g, dissolve 20 ml of\nflavoured vehicle.\ndimethylformamide and add 0.2 ml ofa 0.5 percentw/v solution\nEthosuximideSyrup contains not less than 95.0 per cent and\n...... .. ...\nQ.tthY'-1J_Qlplzth(1leildf!_cjllly!thYlfoil!ZC1:l11rd~,JJt!~t~\\Vjt1iQ. JM .. not more than 105;0 per cent of the stated- ·amount···of\ntetrabutylammonium hydroxide, until a distinct blue colour\nethosuximide, C7HllNOz.\nis produced.\nA. Extract a quantity of the syrup containing 0.5 g of\nEthosuximide with two quantities, each of30ml, ofchlorofom1.,\nfilter the combin~dextracts thr0llgh a plug of cotton and\nevaporate the fIltrate to dryness. Heat 100 mg of the residue\nwith 0.2 g ofresorcinol and 0.1 ml ofsulphuric acid at 140° for\n5 minutes, cool, add 5 mlof water, make alkaline with 5 M\nsodium hydroxide and add 0.2 ml to a large volume of water;\na bright green fluorescence is produced.\nB. In the Assay; the principal peak in the chromatogram\nobtained with test solution (b) has the sameretention time as\n1312\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nthat of the peak due to ethosuximide in the chromatogram\nobtained withreference solution (a)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethosuximide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethosuximide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethosuximide\nC7HnNO2\nMoLWt 141.2\nEthosuximide is (37?5r)-3-ethyl-3-methypyrrolidine-2,5-dione.\n2295\nwww.webofpharma.com\nETHOSUXIMIDE\nEthosuximide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C7HnNO2, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiepileptic\nDescription. A white or almost white powder or waxy solid. It \nshows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethyl-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethyl Acetate","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethyl Acetate\no \nH3C^O^CH3\nC4HsO2 \nMol. Wt. 88.1\nEthyl Acetate is Acetic acid, ethyl ester.\nEthyl Acetate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmorethan 102.0percentofC4Hs02.\nCategory. Excipient.\nDescription. A transparent, colourless liquid.\n2297\nwww.webofpharma.com\nETHYL ACETATE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethyl-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethyl Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethyl Chloride\nh3c^ci\nC2H5CI \nMol. Wt. 64.5\nEthyl Chloride is chloroethane.\nCategory. Anaesthetic.\nDescription. Gaseous at ambient temperatures and pressures \nbut is generally compressed to a colourless, mobile, flammable\nand very volatile liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethyl-oleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethyl Oleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethyl Oleate\nCAO2 \nMol. Wt. 310.5\nEthyl Oleate consists of the ethyl esters of (zfyoleic and\nrelated acids.\nEthyl Oleate contains not less than 100.0 per cent w/w and not\nrnore than 105.0 per cent w/w of the ethyl esters of (Z)-oieic \nai*d related acids, calculated as C20H38O2.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Aclear pale yellower colourless liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethyl-vanillin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethyl Vanillin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethyl Vanillin\nOH\nQH.A\nMol.Wt.166.2\nEthyl Vanillin is 3-Ethoxy-4-hydroxybenzaldehyde.\nEthyl Vanillin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C9Hi0O3, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid. ;\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2Q22:\nETHYLOESTRENOL"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethylcellulose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethylcellulose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethylcellulose\nCellulose ethyl ether\nEthylcellulose is an ethyl ether of cellulose.\nEthylcellulose contains not less than 44.0 per cent and not \nmore than 51.0 per cent of ethoxy (-OC2H5) groups, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white to light tan powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethylenediamine-hydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethylenediamine Hydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethylenediamine Hydrate\nh2n^nh2 .h2o\nCH3N2,H2O \nMol. Wt. 78.1\nEthylenediamine Hydrate is ethane-1,2-diamine monohydrate.\nEthylenediamine Hydrate contains not less than 97.5 per cent \nw/wand not more than 101.5 per centw/w of C2H8N2,H2O.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (for Aminophylline Injection).\nDescription. A clear, colourless or slightly yellow liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethyloestrenol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethyloestrenol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethyloestrenol\nC20H32O\nMol. Wt. 288.5\nEthyloestrenol is 17a-ethylestr-4-en-17p-ol containing a \nvariable amount of methanol of crystallisation.\nEthyloestrenol contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of C2oH320, calculated on the \nanhydrous and methanol-free basis.\nCategory. Anabolic steroid.\n2303\nwww.webofpharma.com\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethylparaben","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethylparaben"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethylparaben\nEthyl hydroxybenzoate; Ethyl parahydroxybenzoate.\nC9H10O3 \nMol. Wt. 166.2\nEthylparaben is ethyl 4-hydroxybenzoate.\n2301\nwww.webofpharma.com\nETHYLPARABEN\nEthylparaben contains not less than 98,0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C9Hio03.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ethynodiol-diacetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ethynodiol Diacetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ethynodiol Diacetate\nip 2022\nEthynodiol Diacetate is 19-Norpregn-4-en-20-yn^3 \ndiol, diacetate, (3fJ, 17a).\nEthynodiol Diacetate contains not less than 97.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C24H32O4.\nDescription. Awhite, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/etidronate-disodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Etidronate Disodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Etidronate Disodium\nH3C OH\nHO |_ + \n| OH\nO Na o\" Na+\nCJENaAEP, \nMol. Wt. 250.0\nEtidronate Disodium is (1 -Hydroxyethane-\nl,l-diyl)diphosphonic acid disodium salt.\nEtidronate Disodium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C2H6Na2O7p2, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Bone resorption inhibitor.\nDescription. A white or yellowish, hygroscopic powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/etizolam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Etizolam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Etizolam\nCrH’£lN4S \nMol.Wt. 342.9\nETIZOLAM\nEtizolam is 4-(2-Chlorophenyl)-2-ethyl-9-methyl-6/-/-thieno[3,2- \nf][l,2,4]triazolo[4,3-a][l,4] diazepine.\nEtizolam contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C17HiSC1N4S, calculated oh the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antianxiety.\nDescription. A white to pale yellowish, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/etodolac","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Etodolac"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Etodolac\nC17H21NO3 \nMol. Wt. 287.4\nEtodolac is l,8-diethyl-l,3,4,9-tetrahydropyrano \n(3,4-d]indole-l -acetic acid.\nEtodolac contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Cl7H2iNO3, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Analgesic; antiinflammatory.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/etoposide-concentrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Etoposide Concentrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Etoposide Concentrate\nEtoposide Concentrate is a sterile solution of Etoposide in a \nsuitable ethanolic vehicle.\nThe concentrate complies with the requirements stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Concentrated Solutions for \nInjection) and with the following requirements.\nEtoposide Concentrate contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \netoposide, C29H32.O13."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/etoposide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Etoposide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Etoposide\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 202?\nETOPOSIDE\noSide is. 4'-demethylepipodophyllotoxin 9-[4,6-O- \nXHdene-P’D-glucopyranoside]-\nEtoposide contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 105-0 per cent of C29H32O13, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\n—Etoposide is potentially cytotoxic. Great care \nihottld be taken in handling the powder and preparing\nsolutions.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/etoricoxib","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Etoricoxib"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Etoricoxib\nCi8H!5C1N2O2S \nMol. Wt. 358.8\nEtoricoxib is 5-Chloro-2-(6-methyl-3-pyri.dinyl)- \n3-(4-methylsulfonylphenyl)pyridine. •\nEtoricoxib contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C18H15C1N2O2S, calculated on the dried \nbasis. -\nCategory. Non steroidal anti-inflammatory drug.\nDescription. An off white to creamish coloured powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/eucalyptus-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Eucalyptus Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Eucalyptus Oil\nEucalyptus Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nof the leaves of Eucalyptus globulous (Fam. Myrtaceae).\nEucalyptus Oil contains not less than 60 per cent and not \nmore than 80 per cent of 1,8 cineol, and not less than 0.5 per \ncent and not more than 10 per cent of a-pinene.\nCategory. Counterirritant, antiseptic, expectorant, \nantirheumatic.\nDescription. A colourless or pale yellow liquid; free from \nsediment,suspended matter, odour aroinatic and \ncamphoraceous; taste pungent followed by a sensation of \ncold. \n- • ■:\nA. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of the Oil under \nexamination; in ethanol (95 per cent). \nf:\nReference solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of eucalyptus \noil IP RS \nethanol (95per centf \n.... \nT,.;\nUse chromatographic system'ld^^be^^|fr^A;ssa^A\nThe peaks in the chromatogram obtained with thetest solution \ncorresponds to the peaks in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution.\n13. Flash point (2.4.44). 52.0° to 56.0°.\n4217\nwww.webofpharma.com\nEUCALYPTUS OIL"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/exemestane","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Exemestane"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Exemestane\nG2oH2402 \n' MoLWt 296.4\nExemestane is 6-methyleneandrosta-l, 4-diene-3,17-dione.\nExemestane contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more \nthan 103 .0 per cent of C20H24O2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Aromatase inhibitors.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/experimental-techniques","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Experimental Techniques"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Experimental Techniques\nThis section describes the methods to be used for the sampl; \npreparation, the dynamic flow gas adsorption technique\n(Method I) and the volumetric gas adsorption technique • \n(Method II).\nSample Preparation\nOutgassing\nBefore the specific surface area of-the sample can be \ndetermined, it is necessary to remove gases and vapors tM \nmay have become physically adsorbed onto the surface after \nmanufacture and during treatment;, handling, and storage. If \noutgassing is not achieved, the specific surface area may be \nreduced or may be variable because an intermediate area of \nthe surface is covered with: molecules of the previous!} \nadsorbed gases or vapors. The outgassing conditions are \ncritical for obtaining the required precision and accuracy of \nspecific surface area measurements on pharmaceutical\n*-\n \nbecause of the sensitivity of the surface of the materials. \n'\nCondition\nThe outgassing conditions must be demonstrated to yield . \nreproducible BET plots, a constant weight of test powder, and | \nno detectable physical or chemical changes in the test powder •\nl||® \nfew ^^0 \nli\nwww.webofpharma.com\ngas.'??'\n2.4.38. SPECIFIC SURFACE AREA\nI? 2023\nfhe outgassing conditions defined by the temperature,\npressure, and time are chosen so that the original surface-of \nthe solid is reproduced as closely as possible. Outgassing of \nmany substances is often achieved by applying a vacuum by \npurging-the, sample in a flowing stream of a nonreactive, dry \ngas or by applying a desorption-adsorption cycling method, \n{neither case, elevated temperatures aresometimes applied to \nincrease the rate at which the contaminants leave the surface. \nCaution should be exercised when outgas sing powder samples \nusing elevated temperatures to avoid affecting the nature of \nthe surface and the integrity ofthe sample.\nIf heating is employed, the recommended temperature and \ntime of outgassing are as low as possible to achieve \nreproducible measurement of specific surface area in an \nacceptable time. For outgassing sensitive samples,-other \noutgassing methods such as the desorption-adsorption \ncycling method may be employed. \n. .\nAdsorbate\nThe standard technique is the adsorption of nitrogen of \nanalytical quality at liquid nitrogen temperature.\nFor powders of low specific surface area (< 0.2 ttog-1'), the \nproportion adsorbed is low. In such cases, the use of krypton \nat the liquid nitrogen temperature is preferred,because the low \nvapor pressure exerted by this gas greatly reduces error. The \nuse.of larger sample quantities,.where feasible (equivalent to\nFig.' 2.4'3 8-1': Schematic diagram of the dynamic flow method apparatus\nIm2 or greater total surface area using nitrogen), may \ncompensate for the errors in determining low surface areas.\nAll gases used must be free from moisture.\nQuantity of Sample\nA quantity ofthe testpowder is accurately weighed such that \nthe total surface of the sample is at least 1 m2 when the \nadsorbate is nitrogen and 0.5 m2 when the adsorbate is \nkrypton.\nLower quantities of sample may be used after appropriate \nvalidation.\nMeasurements\nBecause the amount of gas adsorbed under a given pressure \ntends to increase when the temperature is decreased, \nadsorption measurements are usually, made at a low \ntemperature. Measurement is performed at 77.4 K, the boiling \npoint of liquid, nitrogen.\nMethod!. The Dynamic Flow Method\nPrinciple .\nIn the dynamic flow method (see Fig. 2.4.38-1), the \nrecommended adsorbate gas is dry nitrogen or krypton, while \nhelium is employed as a diluent gas, which is not adsorbed \nunder the recommended conditions.\nSelf seals quick connection\n325\nwww.webofpharma.com\nA minimum of three mixtures of the appropriate adsorbate gas \nwith helium are required within the P/P., range 0.05 to 0.3 0..\nThe gas detector-integrator should provide a signal that is \napproximately proportional to the volume of the gas passing \nthrough it under defined conditions of temperature and \npressure. For this purpose, a"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ezetimibe","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ezetimibe"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ezetimibe\nC24H2iF2NO3 \" \n' \n’ Mol Wt. 409.4\nEzetimibe is (3A,41S)-l-(4-Fluorophenyl)-3-[(35)-3-(4-fluoro- \nphenyl)-3-hydroxypropyl]-4-(4-hydroxypheriyl)-2-azetidinone.\nEzetimibe contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C24H21F2NO3, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\n2323\nwww.webofpharma.com\nEZETIMIBE\nCategory. Antihyperlipidemic.\nDescription. A white to off white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/factor-x","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Factor X"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Factor X \n(Activated) FactorynFFaGtorlXajphospholLpd.Ca^ \nFactor X3\nStep 2\nChromogenic substrate —•Fact?r.li-—peptide + Chromophore\nFigure 2. . ■\nBoth steps employ reagents that may be obtained commercially \nfrom a variety of sources. Although the composition of \nindividual reagents may be subject to some variation, their \nessential features are described in the following Specification- \nDeviations from this description may be permissible provided \nthat it has been shown, using the International Standard for \nhuman blood coagulation factor VIII concentrate as the \nstandard, that the results obtained do not differ significantly-\nIt is important to demonstrate by validation the suitability of \nthe kit used, notably by checking the time course of factor Xa\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\n2.8.7. ASSAY OF HUMAN COAGULATION FACTOR IX\ngeneration in order to determine the time taken to reach \n50 0 per cent of the maximal factor Xa generation.\ngeagenis. The coagulation factor reagent comprises purified \nproteins derived from human or bovine sources. These include \nfactor X, factor IXa, and a factor VIII activator, usually \nthrombin. These proteins are partly purified, preferably to at \nleast 50.0 per cent, and do not contain impurities that interfere \nwith tKe activation of factor VIII or factor X. Thrombin may be \npresent in its precursor form prothrombin, provided that its \nactivation in the reagent is sufficiently rapid to give almost \ninstantaneous activation of factor VIII in the assay. \nPhospholipid may be obtained from natural sources or be \nsynthetically prepared, and must, to a substantial extent, \nconsist of the species phosphatidylserine. The components \nofthe complete reagent are usually divided into at least 2 \nseparate reagents, each lacking the ability to generate factor \nXa on its own. One of the reagents contains calcium ions.\nAfter reconstitution, the reagents may be combined provided \nthat no substantial amounts of factor Xa are generated in the \nabsence of factor VIII. In the final incubation mixture, factor \nVIII must be the only rate-limiting component;\nThe 2nd step comprises the quantification ofthe formed factor \nXa, employing a chromogenic substrate that is specific for \nfactor Xa. Generally this consists ofa derivatised short peptide \nof between 3 to 5 amino acids, joined to a chromophore group. \nOn cleavage of this group from the peptide substrate, its \nchromophoric properties shift to a wavelength allowing its \nspectrophotometric quantification. The substrate must also \ncontain appropriate inhibitors to stop further factor Xa \ngeneration, .e.g. chelating agents, and to suppress thrombin \nactivity."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/factor","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Factor"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Factor\nLoss on drying  (2.4.19). Not more than 2.0 per cent, determined \nby drying 0.5g over phosphorous pentoxide at a pressure not \nexceeding 3 kPa for 24 hours.\nCryoprecipitated Antihaemophilic Factor is a sterile, frozen \nconcentrate of human antihaemophilic factor prepared from \nthe Factor VIII-rich cryoprotein fraction of human venous \nplasma obtained from suitable whole-blood  donors from a \nsingle unit of plasma derived from whole blood or by \nplasmapheresis, collected and processed in a closed system.\nHaemagglutinins, anti-A and anti-B.  Reconstitute the powder \nas  per the manufacturer's instruction. Dilute the solution with \nsaline to produce a solution containing 3 IU of Factor VIII: \nC per ml. Carry out the test for Haemagglutinins, anti-A and \nanti-B using a suitable indirect method such as that as \ndescribed below.\nThe specific activity is not less than 1 IU of Factor VIII: C per \nmg of total protein and not more than 80 per cent of which is \nfibrinogen, before the addition of any protein stabilizer.\nIt contains no preservative. It complies with the test for \npotency by comparison with the Standard Antihaemophilic\nThe Factor VIII fraction is dissolved in a suitable liquid. \nExcipients such as a stabilizer may be added.\nFactor (Factor VIII) or with a working reference that has been \ncalibrated with it, in having an average potency of not less \nthan 80 Antihaemophilic Factor Units per container, made at\nNo antibacterial preservative is added. The solution is passed \nthrough a bacteria retentive filter distributed aseptically into \nthe final containers and immediately frozen. It is subsequently \nfreeze dried and the containers are closed under vaccum or \nunder an inert gas.\nPrepare in duplicate serial dilution of the under examination in \nsaline solution. To each dilution of one series add an equal \nvolume of a 5 per cent v/v suspension of group A, red \nblood cells previously washed 3 times with saline solution. \nTo each dilution of the other series add an equal volume \nof a 5 per cent v/v suspension of group B red blood cells \npreviously washed 3 times with  saline solution. Incubate the \nsuspension at 37\" for 30 minutes and then wash the cells \n3 times with saline solution.  Leave the cells in contact \nwith a polyvalent anti-human globulin reagent for 30 minutes. \nWithout centrifuging, examine each suspension for \nagglutination under a microscope. The 1 in 64 dilutions do not \nshow agglutination.\nintervals of not more than 1 month during the dating period.\nExpiration date. The expiration date is not later than 1 year\nfrom the date of collection of source material.\nStorage. Store in hermetic containers at a temperature of - 18°\nFor products stated to have von Willebrand factor activity \nfor products intended for treatment of von Willebrand's\nor lower.\ndisease it shall he demonstrated that the manufacturing \nprocess yields a product with a consistent composition with\nLabelling. Label it to indicate (1) the ABO blood group \ndesignation and the identification number of the donor from \nwhom the source material was obtained; (2) with the type told \nresult of a serologic test for syphilis, or to indicate that it was \nnon-reactive in such test; with the type and result of a test for \nhepatitis B surface antigen, or to indicate that it was non-\nreactive in such test; with a warning not to use it if there is\nrespect to von Willebrand factor.\nProducts that show flakes or particles after reconstitution \nfor use, if a few small flakes or particles remain when the\npreparation is reconstituted it shall be demonstrated during \nvalidation studies that the potency is not significantly affected \nafter passage of the preparation through the filter provided.\nHepatitis B surface  antigen. Dissolve in water. Examine the \nsolution by a suitable sensitive method such  as \nradioimmunoassay. Hepatitis B surface antigen  is not detected.\nevidence of breakage or thawing; with instructions to thaw it\nbefore use to a temperature between 20° an"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/famotidine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Famotidine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Famotidine\nC8H15N7O2S3 \nMol. Wt. 337.5\nFamotidine is [l-amino-3-[[[2-(diaminomethylene)amino]- \n4-thiazolyl]methyl] thio]propylidene] sulphamide. ■\nFamotidine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.5 per cent of CgH^NvC^Sj, calculated on the dried \nbasis. \n. \n■\nCategory. Antiulcer. :\nDescription. A white br yellowish-white, crystalline powder \nor crystals. ,' \n\" \n’\"\"’\"’ri’\" ; \n\" \n’ ’’ ' \"::"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/favipiravir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Favipiravir"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Favipiravir\nC5H4FN3Oz \nMol.Wt. 157.1\nFavipiravir is 6-fluoro-3-hydroxypyrazine-2-carboxamide.\nFavipiravir contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CSH4FN3O2, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription. An off-white to light yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/felodipine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Felodipine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Felodipine\nC18H19CI2NO4 \nMol, Wt. 384.3\nFelodipine is ethyl methyl (47?.S)-4-(2,3 -dichlorophenyl)- \n2,6-dimethyl-I,4-dihydropyridine-3,5-dicarboxylate.\nFelodipine contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0. per cent of CigH^Cl^NO^. calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or light yellow crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fenbendazole-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fenbendazole Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fenbendazole Oral Suspension \ni is an aqueous, suspension of \nFenbendazole.\nFenbendazole Oral Suspension contains not less ‘than 95: 0\nper cent and not moré than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount\nof fenbendazole, CisHnNjO3S.\nThe oral suspension complies with the requirements stated \nunder: Oral Liquids and with the Jollowing requirements.\nUsual. strength. 100 mg per. ml.."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fenbendazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fenbendazole"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fenbendazole\nMol. Wt. 299.4\nClsH13N30ZS\nFenbendazole is methy15-(phenylthio)-2-\nbenzimidazolecarbamate.\nFenbendazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent of ClsH13N30ZS, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Anthelminthic.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fenofibrate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fenofibrate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fenofibrate Tablets\nFenofibrate Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nfenofibrate,C20H21C1O4.\nCategory. Antihyperlipidmic.\nUsual strengths. 48 mg; 54 mg; 120 mg; 145 mg; 160 mg;\n200 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fenofibrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fenofibrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fenofibrate\nC^CIOa \nMol. Wt. 360.8\nFenofibrate is isopropyl [4-(4-chlorophenyl)-2-phenoxy- \n2-methyl]propionate\nFenofibrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C20H2 |C1O4, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihyperlipidemic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fenspiride-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fenspiride Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fenspiride Hydrochloride\nCisH^O^HCl \nMolWt.296.8\nFenspiride Hydrochloride is 8-(2-Phenylethyl)-l-oxa- \nX8-diazaspiro[4.5]decan-2-one hydrochloride.\nFenspiride Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.Q per cent\nnot more than 102.0 per cent of CI5H20N2O2,HCl, calculated \nonthe dried basis.\nFENSPIRIDE HYDROCHLORIDE\nCategory. Bronchodilator.\nDescription. A white to almost white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fentanyl-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fentanyl Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fentanyl Citrate\nMol. Wt, 528.6\nFentanyl Citrate is A-(phenylethyl-4-piperidinyl)- \ny.phenylpropionamide citrate.\nFentanyl Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C22H28N2O,C6HgO7, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Analgesic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fentanyl-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fentanyl Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fentanyl Injection\nFentanyl Citrate Injection\nFentanyl Injection is a sterile solution of Fentanyl Citrate in \nWater for Injections.\nFentanyl Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of fentanyl,\n2347\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFENTANYL INJECTION\nUsual strength. 50 pgper ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fentanyl","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fentanyl"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fentanyl\nFentanyl is A-phenyl -N- [l-(2-phenylethyl)p ip eridin-4- \nyl]propanamide.\nFentanyl contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C22H2SN2O, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Analgesic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ferric-ammonium-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ferric Ammonium Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ferric Ammonium Citrate\nIron andAmmonium Citrate is a complex ofammonium ferric\ncitrate.\nIron and Ammonium Citrate contains not less than 20.5 per\ncent and not more than, 22.5 per cent of iron, Fe.\nCategory. Haematinic.\nDose. 1 to 3 g.\nDescription. Thin, transparent, dark red scales or granules or\na brownish red powder; odourless; deliquescent in moist air\nand is affected by light."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ferrous-ascorbate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ferrous Ascorbate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ferrous Ascorbate\nC!2H14FeOI2 \n; \nMol. Wt. 406.1\nFerrous Ascorbate is (2R)-2-[( I S)-1,2-dihydroxyethyll-\n4,5dihydroxyfuran-3-one;iron (2+). \n;r /. r\nFerrous Ascorbate contains not less than 13.5 per cent and \nnot more than 15.5 per cent Of ferrous iron arid not less than \n7 8.0 per cent and not more than 87.0 per cent of ascorbic acid,\ncalculated on the dried basis. \nwu.*\nCategory.Hacmatinic. '\nDescription. A fine, dark violet coloured dried powder. . L;\nwww.webofpharma.com\nadd 1 ml of 0.05 M sulphuric acid, 1 ml of2,6- \nÿ^^jpphenolindophenol solution and 1 ml of dinitrophenyl\ntnjUrazine solution. Mix the solutions and keep in boiling \nq^fer-bathfor 10 minutes. The solution gives orange-red\n■■ —; ) '\ngypses reaction (A) of ferroussalts (2.3.1)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ferrous-fumarate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ferrous Fumarate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ferrous Fumarate\n++ \nFe\nC4H2FeO4\nFerrous Fumarate contains not less than 93.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C 4H2FeO4, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Haematinic.\nDose. Prophylactic, 200 mg daily; therapeutic, 400 to 600 mg \ndaily, in divided doses. (200 mg of ferrous fumarate is \napproximately equivalent to 65 mg of ferrous iron).\nDescription. A reddish orange to reddish brown, fine powder; \nmay contain soft lumps that produce a yellow streak when \ncrushed; odour, slight."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ferrous-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ferrous Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ferrous Sulphate\n1g ofFerrous Gluconate in 10 ml ofwater and fIlter; the fIltrate\ncomplies with the following tests.\nFeS04,7H20\nMol. Wt. 278.0\nFerrous Sulphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent ofFeS04,7H20.\ncategory. Haematinic.\nDose. Prophylatic, 300 mg; therapeutic 600 to 900 mg daily, in\ndivided doses. (300 mg of ferrous sulphate is approximately\nequivalent to 60 mg of ferrous iron).\nDescription. Bluish green crystals or a light green, crystalline\npowder; odourless. Efflorescent in air. On exposure to moist\nair, the crystals rapidly oxidise and become brown.\n~cofcFerrous(Jluconatecwithc10cml-of-dilutechydrochloricacid;c~Gives,reactionAofferrous.saltsandthe.reactionscofsulphates..c_ -----\nfIlter and add to the fIltrate 1 ml of barium chloride solution;\n(2.3.1).\nan opalescence may be produced but no precipitateis formed."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fexofenadine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fexofenadine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fexofenadine Hydrochloride\nC3,H39NO4,IIC1 ■ \nMol. Wt.538.1\nFexofenadine Hydrochloride is (7/5) a,a-dimethyl- \n4-[ 1 -hydroxy-4-[4-(hydr6xydiphenylmethyl)-1 piperidinyl] \nbutyl] benzene acetic acid hydrochloride;\nFexofenadine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C32H39NO4,HC1, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis. :\nCategrory. Antihistamiriic.\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fhiphenazine-decanoate-ester","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fhiphenazine Decanoate Ester"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fhiphenazine Decanoate Ester\nC32H44F3N3O2S \nMol. Wt. 591.8\nFluphenazine Decanoate is 2-{4-[3-(2-trifluoro-methyl- \nphenothiazin-10-yl)propyl]piperazin-1 -yl Jethyl decanoate.\nFluphenazine Decanoate contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.5 per cent of C32H44F3N3O2S, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic.\nDescription. Apale yellow, viscous liquid or yellow, crystalline, \noily solid. \n;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fibrin-sealant-kit","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fibrin Sealant Kit"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fibrin Sealant Kit\nFibrin Sealant Kit is essentially composed oftwo components,\nnamely fibrinogen concentrate (component 1), a protein\nfraction containing human fibrinogen and a preparation\ncontaining human thrombin (component 2). A fibrin clot is\nrapidly formed when the two thawed or reconstituted\ncomponents are mixed. Other ingredients (for example, human\ncoagulation factor XlII, a fibrinolysis inhibitor or calcium ions)\nand stabilisers (for example, Human albumin solution may be\nadded). No antimicrobial preservative is added.\nHuman constituents are obtained from plasma that complies\nwith the requirements ofthe monograph on Human Plasma for\nFractionation. No antibiotic is added to the plasma used.\nWhen thawed or reconstituted as stated on the label,\ncomponent 1 contains not less than 4.0 per cent of clottable\nprotein; the thrombin activity of component 2 varies over a\nwide range (approximately 4-1000 IV per ml)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/filgrastim-concentrated-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Filgrastim Concentrated Solution"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Filgrastim Concentrated Solution\nGranulocyte Colony StimulatingFactor Solution\n,---'I\nMTPLGPASSL PQSFLLKCLE QVRKIQGDGA ALQEKLCATY KLCHPEELVL LGHSLGIPWA\nPLSStpSQAL QLAGtLSQLH SGLFLYQGLL QALEGISPEL GPTLDTLQLD VADFATIlWQ\nQMEELGMAPA LQPTQGAMPA FASAFQRRAG GVLVASHLQS FLEVSYRVLR HLAQP\nMol. Wt. 18799\nFilgrastim Concentrated Solution is a solution of a protein\nhaving the structure of the granulocyte colony-stimulating\nfactor (G-CSF) produced and secreted by various human blood\ncell types. The protein stimulates the differentiation and\nproliferation ofleucocyte stem cells into mature granulocytes.\nIt is produced by a method based on rDNA technology, using\nbacteria as host cells.\nFilgrastim Concentrated Solution contains not less than\n0.9 mg per ml, and not less than 1.0 x 108IU offilgrastim per mg\nofprotein.\nPrior to release, the following tests are carried out on each\nbatch ofthe final bulle product, unless the regulatory authority\nhas granted exemption.\nDescription. A clear, colourless to slightly yellowish liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/filgrastim-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Filgrastim Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Filgrastim Injection\nFilgrastim Injection is a sterile solution of a protein having the \nprimary structure of the granulocyte colony-stimulating factor \n(G-CSF), produced and secreted by various human blood cell \ntypes and contains an additional amino acid, an N-terminal \nmethionine (r-met HU G-CSF). The natural form is glycosylated \nwhile the recombinant form is non-glycosylated. The.protein\nstimulates the differentiation and proliferation of leukocytes \nstem cells into mature granulocytes. It is produced by a method \nbased on rDNA technology, using bacteria as host cells.\nFilgrastim injection contains not less than 80 per cent and not \nmore than 125 per cent of the stated amount of filgrastim (not \nless than I .Q ,x 108IU per mg of protein).\nUsual strengths. 150 pg perml;300pgper0.5 ml; 300 pg per \nml; 480 pg per ml\nDescription. Aclear, colourless liquid, free from particles."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/finasteride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Finasteride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Finasteride\nC23H36N2O2\nFinasteride is 17/i-(A-ze/7-butylcarbamoyi)-4-aza--5a-androst-\nl-en-3-one.\nFinasteride contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C23H36N2O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiandrogen.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fingolimod-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fingolimod Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fingolimod Hydrochloride\nC19H33NO2, HC1 \nMol Wt. 343.9\nFingolimod Hydrochloride is 2-amino-2-[2-(4-octylphenyl) \nethyl] propane-1,3-diol hydrochloride.\nFingolimod Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 percent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of Ci9H33NO2,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Immunomodulator\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flucloxacillin-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flucloxacillin Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flucloxacillin Oral Solution\nFlucloxacillin Oral Solution is a mixture consisting of \nFlucloxacillin Sodium with buffering agents and other \nexcipients and a suitable flavoring agent. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water just \nbefore use.\nFlucloxacillin Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nflucloxacillin, C!SH!7C1FN3O5S:\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may be \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of flucloxacillin.\nUsual strengths. 125 mg per 5 ml; 250 mg per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluconazole-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluconazole Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluconazole Oral Suspension\nFluconazole Oral Suspension is a mixture consisting of \nfluconazole with buffering agents and other excipients. It \ncontains a suitable flavouring agent. It is filled in a sealed\ncontainer.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of\nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water.\nFluconazole Oral Suspension contains not less than 90-0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount o \nfluconazole, Ci3Hi2F2H)O.\nUsual strengths. 50 mg per 5 ml; 200 mg per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluconazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluconazole"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluconazole\nMol. Wt. 306.3\nFluconazole is 2-(2,4-difluorophenyl)-1,3-bis(lH-1,2,4-triazo-\n1-yl)propan-2-ol.\nFluconazole contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more\nthan 101.5 per cent of C13H12F2N60, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flucytosine-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flucytosine Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flucytosine Oral Suspension\nFlucytosine Oral Suspension is a suspension of Flucytosine \nin a suitable flavoured vehicle.\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water just \nbefore use.\nFlucytosine Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount 0 \nflucytosine, C4H4FN3O.\nWhen stored at the temperature and for the period stated on \nthe label during which the constituted suspension may \nexpected to be satisfactory for use, it contains not less \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of flucytosine C4H4FH\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFLUCYTOSINE TABLETS\njp'2022'\nUsual strength- 10 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flucytosine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flucytosine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flucytosine Tablets\nUsual strengths. 250 mg; 500 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flucytosine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flucytosine"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flucytosine\nMol. Wt. 129.1\nFlucytosine is 4-amino-5-fluoro-2-(1H)-pyrimidinone.\nFlucytosine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0per cent ofC4H4F'N30, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fludarabine-phosphate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fludarabine Phosphate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fludarabine Phosphate Injection\nFludarabine Phosphate Injection is a sterile solution of \nFludarabine Phosphate in Water for Injections.\nFludarabine Phosphate Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of fludarabine phosphate, CioH^FNjOyP.\nCAUTION: FludarabinePhosphate is cytotoxic; Extra care \nrequired to prevent inhaling and exposing the skin to it.\nUsual strength. 25 mgperml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fludarabine-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fludarabine Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fludarabine Phosphate\nC10Hl3FN5O7P \nMol. Wt. 365.2\nFludarabine Phosphate is [(2R,3R,4S,5R)-5-(6-Amino-2-fluoro- \n9-purinyl)-3,4-dihydroxy-2-oxolanyl]methoxyphosphonicacid.\nFludarabine Phosphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C10H13FN5O7P, calculated \non the anhydrous and solvent free basis.\nCA UTION: Fludarabine Phosphate is cytotoxic; Extra care \nrequired to prevent inhaling and exposing the skin to it.\nCategory. Antineoplastic.\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline hygroscopic \npowder. ' ''"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fludrocortisone-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fludrocortisone Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fludrocortisone Acetate\nC23H31FO6 \nMol. Wt. 422.5\nFludrocortisone Acetate is 9a-fluoro-11 [3,17a,21 -trihydroxy- \npregn-4-ene-3,20-dione 21 -acetate.\nFludrocortisone Acetate contains not less than 96.0 per cent \nand not more than 104.0 per cent of C23H31FO6, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Mineralocorticoid.\nDescription, A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flumazenil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flumazenil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flumazenil\nCl5HMFN3O3 \nMol. Wt. 303.3\nFlumazenil is 4J7-imidazo[l-,5-a][l,4]benzodiazepine- \n3-carboxylic acid, 8-fluoro-5,6-dihydro-5-methyl-6-oxo-, ethyl \nester. \n'\nFlumazenil contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Ci5H14FN3O3, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Benzodiazepine toxcity antidote.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluocinolone-acetonide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluocinolone Acetonide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluocinolone Acetonide\nC24H30F3O6 \nMol. Wt. 452.5\nFluocinolone Acetonide is 6a,9a-difluoro-llp,21-dihydroxy-\n16a,17a-isopropylidenedioxypregna-l,4-diene-3,20-dione.\nFluocinolone Acetonide contains not less than 96.0 per cent \nand not more than 104.0 per cent of C24H30F2O6, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluocinolone-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluocinolone Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluocinolone Cream\nfluocinolone Acetonide Cream\nFluocinolone Cream contains Fluocinolone Acetonide in a\nsuitable cream base.\nFluocinolone Cream contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nfluocinolone acetonide, C24H30F2O6.\nUsual strengths. 0.0025 per cent; 0.00625 per cent; 0.01 per\ncent; 0.025 per cent; 0.2 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluorescein-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluorescein Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluorescein Injection\nReference solution. A solution containing 0.002 per cent v/v \neach of dimethylformamide and dimethylacetamide (internal \nstandard).\nFluorescein Sodium Injection\nFluorescein Injection is a sterile solution of Fluorescein Sodium \nin Water for Injections.\nChromatographic system\n-\na glass column 1.5 m x 4 mm, packed with acid-washed, \nsilanised diatomaceous support (80 to 100 mesh) coated \nwith 10 per cent w/w ofpolyethylene glycol 1000, \n-\ntemperature : \ncolumn 120°, \ninlet port and detector at 120°, \nflow rate: 30 ml per minute, using nitrogen as the carrier \ngas.\nFluorescein Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nfluorescein sodium, C20H i0Na205.\nIn the chromatogram obtained with test solution (b) the ratio \nof the area of any peak corresponding to dimethylformamide \nto the area of the peak due to the internal standard is not \ngreater than the corresponding ratio in the chromatogram \nobtained with the reference solution.\nUsual strengths. 100 mg per ml; 200 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluorometholone-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluorometholone Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluorometholone Acetate\nC24H31FO5 \nMol. Wt. 418.5\nFluorometholone Acetate is Pregna-l,4-diene-3,20-dione, 17- \n(acetyloxy)-9-fluoro-l l-hydroxy-6-methyl- (6a, 1 Ip); 9-fluoro-\n11 P,17-dihydroxy-6a-methylpregna-l,4-diene-3,20-dione, 17 \nacetate.\nFluorometholone Acetate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of CMUFCE, calculated on \nthe dried basis. .\n2389\nwww.webofpharma.com\n-FLUOROMETHOLONE ACETATE\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid: \n.\nDescription. Awhite to yellowish crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluorometholone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluorometholone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluorometholone\nC22H29FO4 \nMol. Wt. 376.5\nFluorometholone is 9a-Fluoro-l 10,17a-dihydroxy-6 a- \nmethylpregna-1,4-diene-3,20-dione\nFluorometholone contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not\nmore than 103.0 per cent of C22H29FO4, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Glucocorticoid.\nDescription. A white to yellowish white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluorouracil-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluorouracil Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluorouracil Injection\nFluorouracil Injection is a sterile solution in Water for Injections \nof fluorouracil sodium, prepared by the interaction of \nFluorouracil and Sodium Hydroxide.\nFluorouracil Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nfluorouracil, C4H3FN2O2.\n2391\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFLUOROURACIL INJECTION\nUsual strengths. 25 mg per ml; 50 mg per ml.\nDescription. A colourless or almost colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluorouracil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluorouracil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluorouracil\nGH3FN2O2 ••••’• \nMol. Wt. 130.1\nFluorouracil is 5-fluoro-\\H,3/-/-pyrimidine-2,4-dione.\nFluorouracil contains notjess than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C4H3FN2O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nCAUTION - Great care should be taken to avoid inhaling \nparticles of Fluorouracil and exposing the skin to it."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluoxetine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluoxetine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluoxetine Hydrochloride\nC17H18F3NOJ1C1 \nMol. Wt 345.8\nFluoxetine Hydrochloride is (AS'jW-methyl-S-phenyl- \n3-[4-trifluoromethyl)phenoxy]propylamine hydrochloride.\nFluoxetine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of Ci7H18F3NO,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFLUOXETINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nIP-2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flupentixol-decanoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flupentixol Decanoate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flupentixol Decanoate\n?\nN___\n8.\nCjsH^NjCbS \nMol. Wt 588.8\nFlupentixol Decanoate is (Z)-2-{4-[3-(2-Trifluoromethyl \nthioxanthen-9-ylidene)propyl]piperazin-l -yl}ethyl decanoate\nFlupentixol Decanoate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C^H^F^aChS, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic (Neuroleptic).\nDescription. A yellow, viscous oil."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flupentixol-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flupentixol Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flupentixol Injection\nFlupentixol Decanoate Injection\nFlupentixol Injection is a sterile solution of Flupentixol \nDecanoate in a suitable vegetable oil.\nFlupentexol Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of flupentixol \ndecanoate, C33H43F3N2O2S.\nUsual strength. 20 mgperml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluphenazine-decanoate-ester","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluphenazine Decanoate Ester"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluphenazine Decanoate Ester\nC32HMF'3N302S\nMol. Wt. 591.8\nFluphenazine Decanoate is 2-{4-[3-(2-\n.. trifluoromethylphenothiazin-1O-yl)propyl]piperazin-1-\nyl}ethyl decanoate.\nFluphenazine Decanoate contains not less than 98.5 per cent\nand not more than 101.5 per cent ofC32l4iF3N302S, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic.\nDose. By deep intramuscular injection, test dose 6.25 to 12.5\nmg, thereafter 4 to 7 days 12.5to 100 mg repeated as required.\nDescription. Apaleyellow, viscous Uq~doryellow, crystalline,\noily solid; odour, faint and ester-like. .u."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluphenazine-decanoate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluphenazine Decanoate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluphenazine Decanoate Injection\nFluphenazine Decanoate Injection is a sterile solution of \nFluphenazine Decanoate in Sesame Oil.\nFluphenazine Decanoate Injection contains not less than 90.0 \nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount \nof fluphenazine decanoate, C 32H44F3N302S.\nUsual strengths. 25 mg per ml; 100 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluphenazine-dihydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluphenazine Dihydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluphenazine Dihydrochloride\nC22H26F3N3OS^HC1 \nMol. Wt. 510.5\nFluphenazine Hydrochloride is 2-{4-[3-(2-trifluoro- \nmethylphenothiazin-10-yl)propyl]piperazin-1 -yl} ethanol \ndihydrochloride.\nFluphenazine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent w/v of \nC22H26F3N3OS,2HC1, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluphenazine-hydrochloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluphenazine Hydrochloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluphenazine Hydrochloride Injection\nFluphenazine Hydrochloride Injection is a sterile solution of \nFluphenazine Hydrochloride in Water for Injection.\nFluphenazine Hydrochloride Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of fluphenazine hydrochloride, C22H26F3N3OS,2HC1.\nUsual strengths. 2.5 mg per ml; 10 mg per ml.\nDescription. Aclear, colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluphenazine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluphenazine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluphenazine Hydrochloride\n·,2HCI\nCuH26F3N30S,2HCl\nMol. Wt. 510.5\nFluphenazine Hydrochloride is 2-{4-[3-(2-\ntrifluoromethylphenothiazin-10-yl)propyl]piperazin-1-\nyl}ethanol dihydrochloride.\n1375\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFluphenazine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per\ncent and not more\nthan\n101.0 per cent w/v of\nC22H26F3N30S,2HCl, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic.\nDose. Orally, 1 to 2 mg twice daily, in anxiety states; 2.5 to 10\nmg daily in 2 to 3 divided doses in schizophrenia and other\npsychoses. By intramuscular injection, 1.25 to 10 mg daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flupromazine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flupromazine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flupromazine Hydrochloride\npropyl)]-2-trifluoromethylphenpthiazine hydrochloride.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nDescription. A white to pale yellowish brown, crystalline \npowder.\nNOTE — In the following procedures, the test and standard \nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\nsubdued light.\nA. Dissolve 20 mg in 10 ml of water, make the solution alkaline \nto litihuspaper with 5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with\ntwo quantities, each of 20 ml, of light petroleum (60° to 8(f). \nCombine the extracts, wash with 10 ml of water, shake with 5 g \nof anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter arid evaporate the filtrate1 \ncarefully to dryness:\nThe residue complies with the following test. Determine by Identification \ninfrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the\nspectrum with that obtained with triflupromazine: hydro­\nchloride IPRS, treated in the same manner or with the reference\nspectrum of triflupromazine hydrochloride \n:\n3868\nB. \nComplies with the test for identification of phenothiazi \nI\n(2.3.3), \nusing as reference solution a 0.2 per cent w/v soluti \nof triflupromazine hydrochloride IPRS in chloroform I\nC. \nWhen examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nin (2.4 7) a \n0.001 per cent w/v solution in 0.1Mhydrochloric acid sho^ |\nan absorption maximum at about 256 nm and a less welfdefu^ 1 \nmaximum at about 305 nm. \n|\nD. A10 per cent w/v solution gives the reaction^ of chlorides \n(2.3.1). \n■ \n■ \n■ \n■ \n/"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flurazepam-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flurazepam Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flurazepam Hydrochloride\nC2iH23C1FN3O,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 424.3\n2405\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFlurazepam Hydrochloride is 7-Chloro-l-[2-(diethyl- \namino)ethyl]-5-(2-fluorophenyl)-1,3-dihydro-2?7-1,4-benzo- \ndiazepin-2-one monohydrochloride.\nFlurazepam Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C\"?IH3!C’1FN ;O.HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Sedative; anxiolytic; anticonvulsant and skeletal \nmuscle relaxant.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flurbiprofen-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flurbiprofen Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flurbiprofen Eye Drops\nFlurbiprofen Eye Drops is a sterile solution of Flurbiprofen \nSodium in Purified Water.\nFlurbiprofen Eye Drops contain not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nflurbiprofen sodium dihydrate, C^H^FNaC^F^O.\nUsual strengths. 0.03 per cent w/v; 0.3 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flurbiprofen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flurbiprofen"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flurbiprofen\nCfsHl3FO2 \nMol. Wt. 244.3\nFlurbiprofen is (7?5)-2-(2-fluorobiphenyl-4-yl)propionic acid.\nFlurbiprofen contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 percent ofC!5H]3FO2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiinflammatory; analgesic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flutamide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flutamide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flutamide Tablets\nFlutamide Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of flutamide\nChHhFsNA.\nUsual strength. 250 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/flutamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Flutamide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Flutamide\nCi|Hi]F3N2O3 \n. Mol. Wt. 276.2\nFlutamide is 2-methyl-A-[4-nitro-3-(trifluoromethyl)phenyl] \npropanamide.\nFlutamide contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more \nthan 103.0 per cent of CiiHiiF3N2O3, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiandrogen,\nDescription. A pale yellow crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluticasone-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluticasone Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluticasone Cream\nFluticasone Cream contains Fluticasone Propionate in a \nsuitable cream base.\nFluticasone Cream contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of fluticasone propionate, \nC25H31F3O5S.\nUsual strengths. 0.05 per cent w/w; 0.5 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluticasone-nasal-spray","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluticasone Nasal Spray"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluticasone Nasal Spray\nFluticasone Nasal Spray is a suspension of Fluticasone \nPropionate in a suitable liquid in a container fitted with an \nappropriate nasal delivery system.\nFluticasone Nasal Spray contains not less than 80.0 per cent \nand not more than ,120.0 per cent of fluticasone propionate, \nC25H3iF3O5S of the amount stated to be delivered by actuation \nof the valve.\nUsual strengths. 50 pg; 100 pg; 125 pg.\nNOTE — Carry out all the following procedures in the dark \nor under long-wavelength light at more than 420 nm and \nprepare solutions immediately before use and protect them \nfrom light.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFLUTICASONE NASAL SPRAY\nl?2022\njdentification\nDetermine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating\nplate with silica gel F254.\nphase. A mixture of 1 volume of glacial acetic acid,\ng volumes of ethyl acetate and 30 volumes of \ndichloromethane.\nTest solution. Dilute a quantity of the nasal spray containing \n? fflg of Fluticasone Propionate to 5 ml with acetonitrile, shake\nfor 3 minutes and filter.\nReference solution. A 0.04 per cent w/v solution offluticasone \npropionate IPRS in acetonitrile.\nApply to the plate 40 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 12 cm. Dry the plate in air and examine under \nultraviolet light at 254 nm. The principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nB. In the Assay, the retention time of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthe peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution (a)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluticasone-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluticasone Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluticasone Ointment\nFluticasone Ointment contains Fluticasone Propionate in a \nsuitable base. \n,\nFluticasone Ointment contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of fluticasone propionate, \nC25H31F3O5S. \n' \n•; ' \n• : - • ;\nUsual strength. 0.005 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluticasone-propionate-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluticasone Propionate Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluticasone Propionate Inhalation\nFluticasone Propionate Inhalation is a suspension of micro fine \nFluticasone Propionate in a suitable liquid filled in a suitable \npressurized container. It may contain suitable pharmaceutical \naids such as surfactants, stabilizing agents.\nFluticasone Propionate Inhalation delivers not less than 80.0 \nper cent and not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount \nof fluticasone propionate, C25H3iF3O5S, per inhalation by \nactuation of the valve.\nUsual strengths. 50 pg per metered dose; 125 pg per metered \ndose; 250 pg per metered dose."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluticasone-propionate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluticasone Propionate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluticasone Propionate\nC25H3|F3O5S \nMol. Wt. 500.6\nFluticasone Propionate is S-fluoromethyl 6a,9a-difhioro-11 fl- \nhydroxy- 16a-methy 1-17a-propiony!oxy-3-oxoandrosta-1,4- \ndiene-17 p-carbothioate.\nFluticasone Propionate contains not less than 96.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of fluticasone, C25H31F3O5S, \ncalculated . on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Corticosteroid.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluvoxamine-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluvoxamine Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluvoxamine Maleate\nCI9H25F3N2O6 \nMol. Wt. 434.4\nFluvoxamine Maleate is Fluvoxamine maleate is (£)-5-methoxy-\nl-[4-(trifluoromethyl)phenyl]-l -pentanone O-(2-aminoethyl) \noxime maleate;\nFluvoxamine Maleate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of Ci9H25F3N2O6, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. Awhite to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fluvoxamine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fluvoxamine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fluvoxamine Tablets\nFluvoxamine Maleate Tablets\nFluvoxamine Tablets contains not less than 92.5 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of\n' fluvoxamine maleate, C:5H2|FN?P?,C.|H.|(X..\nUsual strengths. 25 mg; 50 mg; 100 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/follicle-stimulating-hormone-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Follicle Stimulating Hormone Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Follicle Stimulating Hormone Injection\nFollitropin Injection\nFollicle Stimulating Hormone Injection is a heterodimeric \nglycoprotein having the structure of follicle stimulating \nhormone. It consists of 2.subunits: a 92 aniino acid a-chain \ncommon to other glycoprotein hormones and a specific 111 \namino acid ß-chain.\nFollicle stimulating hormone injection contains not less than \n80 per cent and not more than 125 per cent of the stated potency\nUsual strengths. 75 IU per 0.15 ml, 50 IU per 0.5 ml, 100 IU per \n0.5 ml, 150 IUper 0.3 ml, 225 IU per 0.45 ml, 300 IU per 0.36 ml, \n300 IU per 0.6 ml, 600 IU per 0.72 ml, 900 IU per 1.08 ml, 450 IU \nper 0.75'ml (33 mcg per 0.75 ml); 1050 IUper 1.75 ml (77 mcg per\n1.75 nil) injection. \n' ’ ’ L\nDescription. Clear and colorless solution essentially free of \nparticulates. \n-\nA. It complies with the biological activity as described under \nAssay. .\nB. Determine by Isoelectric focusing (2.4.33).\nTesi solution. Desalt and concentrate the preparation under \nexamination using a suitably validated procedure. Redissolve \nthe recovered material in water to obtain the concentration of \n5 mg per ml.\nReference solution. Desalt and concentrate the reference\nsolution using a suitably validated procedure. Redissolve the \nrecovered material in water to obtain the concentration of 5 \nmg per ml.\n- pH gradient: a combination of ampholytes and electrode\nbuffers giving a functional separation in the isoelectric \npoint pl range of 3.5-5.5 is selected as defined by the \nsystem suitability criteria; where pre-cast gels are \nemployed, proprietary electrode solutions may be used \nin conjunctions; otherwise suitable dilute mineral or \norganic acids and bases are employed at pH levels \nrespectively lower and higher than the functional range \nof ampholytes,\n- catholyte: 2 per cent w/v solution of glycine,\n- anolyte : a mixture containing <0.34 per cent w/v solution\nof aspartic acid and 0.36 per centw/v solution of glutamic \nacid adjusted to pH 2.8-3.8,\n- application: 10 pl.\nDetection. Proceed as described in Isoelectric Focusing\nIn thè electropherogram obtained with the reference solution, \nthe number of bands seen in the pl region 3.5-5.5 corresponds \nto that shown in the electropherogram supplied with follitropin \nIPRS; the distribution of bands in the pl region 3.5-5.5 is\nqualitatively similar to that shown in the electropherogram \nsupplied with follitropin IPRS.\nThe principal band in the electropherogram obtained with the \ntest solution is similar in position to the principal band in the \nelectropherogram obtained with the reference solution.\nC. Examine the chromatograms in the test for follitropin \noligomers. .\nIn the chromatogram, the principal peak obtained with the test \nsolution corresponds to the principal peak obtained with the \nreference solution.\nD. Immunoblotting. Determine by electrophoresis (Sodium \ndodecyl sulphate polyacrylamide gel electrophoresis) (SDS- \nPAGE) followed by immunoblotting (2.4.12).\nTransfer buffer. Mix 0.3 per cent tris, 1.4 per cent glycine and \n20 percent v/v solution of methanol. Perform a SDS-PAGE as\n4623\nwww.webofpharma.com\ndescribed in test for free subunits, except that the gel after \nelectrophoresis is not stained, instead placed in a tray \ncontaining transfer buffer. The tray is placed on a shaker for \n5-10 minutes and then assembled on an electro blotting \ncassette. y; ,-k: ■ \n; \ni \n;\nAssembly of transfer cassette^ Soak two pieces of sponge\nsheets;\"6 sheets of Whatman no. 1 paper and a nitro cellulose \nmembrane in transfer buffer and place in a cassette with the \nnitro cellulose membrane covering the gel; place equal numbers \nof Whatman sheets above and below the gel-membrane setup. \nThen place this whole set up between two sheets of sponge \nand lower, the whole cassette into the transblot apparatus \ncontaining the transfer buffer with the nitro cellulose membrane \nside facing the anode.\nTransfer and development of protein bands. Transfer is done \nat 100 V for 1 hour at room "}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fomepizole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fomepizole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fomepizole\ncAn2 '\nMol Wt.82.1\nFomepizole is 4-methyl-I Hpyrazole.\nFOMEPIZOLE\nFomepizole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C4H6N2, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Antidote to methanol poisoning.\nDescription. A clear colourless liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/for-compressed-pessaries","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"For Compressed Pessaries"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"For Compressed Pessaries\nthe upper open ends of the tubes remain\ns in a vessel of suitable diameter containing \n° . Adjust the level of the liquid by the gradual\ne of the liquid. A suitable device maintains\n(Dimensions in mm) \nFig. 2.5.1-3a: Apparatus for Disintegration o\nf the liquid at 35-39°.\nSuppositories\nr at 36° to 37° until the perforations in the \n...,-;7. \t\n..,-. \ni... \t\n,.....,\n,,....„,.,,,,,,I.,\n-';,  ,'\"----.:•--  \n,.,,,,*„ • \t\n.„.‘„,.\nmetal disc are just covered by a uniform layer of water. Place \none compressed pessary on the upper perforated disc and \ncover the apparatus with a glass plate to ensure a humid \natmosphere. Repeat the operation with a further two \ncompressed pessaries.\nDisintegration is considered to be complete when\na)\nThere is no residue on the perforated plate or\nb)\nIf a residue remains, it consists only of a soft mass having \nno solid core which cannot be pressed with a glass rod.\n2.5.2. Dissolution Test\nThis test is designed to determine compliance with the \ndissolution requirements for solid, semi-solid and suspension \ndosage forms.\nUse Apparatus 1 unless otherwise directed. All parts of the \napparatus that may come into contact with the preparation \nunder examination or with the dissolution medium are \nchemically inert and do not adsorb, react or interfere with the \npreparation under examination. All metal parts of the apparatus \nthat may come into contact with the preparation or the \ndissolution medium must be made from stainless steel, type \n316 or equivalent or coated with a suitable material to ensure \nthat such parts do not react or interfere with the preparation \nunder examination or the dissolution medium.\nNo part of the assembly, including the environment in which \nthe assembly is placed, contributes significant motion, \nagitation or vibration beyond that due to the smoothly rotating \nelement.\nAn apparatus that permits observation of the preparation under \nexamination and the stirrer during the test is preferable.\nApparatus 1 (Paddle Apparatus)\nAn assembly consisting of the following: \na.\nA cylindrical vessel, A, made of borosilicate glass or any \nother suitable transparent material, with a hemispherical \nbottom and with a nominal capacity of 1000 ml unless \notherwise specified in the individual monograph and an\ninside diameter of 98-106 mm (Fig.2.5.2-1). The vessel has \na flanged upper rim and is fitted with a lid that has a \nnumber of openings, one of which is central. Modified \npeak vessels can be used to eliminate coning. In addition \nto vessel parameters like inner diameter and height, \nevaluate parameters like hemisphere radius (49 mm to \n53 mm). \nb.\nA motor with a speed regulator capable of maintaining \nthe speed of rotation of the serialized paddle within 4 per \ncent of that specified in the individual monograph: The \nmotor is fitted with a stirring element whichonsist:S of a \ndrive shaft and blade forming a paddle, II(Fi )2 -2):\nrf=\n2.5.2. DISSOLUTION TEST \n2.5.2. DISSOLUTION TEST \nIP 2018 \t\nIP 2018\nTime. Where a single time specification is given in the \nmonograph, the test may be concluded in a shorter period if \nthe requirement for the minimum amount dissolved is met. \nSamples are to be withdrawn only at the stated times, within a \ntolerance of ± 2 per cent.\nthickness of 0.265 ±0.045 mm diameter and with 0.4 +0.04 mm \nsquare openings unless otherwise specified in the individual \nmonograph, formed into a cylinder with narrow rim of sheet \nmetal around the top and the bottom. Shaft and basket \ncomponents of the stirring element are fabricated of stainless \nsteel, type 316 or any other inert material. The basket may be \nplated with a 2.5 pm layer of gold for use with acidic media. \nThe distance between the inside bottom of the vessel and the \nbasket is maintained at 23 to 27 mm during the test. Evaluate \nthe basket mesh integrity by using a magnifying glass or \nmicroscope at regular intervals.\n4b. 4\n10\n1684-h\nFilter suitability. It may be accomplished by preparing a \nsuitable"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/formoterol-fumarate-and-budesonide-powder-for-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Formoterol Fumarate and Budesonide Powder for Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Formoterol Fumarate and Budesonide Powder for Inhalation \nconsists of Formoterol Fumarate and Budesonide in microfrne \npowder either alone.or admixed with Lactose in a pre-metered \nunit for use in a suitable powder inhaler.\nFormoterol Fumarate and Budesonide Powder for Inhalation \ncontains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than 125.0 \nper cent of the stated amounts of formoterol fumarate\nC42H52N4O12 and budesonide C25H34O6 per pre-metered unit.\nUsual Strengths. Formoterol Fumarate 6 pg and Budesonide\n100 pg; Formoterol Fumarate 6 pg and Budesonide 200 pg; \nFormoterol Fumarate 6 pg and Budesonide 400 pg. ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/formoterol-fumarate-dihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Formoterol Fumarate Dihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Formoterol Fumarate Dihydrate\n(C19H24N2O4)2, C4H4O,. 2H2O \nMol. Wt. 840.9\nFormoterol Fumarate Dihydrate is dihydrate salt of fomaric \nacid, with (7?S’)-2'-hydroxy-5'7[(7?iS)-l -hydroxy-2-[[(7?5)- \np-methoxy-a-methylphenethyl]amino]ethyl]formanilide.\nFormoterol Fumarate Dihydrate contains not less than 98.5 \nper cent and not more than 101.5 per cent of formoterol\nfumarate, C42HS2N4O]2, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\n2424\nIP 2022\nCategory. Bronchodilator.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white or slightly yellow powder"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/formulations","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Formulations"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Formulations \t\nAct, 1940 The Drugs and \nArticles (Users) \t\n(Packaged Products) \t\nCosmetics Rules, 1945\nConsumer \tConsumption/Usage\n6.2. CONTAINERS \t\nIP 2018 \nIP 2018\n6.2. CONTAINERS\nThe selection of a suitable plastic container should be based \non a knowledge - obtained from the supplier - of the \ncomposition of the plastic (e.g. raw materials, colourants and \nadditives) so that potential hazards can be assessed.\n(IS 10146), Polyvinyl Chloride (IS 10151), Polypropylene (IS\nd) Packages that do not meet the requirements of the  in\n10910), Cyclic Olefins, Polyamides [nylon 6 (IS 12247)], \nPolycarbonate (IS 14971), Poly (ethylene-vinyl acetate) (IS\nvitro tests where applicable are required to undergo \nfurther in vivo testing.\n13601). However, if the manufacturer of Pharmaceutical articles \nintends to use plastics made from polymers other than listed \nabove, it should be justified and authorised by appropriate \ncompetent authority. \nThe plastic identification codes as mentioned in IS 14534 may \nbe visibly marked on the plastic containers and/ or the label, \nwherever possible. \nThe plastic containers should be manufactured from materials \nthat do not include in their composition any substances that \ncan be extracted by the pharmaceutical contents in such \nquantities that alter the efficacy orstability of the product or \npresent a toxic hazard. Recycling of excess material (shavings, \ndust, etc. from the container manufacturing plant) of well-\ndefined nature and proportions may be permitted after \nappropriate validation. However, the use of post-\nmanufactured material is strictly prohibited. Re-use of earlier-\nfilled bottles is strictly prohibited. \nFor general information, a summary of the manufacture and \nusage of plastic containers and closures is provided in the \nTable 1.\nPackages that do not meet the requirements of the biological \nreactivity tests [(2.2.23) and (2.2.24), if appropriate] are not \nsuitable as packages for pharmaceutical use.\nThe plastic container chosen for any particular pharmaceutical \nproduct should be such that the ingredients of the product in \ncontact with the container do not significantly interact or \nmigrate into or through the plastic, to a level that affects the \nquality of the product.\nNOTE  3 --Responsibility\nIt shall be the responsibility of the user/manufacturer to ensure \nthe compatibility of the pharmaceutical articles with the package \nand stability of the product in a package.\nTest on Container Material\nTowards this:\nType samples (specimen) of the intended container should be \npacked with the product and tested under conditions that \nreproduce those that would be encountered in use. These \ntests should include examination of the product to ensure \nabsence of any sensory, chemical or physical change, an \nassessment of changes in the quality of contents due to \npermeability of the plastic, detection of changes in pH, an \nassessment of the effects of light, chemical tests and where \nnecessary, biological tests. Containers from bulk production \nshould conform to the type sample (specimen) in every respect. \nIt should be ensured that there is no change in the composition \nor any change in the manufacturing method used by the \nmanufacturer and more importantly, that no use is made of \npost-consumer recycled material. It must be emphasized that \nchanges in the composition of the plastic or reworking or \ninadequate control of processing can bring about changes \nwhich may invalidate the results of type testing. Samples from \nproduction should be tested to ensure conformance to type \nsamples (specimen) and test schedules should be designed \nto check deviations from the characteristic of the type sample \n(specimen).\na)\nThe user/manufacturer shall have in place a system of \nquality assurance with the supplier / convertor to ensure \ncompliance of the components of the package with the \nrequirement stated in this chapter at all times. \nb)\nThe initial protocol for approving the vendor and its \npackaging mater"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fortified-procaine-penicillin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fortified Procaine Penicillin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fortified Procaine Penicillin Injection\nProcaine Penicillin with Benzylpenicillin Injection\nFortified Procaine Penicillin Injection is a sterile mixture offive\nparts of Procaine Penicillin and one part of Benzylpenicillin\nPotassium or Benzylpenicillin Sodium, together with suitable\ndispersing and buffering agents. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by suspending the contents of\nthe sealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water\nfor Injections, immediately before use.\nStorage. The constituted injection should be used within\n24 hours (4 days ifa buffering agent is present) ofpreparation\nwhen stored at a temperature not exceeding 20° or within\n7 days (14 days ifa buffering agent is present) when stored at\na temperature between 2° and 8°.\nFortified Procaine Penicillin Injection contains a quantity of\ntotal penicillins calculated as CI6H17NzNa04S and equivalent\nto not less than 60.0 per cent and not more than 74.0 per cent\nofthe stated amounts ofprocaine penicillin and benzylpenicillin\npotassium or benzylpenicillin sodium and a quantity of\nprocaine, CI3HzoNzOz, equivalent to not less than 36.0 per cent\nand not more than 44.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nprocaine penicillin.\nUsual strengths. Procaine Penicillin, 300 mg (300,000 Units)\nand Benzylpenicillin Potassium or Benzylpenicillin Sodium 60\nmg (100,000 Units), Procaine Penicillin 3 g (3,000,000 Units)\nand Benzylpenicillin Potassium or Benzylpenicillin Sodium,\n600 mg (1,000,000 Units).\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the tests\nstated under Parenteral Preparations (Powders for\nInjection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fosinopril-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fosinopril Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fosinopril Sodium\nC3OH45NNaO7P \n. Mol. Wt. 585.7\nFosinopril Sodium is (45)-4-Cyclohexyl-l-{[(JfS)-2-methyl-\nl-(propionyloxy)propoxy]-(4-phenylbutyl)phosphinylacetyl}- \nL-proline sodium salt.\nFosinopril Sodium contains not less than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C3oH4sNNa07P, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\n2425\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFOSINOPRIL SODIUM\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder. It \nshows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fraction","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fraction"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fraction\nDried Factor VIII Fraction; Freeze-dried Human ;\nCoagulation Factor VIII \n;\nDried Human Antihaemophilic Fraction is a preparation of a \nplasma protein fraction that contains the glycoprotein \ncoagulation factor VIII together with varying amounts of von \nWillebrand factor, depending on the method of preparation. It \nis prepared from human plasma that; complies with the \nmonograph on Plasma for Fractionation.\nThe potency of the preparation, reconstituted as stated on \nthe label, is not less than 20IU of factor VIII : C per milliliter."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/framycetin-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Framycetin Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Framycetin Sulphate\nMol. Wt. 908.9\nFramycetin Sulphate is 2-deoxy-4-(7-(2,6-diamino- \n2,6-dideoxy-a-D-glucopyranosyl)-5-O-[3-O-(2,6-diamino- \n2,6-dideoxy-/?-L-idopyranosyl)-/?-D-ribofuranosyl]-D-strept-. \namine (neomycin B) sulphate. The base is produced by the \ngrowth of selected strains of Streptomyces fradiae or \nStreptomyces decaris or by any other means.\nFramycetin Sulphate contains an amount of framycetin \nsulphate equivalent to not less than 630IU of neomycin B per \nmg, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial (topical).\nDescription. Awhite or yellowish-white powder; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/frovatriptan-succinate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Frovatriptan Succinate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Frovatriptan Succinate\nFrovatriptan Succinate is (I?)-5,6,7,8-Tetrahydro- \n6-methylaminocarbazole-3-carboxamide succinate.\nFrovatriptan Succinate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of G18H25N3O5, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.-\nCategory. Antimigraine.\nDescription. A white or off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fructose-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructose Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fructose Injection\nFructose Intravenous Infusion\nFructose Injection is a sterile solution ofFructose in Waterfor\nInjections.\nFructose Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount offructose,\nC6H120 6• It contains no antimicrobial agent.\nUsual strength. 10 per cent w/v.\nDescription. Aclear, colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fructose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fructose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fructose\nD-Fructose\nC6H12O6 \nMol. Wt. 180.2\nFructose is D-(-)-fructopyranose.\nCategory. Nutrient; fluid replenisher.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder with a very sweet \ntaste."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/frusemide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Frusemide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Frusemide Injection \ni isa sterile solution of Frusemide \ni in Water\nfor Injection prepared with- the aid of Diethanolamine or \nMonoethanolamine.\nFrusemide Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 percent of the stated amount of Frusemide\nCpH:CIN,O;8. \n.\nUsual strength: 50 mg per ml.\npH. 7.0 to 7.8, ifit contains diethanolamine; ‘or between 8.0 to\n9.3, ifit contains monoethanolamine..-\nLabelling. The labél states‘whether’ preparation \n‘1.0 per cent \nprepared using Diethanolamine or with Monoethanolamine.\nBe or rHdontifi cation and Tests refer: 10 IP. Volume tr,\nFurazolidone oo\nCategory: Antibacterial:\nFor Description, Identification and Tests refer \nt to IP. Volume \nif, \n: \n.\nFurazolidone Veterinary Oral. \nSuspension \n_\nFurazolidone Veterinary Mixture; F urazotidone Mixtures\nFurazolidone Drench\nFurazolidone Veterinary Oral Suspension, is is an aqueous \nsuspension of Furazolidone.\nFurazolidorie Veterinary Oral Suspension « contains ‘not less \nthar 90.0 per‘ cent and. not more.than ‘1.10.0 per cent of the \nstated amount of furazolidone, CgH;N3Os, \n|\ni \nUsual strengths: 5.per.cent: w/v;:7.5:per cent. w/v.\n4881\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFURAZOLIDONE ‘VETERINARY ORAL SUSPENSION \nTP 2022:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fulvestrant","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fulvestrant"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fulvestrant\nC32H47F503S \t\nMol. Wt. 607.0\nFulvestrant is 7a49-[(RS)-(4,4,5,5,5-pentafluoropentyl) \nsulfinyllnonyliestra-1,3,5(10)-triene-3,1713-diol.\nFulvestrant contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C32H47F503S, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Oestrogen receptor antagonist.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fumaric-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fumaric Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fumaric Acid\nHOOC . H\nH COOH\nC4HA \n'7 ■ \nMol. Wt. 116.1\nFumaric acid is (2E)-butanedioic acid.\nFumaric Acid contains not less than 99.5 per cent and hot \nmore than 100.5 per cent of C4H4O4, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis\".'' ■ • \n;\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Awhite granules or crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/furazolidone-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Furazolidone Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Furazolidone Oral Suspension\nFurazolidone Ordl Suspension is a suspension of Furazolidone \nin a suitable aqueous flavoured vehicle.\nFurazolidone Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nfurazolidone, C8H7N3O5.\nUsual strength. 25 mg per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/furazolidone-premix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Furazolidone Premix"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Furazolidone Premix\nFurazolidone Premix contains not less than 95.0 per cent and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nfurazolidone, CSH7N30 S'\nUsual strengths. 4.4 per cent w/v; 22.4 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/furazolidone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Furazolidone","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Furazolidone\nC8H7N3O5 \nMol. Wt. 225.2\nFurazolidone is 3-(5-nitrofbrfurylideneamino)oxazolidin-2-one.\nFurazolidone contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0; per cent of C8H7N3O5, calculated on the dried \nbasis.. ■ - . ':\nCategory. Antibacterial; antiprotozoal.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder.\n2437\nwww.webofpharma.com\nFURAZOLIDONE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/furosemide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Furosemide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Furosemide\nUsual strength. 10 per cent w/v.\n5-Hydroxymethylfurfural and related compounds. To 5 ml of \nsolution A add 5 ml of water and measure the absorbance \n(2.4.7) of the resulting solution at the maximum at about \n284 nm; absorbance at about 284 nm, not more than 03 1 ).\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\n60'\nN"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fusidic-acid-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fusidic Acid Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fusidic Acid Cream\nFusidic Acid Cream contains fusidic acid in a suitable cream \nbase.\nFusidic Acid Cream contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of fusidic \naeid,c31H48o6. \n•_ ' :J. . . -.F\nUsual strength. 2 per cent w/w. \n. ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/fusidic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Fusidic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Fusidic Acid\nCaiH^CV/sH.O \nMol. Wt. 525.7\nFusidic Acid is en/-16a-acetyloxy-3p,lip-dihydroxy- \n4p,8P,14P-trimethyl-18-nor-5P,I0a-cholesta(17Z)-17(20), \n24-dien-21 -oic acid hemihydrate, an antimicrobial substance \nproduced by the growth of certain strains of Fusidium \neoccineum or by any other means. ........\nFusidic Acid contains not less than 97.5 per cent arid not more\nthan 101.0 per cent of C3iH48O6, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis. \n■ \n' T . ■ ■\nCategory. Antibacterial. .\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gallamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gallamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gallamine Injection\nGallamine Triethiodide Injection\nGallamine Injection is a sterile solution of Gallamine \nTriethiodide in Water for Injections.\nGallamine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of gallamine \ntriethiodide, C-,oH60l3N303.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or almost colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gallamine-triethiodide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gallamine Triethiodide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gallamine Triethiodide\n; O/\\^N(C2H5)3 :\n(C2H5)3N^^ \n^ N(C2H5)3\n31\nCioHeoW^ < \nMol.Wt.891.5\nGallamine Triethiodide is 2,2’,2\"-(benzene- \n:\n1,2,3-triyltrioxy)tris(tetraethylammonium) triiodide.\nGallamine Triethiodide contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C30H60I3N3O3, calculated \non the dried basis. \n;\nCategory. Skeletal muscle relaxant:\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ganciclovir-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ganciclovir Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ganciclovir Injection\n| Reference solution (b). Dilute 20.0 ml of reference solution(a) \nj andadd 10,0 ml internal standard solution to 100.0 ml .with \n| mobile phase. ■..........."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ganciclovir-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ganciclovir Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ganciclovir Oral Suspension\nBacterial endotoxins. (2.2.3). Not more than 0.84 Endotoxin\nUnit per mg of ganciclovir.\nGanciclovir Oral Suspension is a suspension of Ganciclovir in \na suitable vehicle. \n;\nInject reference solution (a).The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the ganciclovir related compound A and \nganciclovir is not less than 1.4, the column efficiency is not \nless than 5000 theoretical plates, the tailing factor is not more \nthan 1.4.\nI Assay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nThe suspension is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water just \nbefore use. \nc. '\ni Internal standard solution. A 0.015 per cent w/v solution of\n1 hypoxanthine in water.\nTest solution (a). Determine the weight of the contents of\n1 10 containers. Transfer a.weighed quantity of the mixed \n| contents of the 10 containers containing 100 mg of Ganciclovir \n| to a 100.0 ml volumetric flask, add about 80. ml of the water and \n| dissolve by shaking and mixing if necessary, with the aid of \n| ultrasound. Cool and dilute to 100.0 ml with the water.\nInject reference solution (b). The relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 1.0 per cent.\nGanciclovir Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nganciclovir, C9H13N5O4.\nInject reference solution (b) and the test solution.\nThe constituted suspension, when stored, at the, temperature \nand for the period stated on the label during which it may be \nexpected to be satisfactory' for use, contains not less than \n80.0 per cent of the stated amount of ganciclovir, C9H13N5O4.\nCalculate the content of C9H43N5O4.\nStorage. Store in well-closedJ containers, protected from \nmoisture at a temperature below 30°:.\ni Test solution (b).Dilute ,5.0 ml of .test solution (a) and add\n10.0 ml internal standard solution and dilute to 100.0 ml with \nmobile phase.\nNOTE — Avoid skin contact or inhalation of ganciclovir by\n.j Reference solution (a). A 0.Q25 per cent w/y.solution of \n| ganciclovir IPRS in water. ,.J.................. , \\\nusing protective gloves and a fume hood or surgical mask.\nUsual strength. 100 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ganciclovir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ganciclovir"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ganciclovir\nGanciclovir is 2-amino-9-[(2-hydroxy-l-(hydroxymethyl) \nethoxy] methyl]-l,9-dihydro-6H-purin-6-one.\n2451\nwww.webofpharma.com\nGanciclovir contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C9H|3N5O4, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis. \n. \n. 9\nCategory. Antiviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white, powder; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/garcinia-aqueous-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Garcinia Aqueous Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Garcinia Aqueous Extract\nGarcinia Aqueous Extract is obtained by extracting Garcinia \n(Garcinia cambogia Desr., Garcinia gummi-gutta (L.) N: \nRobson, Fam. Guttiferae) fruit rinds with water as calcium salts.\nGarcinia Aqueous Extract contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nw/w to not more than 120.0 per cent w/w of the stated amount \nof hydroxy citric acid, and calcium not less than 15.0 per cent \nw/w calculated on the dried basis. It may . contain suitable \nadded substances. ;; \n,\nC ategory. Antiulcer, sthoulyo, medohara.\nUsual strengths. 50 per cent; 60 per cent. : , J. . . :\nDescription. Apale brown to brown powder.\nTotal ash (2.3.19). Nbt more than iO.Oper cent.\nHeavy metals (2.'3.13). Ev^complieswitht^lfmit test.for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm). : \n¿fff >-'f ■ i\"\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 6.0per cent determined \non 1 g by drying in an oven at 105°.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Complies with the microbial \ncontamination tests.\nAssay, hydroxy citric ,acid -f, Determine by Hquid \nchromatography (2.4.14). \n,w. . - \n...... .\nTest solution. Shake well 0.2 gofthc extract under examination \nadd 2 ml of 3 M hydrochloric acid and add 10 mi of water, \nsonicate it to dissolve, make up to volume 100.0 ml with water \nandmix. ■ \n' ■' \n\"L: \n:\nReference solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of hydroxy \ncitric acid IPRS with 2 ml of 3 Mhydrochloric acid in water:\nwww.webofpharma.com\nChromatographic, system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5pm),\n;; - mobile phase: 0.1 per cent \nacid in\nwater, filter and degas, . . ; .\n- \nflow rate: 0.6 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- \ninjection volume: 20 pl. .\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for the replicate injections is not \nmore than 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of the hydroxy citric acid in the extract.\nCalcium-\nTest solution. Weigh about 0:2 g of the extract and transfer \ninto a 500 ml conical flask. Dissolve in 2 ml of 3 Mhydrochloric \nacid and add 200 ml of water. Add 15 ml of 1 M sodium \nhydroxide and titrate with 0.05 M EDTA using hydroxy \nnaphthol blue as indicator until deep blue colour persist.\n1 ml of 0.05 MEDTA solution is equivalent to 0.002004 g of\ncalcium.\nStorage. Store in air-tight containers, protected from light.\nGinkgo Leaf\nMaidenhair tree; Kew tree; Fossil tree; Temple tree; \nPankha plant; Ginkgo biloba.\nGinkgo consists of dried leaves of Ginkgo biloba L. (Fam.\nTaxaceae). \n? - \n''\nGinkgo leaves contains not less than 0.5 per cent w/w. of \nflavone glycosides, calculated on dry basis.\nCategory. Brain tonic.\n4220\nGARCINIA AQUEOUS EXTRACT\nIP 2022\nDescription. Leaves are green in colourfodour is characteristic \nand agreeable; taste astringent and bitter."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/garcinia","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Garcinia"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Garcinia\nVilayati Imli; Garcinia cqmbogia\nGarcinia is the dried deseeded fruit of Garcinia cambogia \nDesr. {Garcinia gummi-^itialf)^-. Róbsoh}(Fám. Guttiferae).\n4218\nIp 2022;\nGarcinia contains not less than 1.0 per cent of garcinol, 12 0 \nper cent of total hydroxycitric acid and hydroxycitric acid \nlactone calculated on dried basis.\nCategory. Antiulcer, sthoulyo, medohara. ’;.\nDescription. Dark brown to blackish brown fruits. Taste, acidic,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gatifloxacin-infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gatifloxacin Infusion","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gatifloxacin Infusion\nGatifloxacin Infusion contains Gatifloxacin.\nGatifloxacin Infusion contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\ngatifloxacin C19H22FN304'\nUsual strength. 0.2 per cent w/v.\nGATIFLOXACIN INFUSION"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gefitinib-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gefitinib Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gefitinib Tablets\nGefitinib Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than 110.0 per cent of stated amount of gefitinib,\nCbHmQjN^FCI.\nUsual strength. 250 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gefitinib","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gefitinib"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gefitinib\nCTMW'CI\nMol. Wt. 446.9\nGefitinib is /V-(3-chloro-4-fluorophenyl)-7-methoxy-6-[3-(4- \nmorpholinyl)propoxy]-4-quinazolinoamine. .\nGefitinib contains riot less than 98.0 per cent andynot more \nthan 102.0 per1 cent C22H24O3N4FC1, calculated on the dried \nbasis. \n...........................\nCategory. Anticancer. \n> n :\n2454\nGAFITINIB\n$ 2022\nDescription. A white or off white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gelatin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gelatin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gelatin\nGelatin is a purified protein obtained by partial hydrolysis of \nanimal collagen. Gelatin used in the manufacture of capsule \nshells or as a pharmaceutical aid in the manufacture of tablets \nmay contain suitable antimicrobial agents.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (encapsulating agent; \nsuspending agent; tablet binding and coating agent).\nDescription. Light amber to faintly yellow, translucent flakes, \nsheets, shreds, powder or granules; slight. Stable in air but is \nsubject to microbial decomposition when moist or in solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/general","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"General"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"General\n(Ci per ml)\n0 The limits on effective dose apply to the sum of effective \ndoses from external as well as internal sources. The limits \nexclude the exposures due to natural background radiation \nand medical exposures.\nTc-99m \n8 x 104 \n2x105 \n1 x 10-4 \nF-18 \n5x104 \n7 x 104 \n3 x 10-5\n1-125 \n4 x10' \n6 x 10' \n3 x 10-6\nii) Calendar year shall be used for all prescribed dose limits.\nP-32 \n6 x 103 \n3 x 103 \n1 x 10-6\n3.1 Occupational Dose Limits\n1-131 \n9x 10' \n5 x 10' \n2x 10-s\n3.1.1 Occupational Workers\n4.0 Safety Considerations\nThe occupational exposures of any worker shall be so \ncontrolled that the following limits are not exceeded: \na.\nan effective dose of 20 mSv per year averaged over five \nconsecutive years (calculated on a sliding scale of five \nyears); \nb.\nan effective dose of 30 mSv in any year; \nc.\nan equivalent dose to the lens of the eye of 150 mSv in a \nyear; \nd.\nan equivalent dose to the extremities (hands and feet) of \n500 mSv in a year and \ne.\nan equivalent dose to the skin of 500 mSv in a year; \nf Limits given above apply to female workers also. However,\nSpecifies mandatory requirements published as AERB Codes \nand Guides for a nuclear medicine facility, covering the entire \nspectrum of operations ranging from the site approval, \nlaboratory design and setting up of a facility to its ultimate \ndecommissioning, including procedures to be followed during \nan emergency situation. The Code also stipulates requirements \nof qualified personnel's and their responsibilities.\nRadiopharmaceuticals owing to their detrimental effects on \nthe cellular structures, needs to be monitored for their harmful \neffects on the personnel involve in handling them. The critical \noperations include manufacture, storage, transport, \ncompounding, testing, dispensing, and administration to the \npatients.\nComplying as per ALARA (As Low As Reasonable \nAchievable) principle, in order to minimize the harmful effects \nof radiopharmaceuticals, there should be specialized \ntechniques for controlling the risk. A major consideration of \nthe ALARA principle includes exposure time, distance and \nradiation shielding. These factors should be carefully \nconsidered while designing the safety protocols for \nradiopharmaceuticals.\nAERB SAFETY CODE NO. AERB/RF-MED/SC-2 (Rev. 2), \nNUCLEAR MEDICINE FACILITIES, March 2011. This code \ncan be found at:\nhttp://www.aerb.gov.in/T/PU  B LICATION S/CODESGU IDES/ \nSC-MED-02R.pdf\nonce pregnancy is declared the equivalent dose limit to \nembryo/fetus shall be 1 mSv for the remainder of the \npregnancy.\nThere is also an AERB code that elaborates on regulatory \nrequirements and control of use, possession, transport of\nRadioactive Substances and Radiation sources.\nRadiation Sign\n3.1.2 Apprentices and Trainees\nAERB SAFETY GUIDE NO. AERB/NRF-TS/SG-10, SECURITY \nOF RADIOACTIVE MATERIAL DURING TRANSPORT, \nJanuary 2008. This code can be found at :\nRestricted area \t\n< 2 mR/h and < 100 mR/year\nThe occupational exposure of apprentices and trainees \nbetween 16 and 18 years of age shall be so controlled that the \nfollowing limits are not exceeded:\nCaution, Radiation Area \t\nmay exceed 5 mR per hour at \n30 cm from source\n(http://www.aerb.gov.in/T/PUBLICATIONS/CODESGUIDES/\nsg-10.pdf)\na.\nan effective dose of 6 mSv in a year; \nb.\nan equivalent dose to the lens of the eye of 50 mSv in a \nyear; \nc.\nan equivalent dose to the extremities (hands and feet) of\nCaution, High Radioactive \t\nmay exceed 100 mR per hour \nArea \t\nat 30 cm from source\n3.0 The Dose Limits for Exposures from Ionizing \nRadiations for workers and the members of the \npublic from Aerb Safety Code No. Aerb/Rf-med/ \nSc-2 (Rev. 2)\nDanger, Very High Radiation may reach 500 R per hour at \nArea \t\n1 m from source (Not in NM)\n150 mSv in a year and \nd.\nan equivalent dose to the skin of 150 mSv in a year.\nCaution Radioactive Material 100 Ci for Cs-137 and Sr-89\n1 mCi for Mo-99, 1-123 and \nCo-57\n3.2 Dose Limits for Members of the Public 3\nAERB Dircetiire No. 01/2011[Under Rule 15 of the Ato"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gentamicin-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gentamicin Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gentamicin Cream\nGentamicin Sulphate Cream\nGentamicin Cream is a viscous oil-in-water emulsion containing \nGentamicin Sulphate dissolved in the aqueous phase. ,\n2466 \n■IliilllMiiil\nGENTAMICIN SULPHATE\nIP 2022\nGentamicin Cream contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nmore thanl20.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of gentamicin\nUsual strengths. 0.1 per cent w/w and 0.3 per cent w/w"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gentamicin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gentamicin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gentamicin Injection\nGentamicin Sulphate Injection\nGentamicin Injection is a sterile solution of Gentamicin \nSulphate in Water for Injection. \n.\n2468\nIP 2022\nGentamicin Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ngentamicin.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 10 mg per ml and 40 ma \nper ml of gentamicin. \n*\nDescription. A clear, colourless to pale-yellow solution"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gentamicin-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gentamicin Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gentamicin Ointment\nGentamicin Sulphate Ointment\nGentamicin Ointment is a dispersion of Gentamicin Sulphate\n!li ^icrofine powder in white soft paraffin or other suitable \nhydrous greasy basis.\nGentamicin Ointment contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ngentamicin.\nUsual strengths. 0.1 per cent w/w and 0.3 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ginkgo-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ginkgo Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ginkgo Dry Extract\nGinkgo Dry Extract is obtained from whole or cut dried leaves \nof Ginkgo biloba L. by extraction with a suitable solvent \nusing an appropriate procedure.\nGinkgo Dry Extract contains not less than 22.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 27.0 per cent w/w offlavonoids, calculated \non dry basis. .\nCategory. Brain tonic.\nDescription. Yellowish to orange coloured powder, \nhygroscopic.\nDetermine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17), coating the \nplate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. Amixture of 17.5 volumes of water, 67.5 volumes \nof ethyl acetate, 7.5 volumes of acetic acid and 7.5 volumes \nof anhydrous formic acid.\nTest solution. Dissolve 100 mg of the extract in 10 ml of \nmethanol then sonicate for 10 minutes.\nReference solution. Reflux2gof ginkgo IPBRS with 10ml of \nmethanol for id minutes, cool and filter. Reflux the residue \nfurther with 2 x 10 ml of methanol, cool and filter. Combine all \nthe, filtrates and concentrate under vacuum to 10 ml.\nApply to. the plate 10. pl of each solution as band length 8 mm. \nAllow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate at 105° for\n4221\nwww.webofpharma.com\nGINKGO DRY EXTRACT\n10 minutes, and examine under ultraviolet light at 254 nm and \n366nm. \n: \n<\nThe chromatographic profile of the test solution is similar to \nthat of the reference solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ginkgo-tablet","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ginkgo Tablet"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ginkgo Tablet\nGinkgo biloba tablet\nGinkgo tablet contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110,0 per cent of stated amount of flavone \nglycosides. The, tablet may. be.coated. ;:;•. ■,;\nCategory. Brain tonic. \n;\nUsual strength. 120 mg"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ginkgo-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ginkgo Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ginkgo Tablets"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ginseng-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ginseng Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ginseng Dry Extract"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glacial-acetic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glacial Acetic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glacial Acetic Acid\no\nh3c^oh\nC2H4O2 \nMol. Wt. 60.1\nGlacial Acetic acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 100.5 per cent w/w of C2H4O2.\nCategory. Acidifying agent; buffering agent; pharmaceutical \naid (analytical reagent).\nDescription. A crystalline mass or clear, colourless, volatile \nliquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gliclazide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gliclazide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gliclazide\nC15H21N3O3S\nMol. Wt. 323.4\nGliclazide is T-(hexahydrocyclopenta[c]pyrrol-2(l£Z)-yl)-3-[{4- \nmethylphenyl)sulphonyl]urea.\nGliclazide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C15H2iN3O3S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategoryHypoglycaemic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glimepiride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glimepiride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glimepiride\nC24H34N4O5S \nMol. Wt 490.6\nGlimepiride is l-[[4-[2-(3-ethyl-4-methyl-2-oxo-3-pynoline-\n1 -carboxamido)ethyl]phenyl] sulphonyl]-3-/ra«s- \n(4-methylcyclohexyl)urea.\nGlimepiride contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C24H34N4O5S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Hypoglycaemic.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white powder.\nIdentificaition\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum obtained with that glimepiride IPRS\nor with the reference spectrum of glimepiride.\n2476\nwww.webofpharma.com\nJP2022 \nGLIMEPIRIDE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glipizide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glipizide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glipizide\nc21h27n5o4s\nMol. Wt. 445.5\nGlipizide is l-cydohexyI-3-[[4-[2-[[(5-methylpyrazine- \n2-yl)carbonyl] amino] ethyl]phenyl] sulphonyl] urea\nGlipizide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C2]H27N5O4S, calculated.on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Hypoglycaemic.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glutamic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glutamic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glutamic Acid\n¿-Glutamic Acid\nNH2 \nv\nCH9NO4 \nMol. Wt. 147.1\nGlutamic Acid is S-2-aniinopentanedioic acid,\nGlutamic Acid contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 101.5 per cent of C5H9NO4, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory, Amino Acid.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glutaraldehyde-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glutaraldehyde Solution"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glutaraldehyde Solution\nGlycerin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C 3H803, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\n0 H C \t CHC\nDescription. A colourless or almost colourless solution.\nCategory. Lubricant; laxative; pharmaceutical aid (humectant). \nDescription. A clear, colourless or almost colourless, syrupy \nliquid; odourless; very hygroscopic.\nC5H802 \nGlutaraldehyde Solution is a dilution of Strong Glutaraldehyd\ne \nWtl \nraldeh7d.l e\nIdentific\nation\n(95 per \nSolution in a mixture of purified water and c thanol \ncent). For preparation of Glutaraldehyde Solution, ethanol\nA.Heat 1 \nhydroxylar \nWater on a \nThe meltin \nand drying\nml with 10 ml of a solution containing 1 g of \nnine hydrochloride and 2 g of sodium acetate in \nwater-bath for 10 minutes, allow to cool and filter. \nig point of the residue, after washing with water \nat 105°, is about 178° (2.4.21).\n(95 per cent) may be replaced by industrial methylated spint."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glyburide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glyburide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glyburide\nMol. Wt. 494.0\nGlibenclamideis 1-{4-[2-(5-chloro-2-\nmethoxybenzamido)ethyl}benzenesulphonyl}-3-\ncyclohexylurea.\nGlibenclamide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 percent ofC23H2sCIN30sS, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Hypoglycaemic.\n.\n,\n.\nDose. 5mg daily, adjusted according to response; maximum\n15 mg daily, after food.\n.\n.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder.\nIF 2010"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glycerin-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glycerin Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glycerin Oral Solution\nGlycerin Oral Solution contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount, of \nglycerin, C3H8O3.\nCategory. Soothing agent.\nUsual strength. 50 per cent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glycerol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glycerol"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glycerol\nH ,OH\nHOJ~OH\nC3Hs0 3\nMol. Wt. 92.1\nGlycerin ispropane~1 ;2;3~triol.\nGlycerin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent of C3Hs0 3, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\n'\nCategory. Lubricant; laxative; pharmaceutical aid (humectant).\nDescription. A clear, colourless or almost colourless, syrupy\nliquid; odourless; very hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glyceryl-monostearate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glyceryl Monostearate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glyceryl Monostearate\nMonostearin\nGlyceryl Monostearate is a mixture of mono glycerides of stearic \nand palmitic acids, together with variable quantities of di- and \ntriglycerides.\nGlyceryl Monostearate, contains not less than 35.0 percent of \nmonoglycerides, calculated as glyceryl monostearopalmitate, \nC20H40O4, and not more than 7.0 per cent of free glycerin \nC3H8O3, both calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (emulsifying agent).\nDescription^ A white or almost white, hard, waxy mass or \nunctuous powder or flakes."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glycine-irrigation-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glycine Irrigation Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glycine Irrigation Solution\nGlycine Irrigation Solution is a sterile solution of Glycine in \nWater for Injections.\nGlycine Irrigation Solution contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nC2H5NO2. It contains no antimicrobial agent.\nUsual strength. 1.5 per cent w/v.\nDescription, Aclear, colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glycine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glycine"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glycine\nAminoacetic acid\n0\nH2N \t\nC-31H\nC2H5NO2 \t\nMol. Wt. 75.1\nGlycine is 2-aminoethanoic acid.\nGlycine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more than\n101.5 per cent of C 2H5NO2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Bladder irrigation agent; pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glycopyrrolate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glycopyrrolate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glycopyrrolate Injection\nGlycopyrrolateInjection is a sterile solution of Glycopyrrolate \nin Water for Inj ection.\nGlycopyrrolate Injection contains not less than 93.0 per cent \nand not more than 107.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nglycopyrrolate, C!9H28BrNO3.\nUsual strengths. 0.2 mgper ml; 2 mg per 10 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/glycopyrrolate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Glycopyrrolate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Glycopyrrolate\nC19H2SBrNO3\nMol. Wt. 398.3\nGlycopyrrolate is Pyrrolidinium, 3-[(57?)-(cyclopentylhydroxy- \nphenylacetyl)oxy]-1,1 -dimethyl-, |//WS1-jbromide.\nGlycopyrrolate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C19H28BrN63, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Anticholinergic.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gokhru","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gokhru"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gokhru"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/government-of-india","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Government of India"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Government of India\nNirmanBhawan\nNew Delhi-IIO 011.\n(Shri Rajesh Bhushan)\nCo- \nThe Chairperson, Scientific Body, IPC\nChairperson Former Director General, ICMR\nC-191, 9th Floor, City View Apartment\nPlotNo. 199, Sector-35\nNoida-201 VI. '\n(Prof. N. K.. Ganguly) '\nMeinber The Director General (DGHS) \n(ex-officio) Ministry of Health & Family Welfare\nNirmanBhawan ... ;\nNew Delhi-110 011 ■\n(Prof. Sunil Kumar)\nMember The Additional Secretary & Financial Advisor \n(ex-officio) Ministry of Health & Family Welfare\n. Nirman.Bhawan\nNew Delhi-110011.\n(Shri Ashish Srivastava)\nwww.webofpharma.com\nMember The Additional Secretary (Drugs), \\\n(ex-officio) Ministry of Health & Family Welfare\n; NirtnaiiBhawan .\nNew Del hi-1100] 1. '\n(Smt. Roh Singh) \n;\nMember The Joint Secretary (Regulations) \n(ex-officio) Ministry of Health & Family Welfare\nNirmanBhawan \n■ \n■ ■ ■\nt 5. \nNewDelhi-110011.\n(Dr. Mandeep Kumar Bhandari)\nMember \nThe Joint Secretary\n(ex-afficio) Department of Pharmaceuticals '\nMinistry of Chemicals and Fertilizers \nShastri Bhawan\n■. NewDelhi-110011.\nThe Director (Drugs) \n, .\nMinistry of Health & F amily Welfare"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gramicidin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gramicidin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gramicidin\nX\nY\nMol. Formula\nMol. Wt.\nAl\nL-Val\nL-Tip\nC99H140N20017\n1882\nA2\nA-Ile\nL-Trp\nCiqqHI42N20Oi7\n1896\nBl\nT-Val\nT-Phe\nC97H139N19O17\n1843\nCl\nA-Val\nf-Tyr\nCgyH^gN^Ojg\n1859\nC2\nA-lle\nI-Tyr\nC98H141N19O18\n1873\nCategory. Polypeptide Antibacterial.\nGramicidin consists of a family of antimicrobial linear \npolypeptides substance obtained by extraction from tyrothricin \nand produced by fermentation broth of Brevibacillus brevis \nDubos. The main component is gramicidin Al, together with \ngramicidins A2, Bl, Cl and C2 in particular.\nGramacidin has potency not less than 900 Units per mg, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder; \nslightly hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/granisetron-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Granisetron Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Granisetron Hydrochloride\nC18H25aN4O \nMol. Wt. 348.9\nGranisetron Hydrochloride is l-Methyl-7V-[(lR,3r,55)“9-methyl- \n9-azabicyclo[3.3.1]non-3-yl]-177-:indazole-3-carboxamide \nhydrochloride.\nGRANISETRON\nIP 2022\nGranisetron Hydrochloride contains not less than 97 q \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of;GigH25ClN^O \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n4 ’\nCategory. As Antiemetic in cytotoxic drug induced nausea \nand vomiting.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/granisetron-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Granisetron Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Granisetron Injection\nGranisetron Hydrochloride Injection\nGranisetron Injection is a sterile solution of Granisetron \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. It may contain suitable \npreservatives.\nGranisetron Injection contains not less than 93.0 per cent and \nnot more than 107.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ngranisetron, CjSH24N4O.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/green-coffee-bean-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Green Coffee Bean Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Green Coffee Bean Extract\nCoffea arabica \n•,f. m ■\nGreen Coffee Bean Extract’is obtained by extracting green \ncoffee bean {Coffea arabica Linn. Gaertncr Fam. Asteraceae) \nwith methanol or any other suitable solvent. ; 7 1;\nGreen Coffee Bean Extract contains not less than 4.0 per cent \nw/w of chlorogenic acid, calculated on the dried basis. .\nDescription. A greenish brown dry powder. -\nCategory, Central Nervous System Stimulant."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/griseofulvin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Griseofulvin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Griseofulvin\nC17H]7C1O6 \n•. Mol. Wt 352.8\n2502\nGRANISETRON TABLETS\n$ 2022\nGriseofulvin is (l\\S,6’7?)-7-chloro-2\\4,6-trimethoxy- 6’-methyl \n3H-spiro[l-benzofuran-2,l ’-cyclohex[2]ei>:.e]-3)4’..diOlle \nproduced by the growth of certain strains of Penicillin \ngriseofulvum.\nGriseofulvin contains not less than 94.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C!7Hi7C1O6, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. A white to yellowish white powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/guaipbenesin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Guaipbenesin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Guaipbenesin\nOCHs\nOH\nA;O~OH\nV\nClOH140 4\nMol. Wt. 198.2\nGuaiphenesin is (RS)-3-(2-methoxyphenoxy)propane-l;2-diol.\n1429\nwww.webofpharma.com\nGUAIPHENESIN\nGuaiphenesin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.5 per cent ofCIOHI40 4, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Expectorant.\nDose. For an adult, 200 to 400 mg every four hours; for a child\naged 6 to 12 years; 100 to 200 mg every four hours; for a child\naged 2 to 6 years, 50 to 100 mg every four hours.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless or with a slight characteristic odour."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/guaiphenesin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Guaiphenesin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Guaiphenesin\nOCH3 OH\nA A /OH\nCl0Hl4O4 \nMot. Wt. 198.2\nGuaiphenesin is (/tS)-3-(2-methoxyphenoxy)propane-1,2-diol.\nGuaiphenesin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.5 per cent of C10H14O4, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Expectorant.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/guar-gum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Guar Gum"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Guar Gum\nGuar Gum is a gum obtained from the ground endosperms of\nthe seeds ofCyamopsis tetragonolobus (Linn.) Taub or other\nspecies ofCyamopsis (Fam. Leguminosae). It consists mainly\nof a high molecular weight hydrocolloidal polysaccharide,\ncomposed of galactan and mannan units combined through\nglycosidic linkages.\nDescription. An almost white to pale yellowish white powder;\nodour, characteristic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gudmar-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gudmar Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gudmar Dry Extract\nGymnema sylvestre\nGudmar Dry Extract is obtained by extracting Gudmar with \naqueous ethanol'or ethanol or any othèr. suitable solvent and \nevaporation of solvent.\nGudmar Dry Extract contains not less than 90.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 120.0 per cent w/w of stated amount of \ngymnemic acids (calculated as gymnemagenin),. calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidiabetic, febrifuge, antiinflammatory, diuretic, \nmeha, ■ \n■\nDescription. A greenish brown to green: powder"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/guduchi","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Guduchi"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Guduchi\nRS\nTEST\n(Under UV light\nHt 254 nm)\nIP 20lO\nRS\nTEST\n(Under day light ancr spraying\nwith anis<lldchydc sulphuric acid\nRS\nTEST\n(Under UV light at 365 nm)\nreagent)\nTLC Chromatogram ofGudmar\nRS\nTEST\nRS\nTEST\n(Under day light after spraying\nwith anisaldehyde sulphuric acid\n(Under UV light Ht 365 run)\nreagent)\nTLC Chromatogram ofGuduchi\n472\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP2010\nGuggul Resin\nRS\nTEST\nRS\nTEST\nRS\nTEST\n(Under UV light at 254 nm)\n(Under UV light at 365 nm)\nGugulipid\nRS\nTEST\nRS\nTEST\nRS\nTEST\n(Under UV light at 254 nm)\n(Under UV light at 365 nm)\nTLC Chromatogram ofGugulipid\n473\n3.2. THIN LAYER CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND PROCESSED HERBS\n(Under day light after spraying\nwith\n10 per cent, v/v mcthanolic\nsulphuric acid reagent)\nTLC Chromatogram ofGuggul Resin\n(Under day light after spraying\nwith\n10 per cenl, v/v mcthanolic\nsulphuric acid reagent)\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/guggul-resin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Guggul Resin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Guggul Resin\nGuggul; Commiphora ewightii\nGuggul Resin is the oleoresin exudation from Commiphora \nwightii (Arnott) Bhandari (Commiphora mukul /Am.) \nBhandari,: Balsamodendron mukul Hook, ex Stocks) (Fam; \nBurseraceae). \n:\nGuggul Resip contains not less than 1 .O per cent w/w and not \nmore than 1.5 per cent w/w of guggulsterones (Z and E).\nCategory. Antihyperlipidaemic, antirheumatic, aphrodisiac, \nneuroprotcctive, vàtavyadhi. \n\"\nDescription. Light to dark-brown conglomerates of tears, \nrounded or irregular, slightly sticky to touch; odour, faintly \nbalsamic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/guggulipid-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Guggulipid Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Guggulipid Tablets\nGuggulipid Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not: \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nguggulsterones (Z and .E). The tablets may be coated.\nCategory. Antihypcrlip idaemic, antirheumatic, aphrodisiac, \nvatavyadhi, medohara. \n,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/guggulipid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Guggulipid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Guggulipid"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gugulipid-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gugulipid Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gugulipid Tablets\nGugulipid Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not\nmore than 110.0per centofthe stated amount ofgugulsterones\n(Z and E). The tablets may be coated."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/gugulipid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Gugulipid"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Gugulipid\nGugulipid is the ethyl acetate extractive ofGuggul Resin.\nGugulipid contains not less than 4.0 per cent w/w and not\nmore than 6.0 per cent w/w ofgugulsterones (Z and E).\nDescription. A brown, viscous liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/haloperidol-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Haloperidol Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Haloperidol Injection\nHaloperidol Injection is a sterile solution of Haloperidol in \nLactic Acid diluted with Water for Injections.\nHaloperidol Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nhaloperidol, C^H^CIFNO,.\n2509\nwww.webofpharma.com\nHALOPERIDOL INJECTION\nUsual strengths. 5 mgperml; lOmgperml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/haloperidol-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Haloperidol Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Haloperidol Oral Solution\nHaloperidol Oral Drops; Haloperidol Solution\nHaloperidol Oral Solution is a solution of Haloperidol in Purified \nWater prepared with the aid of Lactic Acid.\nHaloperidol Oral Solution contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nhaloperidol, C21H23CIFNO2.\nUsual strengths. 1 mg per ml; 2 mg per ml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/haloxon","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Haloxon","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Haloxon\nC1Jfl4Ch06P\nMol. Wt.415.6\nHaloxone is phosphoric acid bis(2-chloroethyl) 3-chloro-\n4-methyl-2-oxo-2H-1-benzopyran-7-yl ester.\nHaloxon contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more\nthan 100.5 per cent of CI4HI4Cb06P, calculated on the dried\nbasis\nCategory. Anthelmintic.\nDose. Allspecies exceptpoultry. 50 mg per kg ofbody weight.\nPoultry. 50 to 75 mg per kg ofbody weight.\n. Description. A white or almost white powder.\n2661\nwww.webofpharma.com\nHALOXON"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hard-paraffin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hard Paraffin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hard Paraffin\nHard Paraffin is a purified mixture of solid hydrocarbons \nobtained from petroleum or from shale oil.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (stiffening agent).\nDescription. A white or colourless, translucent mass, \nfrequently showing a crystalline structure; slightly greasy to \nthe touch. Bums with a luminous flame. When melted,, the \nliquid is free from fluorescence by daylight."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/haridra-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Haridra Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Haridra Dry Extract\nHaritaki consists of pericarp of the dried; fruit,ofTerminalia\nSolution. Dissolve a quantity of the extract under \nek^nation containing about 50 mg of curcumin in 100 ml of\nHaridra Dry Extract is a partially purified, natural complex of \ndiaryl heptanoid derivatives isolated by,extracting . Haridra \n(Curcuma, longa L., Fam. Zingiberaceae),rhizomes trith- ; \nacetone ox ethanol or any other suitgb(e volvent and \nevaporation of solvent under vacuum. . r \nS?: \nI\nchebula Retz. (Fam. Combretaceae);\nHaritaki contains not less than 5 per cent w/w of chebulagic \nacid and not less than 12.5 per cent w/w of chebulinic acid, \ncalculated on the dried.basis. . \n:\nfuran. Dilute 5.0 ml of this solution to 50.0 ml with \n^einobilephase.\nsolution. A solution containing 0.04 per cent w/v of \nIPRS, 0.01 per cent w/v of clemethoxycurcumin IPRS \n0.002; per cent w/v of b is deme thoxycurcumin IPRS in\nCategory. Purgative; astringent, antiageing, resortative,\nHaridra Dry Extract contains not less than 95.0 per cent w/w \n।\nand not more than 102.0 per cent w/w of die stated amount of\nrasayana.\n4232.\n4233\nwww.webofpharma.com\nHARITAKI\nDescription. It-has a shine oh; its external part and has \nlongitudinal ridges. The colour varies from yellowish brown \nto light black. It has a astringent taste and is also , slightly\nbitter / \n, \n;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/haritaki-aqueous-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Haritaki Aqueous Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Haritaki Aqueous Extract\nHaritaki Aqueous Extract is obtained from the dried fruit \npericarp of Haritaki (Terminalia chef ula:,Retz. pFam.\nCombretaceae) by extraction with water. ,.\nHaritaki Aqueous Extract contains hot less than 90.O per cent \nw/w and not more than 120.0 percent w/wofthestated amount \nof total polyphenols (sum of chebulagic acid, chebulic acid, \ngallic acid, corlagin, 1,3,6 trigalloyl glucose and ellagic acid).\nCategory. Purgative, astringent, antiageing, resortative, \nrasayana.' \n\"\nUsual strength. 40 per cent w/w.\n4235\nwww.webofpharma.com\nHARITAKÏ AQUEOUS EXTRACT\nDescription. Brown tobrown powder; withcharacteristic odour \nand bitter taste. \n,,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/heavy-kaolin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Heavy Kaolin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Heavy Kaolin\nHeavy Kaolin is a purified, natural, hydrated aluminium silicate \nof variable composition.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Fine, white or greyish white, soft powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/heavy-magnesia","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Heavy Magnesia"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Heavy Magnesia\nMgO \nMol. Wt. 40.3\nHeavy Magnesium Oxide contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 100.5 per cent of MgO, calculated \non the ignited basis. \nf.\nCategory. Antacid; osmotic laxative. \n,\nDescription. A fine, white powder. 15g occupies a volume °f\nabout30ml. \n' \n. •\nwww.webofpharma.com\n. jve about 15 mg in 2 ml of 2 Mnitric acid and neutralise \nwith 2 Id sodium hydroxide. The resulting solution gives \npotion (A) bf magnesium salts (2.3.1)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/heavy-magnesium-carbonate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Heavy Magnesium Carbonate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Heavy Magnesium Carbonate\nHeavy Magnesium Carbonate is a hydrated basic magnesium\ncarbonate.\nHeavy Magnesium Carbonate contains the equivalent of not\nless than 40.0 per cent and not more than 45.0 per\nMgO.\nCategory. Antacid; osmotic laxative.\nDose. As antacid, 300 to 600 mg;as laxative,.2p-4\nDescription. A white powder; odourless. 15,g 'occupies a\n•\nvolume of about 30 ml.\nIP 2018 \nIP 2018 \nLIGHT MAGNESIUM CARBONATE\nof bromine with a few drops of stannous chloride solution \nAsT. The resulting solution complies with the limit test for\n(porosity No. 3), allow to cool and dilute to 100.0 ml with \nwater. Evaporate 50.0 ml of the filtrate to dryness and dry the \nresidue at 105°; the residue weighs not more than 10 mg \n(1.0 per cent)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hemihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hemihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hemihydrate\nCI\nCIII-1, 5C12N,Y2H20 \t\nMol. Wt. 241.2\nLorcaserin Hydrochloride Hemihydrate is (R)-8-chloro- 1 - \nmethy1-2,3,4,5-tetrahydro1H-benzo[d]azepine hydrochloride \nhemihydrate.\nLorcaserin Hydrochloride Hemihydrate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C II H I5C12N,\n%FLO, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anorectic.\nDose. 10 mg twice daily.\nDescription. A white to off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/heparin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Heparin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Heparin Injection\nHeparin Sodium Injection\nHeparin Injection is a sterile solution of Heparin Sodium in \nWater for Injection. The pH of the solution may be adjusted \nby the addition of a suitable alkali or acid.\nHeparin Injection contains not less.than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated potency in terms of IU\nper ml. \n. \n,\nUsual strengths. 1000Unitsperml;5000 Unitsperml; 10000 \nUnits per ml.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or straw coloured solution, \nfree from turbidity and matter which deposit on standing. ,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/heparin-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Heparin Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Heparin Sodium\nHeparin Sodium is the sodium salt of sulphated \nglycosaminoglycans present as a. mixture of heterogeneous \nmolecules varying in molecular weights. It is present in \nmammalian tissues and is usually obtained from the intestinal \nmucosa or other suitable tissues of domestic mammals used \nfor food by man. The sourcing of heparin material must be\n2512\nHALOPERIDOL TABLETS\nIP 2022\nspecified in compliance with applicable regular \nrequirements. It is purified to retain a combination of activities \nagainst different fractions of the blood clotting sequence \nIt is composed of polymers of alternating derivatives of \nD-glucosamine (N-sulphated, O-sulphated, or N-acetylated) \nand uronic acid, L-iduronic acid or D-glucuronic acid) joined \nby glycosidic linkages. The component activities of the mixture \nare in ratios corresponding to those shown by the Heparin \nSodium reference standard. Some of these components have \nthe property of prolonging the clotting time of blood. This\noccurs mainly through the formation of a complex of each \ncomponent with the plasma proteins antithrombin III and \nheparin cofactor II to potentiate the inactivation of thrombin \nOther coagulation proteases in the clotting sequence, such \nas activated factor X, are also inhibited.\nHeparin Sodium intended for use in the manufacture of \nparenteral preparation contains not less than 180 IU per mg \nfor Heparin obtained from the intestinal mucosa or other \nsuitable tissues of domestic mammals used for food by man \nexcept bovine source. Heparin sodium intended for use in the \nmanufacture of parenteral preparation contains not less than\n150 IU per mg obtained from the intestinal mucosa or other \nsuitable tissues of bovine and heparin sodium not intended \nfor the use in the parenteral preparation contains not less \nthan 120 IU per mg, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticoagulant.\nDescription. A white or greyish-white powder; moderately \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hingu","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hingu"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hingu"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/histamine-acid-phosphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Histamine Acid Phosphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Histamine Acid Phosphate\nHN <> T ■ 2H3PO4, H2O\nCsH,N3,2H3PO4,H2O \nMol. Wt. 325.2\nHistamine Phosphate is 2-(l#-imidazol-4-yl)ethylamine \ndiphosphate monohydrate.\nHistamine Phosphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C\\H9N3,21 LP().;, calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Diagnostic aid (gastric secretion indicator).\nDescription. Colourless, long prismatic crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/histamine-phosphate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Histamine Phosphate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Histamine Phosphate Injection\nHistamine Acid Phosphate Injection\nHistamine Phosphate Injection is a sterile solution of Histamine \nPhosphate in Water for Injections.\nHistamine Phosphate Injection contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nhistamine phosphate, C5H9N3,2H3PO4,H3O.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/homatropine-hydrobromide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Homatropine Hydrobromide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Homatropine Hydrobromide\nC16H21UO;,nBr\nMol. Wt 356.3\nHomatropine Hydrobromide is (1 R,3r,5S)-3-(RS)~ \nmandeloyloxytropane hydrobromide.\nHISTAMINE PHOSPHATE INJECTION\nIP 2022\nHomatropine Hydrobromide contains not less than 99 q per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C$H2lNO3 HBr \ncalculated on. the dried basis. \n’\nCategory. Anticholinergic.\nDescription: Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/homatropine-methylbromide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Homatropine Methylbromide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Homatropine Methylbromide\nBr\nMol. Wt. 370.3\nHomatropine Methylbromide is (IR,3r,5S)-3-[[(2RS)-2-\nhydroxy-2-phenylacetyl]oxy]-8,8-dimethyl-8-\nazoniabicyclo[3;2;1]octanebromide; .\n- ~~-\nHomatropine Methylbromide contains not less than 98.5 per\ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C 17H24BrN03,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticholinegic\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or\ncolourless crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/horse-chestnut-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Horse Chestnut Dry Extract","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Horse Chestnut Dry Extract\nHorse chestnut; Aesculushippocastanum'''\nHorse Chestnut Dry Extract is obtained by extracting, fruit, of\nHorse Chestnut (Aesculus hippocastanum Fam. Sapindaceae) \nwith methanol or any other suitable solvent?\nHorse Chestnut Dry Extract contains not less than 45.0 per \ncent of the stated amount of total aescin, calculated on the \ndried basis. \n;\nCategory. Nervine Antioxidant.\nDescription. Brown coloured powder, hygroscopic.\n4237\nwww.webofpharma.com\nHORSE CHESTNUT DRY EXTRACT"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/human-albumin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Human Albumin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Human Albumin\nHuman Normal Albumin;Human Albumin Solution\nHuman Albumin is a sterile non-pyrogenic solution of the\nalbumin component obtained from pooled human blood or\nfrom normalplacentae frozen immediately after collection. It is\nobtained by fractionating source material such as blood,\nplasma, serum or placentae from healthy human donors and\ntested individually for the absence of hepatitis B surface\nantigen, HCV antibodies and HIV antibodies and complies\nwith othertests and requirements prescribed by the appropriate\nnational control authority. Source material obtained from\ndonors who do not meet all the requirements stated may be\nused provided that it has been demonstrated to the national\ncontrol authority that the process offractionation will remove\nany known agent capable ofadversely affecting the health of\nsubjects treated with the preparation. It may be prepared from\npooled source materials by precipitation with organic solvents\nunder controlled conditions of pH, ionic strength and\ntemperature or by chromatography or by any other method\nwhich does not affect the integrity of the product and has\nbeen shown to yield consistently a product containing not\nless than 95.0 per cent w/v of the total protein as albumin\nwhich is safe for intravenous injection. Residual organic\nsolvent, if present, is removed by freeze-drying or other\nsuitable treatment. The product is dissolved in sufficient water\nto obtain a suitable concentration, and a suitable stabilising\nagent is added to stabilise it to heat. It is prepared as a solution\ncontaining 15.0 to 25.0 per cent w/v of total protein or as an\nisotonic solution containing 4.0 to 5.0 per cent w/v of total\nprotein. No antimicrobial agent is added at any stage during\npreparation.and.allprocessing stepsareconducted.in..a.rnanner\nto.miI:liIllist;:ri:;lc.ofcolltamiI:latiOilfrOm t;:itl1er micI:O:QrgllIlisIl1s\nor other deleterious matter. The solution is sterilised by\nfiltration and distributed aseptically into containers which are\nthen sealed so as to exclude micro-organisms. The solution is\nIF 2010\nthen heated to and maintained at 60° ± 0;5° for 10 hours so as\nto prevent the transmission ofagents ofinfection transmissible\nby transfusion of blood or blood derivatives. Finally, the\ncontainers are stored for not less than 14 days at 30° to 32° or\nfor not less than 4 weeks at 20° and examined visually. Those\nshowing abnormalities such as abnormal colour, turbidity,\nmicrobial contamination, orpresence ofatypical particles must\nbe discarded.\nAlbumin Solution should be tested in accordance with the\nrequirements decided by the National Regulatory Authority;\nin particular, tests for the absence ofhepatitis B surface antigen\nand HIV antibodies are carried out by suitably sensitive\nmethods.\nHuman Albumin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not\nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount ofprotein. It\ncontains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0\nper cent ofthe contents ofNa and K stated on the label which\nare, in any case, not more than 160 millimoles ofNa per litre\nand 2 millimoles ofK per litre.\nDescription. A clear, slightly viscous liquid, ranging in colour\nfrom almost colourless to greenish-yellow or amber depending\non protein concentration and the method offractionation used."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/human-hepatitis-b-immunoglobulin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Human Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Human Hepatitis B Immunoglobulin\nTest solution; dilute the preparation under examination with a \n0.9 per cent solution of sodium chloride to a suitable \nconcentration. Concentration in the range of 0.4 -1.2 per cent \nand injection of50 - 600 pg of protein are. usually suitable. ;\nCategory. Immunological product."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/human-normal-immunoglobulin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Human Normal Immunoglobulin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Human Normal Immunoglobulin\nNormal Immunoglobulin; Immune Human Serum Globulin;\nHuman GammaGlobulin\nHuman Normal Immunoglobulin is a sterile solution or freeze-\ndried preparation containing immunoglobulins, mainly\nimmunoglobulin G (IgG), together with smaller amounts of\nother plasma proteins."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/human-plasma-for-fractionation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Human Plasma for Fractionation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Human Plasma for Fractionation\nSterility (2.2.11). Complies with the test for sterility.\nIndividual plasma units\nHuman Plasma for Fractionation is the liquid part of human \nblood remaining after separation of the cellular elements from \nblood collected in a receptacle containing an anticoagulant, \nor separated by continuous filtration or centrifugation of \nanticoagulated blood in an apheresis procedure; it is intended \nfor the manufacture of plasma-derived products."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/human-plasma-protein-fraction","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Human Plasma Protein Fraction"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Human Plasma Protein Fraction\nPlasma Protein Solution; Human Albumin Fraction \n(Saline); Plasma Protein Fraction; PPF\n1,000 ml with the same solvent.\nrepresents a negative result.\nPapain solution. Use serological grade papain from;|pecord the endpoint titre as the reciprocal of the highest \ncommercial source, the activity of which has been validate® ^ution that .gives rise to a positive result.\nHuman plasma protein fraction is a sterile isotonic aqueous \nsolution of proteins containing albumin and globulins \nobtained from human Plasma that complies with the \nmonograph of Plasma for fractionation. It is prepared as an\nRed blood cells. Use pooled red blood cells from not iewejThe titre ofthe preparation under examination is not greater \nthan 3 donors of group OR2R2 and 3 donors of group Owhan the titre ofthe reference preparation.\n4551\nwww.webofpharma.com\n. 1\nHUMAN PLASMA PROTEIN FRACTION\nisotonic solution containing 4;0 to 5.0 per cent w/v of total \nprotein. If contains no fibrinogen or antibodies.."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/human-prothrombin-complex","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Human Prothrombin Complex"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Human Prothrombin Complex\nHuman Prothrombin Complex is a plasma protein fraction\ncontaining blood coagulation factor IX together with variable\namounts of coagulation factors II, VII and X; the presence\nand proportion of these additional factors depends on the\nmethod of fractionation. It is obtained from human plasma\nthat complies with the monograph on Human Plasma for\nFractionation.\nThe potency of the preparation, reconstituted as stated on\nthe label, is not less than 20 ill offactor IX per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/human-rabies-immunoglobulin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Human Rabies Immunoglobulin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Human Rabies Immunoglobulin\nCategory. Immunological product.\nRabies Immunoglobulin is a'liquid or freeze-dried preparation \ncontaining immunoglobulins, mainly immunoglobulin G. The \npreparation is intended for intramuscular administration. It is \nobtained from plasma from donors immunised against rabies. \nIt contains specific antibodies neutralising the rabies virus. \nHuman normal immunoglobulin may be added.\nIt complies with the monograph on Human Normal \nImmunoglobulin, except for the minimum number of donors \nand the minimum total protein content."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/human-tetanus-immunoglobulin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Human Tetanus Immunoglobulin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Human Tetanus Immunoglobulin\ni-Histidinc\n16.0\nmg\nCategory. Immunological product.\nt-Isoleucine\n52.0\nmg\nTetanus Immunoglobulin is a liquid or freeze-dried preparation \ncontaining immunoglobulins, mainly immunoglobulin G. The \npreparation is intended for intramuscular administration. It is\nL-Leucinc\n52.0\nmg\nt-Lysine hydrochloride\n74.0\nmg\nTo all the chambers, containing-the dilutions of the referenti \npreparation and the dilutions ;of the preparation .undgj \nexamination, add 0.1 ml of the virus suspension corresponding: I \nto 100 CÓIDso per 0.1 ml (working virus dilution), sìM|I\nobtained .from plasma containing specific antibodies against \nthe toxin of Clostridium tetani. Human normal immuno­\nglobulin may be added.\ni-Phenylaianine\n33.0\nmg\nl-Threonine\n48.0\nmg\nL-Tryptophan\n8.0\nmg\nIt complies with the monograph on Human Normal \nImmunoglobulin, except for the minimum number of donors \nand the minimum total protein content.\nmanually, allow to stand in an atmosphere of carbon dioxide# । \n37° for 90 minutes, add 0.2 ml of the cell suspension, shakei | \nmanually and allow to stand in an atmosphere of carbon dioxi||l \nat 37° for 24 hours. \n\"Ui\nAfter 24 hours, discard the medium and remove the plaslM utamme\nt-Tyrosine\n36.0\nmg\n^Valine\n47.0\nmg\n^Methionine\n15.0\nmg\n0.292 g"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hyaluronidase-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyaluronidase Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hyaluronidase Injection\nHyaluronidase Injection is \"a sterile material consisting of \nHyaluronidase with or without excipients. It is filled in a scaled \ncontainer.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nHyaluronidase Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated number of Units \nof hyaluronidase activity.\nUsual strength. 1500 Units.\nDescription. A white or yellowish-white powder.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hyaluronidase","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyaluronidase"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hyaluronidase\nHyaluronidase is a material containing enzymes, which \ndepolymerise the mucopolysaccharide, hyaluronic acid. It may \nbe prepared from the. testes and semen of mammals and purified \nby fractional precipitation so as to remove inert material and \nto which hydrolysed, gelatin or a suitable non-protein \nstabilising agent may be added. The product is freeze-dried in \nsingle dose containers, which are sealed so as to exclude \nmicro-organisms.\nHyaluronidase contains not less than 300 Units per mg, \ncalculated on the dried basis. It may contain a suitable stabilizer.\nIP 2022\nCategory. Depolymerising enzyme used as spreading factor\nDescription. Awhite or yellowish-white, fluffy powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydralazine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydralazine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydralazine Hydrochloride\nH\nN'NH2 ■ HCI\nC8H8N4.HC1 \nMol, Wt. 196.6\nHydralazine Hydrochloride is phthalaziiirl-yhhydrazine \nhydrochloride,\nHydralazine Hydrochloride contains not less 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C8H8N4,HC1, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Vasodilator; antihypertensive,\nDescription, A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydralazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydralazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydralazine Injection\nHydralazine Hydrochloride Injection\nHydralazine Injection is a sterile material consisting of\nHydralazine Hydrochloride with or without auxiliary\nsubstances. It is filled in a sealed container.\nHYDRALAZINE INJECTION\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the\nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for\nInjections, immediately before use. For intravenous infusion,\nthe injection should be diluted with an appropriate volume of\na suitable diluent.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nHydralazine Injection contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 114.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nhydralazine hydrochloride, CsHsN4,HCl.\nUsual strength. 20 mg.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; very\nhygroscopic.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powders for Injection) and with thefollowing requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrochloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrochloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrochloride Injection \n#161. \n(Whole Cell) and Hepatitis \nB (tDNA) Vaccine’ \n4381 \nAdrenaline Bitartrate and Procaine \n_ \nAdsorbed Diphtheria, Tetanus, Pertussis (Whole Cell) \nHydrochloride Injection \n. \n. \n3354 \nand Haemophilus influenzae Type b Conjugate