\n4828\nAdsorbed Diphtheria, Tetanus, Pertussis: \n5s (Acellular: \nAlbizia lebbeck \n2 eh \n» \nA302 \nComponent) and Inactivated Poliomyelitis Vaccine 4346 \nAlbumin, Bovine \n: \n1068\nAdsorbed Diphtheria, Tetanus, Pertussi \nand. \n, \nAlbumin Fraction (Saline), H \nHuniah: \n4551\n: \nPoliomyelitis (Inactivated) Vaccine \n~ 4348 \nAlbumin; Haman \n“ \n1094, 4535\nAdsorbed Diphtheria, Tetanus, ‘Pertussis, \nAlbumin Phosphate Buffer pH 7.2°. \nol \n1062 \n_polomyelts Cnacvated) ‘and Haemophilus itn 0 \nAlbuterol \n. \n3548\nad “a j Pers ¥ crt \nsitar \n“SOO \nR-Albnterol Hydréebiloride! =: \n©. \n9753 \nsorbed \nPertussis \nVaccine \n(Ace \nar” \n- \npte \ncoe \nComponent) \nide \n“ygg3 \nAlbuterol Inhalation Acrosol: \n: \nom\nAdsorbed Pertussis Vaccine (Acellular, Co-putified) ° \n4356 \nAlbuterol Sulphate \nreg\n‘Adsorbed Diphtheria, Te etanus; Pertussis ‘(Whole’ Cell), \n(R)-Albuterol Sulp hate . . \n~ \n2\nHepatitis B ((DNA) and Haerhiophihis: influenzae: \nAlbuterol Sulpiate Injection \n+. \n3552\nVolume 1: i to xxxiv and 1 to 1276; I-1 to I-] \nVolume 4: xliii to xlvi and 4785 to 5024; Ll\nType b Conjugate Vaccine \n‘4376 \n Aibuterol Sulphate Syrup: \n‘ \n3553\n0; Volume 3: xxxix to xlii and 2991 to 4784,\n1113\nwww.webofpharma.com\nINDEX\nINDIAN“PHARMACOPOEFIA 2022\nAlbuterol Sulphate: Tablets \nwe Rae \n- 3554 \nAlcohol \na \noe \n2286 \nAlcohol, Absolute \n9986\nAlcohol, Dehydrated. \nmols \n, \n- 2286\nAlcohol (95 Per Cent). \n2087\nAlcohol-free. Chioroform.: \n1079\nAlcohol, General Notices \ns+ os. \n2\" \n212:1286; 3000; 4794\nAlcoholic Potassium Hydroxide \noa \n“1114\nAldehyde-free Ethanol \n1089 \nAldehyde-free Ethanol (95 per cent) \n| \n“ 49R9 \nAldehyde-free Methanol \n1102\nAlendronic Acid Tablets \n3592\nAlendronate Sodium \n_ \n4 \n491\nAlendronate Sodium Tablets. \n: \nfp \ntna, B92 \nAlfacaleidol \n269; 4038\nAlfacyclodextrin \n» \n:273,1974\nAlfadex \n» \n1974\nAlfuzosin \nSat \nAOD\nAlfuzosin Hydrochloride \n265, 492, 1394\nAlfuzosin Hydrochloride Table"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrochloride-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrochloride Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrochloride Tablets\nCategory. Local anaesthetic.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the two principal peaks is not less \nthan 4.0.\nDose. For surface anaesthesia of mouth and throat, 20 per \ncent as gel, paste, spray or solution, apply 4 times daily.\nBenzhexol Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 1 10.0 per cent of the stated amount of benzhexol \nhydrochloride, C201-131NO,HCI.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder; \nodourless.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of C 20H31 NO,HClin the tablet.\nUsual strengths. 2 mg; 5 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrochloride \t\nis \t\n445-[bis(2- \nchloroethypamino]-1-methylbenzimidazol-2-yl]butanoic acid \nhydrochloride.\nBendamustine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C I6H2ICl2N302.HCI, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antineoplastic.\nDescription. A white to almost white crystalline powder.\nBENDAMUSTINE INJECTION \n018 \nBENDAMUSTINE HYDROCHLORIDE \t\nIP 2018\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase B\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the relative standard \ndeviation for replicate injections is not more than 2.0 per cent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrochlorothiazide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrochlorothiazide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrochlorothiazide\nCrHsCn^CUSi \nMol. Wt. 297.7\nHydrochlorothiazide is 6-chloro-3,4-dihydro-2//-l ,2. \n4-benzothiadiazine-7-sulphonamide 1,1-dioxide.\nHydrochlorothiazide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C7H8C1N3O4S2, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrocortisone-acetate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrocortisone Acetate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrocortisone Acetate Injection\nCortisol Acetate Injection\nHydrocortisone Acetate Injection is a sterile suspension of a \nvery fine powder of Hydrocortisone Acetate in Water for \nInjections or Sodium Chloride Injection containing suitable \ndispersing agents.\nHydrocortisone Acetate Injection contains not less than 95.0 \nper cent and not more than 105,0 per cent of the stated amount \nof hydrocortisone acetate, C23H32O6.\nUsual strength. 25 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrocortisone-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrocortisone Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrocortisone Acetate\nCortisol Acetate\nC23H3206 \t\nMOI. Wt. 404.5\nHydrocortisone Acetate is 1113,17a-dihydroxy-\n3,20- dioxopregn-4-en-21-y1 acetate.\nHydrocortisone Acetate contains not less than 96.0 per cent \nand not more than 104.0 per cent of C23H3206, calculated on\nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nDose. By intra-articular injection or local infiltration, 5 to\n50 mg.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder, \nodourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrocortisone-eye-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrocortisone Eye Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrocortisone Eye Ointment\nHydrocortisone Acetate Eye Ointment; Cortisol \nAcetate Eye Ointment\nHydrocortisone Eye Ointment contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nhydrocortisone acetate, C23H32O5.\nUsual strength. 2.5 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrocortisone-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrocortisone Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrocortisone Ointment\nHydrocortisone Ointment contains Hydrocortisone in a \nsuitable base.\nHydrocortisone Ointment contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nhydrocortisone, C2!H/c,O5\nUsual strengths. 0.5percentw/w; 1 per cent w/w;2.5 per cent \nw/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrocortisone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrocortisone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrocortisone\nCortisol\nC21H30O5 \n'''' \n■ Mol. Wt. 362.5\nHydrocortisone is 11 fl, 17a ,21-trihydroxypregn-4-ene-3, \n20-dione.\nHydrocortisone contains not less than 96.0 per cent and not \nmore than 104.0 per cent ofC2iH30O5, calculated bn the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022 \nHYDROCORTISONE\npescription. A white to practically white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrogen-peroxide-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrogen Peroxide Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrogen Peroxide Solution\nH2O2 \nMol. Wt. 34.0\nHydrogen Peroxide Solution (20 Vol) is an aqueous solution \nof hydrogen peroxide. It may contain a suitable stabilising \nagent.\nHydrogen Peroxide Solution (20 Vol) contains not less than \n5.0 per cent w/v and not more than 7.0 per cent w/v of H2O2, \ncorresponding to about 20 times its volume of available \noxygen.\nCategory. Antiseptic; deodorant.\nDescription. A clear, colourless liquid. It decomposes in contact \nwith oxidisable organic matter and with certain metals and \nalso if allowed to become alkaline."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrogen-succinate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrogen Succinate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrogen Succinate\nHydrocortisone Hemisuccinate is 110,17a -dihydroxy- \n3,20-dioxopregn-4-en-21 -yl hydrogen succinate.\nHydrocortisone Hemisuccinate contains not less than 98.0 \nper cent and not more; than 101.0 per cent of C25H34O8, \ncalculated oh the dried basis. :\nCategory. Adrenocortical steroid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrogenated-castor-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrogenated Castor Oil"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrogenated Castor Oil\nCastor Wax; Opalwax\nHydrogenated Castor Oil is refined, bleached, hydrogenated\nand deodorised castor oil. It consists mainly ofthe triglyceride\nofhydroxystearic acid.\nDescription. A white to yellow powderofuniform consistency\nand texture. It may have a hard, waxy consistency."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrogenated-vegetable-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrogenated Vegetable Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrogenated Vegetable Oil\nHydrogenated Vegetable Oil is a mixture of triglycerides of \nfatty acids of vegetable origin.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. An almost white, fine powder at room temperature \nand a pale yellow, oily liquid above its melting point."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrous-benzoyl-peroxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrous Benzoyl Peroxide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrous Benzoyl Peroxide\nC14H10O4 \nMol. Wt. 242.2\nHydrous Benzoyl Peroxide is dibenzoyl peroxide.\nHydrous Benzoyl Peroxide contains not less than 70.0 per \ncent and not more than 77.0 per cent of C(4Hi0O4.\nCategory. Antiacne.\nDescription. A white or almost white, granular or amorphous \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydrous-wool-fat","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydrous Wool Fat"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydrous Wool Fat\nHydrous Wool Fat is a mixture of 7 5 pen cent w/w ofWool Fat \nand 25 per cent w/w of Purified Water.\n:: 'X-' ><; ' \n:V;': '•> \n7;! ■•:.■ ! \nJU. ; <; .< .i-JJ I; iUptii:\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (water-in-oil emuision ointment \nbase). ■■ \n:\nDescription. A pale yellow, unctuous substance. On heating, \nit separates at first into two; layers; with continued heating \nwith stirring, water is driven off and the residue which is \ntransparent while warm, cools to form a yellowish) tenacious, \nsoft mass. : \n'■ \n‘ \nd\nIdentification .. .... \n? \n...7\nA/To a solution of 0.5 g in 5 ml of chloroform add-of \nacetic anhydride and 0.1 ml of sulphuric acid; à'gfèencôloÙr \ndevelops. . \n. yy, -.x,, m A x'd, '\nB. To a solution of 50 mg in 5 ml of chlorofarm dâà 5 nd of \nsulphuric ■■ acid and shake; a red ; colour is. produced and a \nstrong fluorescence appears in the lower layer.: Afrbbii"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydroxocobalamin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydroxocobalamin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydroxocobalamin Injection\nHydroxocobalamin Injection is a sterile solution of \nHydroxocobalamin in Water for Injections containing \nsufficient Acetic Acid, Hydrochloric Acid or Sulphuric acid to \nadjusted to pH 4.\nHydroxocobalamin Injection contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nanhydrous hydroxocobalamin, C62HS9CoN13Oi5P.\nUsual strengths. 500 pg per ml; 1 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydroxocobalamin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydroxocobalamin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydroxocobalamin\nCS2HS9CoN13O15P \nMol. Wt 1346.4\nHydroxocobalamin is Cpa-[a-(5,6-dimethylbenzimidazolyl)]- \nCop-hydroxocobamide. It.occurs either as aquocobalamin \nchloride (Cod-[a-(5,6-dimethylbenzimidazolyl)]-iCop- \naquocqbamide chloride) or as aquocobalamin sulphate.\nHydroxocobalamin contains not less than 96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of aquocobalamin chloride, \nC62HS9CoN13O15P,HCl, or of aquocobalamin sulphate, \nC u4H178Co2N2603oP2,H2SO4j both calculated on the driedbasis.\nCategory. Vitamin B 12 analogue used as haematopoietic. ■■\nDescription. Dark red crystals or a crystalline powder; very \nhygroscopic. Some decomposition may Occur on drying. ,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydroxychloroquine-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydroxychloroquine Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydroxychloroquine Sulphate\nCl8H26CiN3O,H2SO4 \nMol Wt. 434.0\nHydroxychloroquine Sulphate is 2-[[(RS)-4-[(7- \nchloroquinolin-4-yl)amino]pentyI](ethyI)amino]ethan-l-ol \nsulphate. \n,\nHydroxychloroquine Sulphate contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and riot more than' 102.0 per cent of C^H^dNaO, H2SO4, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiprotozoal.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydroxyprogesterone-caproate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydroxyprogesterone Caproate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydroxyprogesterone Caproate\nC27H40O4 \nMol. Wt 428.6\nHydroxyprogesterone Hexanoate is 3.20-dioxopregn-4-en-\n17a-yl hexanoate\nHydroxyprogesterone Hexanoate contains not less than \n97.0 per cent and not more than 103.0 per cent of C27H40O4, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Progestogen.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydroxyprogesterone-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydroxyprogesterone Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydroxyprogesterone Injection\nHydroxyprogesterone Caproate Injection;\nHydroxyprogesterone Hexanoate Injection\nHydroxyprogesterone Injection is a sterile solution of \nHydroxyprogesterone Hexanoate in a suitable ester, in a \nsuitable fixed oil or in any mixture of fixed oils.\nHydroxyprogesterone Hexanoate Injection contains not less \nthan 92.5 per cent and not more than 107.5 per cent of the \nstated amount of hydroxyprogesterone hexanoate, ,C27H40O4.\nUsual strength. 250 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydroxyurea-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydroxyurea Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydroxyurea Capsules\nHydroxyurea Capsules contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nhydroxyurea, CH4N2O2.\nUsual strength. 500 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydroxyurea","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydroxyurea"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydroxyurea\no\nLJH2N N\nCH^NA \nMol. Wt. 76.1\nHydroxyurea contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of CH4N2O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydroxyzine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydroxyzine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydroxyzine Hydrochloride\nC^tCINA^HCI • . \nMol. Wt. 447.8\nHydroxyzine Hydrochloride is (AS)-2-[2-[4-[(4-chlorophepyl) \nphenylmethyl]piperazin-I-yl]ethoxy]ethanol dihydrochloride.\nHydroxyzine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C2iH27C1N2O2)2HCI5 \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiallergic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, hygroscopic, crystalline \npowder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hydroxyzine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hydroxyzine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hydroxyzine Oral Solution\nHydroxyzine Oral Solution contains Hydroxyzine \nHydrochloride in a suitable vehicle.\nHydroxyzine Oral Solution contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nhydroxyzine hydrochloride,, C21 H27C1N2O2,2HC1.\nUsual strength. ! 0 mg per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hyoscine-butylbromide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyoscine Butylbromide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hyoscine Butylbromide Injection\nScopolamine Butylbromide Injection\nHyoscine Butylbromide Injection is a sterile solution of \nHyoscine Butylbromide in Water for Injections.\nHyoscine Butylbromide Injection contains not less than \n92.5 per cent and not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated \namount of hyoscine butylbromide, C2iH30BrNO4.\nUsual strength. 20 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hyoscine-hydrobromide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyoscine Hydrobromide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hyoscine Hydrobromide Injection\nCorrection\nfactor\nScopolamine Hydrobromide Injection\nHyoscine Hydrobromide Injection: is a sterile solution of\nHyoscine Hydrobromide in Water for Inj ection.\nHyoscine Hydrobromide Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 1.10.0 per cent ofthe stated \namount of hyoscine hydrobromide, Ci7H2iNO4,HBr,3H2O.\nUsual strength. 400 pg per ml.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nn> 2022 \nHYOSCINE HYDROBROMIDE TABLETS"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hyoscyamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyoscyamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hyoscyamine Injection\nHyoscyamine Sulphate Injection\nHyoscyamine Injection is a sterile solution of Hyoscyamine \nSulphate in Water for Injections.\nHyoscyamine Injection contains not less than 93.0 per cent \nand not more than 107.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nhyoscyamine sulphate, (CnH^NOs^HjSO^^O.\nUsual strengths. 0.5 mg per ml; 2.0 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hyoscyamine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyoscyamine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hyoscyamine Oral Solution\nHyoscyamine Sulphate Oral Solution\nHyoscyamine Oral Solution is a mixture consisting of \nHyoscyamine sulphate with buffering agents and other \nexcipients. It contains suitable flavouring agents. It is filled in \na sealed container.\nThe oral solution is constituted by dispersing the contents of \nthe sealed container in the specified volume of water just \nbefore use.\nHyoscyamine Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nhyoscyamine sulphate, (C17H23NO3)2,H2SO4,2H2O.\nUsual strength. 0.125 mg per ml.\nStorage. Store the constituted solution in a refrigerator 2° to \n8°. Discard any unused portion after 30 days of reconstitution.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Oral Liquids and with the \nfollowing requirements. : ; \n.."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hyoscyamine-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hyoscyamine Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hyoscyamine Sulphate\n(C17H23NO3)2JH2SO4^H2O \nMol. Wt. 712.9\nHyoscyamine Sulphate is bis (lJ?,3r,55)- 8-methyl-8- \nazabicyclo[3.2.1]oct-3-yl(21$)-3-hydroxy-2-phenylpropionate] \nsulphate dihydrate.\nHyoscyamine Sulphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of (Ci7H23NO3)2,H2SO4,2H2O, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis. :\nCategory .Anticholinergic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless needles.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nHYOSCYAMIN SULPHATE\nIP'2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/hypromellose-phthalate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Hypromellose Phthalate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Hypromellose Phthalate\nHYDROXYPROPYL METHYLCELLULOSE PHTHALATE\nHydroxypropyl Methylcellulose Phthalate is 2-hydroxypropyl \nmethylcellulose phthalate.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white to off-white, free flowing flakes or a \ngranular powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ibudilast","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ibudilast"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ibudilast\nC14H18N2O \nMol Wt. 230.3\nIbudilast is 2-Methyl-l-(2-propan-2-ylpyrazolo[l,5-a]pyridin- \n3-yl)propan-l-one.\nIbudilast contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Ci4H18N2O, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antiallergic; antiasthamatic and vasodilator.\nDescription. A white to off white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ibuprofen-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ibuprofen Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ibuprofen Cream\nIbuprofen Cream contains Ibuprofen in a suitable cream base.\nIbuprofen Cream contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of ibuprofen,\nUsual strength. 15 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ibuprofen-gel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ibuprofen Gel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ibuprofen Gel\nIbuprofen Gel is a solution of Ibuprofen in a suitable water- \nmiscible base.\nIbuprofen Gel contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of ibuprofen, \nc13h1802.\nUsual strength. 15percentw/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ibuprofen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ibuprofen"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ibuprofen\nC13HlgO2 \nMol. Wt. 206.3\nIbuprofen is (7?.S)-2-(4“isobutyIphenyl)propionic acid.\nIbuprofen contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of CI3HISO2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anti-inflammatory; analgesic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/idoxuridine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Idoxuridine"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Idoxuridine\no\n,,~!\nHO~\nOH\nMol. Wt. 354.1\nIdoxuridine is 2'-deoxy-5-iodouridine.\nIdoxuridine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent of C9HuINzOs, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Antiviral for topical use.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/if-2018","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"If 2018"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"If 2018 \nDICLOXACILLIN SODIUM\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Tablei s.\nDose. 250 to 500 mg.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder,\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nFIN orocopic.\nTest solution (a). Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 50.0 ml of the mobile phase.\nTest solution. Weigh and powder 20 tablets. Weigh and tr \na quantity of the powder containing about 325 \nParacetamol to a 50.0 ml volumetric flask, add 30 ml of \nphase and disperse with the aid of ultrasound for ab \nminutes, cool and dilute to 50.0 ml with the mobile phase\nansfer \nmg of"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/if-2022","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"If 2022"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"If 2022\nUsual strength. 200 tig per ml.\nDescription. A colourless or very pale yellow solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ifosfamide-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ifosfamide for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ifosfamide for Injection\nIfosfamide for Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nIfosfamide with or without excipients. It is supplied in a sealed \ncontainer.\nCA UTION — Ifosfamide is Cytotoxic. Carry out the procedures \ndescribed below exercising appropriate precautions,.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements for Powders for Injections or Infusions stated \nunder Parenteral Preparations and with the following \nrequirements.\nIfosfamide Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of ifosfamide, \nC7H15C12N2O2P.\nUsual strength. 1000 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ifosfamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ifosfamide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ifosfamide\nci\nC,Hi5C12N2O2P \nMol.Wt.261.1\nIfosfamide is (.&$)-#,3-bis(2-chloroethyl)-1,3,2- \noxazaphosphinan-2-amine 2- oxide.\nIfosfamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C7H15C12N2O2P, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Cytotoxic alkylating agent\nDescription. A white or almost white, fine, crystalline powder, \nhygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/iloperidone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Iloperidone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Iloperidone\nC24H27FN2O4 \nMol Wt. 426.5\nIloperidone is I-(4-{3-{4-(6-Fluoro-l,2-benzoxazol-3- \ny l)p ip er idino] -prop oxy} -3-methoxyphenyl) ethanone.\nIloperidone contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C24H27FN2O4, calculated on the dried \nbasis. '\nCategory. Antipsychotic\nDescription, A white to off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/imatinib-mesylate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Imatinib Mesylate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Imatinib Mesylate Tablets\nImatinib Mesylate Tablets contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nimatinib, Q»H31N7O.\nUsual strengths. 100 mg; 400 mg.\nIMATINIB TABLETS"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/imatinib-mesylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Imatinib Mesylate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Imatinib Mesylate\nH H\nH3c'NNYJ^NYN Y^5^ ’CH3S°3H\no nJ\nch3\nC3OH3S N7SO4 \nMol. Wt. 589.7\nImatinib Mesylate is 4-[(4-Methylpiperazin-l-y l)methyl]-/V- \n[4-methyl-3-[[4-(pyridine.-3-yl)pyrimidin-2- \nyl]amino]phehyl]benzafnidemethanesulforiate.\nImatinib Mesylate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C30H35 N7SO4, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Cytotoxic\nDescription. A white or almost white, slightly brownish or \nyellowish powder, yellow or pale yellow, very hygroscopic, \nfor the amorphous form. It shows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/imatinib-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Imatinib Tablets","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Imatinib Tablets\ntIhnajenc2t .toheperrefceernetn.ce solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more\nDescription. A white to creamish yellow, crystalline powder-\nImatinib Mesylate Tablets"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/imipenem-and-cilastatin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Imipenem and Cilastatin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Imipenem and Cilastatin Injection\nImipenem and Cilastatin Injection is a sterile mixture of \nImipenem, Cilastatin Sodium, and Sodium Bicarbonate.\nThe injection is constituted as per the labelling requirements.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nImipenem and Cilastatin Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 115.0 per cent .of the stated \namounts of imipenem, C]2H17N3O4S and cilastatin, C16H26N2O5S.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powdersfor Injection) and with the following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/imipenem","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Imipenem"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Imipenem\nCOOH \nO /\nHO H H \nH\nC12H17N3O4S,H2O \nMol. Wt 317.4\nImipenem is (57?,6.S)-6-[(7?)-l -hydroxyethyl] -3-J [2-[ (imino- \nmethyl)amino] ethyl] sulphanyl]-7-oxo -1 -azabi- \ncyclo[3.2.0]hept-2-ene-2-carboxylic acid monohydrate.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIMIPENEM\nIP. 2022\nImipenem contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan lOl.O per cent of C^HnNsC^S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory-Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white to almost white or pale yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/imipramine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Imipramine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Imipramine Hydrochloride\nQ9H24NJICI \n, Mol. Wt. 316.9\nImipramine Hydrochloride is 10,1 l-dihydro-5f/-dibcnz|7;,/j \nazepine-5-(dimethylaminopropyl) hydrochloride.\nImipramine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per cent \narid not more than 101.0 per cent of CuHa^jHCI, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/in-the","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"In the"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"In the \nof any\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements.\nof the \nerence\nC43 1153N014 \t\nMO1. Wt. 807.9"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/inactivated-hepatitis-a-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Inactivated Hepatitis A Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Inactivated Hepatitis A Vaccine \n(Adsorbed)\nHepatitis A Vaccine (Inactivated, Adsorbed) is a liquid \npreparation of a suitable strain of hepatitis A virus grown in \ncell cultures, inactivated by a validated method and adsorbed \non a mineral carrier. The vaccine is an opalescent suspension?\nThe vaccine complies with the monograph on Vaccines. ’"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/inactivated-hepatitis-b-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Inactivated Hepatitis B Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Inactivated Hepatitis B Vaccine\nInactivated Hepatitis B Vaccine is a non-infectipus inactivated \nliquid preparation derived from the surface antigen of Hepatitis \nB virus (HbsAg). This preparation is white: or almost white \ntranslucent liquid in which the mineral carrier tends to settle \ndown slowly on keeping but is free from foreign particles/ \nfloccules.\nThe antigen is harvested and purified from the plasma of hurhan \ncarriers of Hepatitis B virus. The surface antigen contains all \nthe three antigen species (S, Pfe-Sl, Pre-S2). The individual \ndonor plasma is shown by sensitive tests to be seronegative \nfor HIV-I and HIV-2 and for HCV. The plasma pool is tested for \nfreedom from adventitious viruses and blood borne \ntransmissible pathogens by appropriate methods. The purified \nantigen is further inactivated by á validated method, usually \nwith formalin or any other inactivating agent, to render the \nhepatitis B virus harmless; The preparation is also tested for \nthe residual HBV DNA using a sensitive test approved by the \ncompetent authority and the level is shown to be less than\n1 pg HBV DNA per 50 doses.\nThe method .used for production of the vaccine must have \nbeen shown, to yield.a product consistently.complying with \nthe requirements of immunogenicity, safety and stability. The \nproduction,method .must also. ;be validated to demonstrate \nthat the products if tested, would comply with the tests for \nsafety and efficacy. y \n.\nReference preparation. Apart of a batch shown to be at least\nas immunogenic as a batch that produced in clinical, studies \nin young healthy adults not less than 95.Ó per cent \nseroconversion, corresponding to a level of neutralizing \nantibody accepted to be protective (HbsAg antibody titre \nnot less than 10 tnIU per ml after a full course of primary \nimmunization determined by Enzyme-LinkedTmrnhnosorbeht \nAssay (ELISA) is used as a reference preparation. / \n/\nCharacterisation of the substance\nDevelopment studies are carried out to characterize the \nantigen. The complete protein, lipid and carbohydrate structure \nof the antigen is established. The morphological characteristics \nof the antigen particles are established by electron microscopy. \nThe buoyant density of the antigen particles: is détermined by \na physico-chemical method (2.4.29), for example gradient \ncentrifugation. The antigenic epitopes are characterized. The \nprotein fraction of the antigen is characterizedin terms of the \nprimary structure (for example, by determination of the amino­\nacid composition, by partial amino-acid sequence analysis).\nPROPAGATION AND HARVEST\nIdentity, microbial purity, plasmid retention and consistency \nof yield are determiried at sú ¡table production stages.\n4405\nwww.webofpharma.com\nINACTIVATED HEPATITIS: B VACCINE\nMycoplasma (2-7.4): Complies with the.test for absence of \nmycoplasma.\nPUKHTED ANTIGEN \n7.\nOnly a purified antigen that complies with the following \nrequirementsmaybe used in the préparation of the final bulk;\nTotal proteint (2.3.49). The total protein is determined by a \nvalidated method. The content is within the limits approved \nfor the specific product. \n-\nAntigen content and identification. The quantity and \nspecificity of HBsAg is determined in comparison with the \nInternational standard for HBsAg subtype ad or an in-house \nreference, by a suitable immunochemical method such as radio \nimmunoassay, (RIA), enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay \n(ELISA), immunoblot (preferably using a monoclonal antibody \ndirected against a protective epitope) or single radial diffusion. \nThe antigen/protein ratio is within the limits approved for the \nspecific product.\nThe molecular weight of the major band in a sodium dodecyl \nsulphate polyacrylamide gel ; electrophoresis (SDS-PAGE) \nunder reduced conditions corresponds to the value expected \nfrom the gene sequence and possible glycosylation. ;\nAntigenic purity. The purity of the antigen is determined by \ncomparison with a reference preparation using liquid \nchrom"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/indapamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Indapamide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Indapamide\nC16H16C1N3O3S \nMol. Wt 365.8\nIndapamide is 4-chIoro-JV-(2-methyl-l-indolinyl)- \n3 -sulphamoylbenzamide.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP.2022\nINDAPAMIDE\nIndapamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 percent of Ci6Hi6CIN3O3S, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/indian-drug-manufacturers-association","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Indian Drug Manufacturers Association"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Indian Drug Manufacturers Association \n(IDMA)\n(Shri D. N. Sahoo)\n(Dr. G N. Singh)\nMember \t\nThe Drugs Control ler General (I) \n(ex-officio) \nDirectorate General of Health Services \nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare \nFDA Bhawan, Kotla Road \nNew Delhi-110 002.\nThe General Body\n102-B, Poonam Chambers, 'A' Wing' \nDr. Annie Besant Road, Worli \nMumbai — 400 018.\nThe composition of the General Body is as follows:\nChairman \nThe Secretary (Health & Family Welfare) \n(ex-officio) \nGovernment of India \nMinistry of Health & Family Welfare \nNirman Bhawan \nNew Delhi-110 011.\n(Mr. S. V. Veerramani)\n(Dr. G N. Singh)\nMember \t\nThe President \n(ex-officio) \nOrganization of Pharmaceutical Producers of \nIndia (OPPI), \nPeninsula Chambers, Ground floor \nGanpat Rao Kadam Marg, Lower Parel \nMumbai- 400 013.\nMember \t\nThe Director \n(ex-officio) \nNational Institute of Biologicals \nA-32, Sector-62, Institutional Area \nNoida-201 309.\n(Shri C. K. Mishra)\nCo- \t\nThe Chairman, Scientific Body, IPC \nChairman \nFormer Director General, ICMR \nC-191, 9th Floor, City View Apartment \nPlot No. 199, Sector-35 \nNoida-201 301.\n(Dr. Surinder Singh)"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/indian-institute-of-technology","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Indian Institute of Technology"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Indian Institute of Technology \n(Banaras Hindu University) \nVaranasi-221 005 \nUttar Pradesh.\nProf. B. Suresh (Chair), Prof. Praveen Aggarwal, Mr. Vinod \nArora, Dr. Manish Gangrade, Prof. Y. K. Gupta, Dr. S. S. Jadhav, \nDr. Prasad V. Kanitkar, Dr. H. G. Koshia, Dr. J. P. Mehta, \nDr. S. M. Mudda, Dr. D. B. Anantha Narayana, Dr. Vinay G Nayak, \nDr. S. Y. Pandey, Dr. G N. Qazi, Dr. Anurag S. Rathore, \nProf. Rakesh Kumar Sharma, Prof. Lalji Singh, Mr. R. Sridharan, \nDr. N. Udupa, Dr. P. V. Venugopal and Prof. M. R. Yaday.\nGeneral Chapters on Dosage Forms\nMr. Vinod Arora (Chair), Dr. Prashant Dixit.\nHerbal Products\nDr. D.B.A. Narayana (Chair), Dr. Amit Agrawal, \nDr. George. Patani, Dr. Pulok K. Mukherjee, Dr. C.K. Katiyar, \nDr. Nancy Pandita, Dr. Reema Shree, Dr. Benna Bhatt, \nDr. Vidhu Aeri, Mr. Ravikanth Kotagiri and Dr. Sanjeev Tripathi.\nMember \t\nDr Rakesh Narayanrao Tirpude \nAssistant Commissioner \nFood & Drugs Administration \nMaharashtra State \nMumbai-51. \nPresently In-charge \nAssistant Director \nFood and Drugs Control Laboratory \nFood & Drugs Administration, Mumbai \nMaharashtra.\nExpert Working Groups\nSub-group on Essential Oils\nReview of IP Work\nDr. Ramakant Harialkha, Mr. B. Murali, Dr. Rahul Singh, \nMs. Bhuvana Nageswaran and Dr. Hema Lohani.\nMr. J.L. Sipahimalani (Chair), Mr. R. Raghunandanan. \nMr. R. Sridharan.\nInhalation Products\nSub-Group for Reviewing IP Work\nMr. R. Sridharan (Chair), Mr. Satish Sharma, Mr. Sanjay Gupta, \nMr. Nagesh Shenoy, Mr. Ganadish Kamat, Mr. Amit Sule and \nMr. S.G Belapure.\nDr. R.A. Singh, Mr. Arvind Kukrety, Mr. Gaurang Oza and \nMr. S.L. Jat\nMember \t\nDr. Anil Kumar Tyagi \nCSO, R&D Department \nAtanIcind Pharma Ltd.\nIPRS Review Committee\nDr. V. G. Nayak (Chair), Dr. Raman \nDr. P. K. Guha, Dr. K. V. Jogi, Prof. Sanjay Sin \nDr. Girish Juneja and Dr. S. K. Mishra.\n, Dr. Hitendra Sahu, Mr. Prakash Bachani, \nr. Ajai Shahi, Mr. Sempal, Dr. Satyanarayan.\nOkhla Industrial Estate, Phase III \nNew Delhi — 110020.\nMicrobiology\nDr. J.P. Mehta (Chair), Dr. Suhas Mangaonkar, \nMr. C. Hariharan, Dr. P. K. Chitnis, Mr. Shinde Nilesh Prabhakar, \nMr. Ashok Desai and Mr. Om Prakash Verma.\nOphthalmics\nDr. S.M. Mudda (Chair), Mr. V. Shiv Kumar, Ms. Shakila S. Pai, \nMr. R.T. Arasu, Mr. Navneet V. Mehta, Ms. S. Asha, \nMr. P. Venkata Reddy and Ms. Aditi Panandikar.\nParenteral Preparations\nMr. Satish R. Kulkarni (Chair), Mr. Hemal Patel, Mr. Vijay V. \nKshirsagar, Mr. H.T. Nazare, Mr. S.L. Jat, Mr. Sudhir Pandya.\nVaccines and I mmunosera for Human Use\nMr. S.S. Jadhav (Chair), Dr. Arun Bhardwaj, \nDr. A. Ramkishan, Dr. Sumant Sharachchandra Karnik, \nDr. Sunil Gairola, Dr. Mahesh K. Bhalgat, Mr. Anil Sood, \nMr. P.M. Patel, Dr. Parag P. Nagarkar and Dr. K. Anand Kumar.\nBiological and rDNA Products\nDr. Anurag S. Rathore (Chair), Mr. S.S. Jadhav, Dr. SriramAkundi, \nMrs. Kinnari Vyas, Dr. Samir Sangitrao, Dr. Renu Jain, \nDr. Rahul Kulkarni, Dr. Satya M. Subrahmanyam, \nMs. Seema Shimpi, Dr. Mahesh K. Bhalgat, Dr. Jayasheel B. G, \nMs. Meenu Batolar, Mr. Parveen Jain, Dr. Maruthi Sai, \nMrs. Sudha V. Gopinath and Dr. G. R. Soni.\nVeterinary Products\nDr. Rishendra Verma (Chair), Dr. A. K. Tiwari, Dr. B. Prithavi \nRaj Kumar, Dr. D. J. Kalita, Dr. K. Srinivasan, Dr. Lalit M. Belwal, \nDr. Nitin Bhatia, Dr. Anil Sood, Dr. S. N. Singh, Mr. Sanjay \nGavkari, Dr. T. V. S. Rao.\nPlastic Containers\nProf. Y. K. Gupta (Chair), Dr. Vijay G. Habbu, Dr. N.C. Saha, \nDr. Vinod Pravin Sharma, Dr. Dinesh Kumar Bhardwaj, \nMs. Nisha Bura, Mr. A.K. Bhatnagar, Mr. A.K. Pradhan, \nMr. J.L. Sipahimalani and Mr. S.L. Jat.\nAllergen\nDr. S.N. Gaur (Chair), Prof. M.K. Agrawal, Dr. Surya Kant, \nDr. A.K. Jain, Dr. Naveen Arora, Dr. W.A. Shaikh, \nDr. Nagendra Prasad. K.V., Dr. S.E. Reddy, Mr. Aseem Sahu, \nDr. Jai Prakash and Dr. Achla Prasad.\nWebsite\nDr. D.B.A. Narayana (Chair), Dr. Venugopal and Mr. G.S. Bedi.\nIPC Secretariat\nScientific and administrative support to the \nGroups have been extended by the followin \nstaff of the IPC."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/indinavir-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Indinavir Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Indinavir Sulphate\nC36H47N5O4,H2SO4, \nMol. Wt. 711.9\nIndinavir Sulphate is [l-(lS\\27?),5-(2Sf)]-2,3,5-trideoxy- \njV-(2,3-dihydro-2-hydroxy-l/f-inden-l-yl)-5-[2-[{ 1,\nI-dimethylethyl)amino}carbonyl]-4-(3-pyridinylmethyl)- \nI-piperazinyl]“2“(phenylmethyl)-D-ery//zro-pentonamide \nsulphate.\nIndinavir Sulphate contains not less; than 98.5 per cent and \nnot more than 101.5 per cent of C36H47N5O4jH3SO4, calculated \non the anhydrous and ethanol-free basis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/indometacin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Indometacin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Indometacin\nC19H16C1NO4 \nMol. Wt. 357.8\nIndomethacin is l-(4-chlbrobenzoyl)-5-methoxy-2-niethyl-\nindol-3-yIacetic acid.\nIndomethacin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of Ci9H16C1NO4, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Antiinflammatory; analgesic.\nDescription. Awhite to pale yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/indomethacin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Indomethacin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Indomethacin\nIndometacin\nCOOH\nOCHs\nC19H16ClN04\nMoL Wt. 357.8\nIndomethacin is 1-(4-chlorobenzoyl)-5-methoxy-2-methyl-\nindol-3-ylacetic acid.\nIndomethacin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent of C19H16ClN04, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Antiinflammatory; analgesic.\nDose. Orally, 50 to 200 mg daily, in divided doses, with food.\nAs suppositories, 100mg atnight andin the morning ifrequired.\nMaximum combined.oral andrectaldose, 150to200mg daily.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow, crystalline powder;\nodourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/industrial-methylated-spirit","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Industrial Methylated Spirit"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Industrial Methylated Spirit\nIMS\nIndustrial Methylated Spirit is a mixture of nineteen volumes \nof Ethanol of an appropriate strength and one volume of \napproved wood naphtha.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (solvent).\nDescription, Clear, colourless, mobile, volatile liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/infectious-coryza-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Infectious Coryza Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Infectious Coryza Vaccine\nInfectious Coryza Vaccine is a suspension ofinactivated culture\nof suitable strains of one or more serotype/s or preferably\nlocally prevalent strain/s of Avibacterium (Haemophilus)\nparagallinarum in a suitable medium."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Infusion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Infusion\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Intravenous Infusion is a sterile \nWerial consisting of Vancomycin Hydrochloride with or. \nwithout excipients. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Intravenous Infusion contains \nnot less than 8 8.0 per cent of Vancomycin B.\nA. In the test for vancomycin B, the retention time of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with the test \nsolution corresponds to that in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the reference solution.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. A 10.0 per cent w/v solution of \nVancomycin Hydrochloride is clear (2.4.1) and the absorbance, \nofthe solution at 450 nm (2.4.7) is not more than 0.1.\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.5, determined on a 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nof Vancomycin Hydrochloride.\nVancomycin B. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nNOTE—Use the solutions within 4 hours of preparation.\nTest solution (a). Dissolve a quantity of the substance under \nexamination in sufficient mobile phase Ato produce a solution \ncontaining 2,000 IU of vancomycin per ml. .\nTest solution (b). Dilute 1.0 ml of test solution (a) to 25.0 ml \nwith mobile phase A.\nTest solution (c). Dilute 1.0 ml of test solution (b) to 40.0 ml. \nwith mobile phase A. '\nReference solution. A 0.05, per cent w/v solution of \nvancomycin hydrochloride IP RS in water. Heat at -65° for \n24 hours and allow to cool.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nendcapped octadecylsilane bonded tb porous silica \n(5 pm) (Such as Hypersil ODS),\n- \n- mobile phase: A. a mixture of 1 volumes of teirahydro- \nfuran, ! volumes of acetonitrile and 92 volumes of\nbuffer solution prepared by diluting 1 ml of triethylamine \nto 500 ml,with water, adjusted to pH 3.2 with \northophosphoric acid,\nB. a mixture of 1 volume of tetrahydro- \nfiiran, 29 volumes of acetonitrile and 70 volumes of\nbuffer solution,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n. , - spectrophotometer set at 280 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\n3923\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Inhalation\nSalmeterol and Fluticasone Propionate Inhalation is a \nsuspension of microfine Salmeterol Xinafoate and Fluticasone \nPropionate in a suitable liquid filled in a suitable pressurised \ncontainer. It may contain suitable pharmaceutical aids such as \nsurfactants, stabilizing agents.\nSalmeterol and Fluticasone Propionate Inhalation delivers not \nless than 80.0 per cent and not more than 120.0 per cent of the \nstated amounts of salmeterol, C25H37NO4 and fluticasone \npropionate, C25IL1 f\\O5S per inhalation by actuation of the \nvalve.\nUsual strengths. Salmeterol, 25 pg and Fluticasone \nPropionate, 50 pg; Salmeterol, 25 pg and Fluticasone \nPropionate, 125 pg; Salmeterol, 25 pg and Fluticasone \nPropionate, 250 pg; Salmeterol, 50 pg and Fluticasone \nPropionate, 100 pg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/inherpes","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Inherpes"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Inherpes\n2140\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \nrelative standard deviation for replicate injections is not more \nthan 2.0 per cent.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution.\nDescription. A white to cream coloured powder.\nCalculate the content of C141-119N504.\nIdentifi :ation\nA.Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with famciclovir RS \nor with the reference spectrum of famciclovir.\nFamciclovir Tablets\nFamciclovir Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nfamciclovir, C14H19N504•\nB.When examined in the range from 200 nm to 400 nm (2.4.7), \na 0.001 p ?,r cent w/v solution in methanol exhibits absorption \nmaxima a t 222 nm, 243 nm and 309 nm (2.4.7).\nUsual strengths. 250 mg; 500 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/inject","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Inject"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Inject \t\nthe test solution. In the\nCategory. Antiviral.\nIn the Assay, the retention time of principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nmore than \ni emdowitththn th\netee\nn\nchromatogram obtained\nrsoa f\n p \n, thrip\nenacrieaa of\nfpaeny\nDose. 1 mg once a day.\narea\nthe\nsecondary is\npeak\n chromatogram obtained with the reference solution \nito\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nin the \t ro"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Injection \n—\nSulphamethoxazole and Trimethoprim Injection\nTrimethoprim and ‘Sulphamethoxazole Injection is a sterile \nsolution in water for injection containing. Trimethoprim and \nSulphamethoxazole in the proportion of five parts. to. part\nrespectively.\nTrimethoprim and Sulphamethoxazole injection contains not\nless than 90.0:per cent not more than 110.00 per cent of the\nstated \namouiits \nof trimethoprim, \nCygHigN.O3, \nand\nsulphamethoxazole, CigHN3038. * \n.\nUsual strength. 80 mg of Trimethoprim and 400 mg of \nSulphamethoxazole perml.\nDescription. A clear colourless to pale yellow solution. ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/inositol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Inositol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Inositol \n-\nmyoTnositol \noo 2.\nCoH 205 \n\"Mol. Wt. 180.2\nInositol is cyclohexane-1,2,3, 5/4 6-hexol.,\nInositol contains not less than 97:0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent of CsH.O,, calculated on | the anhydrous \nbasis. -\nCategory. Vasodilator:\nDescription. Awhite or almost white crystalline powder \noy"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/insulin-aspart-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Insulin Aspart Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Insulin Aspart Injection\nInsulin Aspart Injection is a sterile, neutral, aqueous solution \nof Insulin Aspart.\nInsulin Aspart Injection contains not less than 90 per cent \nand not more than 110 per cent of the stated amount of Insulin \naspart, .\nIP 2022 \nINSULIN ASPART INJECTION\nUsual strength. 100IU per ml.\nDescription. A colourless liquid, free from turbidity and foreign \nmatter; during storage traces of very fine sediment may be \ndeposited."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/insulin-aspart","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Insulin Aspart"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Insulin Aspart\nG! y-1 Îe-Val -G I u -G În-Cys -Cys-T h r-Se r-1 fe-Cys -Ser-Leu-Tyr-G! n-Leu-GI iMsn-Ty r- Cys-Asn\nPhe-Val -Asn-GI n- H is-Leu-Cys-GI y-Ser- Hi s\nG ly- Gy s- Vá -Leu-Ty r-Le u-Ala-Glu- Vai- Leu\nGi u-Arg -G ly- Ph e-Phe-Ty r-Th r-As p- Lys -T hr\nC256H381N65O79S6 \nMol. Wt. 5,826\nInsulin Aspart is 28B -L-Aspartate insulin (human). It is a \n2-chain peptide containing 51 amino acids. The A-chain is \ncomposed of 21 amino acids and the B-chain is composed of \n30 amino acids. It is identical in primary structure to human \ninsulin, except that it has aspartic acid instead of proline at \nposition 28 of the B-chain. As in human insulin, insulin aspart \ncontains 2 interchain disulphide bonds and 1 intrachain \ndisulphide bond.\nInsulin aspart is produced by a method based on recombinant \nDNA (rDNA) technology under conditions designed to \nminimise the degree of microbial contamination of the product.\nInsulin Aspart contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 104.0 per cent of C256H3S1N65O79S6 plus A21Asp. \ninsulin aspart, B3Asp insulin aspart, B3isoAsp insulin aspart \nand B28isoAsp insulin aspart, calculated on the dried basis.\nBy convention, for the purpose of labeling insulin aspart \npreparations, 0.0350 mg of insulin aspart is equivalent to\n1 unit and 6 nmol.\nCategory. Antidiabetic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.\nHost cell DNA. Not more than 10 ng per dose.\nHost-cell-derived proteins. Not more than lOOppm.\nSingle-chain precursor. Not more than lOppm.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nI for 6 hours. Stop the reaction by adding 145 pl of sulphate \n| buffer solution pH 2.0.\nTest solution. Dissolve 200 mg of substance under examination \nin 100 ml of 0.01 M hydrochloric acid. Transfer 25 pl of this \nsolution to a clean tube. Add 100 pl of HEPES buffer solution \npH 7.5 and 20 pl of a 1 mg per ml solution of Staphylococcus\nI Reference solution. Prepare same as that of test solution using \nI insulin aspart IPRS instead of the substance under\nINSULIN ASPART\n| jP'2022\nI examination.\nB. Determine by peptide mapping (2.3.47).\nI .. .....\naureus strain V8protease. Cap the tube and incubate at 25°\nI Chromatographic system\nI ; a stainless steel column 10 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \nI octadecylsilyl silica gel (3 pm) with a pore size of 8 nm"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/insulin-glargine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Insulin Glargine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Insulin Glargine Injection\nInsulin Glargine Injection is sterile, aqueous solution of insulin \nglargine. \n\"'J\nInsulin Glargine Injection contains not less than 95 per cent \nand not more than 105 per cent of the stated amount of insulin \nglargine.\nInsulin Glargine Injection complies with the following \nadditional requirements.\nUsual strength. 100 units per ml.\nDescription. A clear colourless solution, free from turbidity; \nand foreign matter."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/insulin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Insulin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Insulin Injection\nNeutral Insulin; Neutral Insulin Injection; Soluble Insulin;\nPlain Insulin\nInsulin Injection is a neutral, sterile solution of bovine, porcine \nor human insulin.\nInsulin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of IU of Insulin.\nUsual strengths. 40 IU per ml; 80 IU per ml; 100 IU per ml.\nDescription. Acolourless liquid, free from turbidity and foreign \nmatter; during storage, traces of a very fine sediment may be \ndeposited."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/insulin-zinc-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Insulin Zinc Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Insulin Zinc Suspension\nI.Z.S; Insulin Zinc Suspension (Mixed); I.Z.S (Mixed); \nInsulin Lente\nInsulin Zinc Suspension is a sterile, buffered suspension of \nInsulin in the form of a complex obtained by the addition of \nzinc chloride to insulin in a manner such that the insulin is in \na form insoluble in water. It may be prepared by mixing \naseptically about 3 volumes of Insulin Zinc Suspension \n(Amorphous) and about 7 volumes of Insulin Zinc Suspension \n(Crystalline) and distributing the mixture aseptically into sterile \ncontainers which are then sealed so as to exclude micro­\norganisms.\nInsulin Zinc Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated number of IU of \nInsulin.\nUsual strengths. 40 IU per ml; 80 IU per ml.\nDescription. A white suspension which on standing deposits \na white sediment and leaves an almost colourless supernatant \nliquid. The sediment is readily resuspended on gentle shaking. \nWhen examined under a microscope, the majority of the \nparticles in the suspension are seen as rhombohedral crystals, \nwith a maximum dimension greater than 10 pm but rarely\n2606\nIP 2022\nexceeding 40 pm; a considerable number of particles have no \nuniform shape and do not exceed 2 pm in maximum dimension"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/interference","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Interference"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Interference\n■ Interference is anything that causes the signal from an analyte\nin a sample to be different from, the signal for the same\n336\nconcentration of that analyte in a calibration solution. The 1- \nwell-known chemical interference that is encountered in flame y \natomic absorption spectrometry is usually weak in ICP-AES. \ny\nIn rare cases where interference occurs, it may be necessas v \nto increase the RF power or to reduce the inner support-gis 1 \nflow to eliminate it. The interference in ICP-AES can be of ( \nspectral origin or even the result of high concentrations uf I \ncertain elements or matrix compounds. Physical interference £ \n(due to differences in viscosity and surface tension of the \nsample and calibration standards) can be minimised by dilution \nof the sample, matrix matching and use of internal standards r\nor through application of the method of standard additions\nAnother type of interference occasionally encountered in ICP- \nAES is the so-called ‘easily ionised elements (EIEs) effect’. \nThe EIEs are those elements that are ionised much more easily, \nfor example alkaline metals and alkaline earths. In samples that \ncontain high concentrations of EIEs (more than 0.1 per cent), \nsuppression or enhancement of emission signals is likely to \noccur.\nSpectral interference\nThis may be due to other lines or shifts in background intensity \nThese lines may- correspond to argon (observed above \n300 nm), OH bands due to the decomposition of water (af \nabout 300 nm), NO bands due to the interaction ofthe plasma \nwith the ambient air (between 200 nm and 300 nm), and other \nelements in the sample,, especially those present at high \nconcentrations. The interference falls into 4 different \ncategories: simple background shift, sloping background shift, \ndirect spectral overlap, and complex background shift.\nAbsorption interference\nThis arises when part of the emission from an' analyte is \nabsorbed before it reaches the detector. This effect is observed \nparticularly when the concentration of a strongly emittins \nelement is so high that the atoms or ions of that element that \nare in the lower energy state of transition absorb significant \namounts of the radiation emitted by the relevant excited \nspecies. This effect, known as self-absorption, determines \nthe lipper end ofthe linear working range for a given-emission \nline, \n.. \n,\nMulticomponent spectral fitting \ni\nMultiple emission-line determinations are commonly used to \novercome problems with spectral interferences. Abetteq more \naccurate7 method for performing spectral interference \ncorrections is to use the information obtained with advanced \ndetector systems through multicomponent spectral fitting. Tim \nquantifies not only the interference, but also the background \ncontribution from the matrix, thereby creating a correction! \nformula. Multicomponent spectral fitting utilises a multiple $ \nlinear-squares model based on the analysis ’ of pure analyte, \n-\nwww.webofpharma.com\nI- \n(fifes\n2.4.42. INDUCTIVELY COUPLED PLASMA SPECTROMETRY\nIP 2022\nthe matrix and the blank, creating an interference-corrected \npjathernatical model. This permits the determination .of the \nanal^e emissipti in a complex matrix with improved detection \nlimits and accuracy. \n■\nProcedure\nSample preparation and sample introduction\nThe basic gbal for the sample preparation is to ensure that the \nanalyte concentration falls within the working range of the \ninstrument through dilution or preconcentration, and that the \nsample-containing solution can be nebulised in a reproducible\nmanner. .\nSeveral sample-introduction systems tolerate high acid \nconcentrations, but the use of sulphuric and phosphoric acids \ncan contribute to background emission observed in the ICP \nspectra. Therefore; nitric and hydrochloric acids are preferable. \nThe availability of hydrofluoric acid-resistant (for example \nperfluoroalkoxy polymer) sample-introduction systems and \ntorches also allows the use of hydrofluoric acid. In selecting "}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/international-standard","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"International Standard"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"International Standard\nwhich consists of freeze-dried\nprekallikrein activator. The equivalence in International Units\nof the International Standard is stated by the World Health\nOrganisation.\nReagents. Prekallikrein activator in albumin RS is calibrated\nin International Units by comparison with the International\nStandard.\nBuffer A. Dissolve 6.055 g of tris(hydroxymethyl)-\naminomethane, 1.17 g of sodium\nchloride, 50 mg of\nhexadimethrine bromide and 0.100 g ofsodium azide in water.\nAdjust to pH 8.0 with 2 M hydrochloric acid and dilute to\n1000 ml with water.\nBuffer B. Dissolve 6.055 g of tris(hydroxymethyl)-\naminomethane and 8.77 g of sodium chloride in water. Adjust\nto pH 8.0 with 2 M hydrochloric acid and dilute to 1,000 ml\nwith water.\nPreparation ofprekallikrein substrate\nTo avoid coagulation activation, blood or plasma used for\nthe preparation of prekallikrein must come into contact\nonly with plastics or silicone-treated glass sUifaces.\nDraw 9 volumes ofhuman blood into 1volume ofanticoagulant\nsolution (ACD, CPD or 3.8 per cent w/v solution ofsodium\ncitrate) to which 1 mg per ml ofhexadimethrine bromide has\nbeen added. Centrifuge the mixture at 3,600 g for 5 minutes.\nSeparate the plasma and centrifuge again at 6,000 g for 20\nminutes to sedimentplatelets. Separate the platelet-poorplasma\nand dialyse against 10 volumes ofbufferA for 20 hours. Apply\nthe dialysed plasma to a chromatography column containing\nagarose-DEAE for ion exchange chromatography which has\nbeen equilibrated in buffer A and is equal to twice the volume\nofthe plasma. Elute from the column with bufferA at20 ml per\ncm2 per hours. Collect the eluate in fractions and record the\nabsorbance (2.4.7) at 280 nm. Pool the fractions containing\nthe first protein peak so that the volume of the pool is about\n1.2 times the volume ofthe platelet-poor plasma.\nTest the substrate pool for absence of kallikrein activity by\nmixing I part with 20 parts of the pre-warmed chromogenic\nsubstrate solution to be used in the assay and incubate at 37°\nfor 2 minutes. The substrate is suitable if the increase in\nabsorbance is less than 0.001 per minute. Add to the pooled\nsolution 0.7 per cent w/v ofsodium chloride and filter using a\nmembrane filter (porosity 0.45 /lm). Freeze the filtrate inportions\nand store at _25°; the substrate may be freeze-dried before\nstorage.\n2585\nCarry out all procedures from the beginning of the\nchromatography to freezing in portions during a single working\nday.\nMethod. The assay may be carried out using an automated\nenzyme analyser or a suitable microtitre plate system allowing\nkinetic measurements, with appropriate software for calculation\nofresults. Standards, samples and prekallikrein substrate may\nbe diluted as necessary using buffer B.\nIncubate diluted standards or samples with prekallikrein\nsubstrate for 10 minutes such that the volume ofthe undiluted\nsample does not exceed 1/10 of the total volume of the\nincubation mixture to avoid errors caused by variation in ionic\nstrength and pH in\nthe incubation mixture. Incubate the\nmixture or a part thereofwith at least an equal volume of a\nsolution ofa suitable synthetic chromogenic substrate, known\nto be specific for kallikrein (for example, N-benzoyl-L-prolyl-\nL-phenylalanyl~L-arginine 4-nitroanilide acetate or D-\nproly1- L-phenyIaIany1-L-arginine- 4-n itroaniiide-\ndihydrochloride), dissolved in buffer B. Record the rate of\nchange in absorbance per minute for 2 to 10 minutes at the\nwavelength specific for the substrate used. Prepare a blank\nfor each mixture ofsample or standard using buffer B instead\nofprekallikrein substrate.\nDepending on the method used, ~ A per minutes has to be\ncorrected by subtracting the value obtained for the\ncorresponding blank without the prekallikrein substrate. The\nresults may be calculated using a standard curve, a parallel-\nline or a slope ratio assay or any other suitable statistical\nmethod. Plot a calibration curve using the values thus obtained\nfor\nthe\nreference preparation\nand\nth"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/intravenous-infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Intravenous Infusion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Intravenous Infusion\nAciclovir Intravenous Infusion is a sterile material consisting \nof aciclovir sodium, prepared from Aciclovir with the aid of a \nsuitable alkali, with or without auxiliary substances. It is filled \nin a sealed container.\nThe infusion is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nAciclovir Intravenous Infusion contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated \namount of aciclovir, CsHiiN5O3.\nUsual strength. 500 mg per vial.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder. ■\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Injections) and with thefollowing requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/invert-sugar-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Invert Sugar Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Invert Sugar Injection\nInvert Sugar Injection is a sterile solution of a mixture of equal \namounts of Dextrose and Fructose in Water for Injections, or \nan equivalent sterile solution produced by the hydrolysis of \nSucrose in Water for Injections. It contains no antimicrobial \nagent.\nInvert Sugar Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent ofthe labelled amount of C6H12O6.\nUsual strengths. 5,10, and 20 per cent w/v.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured \nsolution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/invert-syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Invert Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Invert Syrup\nInvert Syrup is a mixture of glucose and fructose prepared by \nhydrolysing a 66.7 per cent w/w solution of Sucrose with a \nsuitable mineral acid, such as hydrochloric acid and \nneutralising the resulting solution using calciufn carbonate or \nsodium carbonate. The degree of inversion is at least 95 per \ncent.\nInvert Syrup contains not less than 67.0 per cent w/w of \nthe stated amount of reducing sugars, expressed as invert \nsugar.\nproduce .Category. Sweetener.\nDescription: Aclear, colourless topale straw-coloured syrupy \nliquid. Miscible with water, producing a clear solution; it \ndissolves in ethanol (95 per cent) with the formation of an \ninsoluble residue."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/iodine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Iodine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Iodine\nI, \nMol. Wt. 253.8\nIodine contains not less than 99.5 per cent and not more ■. \nthan 100.5 per cent of I2:\nCategory. Antiseptic; used in the treatment of thyroid \ndeficiency.\nDescription. Greyish violet brittle plates or small crystals with \na metallic sheen. It volatalises slowly at room temperature."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/iodoquinol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Iodoquinol"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Iodoquinol\nOH\nk)yN'1..\n~\nI\n~HsI2NO\nMol. Wt. 396.9\npiiod()hYdroxyquinoline is ~,'7:diiod()qllirl()liIl...S:()l.\nDiiodohydroxyquinoline contains not less than 97.0 per cent\nand not more than 100.5 per cent of CgHsI2NO, calculated on\nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Antiamoebic.\nDose. 1 to 2 g daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A light yellowish to yellowish-brown,\niIlicr6crystallinepbWder;odolitlessor with a faint odolit.··"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ip-201-8","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ip 201 8"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ip 201 8\nChromatographic system\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\ndiluting 2 ml of the substance under examination with suffi cient \n1.5 Macetic acid to produce 10 ml and diluting 0.2 ml of this\na stainless steel column 20 cm x 4 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 gm), \nmobile phase: 2.0 g of sodium octanesulphonate in a \nmixture of 120 ml ofglacial acetic acid, 270 ml of water \nand 730 ml of methanol, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \n-\nspectrophotometer set at 254 nm, \ninjection volume: 10\n solution to 50 ml with \nmethanol."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ip-2018","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ip 2018"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ip 2018 \nHEPARIN SODIUM\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the peaks due to oversulphated\nDescription. A white or greyish -white powder: odourless; \nmoderately hygroscopic.\nof the substance to be examined and S I, S2, S3, S4 for each of \nthe 4 series of dilutions of the reference preparation. To each \nof the 32 tubes add 100 tl of antithrombin III solution and \n50 gl of the appropriate dilution of the substance under \nexamination or the reference preparation. After each addition, \nmix but do not allow bubbles to form. Treating the tubes in 2 \nsubsequent series in the order Si, S2, S3, S4, T1, T2, T3, T4, Ti, \nT2, T3, T4, S t , S2, S3, S4, allow to equilibrate at 37° (water-bath \nor heating block) for at least 1 minute and add to each tube \n25 pi of human thrombin solution. Incubate for exactly 1 minute \nand add 50 .tl of a chromogenic substrate specific to factor IIa \nat a concentration suitable for the assay (for example, D-\nphenylalanyl -L -pipecolyl -L -arginine-4 -nitroanilide\nchondroitin sulphate and heparin is not less than 1.5. The \nretention time of heparin is about 30 minutes and of"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ip-2022","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ip 2022"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ip 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ipecac-tincture","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ipecac Tincture"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ipecac Tincture"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ipratropium-bromide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ipratropium Bromide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ipratropium Bromide\nC20H3oBrNO3,H2O \nMol. Wt. 430.4\nIpratropium Bromide is: 3a-Hydr oxy-8-isopropyl-1 \ntropanium bromide (2?>S)-tropate monohydrate.\nIpratropium Bromide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per,cent of C20H30BrNO3, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticholinergic; bronchodilator, a\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ipratropium-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ipratropium Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ipratropium Inhalation\nIpratropium Bromide Inhalation\nIpratropium Inhalation is a solution or suspension of \nIpratropium Bromide in a suitable liquid in a suitable \npressurised container.\nThe pressurised inhalation complies with the requirements \nstated under Inhalation Preparation and.with the following \nrequirements.\nIpratropium Inhalation delivers not less than 85.0 per cent and \nnot more than, 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nipratropium bromide, C26H30NO3Br,H2O, per inhalation by \nactuation of the valve.\nUsual strengths. 20 pg per metered dose; 40 pg per metered \ndose."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/irbesartan","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Irbesartan"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Irbesartan\nC25H28N6O \n- ■ Mol. Wt. 428.5\nIrbesartan is 2-butyl-3-{4-[2“(l//-tetrazol-5-yl)phenyl]benzyl}-\n1,3-diazaspiro[4.4]non-1 -cn-4-one.\nIrbesartan contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C^^NgO, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/irinotecan-hydrochloride-trihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Irinotecan Hydrochloride Trihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Irinotecan Hydrochloride Trihydrate\nC33 H38N4O6.HCI, 3H2O \nMol. Wt. 677.2\nIrinotecan Hydrochloride Trihydrate is (4iS)r4,11-diethyl- \n3,4,12,14-tetrahydro-4-hydroxy-3,14-dioxo-1 77-pyrano [3 ’,4' \n:6,7]indolizinoil ,2-&]quiholin-9-yl [1 ,4'-bipipcridine]-T- \ncarboxylate hydrochloride trihydrate.\nIrinotecan Hydrochloride Trihydrate contains not less than \n98.0 per cent and not more than 102.0 per Cent of C33 H38NX)6, \nHC1, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticancer\nDescription. A pale yellow to yellow crystalline powder.\nCAUTION— Irinotecan Hydrochloride Tri hydrate is \npotentially cytotoxic. Great care should be taken in handling\nthe powder and preparing solutions. \n' \n■ L\\\nIRINOTECAN HYDROCHLORIDE TRITHYDRATE\nIP 2022;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/irinotecan-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Irinotecan Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Irinotecan Injection\nIrinotecan Hydrochloride Inj ection\nIrinotecan Injection is a sterile solution of Irinotecan \nHydrochloride Trihydrate in water.\nIrinotecan Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of irinotecan \nhydrochloride trihydrate, C33H38N4O6,HC1,3H2O.\nUsual strength. 20 mg per ml.\nDescription. A light yellow coloured solution, free from visible \nparticles."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/iron-and-ammonium-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Iron and Ammonium Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Iron and Ammonium Citrate\nFerric Ammonium Citrate\nIron and Ammonium Citrate is a complex of ammonium ferric\nUsual strength. Ferrous sulphate equivalent to 100 mg \nelemental ferrous iron and 0.5 mg of folic acid per 5 ml.\ncitrate.\n100 ml. To 10 ml add 1 g of citric acid and 0.1 g of resorcinol, \nneutralise with dilute ammonia solution, using thvmol blue \nsolution as indicator, and shake for 1 minute with two \nsuccessive quantities, each of 20 ml, ofdithizone solution. To \nthe combined extracts add 10 ml of 0./ Al hydrochloric acid, \nshake for 1 minute, separate the acid layer and wash it with \n2 ml of chlorofbrm. To the acid layer add 3 ml of 1 M hydro-\nchloric acid and 20 ml of ammonium chloride solution and \nadjust the volume to 50 ml with water. Add 1 ml of potassium\nIron and Ammonium Citrate contains not less than 20.5 per"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/iron-dextran-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Iron Dextran Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Iron Dextran Injection\nIron Dextran Injection is a sterile colloidal solution containing \na complex of ferric hydroxide with practically hydrolised \ndextrans of average molecular weight about 1000.\nIron Dextran Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent w/v of the stated amount of iron \nand not more than 0.5 per cent of phenol as a preservative.\nCategory. Haematinic.\nDescription. A dark brown slightly viscous liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isobutane","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isobutane"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isobutane\nH3Cx^CH3\nCH3\nC4H!0 \nMol.wt.58fl\nIsobutane is 2-methylpropane.\nIsobutane contains not less than 95.0 per cent of C4H10.\nCategory. Phannaceutical aid.\nCA UTION Isobutane is highly flammable and explosive."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isoflurane","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isoflurane"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isoflurane\nHz ACI\nF3C'/^O'/X\"F anc* enantiomer\nMol. Wt. 184.5\n2630\nwww.webofpharma.com\n'Isoflurane is (2 RS)-2-Chloro-2-( difluoromethoxy)-1,1,1-\n(rifluoroethane.\nCategory. General anaesthetic.\npescription. A clear, colourless, mobile and heavy liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isoniazid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isoniazid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isoniazid\nIsonicotinylhydrazid; INH\nC6H7N3O\nMol. Wt. 137.1\nIsoniazid is isonicotinic acid hydrazide.\nIsoniazid contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C6H7N3O, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antituberculosis.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isoprenaline-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isoprenaline Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isoprenaline Injection\nIsoprenaline Hydrochloride Injection; Isoproterenol Injection;\nIsoproterenol Hydrochloride Injection\nIsoprenaline Injection is a sterile solution of Isoprenaline\nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. It may contain suitable\nstabilising agents.\nIsoprenaline Hydrochloride Injection contains not Jess than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated\namount ofisoprenaline hydrochloride, C11H 17N03,HCl.\nUsual strength. 200 Ilg per ml.\nDescription. A colourless or very pale yellow solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isoprenaline-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isoprenaline Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isoprenaline Tablets\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nIsoprenaline Sulphate Tablets; Isoproterenol Tablets; \nIsoproterenol Sulphate Tablets"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isopropanol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isopropanol"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isopropanol\n894\n938\nIron-free Ammonia Solution\n(Adsorbed, Human)\nIsopropyl Alcohol\n235, 938, 2326\n904\n3645 \nJapanese Encephalitis Live Vaccine (Human)\nIron-free Citric Acid\nIsopropylamine\n920\n917\n3642 \nJapanese Encephalitis Vaccine (Human)\nIron-free Hydrochloric Acid\nIsopropyl Ether\n908 \n236, 921, 2326\n139\n3640 \nJatamansi\nIron, Limit Test for\nIsopropyl Myristate\n741, 830, 3810 \nJeera ka tail\n920\nIron Salicylate Solution\nIsopropyl Palmitate\n236, 2327\n175\n3775 \nJelly Strength\nIron Solution AAS\nIsopropyl Rubbing Alcohol\n2328\n964\n208 \nJuniper Oil\nIron Standard Solution (2 ppm Fe)\nIsopropyl Tetradecanoate\n921\n3811 \nJuniperus Com munis\n964\nIron Standard Solution (8 ppm Fe)\nIsoproterenol Hydrochloride\n2322\n3811 \nJohnin Purified Protein Derivative \t\n4366\n964\nIron Standard Solution (10 ppm Fe)\nIsoprotrenol Hydrochloride Injection \nIsoprotrenol Injection\n2323\n964\nIron Standard Solution (20 ppm Fe)\n2323\n914\nIron(III) Chloride\nIsoprotrenol Sulphate\n2324\nK\n914\nIron(III) Chloride, Anhydrous\nIsoprotrenol Sulphate Tablets \nIsoprotrenol Tablets\n2325\n914\nKali Mirchi ka tail\nIron(III) Chloride, Hexahydrate\n3763 \nKalmegh\n2325\n914\nIron(III) Nitrate\nIsosorbide Dinitrate\n741, 831, 3812 \nKalmegh Dry Extract\n552\n914\nIron(III) Nitrate Solution\nIsosorbide Dinitrate, Diluted\n742, 832, 3813 \nKanamycin Injection\n236, 2328\n914\nIron(III) Sulphate Pentahydrate\nIsosorbide Dinitrate Tablets\n2347 \nKanamycin Acid Sulphate\n2330\n3807\nIsapgol Husk\nIsosorbide Mononitrate, Diluted\n236, 2346 \nKanamycin Sulphate\n236, 2331\n896\nIsoamyl Alcohol\nIsosorbide Mononitrate Tablets \nIsotretinoin\n2333\n236, 2345 \nKaolin, Heavy\n919\nIsobarbituric Acid\n236, 2349 \nKaolin, Light\n236, 2334\n2319\nIsotretinoin Capsules\nlsobutane\n236, 2349, 4250 \nKaolin Mixture\n2336\n920\nIsotretinoin Gel\nIsobutyl Acetate\n2337\n4250 \nKaolin Veterinary Oral Suspension \t\n4250 \nKaolin Veterinary Mixture\n920\nIsoxsuprine Hydrochloride\nIsobutyl Alcohol\n236, 553, 2338\n954\nIsoxsuprine Hydrochloride Injection\nIso-octane\n2339\n4250 \nKarl Fischer Reagent\n266\nIsoxsuprine Hydrochloride Tablets\nIsoelectric Focusing\n2339\n921 \nKasani\n235,551,4244\nIsoxsuprine Injection\nIsoflupredone Acetate\n2339\n3814 \nKasni\n4246\nIsoxsuprine Tablets\nIsoflupredone Acetate Injectable Suspension\n2339\n742, 833, 3814 \nKaunch\n235,2319\nIspaghul a Husk\nIsoflurane\n3807\n743, 834, 3815 \nKavach\nIvermectin\n235,551,920,2321\n236,\n553, 2340, 4246\nIsoniazid\n3815 \nKerosene\n1992\nIvermectin Injection\nIsoniazid and Ethambutol Hydrochloride Tablets\n2342,4248\n921 \nKew Tree\n920\nlvermectin Oral Paste\n4248\nIsoniazid Solution\n3785 \nKetamine  Hydrochloride \t\n236, 554, 2350 \nKetamine Hy_drochloride  Injection\n23. \nIsoniazid T \nIsoilicotiny;14azid\nIvermectin Pour-on\n2322\n4249\nIvy Leaf\n2321\n739, 827,3807\n2351 \nKetamine Injection\nIvy Leaf Dry Extract\n2322\n'740, 828, 3808\noriicotiul.hy4razid Tablets\n2351 \nVolume I: i to xxx and I  to 1068; I-1 to 1-102 Voluttie kx.xxi xxxiv and 1069 to -2658; Volume  3: xxxv to xxxviii and 2659 to 4144; \nVolume 4:  xxxix to xxxxii and 4145 to 4385 ; ►-(03 to \n7:v \t\n \n4\n1-53\n•"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isopropyl-alcohol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isopropyl Alcohol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isopropyl Alcohol\nPropan-2-ol\nH .OH\nh3c/^ch3\nC3H8O \n: \nMol. Wt. 60.1\nIsopropyl Alcohol is propan-2-ol.\n2636\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nIsopropyl Alcohol contains not less than 99.0 per cent v/v. of \nGHA\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (solvent).\nDescription. A clear and colourless liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isopropyl-myristate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isopropyl Myristate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isopropyl Myristate\n0 ch3\nH 3C (H2G)-, 1H 2C^O^C H3\nC17H34O2 \nMol. wt; 270.5\nIsopropyl Myristate is isopropyl tetradecanoate.\nIsopropyl Myristate contains not less than 90.0 per cent of\nC17H34O2.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Aclear, colourless, oily liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isopropyl-palmitate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isopropyl Palmitate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isopropyl Palmitate\nH3C$TVovCH3\nM13 n T\no W\nCi9H38O2 \nMol.Wt.298.5\nIsopropyl Palmitate is Isopropyl hexadecanoate.\nIsopropyl Palmitate contains not less than 90.0 per cent of \nCi9H38O2.\nCategory. Excipient.\nDescription. A clear, colourless, oily liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isopropyl-rubbing-alcohol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isopropyl Rubbing Alcohol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isopropyl Rubbing Alcohol\nIsopropyl Rubbing Alcohol contains not less than 68.0 per\ncent v/v and not more than 72.0 per cent v/v of isopropyl \nakohol, C3H8O with the remainder consisting of water, with or\nwithout suitable stabilizers, perfume oil and colour permitted \nfor the use in drugs.\nCategory. Skin disinfectant."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isoproterenol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isoproterenol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isoproterenol Hydrochloride\n,Hel\nCllH17N03,HCI\nMol. Wt. 247.7\nIsoprenaline Hydrochloride is (RS)-1-(3,4-dihydroxy-\nphenyl)-2-isopropylaminoethano1 hydrochloride.\nIsoprenaline Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per\ncent and not more thaI! 101.5 per cent of Cl1H17N03,HCI,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Sympathomimetic.\nDose. By intravenous infusion, 0.5 to 2 /-Ig per minute, usually\nadministered after dilution with Dextrose Injection.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nalmost odourless. Gradually darkens on exposure to air and\nlight; even in the absence oflight, it is gradually degraded on\nexposure to a humid atmosphere, the decomposition being\nfaster at higher temperatures. Aqueous solutions become pink\nto brownish on standingexposed to airand almost immediately\nafter being made alkaline."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isoproterenol-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isoproterenol Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isoproterenol Sulphate\n(Gj jHnNCh^SO.fclHaO \nMol. Wt. 556.6\nIsoprenaline Sulphate is (7?.S)-l-(3,4-dihydroxyphenyI)- \n2-isopropylaminoethanol sulphate dihydrate.\nIsoprenaline Sulphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of (CuH^NCh^J^SOi, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Sympathomimetic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isotretinoin-gel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isotretinoin Gel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isotretinoin Gel\nIsotretinoin Gel is a solution of Isotretinoin in a suitable \nalcoholic basis.\nIsotretinoin Gel contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 percent ofthe stated amount of isotretinoin,\nC20H28O2.\nUsual strengths. 0.012 per cent w/w; 0.025 per cent w/w; 0.05 \nper cent w/w.\nNOTE — Carry out all the operations as rapidly as possible \nand protectedfrom light."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isotretinoin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isotretinoin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isotretinoin\nIsotretinoin is (2Z,4£r,6/i,8£’)-3,7-dimethyl-9-(2,6,6- \ntrimethylcyclohex-l-enyl)nona~2,4,6,8-tetraenoic acid.\nIsotretinoin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CjiUssfb, calculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. Ayellow or light orange, crystalline powder.\nCategory. Antiacne.\n2646\nIP 2022\nNOTE — It is sensitive.to air, heat and light, especially in \nsolution. Carry out all operations as rapidly as possible and \navoid exposure to actinic light; use freshly prepared \nsolutions."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isoxsuprine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isoxsuprine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isoxsuprine Hydrochloride\nOH H\n.J OH3 ch3\nCisH23NO3,HC1 \n. Mol. Wt. 337.8\nIsoxsuprine Hydrochloride is(l.ffS,2S7?)-l-(4-hydroxy-phenyl)- \n2-[(17?S')-l-methyl-2-phenoxyethylamino]propan-l-ol \nhydrochloride.\nIsoxsuprine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of Cjs^NO^HCI, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Vasodilator; uterine relaxant.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/isoxsuprine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Isoxsuprine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Isoxsuprine Injection\nIsoxsuprine Hydrochloride Injection\nIsoxsuprine Injection is a sterile solution of Isoxsuprine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nIsoxsuprine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nisoxsuprine hydrochloride, Ci8H23NO3,HCl.\nUsual strength. 5 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ispaghula-husk","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ispaghula Husk"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ispaghula Husk\nIsapgol Husk; Plantago ovata\nIspaghula Husk consists of the epidermis and collapsed \nadjacent layers removed from the dried ripe seeds of Plantago \novata Forssk. (Fam. Plantaginaceae).\nCategory. Bulklaxative, antidiarrhoeal.\nDescription. Pale buff, brittle flakes, more or less lanceolate, \nup to 2 mm long and 1 nun wide at the centre, much broken into \nsmaller fragments; many of the flakes having a small, brownish, \noval spot, about 0.8 to 1.0 mm long, in the centre; the material \nswells rapidly in water, forming a stiff mucilage, ;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/itopride-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Itopride Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Itopride Hydrochloride\nGUkNoCL, HC1 \nMol. Wt. 394.9\nItopride Hydrochloride is N-[[4-[2-(dimethyIamino)ethoxy] \nphenyl] methyl]-3 ;4-dimethoxybenzamide; hydrochloride.\n2651\nwww.webofpharma.com\nItopride Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per cent \narid not more than 101.0 per cent C20H26N2O4, HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Gastroprokinetic Agent, Propulsives.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/itraconazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Itraconazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Itraconazole\nn^n\nC35H38C12N8O4 \nMol. Wt. 706.0\nItraconazole is 4-[4-[4-[4-[[cis-2-(2,4-Dichlorophenyl)-2-(.l//-\nl,2,4“triazol-l-ylmethyl)-l,3-dioxolan-4-yl]methoxy] \nphehyl]piperazin-l-yl]phehyl]-2-[(17?1S')-l-methylpropyl]-2,4-\ndlhydro-3/M,2,4-triazol-3-one. . \n'\nItraconazole contains not less than 99.0 per cent arid not more \nthan 101'0 per cent of CfJT^CTNsO.;, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antifungal\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ivermectin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ivermectin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ivermectin Injection\nIvermectin Injection is a sterile solution.of Ivermectin with or \nwith out one or more anaesthetics, preservatives and solvents.\nIvermectin Injection “contains not less than 90’ per cent and\nnot more than 110 per cent of H;B,a, and not more than 5 per\ncent of H,B,b.\nThe content of H;B,,+ H»B,, is not less than 95 per cent and\nnot more than 110 per cent of the stated amount of Ivermectin.\nDescription. A clear, colourless to yellow colour solution:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ivermectin-oral-paste","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ivermectin Oral Paste"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ivermectin Oral Paste\nIvermectin Oral Paste contains Ivermectin in a suitable basis.\nIvermectin Oral Paste contains not less than 95.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the sum of HB la (Caw)\nand H,By, (CyHy2O14):\n4888\nThe ratio of the contents H,B,, / (H,Bi; + H2B,,) is not-less \nthan 90:0 per cent of the stated amount of ivermectin.\nUsual strengths. 120 mg per 6.42 g: 18.7 mg per g (for horse)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ivermectin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ivermectin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ivermectin\nIvermectin is(10£,14£,16£,22Z)-(lR,45,5’5,6R, &R,8R, 125,135, \n20R,21R,245)-6’-[alkyl]-21,24-dihydroxy-5’,H,13,22- \ntetramethyl-2-oxo-(3,7,19-trioxatetracyclo[15.6.1.14,s-020’24] \npentocsa-10,14,16,22-tetraene)-6-spiro-2’-(perhydropyran)-12- \nyl-2,6-dideoxy-4-0-(2,6-dideoxy-3-0-methyl-a-L-arabino- \nhexopyranosy)-3-C>-methyl-a-Z“arabinohexopyr anoside.\nIvermectin contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of H2Bla+ H2Blb,; calculated'on the \nanhydrous and solvent free basis.\nThe ratio H2BIa/(H2Bla+ H2Blb), determined by liquid \nchromatography is not less than 90.0 per cent.\nCategory .Anthelmintic. \n;\nwww.webofpharma.com\n5.\n| I? 2022\nJ\n| pescription. A white or yellowish-white crystalline powder,\nj slightly hygroscopic.\nj identification\n• A. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometery\ni (2.4.6), Compare the spectrum with that obtained with\nI ivermectin IPRS or with the reference spectrum of ivermectin.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram\n; obtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the\nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ivy-leaf","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ivy Leaf"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ivy Leaf"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/japanese-encephalitis-live-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Japanese Encephalitis Live Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Japanese Encephalitis Live Vaccine \n(Human)\nJapanese Encephalitis live vaccine for human is a freeze dried \nìpreparation of Japanese Encephalitis..Virus grown in an \n^approved cell substrates by a validated method. ,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/japanese-encephalitis-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Japanese Encephalitis Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Japanese Encephalitis Vaccine \n(Human)\nJapanese; Encephalitis Vaccine for human use is a liquid or \nfreeze dried preparation of Japanese encephalitis virus \ngrown in approved substrate and inactivated by a validated \nmethod."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/jatamansi","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Jatamansi"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Jatamansi"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/johnin-purified-protein-derivative","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Johnin Purified Protein Derivative"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Johnin Purified Protein Derivative\nJohnin Purified Protein Derivative is a preparation of a fluid\nsynthetic medium in which Mycobacterium paratuberculosis\nhas been grown and which has been freed of the bacilli by\nfiltration. The active fraction of the filtrate, which is\npredominantly protein in nature, is isolated by precipitation,\nwashed and re-dissolved. It is then distributed in sterile glass\ncontainers and sealed so as to exclude micro-organisms. It\nmay contain a suitable preservative. It reveals delayed\nhypersensitivity in animals sensitised by M paratuberculosis.\nDescription. A yellowish-brown liquid.\n2751\nJOHNIN PURlFIED PROTEIN DERIVATIVE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/kalmegh-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kalmegh Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Kalmegh Dry Extract\nKalmegh Dry Extract is obtained by extracting Kalmegh with \naqueous ethanol or ethanol or any other suitable solvent.\nKalmegh Dry Extract contains not less than 90.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 12Q.0 per cent w/w of the labelled amount \nof andrographoIides (sum of andrographolide, neo- \nandrographolide and andrograpanin) The content of 14-deoxy-\nn,12-didehydroandrogapholide content shall not be more than \none sixth of the amount of andrographolide. • \n. -\nCategory. Hepatoproteçtive, bittertonic, febrifuge, blood \npurifier, dipana.\nDescription. A light green to dark green powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/kalmegh","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kalmegh","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Kalmegh\nAndrographis paniculata\nKalmegh consists of the·dried aerial parts, mainly stems and\nleaves, of Andrographis paniculata Nees.\n(Fam,\nAcanthaceae).\nKalmegh contains not less than 1.0 per cent w/w of\nandrographo1ide, calculated on the dried basis.\nDescription.Taste, intensely bitter.\nKALMEGH"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/kanamycin-acid-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kanamycin Acid Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Kanamycin Acid Sulphate\nKanamycin Acid Sulphate is a form of kanamycin sulphate \nprepared by adding Sulphuric Acid to a solution of Kanamycin\nSulphate and subsequent drying. \n.\nKanamycin Acid Sulphate has a potency of not less than \n650 Units .per mg, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription; A white or almost white powder; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/kanamycin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kanamycin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Kanamycin Injection\nKanamycin Injection is either a sterile solution of Kanamycin \nSulphate in Water for Injections containing Sulphuric Acid \nand suitable buffering and stabilising agents or, is a sterile\nmaterial consisting of Kanamycin Acid Sulphate with buffering \nagents and other excipients. It is filled in a sealed container.\nA. Kanamycin Injection (Solution)\nKanamycin Injection contains Kanamycin Sulphate equivalent \nto not less than 97.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent \nof the stated number of Units of kanamycin.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of250 mg (250,000 Units) of \nkanamycin per ml or per sealed container.\nDescription. A colourless to pale yellow solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/kanamycin-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kanamycin Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Kanamycin Sulphate\nC|8H36N4O113H2SO4)H2O \n' Mol. Wt.’600.6\nKanamycin Sulphate is 6-O-(3-amino-3-deoxy- \na-D-glucopyranosyl)-4-<9-(6-amino-6-deoxy- \na-D-glucopyranosyl)-2-deoxy-D-streptamine sulphate \nmonohydrate, ah antimicrobial substance produced by the \ngrowth of certain’ strains of Streptomyces kanamyceticus.\nKanamycin Sulphate has a potency of .not Jess than 750.Units \nper mg, calculated on the driedbasis. \n,\nCategory, Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/karipatta","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Karipatta"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Karipatta"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/kasni","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kasni"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Kasni\nKasani, Hinduba, Kasni, Chicory, Cichorium intybus\nKasni consists of fruits and seeds of Cichorium intybus Linn \n(Pam.Asteraccac). ■ \n- \n.\nKasni contains not less than 0.7 per cent w/w of chlbrdgenic\nacid calculated on the dried basis. \n:\nKASNI\nCategory. Hepatoprotective, antidiabetic and hypolipidimic.\nDescription. The dried fruit and seed is yellowish brown in \ncolour, 1 to 3 mm in length, 1 to 2 mm in breadth, 1 mm in \nthickness triangle shaped and has-characteristic Odour.\nA. Macroscopic — Fruits. The fruits arc achenes which are \nsmooth, 5-angled, pale brown to black, crowned with a ring of \npappus scales.\nSeed. Brown, minutely puberulous:\nB. Microscopic—The transverse section of mature fruit shows \nouter single layered tangentially elongated epidermis, with \nthick cuticle. Numerous rod shaped unequal epicuticular wax \ndeposits of different size are present oh cuticle which are \nperpendicular to the surface of epidermis. The epidermis is \nfollowed by 6 to 10 layers of thick sclerenchymatous cells. \nMost of sclerenchymatous cells contain prism shaped calcium \noxalate crystals.\nC. Determine by thin-layer chromatography(2.4.17), coating \nthe plate with silica gel GF254.\nMobile phase. A mixture of 70 volumes of toluene, 3 0 volumes \nof ethyl acetate and 1 volume of glacial acetic acid.\nTest solution. Reflux 1 g of coarsely powdered substance under \nexamination with 25 ml of methanol on a water bath. Cool and \nfilter. Reflux the residue with 3 x 25 ml of methanol. Combine \nthe extracts and concentrate under vacuum to 25 ml.\nReference solution. Reflux 1g of kasni fPBRS with 25 .ml of \nmethanol on a water bath. Cool and filter. Reflux the residue \nwith 3 x 25 ml of methanol. Combine the extracts and \nconcentrate under vacuum to 2 5 ml. \n.\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution as band length 8 mm. \nAllow the mobile phase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air and \nexamine in ultraviolet light at 254 and 366 nm. Spray with a \nanisaldehyde sulphuric acid reagent. Heat at 110° .for\n10 minutes and examine in the visible light. \nThe chromatographic profile of the test solution is similar to \nthat of the reference solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/kaunch","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kaunch"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Kaunch"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ketamine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ketamine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ketamine Hydrochloride\nCk \n0 rd\n, HCI\nQ J^nhch3\nC13H16CINO,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 274.2\nKetamine Hydrochloride is (/?5)-2-(2-chlorophenyl)- \n2-methylaminocyclohexanone hydrochloride.\nKetamine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C j3H16C1NO,HC1.\nCategory, General anaesthetic.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ketamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ketamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ketamine Injection\nKetamine Hydrochloride Inj ection\nKetamine Injection is a sterile solution of Ketamine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nKetamine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent.and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of ketamine, \nC13H36ONO.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 10 mg per ml; 50 mg per \nml; 100 mg per ml (11.54 mg of ketamine hydrochloride is \napproximately equivalent to 10 mg of ketamine)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ketoprofen-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ketoprofen Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ketoprofen Capsules\nCategory. Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory.\n-\na stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm), \n-\nmobile phase: a mixture oft volumes of freshly prepared \nphosphate buffer pH 3.5, 43 volumes of acetonitrile\nDose. Initially, by intramuscular injection, 30 to 60 mg; then \n10 to 30 mg every 4 to 6 hours; maximum 150 mg for first day \nand 120 mg daily; then after orally, 20 mg initially; followed \nby 10 mg 4 to 6 hours; maximum dose of 40 mg daily.\nKetoprofen Capsules contain not less than 92.5 per cent and \nnot more than 107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \nketoprofen, C I6H 1403 •\nand 55 volumes of water, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nb. \nspectrophotometer set at 233 nm, \n-\ninjection volume: 20 p1\nUsual strengths. 50 mg; 100 mg.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ketoprofen","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ketoprofen"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ketoprofen\nh\nCOOH\nCj6H14O3- \n...... Mol. Wt. 254.3\nKetoprofen is (7fS)-2-(3-benzoylphenyl)propionic acid.\nKetoprofen contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 100.5 per cent of Ci6Hi4O3, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anti-inflammatory; analgesic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ketorolac-trometamol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ketorolac Trometamol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ketorolac Trometamol\nCiSH13NO3,C4H11NO3 \nMol. Wt. 376.4\nKetorolac Tromethamine is salt of (2?5)-5-benzoyl-2,3-dihydro-\n1 77-pyrrolizine-l -carboxylate with 2-amino-2-(hydroxymethyl) \npropane-1,3-diol,\nKetorolac Tromethamine contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.5 per cent of Ci5H13NO3,C4H11N035 \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Non-steroidal Anti-inflammatory.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ketorolac-tromethamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ketorolac Tromethamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ketorolac Tromethamine Injection\nKetorolac Trometainol Injection\nKetorolac Tromethamine Injection contains not less than 90.0 \nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount \nof ketorolac tromethamine C;5 H13NO3,C4H1 ]NO3.\nCorrection. ..r\nfactor\nUsual strengths. 15 mg per ml; 30 mg per ml. ,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ketotifen-fumarate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ketotifen Fumarate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ketotifen Fumarate\no\nKETOROLAC TROMETHAMINE TABLETS\nIF 2010\nKetotifen Fumarate is 4,9-dihydro-4-(1-methylpiperidin-4-\nylidene)-1OH-benzo[4,5]cyclohepta[1,2-b]thiophen-lO-one\nhydrogen fumarate.\nKetotifen Rurnarate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 101.0percentofC19H19NOS,C~04'calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistarninic.\nDescription. A white to brownish-yellow crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ketotifen-hydrogen-fumarate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ketotifen Hydrogen Fumarate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ketotifen Hydrogen Fumarate\nC19H19NOS,C4H4O4 \nMol. Wt. 425.5\nKetotifen Fumarate is 4,9-dihydro-4-(l-methylpiperidin-4- \nylidene)-10Zf-benzo[4,5]cyclohepta[ 1,2-Z»]thiophen-10-one \nhydrogen fumarate. ' '\nKetotifen Fumarate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of Cj9H 1;1NOS,C4H4()4, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic.\nDescription. Awhite to brownish-yellow crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/kunduru-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kunduru Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Kunduru Dry Extract"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/kutki","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Kutki"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Kutki\nPicrorhiza kurroa\nKutki consists of dried roots of Picrorhiza kurroa Royle ex\nBenth. (Fam. Scrophulariaceae).\nKutki contains not less than 5 per cent w/w oflcutkin, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Hepatoprotective, bitter tonic, antiulcer, kamala.\nDescription. Rhizomes are sub cylindrical, straight or slightly \ncurved, externally greyish with wrinkled surfaces, circular scars \nof roots and bud scales, with cork exposed at places."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/labetalol-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Labetalol Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Labetalol Injection\nLabetalol Hydrochloride injection\nLabetalol Injection is a sterile solution of Labetalol \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nLabetalol Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of labetalol \nhydrochloride, Cl9H24N2O3,HCI.\nUsual strength. 5 mg per ml.\nDescription. A colorless or pale yellow solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/labetalol-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Labetalol Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Labetalol Tablets\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nLabetalol HydrochlorideTablets\nLaciktipine is' diethyl (E)-4-2-[(tert-butoxycarbonyl) \nvjnylipheny174,4-dihydro-2,6- dimethylpyridine-3,5-\nLabetalCitTab-kts contain not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore Than 105:0 per cent of the stated amount of labetalo\nl\nhydrochloride, C4124N203,HC1.\nLacidipine contains not less than 97.5 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C 26H33N06, calculated on the anhydrous \nand propan-2-ol free basis.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow crystalline powder.\nNOTE - Carry out all of the following procedures protected \nfrom light and prepare solutions immediately before use."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lacidipine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lacidipine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lacidipine\nC261133NO6 \n:: \n. Mol.Wt.455.6\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nLacidipine is diethyl (L)-4-2-[(/('/7-butoxy carbonyl) \nVinyl]phenyl-l,4-dihydro-2,6- dimethylpyridine-3,5- \ndicarboxylate.\nLacidipine contains not less than 97.5 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C26H33NO6, calculated on the anhydrous \nand propan-2-ol free basis.\nDescription. A white to pale yellow crystalline powder.\nNOTE — Carry out all of the following procedures protected \nfrom light and prepare solutions, immediately before use."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lactic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lactic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lactic Acid\nH OH\nh3c/xcooh\nC3H6O3 \nMol. Wt. 90.1\nLactic Acid consists of a mixture of 2-hydroxypropionic acid \nits condensation products, such as lactoyl-lactic acid and \nother polylactic acids and water. The equilibrium between lactic \nacid and polylactic acids depends on concentration and \ntemperature.\nIt is usually in the form of the racemate [(RS)-lactic acid], but \nin some cases the (/L-isomer may predominate.\nLactic Acid contains the equivalent of not less than 88.0 per \ncent and not more than 92.0 per cent w/w of C3HSO3.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A colourless or slightly yellow, viscous liquid; \nhygroscopic. :"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lactose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lactose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lactose\nLactose Monohydrate; Milk Sugar\nCuHaOn^O \nMol. Wt 360.3\nLactose is C*-0-D-galactopyranosyl-(l • > 4)-a-D- \nglucopyranose monohydrate.\nCategory, Pharmaceutical aid (excipient).\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lactulose-oral-powder","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lactulose Oral Powder"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lactulose Oral Powder\nLactulose Oral Powder consists of lactulose with or without \nlesser amounts of other sugars including lactose and galactose.\nLactulose Oral Powder contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nlactulose, C12H220h."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lactulose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lactulose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lactulose\nC12H22On \nMol. Wt. 342.3\nLactulose is 4-U-P-D-galactopyranosyl-D-fructose.\nLactulose contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of lactulose, C^H^On, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nwww.webofpharma.com\n[g;2022 \nLACTULOSE\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lamivudine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lamivudine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lamivudine Oral Solution\nLamivudine Oral Solution is a solution of Lamivudine in a \nsuitable flavoured vehicle.\nLamivudine Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nlamivudine, CsHi]N3O3S.\n2693\nwww.webofpharma.com\nUsual strengths. 25 mg in 5 ml; 50 mg in 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lamivudine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lamivudine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lamivudine\nCsH||N3()3S ....... \n■ ■ \nM.ol.Wt.229.3\nLamivudine is (2R,5S)-4-amino-l-[2-(hydroxymethyl)-l,3- \noxathiolan-5yl]-2(l #)-pyrimidinone.\nLamivudine contains not less than 98:0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 percent of C8Hj tN3O3S, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis. ' '\nCategory. Antiretroviral. ’\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lamotrigine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lamotrigine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lamotrigine\nC9II7C12N5 \n, \nMol,. Wt. 256.1\nLamotrigine is 6-(2,3-dichlorophenyl)-l ,2,44riazine-3,5- \ndiaminc. '\nLamotrigine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.5 per cent of C9H7C12N5, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant\nDescription. A white to off - white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lansoprazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lansoprazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lansoprazole\nC16H14F3N3O2S \nMol. Wt. 369.4\nLansoprazole is (J?S)-2-[[[3-methyl-4-(2^^-trifluoroetho?g/)- \n2-pyridinyl]methyI]sulphinyl]-1//-benzimidazole.\nLansoprazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 percent of C^H^^C^S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\n2707\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCategory. Antiulcer.\nDescription. A white to brownish-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lapatinib-ditosylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lapatinib Ditosylate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lapatinib Ditosylate\nC^mOFNAS^CtHAS . \nMol. Wt 925.5\n(anhydrous)\nC^H^IFNAS^CzHgOjSAO \nMol. Wt. 943.5\n(monohydrate)\nLapatinib Ditosylate is 7V-[3-chloro-4-[(3-fluorophenyl) \nmethoxy]pheny11-6- [5-[(2-methylsulfonyl ethylamino)methyl]- \n2-furanyl]-4-quinazolinamine ditosylate.\nLapatinib Ditosylate contains not less than 97.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C29H26C1FN 4O4S, 2C7HsO3S, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lasuna","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lasuna"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lasuna\nGarlic; Allium sativum\nLasuna consists of the .fresh or dried compound bulbs;of \nAllium sativum Linn. (Fam. Liliaceae).\nLasuna contains not less than 0.2 per cent w/w of alliin, \ncalculated oh the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihyperlipidaemic, antibacterial, digestant, jima.\nDescription. Bulbs made up jof. cloves and is. .wrapped in.a \nwhite papery sheath with pungent taste and odour"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/latanoprost-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Latanoprost Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Latanoprost Eye Drops\nLatanoprost Eye Drops is a sterile solution of Latanoprost in \nPurified Water.\nLatanoprost Eye Drops contain not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the, stated amount of \nLatanoprost,C26H40O5.\nUsual strength. 0.005 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/latanoprost","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Latanoprost"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Latanoprost\nMol. Wt. 432.6\nLatanoprost is isopropyl (Z>7-[(1 /< 2R, 3R, 5iS>3,5-ctihydroxy- \n2-[(3?)-3-hydroxy-5 -phenylpentyl] cyclopentyl]~5-heptenoate.\nLatanoprost contains not less than 94.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C^H^Os,.calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis. \n. ..\nCategory. Antiglucoma.\nDescription. A colourless to slightly yellow oil."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lavang","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lavang"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lavang\n(Under UV light at 254 nm)\n(Under UV light at 366 nm \nafter spraying with vanillin\n(Under UV light al 366 nm)\nLodhra\n3.2.2. THIN LAYER CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND HERBAL PRODUCTS\nIP 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lavender-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lavender Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lavender Oil\nLavender Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nof recently picked lavender flowers, Lavendula angustifolia\nP. Miller (Fam. Lamiaceae). \nA ' . \n■\nLavender Oil (Indian variety) contains not less than 35 per \ncent and not more than 60 per cent of linalyl acetate, and not \nless than 25 per cent and not more than 35 per cent of linalool.\nDescription. A clear, pale yellow liquid, free from sediment, \nsuspended matter; characteristic, rustic and recalling odour.\nIdentification ; / a ; b . \nv\nA. Determine by gas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTest solution. A 2.0 per cerit w/v solution of the oil under \nexamination in ethanol (95per cent); : A) A .\nReference solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution oi lavender\noil IPRS in ethanol (95 pen cent): : ,• \n1 \n<-. • ■ / /\n4254\nUse chromatographic system described ? ih the Assay i A\nThe peaks in the chromatogram obtained With thh tèht soliitiôè \ncorresponds to the peaks in the chromatogram obtained with \nthe reference solution.. • ■ \nL c; \n,\nB. Flash point (2.4.44). Not less than 100e. /ft‘‘ft“!"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lecithin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lecithin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lecithin\nR = Fatty acids\nLecithin is a complex mixture of acetone-insoluble \nphosphatides, which consist chiefly of phosphatidyl choline, \nphosphatidyl ethanolamine, phosphatidyl serine and \nphosphatidylinositol, combined with various amounts of other \nsubstances such as triglycerides, fatty acids and \ncarbohydrates, as separated from the crude vegetable oil \nsource.\nLecithin contains not less than 50.0 per cent of stated amount \nof acetone-insoluble matter.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (excipient)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lemon-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lemon Oil"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lemon Oil\nNimbu ka tail\nLemon Oil is the essential oil obtained by steam distillation \nfrom the epicarps of fresh fruits of Citrus limon (L.) Burm., \n(Fam. Rutaceae).\nLemon Oil contains not less than 10 per cent and not more \nthan 16.5 per cent of (3-pinene, not less than-6qier -cent and \nnot more than 69 per cent of limonene and not 108.thatil-per \ncent and not more than 12 per cent of y-terptoene."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lenvatinib-mesylate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lenvatinib Mesylate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lenvatinib Mesylate\nC21H9CIN4O4CII4O3S \n. Mol. Wt. 523,0\nLenvatinib Mesylate is4-[3-chloro-4-(N’-cyclopropylureido) \nphenoxy]-7-methoxyquinoline-6-carboxamide methane \nsulphonate.\nLenvatinib Mesylate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of C2iH19ClN4O4.CHilO3S, \ncalculated on anhydrous basis.\nCAUTION — Lenvatinib Mesylate is cytotoxic, extra care \nrequired to prevent inhaling particles and exposing the skin \nto it.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white to pale reddish yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/letrozole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Letrozole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Letrozole\nCI7HnN5 \n. : Mol Wt. 285.3\nLetrozole is benzonitrile, 4,4’-( 1H-1,2,4-triazol-1 -ylmethylene) \nbis; 4,4’-( 1H-1,2,4-Triazol-l-ylmethylene) dibenzonitrile.\nLetrozole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan. 102.0 per cent of Ci7HuNs, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis. ’ .\nCategory. Aromatase inhibitor; treatment of breast cancer.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levamisole-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levamisole Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levamisole Hydrochloride\nChH12N2S,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 240.8\nLevamisole Hydrochloride is (5)-2,3,5,6-tetrahydro- \n6-phenyiimidazo[2 J-//] thiazole hydrochloride.\nLevamisole Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of CnHl2N2S,HCl, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Anthelmintic.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levamisole-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levamisole Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levamisole Injection\nLevamisole Hydrochloride Injection\nLeavamisole Injection is a sterile solution of Levamisole \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections: -\nLevamisole Injection contains not-less than 95:0 per cent.and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated.amount of levamisole \nhydrochloride, Cy HpN,S,HCl, ,\nUsual strength. 75mg in \nI mal."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levetiracetam-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levetiracetam Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levetiracetam Oral Solution\nLevetiracetam Oral Solution is a solution of levetiracetam in a \nsuitable aqueous vehicle.\nLevetiracetam Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 percent of the stated amount of\nlevetiracetam C8H14N2O2.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\nLEVETIRACETAM ORAL SOLUTION\nUsual strength, 500 mg per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levetiracetam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levetiracetam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levetiracetam\nCgHi4N2O2 /\\ . ■ \n' Mol. Wl.170.2\nLevetiracetam is 1-Pyrrolidineacetamide, a-ethyl-2roxo-, (aS).\nLevetiracetamrcontains not .less than 98.0'per cent-and not \nmore than; 102.0 per cent of CgHMN2O2 calculated on the\nanhydrous and solvent-free basis. .. \n■\nCategory. Antiepileptic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levocarnitine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levocarnitine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levocarnitine\nH?A /CH3DH O\nh3cx+'^x^/AxO'\nC7H15NO3 \nMol. Wt. 161.20\nLevocamitine is (7?)-3-Carboxy-2-hydroxy-7V7V^V-trimethyfl- \npropanaminium, inner salt; (7?)-(3-Carboxy-2-hydroxypropyl) \ntrimethylammonium, inner salt.\nLevocarnitine contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103,0 per cent of C7Hi5NO3, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Carnitine deficiency.\nDescription. A white crystals or crystalline powder; \nhygroscopic.\nDry the sample and reference standard under vacuum at 50° \nfor 5 hours and determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained'with levocarnitine IPRS or with the reference \nspectrum of levocamitine.\nSpecific optical rotation (2.4.22). “-32.0° to-29.0°, determined \nin a 10.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon dioxide-free water.\npH (2.4.24). 5.5 to 9.5, determined in a 5.0 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon dioxide-free water.\nChloride. Not more than 4000 ppm;\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.09 g of levocamitine in 30 to 40 ml of \nwater, neutralise the solution with nitric acid, to litmus. Add\n1 ml of nitric acid and 1 ml of .silver nitrate solution, make up \nthe volume to 50.0 ml with water.\nReference solution. Mix 0.5 ml of 0.02 Mhydrochloric acid in\n30 to 40 ml of water. Add 1 ml ofnitric acid and 1 ml of silver \nnitrate solution, make up the volume to 50.0 ml with water.\nAllow to stand for 5 minutes protected from direct sunlight. \nCompare the turbidity of test solution, if any, with that \nproduced with reference solution.\n4089\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levocetirizine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levocetirizine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levocetirizine Hydrochloride\nLevocetirizine Dihyrochloride ...\nC21H25N2O3C1,2HC1\nMol. Wt. 461.8\nLevocetirizine Hydrochloride is (/?)-2-[2-{4-[(4-chloro-pheiiyl) \nphenylmethyl]piperazin-l-yl]ethoxy]acetic acid di hydro­\nchloride.\nLevocetirizine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per \ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of C2iH25N2O3Cl,2HCl \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihistaminic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levofloxacin-hemihydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levofloxacin Hemihydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levofloxacin Hemihydrate\no^'CH3\n'1/2H2° \nf^^^cooh\no\nC18H20FN3O4, ’/2 H2O \nMol. Wt. 370.4\nLevofloxacin Hemihydrate is (5')“9“fluoro-2,3-dihydro- \n3-methyl-10-(4-methylpiperazin-1 -yl)-7-oxo-7/7-pyrido[ 1, \n2,3-£?e]-l,4-benzoxazine-6-carboxylic acid hemihydrate.\nLevofloxacin Hemihydrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of Ci8H2oFN304, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A yellowish white to yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levofloxacin-infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levofloxacin Infusion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levofloxacin Infusion\nLevofloxacin Infusion is a sterile solution of levofloxacin in 5 \nper cent dextrose injection or in 0.9 per cent sodium chloride\ninjection.\nLevofloxacin Infusion contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nlevofloxacin, Ci8H2oFN304.\nUsual strength. 5 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levofloxacin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levofloxacin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levofloxacin Injection\nLevofloxacin Injection is a sterile solution of levofloxacin in \nWater for Inj ections.\nLevofloxacin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nlevofloxacin, ClgH2oFN304.\nUsual strengths. 25 mgperml; 50 mgperml.\nDescription. A clear, yellowish colour solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levofloxacin-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levofloxacin Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levofloxacin Oral Solution\nLevofloxacin Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of levofloxacin, \nC18H20FN3O4.\nUsual strength. 125mgper5ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levonorgestrel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levonorgestrel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levonorgestrel\nC2lH2SO2 \nMol. Wt. 312.5\nLevonorgestrel is .13 P -ethyl-17 [3 -hydroxy-18,19-dinor- 17 a- \npregn-4-enT-20-yn-3-one.\nLevonorgestrel contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C2]H28O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis. - \n- ■\nCategory. Progestogen. :\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levosalbutamol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levosalbutamol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levosalbutamol Hydrochloride\nR-Albuterol Hydrochloride\nC13H21NO3Ha \nMol.Wt. 275.8\nLevosalbutamol Hydrochloride is (R) od-Rtert-\nButylamino)methyl]-4-hydroxy-m-xyIene- a, a1-diol \nhydrochloride,\nLevosalbutamol Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 \nper cent and not more than 102.0 per cent of CjjHzjNOj, HC1, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Bronchodialator.\nDescription. A white to off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/levosalbutamol-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Levosalbutamol Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Levosalbutamol Sulphate\n(7?)-Albuterol Sulphate\n(C13H21NO3)2.H2SO4\nMol. Wt. 576.7\nLevosalbutamol Sulphate is (7?)-2-ter?-butylamino-l-(4- \nhydroxy-3-hydroxymethylphenyl)ethanol sulphate.\nLevosalbutamol Sulphate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more-than 102.0 per cent of (CnHkiNC^h. H2SO4, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. p2-adrenoreceptor agonist.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lidocaine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lidocaine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lidocaine Hydrochloride\nLignocaine Hydrochloride is 2-diethylaminoaceto-\n2',6'-xylidide hydrochloride monohydrate.\nLignocaine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent\nand not more than 101.0per cent ofCI4HzzNzO,HCI, calculated\non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Local anaesthetic; antiarrhythmic.\nDose. As local anaesthetic, upto 200 mg as a single dose,\nwhen given with adrenaline; as antiarrhythmic, by intravenous\ninfusion, 50 to 100 mg at the rate of 1 to 2 mg per minute.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; odourless or\npractically 6dolirless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lidocaine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lidocaine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lidocaine\nCH3 h.\n/k\nh j n N CH3\nch3\nC14H22N2O \nMol. Wt. 234.3\nLignocaine is acetamide, 2-(diethylamino)-,V-(2,6- \ndimethylphenyl)-; 2-(Diethylamino)-2’,6’-acetoxylidide.\nLignocaine contains not less than 97.5per cent and not more \nthan 102.5 per cent of C14H22N2O,\nCategory. Local anaesthetic.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/light-kaolin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Light Kaolin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Light Kaolin\nLight Kaolin is a native hydrated aluminium silicate, freed \nfrom most of its impurities by elutriation and dried. It may \ncontain a suitable dispersing agent.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid\nDescription: Light, white powder free from gritty particles; \nunctuous to the touch."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/light-liquid-paraffin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Light Liquid Paraffin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Light Liquid Paraffin\nLight Mineral Oil; Light Liquid Petrolatum\nLight Liquid Paraffin is a purified mixture of liquid saturated \nhydrocarbons obtained from petroleum. It may contain a \nsuitable stabiliser.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (vehicle).\nDescription. A transparent, colourless, oily liquid, free from \nfluorescence by daylight."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/light-magnesia","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Light Magnesia"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Light Magnesia\nMgO \nMol. Wt. 40.3\nLight Magnesium Oxide contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of MgO, calculated on the \nignited basis.\nCategory. Antacid; osmotic laxative. .\nDescription. Avery fine, light, white powder. 15g occupies a \nvolume of about 150 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/light-magnesium-carbonate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Light Magnesium Carbonate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Light Magnesium Carbonate\nLight Magnesium Carbonate is a hydrated basic magnesium \ncarbonate. • \n■ -\nthe aqueous layer to dryness, dissolve the residue in 1ml of \n5 M acetic acid arid dilute to 20 ml with Water. 12 rriloMe \nresulting solution complies with the limit test for heavy rnetgfc, \nMethod D (20 ppm). Use lead standard solution (Tppmty \nfor standard preparation.\nLight Magnesium Carbonate contains the equivalent of not\nCopper. Dissolve 1 g in 5 ml of hydrochloric acid and 25 ml of \nwater, boil to remove carbon dioxide,- make alkaline with dilute \nammonia solution; no blue colour is produced. :\nless than 40.0 per cent and not more than 45.0 per cent of\nMgO. /\nCategory. Antacid; osmotic laxative.\nIron (2.3.14). Dissolve 0.1 g in 3 ml of 2 M hydrochloricfdd \nand dilute to 10 ml with water. The resulting solution jcomp^; \nwith the limit test for iron (400 ppm).... \n’\nSoluble substances. Mix 2.0 g with100 ml of water, boil for \n5 minutes, filter whilst hot through a sintered-glass filter \n(porosity No. 3), allow to cool anddilutc to I00;0 ml with \nwater. Evaporate 50.0 ml of the fíltrate to dryness and dry the \nresidue at 105°; the residue weighs not more than 10 mg \n(l.Opercent).\nDescription A very light, white powder. 15 g occupies a \nvolume of about 125 ml.\nChlorides (2.3.12). 5.0 ml of solution A complies withthe Hniit \ntest for chlorides (0.1 per cent). \ncd"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lignocaine-and-adrenaline-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lignocaine and Adrenaline Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lignocaine and Adrenaline Injection\nLidocaine Hydrochloride and Adrenaline Bitartrate \nInjection; Lidocaine and Adrenaline Injection; Adrenaline \nBitartrate and Lidocaine Hydrochloride Injection\nLignocaine and Adrenaline Injection is a sterile solution of \nLignocaine Hydrochloride and Adrenaline Bit artrate in Water \nfor Injections.\nLignocaine and Adrenaline Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of lignocaine hydrochloride, Ci4H22N2O,HCl,H2O and \nnot less than 87.5 per cent and not more than 112.5 per cent of \nthe stated amount of adrenaline, C9Hi3NO3.\nUsual strength. Lignocaine Hydrochloride, 20 mg per ml and \nadrenaline, 0.01 mgperml.\nDescription. A clear colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lignocaine-and-dextrose-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lignocaine and Dextrose Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lignocaine and Dextrose Injection\nLignocaine Hydrochloride and Dextrose Injection; \nLidocaine Hydrochloride and Dextrose Injection; \nLidocaine and Dextrose Injection\nLignocaine and Dextrose Injection is a sterile solution of \nLignocaine Hydrochloride and Dextrose in Water for Injections.\nLignocaine and Dextrose Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namounts of lignocaine hydrochloride, C l4H22N2O,HCl,H2O and \ndextrose, C6HI2O6,H2O.\nUsual strength. Lignocaine Hydrochloride 50 mg per ml and \nDextrose 75 mg per ml.\nDescription. A clear colourless or slightly yellow solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lignocaine-and-prilocaine-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lignocaine and Prilocaine Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lignocaine and Prilocaine Cream\nLidocaine and Prilocaine Cream\nLignocaine and Prilocaine Cream contains not less than 90.0 \nper cent and not more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amounts \nof lignocaine, CuH^HjO and Prilocaine, Ci3H20N2O.\nUsual strength. Lidocaine 2.5 percent, Prilocaine 2.5 per cent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lignocaine-gel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lignocaine Gel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lignocaine Gel\nLignocaine Hydrochloride Gel; Lidocaine Hydrochloride \nGel\nLignocaine Gel is a sterile solution of Lignocaine Hydrochloride \nin a suitable water-miscible base. It may contain suitable \nantioxidants, stabilisers and antimicrobial preservatives.\nLignocaine Gel contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of anhydrous \nlignocaine hydrochloride, C14H22N2O,HC1.\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 1 per cent and 2 per cent \nw/v of anhydrous lignocaine hydrochloride."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lignocaine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lignocaine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lignocaine Hydrochloride\nLidocaine Hydrochloride\nCl4H22N2O,HCl,H2O\n2758\nLIGNOCAINE ORAL TOPICAL SOLUTION\nIP 2022\nLignocaine Hydrochloride is 2-diethylaminoaceto- \n2',6'-xylidide hydrochloride monohydrate.\nLignocaine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci4H22N2O,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Local anaesthetic; antiarrhythmic.\nDescription. Awhite, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lignocaine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lignocaine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lignocaine Injection\nLignocaine Hydrochloride Injection; Lidocaine\nHydrocWoride Injection; Lidocaine Injection\nLignocaine Injection is a sterile solution of Lignocaine\nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nLIGNOCAINE INJECTION\nLignocaine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount oflignocaine\nhydrochloride, CI4H22N20,HCI,H20.\nUsual strengths. 0.2 per cent w/v; 1.0 per cent w/v; 5.0 per\ncent w/v.\nDescription. A clear colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lignocaine-oral-topical-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lignocaine Oral Topical Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lignocaine Oral Topical Solution\nLidocaine Oral Topical solution\nLignocaine Oral Topical Solution contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of lignocaine, C14H22N2O. It contains a suitable flavour.\nUsual strengths. 2 per cent w/v; 4 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lime-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lime Oil"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lime Oil\nDescription. A clear, pale yellow to dark green liquidAr e from \nsediment, suspended matter and characteristic fresh lemon \npericarp odour.\nInject reference solutions (a), (b) and the test solution.\nCalculate the D-limonene, and A-terpinene contents in the oil \nusing area normalisation procedure. \nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nNimbu ka tail\nLime Oil is obtained by centrifuging the emulsion of juice and \noil obtained by crushing the whole fruits of Citrus aurantiifolia \n(Christman) Swingle, (Fam.Rutaceae)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/limit","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Limit"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Limit \n(ppm)\n(Maximum daily\ndose)\nCandesartan (32 mg)\n3.00\n0.83\nIrbesartan (300 mg)\n0.32\n0.088\nLosartan(lOOmg)\n96\n0.96\n26.5\n. 0.27\nOlmesartan (40 mg)\n2.40\n0.66\nValsartan (320 mg)\n0.30\n0.083\nThese limits are applicable only if a drug substance and drug \nproduct contains a single nitrosamine. If more than one of the \nnitrosamine impurities identified in Table 1 is detected and the \ntotal quantity of nitrosamine impurities exceeds 26.5 ng per \nday (the AI for the most potent nitrosamines) based on the \nmaximum daily dose (MDD), the manufacturer should contact\n5-1-1. NITROSAMINE IMPURITIES.\nIP 2022\nand consult with the relevant health authority or the \nregulatory authorities for evaluation to determine the specific \npath forward.\nFor drug products with an MDD of less than 880 mg per day, \na recommended limit for total nitrosamines of 0.03 ppm is not \nmore than 26.5 ng per day and is considered acceptable. For\ndrug products with an MDD above 880 mg per day, the limit \nfor total nitrosamines should be adjusted so as not to exceed \nthe recommended limit of 26.5 ng per day.\nIf nitrosamines AI limits are not found for a drug product, \nmanufacturers should use the approach outlined in ICH M7 \n(RI) to determine the risk associated with the nitrosamine and \ncontact the regulatory authorities about the acceptability of \nany proposed limit.\nTesting for the Presence of Nitrosamines\nUpon completion of the risk assessment, exploratory testing \nmay need to be performed to confirm the conclusions of the \nrisk assessment and proposed control strategy. If a risk of \nnitrosamines in a drug product is identified, confirmatory \ntesting of batches should be conducted using sensitive and \nappropriately validated methods. If a nitrosamine impurity is \ndetected, manufacturers should investigate the root cause \nand implement changes in the manufacturing process to \nmitigate or reduce nitrosamine impurities.\nTest Method Performance Characteristics\nThe AIs associated with nitrosamines require the application \nof sensitive analytical procedures. In many cases, the most \nreliable procedures take advantage of the sensitivity and\nselectivity of chromatographic separation techniques coupled \nwith quantitation by mass spectrometry (e.g., HPLC-MS/MS, \nGC-MS/MS). Appropriate sample preparation is a critical step \nin trace impurity analyses such as those required to evaluate \nthe levels of nitrosamines in drug substances or drug products\nand this is also particularly critical to prevent the loss or \ngeneration of nitrosamines.\nLimit \n(ppm)\nThe recommended method performance characteristics that \nneed to be evaluated for quantitative analysis.of nitrosamines\ninclude range, accuracy, repeatability, intermediate precision, \nand limit of quantitation. If a limit test is intended for use, the \nrecommended method performance characteristics to be \nassessed include specificity, recovery, detectability, and \nsolution stability. The performance criteria for these parameters\nshould be properly set and confirmed through validation to \nensure that the method is suitable for its intended use based \non the specific analytes, matrices, and required precision and \naccuracy of the analytical procedures. Precision and recovery \ndepend highly on concentration and matrix complexity, and \nthe final proposed acceptance criteria need to be justified in \nthe procedure of validation documentation.\n1214\nwww.webofpharma.com\n5.11, NITROSAMINE IMPURITIES\nIP 2022\nGenerally, sensitive methods with limits of quantitation (LOQ) \nin the parts-per-billion (ppb) range are needed to meet the low \nAis recommended for nitrosamines. Manufacturers of APIs \nanddrug products should use methods with LOQs at or below \n0.03 ppm. Manufacturers should establish methods for which \nthe LOQ and limit of detection (LOD) are as low as reasonably \npractical for products for which the maximum daily dose is \nhigh (e.g., more than 1 g). If more than one nitrosamine listed \nin Table 1 is detected, then the analytical metho"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lincomycin-premix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lincomycin Premix"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lincomycin Premix\nLincomycin Hydrochloride Premix.\nLincomycin Premix contains Lincomycin Hydrochloride.\nLintcomycin Prertiix contains \nnot less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nlinecomycin, CisHssN,O,8. \nyap \nsens\nUsual strength. 4.4 per cent why.\nIdentification...\nIn the Assay, the chromatogram obtained with the test solution. \ncorresponds to the chromatogram obtained with the reference\nsolution... \nos \nmo \n: \n: \nee\nTests, \nads\n   \nwww.webofpharma.com\nLincomycin B: Examine test solution \nas deséribed under \nAssay but increasing the sensitivity \nby \n8:to 10:times while \nrecording the’ péak’ due to ‘the trittiethylsily! derivative of \nlincomy¢in B,-whith is eluted immediately-before the’ \ntrimethylsily! derivative of lincomyciti. The ared of the peak\ndue to the trimethylsilyl derivative of lincomycin B; after.\ncorrection for.the sensitivity factor, is.not more than:5 per cent\nof the area.of the peak due to. the, trimethylsilyl derivative of.\njincomycin, \n: \n- \n: \n. \nre \ned \nes \nae\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14), \n.\nTest solution: Dissolve a quantity of premix containing about\n12 mg of Lincomycin:- Hydrochloride in. 10 ml-of the mobile, \nphase, \n, \nDey \n: \nse\nReference solution. A0:12 per tent wiv solution of lincomyein \nhydrochloride IPRS in the mobile phase.\n: Chromatographic system pes\n‘— \na stainless steel column'25.cm-x 4.6 mm, packed with\n: octylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 m),.::\n“— \ncolumntemperature.45°,° \n3°) oss \nos\n~ mobile:phase: a-mixture-of 78 volumes a:solution\nprepared by diluting 13.5 ml of orthophosphoric acid\nto 1000 ml af Wvarer, adjusted to pH 6.0.with ammonium\nea\nIP 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/linearity","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Linearity"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Linearity\nThe linearity of an analytical procedure is its ability (within a \ngiven range) to obtain test results that are directly proportional \nto the concentration (amount) of analyte in the sample. '\nLinearity is usually demonstrated by visual inspection of a \nplot of signals as a function of analyte concentration or \ncontent. If there is a linear relationship, test results should be \nevaluated by appropriate statistical methods, for example, by \ncalculation of a regression line by the method of least squares. \nIn some cases, to obtain linearity between assays and sample \nconcentrations, the test data may need to be subjected to a \nmathematical transformation prior to the regression analysis. \nData from the regression line itself may be helpful to provide \nmathematical estimates of the degree of linearity.\nThe correlation coefficient, y-intercept, slope ofthe regression \nline and residual sum of squares should be calculated. A plot \nofthe data should be included. In addition, an analysis ofthe \ndeviation of the actual data points from the regression line \nmay also be helpful for evaluating linearity.\nA minimum of five concentrations is recommended. Other \napproaches should be justified.\nAccuracy\nThe accuracy of an analytical procedure expresses the \ncloseness of agreement between the value which is accepted \neither as a conventional true value or an accepted reference \nvalue and the value found. This is sometimes termed trueness; \nAccuracy should be established across the specified range of \nthe analytical procedure.\nAccuracy is usually demonstrated by adding known amounts \nof analyte(s) to the sample matrix and determining the measured \nresult using the analytical procedure. The recovery of \nmeasured against actual amounts is then calculated. Usually \na minimum of three determinations at each of three \nconcentrations across the intended range, is recommended.\nAccuracy may also be demonstrated by the method of standard\nadditions, or by cross-correlation of results with a second, \nindependent, procedure. Accuracy may be inferred once \nprecision, linearity and specificity have been established.\nPrecision\nThe precision of an analytical procedure expresses the\ncloseness of agreement (degree of scatter) between a series \nof measurements obtained from multiple sampling of the same \nhomogeneous sample under the prescribed conditions, \nPrecision may be considered at three levels: repeatability, \nintermediate precision and reproducibility.\nPrecision should be investigated using homogeneous, \nauthentic samples. However, if it is not possible to obtain a \nhomogeneous sample it may be investigated using artificially \nprepared samples or a sample solution.\n2.5;1 o: VALIDATION OF ANALYTICAL PROCEDURES\nIP 2022\nThe precision of an analytical procedure: is usually expressed \nas the variance, standard deviation or coefficient of variation \nof a series of measurements.\nRepeatability (Intra-Assay Precision)\nRepeatability expresses the precision under the same operating \nconditions over a short interval of time. Repeatability is also \ntermed as intra-assay precision.\nRepeatability is usually demonstrated by repeated \nmeasurements of a single sample (e g. use of the analytical \nprocedure within a laboratory over a short period of time using \nthe same analyst with the same equipment). A minimum of \nthree determinations at each of three concentrations across \nthe intended range, or a minimum of six determinations at the \ntest concentration is recommended.\nIntermediate Precision\nIntermediate precision expresses within-laboratory variations: \ndifferent days, different analysts or equipment, etc.\nIntermediate precision is usually demonstrated by repeated \nmeasurements of the sample used in the repeatability \nexperiment witbin the same laboratory. Usually the repeatability \nexperiment is repeated bn the same sample by a different \nanalyst, on a different day, using different equipment if \npossible."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/linezolid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Linezolid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Linezolid\nC16H20FN3O4 \nMol.wt, 337.4\nLinezolid is A-[[(5S)-3“[3-fluoro-4-(4-morpholinyl)phenyl]- \n2-oxo-5-oxazolidinyl]methyl]acetamide. .\nLinezolid contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of Ci6H2oFN304, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/liposomal-amphotericin-b-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Liposomal Amphotericin B Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Liposomal Amphotericin B Injection\nLiposomal Amphotericin B Injection is a sterile freeze dried\nmixture of Amphotericin B and along with phospholipids,\ncholesterol, antioxidant and oneor more buffering agents. It\nis filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted with dissolving the content ofthe\nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for\nInjections, immediately before use.\nThe constituted dispersion complies with the requirements\nfor Particulate matter stated under Liposomal preparations.\nStorage. The constituted dispersion should be used\nimmediately afterpreparationbut, in anycasewithin the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nIP 2010\nLiposomalAmphotericinB. Injection contaills .not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 120.0 percent of the stated\namount ofamphotericin B,C47H73N017'\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Liposomal Preparations and with\nthe following requirements."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/liquid-amitraz-dip-concentrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Liquid Amitraz Dip Concentrate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Liquid Amitraz Dip Concentrate\nLiquidAmitraz Dip Concentrate containsAmitraz in a suitable\nemulsifiable vehicle. It may contain a suitable stabilising agent.\nLiquid Amitraz Dip Concentrate contains not less than\n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated\namount ofamitraz, C19H23N3.\nUsual strength. 12.5 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/liquid-maltitol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Liquid Maltitol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Liquid Maltitol\nAqueous solution of a hydrogenated, partly hydrolysed\nstarch, composed of a mixture of mainly 4-O-a-D- \nglucopyranosyl-D-glucitol (D-maltitol) with D-glucitol \n(D-sorbitol) and hydrogenated, oligo- and polysaccharides.\nLiquid Maltitol contains not less than 50.0 per cent of \nD-maltitol, C^H^On, calculated on the anhydrous basis and\n95.0 per cent to 105.0 per cent of the content stated on the \nlabel; not more than 8.0 per cent of D-sorbitol, C6H]4O6 , \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis; not less than 68.0 per cent \nand not more than 85.0 per cent, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A clear, colourless and syrupy liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/liquid-paraffin-oral-emulsion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Liquid Paraffin Oral Emulsion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Liquid Paraffin Oral Emulsion\nLiquid Paraffin Emulsion is an oral emulsion of Liquid Paraffin \nin Purified Water.\nLiquid Paraffin Emulsion contains not less than 44.0 per cent \nand not more than 49.0 per cent w/w of liquid paraffin.\nCategory. Laxative; faecal softener."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/liquid-paraffin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Liquid Paraffin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Liquid Paraffin\nWhite Mineral Oil; Liquid Petrolatum\nLiquid Paraffin is a purified mixture of liquid hydrocarbons \nobtained from petroleum to which not more than 10 ppm of \ntocopherol or of butylated hydroxytoluene may be added.\n3200\nIP 2022\nCategory. Laxative; faecal softener.\nDescription. A transparent, colourless, oily liquid, free froth \nfluorescence by daylight..."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/liquid-paraffm-oral-emulsion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Liquid Paraffm Oral Emulsion"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Liquid Paraffm Oral Emulsion\nLiquid Paraffin Emulsion is an oral emulsion ofLiquid Paraffin\nin Purified Water.\nLiquid Paraffin Emulsion contains not less than 44.0 per cent\nand not more than 49.0 per cent w/w ofliquid paraffin.\nCategory. Laxative; faecal softener.\nDose. 10 to 30 m!."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lisinopril","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lisinopril"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lisinopril\nnh2\nCjjHajNaOs, 2H2O \nMol. Wt. 441.5\nLisinopril is (5)-l-[A^-(l-carboxy-3-phenylpropyl)-L-lysyl]-\nL-proIinedihydrate. / ■ \n'■\nLisinopril contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of \ncalculatedon the anhydrous\nbasis. ■ \n, : \n:>\n2770\nABSORBENT LINT\nIP 2022\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. Awhite crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lispro","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lispro"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lispro\nDescription. A white suspension.\n4638\n4639\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022 :"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lithium-antimony-thiomalate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lithium Antimony Thiomalate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lithium Antimony Thiomalate\n(Looc-c-CHCOOL}, \n9H.O-\n2 S-Sb~\nCuHsLi,O,.$;8b,9H,0 \n‘Mol. Wt. 766.9\n‘Lithium Antimony Thiomalate contains not-less than 15.5-per\ncent and not more than. 16.5 per.cent.of Sb and not less than\n5.1 per cent and not more than 5.7. percent of Li, calculated on.\nthe dried, solvent-free basis.\nCategory. Anthelmintic against trematodes.\nDescription. A pinkish white or creamy powder; hygroscopic..."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lithium-carbonate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lithium Carbonate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lithium Carbonate\nLi2CO3 \nMol.Wt.73.9\nLithium Carbonate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of Li2CO3.\nCategory. Antidepressant\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lodhra","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lodhra"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lodhra\nLodh; Symplocos racemosa\nReference solution. A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of lemon oil \nRS  in ethanol (95 per cent).\nDescription. A clear, amber green to dark green liquid, free \nfrom sediment, suspended matter and characteristic odour.\n11 111111111 \n9\n8 Illik NOM\nUse chromatographic system described in the Assay."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lomustine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lomustine"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lomustine\nC9Hl6CIN30Z\nMol. Wt. 233.7\nLomustine is 1-(2-chloroethy1)-3-cyclohexy1-1-nitrosourea.\nLomustine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more\nthan 100.5 per cent of C9H16ClN30Z, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDose. 120 to 130 mg per sq. m. body-surface every 6 to 8\nweeks.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder.\nCarry out the tests protected from light and prepare the\nsolutions immediately before use."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/loperamide-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Loperamide Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Loperamide Hydrochloride\nC29H33CIN2O2,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 513.5\nLoperamide Hydrochloride is 4-(4-chlorophenyl)-4-hydroxy- \npiperidino] -NfV-dimethyl-2,2-dipheny Ibutyramide \nhydrochloride.\nLoperamide Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C29H33CIN2O2,HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidiarrhoeal.\nDescription, A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lopinavir-and-ritonavir-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lopinavir and Ritonavir Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lopinavir and Ritonavir Tablets\nLopinavir and Ritonavir Tablets contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namounts of lopinavir, C37H48N4O5 and ritonavir,\nUsual strength. Lopinavir 200 mg and Ritonavir 50 mg. \n:\n2782\nIP 2022\nLOPINAVIR AND RITONAVIR CAPSULES"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lopinavir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lopinavir"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lopinavir\nC37IUNTL \nMol. Wt. 628.8\nLopinavir is (a*S)-/V-[(15',35, 41S)-4-[[(2,6-dimethyl- \nphenoxy)acetyl] amino-3-hydroxy-5-phenyl-1-(phenyl- \nmethyl)pentyl]tetrahydro-a-( 1-methylethyl)-2-oxo- ;\nl-(2H)-pyrimidineacetamide.' \n' \n1 :\nLopinavir contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C37H48N4O5, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis. ' \n' ■' '\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/loratadine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Loratadine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Loratadine Tablets\nLoratadine Tablets contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of C22H23C1N2O2.\nUsual strengths. 5 mg; 10 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/loratadine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Loratadine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Loratadine\nCzzH^CINA- \n■ : \nMoLWt.382.9\nLoratadine is ethyl 4-(8-chloro-5,6-dihydro-lIH- \nbenzo[5,6]cyclohepta[l,2-£>]pyridin-l 1-ylidene) piperidine-1- \ncarboxylate.\nLoratadine contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.5 per cent of C22H23CIN2O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Hi receptor antagonist; antihistamine.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lorazepam-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lorazepam Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lorazepam Injection\nLorazepam Injection is a sterile solution of Lorazepam in a \nsuitable solvent.\nLorazepam Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of lorazepam, \nC15H10Cl2N2O2.\nUsual strengths. 2 mg per ml; 4 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lorazepam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lorazepam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lorazepam\nC15H{oC12N202 \nMol. Wt. 321.2\nLorazepam is (3R5)-7chloro-5-.(2-chlorophenyl)-3-hydroxy-\n1,3-di hydro-2//-1,4-benzodiazepin-2-one.\nLorazepam contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C15H10C12N2O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis. , \n. . •\nCategory. Antianxiety.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder. It\nshows polymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/losartan-potassium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Losartan Potassium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Losartan Potassium\nCl\nCaHaCIKNeO\nMol. Wt. 461.0\nLosartan Potassium is monopotassium salt of 2-butyl- \n4-chloro-1 -[ [2'-(I//-tetrazol-5-yl)[ 1,1 '-biphenyl]-4-yl]methyl]- \nl/f-imidazole-5-methanol.\nLosartan Potassium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and \nj not more than 102.0 per cent of C22H22C1KN6O, calculated on \nj the anhydrous basis.\nj Category. Antihypertensive.\ni Production\n1 As Nitrosamines are classified as probable human.carcinogens, \nj their presence in losartan potassium should be avoided or\nj limited as much as possible. For this reason, manufacturers\nof losartan potassium for human use are expected to perform \n3 an assessment of the risk of W-nitrosamine formation and \nj contamination during their manufacturing process; if this \nj assessment identifies a potential risk, the manufacturing' \n| process should be modified to minimise contamination and a\n3 control strategy implemented to detect and control N- \n| : ■ liitrosamirie impurities in losartan potassium. The general\n| chapter 5.11. Nitrosamine Impurities is available to assist \nI ■ :■ manufacturers.'\nLOSARTAN POTASSIUM'\nIP 2022\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lovastatin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lovastatin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lovastatin\nCWkA \n: \nMol. Wt. 404.5\n/ Lovastatin is butanoic acid, 2-methyl-,l .2,3,7,8,8a-hcxahydro-\n3,7-dimethyl-8-[2-(tetrahydro-4-hydroxy-6-oxo-2//-pyran-2“.\nIP 2022\nLOVASTATIN\nyl)-ethyl]-l-naphthalenyl ester, [lS-[la(R*),3a,7P,8p \n(2S*,4S*).Sajj]J; (S)-2-methylbutyric acid, 8-ester with(4J?,67?)~ \n6-[2-[(lS, 25,6R,85,8a/?)-l,2,6,7,8,8a-hexahydro-8-hydroxy-2,6- \ndimethyl-1-naphthyl] ethyl] tetrahydro-4-hydroxy-2ZZ-pyran- \n2-one.\nLovastatin contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C24H36O5, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antihyperlipidemic.\nDescription. A white to off-white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lubiprostone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lubiprostone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lubiprostone\nFl I\nHOJ JL / 0\nF F\nC2;)Ik,F2O5 \nMol Wt. 390.5\nLubiprostone is 7-[(17?,37?,62?,7/f)-3-(l,l-Difluoropentyl)- \n3-hydroxy~8-oxo-2-oxabicyclo[4.3.0]non-7-yl]heptanoic acid.\nLubiprostone contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C2riH?2F2O5. calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Constipation agent ,Gastro intestinal drug.\nDescription. A white to off white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/luliconazole-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Luliconazole Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Luliconazole Cream\nLuliconazole Cream contains not less than 90.6 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nluliconazole, Ci4H9C12N3S2.\nUsual strength. 1.0 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/luliconazole-lotion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Luliconazole Lotion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Luliconazole Lotion\nLuliconazole Lotion contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nluliconazole, C^CljNsSa.\nUsual strength. 1.0 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/luliconazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Luliconazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Luliconazole\nC14ILC12N3S2 \nMol Wt. 354.3\nLuliconazole is (2E)[4-(2,4-Dichlorophenyl)-l,3-dithiolan-2- \nylidene] (1 H-imidazol-1 -yl)acetonitrile.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nLuliconazole contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of the stated amount of luliconazole, \nCi4H9Cl2N3S2, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antifungal.\nDescription. An off white to pale yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lumefantrine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lumefantrine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lumefantrine\nC3oH32C13NO \nMol. Wt 528.9\nLumefantrine is (±)-2,7-Dichloro-9-[(z)-p-chlorobenzylidine]- \na[(dibutylamino)methyl]-fluorene-4-methanol.\nLumefantrine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C3qH32C13NO.\nCategory. Antimalarial.\nDescription. Ayellow Crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lutein","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lutein"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lutein\nC40H56O2 \nMol. Wt. 568.9\nLutein is 0-E-Carotene-3,3 ’ -diol (3R,3 7?, 6 7?). It is the purified \nfraction obtained from saponification of the olebresin of Tagetes \neracta L. \nf' ...\nLutein contains not less than 80.0 per cent of total carotenoids \ncalculated as C40H56O2. It contains not less than 74.0 per cent \nof lutein and hot more than 8.5 per cent of zeaxdnthin, both \ncalculated as C40H56O2 on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antioxident \n7 \n’ ’\nDescription. A red crystalline powder. ' \n', 'l 1 ;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lynestrenol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lynestrenol"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lynestrenol\nC2oH280 \nMol. Wt. 284.4\nLynoestrenol is 19-nor-17cc-pregn-4-en-20-yn-l 7[3-ol.\nLynoestrenol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C20H28O, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Progestogen.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/lynoestrenol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Lynoestrenol"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Lynoestrenol\nLynestrenol\nH3C OH -CE-CH\nH H\nC2o11280\nLynoestrenol is 19-nor-17oc-pregn-4-en-20-yn-17ii-ol.\nLynoestrenol contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 102.0 per cent of C2o1-12s0, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Progestogen.\nDose. 5 to 10 mg.\nA white or almost white, crystailifiepoWder:\nDescription.\nINDIAN PHARMACOPOEIA 2018\nMONOGRAPHS\nM"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/macrogol-1500","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Macrogol 1500"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Macrogol 1500\nPolyethylene Glycol 1500 is a mixture of the polycondensation \nproducts of ethylene oxide and water obtained under \ncontrolled conditions. It is represented by the formula \nHOCH2[CH2OCH2]nCH2OH, where n is between 28 and 36. ’\nCategory; Pharmaceutical aid (ointment base). .\nDescription. A white or creamy white, soft, wax-like solid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/macrogol-6000","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Macrogol 6000"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Macrogol 6000\nPolyethylene Glycol 6000 is a mixture of the polycondensation \nproducts of ethylene oxide and water obtained under \ncontrolled conditions. It is represented by the formula \nHOCH2[CH2OCH2]nCH2OH, where n is between 112 and 158.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (ointment base and tablet \nexcipient).\nDescription. A white to creamy white, wax-like solid or flakes; \nodour, faint and characteristic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/magaldrate-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magaldrate Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Magaldrate Oral Suspension\nMagaldrate Suspension\nMagaldrate Oral Suspension contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nanhydrous magaldrate, AlsMg^CfrffitSOff.\nUsual strength. The equivalent of 800 mg of anhydrous \nmagaldrate per 5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/magaldrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magaldrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Magaldrate\nAlsMg10(OH)31(SO4)2;xH2O Mol. Wt. 1097.4 (anhydrous)\nMagaldrate is a chemical combination of aluminium and \nmagnesium hydroxides and sulphates corresponding \napproximately to the formula MjMg^OH^SO^xI^O.\nMagaldrate contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more \nthan 105.0 percent ofAl5Mg10(OH)3i(SO4)2, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Antacid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/magnesium-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magnesium Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Magnesium Chloride\nMgCl2,6H2O \nMol. Wt 203.3\nMagnesium Chloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand riot more than 101.0 per cent of MgCl2,6H2O.\nCategory. Constituent of peritoneal dialysis solutions.\nDescription. Colourless crystals; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/magnesium-hydroxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magnesium Hydroxide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Magnesium Hydroxide\nMg(OH)2 \nMoi. Wt. 58.3\nMagnesium Hydroxide contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of Mg (OH)2, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Antacid; osmotic laxative.\nDescription, Abulky white powder,\n2816\nMAGALDRATE AND SIMETHICONE ORAL SUSPENSION\nIP 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/magnesium-stearate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magnesium Stearate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Magnesium Stearate\nMagnesium Stearate consists mainly of magnesium stearate \n(Ci7H35CO2)2Mg with variable proportions of magnesium \npalmitate, (CnHfiCC^Mg and magnesium oleate, \n(C17H33CO2)2Mg.\n2817\nwww.webofpharma.com\nMagnesium Stearate contains not less than 3.8 per cent and \nnot more than 5.0 per cent of Mg, calculate on the dried basis. \nThe fatty acid fraction contains not less than 40.0 per cent of \nstearic acid and the sum of stearic acid and palmitic acid is not \nless than 90.0 per cent.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (lubricant).\nDescription. A very fine, light, white powder; unctuous and \nfree from grittiness."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/magnesium-sulphate-heptahydrate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magnesium Sulphate Heptahydrate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Magnesium Sulphate Heptahydrate Injection\nedetate and 15 mg of mordant black 11 mixture, heat to 45° to\nMgSO4,7H20 \t\nMol. Wt. 246.5 \nMagnesium Sulphate contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 100.5 per cent of MgSO 4, calculated on \nthe dried basis.\n50° and titrate with 0.1 Mzinc sulphate until the colour changes \nfrom blue to violet. Repeat the operation without the substance \nunder examination. The difference between the titrations \nrepresents the amount of disodium edetate required.\nMagnesium Sulphate Injection is a sterile solution of \nMagnesium Sulphate Heptahydrate in Water for Injection.\nMagnesium Sulphate Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount of MgSO4, 7H20.\nCategory. Osmotic laxative; used in the treatment of electrolyte \ndeficiency.\n1 ml of O./ Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.002431 g of\nMg.\nUsual strength. 500 mg per ml. \nDescription. A clear, colourless solution.\nDose. 2 to 16 g.\nFatty acid composition. Determine by gas chromatography\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder.\n(2.4.13)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/magnesium-sulphate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magnesium Sulphate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Magnesium Sulphate Injection\nMagnesium Sulphate Heptahydrate Injection\nMagnesium Sulphate Injection is a sterile solution of \nMagnesium Sulphate Heptahydrate in Water for Injection.\nMagnesium Sulphate Injection contains.not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated\namount of MgSO4,7H2O. \n(......., : : a ,\nUsual strength. 500 mg per ml. \ns ,\nDescription. A clear, colourless solution. . \n-; ■ =: L-;:; ~ -\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/magnesium-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magnesium Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Magnesium Sulphate\nMgS04,7H20\nMol.Wt. 246.5\nMagnesium Sulphate contains not less than 99.0 per cent\nand not more than 100.5 per cent of MgS04, calculated on\nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Osmotic laxative; used in the treatment ofelectrolyte\ndeficiency.\n'\nMAGNESIUM STEARATE\nIP 2010\nDose. 2 to 16 g.\nDescription. Colourless crystals or a white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/magnesium-trisilicate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Magnesium Trisilicate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Magnesium Trisilicate\nMagnesium Trisilicate is a hydrated magnesium silicate of the \napproximatecomposition 2MgO, 3 SiO^x^O.\nMagnesium Trisilicate contains not less than the equivalent \nof 29.0 per cent of MgO and not less than the equivalent of \n65.0 per cent of SiO2, both calculated oh the ignited basis.\nCategory .Antacid.\ndescription. A fine, white or nearly white powder, free from \ngrittiness; slightly hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/maleic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Maleic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Maleic Acid\nH COOH\nH^COOH\nQIA \nMol. Wt. 116.1\nMaleic Acid is (Z)-butenedioic acid.\nMaleic Acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C4H4O4, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/malic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Malic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Malic Acid\no\nhoJVY°H\nOH O\nCH A \nMol. Wt. 134.1\nMalic Acid is (7?5)-hydroxybutanedioic acid.\nMalic Acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C4H6O5, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/malt-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Malt Extract"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Malt Extract\nReference solution (/). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of limonene \nRS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nMalt Extract is a product obtained by extracting malted grains \nof cereals (barley, cholam or wheat) with water at a suitable \ntemperature and evaporation of the strained liquid until a \nviscous product is obtained. It may be mixed with 10 per cent\nMandarin oil contains not less than 1.6 per cent and not more \nthan 3.0 per cent of a-pinene, not less than 1.2 per cent and \nnot more than 2.0 per cent of13-pinene, not more than 0.3 per \ncent of sabinene, not less than 1.5 per cent and not more than \n2.0 per cent of 13-myrcene, not more than 1.0 per cent of \nP-cymene, not less than 65.0 per cent and not more than 75.0 \nper cent of limonene, not less than 16.0 per cent and not more \nthan 22.0 per cent of y-terpinene, not less than 0.30 per cent \nand yniaontthmraonrileatet.han 0.60 per cent of methyl N- \nmeth\nReference solution (g). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of \ny-terpinene RS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nReference solution (h). A 2.0 per cent w/v solution of  methyl \nN-methylanthranilate RS in ethanol (95 per cent).\nby weight of Glycerin.\nMalt Extract contains nitrogen equivalent to not less than\nChromatographic system\n4.0 per cent w/w of protein.\na capillary column 30 m x 0.25 mm, coated with methyl 5 \nper cent phenylpolysiloxane, \n-\ntemperature: \ncolumn. 60° for 1 minute, increase from 60° to 230° at a \nrate of 4° per minute, and maintain at this temperature \n7\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid\nDescription. A sweet, viscous, light brown liquid; odour,\nCategory. Antiseptic, antispasmodic, carminative. \nDescription. A greenish yellow or greenis \nIntensely sweet, citrusy, tangy, fresh odour.\n pleasant and characteristic.\n- for IQ minutes,\nTeti*\ninlet port at 230° and detector at 240',\nRfractive index (2.4.27). 1.489 to 1.498, determined at 20°•\ndetection by flame ionization detector,\n-\nN =\n-\nflow rate: 1 ml per minute using helium as carrier gas, \n-\ninjection volume: 0.5 !al, \n-\nsplit ratio: 1: 200.\nInject reference solutions (a), (b), (c), (d), (e), (f), (g), (h) and \nthe test solution.\nCalculate the content of a-pinene, 13-pinene, sabinene, \n13-myrcene, 13-cymene, limonene, i-terpinene, \nN-methylanthranilate in the oil using area normalization\nprocedure.\nStorage. Store in cool, dry place in tightly sealed containers, \nprotected from heat and light."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/maltodextrin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Maltodextrin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Maltodextrin\nMaltodextrin is a mixture of glucose, disaccharides and \npolysaccharides, obtained by the partial hydrolysis of starch.\nThe degree bf hydrolysis, expressed as dextrose equivalent \n(DE), is not more than 20 (nominal value).\nlillllilllilll \n2823\nwww.webofpharma.com\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or almost white, slightly hygroscopic \npowder or granules."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/mandukaparni-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mandukaparni Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Mandukaparni Dry Extract\nGotu Kola; Cehtella asiatica\nMandukaparni Dry Extract is obtained by extracting \nMandukaparni aerial parts with aqueous ethanol or ethanol \nor any other suitable solvent and evaporation of solvents.\nMandukaparni Dry Extract contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent w/w and not more than 110.0 per cent w/w of the stated \namount of total triterpenes (sum of asiaticoside, asiatic acid \nas asiaticoside and madecassoside (mixture of asiaticoside B \nand madecassoside); madecassic acid (mixture of terminolic \nacid and madecassic acid) as madecassoside) calculated on \nthe dried basis. It may contain suitable added substances.\nCategory. Brain tonic, anabolic, anxiolytic, alterative, raktapitta;\nDescription. A greenish brown to green powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/mandukaparni","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mandukaparni"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Mandukaparni\nGotu Kola; Centella asiatica\nMandukaparni consists of the dried aerial parts of Centella \nasiatica (Linn.) Urban. (Fam. Umbelliferac).\nMandukaparni contains not less than 0.5 per cent w/w of \nasiaticoside, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Brain tonic, anabolic, anxiolytic, alterative, raktapitta.\nDescription. A green to greenish yellow in colour, taste, \nslightly bitter and sweet."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/mannitol-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mannitol Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Mannitol Injection\nMannitol Intravenous Infusion\nMannitol Injection is a sterile solution of Mannitol in Water \nfor Injections.\n2825\nwww.webofpharma.com\nMannitol Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of mannitol, \nc6h14o6.\nUsual strengths. 10,15,20 and 25 per cent w/v.\nDescription. A colourless or almost colourless clear solution. \nParticle, if any, present should dissolve on warming."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/manufacturers","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Manufacturers"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Manufacturers\n(Raw material)\nPolyethylene (PE)=IS 10146 \nPolyvinyl chloride (PVC)=IS 10151 \nPolypropylene (PP) = IS 10910 \nPolyamides [nylon 6] = IS 12247 \nPolycarbonate = IS 14971\nPoly (ethylene-vinyl acetate) = IS 13601\ncomposition of various \npackaging polymers, , \n(plastic resins)\nBottles, Containers, Tubes, Caps, Closures \nand other forms of Packages\nIdentification Codes\nCodeNo. 1=PET\nCode No.2 = HDPE (High Density PE)\nCode No.3 = V (P VC, Polyvinyl Chloride)\nCode No.4 = LDPE (Low Density PE) \nCodeNo.5=PPCode No.6 = PS (Polystyrene) \nCode No.7 = Other plastics.\nBIS Standard for Resin\nIdentification Codes\nIS-14534\n' Substances, Preparations, Articles ■\nThe Drugs and Cosmetics \nAct, 1940 The Drugs and \nCosmetics Rules, 1945\nConsumer.\nConsumption/Usage\nPatients, Health care chain (Doctors, \nHospitals)\nVarious responsibilities on Manufacturers, \nLocal Bodies, Citizens, etc.\n1228\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2022\n6.2. CONTAINERS\nThe selection of a suitable plastic container should be\nbased on a knowledge - obtained from the supplier - of the \ncomposition of the plastic (e.g. raw materials, colourants and \nadditives) so that potential hazards can be assessed.\nThe plastic container chosen for any particular pharmaceutical \nproduct should be such that the ingredients of the product in \ncontact with the container do not significantly interact or \nmigrate into or through the plastic, to a level that affects the \nquality of the product.\nTest on Container Material\nType samples (specimen) of the intended container should be \npacked with the product and tested under conditions that\nreproduce those that would be encountered in use. These \ntests should include examination of the product to ensure \nabsence of any sensory, chemical or physical change, an \nassessment of changes in the quality of contents due to \npermeability of the plastic, detection of changes in pH, an \nassessment of the effects of light, chemical tests and where \nnecessary, biological tests. Containers from bulk production\nshould conform to the type sample (specimen) in every respect. \nIt should be ensured that there is no change in the composition \nor any change in the manufacturing method used by the \nmanufacturer and more importantly, that no use is made of \npost-consumer recycled material. It must be emphasized that \nchanges in the composition of the plastic or reworking or \ninadequate control of processing can bring about changes \nwhich may invalidate the results of type testing. Samples from \nproduction should be tested to ensure conformance to type \nsamples (specimen) and test schedules should be designed \nto check deviations from the characteristic of the type sample \n(specimen).\nThe biological and chemical tests described below are \nintended for plastic containers in which pharmaceutical \nformulations are packaged. It should be appreciated that these \ntests by themselves are not sufficient to establish safety or \nsuitability of the plastic containers for the preparations and it \nis necessary to consider the results of the tests in conjunction \nwith the information given above. Specification should be \nagreed with the container manufacturer and should be revised \nif the composition of the plastic or the ingredient quality is \naltered or the processing treatment is changed.\nNOTE 2 — Applicability of Biological Tests for Plastic \nPackages\na) \nPackages used for non-parenteral dosage forms do not \nrequire any biological testing.\nb) Packages used forparenteral dosage forms require in vitro\ntesting (2.2.23).\nc) \nPackages that meet the requirements of the in vitro tests \nare not required to undergo any further in vivo, testing \n(2.2.24).\nd) \nPackages that do not meet the requirements of the in \nvitro tests where applicable are required to undergo \nfurther in vivo testing.\nPackages that do not meet the requirements of the biological \nreactivity tests [(2.2.23) and (2.2.24), if appropriate] are not \nsuitable as packages for pharmaceutical use.\nNOTE 3 —Responsibility\nIt shall be"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/marbofloxacin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Marbofloxacin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Marbofloxacin Injection\nMarbofloxacin Injection isa sterile solution o: of Marbofloxacin\nin water for injections.\nMarbofloxacin Injection contains not léss than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.00 per cent of the stated amount. of \nmarbofloxacin, C)jHisFN,O,.\nUsual strength. 100-mg per ml:\nDescription, A clear colourless solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/marbofloxacin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Marbofloxacin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Marbofloxacin\nMol. Wi. 362.4\nIP-2022 \n~ MARBOFLOXACIN\nMarbofloxacin \nis 9-Fluore-2,3-dihydro-3-methyl- \nName \nRelative \nCorrection \n°-. \n10-(4-methylpiperazin-1-yl)-7-oxo-7/#-pyrido[3,2;1-i] \nretention time \n. \nfactor \n[4,1,2]benizoxadiazine-6-carboxylic acid. \n-.. \nMarbofloxacin impurity \nB! \n05 \noe\nMarbofloxacin contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nMarbofloxacin impurity A? \nO7 \n¢ \ncoe\nmore than 101.0 per cent of Cy HisFNOy calculated on the \nMarbofloxacin impurity C2 \n09 \n_\ndried basis. \n; \na \n“ \na\n: \nMarbofloxacin (Retention time: \nCategory. Fluroquinolone antibacterial. \nabout 33 minutes) \n. \n10 \n~_.\nDescription. A light yellow, crystalline powder. : \nMarbofloxacin impurity D* \n13 \n—\n_ \nSe \n: \n, \nMarbofloxacin impurity B:- \n-'. \n15 \na \n15 \nIdentification.\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometery 2. 4, 6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with miarbofloxacin \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of marbofloxacin."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/maricha","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Maricha"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Maricha\nBlack pepper; pepper; Piper nigrum\nMaricha consists of theunripc fruits ofPiper nigrum Linn.\n(Fam.Piperaceae). \n..\nMaricha contains not less than 2.5 per cent w/w of piperine, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticonvulsant, bioavailability enhancer, appetizer, \nanthelmintic, swasa. 1 - :\n4262\nIP 2022\nDescription. Fruits are globular or oblong; They have a blackish \nbrown cover, with raised reticulated wrinkles. The odour is \naromatic and thé taste is strong and pungent. \n;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/me-me","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Me Me"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Me Me\nA. Triturate 0.5 g with 10 ml of water and adjust to pH 8.0 by \nthe addition of 1 M sodium hydroxide using cresol red \nsolution as indicator. Divide the resulting solution into two\nC9H19N04 \t\nMol. Wt. 205.3\nFor lipase activity-- To 95 ml of ivater, add 6.5 ml of triacetin \nand 0.2 ml of a 0.1 per cent w/v solution of bromocresol purple, \nneutralise with 0.5 M sodium hydroxide and add sufficient \nwater to produce 110 ml. Place 50 ml of this solution in each of \ntwo large tubes 3 cm x 20 cm A and B contained in a thermostat \nat 30°. Insert in each tube a rubber stopper having two holes, \none for the tip of a burette and the other for a short glass tube \nthrough which passes a thread operating a glass stirring coil. \nStir the contents of the tube until they attain the temperature \nof the thermostat. Prepare a solution of 0.1 g of the substance \nunder examination in 10.0 ml of water. To tube A add 1.0 ml of \nthe solution, to tube B add 1.0 ml of the solution previously \nboiled. Adjust and maintain the pH of the solutions in the two \ntubes to 6.2 to 6.4 by the addition of 0.05 M sodium hydroxide \ndropwise, stirring frequently. After 30 minutes, the difference \nbetween the volumes of 0.05 M sodium hydroxide added to \nthe two tubes is not less than 1.0 ml.\nD-Panthenol is (R)-2,4-dihydroxy-W-(3-hydroxypropy1)-3, 3- \ndimethylbutanamide.\nequal portions. Boil one portion [solution (1)] and leave the \nother untreated [solution (2)]. To each add a few shreds of \ncongo red fibrin, warm to 39° ± 1° and maintain at this \ntemperature for 1 hour. Solution (2) is stained red and solution \n(1) is colourless or not more than slightly pink.\nD-Panthenol contains not less than 6.60 per cent and not more \nthan 6.95 per cent of nitrogen, N.\nCategory. Vitamin B (enzyme co-factor).\nDose. 5 mg to 50 mg.\nB. Triturate 0.25 g with 10 ml of water and adjust to pH 8.0 by \nthe addition of 1 M sodium hydroxide using cresol red \nsolution as indicator. Divide the resulting solution into two\nDescription. A clear, colourless or slightly yellow, viscous \nliquid; odourless.\nequal portions. Boil one portion [solution (1)] and leave the \nother untreated [solution (2)]. Dissolve 0.1 g of soluble starch \nin 100 ml of boiling water, boil for 2 minutes, cool and dilute to"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/mebendazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mebendazole"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Mebendazole\nC16Hi3N3O3 \nMol. Wt. 295.3\nMebendazole is Carbamic acid,(5-benzyI-lH-binzimidazol-2- \nyl),methyl ester.\nMebendazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of Ci6H]3N3O3, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Anthelmintic.\nDescription. A white to slightly yellow, amorphous powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/mebeverine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mebeverine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Mebeverine Hydrochloride\nC25H35NO5,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 466.0\nMebeverine Hydrochloride is (/?5’)-4-[ethyl(4-methoxy- \na-methylphenethyl)amino] butyl veratrate hydrochloride.\nMEBEVERINE HYDROCHLORDE\nMebeverine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C25H3sNO5,HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antispasmodic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/meclizine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Meclizine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Meclizine Hydrochloride\nMeclizine Dihydrochloride; Meclozine Hydrochloride; \nMeclozine Dihydrochloride\nC2SH27CIN2^HC1 \nMol. Wt. 463.9\nwww.webofpharma.com\nMeclizine Hydrochloride is . (/?)S)-l-(4-chlorobenzhydryl)- \n4-(3-methylbenzyl)piperazine dihydrochloride.\nMeclizine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of Cjj^CIN^HCI, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antiemetic.\nDescription. A white or yellowish white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/meclofenamic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Meclofenamic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Meclofenamic Acid\nMol. Wt. 296.2\nMeclofenamic acid is N-(2,6-dichloro-3-methylphenyl)\nanthranilic acid.\nMeclofenamic Acid contains not less than 98.5 per cent and\nnot more than 100.5 per cent of the stated amount of\nCt4HttChN02, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Anti-inflammatory; analgesic; antipyretic.\nDose. Horses. 2.2 mg per kg of body weight daily, for 5 to 7\ndays.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/medroxyprogesterone-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Medroxyprogesterone Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Medroxyprogesterone Acetate\nQ4H34O4 \nMol. wt. 386.5\nMedroxyprogesterone Acetate is 6a-methyl-3,20-dioxo-prcgn- \n4-en-17a-yl acetate.\nMedroxyprogesterone Acetate contains not less than \n97.0 per cent and not more than 103.0 per cent of C24H34O4, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Progestogen.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/medroxyprogesterone-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Medroxyprogesterone Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Medroxyprogesterone Injection\nMedroxyprogesterone Acetate Injection\nMedroxyprogesterone Injection is a sterile suspension bf \nMedroxyprogesterone Acetate in a suitable vehicle. ;\nMedroxyprogesterone Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent 'of the stated \namount of medroxyprogesterone acetate, C24H34O4.\nUsual strength. 150mgperml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/mefenamic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mefenamic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Mefenamic Acid\nMol. Wt. 241.3\nMefenamicAcid is N-(2,3-xylyl)anthranilic acid.\nMefenamic Ac.id contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not\nmore than 100.5 per centofC15H15N02, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nMEFENAMIC ACID\nCategory. Antiinflammatory; analgesic.\nDose. 250 to 500 mg thrice daily, after food.\nDescription. A white to greyish-white, microcrystalline\npowder; odourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/mefloquine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Mefloquine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Mefloquine Hydrochloride\nC17H17CIF6N2O \nMol. Wt. 414.8\nMefloquine Hydrochloride is (7?5)-[2,8-bis(trifluoromethyl) \nqiiinolin-4-yl][(21S7?)-piperidin-2-yl|mcthanol hydrochloride. .\nMefloquine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci7H17ClF6N2O \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antimalarial.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/megestrol-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Megestrol Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Megestrol Acetate\nC24H32O4 \nMol. Wt 384.5\nMegestrol Acetate is 6-methyl-3,20-dioxopregna-4,6-dien-l 7- \nyl acetate.\nMegestrol Acetate contains not less than 97.0 per cent and \nnot more than 103.0 per cent of C24H32O4, calculated on the \ndried basis. ■\nCategory. Progestogen.\nDescription. A white to creamy-white, crystalline powder. ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/meloxicam-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Meloxicam Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Meloxicam Injection\nMeloxicam Injection-is a sterile solution of Meloxicam in Water \nfor Injections.\nMeloxicam Injection contains. not less than 95.0: per cent.and\nnet more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of meloxicam,\nC4H3N;0,82 :\nUsual strengths. 5 mg per ml, 20 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/meloxicam-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Meloxicam Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Meloxicam Oral Suspension\nMeloxicam Oral Suspension is a suspension of Meloxicam in \na suitable vehicle.\nMeloxicam Oral Suspension contains not less than 95.0 per \ncent and riot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nmeloxicam, C14H13N3O4S2.\nUsual strengths. 7.5 mg; 15 mg. > ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/melphalan-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Melphalan Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Melphalan Injection\nMelphalan Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nMelphalan with or without auxiliary substances. It is filled in a \nsealed container.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per vial\nThe inection is prepared immediately before use by dissolving \nthe contents of the sealed container which contains Melphalan \nHydrochloride with or without auxiliary substances in a \nsuitable solvent and then diluting with the requisite volume \nof a suitable diluent in accordance with the manufacturer’s \ninstructions.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirement for \nParticulate matter stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Injections) and with, the following tests."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/melphalan","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Melphalan"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Melphalan\nC)3H18C12N2O2 . \nMol. Wt. 305.2\nMelphalan is 4-bis(2-chloroethyl)amino-L-phenylalanine.\nMelphalan contains not less than 93.0 per cent and not more \nthan 100,5 per cent of CuHjgCkNjCh, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/memantine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Memantine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Memantine Hydrochloride\nC12H21N,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 215.8\nMemantine Hydrochloride is tricyclo [3.3.1.13,7] decan-1-amine, \n3,5-dimethyl-hydrochloride; 1 -aminb-3,5-dimethyT-adamantane \nhydrochloride.\nMemantine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per.cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C12H2iN,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antialzheimer.\nDescription. A white to off white, coloured powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/member-the-president","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Member The President"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Member The President\n(ex-officio) Indian Pharmaceutical Alliance (IPA)\nMumbai.\n(Shri Samir Mehta)\nMember- The Secretary-cum-Scientific Director \nSecretary . Indian Pharmacopoeia Commission\n(ex-officio) Sector-23^Raj Nagar\nGhaziaoad 20 i (102.\n[Dr. Rajeev Singh.Raghuvanshi)\nThe Scientific Body\nThe composition of the Scientific Body is given below:\nChairperspn ProfN.K.Ganguly\nFormer Director General, ICMR\nC-191, 9tt Floor, City View Apartment\nPlot No. 199, Sector-35\nNoida-201 301.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nMember r\nDr. Nitin Bhatia \n-■<- \n-A--\nAssociate Vice President\n(Tech. & Veterinary Regulatory) \n,..:\nIntas Animal Health \n■ \n' '\n. Intas Pharmaceuticals Ltd.\nCorporate House\nNear Sola Bridge, S. G.. Road, Thaltej \nAhmedabad-380 054.\nProf. Naresh Bhatnagar\nDepartment of Mechanical Engineering.\nIndian Institute of Technology, \nDelhi, Hauz- Khas, \nNew Delhi-110 016.\nMember\nMember\nProf. Vinod Kumar Dixit\nFormer Dean, Faculty of Pharmacy & \nRetired Professor of Pharmacognosy\nDr. Hari Singh Gour University, \nSagar- 470 003.\nMember\nDr. Sunil Gairola\nExecutive Director\nSerum Institute of India Pvt. Ltd.,\n212/2, Hadapsar, \nPune-411 028.\nMember\nDr. Nithya Gogtay\nAssociate Professor\nDepartment of Clinical Pharmacology \nKEM and SethG, S. Medical College \n'Mumbai-400 012.\nMember\nProf. Ramesh. K. Goyal\nVice Chancellor\n. Delhi Pharmaceutical Sciences & Research\nUniversity\nDi. Raman Mohan Singh \n■\nMember\nDr. PraveenKhullar\nHead ofMSAT,GenMed Cluster.\nEurope &. International, IA GenMed \nSanofi Healthcare (India) Pvt. Ltd.\nL-121, Phase IHA, GIDC\nVerna Industrial Estate\nVerna, Goa-403 722. \n\" ■\n\\, Member\nProf. Sanjay Singh \n■\nMember\nDr. Arun Kumar Mishra\nHead, Global Regulatory\nAf