e at about 335 nm, measure the\nabsorbance at ‘about 280 nm (2.4:7); of the final solution \n‘obtained in. the Assay, The ratio of the absorbance at about\n280 nm to that at about 335 nim \nis 1.59 to 1. 69,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/suxamethonium-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Suxamethonium Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Suxamethonium Chloride\nMol. Wt. 397.3\nSuccinylcholine Chloride is 2,2'-succinyldioxy-\nbis(ethyltrimethylammonium) dichloride dihydrate.\nSuccinylcholine Chloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent\nand not more than 101.0 per cent ofC14H30ChN204, calculated\non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Depolarising skeletal muscle relaxant.\nDose. By intravenous injection, 20 to 100 mg, according to the\nneeds of the patient.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nalmost odourless; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/syrup","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Syrup"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Syrup \n• j\nDextromethorphan Hydrobromide Syrup is a solution of | \nDextromethorphan Hydrobromide in a suitable flavour | \nvehicle. \n|\nDextromethorphan Hydrobromide Syrup contains not less I \nthan 95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the \n}\nstated amount of dextromethorphan hydrobromide I \nCi8H25NO,HBr,H2O. ' \n: \n|\n■■ ■■ ■ \n|\nUsual strength. 13.5mgin5ml. \n|"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tablets\nOxybutynin Tablets contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 105^0 per cent of the stated amount of \noxybutynin hydrochloride, C22H3iNO3,HCl.\nUsual strengths, 2.5 mg; 5.0 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tadalafil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tadalafil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tadalafil\nTadalafil is (6R, 12a/?)-6-( 1,3 -benzodipxol-5 -y l)-2-methyb \n2,3,6,7,12,12a-hexahydropyrazino[l \\2’: 1,6]-pyrido[3,4.\nindole-1,4-dione. \n; ; ,\nTadalafil contains not less than 97.5 per cent and not more \nthan 102.5 per cent of C22H]9N3O4, calculated1 on the dried \nbasis. .... \n- \n;\nCategory. Erectile disfunction. \nymumi .\nDescription. A white or almost white powder. \n“ i:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tamoxifen-citrate-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tamoxifen Citrate Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tamoxifen Citrate Tablets\nCategory. Antioestrogen.\nDose. The equivalent of 20 to 40 mg of tamoxifen daily as a \nsingle dose or in 2 divided doses.\nTamoxifen Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of tamoxifen,\nInject reference solution (b). In the chromatogram obtained\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nC26H29N0.\nwith reference solution (b) a peak due to E-isomer appears \nimmediately following the peak due to Z-tamoxifen. Adjust the \nsensitivity of the instrument so that the height of the peak\nUsual strengths. The equivalent of 10 mg, 20 mg and 40 mg of \ntamoxifen."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tamoxifen-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tamoxifen Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tamoxifen Citrate\nCisHjgNiACgHgOv < \n. MoL Wt<563.6\nTamoxifenCitrate is(Z)-2-[4--(l,2-cliphenylbut-l-enyl)- >\nl-phenoxy]ethyldimethylaminc citrate, ■' . jrucuuj? X\nTamoxifen Citrate contains riot less than 99.0 per Cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C26H29NO, C6II8O7, calculated oh \nthe dried basis. \n'\nCategory. Antioestrogen.\nDescription; A white or almost white,/crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tamsulosin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tamsulosin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tamsulosin Hydrochloride\n, Hel\nCzOHZ8NzOsS,HCL_..\n_ .._._\n_MoLWt.A45.0.\nTamsulosin Hydrochloride is (R)..5..(2 ([2\"(0\"ethoxyphenoxy)\nethyl]aminopropyl)-2-methoxybenzenesulfonamide\nhydrochloride.\nIP 2010\nTamsulosin Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per\ncent and not more than 102.0 per cent of CzoHz8NzOsS,HCI,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. Awhite to offwhite powder.\nDose. 400 to 800 Jlg daily."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tartaric-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tartaric Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tartaric Acid\nC ¿.Tartaric Acid\n- H OH ,\n: h 0H\nHOOC-V000«\nC,HA \nMol. Wt. 150.1\nTartaric Acid is (27?,37?)-2,3-dihydroxybutanedioic acid.\nTartaric Acid contains not less than 99.5 per cent and not \nfliore than 1'01.0 per cent of C4H6O6, calculated on the dried \nbasis>-:; \n.:••••. \n■\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (buffering agent).\nDescription, Colourless crystals or a white or almost white \n'crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tazobactam-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tazobactam Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tazobactam Sodium\nCkjHj 1N4NaOsS \nMol. Wt. 322.3\nTazobactam Sodium is Sodium (2S,3 S,5R)-3-methyl-7-oxo-3T \n(1H- 1,2,3-triazol-1 -ylmethyl)-4-thia-1 -azabicyclp[3.2.0] \nheptane-2-carboxylicacid,4,4-dioxide. \n. \n.\nTazobactam Sodium contains not less than 98.0, per cent and \nnot more than 102.0 per cent of .CioHnN4Na05S, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. ’\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor.\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline hygroscopic \npowder. \n,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tazobactam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tazobactam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tazobactam\nC10H!2N4O5S . \nMol. Wt. 300.3\nClrtH12N;O5S,‘/2H2O ’ \nMoI.Wt.309T\nTazobactam is (2S,35,5/U-3-mcthyh7-oxo-3-(1//-l,2,3-triazok\nl-ylmethyl)-4“thia-l-azabicyclo[3.2.0]heptane-2-carboxylic \nacid,4,4-dioxide hemihydrate. \n. \n...\nTazobactam contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C|0Hi2N4OsS, calculated?bh\"the \nanhydrous basis. \n''•V.”.\";’. :\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor. \n■ ■F ' 4\nDescription. A white to pale;yellow, non-hygroscopic, \ncrystalline powder. ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/teicoplanin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Teicoplanin Injection","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Teicoplanin Injection\nThe area of any secondary peak corresponding to teicoplanin\nTeicoplanin Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nTeicoplanin with or without auxiliary substances. It is filled in \na sealed container.\nA2 group is not less than 80.0 per cent; teicoplanin A2.2 group \nis not less than 35.0 per cent and not more than 55.0 per cent. \nTeicoplanin A2.1 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.3 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent, teicoplanin A24 group is not more than 20.0 per cent, \nteicoplanin teicoplanin A2.5 group is not more than 20.0 per \ncent and teicoplanin A3 group is not more than 15.0 per cent. \nThe sum of the areas of all other secondary peak other than \nmesityl oxide with a relative retention time 1.25 times is not \nmore than 5.0 per cent. Ignore the peak due to teicoplanin\n1.25. \nCalculate the content of the different components using the \nfollowing equations.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of Sterile Water for \nInjections, immediately before use.\nteicoplanin A 2 group = \t\nx 100 \nS. \nS. + 0.83 x S„ + S,\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nS. + 0.83 x Si, + S, \n\t\nx100 \nS 2\nteicoplanin A 2_,\nA2-2 with an area less than 0.5 times of the principal peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution (b) (0.25 \nper cent).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nSi \n\tx 100 \nteicoplanin A,_, group = S. + 0.83 \nS b\nImpurity A. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nTeicoplanin Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nTeicoplanin.\nUse the Chromatographic system as described under test for \nComposition and related substances.\nteicoplanin A 23 group = \nS3 \n\tX 100\nS a + 0.83 x S, + S c\nName\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations (Powders\nRelative \nretention time\nS4 \n\t\nX 100 \nteicoplanin A 2_4\nSa + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nfor Injection) and with the following requirements.\nTeicoplann impurity A' \t\n.0.6 \nTeicoplann A2_2 (Retention time: about 18 minutes) 1.0-\nU.suil..Strerigths. 200 mg; 400 mg.\nteicoplanin A 2_, group = \t\n \nS5 \t\nx 100 \nS. + 0.83 x St, + Sc\nDesCription. A white or off white to yellowish powder.\n'4-methylpent-3-en-2-one  (mesityl oxide).\n- \t\n\"\n33-17\nTEICOPLANIN INJECTION"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/teicoplanin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Teicoplanin"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Teicoplanin\nwater (2.3.43). Not more than 0.6 per cent for anhydrous form \nand between 2.2 per cent to 3.8 per cent for hemihydrate form.\nCategory. Beta-lactamase inhibitor.\nMicrobial contamination  (2.2.9). The total viable aerobic count \ndoes not exceed 103  cfit per g and the total combined moulds \nand yeasts count does not exceed 10 2 cfu per g.\nHO \t\n0 \nOH OH \nOH\nDescription. A white to pale yellow, non-hygrosc \ncrystalline powder.\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nOH"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/telmisartan-and-amlodipine-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Telmisartan and Amlodipine Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Telmisartan and Amlodipine Tablets\nTel m i s ar t an an d Anil o d ipi ne B e s y 1 at e Tablets • \nTelmisartan and Amlodipine Besilate Tablets >, < \n7\nTelmisartan and Amlodipine Tablets contains riot less:ihah \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of telmisartan, \namlodipii^\nQoH^ClNoQ,\nUsual strengths. Telmisartan, 40 mg and amlodipine, 5 mg; \nTelmisartan, 80 mg and amlodipine, 10 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/telmisartan","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Telmisartan"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Telmisartan\nMol. Wt. 514.6\nTelmisartan is 4' -([4-methyl-6-(1-methyl-lH-benzimidazol-2-\nyl)-2-propyl-lH-benzimidazol-l-yl]methyl}-2-\nbiphenylcarboxylic acid.\nTelmisartan contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent of C33H30N40z, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDose. 40 mg once a day.\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/temozolomide-capsules","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Temozolomide Capsules"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Temozolomide Capsules\n0\n95\n5\nCI9H301•1501oP,C41-1404 \t\nMol. Wt. 635.5\nTemozolomide Capsules contain not less than 90.0  Ai \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amo \ntemozolomide, C6H6N602.\n10\n50\n50\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate salt of bis( isopropyloxy-\ncarbonyloxymethyl ester of (R)-9-(2-phosphonomethoxy-\npropyl)adenine with fumaric acid.\n25\n50\n50\n50\n20\nCAUTION -Temozolomide is cytotoxic; extra care \nto prevent inhaling particles and exposing the skin to'\n80\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate contain not less than 97.0 per \ncent and not more than 107 (•) 0 \n. \t\nper cent of CI9H30N5010P,C4H404., \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\n60\n95\n5\nUsual strengths. 20 mg; 100 mg; 250 mg.\n65\n95\n5\nCategory. Antiviral.\nInject reference solution (a). The test is not valid unless the \ntailing factor is not more than 2.0 and the column efficiency in \nnot less than 2000 theoretical plates."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/temozolomide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Temozolomide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Temozolomide Injection\nTemozolomide Injection is a sterile, lyophilized material \nconsisting of Temozolomide with of without excipients. It is \nfilled in a sealed container.\nTemozolomide Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntemozolomide,C6H()N6O2. -■ ? \n■\nNOTE — Temozolomide is cytotoxic; extra care required to \nprevent inhaling particles and exposing thé 'skin to it.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of:solution, and Particulate matter stated, under \nParenteral Preparations. (Injections). .\nUsual strength. 100 mg. \n. ; P \n, b\n3734\nStorage^ The reconstituted solution stand atroomtempei \nmust be used within 14 frours, including infusion time.\nThe content of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparation (Poyfo \nfor Injection) and with the following requirement. ' ' 'x"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/temozolomide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Temozolomide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Temozolomide\no\nC;,H6N6O2 \nMol. Wt 194.2\nTemozolomide is 3,4-dihydro-3-mcthyl-4-oxoimidazo[5,l-i/]-\n1,2,3,5-tetrazine-8-carboxamide.\nTemozolomide contains not less than 98.0 per centand not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C6H6N6O2, calculated on anhydrous \nbasis. \n!\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A white to light pink or light tan powder.\nNOTE— Temozolomide is cytotoxic; extra care required to \nprevent inhaling particles and exposing the skin to it. y"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tenofovir-disoproxil-fumarate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate\nHXGaaH\n,\nI\nHaaG\nH\nMol. Wt. 635.5\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate salt ofbis(isopropyloxy-\ncarbonyloxymethyl ester of(R)-9-(2-phosphonomethoxy-\npropyl)adenine with fumaric acid.\nTenofovir Disoproxil Fumarate contain not less than 97.0 per\ncent and not more than 102. 0 per cent ofCI9H30N501OP,C4~04,\nc!ll~lll_at~~ on tl1eanb:ydr()us basis.\nCategory.Antiviral.\nDose. 300 mg daily.\nDescription. A white to off- white crystalline powder.\n2188\nIP 2010\nTENOFOVIR DISOPROXIL FUMARATE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tenoxicam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tenoxicam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tenoxicam\nTenoxicam is 4-Hydroxy-2-methyl-N-(pyridin-2-yl)-2H-\nIhieno [2,3 -e] 1,2-thiazine-3-carboxamide 1,1 -dioxide.\nTenoxicam contains not less than 9.9.0 per cent and not more \nthan 10Lb per cent of C^HuNat^Si, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory.Analgesic; anti inflammatory.\nDescription. A yellow crystalline powder. It shows \npolymorphism(2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terazosin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terazosin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terazosin Hydrochloride\nTerazosin Hydrochloride Dihydrate\nCl9H25N5O4,HCl,2H2O ; e , Mol. Wt. 459.9\nTerazosin. Hydrochloride is (7?5')-6,7-dimethoxy-2-[4- \n(tetrahydrofpran-2-carbonyl)piperazin“ 1-yl) quinazolin-4- \nylamine hydrochloride dihydrate.\nTerazosin Hydrochloride contains not less than’99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C19H25N5O4,HC1, calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow, crystalline powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with terazosin \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of terazosin \nhydrochloride.\nB. It gives reaction (A) of chlorides (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. Al. 0 per cent w/v solution in carbon­\ndioxide free water is clear (2.4.1) and not more intensely \ncoloured thanreference solution YS7 (2;4.1). - ' ■ ’ ■\npH (2.4.24). 3.0 to 5.0, determined in 2.0 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon-dioxide free water. \n.\nImpurities N and O. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n; \n? \n'\"'7 \n•'\nSolvent mixture. 20 volumes of acetonitrile, and 80-volumes \noiyvater.,.. . .. ,.=:. rc..,, =\nTest solution. - Dissolve\" 501 mgof the - substance under \nexamination in 50 ml of the solvent mixture. ■ ■ . '\nReference solution (a). Dissolve'5 mg each 'of2-chloro-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-4-amine (terazosin impurity A iP'RS)\nand-. A-[[(2RS)~tetrahydr,ofuran-2-yl] carbonyl]piperazine \n1PRS (terazosin impurity NJIPRS in acetonitrile, add 5 ml of \nthe test solution and dilute to 50 ml with acetonitrile. Dilute\n10.0 ml of the solution to 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\n3753\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTERAZOSIN HYDROCHLORIDE\nReference solution (b). Dilutei 0.0 ml of reference solution (a) \nto 100.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.0 mm, packed with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- mobile phase: dissolve 2.8 g of sodium laurylsulphate\nin 1000 ml ofwater and add 11 ini ofa solution containing \n20.24 per cent w/v of triethylàminè and 23.0 per cent \nw/v of orthophosphoric acid and adjusted to pH 2.5 \nwith orthophosphoric acid', mix 60 volumes of this \nsolution with 40 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1.0 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 210 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference soldi ion (b). The relative retention time \nwith reference to terazosin for l,4-bis[(tctrahydrofuran- \n2-yl)carbonyl]piperazine (terazosin impurity O) is about 0.2, \nfor terazosin impurity N. is about 0.3 and for terazosin impurity \nA is about 0.4.;The test is not valid unless the resolution \nbetween the peaks due to terazosin impurity A and N is not \nless than 1.5.\nInject reference solution (b) and thè test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution the area \nof each peak corresponding to terazosin impurity N and \nterazosin impurity O is not more than the area of the peak due \nto terazosin in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nsolution(b)(0.1 percent). ■. m «\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14). \n. . • . i- ; :■■■■'■ \n,-\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in 100 ml of the mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 2.0 ml of the test solution to\n100 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 5,0 ml of the solution to \n100 ml with the mobile phase.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve the contents of a vial of\nterazosin, far system suitability IPRS (containing, terazosin \nimpurity À, terazosin impurity B (l-(4-hydroxy-6,7- \ndimethoxyquinazolin-2-yl)-4-[[(2R5)-tetrahydrofuran-2-yl] \ncafbonÿl]piperazirie)', téràzosin impurity C (6,7-dimethoxy-2- \n(piperazin-1 -yl)quinazolin-4-amine), terazosin impurity I f D(4- \namino-6,7-dimcthoxyquinazolin-2-yl)-4-[(2/riS')-2-hydroxy- \npentanoyl]piperazine), terazosin impurity K (prazosin) and \nterazosin impurity M (1,4-bis(f’"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terbinafine-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terbinafine Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terbinafine Cream\nTerbinafine Hydrochloride Cream\nTerbinafine Cream contains ribt less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot morethan 110.0 pet cent of the stated amount of terbinafine \nhydrochloride, C21H25N,HC1 in a suitable cream base:\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terbutaline-inhalation","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terbutaline Inhalation"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terbutaline Inhalation\nTerbutaline Sulphate Inhalation; Terbutaline Inhalation \nAerosol (Terbutaline Sulphate Inhalation Aerosol\nTerbutaline Inhalation is a suspension of Terbutaline Sulphate,\näs^a' superfine powder, in a suitable liquid in a suitable \npressurised container. It may contain suitable pharmaceutical \naids such as-surfactants, stabilising agents, etc. '\nTerbutaline Inhalation delivers riot less than 75.0 per cent and\nnot more than 125.0 per cent of the stated amount of terbutaline \n»>>Ptate.(C12H19NO3)2,H2SO4, per inhalation, by actuation of \nthe valve.\nMsual strength. 250 pg in each metered dose."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terbutaline-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terbutaline Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terbutaline Injection\nTerbutaline Sulphate Injection\nTerbutaline Injection is a sterile solution of Terbutaline\nSulphate in Water for Injections.\nTerbutaline Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofterbutaline\nsulphate (CI2HI9N03)2, H2S04•\nUsual strength. 500 Ilg per m!."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/terbutaline-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Terbutaline Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Terbutaline Sulphate\n(Cï2H!9NO3)2,H2SO4 ' \n■ Mol. Wt. 548.7\nTerbutaline Sulphate is {^S)-2-(iert--bûtÿlamiiio)“l\"(3,5-\ndihydroxyphenyl)ethanol sulphate.\nTerbutaline Sulphate contains hot less than 98.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of'(C^Hj^Ô^, H2SO4, calculated \non the dried basis! \n/\nCategory. Beta-adrenoceptor agonist. \n>\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.: :"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/teriparatide-concentrateci-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Teriparatide Concentrateci Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Allergen products are. pharmaceutical preparations derived \nfrom extracts of naturally occurring source materials containing \nallergens, which are substances that lead to and/ or provoke \nallergic reactions. The allergenic components are most often \nof a proteinaceous nature. Allergen products are intended for \nin vivo diagnosis or treatment of allergic diseases attributed \nto these allergens.\nAllergen products are available as finished products and as \nfinished products used as customized therapeutic \npreparations. Allergen products are generally presented as \nparenteral preparations, eye preparations, preparations for \ninhalation, preparations for oral use, sublingual preparations \nor preparations for skin tests.\nFor diagnostic use, allergen products are usually prepared as \nunmodified extracts in a 50 per cent V/V solution of glycerol \nfor skin-prick testing. For intradermal diagnosis,or for \nprovocation tests by nasal, ocular or bronchial administration, \nsuitable dilutions of allergen products may be prepared by \ndilution of aqueous or glycerinated extracts, or by \nreconstitution of unmodified freeze-dried extracts.\nFor specific immunotherapy, allergen products are usually \nunmodified extracts. ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tesfs","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tesfs"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tesfs \n. ■> --wm v- ■\nCategory. Antimigraine.\nWater (2.3.43). 5.0 to 8.0 per cent, determined on l.O gina I \nmixture of equal volumes of acetic acid and formamide^ I\npH (2.4.24), 4.5 to 7.0, determined in a solution constituted as\nDescription. A white to cream, crystalline powder.\ndirected on the label,, in Water for Inj ections.. : : \n<"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/testosterone-propionate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Testosterone Propionate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Testosterone Propionate Injection\nTestosterone Propionate Injection is a sterile solution of \nTestosterone Propionate in Ethyl Oleate or any ¿other spitable \nester, in a suitable fixed oil or in any mixture of these. .. ;;\nTestosterone Propionate Injection; contains not less than \n92.5 per cent and not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated \namount of testosterone propionate,\n■Nff f; ■ , ■' {■ ('f: ’ : : ‘'.p' i ’•?’ T . .-'a ’;. : \n' • • • •': . 7 '«•.• ' • - Hi'.' \n'\nUsual strength. 25 mg per • ml; 50 mg per ml. \n,; n ji a;inl\nwww.webofpharma.com\nnlÉìvea volunic containing 50 mg of Testosterone Propionate\n(40?t0 60?) and extract with three \n-;|g|ssl||g^ieach of 8 ml, of a mixture of 7 volumes of glacial\n$^A^v# and 3 volumes of water. Wash the combined \n■ with 10 mJof light petroleum (40° to 6(f), dilute with\nthe; solution becomes turbid,, allow to stand for \nm ice and filter. The precipitate, after washing with\nat 105°, complies with the following tests.\nT^^rrnlne by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\nrapare the ¡spectrum with that obtained with testosterone \n^^PnatedPRS or with the reference spectrum of\nl^tetone propionate. \nc \n;\ng-ffétéfinine by thin-layer chromatography (2:4.17), coating \n^IJt^ithsihhtìfgefG. \n;\nSotyent mixture. A mixture of .90 volumes of light petroleum\nan<^ 1 $ v°himes of liquid paraffin...\nMobile phase. A mixture of 30 volumes of water and \n¿Oyp.lumes of glacial acetic acid..\nfesfs&l&ioh; Dissolve 25 mg of the residue in 10 ml of the \nsolvent mixture. ;\nReference solution (a). A 0.25' per cent w/v solution ,of \ntestosterone propionate IPRS in the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Mix equal volumes of the test solution \nand reference solution (a). \n- -\nPlace the dry plate in a tank containing a shallow layer of the \nsolvent mixture, allow the solvent mixture to ascend to the \ntop, remove the plate from. the. tank and. allow the solvent to \nevaporate. Use within 2 hours, with the flow of the mobile \nphase in the direction in which the aforementioned treatment \nwas done.\nApply tfr the plate 2 p.1 of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to fise 12 cm. Dry the plate in a current of warm air, allow \nthe solvent to evaporate, heat at 120° for d 5 minutes and spray \nthe hot plate with ethanolic sulphuric acid (20 per cent v/v). \nHeat at 120° for a further 10 minutes, allow to cool and examine \nin daylight and under ultraviolet light at 365 nm. The principal \nspot in the chromatogram obtained with the test solution \ncpnespqnds ,to that in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (a), The principal spot in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (b) appears as a single,\ncompact spot A.\nTests . \n............\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral\npreparations (Injections).\nAssay. To an measured volume containing about Ò.T g of \nTestosterone Propionate add sufficient chloroform to produce"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/testosterone-propionate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Testosterone Propionate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Testosterone Propionate\no\n~2H3203\nMol. Wt. 344.5\nTestosterone Propionate is 3-oxoandrost-4-en-17~-yl\npropionate.\nTestosterone Propionate contains not less than 97.0 per cent\nand not more than 103.0. per cent ofC22H320 3, calculated on\nthe dried basis.\nCategory. Androgen; anabolic steroid.\nDose. By intramuscular injection, 5 to 25 mg, once or twice\nweeldy.\n2199\nTESTOSTERONE PROPIONATE\nDescription. A white or almost white powder or colourless\ncrystals; odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tetanus-antitoxin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tetanus Antitoxin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tetanus Antitoxin\nTetanus Antitoxin is a preparation containing the specific \nantitoxic globulins or their derivatives obtained by purification \nof hyperimmune serum of horses or other suitable animals \nand have the specific activity of neutralising the toxin formed \nby Clostridium tetani. The liquid preparation.may contain a \nsuitable antimicrobial preservative. \n./\nTetanus Antitoxin has a potency of not less than 1000 Units \nper ml when intended for prophylactic use and not less thap \n3000 Units per ml when intended for therapeutic use. . ..\nDescription. A clear, colourless or pale yellow liquid or a freeze- \ndried, cream-coloured powder or pellet."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tetracycline-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tetracycline Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tetracycline Hydrochloride\n, Hel\nMol. Wt. 480.9\nTetracycline Hydrochloride is (4S,4aS,5aS,6S,12aS)-\n4- dimethylamino-1 ,4,4a,5,5a,6,11,12a-octahydro-\n3,6,10,12,12a-pentahydroxy-6-methyl-1,11-dioxo-\nnaphthacene-2- carboxamide hydrochloride.\nTetracycline Hydrochloride contains not less than 95.0 per\ncent and not more than 100.5 per cent of CzzHz4NzOs, HCI,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDose. Orally, 250 mg every 6 hOUTS, increased in severe\ninfections to 500 mg every 6 to 8 hOUTS; by intramuscular\ninjection, 100 mg every 8 to 12 hOUTS, increased in severe\ninfections to every 4 to 6 hOUTS; by intravenous infusion, 500\nmg every 12 hOUTS; maximum 2 g daily.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tetracycline-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tetracycline Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tetracycline Ointment\nY\"' r‘.\"'■ ■ \n>■ \n■\nTefracycline Hydrochloride Eye Ointment\nTetracycline Ointment contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntetracycline hydrochloride, C22H24N2O8, HC1.\nUsual strengths: 0.5 per cent w/w; 1 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tetracycline","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tetracycline"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tetracycline\nCaH24NA .7.:. -. . \nMol.Wt444.5\nTetracycline is (4S,4aS,SoiSfiS, 12aS)-4-dimethylamino-\n1,4,4a,S,5 a,6,1-1,12a-octahydro-3,6, 10,12,12a-pentahydroxy- \n6-methyH, 11 -dioxonaphthacene-2-carboxamide. It contains \na variable quantity of water;\nTetracycline contains not less than 88.0 per cent of CAb-AfCf, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tetramethylene-phosphonate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tetramethylene Phosphonate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tetramethylene Phosphonate \n(EDTMP) Injection\nCategory. Therapeutic.\nDescription. A clear, colourless to pale yellow, solution. \nSamarium (,53Sm) Ethylene Diamine Tetramethylene \nPhosphonate Injection is a sterile solution and contains not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated Samarium-153 radioactivity at the date and time stated\n4749\nwww.webofpharma.com\non the label.-Not less than 95 per cent of the radioactivity \ncorresponds to sanianurn-1.53 in the form of Samarium-EDTMP. \nUse within three days from the reference date."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/theophylline-and-ethylenediamine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Theophylline and Ethylenediamine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Theophylline and Ethylenediamine\n(CWWi,C2H8N2 \nMol. Wt. 420.4 (anhydrous)\nAminophylline is a stable mixture or combination of \ntheophylline and ethylenediamine. It may be anhydrous or \nmay contain not more than two molecules of water of \nhydration.\nAminophylline contains the equivalent of not less than\n84.0 per cent and not more than 87.4 per cent of theophylline, \nC7HgN4O2, and the; equivalent of not less than 13:5 per cent \nand not more than 15.0 per cent of ethylenediamine, C2H^N2,' \nboth calculated on the. anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Bronchodilator.\nDescription. A white or slightly yellowish granules or powder. \nOn exposure to air it gradually loses ethylenediamine and \nabsorbs carbon dioxide with liberation of free theophylline. \nEven in the absence of light, it is gradually decomposed on \nexposure to a humid environment, the degradation being faster \nat higher temperatures;' \n'"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/theophylline-in-dextrose-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Theophylline in  Dextrose Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Theophylline in  Dextrose Injection\nChromatographic system\nTheophylline Injection is a sterile solution of Theophylline \nand Dextrose in Water for Injections.\nMol. Wt. 180.2 (anhydrous)\nC71-18N402\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4 mm, packed with\nMol. Wt. 198.2 (hydrate)\n C4101402,H20\noctadecylsilane bonded to  porous silk # \t - \n- mobile phase: a mixture of 7  volumectfretcetoilitrile\nThe,epifylline -Thjection contains not less than 92.5 per cent \nand not more Than  107.5 per cent of the stated amount of \nanhydrous theophylline, CA-I gN402, and not less than 95.0 per\nThe4byllint is 1,3-dimethy1-3,7-dihydro-1H-purine-2,\n 6-dione. It contains one molecule of water or is \n anhydrous.\nand 93 volumes of 0.14 per cent w/v soltition of sodium\n33-49\ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ndextrose, C6H,,06, F1,0.\nUsual strengths. Anhydrous theophylline, 0.4, 0.8, 1.6, 2, 3.2, \n4 mg per ml in 5 per cent w/v Dextrose."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/theophylline-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Theophylline Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Theophylline Injection\nTheophylline in Dextrose Injection\nTheophylline Injection is a sterile solution of Theophyllin0 \nand Dextrose in Water for Injections.\nTheophylline Injection contains not less than 92.5 per cent \nand not more than 107.5 per cent ofthe stated amount of \nanhydrous theophylline, CLHSN4O2, and not less than 95.0per \ncent and not more than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount of\ndextrose, CßH^OßjHjO. \n, ;;\nwww.webofpharma.com\nlp2022^^\n(jjyaBtrengths- Anhydroustheophylline, 0.4,0;8,1.6,2,3.2, \n4 ^gperml in5 percent w/v Dextrose.\niSnfification\nA When examined in the range 230 nm to 3 60 nm (2.4.7), the \nsolution obtained in the Assay for theophylline shows an \nabsorption maximum at about 274 nm.\nB Add 0.2 ml of the injection to 5 ml of potassium cupri­\nfartrate solution and heat to boiling; a red to orange precipitate\nisformed."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/theophylline","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Theophylline"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Theophylline\nC7H8N4O2 \nMol. Wt. 180.2 (anhydrous)\nMol. Wt. 198.2 (hydrate)\nTheophylline is 1,3-dimethyl-3,7-dihydro-l/Z-purine-2, \n6-dione. It contains one molecule of water or is anhydrous.\nTheophylline contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C7H8N4O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Xanthine bronchodilator.\nDescription: Awhite, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiabendazole-premix","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiabendazole Premix"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiabendazole Premix\nThiabendazole Premix contains not less than 95.0 per cent and\nnot more than 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nthiabendazole, C IOH7N3S.\ncentw/w.\n•....................."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiacetazone-and-isoniazid-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiacetazone and Isoniazid Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiacetazone and Isoniazid Tablets\nThiacetazone and Isoniazid Tablets contain one part by weight \nof Thiacetazone and two parts by weight of Isoniazid.\nThiacetazone and Isoniazid Tablets contain not less than \n92.5 per cent and not more than 107.5 per cent of the stated \namounts of thiacetazone, Ci0Hi2N4OS, and isoniazid, C6H7N3O2-\nCategory. Antitubercular.\nUsualstrengths. Thiacetazone, 37.5 mg and Isoniazid, 75 mg; \nThiacetazone, 75 mg and Isoniazid, 150 mg; Thiacetazone,\n150 mg and Isoniazid, 300 mg.\n3774\nb f h\nIP 20^\nTHIACETAZONE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiacetazone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiacetazone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiacetazone\nC10Hl2N4OS \nMol. Wt. 236.3\nThiacetazone is 4-acetamidobenzaldehyde thiosemicarbazone.\nThiacetazone contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of CWH12N4OS, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCategory. Antitubercular.\nDescription: Pale yellow crystals or a crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiamine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiamine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiamine Hydrochloride\nAneurine Hydrochloride; Vitamin B 1\n-\nCI, HCI\nC1zH17ClN40S, HCl\nMol. Wt. 337.3\nThiamine Hydrochloride is 3-[(4-amino-2-methylpyrimidin-\n5-yl)methyl]-5-(2-hydroxyethyl)-4-methylthiazolium chloride\nhydrochloride.\nThiamine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 per cent\nand not more than 101.5 per cent of C12H 17CIN40S, HCl,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. B-group vitamin.\nDose. Prophylactic, orally, 2 to 5 mg once daily; therapeutic,\norally or by subcutaneous or intramuscular injection, 25 to\n100 mg daily. Inmultivitaminpreparations, prophylactic, orally,\nI to 2 mg daily; therapeutic, orally, 4.5 to 10 mg daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or\nsmall colourless crystals; odour, slight and characteristic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiamine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiamine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiamine Injection\nThiamirie Hydrochloride Injection; Aneurine Hydro­\nchloride Inj ection; Vitamin B! Injection\nThiärmW Injection is a sterile solution of Thiamine Hydro­\nchloride in Water for Injection.\nThiamine Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and \nnotmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of thiamine \nhydrochloride, C12H17CIN4OS,HC1.\nUsual strength. 100 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiamine-nitrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiamine Nitrate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiamine Nitrate\nClzH17Ns04S\nMol. Wt. 327.4\nThiamine Mononitrate is 3-[(4-amino-2-methylpyrimidin-\n5-yl)methyl]-5-(2-hydroxyethyl)-4-methylthiazolium nitrate.\nThiamine Mononitrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent\nand not more than 102.0 per cent of ClzHI7Ns04S, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. B-group vitamin.\nDose. Prophylactic, 2 to 5 mg daily; therapeutic, 25 to 100 mg\ndaily. In multivitamin oral preparations, prophylactic,\n1 to 2 mg daily; therapeutic, 4.5 to 10 mg daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or\nsmall, colourless crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thimcrosal","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thimcrosal"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thimcrosal \nf\n: \nj^^/COONa\nCgH9HgNaO2S \nMol. Wt. 404.8 I\nThiomersal is, the sodium salt of-f(2-carboxyphenyl)thio] I \nethylmercury...'.- ' \n............. - |\nThiomersal contains not lessthan 97 .0 per cent and hot more I\nthan 101.0 per cent of C9H9HgNaO2S, calculated onthedried | \nbasis. \n... \n... ....... . I\nCategory. Antiseptic; pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial I \npreservative).;.,,. \n; \n■ \n1\nDescription. A light cream, crystalline powder. \nI"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thimerosal","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thimerosal"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thimerosal\nSHgCH2CH3\n~COONa\nV\nC9H9HgNa02S\nMol. Wt. 404.8\nThiomersal is the sodium salt of[(2-carboxyphenyl)thio]\nethylmercury.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTHIOMERSAL\nThiomersal contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent ofC9H9HgNaOzS, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Antiseptic; pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial\npreservative).\nDescription. A light cream, crystalline powder; odour, slight\nand characteristic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thioglycerol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thioglycerol"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thioglycerol\nOH \nHO \tSH\nC3H802S \t\nMol. Wt. 108.2\nMonothioglycerol is 3-mercaptopropane-1,2-diol.\nMonothioglycerol contains not less than 97.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C 3H,,O2S, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A colourless to pale yellow, viscous liquid; odour \nresembling that of sulphides; hygroscopic.\nMOMETASONE OINTMENT \nIP 2018 \nIP 2018 \nMONTELUKAST GRANULES\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nobtained with reference solution (b) (0.3 per cent). The sum of \nareas of all the secondary peaks is not more than 3.3 times the \narea of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained with \nreference solution (b) (1.0 per cent)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thioguanine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thioguanine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thioguanine\nC5H5NsS (anhydrous)\nMol.Wt. 167.2\nMol.Wt. 176.2\nThioguanine is 6H -Purine-6-thione,2-amino-l,7-dihydro; 2- \nAnunopurine-6(1H )-thione hemihydrate.\nThioguanine is anhydrous or contains one-half molecule of \nwater of hydration. It contains not less than 96.0 per cent and \nnot more than 100.5 per cent of C5H5N5S, calculated on the \ndried basis.\nCAUTION— Thioguanine is potentially cytotoxic; Great \ncare should be taken in handling the powder and preparing \nthe solutions.\nCategory. Cytotoxic. .\nDescription. A pale yellow, crystalline powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with thioguanine \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of thioguanine.\n3777\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTHIOGUANINE\nB. Transfer about 100 mg of ofthe substance under examination, \npreviously dried, to a 100.0 ml volumetric flask. Dissolve in a \nmixture of 15 ml of water and 1.5 ml of / Msodium hydroxide, \ndilutewith water to volume/and?mix. Transfer 1.0 ml ofthe \nsolution to a 200.0-ml volumetric flask and add 1 per cent v/v \nsolution of hydrochloric acid to volume, and mix. The UV \nabsorption spectrum (2.4,7) of the solution so obtained, \nexhibits maxima and minima at the same wavelengths as that \nobtained with thioguanine IPRS treated in the same manner.\nTests.\nSulphur compounds. Dissolve 50 mg of thioguanine in 5.0 ml \nof 1 M'sodium hydroxide; the resulting solution obtained is \nclear.\nInorganic phosphate. Not more than 0.03 per cent as \nphosphate, determined by the following method.\nAmmonium molybdate solution. Dissolve 8,3 gof ammonium\nmolybdate in 40.0 ml of water, add 33.0 ml cd dilute sulphuric \nacid and dilute with water to, 100.0 ml.\nNOTE— This solution is stable for about 2 weeks.\nWeigh 50.0 mg of the substance under examination to a large \ntest tube, add 1.0 ml of dilute sulphuric acid and heat in a \nwater bath for 5 minutes. Cautiously add nitric acid, dropwise, \ncontinue heating until the mixture becomes colourless, and \nthen heat for further one minute. Cool, dilute with water to\n10 ml, and transfer the solution to a 25.0 ml volumetric flask \nwith the aid of a few ml of water. To the flask add 0.75 ml of \nammonium molybdate solution and 1.0 ml of aminonaphthol- \nsulphonic acid, dilute with water to volume, mix and measure \nthe absorbance of the resulting solution at the maximum at \nabout 620 nm (2.4.7), using a blank solution prepared in the \nsame manner but omitting the substance under examination. \nThe absorbance is not greater than that produced by repeating \nthe operation using a. solution prepared in the same manner \nusing 1.5 ml of phosphate standard solution (lOppmPOJ.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14), as described under Assay with the following \nmodifications.\nTest solution (a). 0.04 per cent solution of the substance \nunder examination in 0.1 M sodium hydroxide.\nTest solution (b). Dilute 1.0 volume of test solution (a) to 10.0 \nvolumes with mobile phase.\nReference solution (a). A 0.004 per cent w/v solution\nof guanine IPRSin 0.0! Msodium hydroxide.\nReference solution (b). Dilute 1.0 volume of reference solution \n(a) to 100.0 volumes with mobile phase.\nReference solution (cf Transfer 1.0 ml of reference solution\n(a) into a 100.0 ml volumetric flask, and dilute with test \nsolution (b) to volume.\n3778\nIp 2°22 IO\nThe relative retention times are about 0.6 for guanine and 1 (j' W' i.- \nfor thioguanine respectively; -\nInject reference solution (b) and (c). The test is not valid unless $ I \nthe resolution between the peaks due to guanine and I \nthioguanine is not less than 3.0, in the chromatogram obtained 1\nwith reference solution (c). In the chromatograin obtained I \nwith reference solution (b), the tailing factor is not more than ■ I\n2.0 and the relative standard deviation for replicate injections I \nis not more than 5.0 per cent. \n■ I\nInject reference solution (b) and test solution (b). .In the I \nchromatogram obtained with test solution"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiopental-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiopental Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiopental Sodium\nMol. Wt. 264.3\nThiopentone Sodium is a mixture ofsodium (RS)-5-ethyl-\n5-(1-methylbutyl)-2-thiobarbiturate and anhydrous sodium\ncarbonate.\nThiopentone Sodium contains not less than 84.0 per cent and\nnot more than 87.0 per cent ofCjjHjsNzOzS and not less than\n10.2 per cent and not more than 11.2 per cent of Na, both\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. General anaesthetic.\nDose. By intravenous injection, initially, 100 to 500 mg, repeated\nifnecessary after 20 to 30 seconds; maximum dose, 2 g.'\nDescription. A yellowish white powder; odour, faintly\nresembling garlic; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiopentone-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiopentone Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiopentone Injection\nThiopentone Sodium Injection; Thiopental Injection\nThiopentone Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nThiopentone Sodium with or without auxiliary agents. It is\nfilled in a sealed container. \nA.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents ofthe \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInj ections, immediately before use.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nrkycdnstituted so^lilion complies with the requirements for \nx^jj^rpf solution and Particulate matter stated under"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiopentone-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiopentone Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiopentone Sodium\nThiopentalSodium\nTests.\nGnH17N2NaO2S \nMol. Wt. 264.3\nThiopentone Sodium is a mixture of sodium (7?S)-5-ethyl- \n5-(Dniethylbutyl),-2-thiobarbiturate and anhydrous sodium \ncarbonate. \n.,\nThiopentone Sodium contains not less than 84.0 per cent and \nnot more than 87.0 per cent of CnHigNjC^S and not less than\n10.2 per cent and not more than 11.2 per cent of Na, both \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. General anaesthetic.\nDescription. A yellowish white powder; hygroscopic.\nTest A may be omitted if tests B, C, D and E are carried out. \nTests B and D may be omitted if tests A, C and E are carried \nout.\nA. Acidify 10 ml of a 10 per cent w/v solution in carbon dioxide­\nfree 'water ;with!2''M'hydrochloric acid', the solution\neffervesces. Shake the solution with 20 ml of ether, separate \nthe ether layer, wash with 10 ml of water and dry. over \nanhydrous sodium sulphate. Filter, evaporate the filtrate to \ndryness and dry the residue at 105°.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared . absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4,6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with thiopentone IPRS or with the reference spectrum \nof thiopentone.\nB. Complies with the test for identification of barbiturates \n(2.3.2), but using the following solutions.\nTest solution. A 0.1 per cent w/v solution of the substance \nunder examination in water,\nReference solution. Dissolve 85 mg of thiopentone IPRS in\n10 ml of 2 Msodium hydroxide and dilute to 100 ml with water.\nC. Acidify 5 ml of a 5 per cent w/v solution with dilute acetic \nacid and filter. Wash the precipitate with water, recrystallise \nfrom w/er and dry .at 70°; the crystals melt at about ,160° \n(2.4.21).\nD. Dissolve 1 mg of the crystals obtained in test Ain 1 ml of\n0.1 M sodium 'hydroxide. Add about 1 mg of sodium \nnitroprusside and, after 15 minutes, 1 ml of dilute hydrochloric \nacid\\ a reddish violet colour is-produced.\nE. It gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. A 10.0 per cent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxide free water (solution A) is clear (2.4.1), and hot more \nintensely coloured than reference solution GYS3 (2.4.1).\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\n(2,4.14) \n.\nTest solution. Dissolve 20.0 mg of the substance under, \nexamination in the mobile phase and dilute to 20.0 ml with the \nmobile phase.\n3781\nwww.webofpharma.com\niXXj OUU1UM\nReference solution. .Dilute 1.0 ml of the test .solution to\n100.0 ml with the mobile phase. Dilute 5.0 ml ofthe solution to \n10.0 ml with the mobile phase\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nend-capped octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 \npm)\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 65 volumes of 0.1 per cent\nw/v solution of phosphoric acid and 35 volumes of \nacetonitrile,\n- flow rate: 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 225 rnn,\n- injection volume: 10 pl.\nAllow the chromatography to proceed for twice the retention \ntime of thiopental.\nName \nRelative Correction\nretention time factor\nThiopental impurity A1 \n0.3\n—\nThiopental impurity B2 \n0.4\n1.5\nThiopental impurity C3 \n0.9\n—\nThiopental (retention time about: \n20 minutes) \n1.0\nThiopental impurity D4 \n1.3\n*5-[(1-methylbutyl]-2-th ioxo-2,3-dihydropyfimid ine- \n4,6(lH,5fl)-dione, '■\n25-ethyl-5-[(17^-l-methylbutyl]pyrimidine-2J4,<5(l.fr,37/,5i0-trione,\n35-ethyl-5-(l-ethylpropyl)-2-thioxo-2,3-dihydropyrimidine- \n4,6(177,5tf)-dione,\n“mixture of (2-/?S,3-RS)'2-(carbainothioylcarbamoyl)-2-ethyl-3- \nmethylhexanoic; acid and (27?S,3S7?)-2-(carbamothioylcarbamoyl)-2- \nethyl-3-methyihexan.oic. acid.\nInject the reference solution. The test is not valid unless the \ncolumn efficiency is not less than 2000 theoretical plates and \nthe tailing factor is not more than 2.0.\nInject the reference solution and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution the area of any \npeak corresponding to thiopental impurity C is not more than \n6 times the area ofthe principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with refere"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiotepa-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiotepa Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiotepa Injection\nThiotepa Injection is a sterile material consisting of Thiotepa \nwith or without auxiliary agents. It is filled ina sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the\nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nInjections, immediatelybefore use. ;\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately \nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nThiotepa Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of thiotepa, \nC6III2N3PS.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations \n(Powders for Injection) and with the following requirements.\nUsual strength, 15 mg. ;\nDescription. A white powder.\nA. Determine on 20 mg by the oxygen-flask method (2.3.34), \nusing 5 ml of 1.25 M sodium hydroxide as the absorbing \nliquid. When the process is complete, dilute to 25 ml with \nwater (solution A). To 5 ml of solution A add 0.1 ml of hydrogen \nperoxide solution (100 vol) and 1 ml of 1 M hydrochloric\nacid, mix and add 0.05 ml of barium chloride solution', a \nturbidity is produced.\nB. To 2 ml of solutionAadd 40 ml of wafer and4 ml ofammonium \nmolybdate-sulphuric acid solution, mix, add 0.1. g of \nL-ascorbic acid and boil for 1 minute; a blue colour is \nproduced.\nAppearance of solution. Dissolve a quantity containing 15 mg \nof Thiotepa in 4 ml of water', the solution is clear (2.4.1).\npH (2.4.24). 5.5 to 7.5, determined in a 1.0 per cent w/v solution \nin carbon dioxide-free water.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nNOTE — Use freshly prepared solutions.\nTest solution. Dissolve a quantity of the contents of the sealed \ncontainer containing 15 mg of Thiotepa in 4 ml of water, filter.\nReference solution (a); Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with water. Further dilute 1.0 ml to 10.0 ml with water.\n3785\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTHIOTEPA INJECTION\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 10 mg of thiotepa IPRS in 2 \nml of methanol in a ground-glass-stoppered tube, add 50 pl \nof a 0.1 per cent v/v solution of orthophosphoric acid, stopper \nthe tube arid heat in a water-bath at 65° for 50 seconds \n(generates methoxy-thiotepa). Allow the solution to cool and \nadd1 ml of methanol.\nReference solution (c). Dissolve 15 mg of t/n'otepa 7?S in 10 ml\nof water, add 1 g of sodium chloride, boil in a water-bath for\n10 minutes and cool (generates chloro-adduct impurity).\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\nendcapped octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica \n(5 pm) (Such as Nucleosil C18),\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 15 volumes of acetonitrile\nand 85 volumes of 0.1M mixed phosphate buffer pH 7.0, \n- flowrate: 1 mlperminute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 215 nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nThe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) shows \na peak corresponding to methoxy-thiotepa with a retention \ntime relative to thiotepa of about 1.3 and the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) shows a peak \ncorresponding to the chloro- adduct impurity with a retention \ntime relative to thiotepa of about 3.1.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between the two principal peaks is not less than \n3.0. \n- ; \n-\nInject reference sQlutipn (a) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 4 times theretention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area \nof any peak corresponding to the chloro-adduct impurity is \nnot more than 1.5 times the area of the principal peak in the \nchromatograrri obtained with referehce solution (a) (0,15 per \ncent), the area of any other secondary peak is not more than \ntwice the area of the principal peak in the"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thiotepa","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thiotepa"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thiotepa\ns\n[:::N-~-N::J\nIN\nD\nC6HI2N3PS\nMol. Wt. 189.2\nThiotepa is tris(l-aziridinyl)phosphine sulphide.\nThiotepa contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent ofC6HI2N3PS, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDose. By injection, 300 to 400 ~g per kg of body weight at\nintervals of 1 to 4 weeks.\nDescription. Fine, white, crystalline flakes; odourless or almost\nodourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thymol","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thymol"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thymol\nMol. Wt. 150.2\nThymol is 2-isopropyl-5-methylphenol.\nThymol contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent ofC IOH I40.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (antimicrobial preservative;\nantiseptic).\nDescription. Colourless crystals; odour, characteristic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/thyroxine-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Thyroxine Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Thyroxine Sodium\nLevothyroxine Sodium; ¿-Thyroxine Sodium\nOH\nC isll ioltNNaOi,xI 1,0 \nMol. Wt. 798.9 (anhydrous)\nThyroxine Sodium is sodium </-(4-hydroxy-3,5-diiodo- \nphenyl)-3,5-diiodo-i-tyrosinate and contains a variable \nquantity of water of crystallisation^\nThyroxine Sodium contains not less than 97.0 per cent and \nnot more than 103.0 per cent of C15HioWMNa04, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Thyroid hormone.\nDescription. A white or slightly brownish yellow powder, \nslightly coloured, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tiabendazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tiabendazole"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tiabendazole\nCIOH7N3S\nMol. Wt. 201.3\nThiabendazole is 2-(1,3-thiazol-4-yl)-IH-benzimidazole.\nThiabendazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent of C IOH7N3S, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anthelmintic.\nDose. In the treatment of nematode infestation, 1.5 g twice\ndaily for three days.\nDescription. Awhite or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tibolone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tibolone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tibolone\nC21II28O2 \nMol. Wt.312.5\nTibolone is 17-hydrbxy-7 a-methyl-19-nor-17a-pregn-5( 10)-en- \n20-yn-3-one. \n: \n■\nTibolone contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of Co i \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Steroid with estrogenic- and progestogenic \nproperties.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncrystals. It shows polymorphism (2.5.11).\n3790\n» 2022 .\nTHYROXINE TABLETS"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ticagrelor","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ticagrelor"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ticagrelor\nCaH28F2N6O4S \nMol.WtS2U\nTicagrelor is 1,2-Cyclopentanediol, 3-[7-[[( I R,2S)-2-(3,4- \ndifluorophenyl) cyclopropyl] amino]-5-(propylthiq)-3H-U3- \ntriazolo[4,5-d] pyrimk!in-3y/l]-5-(2-hydroxyethoxy)-(l 5,2S,M\n55) \n. \n' \n’ ■■ ■ \n' \n;;\nTicagrelor contains not less than 97.5 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent ofC23H2SF2N6O4S, calculated on anhydrous \nbasis.\nCategory. Anti-platelet.\nDescription. A white or almost white to pale pink powder?"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tigecycline-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tigecycline Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tigecycline Injection\nTigecycline Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nTigecycline with or without excipients. It is filled in a sealed \ncontainer.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile, Water for \nInjections brother suitable, solvent,immediately before use.,\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nthe Clarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections). \n;\n3800\nI\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediat S \nafter preparation but, in any case,, within the pefjl \nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nTigecycline Injection contains not less than 96.0 per cent all \nnot more than 116.0 per cent of the stated amount^ \ntigecycline, C29l I39N5O8.\nUsual strength. 50 mg per vial.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with \nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparation \n(Powders for Injections) and with thefollowing requirement^"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/timolol-maleate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Timolol Maleate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Timolol Maleate\n(eOOH\n'~eOOH\nMol. Wt. 432.5\nTimolol Maleate is (S)-I-tert-butylamino-3-(4-morpholino-\n1,2,5-thiadiazol-3-yloxy)propan-2-01 hydrogen maleate.\nTimolol Maleate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent ofC13H24N403S,C4H404, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory. Beta-adrenoceptor antagonist (Antiglaucoma).\nDose. In hypertension, initially 5 mg twice daily or 10 mg once\ndaily; maximum 60 mg daily. In angina, initially 5 mg 2 to 3\ntimes daily; maintenance dose, 15 to 45 mg daily. For\nprophylaxis after infarction, initially 5 mg twice daily, started 1\nto 4 weeks after infarction. For migraine prophylaxis, 10 to 20\nmgdaily.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or\ncolourless crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tinidazole","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tinidazole"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tinidazole\nMol. Wt. 247.3\nTinidazole is 1-[2-(ethylsulphonyl)ethyl]-2-methyl-\n5-nitroimidazole.\nTinidazole contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 100.5 per cent of CSHI3N304S, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Antiprotozoal.\nDose. For trichomoniasis, 2 g as a single dose and repeat after\n3 to 5 days rest, ifnecessary. For giardiasis, 150mgtwice daily\nfor seven days or as a single dose of 2 g. For intestinal\namoebiasis, 600 mg as a single dose daily for 2 to 6 days. For\nextraintestinal amoebiais, 2 g as a single daily dose for 3 days.\nDescription. Pale yellow crystals or a crystalline powder;\nodour, slight and characteristic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tiotropium-bromide-monohydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tiotropium Bromide Monohydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tiotropium Bromide Monohydrate\nC19H22BrN04S2>H,O \nMol. Wt 490.4\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate is (lR,2R,4S,5S,ls)-1- \n[[Hydroxy[di(thiophen-2-yl)]acetyl]oxy]-9,9-dimethyl-3-oxa-9- \nazatricyclo[3.3.1,02’4]nonan-9-ium bromide monohydrate.\nTiotropium Bromide Monohydrate contains not less than 98.5 \nper cent and not more than 101.5 per cent of tiotropium \nbromide, C19H22NO4S2Br, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Bronchodilator.\nDescription. A white to off-whitepowder or crystals.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tiotropium \nbromide monohydrate IP RS or with the reference spectrum of \ntiotropium bromide monohydrate. \n■ -\nB.It gives reaction (a) of bromide (2.3.1).\nAppearance of solution. A1.0 per cent w/v solution is clear \n(2.4.1) and not more intensely coloured than reference solution \nYS6 (2.4.1).\nImpurities G and H. Determine by thin-layer chromatography \n(2.4.17), coating the plate with s ilica gel GF254.\nNOTE — Prepare the solutions immediately before use.\nSolvent mixture. Dilute 1 ml of 3Mhydrochloric acid to 100 \nml with methanol. \ni .... ,,\nMobile phase. A mixture of 10 volumes of water; 15 volumes of \nanhydrous formic acid, 35 volumes of acetonitrile and 50 \nvolumes of methylene chloride.\n3807\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTIOTROPIUM BROMIDE MONOHYDRATE\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.4 g of the substances under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 10.0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture. ,\nReference solution (a). Dissolve the contents of a vial of\ntiotropium impurity mixture IPRS (containing impurities G \nand H) in 1.0 ml of the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (b). Mix 0.1 ml of the test solution with 0.1 \nml of reference solution (a).\nApply to the plate 10 pl of the test solution and reference \nsolution (a), and 20 pl of reference solution (b). After \ndevelopment, dry the plate in air and expose to iodine vapors \ntill spots appear (about 15 minutes), remove the plate and \nexamine immediately. In the chromatogram obtained with the \ntest solution, any secondary spot corresponding to, each of, \ntiotropium impurity G and H is not more intense than the \ncorresponding spot of tiotropium impurity G and H in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a) (0.1 per \ncent). The test is not valid unless the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (b) shows three clearly separated spots, \nof each, tiotropium impurity G and H and tiotropium with \nRfvalues of about 0.33,0.38 and 0.64, respectively.\nRelated substances.Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2:4.14):\nNOTE — Carry out the test protected from light.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in mobile phase B and dilute to 25.0 ml with mobile \nphase B.\nReference solution (a). Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with mobile phase B. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution to \n10.0 ml with mobile phase B.\nReference solution (b). Dissolve 4 mg of tiotropium for system \nsuitability IPRS (containing impurity C) in 2.0 ml of mobile \nphase B.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 15 cm x 3.0 mm packed with\npropylsilane bonded to porous .silica (3.5 pm),\n- column temperature: 50°,\n- mobile phase A: dissolve 1.0 g of sodium methane­\nsulphonate and 5.0 g of potassium dihydrogen \nphosphate in 980 ml of water adjusted to pH 3.0 with\ndilute phosphoric acid and dilute to 1000 ml with water,\n. B: a mixture of 10 volumes of methanol, \n40 volumes of acetonitrile and 50 volumes of mobile\n' phase A, \n:\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below,\n— flow rate: 1.2 ml per minute,\n= 4 ; spectrophotometer set at 240 nm,.........................\n- injection volume: 5 pl. \n'\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobiie phase B\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v) ■\n■ (per cent v/v)4 J\n0\n90\n777°' '10'\n3\n90\n.\"\"7 7'777^i\n17\n80\n. f. 20\n28\n25\n30\n7 .25 '\n■ ;;77iS7\"w\n35\n90\nThe relative retention time with reference, to tiotropi^ \n(retention time: about 15 minutes) for tiotropium impurityCi \na"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/titanium-dioxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Titanium Dioxide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Titanium Dioxide\nTiO, \nMol. Wt. 79.9\nTitanium Dioxide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of TiO2.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid and topical protectant.\nDescription. A white or almost white, infusible powder.\nA. When strongly heated it becomes pale yellow; the colour \nis discharged on cooling.\nB. To 0.5 g, add 5 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate arid 10 ml \nof water arid mix. Add 10 ml bf sulphuric acid and boilgently \nuntil clear; cool, add slowly 30 ml Of a 25 per cent v/v solution \nof sulphuric acid and dilute with water to 100 nil (solutioriA). \nTo 5 ml of solution A add 0.1 ml of strong hydrogen peroxide \nsolution; an orange-red colour is produced.\nC. To 5 ml of solution A add 0.5 g of zinc, in granules; after \n45 minutes a violet-blue colour is produced.\nAppearance of solution. Solution A is not more opalescent \nthan opalescence standard OS2 (2.4.1), and colourless (2.4.1).\nAcidity or alkalinity. Shake 5.0 g with 50 rhl of carbon dioxide- \nfree water for 5 minutes and centrifuge until a clear solution is\nobtained. To 10 ml of the solution addb.I mlof bromothymol \nblue solution. Not more than 1.0 ml of either 0.01 M hydro­\nchloric acid or 0P1M sodium hydroxide is requited to change \nthe colour of the solution.\nWater-soluble substances. Not more than 0.5 per cent,\ndetermined by the followingmethod. Boil 10.0 g for 5 minutes\n3809\nwww.webofpharma.com\nwith 150 ml of water containing 0.5 g of ammonium sulphate. \nCool, dilute to 200 ml with water and filter until a clear solution \nis obtained. Evaporate 100 ml of the filtrate to dryness ignite \nand weigh.\nArsenic (2.3.10). 'lb 0.2 g in a 100 ml Kjeldahl flask, add 2 g of \nanhydrous sodium sulphate, 7 ml of sulphuric acid and 5 ml \nof nitric acid: Heat gently until a clear solution is obtained, \ncool, add 10 ml of water, cool again and add 5 g of hydrazine \nreducing mixture and 10 ml of hydrochloric acid. Immediately \nattach an air condenser and distil into 15 ml of cooled water \nuntil a total volume of 30 ml is obtained. Rinse the condenser \nwith 5 ml of water and dilute the combined distillate. and \nrinsings to 40 ml with water. 20 ml of the resulting solution \ncomplies with the limit test for arsenic. Use a mixture of 0.5 ml \nof arsenic standard solution (1 ppm As) and 24.5 ml of water \nto prepare the standard (5 ppm).\nBarium. Shake 20.0 g with 30 ml of hydrochloric acid, add\n100 ml of distilled water and boil. Filter while hot through a \nhardened filter paper until a clear filtrate is obtained. Wash the \nfilter with 60 ml of distilled water and dilute the combined \nfiltrate and washings to 200 ml with distilled water. To 10 ml of \nthe solution add 1 ml of 1 Msulphuric acid. After 30.minutes \nany opalescence is not more intense than that of a mixture of\n10 ml of the test Solution and 1 ml of distilled water.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Dilute 10 ml of the solution prepared in \nthe test for Barium to 20 ml with water. 12 ml of the solution \ncomplies with the limit test for heavy metals, Method D \n(20 ppm), using 10 ml of lead standard solution ■() ppm pb).\nIron. To 8 ml of solution A, add 4 ml of water, mix and add \n0.05 ml of bromine water, allow to stand for 5 minutes, remove \nthe excess, of bromine with a current of air and add 3 ml. of \npotassium thiocyanate solution. Any colour in the .solution\nis not more intense than that, in a standard prepared at the \nsame time and in the same manner using a mixture of 4 mi of \niron standard solution (2 ppm Fe) and 8 ml of a 20 per cent \nw/v solution of sulphuric acid (200 ppm).\nAssay. Weigh 0.5 g, transfer to a 300 ml Kjeldahl flask, add 5,g \nof anhydrous sodium sulphate and 10 ml of water, mix and \nadd 10 ml of sulphuric acid. Boil gently until clear, cool, add \nslowly 40 ml of cooled sulphuric acid (25 per cent), cool \nagain and dilute with water to 100.0 ml (solution B); To 300 g \nof zinc, in granules, add 300 ml of a 2 per cent w/v solution of \nmercuric nitrate, and 2 ml of nitric acid, shake for 10 minu"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tizanidine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tizanidine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tizanidine Hydrochloride\nTizanidine Hydrochloride is 5-chloro-W-(2-imidazolin-2-yl>\n2,1,3-benzothiadiazoM-yiamine. \n1 <\nTizanidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 percent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C9H8C1N5SHCÎ, calculated\non the dried basis.\nCategory, a-adrenergic agonist. \n!\nDescription. Awhite to yellowish white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tobramycin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tobramycin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tobramycin Injection\nTobramycin Sulphate Injection , ;.: \"7 ]\nTobramycin Injection is a sterile solution of Tobramycis I\nSulphate in Water for Injections or of Tobramycin in j \nfor Injections containing sufficient Sulphuric Acid to adiu^ !\ntO pH 3.5 tO 6.0. , . \n■ v ' : 7 7 \n. j\nTobramycin Inj ection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and j \nnot more ,than 120.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntobramycin,Ci8H37N5O9. \n... \nj\nUsual: strengths. 10 mg per ml; 40 mg per ml. < ;. 7 ; ( ; .\nDescription. A colourless solution. \n'a1 \n<"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tobramycin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tobramycin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tobramycin\nC18H37N5O9 \nMol. Wt. 4673\nTobramycin is 6-0-(3-amino-3-dcoxy-a-D-glucopyranosyl)-2- \ndeoxy-4-(7-(2,6-diamino-2,3,6-trideoxy-fr-D,i./^(i_\nhexopyranosyl)-D-streptamine, an antimicrobial substance \nproduced by Streptomyces tenebrarius or by any other means.\nTobramycin has potency not less than 900 mcg of CTTNA \nper mg, calculated on anhydrous basis. \n, ~\nCategory.Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder.; P"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tofacitinib-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tofacitinib Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tofacitinib Citrate\nC22I128NA ■ \nMol.Wt.504.5\nTofacitinib Citrate is 3-{(3Æ,4/?)-4-mcthyl-3-[methyl(^' \npyrrolo[2,3-d]pyrimidin-4-yl)ammo]pipcridin-l-yi} -3-c*° \npropanenitrile 2-hydroxypropane-l ,2,3-ir ¡carboxylic acid;\nwww.webofpharma.com\n1P2022\nTOFACITINIB CITRATE\n£.ci+iiiib Citrate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nre \nPcr cent of C22HagN6pg, calculated on the\nSiydtous' basis.\nCateg0Iy.Rheumatoid Arthritis.\npescrijition. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tofluprost","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tofluprost"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tofluprost\nC22H28F2O5 \nMolWt.410.5\nTofluprost is Isopropyl' (52)-7-{(l/?,27?,3J;,55)-2-[(l£)-3,3- \ndifluoro-4-phenoxybut-l -cn-1-yl]-3,5-dihydroxycyclopentyl }- \n5-hepicnoaie. .\nTofluprost contains.not less than 96.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C22H28F2O5, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis. \n' : ■ •• ', . ■\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. Yellow to light yellow oil."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolazamide-tablets","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolazamide Tablets"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolazamide Tablets\nDose. 100 to 250 mg daily; maximum 1 g daily.\nusing phenolphthalein solution as indicator. Carry out a blank \ntitration.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder .\nTolazamide Tablets contain not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than  105.0 per cent of the stated amount of tolazamide,\n1 ml of 0.1 M sodium hydroxide is equivalent to 0.03114 g of \nCI4H2IN303S."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolazamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolazamide"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolazamide\nN\nH \nH C\nC 4112 IN 303S \t\nMol. Wt. 311.4\nTolazamide is 1-perhydroazepin-l-y1-3-tolyl-p-sulphonylurea.\nTolazamide contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 101.0 per cent of C 14112, N 303S, calculatc5ion the.dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Hypoglycaemic.\nrP 2018 \njp 20 18"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolbutamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolbutamide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolbutamide\nMol. Wt. 270.4\nTolbutamide is 3-butyl-1-[(4-methylphenyl)sulphonyl]urea.\nTolbutamide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent ofCI2HlsNz03S, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Hypoglycaemic.\nDose. 500 mg to 1.5 g daily as a single dose with breakfast or\nin divided doses.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolnaftate-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolnaftate Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolnaftate Cream\nTolnaftate Cream contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nriibre than 110.0 per centof the stated amount of tolnaftate, \nCi9H17NOS. ;\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w.\nDetermine by thin-layer chromatography (2.4.17)f coating the \nplate with silica gel GF254. \n• . • • • \n;\nMobile phase. Toluene. \n. I.....\nTest solution. Evaporate 10 ml of the Solution Aon steam bath \nto dryness and dissolve the residue in 1.0 ml of ethanol (95 \nper cent).\nReference solution. K 0.1 per cent w/v solution of to Inaflate \n1PRS in ethanol (95per cent),. ...:. \n.--.v ; \n;\nApply to the plate 10 pl of each solution. Allow the mobile \nphase to rise 8 cm. Dry the plate in air;’and examine under \nultraviolet light at 25.4 nm. The’principal spot in the\n3827\nwww.webofpharma.com\nchromatogram obtained, withthe test solution corresponds to \nthat in the chromatogram obtained;with the reference solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolnaftate-gel","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolnaftate Gel"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolnaftate Gel\nTolnaftate Gel contains not less than 90.0 per cent and not \nmore than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of tolnaftate, \nCI9HI7NOS: \n; \n'\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolnaftate-topical-powder","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolnaftate Topical Powder"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolnaftate Topical Powder\nTolnaftate Topical Powder contains;not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than110:0 per: cent ofthestated amount of \nC19HI7NOS.\nUsual strength. 1 percent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolnaftate-topical-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolnaftate Topical Solution"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolnaftate Topical Solution\nTolnaftate Topical Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntolnaftate, C I9H ',NOS.\nUsual strength. 1 per cent w/v."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolu-balsam","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolu Balsam"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolu Balsam\nBalsam of Tolu\nTolu Balsam is a solid or semi-solid, balsam obtained by incision \nfrom the trunk of My r oxy Ion balsamum (Linn.) Harms \n(Fam. Leguminosae).\nTolu Balsam contains not less than 35.0 per cent and not more \nthan 50.0 per cent of total balsamic acids, calculated as \ncinnamic acid,’ C9HSO3, on the dry, ethanol-soluble matter.\nCategory. Expectorant, antiseptic.\nDescription. A soft, tenacious, brownish yellow or brown \nmass, when first, collected; subsequently, becoming harder \nand finally brittle. Transparent in thin films; odour, aromatic \nand vànillà-Iikè. Warmed and pressed between pieces of glass \nand examined with a lens, it exhibits crystals of cinnamic acid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tolvaptan","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tolvaptan"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tolvaptan\nC26H25C1N2O3 \nMol Wt .448.9\nTolvaptan is TV-{4-{[(5Æ)-7-chloro-5-hydroxy-2,3,4,5- \ntetrahydro-1 H-1 -benzazepin-1 -yl]carbonyl} -3 -methylphenyl} - \n2-mcthylbcnzamidc.\nTolvaptan contains not less than 98.0 per cent and, not more \nthan 102.0 per cent, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Vasopressin Receptor Antagonist. v yf-v..\nDescription. A white to off-white crystalline powder. ; ; < ? ;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/topiramate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Topiramate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Topiramate\nis\n2,3 :4,5-di-O-l-isopropylidene-~-D­\nfructopyranose sulphamate.\nTopiramate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0per cent ofClzHzlNOsS,calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\nCategory. Anti-convulsant.\nDose. 25 mg daily.\nDescription. A white to off-white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/topotecan-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Topotecan Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Topotecan Hydrochloride\nTopotecan Hydrochloride is (45)-1 ()-| (dimethylamino) methyl} \n4-ethyl-4,9-dihydroxy-l/f-pyrano[3,,4/:6i,7j]ind<iHzino[l!-\nb\\quinoline-3,14(477,1277)-dione hydrochloride. íPa;\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTOPOTECAN HYDROCHLORIDE\nlp2022\ntecan Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \n°dnot more than 102.0 per cent.ofCsaH^NjC^HCl, calculated\non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory.Ant>eancer.\nDescription A light yellow to greenish yellow powder.\nQ^j^jQj^^ top°tecan Hydrochloride is cytotoxic; extra\ncare is required to prevent inhaling particles and exposing\nthe skin tq fl \n'"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/topotecan-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Topotecan Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Topotecan Injection\nTopotecan Hydrochloride Injection\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nTorsemide\nTopotecan Injection is a sterile, stabilised solution of\nTopotecan Hydrochloride in Water for Injection.\nSulphated ash (2.3.18). Not more than 0.1 per cent. \t\n1,\n0 CH 3\nTopotecan Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of the \ntopotecan, C23}123N305 .\nWater (2.3.43). Not more than 13.0 per cent, determined on 0.1g.\nCH 3 \nH H \nNH\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 16 Endotoxin Units \nper mg of topotecan hydrochloride.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Total aerobic viable count is \nnot more than 102 CFU per g. 1 g is free from Escherichia coli, \nStaphylococcus aureus and Pseudomonas aeruginosa. 10 g\nDescription. A clear, light yellow solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/torsemide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Torsemide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Torsemide \nv'\nCi6H20N4O3S \n■: , , \n' Mol..Wt.348.4\nTorsemide is 3-pyridinesulfonamide, A(-[[(lrmpthylethyi) \namino Jcarbony l]-4-[(3-methylphenyl)amino] -1-Isopropyl-3 [(4- \nm-toludino-3-pyridyl) sulfonyl] urea. . \n,,V1..\nTorsemide contains hot less than 9 8.0' per cent and not more \nthan. 102.0 per cent of Ci6II?.oN403S, calculated qn;the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Diuretic.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder, , (;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tragacanth","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tragacanth"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tragacanth\nis no precipitate.\nTragacanth is the air-hardened gummy exudate, flowing \nnaturally or obtained by incision, from the trunk and branches\nKaraya gum. Boil 1 g with 20 ml of water until a mucilage is \nformed, add 5 ml of hydrochloric acid and again boil for \n5 minutes; no pink or red colour develops.\nof Astragalus gummifer Labill. and certain other species of \nAstragalus (Fam. Fabaceae).\nColophony. Add 5 g to 25 ml of carbon disulphide, warm gently \non a water-bath under a reflux condenser, filter, evaporate the \nsolution to dryness, dissolve the residue in 6 ml of light \npetroleum (40° to 60°) and shake with 10 ml of a 0.5 per cent\nSterculia. A. Shake 0.2 g of the powdered material with 10 ml \nof ethanol (60 per cent) in a 10-m1 stoppered cylinder; any \ngel formed occupies not more than 1.5 ml.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Pale yellow, thin, flattened ribbons or brittle \npieces; odourless and almost tasteless. On the addition of \nabout 10 times its weight of water, it forms a mucilaginous gel.\nw/v solution of cupric acetate; the light petroleum layer is \nnot coloured green.\nB. Shake 1 g of the powdered material with 100 ml of water and \ntitrate with 0.01 M sodium hydroxide, using methyl red \nsolution as indicator. Not more than 5.0 ml is required to change\nEster value (2.3.26). 47 to 95, calculated on the dry, ethanol-\nsoluble basis.\nIt has the macroscopic and microscopic characteristics \ndescribed under Identification tests A and B.\nthe colour of the solution.\nSaponification value (2.3.37). 170 to 230, calculated on the \ndry, ethanol-soluble basis.\nForeign matter. Not more than 1.0 per cent, determined by the \nfollowing method. To 2.0 g of the powdered material in a \n250.0 ml round-bottomed flask add 95 ml of methanol, swirl to \nmoisten the powder and add 60 ml of 7 M hydrochloric acid. \nAdd a few glass beads and heat under a reflux condenser in a \nwater-bath for 3 hours, shaking occasionally. Remove the glass \nbeads and filter the hot suspension under reduced pressure \nthrough a sintered-glass crucible (porosity No. 1). Rinse the \nflask with a small quantity of water, passing the rinsings \nthrough the filter. Wash the residue on the filter with about \n40 ml of methanol and dry to constant weight at 110'."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tramadol-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tramadol Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tramadol Hydrochloride\nC16H76C1N()2 \n; \n. Mol. Wt. 299.8\nTramadol Hyrochloride is (12?S, 22?S)-2-:[(dimethylainmo)- \nmethyl]-l -(3-methoxyphenyl)cyclphexanol hydrochloride..\nTramadol Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C16H26C1NO2) calculated \non the anhydrous basis.\nCategory.Analgesic.' \n”•\nDescription. A white or almostwliite, crystalline powder.\nTest B may be omitted if tests A and C are carried but: Test A \nmay be omitted if tests B and C are carried out. ;\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4,6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with tramadol \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of tramadol \nhydrochloride.\nB. In the test 'for impurity\"E, the principal spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (b) corresponds to \nthe principal spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference \nSOluiiOn (a). \n■ • . ■\nC.Itgivesreaction(A)ofchlorides(2^3.1). r '' '■"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trandolapril","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trandolapril"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trandolapril\nC24H34N20s\nMol. Wt. 430.5\nTrandolapril is (2S,3aR,7as)-1 [(5)-2-[[(8)-1-(ethoxycarbonyl)-\n3-phenylpropyl]amino]-1-oxo-propyl]octahydro-1H-indole-2-\ncarboxylic acid.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRANDOLAPRlL\nTrandolapril contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not more\nthan 101.5 per centofC24H34N20s,calculated onthe dried basis.\nCategory. Antihypertensive.\nDescription. A white to offwhite crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/travoprost-eye-drops","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Travoprost Eye Drops"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Travoprost Eye Drops\nTravoprost Eye Drops contains not less than 9Ó.0 per cent \nand not more than 110,0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntravoprost, C26H35F3O6.\nUsual strength. 40 pg per, ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/travoprost","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Travoprost"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Travoprost"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trazodone-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trazodone Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trazodone Hydrochloride\nCiÄ2C]N5O,HCI\nMol. Wt 408.3\n• Trazodone Hydrochloride is l,2,4-Triazolo[(4,3-a]pyridin- \n3(2H)-one, 2-[3-[4-(3-chlorophenyl)-l -piperazinyl]propyl]-,\nm°nohydrochloride.\nTRAZODONE HYDROCHLORIDE\nTrazodone Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C19H22C1N5O,HC1, \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant.\nDescription. A white to off white, crystalline powder.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with trazodone \nhydrochloride IPRS or with the reference spectrum of \ntrazodone hydrochloride.\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by liquid chromatography \n(2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. 80 volumes of mobile phase A and 20 volumes \nof mobile phase B.\nTest solution. Dissolve 50 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 50,0 ml with \nthe solvent mixture.\nReference solution (a). A 0.0001 per cent w/v solution of\ntrazo do ne hydrochloride IPRS in the solvent mixture, ..\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.0001 per cent \nw/v, each of, trazodone related compound C IPRS and \ntrazodone related compound D IPRS in reference solution\nChromatographic system \n<\n- a stainless steel column 7.5 cm x.4.6 mm, packed,with\noctadecylsilane bonded to porous silica (3.5 pm),\n- mobile phase: A. a O.Olper cent v/v solution of\ntriethylamine in water, \n. \n,\nB. a O.Olper cent y/v solution of \ntriethylamine in' acetonitrile,\n- a gradient programme using the conditions given below, \n- flow rate: 2 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometer set at 254 nm,\ninjection volume: 10 pl.\nTime\nMobile phase A\nMobile phase E\n(in min.)\n(per cent v/v)\n(per cent v/v)\n0\n80\n20\n12\n; '?.:3i2';X\n, ’ ' 68 ' '\n. 12.01\n. 80\n20\n15\n■ 80\n20\n3851\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRAZODONE HYDROCHLORIDE\nretention time factor\nName, \nRelative Correction\nTriazolopyridinone1\n0.1\n2.08\nTrazodone Y-oxide2\n0.40\nDeschloro trazodone3\n0.65\n1.41 ■\nTrazodone related compound C\n0.96\n—\nTrazodone\n1.0\n—\nTrazodone related compound D\n1.1\n— . ■■■\n4-Ethyl trazodone4\n1.4\n—\nTrazodone isobutyl ether analog5 2.0 .X,\nBispiperazine analog6\n2.1\n0.77\n'[l,2,4]Triazolo[4,3-a]pyridin-3.(2J7)-one,\n24-(3-Chiorophenyl)-r-[3-(3-oxo-[l,2,4]triazolo[4,3-a]pyridin-2(3iZ)- \nyl)propyl]piperazine 1-oxide,\n32-[3-(4-Phenylpiperazin-l-yl)propyl]-[ 1,2,4] tri azolo[4,3-a]pyridin- \n3(2//)-one,\n42 - {3-[4-(3-Chloro-4-ethylphenyl)piperazin - l-yl]propyl} - \n[l,2,4]triazolo[4,3-a]pyridin-3(2iT)-one,\n5l-(3-Chlordphenyi)-4“(3-isobutoxypropyl)piperazine,\n61,3- Bis(4-(3-chlorophenyl)piperaziit-l-yl)propaiie.\nNOTE—A mixture of 40 volumes of acetonitrile, 30 volumes \nof2-prdpano.l, 30 volumes of acetone And 0.2 volume of formic \nacid may be usedfor injector wash to minimize sample carry­\nover. \n( \n........ .\nInject reference solution (a) and (b). The test is not valid unless \nthe resolution between the peaks, corresponding to trazodone \nrelated,compound C and trazodone is not less than 1.5,and the \npeaks corresponding to trazodone and trazodone related \ncompound D is not less than 2.8 in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference solution (b) and the relative standard deviation \nfor replicate injections is not more than 5.0 per cent in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (a);\nInject reference solution (a); and the test solution. In the \nchromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area of any \npeak corresponding to triazplopyridinone, trazodone n-oxide, \ndeschloro trazodone, trazodone related compound C, \ntrazodone related compound D, 4-ethyl trazodone, trazodone \nisobutyl ether analog and bispiperazine analog, each of, is not \nmore than the area of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (a) (O.Tper cent), the area of \nany other secondary peak is not mbre than the"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone-acetonide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide Injection\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection is a sterile suspension of \nTriamcinolone Acetonide in very fine particles in Water for\nInjections containing suitable dispersing agents.\nTriamcinolone Acetonide Injection contains not less than \n90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated \namount of triamcinolone acetonide, C24H31 F06.\nUsual strength. 40 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone-acetonide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamcinolone Acetonide\no\nCZJf31F06\nMol. Wt. 434.5\nTriamcinoloneAcetonide is 9a-fluoro-I1 ~,21-dihydroxy­\n16a,17a-isopropylidenedioxy-l ,4-pregnadiene-3,20-dione.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIAMCINOLONE ACETONIDE\nTriamcinolone Acetonide contains not less than 96.0 per cent\nand not more than 104.0 per cent ofCz4H3IF06, calculated on\nthe anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Corticosteroid.\nDose. By deep intramuscular injection, 40 mg to 100 mg.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamcinolone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamcinolone"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamcinolone\nTriamcinolone is 9a~ fluoro-11 ß, 16a, 17a,21 -tetrahydroxy- \npregna-1,4-diene-3,20-dione. \n\\;;\nTriamcinolone contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not \nmore than 103.0 per cent of C2 JI27FO6. calculatedon thedri\nbasis.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIAMCINOLONE TABLETS\n.............................................................................................\n^^Corticosteroid.\n' •Xtinn A white or almost white, crystalline powder; \nne5cnpllun- .\nSl^^r0SC0P’C*\npetennine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6).\n* -»»¿fhe spectrum with that obtained with triamcino lone \njpjjy^xvlththe reference spectrum of triamcinolone.\ng^heii examined in the range 220 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \nO^ feH'W^v.solution in methanol shows an absorption\ninaXiinumatabout238 nm; absorbance at about 238 nm, about\n■ ■ '■ \n■ i\nC O *n 0 bf ethanol (95 per cent), add .5 ml of \na 1 per cent w/v solution of butylated hydroxytoluene in \nethanol (95 cwt) an^ 5 ml of 1. M sodium hydroxide and \nheat ¿n a Watei-bath under a reflux condenser for 20 minutes; \napihkish lavender colour is produced."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triamterene","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triamterene"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triamterene\nMol. Wt. 253.3\nTriamterene is 2,4,7-triamino-6-phenylpteridine.\nTriamterene contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent ofC1ZHlIN7, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Potassium sparing diuretic.\nDose. 150 to 250 mg daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A yellow, crystalline powder; odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tributyl-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tributyl Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tributyl Citrate\nMol. Wt. 360.5\nTributyl Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent of\nC1sH320 7, calculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Excipient."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triclofos-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triclofos Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triclofos Oral Solution\nDetermine the .weight per ml of the oral solution (2.4.29) and \ncalculate the content of C2H3Cl3NaO4P.\nTest solution. Disperse 1.0 ml of the substance under \nexamination in 50.0 ml of dichloromethane.\nTriclofos Oral Solution contains not less than.; 95.0 per cent | \nand not more than 105.0 per cent of C2H3Cl3NaO4P. \n|\nReference solution. Disperse 1.0 ml of the substance under \nexamination and 0.5 ml of methyl tridecanoate in 50.0 ml of \ndichloromethane.\nTriethyl Citrate\n.0 \nr^O”CH2CH3"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triclofos-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triclofos Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triclofos Sodium\nCI3C O\" xONa\nC2H3CI3NaO4P \nMol.Wt.251.4\nTriclofos Sodium is sodium 2,2,2-trichloroethyl hydrogen \northophosphate.\nTriclofos Sodium contains not less than 41:3 per cent and not \nmore than 43.2 per cent of Cl and not less than 97.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C2H3C13NaO4P, both \ncalculated on the dried basis.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder; hygroscopic.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the; spectrum with that obtained with triclofos sodium \nIPRS or with the reference spectrum of triclofos sodium.\nB. It gives the reactions of sodium salts (2.3.1).\n3863\nwww.webofpharma.com\nC, Heat 0.1g with 1 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate to a \ndull red heat and maintain for 10 minutes, cool, extract the \nresidue with water and filter. The filtrate gives the reactions\nof chlorides (2.3 .Hand of phosphates Í2.3.T). ;\nTests •. \n„..i.\npH (2.4.24).3.0 to 4.5, determined ona2.0 per centw/v solution.\nAppearance of solution. A 2.0,per cent w/y solution is clear \n(2.4.1). \n. ;? ,7;\nChlorides (2.3.12). Dissolve 0.5, g in 50 ml of water. 15 ml of \nthe solution complies with the limit test for chlorides (0.17 per \ncent). \n,\nPhosphates. Not more than 1.0 per cent, calculated as PO4, \nwhen determined by dissolving 25 mg in 10. ml of water, add \n4 ml of 1 M sulphuric acid, 1 ml of ammonium molybdate \nsolution and 2 ml of methylaminophenol-sulphite reagent\nand allow to stand for 15 minutes and dilute to 25 ml with \nwater, allow to stand for a further 15 minutes and measure the \nabsorbance of the solution at 730 nm (2.4.7): Calculate the \ncontent of phosphate from a calibration curve prepared by \ntreating suitable volumes of a 0.00143 per cent w/v solution of \npotassium dihydrogen orthophosphate in the same manner.\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). 1.0 g complies with the limit test for \nheavy metals, Method B (20 ppm).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Notmore than 5.0 percent, determined \non 1 g by drying at 100°, at a pressure not exceeding 0.7 kPa \nfor 3 hours.\nAssay. For Cl — Mix 0.25 g with 1 g bi anhydrous sodium \ncarbonate in a nickel crucible about 3 cm in diameter, fill the \ncrucible completely with anhydrous sodium carbonate and \ninvert into a nickel crucible about 4 cm in diameter; cover the \nsmaller crucible with anhydrous sodium carbonate, well \npressed down, using 25 g of anhydrous sodium carbonate in\nall. Heat for 30 minutes at a dull red heat, cool, transfer to a \n400 ml beaker, add 150 ml of water and boil gently for \n10 minutes. Filter through absorbent cotton into a 600 ml beaker, \nwashing the residue with hot water, until 400 ml of filtrate has \nbeen collected. Cool, cautiously add nitric acid until the \nsolution is neutral to litmus paper and add 3 ml of nitric acid \nin excess. Add 50 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate, allow to stand \nuntil precipitation is complete, filter, wash the precipitate with \nwater and titrate the combined filtrate and washings with \n0.1 M ammonium thiocyanate using ammonium iron(III) \nsulphate solution as indicator.\n1 ml of 0.1M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.003545 g of Cl.\nFor CdCCliNaO^P—Heat 0.2 g in a Kjeldahl flask with 2 ml \nof sulphuric dcid; and 2.5 ml of nitric acid until brown fumes \ncease to be evolved, cool, add 1 ml of nitric acid and heat \nagain. Continue adding nitric acid and heating until brown\n3864 \nMmBmIWI!\nTRICLOFOS SODIUM\n^2022\nTRIETHYL CITRATE\nfumes are no longer evolved and the solution is colour} \nwhen cold. Heat until dense, white fumes are evolved e \ntransfer the solution to a flask with the aid of 150 ml of\n,Q^iFf sulphuric acid to the combined aqueous layer \nthings, extract with four 10-ml quantities of isoamyl \nihol$0^ cac^ extract t0 stand until good separation \nf y ^^ed,combine the isoamyl alcohol extracts, remove\nB. To 0.5 ml add 5 ml of ethanol (95 per cent) and 4 ml of \ndilute sodium hydroxide solution. Boil under reflux for about\n■■■■ Í\nadd 50 ml of citric-molybdic acid solution and heat slowlv ’’ I \nboiling. Swirli"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triethyl-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triethyl Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triethyl Citrate\nMol. Wt.276.3\nTriethyl Citrate is triethyl 2-hydroxypropane-l, 2, 3-\ntricarboxylate.\nTriethyl Citrate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent of ClzHzo07, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Excipient.\n.Description. A clear, viscous, colourless or almost colourless,\nhygroscopic liquid.\n2258\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trifluoperazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trifluoperazine Injection\nTrifluoperazine Hydrochloride Injection\nTrifluoperazine Injection is a sterile solution of \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. :\nTrifluoperazine Injection contains riot less than 90:0 pet ce$ \narid not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount\ntrifluoperazine,C2!H24F3N3S; \n;\n3866\nwww.webofpharma.com\nstrength The equivalent of 1 mg of trifluoperazine per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triflupromazine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triflupromazine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triflupromazine Hydrochloride\nFlupromazine Hydrochloride\nI Hel\nMoL Wt. 388.9\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride is 10-[3-(dimethylamino)-\npropyl)]-2-trifluoromethylphenothiazine hydrochloride.\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride contains not less than\n97.0percent and not more than 103.0 per centofClsHl9F3N2S,\nHCI, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Antipsychotic; antiemetic.\nDose. As antipsychotic, orally, 30 to 150 mg oftriflupromazine\ndaily, in divided doses; by intramuscular injection, 10 mg\nrepeated 4-hourly, ifnecessary; by intravenous injection, 1to\n3 mg repeated 4-hourly, ifnecessary. As antiemetic, 20 to 30\nmg oftriflupromazine daily; by intramuscular injection 1to 3\nmg.\nDescription. A white to pale yellowish brown, crystalline\npowder; odour slight and characteristic.\nNOTE -\nIn the following procedures, the test and standard\nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be\nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\nsubdued light."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triflupromazine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triflupromazine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triflupromazine Injection\nTriflu p rom azine Tablets\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride Injection; Flupromazine \nHYdrochloride Injection; Flupromazine Injection\nTriflupromazine Hydrochloride Tablets; Flupromazine \nHydrochloride Tablets; Flupromazine Tablets\nTriflupromazine Injection is a sterile solution ofTriflupromazine \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections.\nTriflupromazine Tablets contain not less than 90.0 per cent \nand than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntrithiPrcelaiine hydrochloride, C 181-1 19F3N2S, HCI.\nTriflupromazine Injection contains not less than-90.0 perCent , \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated -:,amount-of \ntriflupromazine hydrochloride, C I8H 19F3N2S. Hel.\nkfsitiil strengths. 10 mg; 25 mg.\nNOTE - In the following procedures, the test and standard \nspecimens and the solutions containing them should be \nprotected by carrying out the tests without delay and in\nsubdued light.\nI dentification\nA. Shake a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 20 mg \nof Triflupromazine Hydrochloride with 30 ml of 1 M \nhydrochloric acid for 10 minutes, filter, make the filtrate alkaline \nto litmus paper with 5 M sodium hydroxide and extract with \ntwo quantities, each of 20 ml, of light petroleum (boiling \nrange 60° to 80°). Combine the extracts, wash with 10 ml of \nwater, shake with 5 g of anhydrous sodium sulphate, filter and \nevaporate the filtrate carefully to dryness.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4.6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with triflupromazine hydrochloride RS, treated in \nthe same manner or with the reference spectrum of \ntriflupromazine hydrochloride.\nB. Extract a quantity of the powdered tablets containing 5 mg \nof Triflupromazine Hydrochloride with 5 ml of  acetone, filter \nand evaporate the filtrate to dryness.\nWhen examined in the range 230 nm to 360 nm (2.4.7), a \n0.001 per cent w/v solution in 0.1 M hydrochloric acid shows \nan absorption maximum at about 256 nm."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trifluridine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trifluridine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trifluridine\nTriflurothymidene,\nCi0HnF3N2O5 \nMol. Wt 296.2\nTrifluridine is 5-Tri fluor omet h y 1-2 ’-deoxyuridine.\nTrifluridine contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of CioHiiF3N205, calculated on the dried \nbasis.\nCategory. Pyrimidine nucleoside analogue; antiviral (herpes \nviruses). ' '\nDescription. A white, crystalline ponder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trihexyphenidyl-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trihexyphenidyl Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trihexyphenidyl Hydrochloride\n,HCI\nC20H3|NO,HC1 \nMol. Wt. 337.9\nBenzhexol Hydrochloride is (AS)-1-cyclohexyl-1-phenyl- ■ \n3-piperidinopropan-l -ol hydrochloride.\nBenzhexol Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 101.0 per cent of C20H31NO,HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antiparkinsonian.\nDescription. A white or creamy-white, crystalline powder; \nodourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimetazidine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimetazidine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimetazidine Hydrochloride\nh3co\nh3co\nMol. Wt 339.3\nC14H22N2O3,2HC1\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride is I-[(2,3,4-trimethoxyphenyl)-\nmethyl]piperazine dihydrochloride.\nTrimetazidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.5 \ncent and not more than 101.5 per cent, calculated on the.dn :\nbasis. ’ \n.... \n....\nCategory. Antiischemic metabolic agent.\n3872\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTRIMETAZIDINE HYDROCHLORIDE\nIi>2Q2?\npescripii°n‘ A white or almost white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimethoprim-and-sulphamethoxazole-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimethoprim and Sulphamethoxazole Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimethoprim and Sulphamethoxazole Oral Suspension\ncontains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\n110.0 per cent of the stated amounts of trimethoprim,\nC14HISN403, and sulphamethoxazole, CIOHIIN303S.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDose. For children upto 1 year, Trimethoprim, 40 mg and\nSulphamethoxazole, 200 mg daily,in divided doses. Forchildren\n1 to 5 years of age, Trimethoprim, 80 mg and Sulpha-\nmethoxazole, 400 mg daily, in divided doses. For adults,\nTrimethoprim, 160 to 480 mg and Sulphamethoxazole, 800 mg\nto 2.4 g daily, in divided doses.\n2265\nwww.webofpharma.com\nUsual strengths. Trimethoprim40 mg and Sulpha- methoxazole\n200 mg in 5ml; Trimethoprim 80 mg and Sulphamethoxazole\n400 mg in 5ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trimethoprim","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trimethoprim"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trimethoprim\nMol. Wt. 290.3\nTrimethoprim is 5-(3,4,5-trimethoxybenzyl)pyrimidine-\n2,4-diamine.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nIP 2010\nTrimethoprim contains not less than 98.5 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 per cent of CJ4HJ8N403, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDose. 200 mg twice daily.\nDescription. A white or yellowish white powder; odourless or\nalmost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/triprolidine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Triprolidine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Triprolidine Hydrochloride\nNf)\nH\nN\ny\nCI9HzzNz,HCl,HzO\nMol. Wt. 332.9\nTriprolidine Hydrochloride is (E)-2-(3-pyrrolidin-l-yl-\n1-(p-tolyl)prop-l-enyl)pyridine hydrochloride monohydrate.\nTriprolidine Hydrochloride contains not less than 98.0 per\ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C19HzzNz, HCl,\ncalculated on the anhydrous basis.\nCategory. Histamine HI-receptor antagonist.\nDose. 10 to 20 mg daily, in divided doses.\nDescription. A white, crystalline powder; almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trisodium-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trisodium Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trisodium Citrate\nHOCOONa oun\nv,ZH2vJ\nTrn i ? NaOOC -V V^COONa\nC6H5Na3O7,2H2O \nMol. Wt 294.1\nSodium Citrate is trisodium 2-hydroxypropane-L2,3-\ntricarboxylate dihydrate.\nSodium Citrate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 101.0 per cent of C6H5Na3O7, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Systemic alkalinising agent.\nDescription. White, granular crystals or a white, crystalline \npowder. \n."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tropicamide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tropicamide"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tropicamide\nMol. Wt. 284.4\nTropicamide is (RS)-N-ethyl-3-hydroxy-2-phenyl-N-(pyrid-\n4-ylmethyl)propionamide\nTropicamide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not more\nthan 101.0 per cent of C17HzoNzOz, calculated on the dried\nbasis.\nCategory. Mydriatic; cycloplegic.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/trospium-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Trospium Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Trospium Chloride\nCI\nC25H30CINO3 \t\n.Mol.Wt, 428.0\nTrospium Chloride is 3-(2-Hydroxy-2,2-diphenylaCetoxy)Spiro \n[bicyclo[3.2.1]octane-8,1 1-pyrrolidi n]-1'-i um chloride.\n1 iZOPICAM1DE EYE DROPS \n0)201i \n-------\nTROSPIUM CHLORIDE TABLETS\nTrospium Chloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent a nd \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C 25H30C1NO3, calculated on the\nAssay. Dissolve 0.3 g in 50.0 ml of water Titrate with 0.1 M \nsilver nitrate determining the end-point potentiometrically\n2hydroxydiphenylacetic acid (benzilic acid).\nInject reference solution (c). The test is not valid unless the\n dried basis.\n(2.4.25). Carry out a blank titration.\nresolution between the peaks due to trospium impurity B and \ntrospium is not less than 3.0.\nCategory. Anticholinergic.\n1 ml of 0.1 M silver nitrate is equivalent to 0.0428 g of\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder.\nC25H30CNO3.\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 3 times the retention time of the principal peak.\nStorage. Store protected from light."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/troxidone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Troxidone"},"edition":"ip-2022","cate