,Method B (20 ppm). . \n. -. - . - , \n. ,r\nSulphated ash (2.3 > 18) . Not more than' 0.1 per cent.\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 0.5 per cent, determined \nop L.0rg.l3^;^in^ oyer anhydrous silica gel for 6 hours.\nAssay.Determinebygas chromatography (2.4.13).\nTestsblution. Weigh 0.1 g of the substance under examination\nand dissolve in 16 ml of the solutions prepared by dissolving \n$•125 g ofl-decanol (intcrnal standard) in sufficient ethanol\nto produce 25 0 ml (solution B). \n1 ’ ' ’' ! j!! ! ' •\n^f^pce^sol^on,. Dissolve 0,1 g of troxidone IP RS in\nsu^ient solution B to produce 10.0 ml. \n,\n^P^9gTaphic system .. . ■ \n...\n“ a stainless steel column 0.75 m x 3, mm, packed with\ni ; porous polymer beads (120 to 150 pm),\n... '^Jlei^^^tUre:-'''-'-■ \n....... : \n'.'■■■ •\n“ cblumh216a, \n' 1 ■ ’■••••• \n■\ninlet port at 240° and detector at 270°, \nflqw rate: 30 ml per minute, using nitrogenas the carrier \ngas. ■ \n.....\nCalcúlate the content of C¿ri9NO3:; : \n;:fl■ ■;; ! ¡\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture\nTroxidone Capsules\nTrimethadione Capsules\nTroxidone Capsules contain not less than 95.0 per cent and\nUsual strength. 250 mg."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tuberculin-purified-protein-derivative","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tuberculin Purified Protein Derivative"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tuberculin Purified Protein Derivative\nBacterial and fungal contamination. Carry out test for sterility \n(2.2.11) using 10 ml of bulk for each sterility medium.\nTuberculin PPD; Tuberculin Purified Protein Derivative for \nHuman Use\nTuberculin Purified Protein Derivative is a preparation made \nfrom the heat-treated products of growth and lysis of one or\n- \nrn_orc..wains: of Mycobacterium tuberculosis that reveal\nDetermination of potency of the vaccine. \nthan three suitable dilutions of the vaccine u\nhat is satisfactory with respect to each of the \nven below under Identification, Tests and\nless \nalien\nTUBERCULIN PURIFIED PROTEIN DERIVATIVE\ndelayed hypersensitivity in animals sensitised by a micro-\norganism of the same species."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tubocurarine-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tubocurarine Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tubocurarine Chloride\nC37H41C1N2O6,HC1,5H2O \nMol. Wt. 771.7\nTubocurarine Chloride is 7', 1 ^'-dihydroxy-ôjô'-dimethoxy- \n^^'^'-trimçthyltuboçuraranium ; chloridet hydrochloride \npentahydratc. \n. ■/ mm.ml mm :m ■ •••m <•\nTùbbcùrârihe; Chloride: contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand -riot more than ■ 102.0 per cent of C37H4iC1N2O6,HC1, \ncalculated on the anhydrous basis. \nr - <\nCategory. General anaesthetic. \n?;\nDescription. A white or yellowish white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tubocurarine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tubocurarine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tubocurarine Injection\nTubocurarine Chloride Injection\nTubocurarine Injection is a sterile'solution of Tubocurarine \nChloride in Water for Injections. It may contain suitable \nbuffering agents.\nT . s'dhl/ff.....\n10 ml of water,filter the chloroform solution into a tared beaker \nand. wash, fixe/filter with two successive, quantities, each of\n.Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent \nsnd riot mörethan 105.0 per cent of the stated amount of \ntubocurarine chloride, C37H41C1N2O6,HC1,5H2O.\n5 ml, of chloroform. Add the. washings to the, filtrate. Remove \nthe chloroform on a water-bath,, dry the residue at J05° for\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more thanTO.O Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of tubocurarine chloride.\n1 hour, cool and weigh. The residue does hot dissolve in 10 nd \nof water blit dis solves on the1 add it ion ' of 1 ■ ml of 2 M\nUsual strength. 10 mg per ml.\nOther tests. Comply with thé tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nhydrochloric acid. ■ '■: ' ! 7 \n:: : ;•\" ': ; br\nDescription.Acolouriess or faintly coloured solution.\nRelated substances. Determine by thin-layer chromatography |\nAssay. Dilute a volume containing about 50 mg of Tubocurarine \nChloride to 1000.0 ml with water and measure the absorbance \nof the resulting solution at the maximum at about 280 nm (2,4,7)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tulasi-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tulasi Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tulasi Dry Extract\nTulasi Dry Extract is obtained from the Tulasi leaves (Ocimum \nsanctum Linn, Fam. Laminaceae) by extraction with methanol \nbr other any suitable solvent and evaporation of solvent.\nTulasi Dry Extract contains not less than 90.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 120.0 per cent w/w of the stated amount of \nthe ursolic acid, calculated on the dried basis. It may contain \nsuitable added substances.\nCategory. Antiasthmatic, immunompdulator, expectorant, \nWsa;‘-;\nUsual strengths. 0.1 per cent w/w; 0.2 per cent w/w.\nDescription. Apale green powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tulasi","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tulasi"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tulasi\nBasil; Ocimum sanctum\nTulasi consists ofleaves. of Ocimum sanctum .Linn \n(Fam. Lamiaceae).\nTulasi contains not less than 0.40 per cent w/w of eugenol, \ncalculated on the dried basis. '\nCategory. Antiasthmatic, immunomodulator, expectorant, \nswasa.\nDescription. Greyish-black in colour having characteristic \nodour with slightly pungent and aromatic taste."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tylosin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tylosin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tylosin Injection\nTylosin Injection is a sterile solution ofTylosin in a mixture of\nequal volumes ofPropylene Glycol and Water for Injections.\nTylosin Injection contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not\nmore than 105.0 per cent ofthe stated amount oftylosin. The\ncontent oftylosin A is not less than 80.0 per cent and the sum\nofthe contents oftylosin A, tylosin B, tylosin C and tylosin D\nis not less than 90.0 per cent.\nUsual strengths. 2.5 gin 50 ml; 20 g in 100 ml.\nDescription. A pale yellow to amber-coloured solution."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/tylosin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Tylosin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Tylosin\no\nMol. Wt. 916.1\nTylosin is a macrolideantibiotic isolatede from a strain of\nStryptomycetes fradiae found in soil from Thailand.\nTylosin has a potency of not less than 900 Units per mg,\ncalculated on the dried basis. The content of tylosin A is not\nless than 80.0 per cent and the sum ofthe contents oftylosin\nA, tylosin B, tylosin C and tylosin D is not less than 95.0 per\ncent.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDose. Allspecies exceptadult ruminants. Orally, 20 t045 mg\nperkg ofbody weight daily in divided doses. By intramuscular\ninjection,.2 to 10 mg per kg ofbody weight.\n2697\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTYLOSIN\nDescription. Almost white or slightly yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/type-of-reagent-test-red-blood-cells-avidity","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Type of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells Avidity"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Type of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells Avidity \nwhich lacks the antigen corresponding to reagent antibody \nand the tube is centrifuged at 1000 rpm for 1 minute and \nobserved microscopically for rouleaux formation. \nAnti-D (IgM) \t\n0+ve (R i r or R 1 R2) 5-10 seconds \n(Monoclonal)\nThe types of monoclonal anti-D reagents are:\n.nom\n11W  `rd\n1.\n1gM anti-D monoclonal reagent,\n2.\nBlend of IgM and IgG monoclonal antibodies reagent,\n3.\nBlend of monoclonal IgM and polyclonal (human)Ig \nGanti-D. \nIgM anti-D monoclonal antibodies are highly specific and \nsaline reacting equally well at room temperature and at 37°. \nThey are good for slide test or immediate spin tube tests as\nType of Reagent\nIntensity. It is defined by the appearance of agglutination \nobtained after mixing undiluted Anti-D (IgM) Monoclonal \nblood grouping reagent with washed red blood cell \nsuspension. For this 1 volume of the reagent is added to \nI volume of 40 per cent v/v red cell suspension on a slide and \nmixed for 2 minutes. At the end of 2 minutes, reaction grade is \ncompared with the strength of reaction obtained with the \nreference preparation.\nTest Red Blood Cells Rouleaux\nAnti-D (IgM)\n0-ve (rr or r'r or Cr)"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/type-of-reagent-test-red-blood-cells-haemolysis","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Type of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells Haemolysis"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Type of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells Haemolysis\nAnti-A \t\nA l \t Absent\n(Monoclonal)\nA2 \t\nAbsent\nA2B \t Absent\nB \t Absent \n0 \t Absent\nRouleaux. It is defined as a form of pseudo agglutination in \nwhich the red blood cells look like pile of coins. The red blood \ncells lacking the antigen corresponding to the reagent antibody \nshould not show rouleaux formation. For this 1 volume of \nAnti-A Monoclonal Blood Grouping Reagent is mixed in a \ntest tube with 1 volume of 2-3 per cent red cell suspension \nwhich lacks the antigen corresponding to reagent antibody \nand the tube is centrifuged at 1000 rpm for 1 minute and \nobserved microscopically for rouleaux formation.\nTipe of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells Rouleaux\nAnti-A \t\nB \t Absent\n( Monoclonal) \t\n0 \t Absent\nProzone. It is defined as a phenomenon in which negative \nreactions are obtained with low dilutions of an antibody, while \na positive reaction is obtained with higher dilutions. For this 1 \nvolume of Anti-A Monoclonal Blood Group*g . Reagetit is\nmixed with 1 volume of 2-3 per cent red cell susrignsion In test \ntubes which exhibit the antigen corresponding to the reagent\n_\n111111 %\nIP 2018 \t\nIP 2018 \nANTI-A BLOOD GROUPING SERUM \nANTI -B BLOOD GROUPING SERUM \n•"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/type-of-reagent-test-red-blood-cells-prozone","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Type of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells Prozone"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Type of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells Prozone\nA , B\nAnti-B \n(Monoclonal)\nB\nExpiration date. The expiration date for Anti-B blood grouping \nreagent is not less than 1 year at 2° to 8°.\nStorage. Store at a temperature between 2° and 8°.\nLabelling. The label states that the source material was not \nreactive for hepatitis B surface antigen, but that no known \ntest method offers assurance that products derived from human \nblood will not transmit hepatitis in case of polyclonal. Label \nalso states that it is for in vitro diagnostic use.\nNOTE—The labelling is in black lettering imprinted on paper \nthat is white or is coloured completely or in part to match \nthe specified yellow colour standard.\nAnti-AB Blood Grouping Reagent\nAnti-AB Blood Grouping reagent is a sterile, liquid containing \nblood group antibodies which agglutinates human red cells \ncontaining A and B antigens that is of blood groups A, B and \nAB. It contains a suitable antimicrobial preservative. It is of \ntwo types polyclonal and monoclonal."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/type-of-reagent-test-red-blood-cells-specificity","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Type of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells Specificity"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Type of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells Specificity\nAnti-AB \t\nA I \t Positive \n(Monoclonal)\nExpiration date. The expiration date for Anti-AB blood \ngrouping reagent is not less than 1 year  at 2° to 8°.\nA2 \t\nPositive\nTipe of Reagent Test Red Blood Cells \t\nTitre\nAx \t Positive\nAnti-AB \t\nA I \t\n: > ')56\nStorage. Store at a temperature between 2° and 8\".\nB \t Positive \n0 \t Negative\n(Monoclonal)\nA2 \t\nI : > 128\nLabelling. The label states that the source material was not \nreactive for hepatitis B surface antigen, but that no known \ntest method offers assurance that products derived from human \nblood will not transmit hepatitis in case of polyclonal. Label \nalso states that it is for in vitro diagnostic use.\nB \t I : 256\nReactivity\nAvidity. It is defined as the time taken for agglutination to \nappear first to the unaided eye when 1 volume of the Anti-AB \nmonoelonal_BIOod Grouping Reagent is mixed with 1 volume \n- of a 4t. per eeqt, v/v suspension of human red corpuscles'gf\nHaemolysis. It is defined as the release of hemoglobin from \nthe red cells as a result of osmotic effect or due Wilie breaking, \nup of the red blood cells. No immune haerriolySis. should be \nobserved with the red blood cells used for specificity.\nNOTEL-The lethelling is in black lettering imprinted on paper \nthat*: 'white -or is coloured completely or in part to match \nthe Specified yellow colour standard.\nspecific group or sub group and compared with the time taken.\n. .\n3891"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/typhoid-paratyphoid-a-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Typhoid Paratyphoid A Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Typhoid Paratyphoid A Vaccine\nTyphoid Paratyphoid A Vaccine is a sterile suspension or a \nfreeze-dried solid prepared from one or more strains of \nSalmonella typhi and 8. paratyphi A that are smooth and \nhave the full complement of O, H and Vi antigens in S. typhi \nstrain, and ,0 and H antigens in S. paratyphi A strain. The \nbacteria are killed by heat or by a bactericide such as phenol \nor formaldehyde or by a chemical such as acetone. The liquid \nvaccine arid any diluent supplied by the manufacturer with \nthe freeze-dried vaccine contains a suitable preservative. The \ndried vaccine contains no preservative."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/typhoid-polysaccharide-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Typhoid Polysaccharide Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Typhoid Polysaccharide Vaccine\nFINAL BULK VACCINE\nTyphoid Polysaccharide Vaccine is a preparation of purified\nOne or more batches of purified Vi polysaccharide are \ndissolved in a suitable solvent, which may contain an \nantimicrobial preservative, so that the volume corresponding \nto one dose contains 25 pg of polysaccharide and the solution \nis isotonic with blood (250 mosm/kg to 350 mosm/kg).\nVi capsular polysaccharide obtained from Salmonella typhi \nTy2 strain or some other suitable strain of known origin and\nhistory that has the capacity to  produce  Vi polysaccharide ,\n and which have ben characterized by suitable \n biochemical ,\nPurified Vi Polysaccharide\nphysicochemical, serological or molecular methods.\nThe polysaccharide is purified, after dissociation of the Only a final bulk vaccine that complies with the following \nNlYsaccharide/cetrimonium  bromide complornifit'Ablo \ntequwei\n___„„ments may be used in the preparation of the final lot; \nProcedures to eliminate successively nuclei 4cids, prbtcin$7 .  Sterility (2.2:11). Carry out test for sterility using 10 ml of \nand lipopolysaccharides. The polysacchari \t\na. el\nipitated bulk for each sterility medium.\n,,;,,:-.:`ccharide is a partly 3-0-acetylated repeate d \n•• , \nlamino-2-deoxy-D-galactopyranuronic acid\nkages.\n470\n•\nAntimicrobial preservative. Where applicable, determine the \namount of antimicrobial preservative by a suitable chemical \nmethod. The content is not less than 85.0 per cent and not \ngreater than 115.0 per cent of the intended amount. If phenol \nhas been used in the preparation, the content is not more than \n2.5 el.\nFINAL LOT\nThe final bulk vaccine is distributed and filled aseptically into\nsterile containers. The containers are closed so as to prevent \ncontamination.\nOnly a final lot that is satisfactory with respect to each of the \nrequirements prescribed below under Identification, Tests and \nAssay and with the requirements for Bacterial endotoxins may \nbe released for use. Provided the tests for free formaldehyde\nand antimicrobial preservative have been carried out on the \nfinal bulk vaccine, they may be omitted on the final lot.\nThe vaccine contains minimum of 25 .tg purified Vi-\nPolysaccharide per dose of 0.5 ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/typhoid-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Typhoid Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Typhoid Vaccine\nTyphoid Vaccine is a sterile suspension of inactivated \nSalmonella typhi containing not less than 5 x 10s and not \nmore than 1 x 109 bacteria per human dose. The human dose \ndoes not exceed 1.0 ml.\nThe vaccine is prepared using a seed-lot system from a \nsuitable strain of 8. typhi such as, Ty 2. The final, vaccine \nrepresents not more than 3 subcultures from the strain on \nwhich were made the laboratory and clinical tests that showed \nit to be suitable. The bacteria are inactivated by acetone, by \nformaldehyde, by phenol or by heating or by a combination\nof the last two methods.\nThe production method is validated to demonstrate that the \nproduct, if tested, would comply with the test for abnormal \ntoxicity (2.2.1 ) modified to the extent that 0.5 ml of the vaccine\nis injected subcutaneously or intramuscularly or \nintraperitoneally into each mouse and 1.0 ml into each guinea- \npig-\nIt is identified by specific agglutination.\nPhenol (2.3.36).- If phenol has been used in the preparation \nthe concentration is not more than 0.5 per cent w/v.\nAntigenic power. When injected into susceptible laboratory \nanimals, it elicits anti-O, anti-H and, to a lesser extent, anti-Vi \nagglutinins.\nBacterial and fungal contamination. Complies with the test \nfor sterility (2.2.11).\nLabelling. The label states (1) the method used to inactivate \nthe bacteria; (2) the number of bacteria per human dose.\nTyphoid Vaccine (Freeze Dried)\nFreeze Dried Typhoid Vaccine is a freeze-dried preparation of \ninactivated Salmonella typhi. The vaccine is reconstituted as \nstated on the label to give a uniform suspension containing \nnot less than 5 x 108 and not more than 1 x 109 bacteria per \nhuman dose. The human dose does not exceed 1.0 ml of the \nreconstituted vaccine.\nThe vaccine is prepared from a seed-lot system from a suitable\nstrain of 8. typhi, such as Ty 2. The final vaccine represents \nnot more than 3 subcultures from the strain on which were \nmade the laboratory and clinical tests that showed it to be\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTYPHOID Vi CONJUGATE VACCINE\nsuitable. The bacteria are inactivated either by acetone or by \nformaldehyde or by heat Phenol is not used in the preparation\nThe vaccine is distributcd into sterile containers and freeze \ndried to a moisture content favourable to thé stability of the \nvaccine. \n: \n:• \n.■ . ■, ;.. ..\nThe production method is validated to demonstrate that the \nproduct, if tested, would comply with test for abnormal toxicity \n(2.2.1), modified to the extent that 0.5 ml of the vaccine is \ninjected subcutaneously or intramuscularly or\nintraperitoneally into each mouse and 1.0 ml into each guinea"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/typhus-vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Typhus Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Typhus Vaccine\nTyphus Vaccine is a sterile suspension of killed rickettsiae of \na strain or strains of epidemic typhus rickettsiae (Rickettsia\nprowazeki) selected for antigenic efficiency.\nThe vaccine is prepared by injecting virulent rickettsiae into \nthe yolk sacs of fertile eggs which have been incubated for 7 \ndays. Within 9 to 13 days after the injection, the yolk sacs are \ncollected under aseptic conditions and subjected to suitable \ntreatment to liberate the maximum number of rickettsiae. The \nmaterial is suspended in  saline solution or other suitable \nsolution isotonic with blood to which formaldehyde solution \nhas been added so that the concentration of formaldehyde is \n0.2 per cent to 0.5 per cent. The suspension so formed contains \n10 per cent to 15 per cent w/w of yolk sac tissue. It is purified \nby treatment with ether. The aqueous middle layer of the\nresultant mixture is collected and distributed under aseptic \nconditions into sterile containers which are then sealed so as \nto exclude micro-organisms.\nThe vaccine may also be prepared from the lungs of small \nrodents in which rickettsial pneumonias have been caused by\ninhalation of massive doses of virulent rickettsiae, or from the \nperitoneal cavities of gerbils which have received\nintraperitoneal injections of rickettsiae.\nDescription. A  slightly turbid liquid; a white deposit of the \nrickettsiae may separate on standing which can be readily \nredistributed on shaking."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ulipristal-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ulipristal Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ulipristal Acetate\nCH3 ' \n: 2\nCwHrzNCh \nMol Wt. 475.6\nUlipristal Acetate is 17a-acetoxy-l ip-(4-dimétylàminophênyl)- \n3.20-dioxo-19-nor-4,9-pregnadicne. ,J ........ . >\nUlipristal Acetate contains riot less than 98.0 per bent arid not \nmore than 102.0 per cent of C30H37 NO4, calculated on dried \nbasis. \n' '\nCategory. Sélective progesterone receptor modulator (SPRM).\nDescription. A white to yellow powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/undecylenic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Undecylenic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Undecylenic Acid\nH\nH2C~COOH\nC,JI200 2\nMol. Wt. 184.3\nUndecenoic Acid is 10-undecenoic acid.\nUndecenoic Acid contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not\nmore than 102.0 per cent ofC llH200 2•\nCategory. Antifungal (topical).\nDescription. A white or very pale yellow, crystalline mass or\ncolourless or pale yellow liquid; odour, characteristic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/urea-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Urea Cream"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Urea Cream\nUrea Cream contains Urea in a suitable cream base.\nÙrèa Cream contains nói less than 90.0 per cent and not more \nthan 110.0 per cent of the stated amount o f urea, CH4N2O.:\nUsual strength. 10 per cent w/w."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/urokinase","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Urokinase"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Urokinase\nUrokinase is an enzyme, obtained from human urine that\nactivates plasminogen. Itconsists ofa mixture oflow molecular\nweight and high molecular weight forms, the high molecular\nweight form being predominant. The molecular weights ofthe\nlow and high molecular weight forms are 33,000 and 54,000\nrespectively.\nIt is prepared in conditions designed to minimise microbial\nand viral contamination. In particular, adequate measures, such\nas heating the substance in solution at 60° for 10 hours, are\ntaken to inactivate viruses.\nUrokinase contains not less than 70,000 Units of urokinase\nactivity per mg ofprotein.\nCategory. Fibrinolytic enzyme.\nDose. By instillation into arteriovenous shunt and for\nintraocular administration, 5000 to 37,500 Units in 2 to 3 ml of\nSodium Chloride Injection.\nDescription. A white or almost white, amorphous powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/ursodeoxycholic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Ursodeoxycholic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Ursodeoxycholic Acid\nC24H.;o04 \nMol. Wt. 392.6\nUrsodeoxycholic Acid is 3a,7p-dihydroxy-5£-cholan-24-oic \nacid. - e, rmmJw \nrm i.-h. jiBhsæi 'O\nUrsodexoxycholic acid contains not less itiaii 99.0 per cent \nand not more than 10L0 per cent of C24IL0O4, calculated on \nthe dried basis. \n'■\nCategory. Treatment of gallstones. \n.?. <\nDescription À white or almost white powder.\nIdentification ; \n/, \n; uu .\nTests B and Cmay be omitted if test A is carried out. Test A \nmay be omitted if tests B and C are carried out.\nA< Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained vvith wrA'üi/eojy-Uwfc \nacid IPRS or with the reference spectrum of ursodeoxycholic\nacid. _ \ny \"\nB. ; In the test i for Impurity C; the principal ; spot in the \nchromatogram obtained with test-solutionj(h) corresponds to \nthe .principal spot in the chromatogram, obtained with referent\nsolution (a). \n: \n•■•ku \n\"\nwww.webofpharma.com\np: \n...-..................................... ............ ..............................\n• nlveTO’fog infr of sulphuric acid. Add 0.1 ml of \n^ Idehyde solution and allow to stand; for 5 minutes, add \n'^qf susPens*on obtained is greenish-blue.\nsneUficopfo->1 rotation (2.4.22). +58.0° to +62.0°, determined \n¿To per centw/v, solution in ethanol.\n• Pppp<-L '\nSolvent rnixture- 10 volumes of water and 90 volumes of\n^^gtpetennineby thin-layer chromatography (2.4,17), \nXtmgttep1^ 'vilh silica Sel;\nlabile phase. Amixture of 1 volume of glacial acetic acid.\n30 volumes of acetone and 60 volumes of dichloromethane.\nTest-solution (^/ Dissolve 0.4 g of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 10.0 ml with\nthe solvent mixture.\nTest solution (h).Dilute 1.0 ml of test solution (a) to 10.0 ml \nwiththesolvcntmixture. ;\nReference solution (a). A 0.4 per cent w/v solution of \nwry^mycteZ/c^czk/ //J/?.S' in the solvent mixture.\n■ ■/■iiuuWu}io vw u.■■ \n■\nRefer^nce^ofutipp (b). A 0.2 per cent w/v solution of \nlithqch^ii^..,(ieid(iPtRS. ( ursodeoxycholic acid impurity C\n2 chenodeoxycholic acid.\n1PRS) in the solvent mixture (Solution A). Dilute 2.0 ml of the \nsolutioii to l00.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nReference solution (c). To 5.0 ml of solution A, add 10 mg of \nchenqdebxychblic acid IPRS (ursodeoxycholic acid impurity \nAIPRS)'apd dilute to 50.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nApplyttpfheipl^te.S: pi of :each solution.' Allow the mobile \nphase to rise l 5 cm. Dry the plate at 120° for 10 minutes and \nspray immediately with a 4.76 per cent w/v solution of \nphosphomolybdic acid ip a mixture of 1 volume of sulphuric\nand.20, volumes of glacial acetic acid and heat at 120° \n^^bblueiSppts appear on a lighter background. In the \nchrpmatpgram obtained, with reference solution (c), the \nchromatogram shows 2 clearly separated spots. In the \nchromatogram obtained with test solution (a), any spot due to \n^deoxycholicacidimpurilyC is not more intense than the\npnncipal spot in the chromatogram obtained with reference \n^oiifo) (0.1 per cent).\n^^^Iwiahces. Determine by liquid chromatography\nio volumes of methanol and 90 volumes of\n^rcobile;phaSe, .... . ■ . \n. . \n. ... . ,...\nsbltitibh. Dissolve 60 mg of the substance under \nexamination in the solvent mixture and dilute to 20.0 ml with \n!he solvent mixture. ; \n, •\nURSODEOXYCHOLIC ACID\nReference solution. Dilute 1.0 ml of the test solution to\n100.0 ml with the solvent mixture. Dilute 1.0 ml of the solution \nto 10.0 ml with the solvent mixture.\nChromatographic system\n- \na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \nendcapped octadecylsilane bonded to porous silica \n(5pm), \n. ; _\n- column temperature: 40°, \n\" \n‘\n- mobile phase: a mixture of 30 volumes pi acetonitrile,\n37 volumes of a 0.078 per cent w/v solution of sodium \ndihydrogen phosphate, adjusted to pH 3-0 with \northophosphoric acid and 40 volumes of methanol,\n- flow rate: 0.8 ml per minute,\n- refractometer detector at 35°,\n- injection volume: 150 pl.\nName\nRelative \nretentiontime\nUrsodeoxycholic"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vaccine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vaccine"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vaccine\nMeningococcal Polysaccharide Vaccine is â freeze-dried \npjepa£atiop.of one .or more purified capsular polysaccharides \nobtained-from one or more suitable strains of Neisseria \nmeningitidis group A, group C, group Y and group W135 that \narecapable of consistently producing polysaccharides known \nth be safe and effective in man.\nJpj ic- : .c- ■ ; \n'w': ■ \n■. \n. ' ' ■■■ \n■\nWhupiingitidis group A polysaccharide consists of partly\nO-acetylated repeating units of N-acetylmannosamine, linked \nwith la->6 phosphodiester bonds?\n^meningitidis group C polysaccharide consists of partly \nQ-acetylated repeating units of sialic, acid, linked with 2a->9 \nglycosidic bonds. . \n- . . . ■,. \n; ; ... \n. . \n.\nh^nienihgiiidis^ group Y: polysaccharide consists of partly \nO-acetylatcd alternating units of sialic acid and D-glucose, \nlinked with 2a->6 and la-^4 glycosidic bonds.\n^.meningitidis group W135 polysaccharide consists of partly \n(^cetylated alternating units oftsialic acid and D-galactose. \nfaked wjth 2a—>6 and\" 1 a- »4 glycosidic bonds... . . r . - - ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valerian-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valerian Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Valerian Dry Extract\nValerian Dry Extract is produced from dried root & rhizomes of \nvaleriana officinalis L or valeriana wallichi DC or a mixture of \nboth by extraction with a suitable solvent using an appropriate \nprocedure.\nValerian Dry Extract contains 90.0 to 110.0 per cent of the \nlabeled amount of sesquiterpenic acid expressed as valerenic \nScid(C]SH22O2), on dried basis."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valerian-root","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valerian Root"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Valerian Root"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valproate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valproate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Valproate Injection\nValproate Sodium Injection\nValproate Injection is a sterile solution ofsodium valproate in\nWater for Injections with one or more suitable buffering or\nsequestering agents.\nValproate Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 110.0 per cent ofthe stated amount ofvalproic\nacid, CSH I60 2•\nUsual strength. 20 mg per ml."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valproic-acid-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valproic Acid Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Valproic Acid Oral Solution\nValproic Acid Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount p/\nvalproic acid, CsHl6O2.\nUsual strengths. 40mgpcrml; 50mgper ml. ' \n2^"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/valproic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Valproic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Valproic Acid\nMol. Wt. 144.2\nValproic acid is 2-propylpentanoic acid.\nValproic acid contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not\nmore than 101.0 percent of CSH I60 2•\nCategory. Anticonvulsant.\nDescription. A colourless orvery slightly yellow liquid, slightly\nviscous."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin-hydrochloride-for-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution is a dry powder\nconsisting of Vancomycin Hydrochloride with or without\nexcipients. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Powders and Granules for Oral\nSolutions and Oral Suspensions stated under Oral Liquids\nand with the following requirements.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution contains not less\nthan 88.0 per cent ofVancomycin B."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vancomycin Hydrochloride\n, Hel\nMol.Wt. 1485.7\nVancomycin Hydrochloride is a mixture ofrelated\nglycopeptides, consisting principally of the\nmonohydrochloride of(3S,6R,7R,22R,23S,26S,30aS,36R,\n38aR)-3-(2-amino-2-oxoethyl)-44-[[2-0-(3-amino-2,3,6-\ntrideoxy-3-C-methyl-{3-L-lyxo-hexopyranosyl)-{3-D-\nglucopyranosyl]oxy]-10,19-dichloro-7,22,28,30,32-\npentahydroxy-6-[[(2R)-4-methyl-2-\n(methylamino)pentanoyl]amino]-2,5,24,38,39-pentaoxo-\n2,3,4,5,6,7,23,24,25,26,36,37,38,38a-tetradecahydro-22H-\n8,11:18,21-dietheno-23,36-(iminomethano)-13,16:31,35-\ndimetheno-lH,13H-[1,6,9]oxadiazacyclohexadecinol[4,5-m]\n[10,2,16]benzoxadiazacyclotetracosine-26-carboxylic acid\n(vancomycin B).\nVANCOMYCIN HYDROCHLORIDE\nIt is produced by certain strains ofAmycolatopsis orientalis\nor obtained by any other means.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride contains not less than 1050 ill\nper mg ofC66H7sClzNg024, RCI, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis and not less than 93.0 per cent ofvancomycin B.\nCategory. Antibacterial.\nDescription. A white or almost white hygroscopic powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin-intravenous-infusion","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin Intravenous Infusion"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vancomycin Intravenous Infusion\nVancomycin Intravenous Infusion is a sterile solution of \nVancomycin Hydrochloride in Water for Injections. It is \nprepared by dissolving Vancomycin.Hydrochloride for \nIntravenous Infusion in Water for injections and then diluting\nwith the requisite volume of a suitable diluent in accordance \nwith the manufacturer’s instructions.\nVancomycin Intravenous Infusion contains not less than 90.0 \nper,cent and not more than 115.0 per cent of the stated amount \nof vancomycin, C/.0H -<Cl2N9O24.\nThe intravenous infusion complies with the tests stated under \nParenteral Preparations.,\nStorage. , Vancomycin Intravenous Infusion should be used \nimmediately after preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer when prepared and stored \nstrictly in accordance with the manufacturer’s instructions.\nUsual strengths. 500 mg; 1 g.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Intravenous"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vancomycin-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vancomycin Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vancomycin Oral Solution\nVancomycin Oral Solution is a solution of Vancomycin \nHydrochloride in a suitable flavoured vehicle. It is prepared\n3924\n..... ................ . .......... .............. .........................................\nby dissolving Vancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution \nthe requisite amount of a suitable diluent. : : i : \n,\nThe oral solution complies-with the tests stated under Oral \nLiquids. \n. . \n,.\nStorage. Vancomycin. Oral Solution should be used \nimmediately after preparation but, in any case, within the period \nrecommended by the manufacturer when prepared and stored \nstrictly in accordance with the manufacturer’sinstructionsV\nUsual strength. 50 mg per mi. \n' :\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution is a dry powder \nconsisting of Vancomycin Hydrochloride with or without \nexcipients. It is filled in a sealed container.\nThe contents of the sealed container, comply with the \nrequirements, stated under Powders and Granules for Oral \nSolutions and Oral Suspensions stated under Oral Liquids, \nand with the following requirements.\nVancomycin Hydrochloride for Oral Solution contains not less \nthan 88.0 per cent of Vancomycin B."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vanillin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vanillin"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vanillin\nCHOo\nyOCH3\nOH\nCSHS0 3\nMol. Wt. 152.2\nVanillin is 4-hydroxy-3-methoxybenzaldehyde.\nVanillin contains not less than 99.0 percent and not more than\n101.0 per cent ofCSHS0 3, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (flavouring agent).\nDescription. A white or slightly yellow powder or crystalline\nneedles; odour, characteristic of vanilla."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vasaka","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vasaka"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vasaka\nAdulasa; Adhatoda vasica\nIF*2022:\nVasaka consists of the dried mature leaves of Adhatoda vasica \nNees.(Fam. Acanthaceae). • \n.;\nVasaka contains not less than 0.6 per cent w/w of vasicine \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n’\nCategory. Expectorant, bronchodilator, kustha.\nDescription.Taste, bitter."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vasopressin-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vasopressin Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vasopressin Injection\nVasopressin Injection is sterile solution of vasopressin in \nsuitable diluent.\nEach mJ of Vasopressin Injection contains an activity of not \nless than 90.0 per cent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the \nstated on the label in Vasopressin Units. It may contain a \nsuitable preservative.\nUsual strength. 20 Units per ml.\nIn the Assay, the principalpeak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with the reference solution.\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.5.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Less than 17.0 Endotoxin Units \nper Unit of Vasopressin.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Injections).\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).\nSolvent mixture. Dissolve 5.0 g ofchlorobutqnol in 5.0 ml of \nglacial acetic acid, add 1.1 g of sodium acetate, 5.0 g of\nethanol (95 per cent), and dilute to 1000 ml with water.\nTest solution. Dilute 2.0 ml (20 Units per ml) of the injection to \n25.0 ml with 0.25 per cent w/v of glacial acetic acid.\nReference solution (a). Dissolve the contents of one vial of \nvasopressin IPRS in a known volume of the solvent mixture to \nobtain the same concentration of test solution.\nReference solution (b). A solution containing 0.0025 per cent \nw/v, each of, lypressin IPRSand vasopressin IPRS in 0.25 per \ncent v/v glacial acetic acid.\nChromatographic system\n- a stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with\n' octadecylsilanê bonded to porous silica (5 pm),\n- column temeperature: 40°,\n- \nmobile phase : a mixture of 87 volumes of buffer solution \nprepared by dissolving 6.6 g of dibasic ammonium \nphosphate in about 950 ml of water, adjusted to pH 3.0\nwith orthophosphoric acid, dilute to 1000 ml with water \nand 13 volumes of acetonitrile,\n- flow rate : 1 ml per minute,\n- spectrophotometersetat220nm,\n- injection volume: 20 pl.\nInject reference solution (b). The test is not valid unless the \nresolution between vasopressin and lypressin is not less than\n1.1 and relative standard deviation for replicate injections is \nnot more than 2.0 per cent.\nInject reference solution (a) and the test solution.\nCalculate the content of \nin the injection.\nStorage. Store in single-dose or multiple-dose containers, \npreferably of type I glass. Do not freeze.\nLabelling. The label states (1) the number of Units of the \nVasopressin Units per ml; (2) indicate its origin (animal or \nsynthetic).\n3927\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vecuronium-bromide-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vecuronium Bromide Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vecuronium Bromide Injection\nVecuronium Bromide Injection is a sterile material consisting \nof Vecuronium Bromide with or without auxiliary agents. It is \nsupplied in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the\nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile Water for \nEjections, immediately before use. -"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vecuronium-bromide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vecuronium Bromide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vecuronium Bromide\nC34H57BrN2O4 \n' MoI.Wt.638\nVecuronium Bromide is l-(3a,17p-Diacetoxy-2p-piperidino- \n5a-androstan-16 (3 -yl) -1 -methylpiperidiniurh bromide.\nVecuronium Bromide contains pot less than 99.0 per cent and \nnot more than 101.0 per cent of C34H57BrN2O4, calculated on \nthe anhydrous basis. \n-r---■■'■■■ ■■>■\nCategory. Non depolarizing neuromuscular blocker.\nDescription. A white or almost white crystals or crystalline \npowder. ... \n. \n...... rf.... ....."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/venlafaxine-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Venlafaxine Hydrochloride\nCI7H28C1NO2 \n< Mol. Wt. 313.9\nVECURONIUM BROMIDE INJECTION\nIp202i\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride is l-[(17?iST)-2-(dimethylamino)-uir \nmethoxyphenyl)elhyl|cyclohexanol hydrochloride.\nVenlafaxine Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 percent\nand, not more than 101.0 per cent of Ci7ll2SClNO2, calculate \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Antidepressant. \nU’/y.'\nDescription. A white or almost white powder. It shows \npolymorphism (2.5.11)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/verapamil-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Verapamil Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Verapamil Hydrochloride\n■^apanril Chloride; Iproveratril Hydrochloride\n^H38N2O4, HC1, \nMol. Wt. 491.1\nye^apamil Hydrochloride is (R5)-2-(3,4-dimethpxyphenyl)-\n5-[[2-(3,4-dimethoxyphenyl)ethyl](methyl)amino] -\n, 2-(l -mcihyiethyljpentanenitrile hydrochloride.\nVerapamil Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per cent \naid not more than 101.0 per cent of C27H3SN2O4, HC1, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Calcium channel blocker.\n^scription. A white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/verapamil-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Verapamil Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Verapamil Injection\nVerapamil Hydrochloride Injection; Verapamil Chloride \nInj ection; Iproveratril Hydrochloride Inj ection\nVerapamil Injection is a sterile: solution of. Verapamil \nHydrochloride in Water for Injections. ■\nVeräpamirInjection contains not less than 90.0 per Cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of verapamil \nhydrochloride, CVILsVO.-,, HCl. \n\"\nUsual strength. 2.5 mg per ml. , ."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/veterinary-oral-suspension","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Veterinary Oral Suspension"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Veterinary Oral Suspension\nSulphadiazine and Trirnethoprim Mixture; Trimethoprim\nand Sulphadiazine Veterinary Oral Suspension;\nCo-trimazine Oral Suspension; Co-trimazine Mixture\nSulphadiazine and Trimethoprin'-t Veterinary Oral Suspension\nis a suspension of Sulphadiazine and Trimethoprim in the\nproportion of five parts to one part respectively, containing\nsuitable suspending and dispersing agents. It may contain\nsuitable antimicrobial preservatives.\nSulphadiazine and Trimethoprim Veterinary Oral Suspension\ncontains not less than 90.0 per cent and not more than\n110.0 per cent of the stated amounts of sulphadiazine,\nCIOHION40 2S, and oftrimethoprim, C14HISN403'\nUsual strengths. 40 per cent w/v of Sulphadiazine and 8 per\ncent w/v ofTrimethoprim; 4.55 per cent w/v ofSulphadiazine\nand 0.91 per cent w/v ofTrimethoprim."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vidanga","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vidanga"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vidanga\n3.2.2. THIN LAYER CHROMATOGRAMS OF HERBS AND HERBAL PRODUCTS\nIP 2022"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vildagliptin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vildagliptin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vildagliptin\nVildagliptin is (S)-1 -[2-(3-Hydroxyadamant-1 -ylamino)acetyl] \npyrrôlidine-2-carbonitrile.\nVildagliptin contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 1Q2.0 per cent of C17H25N3O2, calculated on the dried \nbasis..-./.\nCategory. Antidiabetic.\nDescription. A white to off white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vinblastine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vinblastine Injection","la":"Identification"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vinblastine Injection\nVinblastine Sulphate Injection\nVinblastine Injection is a sterile material consisting of\nVinblastine Sulphate with or without auxiliary substances. It\nis filled in a sealed container.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents ofthe\nsealed container in the requisite amount ofsterile 0.9 per cent\nw/v solution ofSodium Chloride, immediately before use.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nVinblastine Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent and\nnot more than 11 0.0 per cent of the stated amount of\nvinblastine sulphate, C46H5sN409,H2S04.\nUsual strength. 10 mg.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Injection) and with thefollowing requirements.\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVINBLASTINE INJECTION"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vinblastine-sulphate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vinblastine Sulphate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vinblastine Sulphate Injection\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDose. By intravenous injection, according to the n \npatient.\nVinblastinc Injection is a sterile material consisting of \nVinblastine Sulphate with or without auxiliary substances. It \nis filled in a sealed container.\nReference solution (c). A 0.002 per cent w/v solution of \nvinblastine sulphate RS in water.\nDescription. A white or yellowish, amorphous or crystalline \npowder; very hygroscopic.\nReference solution (d).  A 0.0001 per cent w/v solution of \nvinblaitine sulphate RS in water.\nThe injection is constituted by dissolving the contents of the \nsealed container in the requisite amount of sterile 0.9 per cent \nw/v solution of Sodium Chloride, immediately before use.\nCAUTION- Handle Vinhlastine Sulphate with \nsince it is a potent cytotoxic agent. Avoid contact wi\nChromatographic system\na stainless steel column 25 cm x 4.6 mm, packed with \noctylsilane bonded to porous silica (5 rim), (b) a guard \ncolumn packed with a suitable silica gel placed between \nthe pump and the injection device, \nmobile phase: a mixture of 50 volumes of methanol, \n38 volumes of a 1.5 per cent v/v solution  of diethylamine \nadjusted to pH 7.5 with phosphoric acid and 12 volumes \nof acetonitrile, \nflow rate: 1 ml per minute, \nspectrophotometer set at 262 nm, \ninjection volume: 10 p3.\nirritant to tissues.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for \nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under \nParenteral Preparations (Injections)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/vinblastine-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Vinblastine Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Vinblastine Sulphate\nC4óHS8N4O9.H2SO4 \n- - Mol. Wt. 909.1\nVINBLASTINE SULPHATE\n.........................................................................................\nVinblastine Sulphate is methyl (3a/?,4R,5S,5aK, 10bR, 13 a/?)- 4- \nacetoxy-3a-ethyl-9-^[(5tS,l77?^9iS')-5-ethyl-5^hydroxy- \n9-methoxy carbonyl-1,4,5,6,7,8,9,10-octahydro-2/Z-3,7- \nmethanoazacycloundecino[(5,4-h)indoI-9-yl]-5-hydroxy-8- \nmcthoxy-6-methyl-3 a, 4,5,5 a,6,11,1'2; 13a-octahydro-1/7- \nindolizino[8,l-ci/]carbazole-5~carboxylate sulphate.\nVinblastine Sulphate contains hot less than 95.0 per cent and \nnot more than 104.0 per cent of C46i LSN4O9, H2SQ4, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Anticancer.\nDescription. A, white or yellowish/ amorphous or crystalline \npowder; very hygroscopic.\nCAUTION—Handle Vinblastine Sulphate with great care \nsince it is a potent cytotoxic agent. Avoid contact with eyes; \nirritant to tissues.\nA. Determine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6). \nCompare the spectrum with that obtained with vinblastine \nsulphate IPRS or with the reference spectrum of; vinblastine \nsulphate: \", < \n■ •?-.. • c\nB. In the Assay, the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with the test solution corresponds to the peak in the \nchromatogram obtained with reference solution (b).\nC. A 10 per cent w/v solution gives the reaction of sulphates \n(2.3.1). \n( \nJ\nTests ■ ■ ■ \n■ ■\nAppearance of solution. A 0.5 per cent w/v solution in carbon \ndioxide-free water is clear (2.4.1), and hot more intensely \ncoloured than reference solution YS5 (2.4.1).\npH (2,4.24)- 3.5 to 5.0,dctemiined in a 0.15 per cent w/v solution.\nRelated substances. In the Assay, the area of any secondary \npeak in the chromatogram obtained with the test , solution is \nnot greater than that of the principal peak in the chromatogram \nobtained with reference solution (c) and the sum of the areas \nof any such peaks is not greater than 2.5, times the area of the \nprincipal peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference\nsolution (c). Ignore any peak with an area less, than that \nof the peak in the chromatogram Obtained with reference \nsolution (d).\nLoss on drying (2.4.19). Not more than 15.0 per cent,\ndetermined by Method B, on an appropriately calibrated \ninstrument using about 3.0'ing, weighed! Heat the substance \nunder examination at the rate of 5° per minute between ambient \ntemperature and 200° in a current of nitrogen for \nchromatography with a flow rate of 40 ml per minute. From the \nthermogram, determine the accumulated loss in weight between\n3945\nwww.webofpharma.com\nVINBLASTINE SULPHATE\nambient temperature and a point; on the plateau before \ndecomposition is indicated (àt’about 160°). A-j ; / r.-mm;\nAssay. Determine by liquid chromatography (2.4.14).! : J f ' \n■■/A-/,' a-: ri <. \n''AU..? û.;U \n;îî wb'.-m; xa' ; ; rflw ■\nTest solution. Dissolve 0.1 ;g .qf the substance under \nexamination in.100 ml of water. . ; . \nA . ; .\nReference solution (a). A solution containing. 0.1 per cent\nw/v.each of vinblastine sulphate IPRS and