while stirring \ncontinuously, ■•mr... i u* \n.\n^¿flowrate: 1 ml per minute, \n; a\n-^spectrophotometer at 25.6 nm,\ninjection volume: 20 pl. \n=\nName\nRelative \nretention time\nCorrection\nfactor\nVoriconazole impurity A1\n025\n\\ 0-7\nVoriconazole impurity C2\n. . 0.3\n°-7\nVoriconazole impurity B3:\n. \n0.6 ....\n2.1\nVoriconazole (Retention time: \nabout 8 minutes) *\n1.0\n'l-(2,4-difluorophenyl)-2-(l H-l,2,4“triazol-: t-yl)ethanpne,\n24-ethyl-5-fluoropyrimidine,\n’(2Æ.S',35?î)-2-(2,4-difluorophenyl)-3-pyrimidin-4-yl-1-( 1 II-1,2,4- \ntriazol-1- yl)butan-2-oL\nUse the chromatogram obtained with reference solution (b) to \nidentify the peaks due to voriconazole impurities A and C and \nusetihe chromatogram obtained with reference solution (d) to\nidentify the peak dtie to voriconazole impurity B. '\nInject reference solution (b).The test is not valid unless thé \nresolution between thé peaks due to voriconazole impurity À \nand voriconazole impurity € is not less than 1 . 8\nInject reference solution (c) and the test solution. Run the \nchromatogram 3 times the retention time of the principal peak. \nIn the chromatogram obtained with the test solution, the area \nof any secondary peak due to voriconazole impurities A, B \nand C is not more than 1.5 times the area of the principal peak \nin the chromatogram obtained with réference solution (c) (O'. 15 \nper cent), the area of any other secondary peaknot more than \nthe area of the principal peak in the chromatogram obtained \nwith reference;solution.(c) ({M per cent) and the sum oftiie \nareas of all the secondary peaks is, not more than .5 times the \narea of the principal peakin thechrpmatogramobtained with \nreference sbhition:(c) (Q.5;per cent). Ignore any peak with an \narea less than 0.5 times"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/warfarin-sodium-clathrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Warfarin Sodium Clathrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Warfarin Sodium Clathrate \n-\nWarfarin Sodium Clathrate is a clathrate form of Warfarin \nSodium consisting principally; of Warfarin Sodium and \nIsopropyl Alcohol ip a 2:1 molecular ratio. \nAz :m - ;\nWarfarin Sodium Clathrate1 contains not less than 97.0 per \ncent and more than 102.0 per cent of Cl9H15NuO4caiculatedori \nthe anhydrous, isopropyl alcohol-free basis and not less than\n8.0 per cent and not more than 8.5 per cent of isopropy alcohol.\nCategory. Anticoagulant. .......... .\nDescription. A white crystalline powder; hygroscopic.\nIdentification ; z ; < \n\\ z /\nTest A may be omitted if test B, C, D and E are carried out. \nTests B and D may be omitted if tests A, C grid E are carried \nout. \n\" ' '................‘\nA.' Dissolve about 1 g in 25 ml of water, add 2 ml of 2 M \nhydrochloric acidand filter. Wash the precipitate 5 to .6 times \nwith water. Dry the residue over phosphorus pentoxide.\nOn the residue, determine by infrared absorption \nspectrophotometry (2.4,6). Compare the spectrum with that \nobtained with warfarin sodium 777?//treated in the same \nmanner or with the reference spectrum of warfarin, u r>.. ■. ; ;\nB.' in the test for delated substances, die principal peak in thé \n,qmoriiatogram obtained with'tljé'test' solution coriespondsfo\nthé peak in the chromatogram obtained with reference solution\nCoDissolve l;g in 10:ml '.ofwater, add 5 ml of nrir/c acid and \nfilters To the filtrate; add 2 ml cfpptassium dichromate solution, \nshake for; 5 minutes and allow to’ stand; for 20 minutes; the \nsolution is not greenish-blue when compared with a blank.\nD. The residue obtained in test A, after washing with water \narid dry ing al 105° melts at 159° to 163°. \n■■■■■■■■' r -j- ■ ■\nE. The filtrate; obtained in test A gives the reactions of sodium \nsalts (2.3.1). \n...m\nIP 2022\nWARFARIN SODIUM CLATHRATE"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/warfarin-sodium","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Warfarin Sodium"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Warfarin Sodium\no\nMol. Wt. 330.3\nWarfarin Sodium is sodium 2-oxo-3-[(1RS)-3-oxo-l-phenyl-\nbutyl]-2H-l-benzopyran-4-0Iate.\nWarfarin Sodium contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not\nmore than 102.0 per cent of CI9HlSNa04, calculated on the\nanhydrous basis.\nCategory. Anticoagulant.\nDose. 10 to 15 mg.\nDescription. A white powder; hygroscopic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/water-for-injection-to","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Water for Injection to"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Water for Injection to \n100ml\nNOTE — 14 ml is to be usedfor 100 ml of whole blood.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/water-for-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Water for Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Water for Injection\n100 ml\n100 ml\nNOTE — 15 ml of solution A or 25 ml of solution B are to be \nusedfor 100 ml of whole blood.\nDescription. A clear, colourless or faintly straw-coloured liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/water-for-injections","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Water for Injections"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Water for Injections\nH2O . \nMol. Wt. 18.0\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid. i , n : \n7.\nWater, for Injections is water intended for use in the \npreparations of medicines for parenteral administration when \nwater is used as a vehicle (Water for injections inbulk) and for \ndissolving of diluting substances or preparations, forihjecfable \npreparations (Sterile Water for Inj ections). \n: ’ 'T\"\nWater for Injections in Bulk\nWater for Injections is prepared by using purified water. This \nwater is intended for use in the preparations of medicines for \nparenteral administration or for ophthalmic preparations; It is \nused as a vehicle for dissolving or diluting substances or for \npreparation of Sterile .Water for Injections.\nIt is prepared from Purified Water through distillation (phase \nseparation) process or any other process that is equivalent or \nsuperior to distillation in the removal of chemicals; and \nmicroorganisms such as reverse osmosis, coupled with \nappropriate techniques. Distillation stills ârë constructed of \nneutral glass, quartz or suitable metal & must be'suitably \nmaintainedto ensure the production of water freefrom bacterial \nendotoxin. It is collected and stored in conditions designed to \nprevent the growth of micro-organisms and to avoid any other \nre-contamination such as bacterial endotoxin.\nDuring production and subsequent storage, adequAt9 \nmeasures are taken to enàüre thàt the microbial quality is \ncontrolled and monitored. The total viable count (2:2.9),is\n10 CFU per >100 ml, detennincd by membrane filtration using at \nleast 200 ml of this water is to be maintained.; Appropriate \naction limits are set so as to detect any adverse notice . >\nAdequacy of water purification measures is demonstrated by\nfollowing tests. ••<<-••••« \n-\nMicrobial contamination (2.2.9). Total viable count not more \nthan 10 CFU per 100 ml. \n.vu;.-/\nTotal organic carbon (2.4.30). Not more than 0.5 mg per litre \n(500ppb). \n' : \n'\nConductivity (2.4.9). Meets the requirements of the test. '\nDescription. A clear, colourless and odourless liquid. <\nAcidity or alkanity. To 10 ml, freshly bbiled and cooled in a \nborosilicate glass; flask; add 0.05 ml of methyl.red solution-,\n3965\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/weight-per-millilitre-and-relative-density","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Weight Per Millilitre and Relative Density"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Weight Per Millilitre and Relative Density\n303\n(Specific Gravity)\n3\