133, 4133 \nXylometazoline Nasal Drops \n\"3975 \nZine Chloride, 0.1M \n1150 \nXylometazoline Hydrochloride and Sorbitol \n, \nZinc Chloride-Formic. \nAcid Solution \n“1134\n~ Nasal Drops \n3976 \nZinc Chloride Injection \n4133 \np-Xylopyranose \n3977 \nZine Chloride Solution \nee \n1134 \nXylose \n\"297, 1133,3977 \nZine Chloride Solution, Todinated \n\"1134 \nD-Xylose \noy 37 \nZine Citrate \n| \n297, 4134 \nXylose-Lysine-Desoxycholate Agar Me ¢ \n50 \nZine Cream \n\"3993\n. \nbe \nbee \n. cE \nSuge \ncats \n. \nZincon \n\"TB4 \nyoo \na \ncortzns , + \n Zineon Solution \n1134\nSole \nMeslay fe: \n© \nZine Dithiol Reagent \n1134\nYasti \n_.287,1056,4320 \nZine Dust \n_ 1134 \nYasti Dry Extract” \n*_,928,1057,4321 \nZinc, Granulated’ \nneed 134, 144\n1-214\n. 1433 \nZidovudine Tablets \n- 3989\n  \n \nwww.webofpharma.com\nINDIAN PHARMACOPOEIA 2022\n297,3992\nZinc Oxide\n3993, 4943\nZinc Oxide Cream\n1134\nZinc Powder\n3993\nZinc Oxide and Salicylic Acid Paste\n169\nZinc Salts, Tests for\n1134\nZinc Shot\n1143\nZine Standard Solution (10 ppm Zn)\n1143\nZinc Standard Solution (25 ppm Zn)\n1143\nZine Standard Solution (100 ppm Zn)\n298, 3994\nZine Stearate\n298, 1134, 4134\nZinc Sulphate\n1150\nZinc Sulphate, 0.1 M\n3004\nZine Sulphate Eye Drops\n1134\nZinc Sulphate Solution\n1134 \n298,4135\nZine Sulphate, x M\nZine Sulphate Monohydrate\n4135\nZine Sulphate Dispersible Tablets\n4135\nZine Sulphate Tablets, Dispersible\n4135\nZinc Sulphate Oral Solution\n298, 11343995\nZinc Undecenoate\n3995\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment\n3995\nZinc Undecylenate\n3095\nZinc Undecylenate Ointment\nVolume 1: i to xxiv and 1 to 1276; 1-1 to 1-4 \nVolume 4: xliii to xlvi and 4785 to 5024; I"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/white-beeswax","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"White Beeswax"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"White Beeswax\nWhite Beeswax is obtained by bleaching Yellow Beeswax.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid. ?\nDescription. Yellowish-white pieces or plates, translucent when \nthin, with a fine-grained, matt, non-crystalline fracture; \nbecomes soft and pliable when warmed by hand."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/white-petroleum-jelly","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"White Petroleum Jelly"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"White Petroleum Jelly\nWhite Soft Paraffin is a purified, semi-solid mixture of \nhydrocarbons obtained from petroleum and bleached.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (ointment base).\nDescription. A white, translucent, soft unctuous mass, \nretaining these characteristics on storage and when melted \nand allowed to cool without stirring; not more than slightly \nfluorescent by daylight, even melted; odourless when rubbed \non the skin."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/white-soft-paraffin","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"White Soft Paraffin"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"White Soft Paraffin\nWhite Petroleum Jelly ■ ;\nWhite Soft Paraffin is a purified, semi-solid mixture of \nhydrocarbons obtained from petroleum and bleached.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (ointment base). , ,\nDescription. A white, translucent, soft unctuous mass, \nretaining these characteristics on storage and when melted \nand allowed to cool without stirring; not more than slightly \nfluorescent by daylight, even melted."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/whole-human-blood","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Whole Human Blood"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Whole Human Blood\nWhole Blood (Human)\nHuman tetanus immunoglobulin reference  preparation is \ncalibrated in International Units by comparison with the\nWhole Human Blood is blood drawn aseptically from selected \nhuman donors and mixed with a suitable anticoagulant.\nInternational Standard. \nU \nSelection of animals. Use mice weighing 16g to 20g.\nWhole Human Blood is the final mixture of blood and \nanticoagulant solution contains not less than 9.7 per cent \nw/v of haemoglobin, calculated from the haemoglobin content \nof the donor's blood and the dilution due to the anticoagulant \nsolution. It is obtained from healthy donors who must:\nPreparation of the test toxin. Prepare the test toxin by a \nsuitable method from the sterile filtrate of a culture in liquid \nmedium of C tetani.  The two methods shown below are given \nas examples and any other suitable method may be used.\nThe test is not valid unless all the mice injected with mixtures \ncontaining 2.0 ml or less of the solution of the reference \npreparation show paralysis and all those injected with mixtures \ncontaining more do not.\n(a) be in the age range of 18 to 60 years and be in good health\n1. To the filtrate of an approximately 9-day culture, add 1 to \n2 volumes of glycerol and store the mixture in the liquid \nstate at a temperature slightly below 0°.\nas indicated in part by normal temperature and blood \npressure within normal limits;"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/wintergreen-oil","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Wintergreen Oil"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Wintergreen Oil\nCOOCH3 .\nCgH8O3 \n. \nMol. Wt. 152.2\nMethyl Salicylate is 2-hydroxybenzoic acid methyl ester.\nMethyl Salicylate contains not less than 99.0 per cent w/w \nand not more than 100.5 per cent w/w of C8HgO3.\nCategory. Counter-irritant.\nDescription. A colourless or slightly yellow liquid."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/woods","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Woods"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Woods\nWood normally is the central portion ofthe stem or the root of \nthe perennial trees. Commercial supplies of wood may be in \nlarge and dense (Sandal wood) pieces either horizontally or \ntransversely cut pieces or as shavings, raspings and chips \n(Quassia wood) to aid drying.\nWood is differentiated as heartwood or duramen and the\nSeeds\nsapwood or alburnum consisting of the conducting tissues. \nHeartwood is the dead tissue darker in colour and adds only \nto the mechanical strength. Tannins and resins are deposited \nm this tissue. Sapwood forms the drug.Terms that describe \ndifferent characteristics of fracture are ring, porous and \ndiffused. Markings are described with terms like annual rings,\nstraight, grained or interlocked grains.-Woods may be either \nheavy or light in nature.\nRoots and under-ground modifications of stem\nHerbs both roots and under-ground modifications of stem \ninclude rhizomes, corm, stolon, tubers and annular roots.\nBroad guidance is provided related to roots or modified \nunderground stems.\nTable 1 .Herbs from roots, stems and their modifications\nPart \nExamples\nRoots only \nAconitum napellus (tuberous), Atropa\nbella-donna, Rauvolfla serpentina, \nCarapichea ipecacuanha (annular \nroots)\nStem modifications\nColchicum autumnale (corm), \nDioscorea alata (tuber), Zingiber\nofficinale (rhizome), Curcuma longa \n(rhizome), Rheum (rhizome)\nHerbs containing \nroot and rhizome\nGentian, Picrorhiza kurroa, Podo­\nphyllum peltatum, Valeriana\nofficinalis\nHerbs containing \nroot and stolon\nGlycyrrhiza glabra\nTable 2 - Morphological difference between stem and root\nCharacter\nStem & Under- \nRoot\nground stem \nmodifications\nPosition\nAerial \nUnderground\nGrowth\nVertical, Horizontal, Vertical \nor Oblique\nForm\nStraight \nTortuous\n. Present \nAbsent\nLeaves, scale \nleaves or scars\nNode and inter nodes Present \nAbsent\nPresent \nAbsent\nTerminal or axillary \nbuds\nCentre (dicotyledons) Soft (pith) \nHard\nThe Seed is represented by embryo (plumule, radical and one \nor two cotyledons), endosperm (storage tissue containing \nprotein and fat), nucellus (perisperm containing starch), seed \ncoat, coverings of the ovule (testa and tegmen), pericarp \n(separate or fuse with the seed coat).There are two types of \nseeds: Endospermous or Albuminous and Non-endospermous \nor Exalbuminous.\n389\nwww.webofpharma.com\nTable 3- Types of seeds\nNon-endosperrhous or\nEndospermous or \nAlbuminous.\nExalbuminous\nFood storage outside embryo\nFood storage in the embryo\nThe embiyo is small and \nendosperm remains as a \npermanent food storage \ntissue of the seed\nThe growing embryo \ncompletely uses up the \nendosperm resulting in the \nembryo occupying the \ncomplete seed cavity. A thin \nlayer may be present some \ntimes as inPrunus \namygdaluslfrmonC) seeds\ni. Herbs as only endosperm Herbs as oily cotyledons:\nexample Strychnos mix-- SKarafre Amygdalus\n■vomica and Plant ago \ncommunis (bitter almond),\n.. (isphagula) \nArachis hypogaea\n(groundnut) and Brassica \nnigra (mustard) seed\nii Herbs as endosperm\nand perisperm example \nMyristica fragrans \n(nutmeg), Elettaria \ncardamomum (cardamom) \nand Areca catechu\nKernel forms the herb in many seeds. It is embryo with \ncotyledons covered with a thin testa. The markings of the \nseeds can be seen on the kernel as depressions or grooves \nindicating the positions on the seed. Different parts of ovule \nare seen on the seed: seed coat, the outer and inner \ninteguments of the ovule form the testa and tegmen of the \nseed. The characteristics of the seed coat help in the \nidentificationof the seed at species, genus or family level!\nHilum, the point of attachment of the stalk or funicle; \nmicropyle, the small opening at the apex of the integuments;\nAmphitropous ovule ‘\nRaphe is short; half the length\n■\"-of seed\n2.6.6. EVALUATION OF HERBS AND,PROCESSED HERBS\nIP 2022\nchalaza, the point not, visible but can be seen as a depression \nin some seeds; Raphe, the extension of funicle growing along \nside ofthe ovule,in some seeds there are outgrowths of tissues \n(\n"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/wool-fat","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Wool Fat"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Wool Fat\nHeavy metals (2.3.13). Evaporate 150 ml to 15 ml on a water-\nbath. 12 ml of the solution complies with the limit'test for \nheavy metals, Method D (0.1 ppm). Use lead standard solution \n(I ppm Ph) to prepare the standard.\nAnhydrous Lanolin\nWool Fat is purified, anhydrous, waxy material obtained from \nthe wool of sheep. It may contain Butylated Hydroxytoluene\nChlorides. Boil 1.0 g with 20 ml of ethanol (90 per cent) under \na reflux condenser for 5 minutes, cool, add 40 ml of water and \n0.5 ml of nitric acid and filter. To the filtrate add 0.15 ml of a \n1 per cent w/v solution of silver nitrate in ethanol (90 per \ncent). After 5 minutes, protected from light; any opalescence \nproduced is not more intense than that obtained by adding \n0.15 ml ofa 1 per cent w/v solution of si Iver nitrate in ethanol \n(90 per cent) to a mixture of 0.2 ml of 0.02 M hydrochloric \nacid, 20 ml of ethanol (90 per cent), 40 ml of water and 0.5 ml \nof nitric acid (150 ppm).\nChlorides.To 10 ml, add 1 ml of 2 M nitric acid and 0.2 ml of \n0.1 M silver nitrate; the appearance of the solution does not \nchange for at least 15 minutes.\nas an antioxidant.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (absorbent ointment base).\nDescription. A pale yellow, unctuous substance; odour, \ncharacteristic.\nFor containers with a nominal volume of 100 ml or less, 15 ml \ncomplies with the limit test for chlorides (2.3.12) (0.5 ppm), \nusing a standard solution prepared by mixing 1.5 ml of chloride \nstandard solution (5 ppm Cl) and 13.5 ml of water."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/working","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Working"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Working\n(Celis at or \nbeypnd the\ncell \nbank \n(WCB)\ncell \nbank \n(MCB)\nmaximum \npopulation\ndoubling\nlevel\nused for \nproduction)\n1. Identity and Purity\n+\nMorphology \n+\nT\n4-\n+’ '\n+ :"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/xanthan-gum","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Xanthan Gum"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Xanthan Gum\nXanthan Gum is high-molecular-mass anionic polysaccharide\nproduced by fermentation ofcarbohydrates withXanthomonas\ncampestris. It consists ofa principal chain ofb(1-lJ1.)-linked\no-glucose units with trisaccharide side chains, on alternating\nanhydroglucose units, consisting of 1 glucuronic acid unit\nincluded between 2 mannose units. Most ofthe terminal units\ncontain a pyruvate moiety and the mannose unit adjacent to\nthe principal chain may be acetylated at C-6.\nXanthan Gum has a relative molecular mass ofapproximately\n1 x 106. It exists as the sodium, potassium or calcium salt.\nXanthan Gum contains not less than 1.5 per cent ofpyruvoyl\ngroups, (C3H30 2; Mol. Wt. 71.1), calculate on the dried basis.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (Excipient).\nDescription. Awhite or yellowish-white, free-flowing powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/xylometazoline-hydrochloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Xylometazoline Hydrochloride"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Xylometazoline Hydrochloride\nCI6H2~2,HCI\nMol. Wt. 280.8\nXylometazoline Hydrochloride is 2-(4-tert-butyl-\n2,6-dimethylbenzyl)-2-inlidazoline hydrochloride.\nXylometazoline Hydrochloride contains not less than 99.0 per\ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of C16H24N2' HCl,\ncalculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Sympathomimetic amine.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder;\nodourless or almost odourless."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/xylose","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Xylose"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Xylose\nD-Xylose; D-Xylopyranose\nQ5H10O5\nXylose contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more\nthan' 102.0 per cent of C5Hi0O5, calculated on the dried basis.\nCategory. Diagnostic aid in intestinal malfunction.\nDescription. Colourless needles or a white, crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/yasti-dry-extract","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Yasti Dry Extract"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Yasti Dry Extract\nYasti Dry Extract is obtained by extracting Yasti with water or \naqueous ethanol or any other suitable solvent.\nYasfiDry Extract contains not less than 90.0 per cent w/w and\n.not more than 120.0 per cent w/w of the stated amount of\nglycyrrhizinic acid.\nCategory. Expectorant, antiulcer, stomachic, antiinflammatory, \nkasa.\nDescription. A yellowish-brown to dark brown powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/yasti","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Yasti"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Yasti\nTLC Chromatogram of Vijayasara"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/yellow-beeswax","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Yellow Beeswax"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Yellow Beeswax\nYellow beeswax is the wax obtained by melting the walls of the \nhoneycomb of the bee, Apis mellifera Linn, with hot water \nand removing the foreign matter.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. Yellow or light brown pieces or plates, with a \nfine-grained, matt, non-crystalline fracture; becomes soft and \npliable when warmed by hand. It is tasteless and does not \nstick to the teeth."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/yellow-petroleum-jelly","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Yellow Petroleum Jelly"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Yellow Petroleum Jelly \n;\nYellow Soft Paraffin is a purified, semi-solid mixture Of \nhydrocarbons obtained from petroleum. ■ \n; ?\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (ointment base).\nDescription. A pale yellow to yellow, translucent, soft \nunctuous mass, retaining these characteristics on storage and \nwhen melted and allowed to cool without stirring; not more \nthan slightly fluorescent by daylight, even, melted."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zanamivir","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zanamivir"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zanamivir\nC12H20m,xH2O \nMol. Wt. 332.3\n(anhydrous form)\nZanamivir is D-glycero-£>-galacto-Non-2-enonic acid, 5- \n(acetyIamino)-4-[(aminoiminomethyl)amino]-2,6-anhydro- \n3,4,5-trideoxy.\n3983\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZANAMIVIR\nZanamivir contains not less than 98.0 per cent and not more \nthan 102.0 per cent of C12H20N4O7, calculated on the anhydrous \nbasis. ■\nCategory . Neuraminidase inhibitor.\nDescription. A white crystalline powder, it contains variable \nquantities of water."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zidovudine-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zidovudine Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zidovudine Injection\nZidovudine Capsules contain not less than 90.0 per cent and \nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nzidovudine, C10II13N5O4. \n;\nCalculate the content of CwHb W-T in the medium.\nZidovudine Injection is a sterile solution of Zidovudine in\nQ. Not less than 75 per cent of the stated amount of\nWater for Injections. - ~ \n—\nZidovudine Injection contains not less than.90.0 per cent and\nCioHijNjO.-\nElated; substances. Determine by liquid chromatography\nUsual strength. 100 mg. \n:\nnot more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zidovudine-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zidovudine Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zidovudine Oral Solution \nJ W\ntest.\nZidovudine Oral Solution is a solution of Zidovudi^^; \nsuitable flavoured vehicle.\nTrans^r W-P ml of the reference \nritipff^rence splutioa(b) to a,IQO-ml \n^^^^^ask>and make up. lo the volume with the: mobile\nDetermine by infrared absorption spectrophotometry (2.4.6)'. \nCompare.the spectrum with that obtained with zidovudine \nIP RS or with the reference spectrum of zidovudine. ,\nZidovudine Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0^^\nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated àhioig^ \nzidovudine, CioH^NsO^ \n'"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zidovudine","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zidovudine"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zidovudine\nMol. Wt. 267.2\nZidovudine is 1-(3-azido-2,3-dideoxy-{3-o-ribofuranosyl)-\n5-methylpyrimidine-2,4(lH,3H)-dione.\nZidovudine contains not less than 97.0 per cent and not more\nthan 102.0 per cent ofC IOH13Ns0 4, calculated on the anhydrous\nbasis.\nCategory. Antiretroviral.\nDose. 300 mg twice daily.\nDescription. A white or almost white powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-chloride-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Chloride Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Chloride Injection\nZinc Chloride Injection is a sterile solution of Zinc Chloride in \nWater for Injection.\nZinc Chloride Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent \nand not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nzinc, Zn.\nUsual strength. 1 mg per ml.\nIn the Assay, the test solution exhibits an absorption maximum \nat about 213.8 nm.\npH (2.4.24). 1.5 to 2.5.\nBacterial endotoxins (2.2.3). Not more than 25.0 Endotoxin \nUnits per mg of zinc.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Parenteral \nPreparations (Inj ections).\nAssay. Sodium chloride solution. Dissolve 450 mg of sodium \nchloride in water and dilute to 500 ml with water.\n4133\nwww.webofpharma.com"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-chloride","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Chloride"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Chloride\nZnCI2\nMol. Wt. 136.3\nZinc Chloride contains not less than 95.0 per cent and not \nmore than 100.5 per cent of ZnCl2.\nCategory . Pharmaceutical aid.\nDescription. A white or practically white, crystalline powder; \nvery deliquescent."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-citrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Citrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Citrate\nCi2Hi0O14Zn3,2H2Q: \n, Mol. Wt. 610.4\n4134\n. -..................................:............ \nf\nZinc Citrate is 2-1 lydroxy-1,2,3-propanetricarboxylic acidzinc\nZinc Citrate contains not less than 31.3 per cent ofz^ \ncalculated on the dried basis. \n'\nCategory. Mineral supplement\nDescription. A white or almost white powder. ’ \ny"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-oxide-and-salicylic-acid-paste","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Oxide and Salicylic Acid Paste"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Oxide and Salicylic Acid Paste\nZinc Oxide and Salicylic Acid Paste contains not less than \n22.5 per cent and not more than 25.5 per cent w/w of zinc \noxide, ZnO and not. less than 1.9 per cent and not more than \n2 J-per cent w/w bf salicylic acid, C7H6O3.\nZinc oxide, finely sifted \n240 g\nSalicylic acid, finely sifted \n20 g\nStarch, finely sifted \n240 g\nWhite Soft Paraffin \n. 500 g\nExtemporaneous Preparation. Melt the soft paraffin, \nincorporate the Zinc Oxide, the Salicylic Acid and the starch \nand stir until cold.\nUsual strength, 24 per cent of zinc oxide and 2 per cent of \nsalicylic acid w/w. ' . \n, \n- ;\nA. Shake 1 g of substance under examination in lOmlofrra/er, \nand filter. To thé filtrate, add 1.0 ix& oí ferric chloride solution; \nan intense reddish violet colour is produced. To the solution, \nadd 1 ml of acetic acid; the colour is not dispersed. To the \nsolution, add 2.0 ml of 2 Mhydrochloric acid; the colour is \ndispersed and a white crystalline precipitate is formed? ’\nB. Heat 0.5 g gently in a porcelain dish over a very small flame \nuntil the base is completely volatile or charred. Increase the \nheal until all the carbon is removed. The residue is yellow; \nwhen hot and white; when cool, . _. . . . \n,.\nOther tests. Comply with thé tests stated under Topical Semi­\nSolid Preparations. ,\nAssay ; For''Salicylic Acid— : Shake-500 mg of the paste in\n10 ml of 1 M hydrochloric acid and add 10 ml of ether until \nfully dispersed. Decant and reserve the aqueous layer. Extract \nthe ether layer with two further 10 ml quantities of 1 M \nhydrochloric acid, combine the:, aqueous extracts with the \nreserve aqueous layer, wash with 10 ml of ether and reserve \nfor the Assay of Zinc Oxide. Combine the ether extract, add\n15 ml of petroleum spirit (boiling range, 40° to 60°)and \nextract with successive quantities of 20 mJ, 10 ml and 10 ml of \na mixture of equal volumes of ethanol (95 per cent) and 1 M \nsodium hydroxide. Dilute the combined extracts to 100 ml \nwith 2 Mhydrochloric «czX further dilute 15 ml bf the resulting \nsolution to 50 ml with the same solvent and measure the \nabsorbance of the final solution at the maximum at 302 nm \n(2.4.7.) Calculate the content of C-H6O3 using260 as a specific \nabsorbance at 302 nm.\nFor Zinc Oxide ~ To the combined aqueous extracte obtained\nin the Assay for Salicylic Acid,, add 20 ml of■ 1 M sodium \nhydroxide and 50 mg of xylenol .orange triturate.. To the\n3993\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZINC STEARATE \n.up/- •\nresulting solution, add sufficient hexamine to change the \ncolour of the solution to red and then a further 3 g of hexamine \nand titrate with 0.1M disodium edetate. Carry but a blank \ntitration. HUrKiTí/ VU/'P //■; or:/ /.Ui ■■■...■ ..\nÍ ml of 0.1 Mdisodium edetate is equivalent to 0.008139 g of \nzinc oxide, ZnO.\nStorage. Stoic protected frommoisture.\nZinc Stearate .. . ..^,.....¿.3 7....\n(CI7H35COO)2Zn r / A Mol. Wt.632.3\nZinc Stearate consists mainly of zinc stearate hut many \ncontain variable proportions of zinc palmitate (Cj 5H3! COO)2 \nZn, and zinc oleate (Ci7H33COO)2Zn.\nZinc Stearate contains not less than 10.0 per -cent and not \nmore than 12.0 per cent of zinc, Zn.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (dustingponder).\nDescription. Afine, white,¡bulky,amorphous powder, free from \ngrittiness. \n. •--v. •"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-oxide-cream","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Oxide Cream"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Oxide Cream\nZinc Cream\nZinc Oxide Cream contains 32 per cent w/v ofZinc Oxide in a\nsuitable water-in-oil emulsified base.\nZinc Oxide Cream contains not less than 30.0 per cent and not\nmore than 34.0 per cent w/w ofzinc oxide, ZnO."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-oxide","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Oxide"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Oxide\nÀi-\nZnO\nMol. Wt 81.4\nZinc Oxide contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not ■ ;'f' \nmore than 100.5 per cent of ZnO, calculated on the ignited a \nbasis. . \n; \n;\nCategory. Mild astringent; topical protectant? 1 \n; ‘; \n‘\nDescription. A soft, white or faintly yellowish white \namorphous powder, free from grittiness. It gradually absorbs \ncarbon dioxide from air. \n: A"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-stearate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Stearate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Stearate\nZn \t\nMol. Wt. 632.3\nZinc Stearate consists mainly of zinc stearate but many \ncontain variable proportions of zinc palmitate (CI5H3IC00)2 \nZn, and zinc oleate (C I,H33C00)2Zn.\nZinc Stearate contains not less than 10.0 per cent and not \nmore than 12.0 per cent of zinc, Zn.\nCategory. Pharmaceutical aid (dusting powder).\nDescription. A fine, white, bulky, amorphous powder, free from \ngrittiness; odour, faint and characteristic."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-sulphate-monohydrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate\npH (2.4.24). 2.5 to 4.5.\nZnSO4,H2O \nMol. Wt. 179.5\nZinc Sulphate Monohydrate contains not less than 99.0 per \ncent and not more than 101.0 per cent of ZnSO4,H2O.\nCategory. Astringent.\nDescription. A white or almost white, crystalline powder or \ncolourless, transparent crystals."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-sulphate-oral-solution","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate Oral Solution"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate Oral Solution\nZinc Sulphate Oral Solution contains not less than 90.0 per \ncent and not more than 110.0 per cent of the stated amount of \nzinc sulphate monohydrate, Zn SO4.H2O. It may contain one \nor more suitable flavors and sweeteners.\nA. Complies with the test for sulphates (2.3.1).\nB. Complies with the test for zinc (2.3.1).\nWeight per ml (2.4.29). 1.18 g to 1.24 g.\nOther tests. Comply with the tests stated under Oral Liquids.\nAssay. To a 250-ml flask, transfer a volume of oral solution \ncontaining about 99 mg of Zinc Sulphate Monohydrate, add \n50 ml of water and 10 ml of ammonia- ammonium chloride \nbuffer and 0.3 ml of eriochrome black Tsolution. Titrate with \n0.05 Mdisodium edetate to a green colour.\n1 ml of 0.05 Mdisodiulm edetate is equivalent to 0.008973 g \nofZnSO4.H2O.\nStorage. Store protected from light and moisture.\nLabelling. Label the oral solution in terms of zinc sulphate \nmonohydrate and in terms of elemental zinc.\nZinc Sulphate Dispersible Tablets\nDispersible Zinc Sulphate Tablets\nZinc Sulphate Dispersible Tablets contain not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated\n4135\nwww.webofpharma.com\nZINC SULPHATE DISPERSIBLE TABLETS\namount of zinc sulphate monohydrate, ZnSO4)H2O. It may \ncontain one or more suitable flavours and sweeteners.\nUsual strengths. 10 mg; 20 mg of elemental zinc (as zinc \nsulphate monohydrate)."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-sulphate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Sulphate\nZnSO4,7H2O\nMol. Wt. 287.5\nZinc Sulphate contains not less than 99.0 per cent and not \nmore than 104.0 per cent of ZnSO4,7H2O. ■\nCategory. Astringent. \n' \n'( \n'\nDescription. Colourless, transparent crystals or a white, \ncrystalline powder; efflorescent \n. ,"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-undecenoate-ointment","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Undecenoate Ointment"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Undecenoate Ointment\nZinc Undecylenate Ointment\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment contains 20 per cent w/v ofZinc\nUndecenoate in a suitable ointment basis.\nZinc Undecenoate Ointment contains not less than 18.0 per\ncent and not more than 22.0 per cent of zinc undecenoate,\nC22H3S04Zn, and not less than 4.5 per cent and not more than\n5,5 per cent offree undecenoic acid, CIIH2002."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zinc-undecenoate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zinc Undecenoate"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zinc Undecenoate\nCZZH3S04Zn\nMol. Wt. 431.9\nZinc Undecenoate is zinc di(undec-lO-enoate).\nIP 2010\nZinc Undecenoate contains not less than 98.0 per cent and\nnot more than 102.0 per cent ofCzzH3s04Zn, calculated on the\ndried basis.\nCategory. Antifungal (topical).\nDescription. A white or almost white, fine powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zoledronic-acid-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zoledronic Acid Injection"},"edition":"ip-2010","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zoledronic Acid Injection\nZoledronic Acid Injection is prepared by dissolving the\ncontents of a sealed container containing Zoledronic Acid\nwith or without auxiliary substances in the requisite a.mount\nofwater for injection as directed on the label.\nThe constituted solution complies with the requirements for\nClarity of solution and Particulate matter stated under\nParenteral Preparations (Injections).\nUsual strength. 4 mg per ml.\nStorage. The constituted solution should be used immediately\nafter preparation but, in any case, within the period\nrecommended by the manufacturer.\nZoledronic Acid Injection contains not less than 90.0 per cent\nand notmore than 110.0 per centofstated amount ofzoledronic\nacid, CSHION207P2.\nThe contents of the sealed container comply with the\nrequirements stated under Parenteral Preparations\n(Powdersfor Injection) andwith thefollowing requirements:"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zoledronic-acid","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zoledronic Acid"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zoledronic Acid\nZoledronic Acid Monohydrate? ;\nP<\n0' 0H\nG,H10N2O7P2,H2O \n■ MoLWt 290.1\nZoledronic Acid is [1 -hydroxy-2-( 1 H-imidazol-\nl-yl)ethylidene]dipliosphbnic acid, monphydrate.\nZoledronic Acid contains not less than .98.0 per cent and not \nmore than 102,0 per cent of Ç5H10N2Q7P2, calculated on the \nanhydrous basis. ,.-,l • i-.c ?; n.<>r: ■. ■?:\nCategory. Bone resorption inhibitor.\nDescription. A white to almost White crystalline powder."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zolpidem-tartrate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zolpidem Tartrate"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zolpidem Tartrate\n(Ci9H2iNjÔ)2/C4H6O6 \nMol. Wt. 765.0\nZolpidem Tartrate is bis[X^-dimethyl-2-[6-methyl-2-(4- \nmethÿlphényl)imidazo[r,2-a]pyridine-3-yl]acetaniidej tartrate.\nZolpidem Tartrate contains not less than 98.5 per cent and hot \nmore than 101.0 per cent of (C|9l-I2;N3O)2, C4H6C\\ calculated \non the anhydrous basis. ’ * \n'\nCategory.Sedatives.\nDescription ^ white or almost white crystalline powder.\nt • / ,<•’ \nt' - : ' ; \n. \n• •>: \n-"}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zuclopenthixol-acetate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Acetate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Acetate Injection\nZuclopenthixol Acetate Injection is a sterile solution of \nZuclopenthixol Acetate in a suitable vegetable oil.\nZuclopenthixol Acetate. Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of C24H27CIN2O2S."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zuclopenthixol-acetate","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Acetate"},"edition":"ip-2018","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Acetate\nC24H27C1N2O2S \t\nMol. Wt.443.0\nZuclopenthixol Acetate is (Z)-2-14-[3-(2-chlorothioxanthene-\n9-ylidene)propyl]piperazin-l-y1} ethyl acetate.\nZuclopenthixol Acetate contains not less than 98.0 per cent \nand not more than 102.0 per cent of C241 -127C1N202S, calculated \non the dried basis.\nCategory. Neuroleptic.\nDescription. A yellowish, viscous oil."}},{"@id":"https://www.openphar.org/data/ip/monographs/zuclopenthixol-decanoate-injection","@type":"IndexedMonograph","prefLabel":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Decanoate Injection"},"edition":"ip-2022","category":"monographs","definition":{"en":"Zuclopenthixol Decanoate Injection\nZùclopenthixoi Decanoate Injection is a sterile solution of \nZuclopenthixol Decanoate in a suitable vegetable oil.\nZuclopenthixol Decanoate Injection contains not less than \n95.0 per cent and not more than 105.0 per cent of the stated \namount ofzuclopenthixol decanoate,"}}]}